('a95d19b9-097c-59a0-b10c-8586ba2e7e14'::uuid,'ZQ-0001','adult-medicine','cardiology','A 78-year-old woman sees her general practitioner in Ballarat, Victoria, after the practice nurse noticed an irregular pulse during a routine health assessment. She has had occasional palpitations for two months but no chest pain, syncope or breathlessness. She has hypertension treated with perindopril 5 mg daily and knee osteoarthritis, for which she takes paracetamol 1 g up to three times daily. She has no history of stroke, transient ischaemic attack, diabetes, heart failure, vascular disease or bleeding. She drinks one standard drink a week and has never smoked.

Her pulse is 84/min and irregularly irregular, and her blood pressure is 136/82 mmHg. She weighs 57 kg. The rest of the examination is unremarkable. A 12-lead ECG confirms atrial fibrillation with a ventricular rate of 82/min. Transthoracic echocardiography shows a structurally normal heart with no mitral stenosis and no prosthetic valve.

Investigations:
- Haemoglobin 128 g/L (115–160)
- Platelets 245 × 10^9/L (150–400)
- Serum creatinine 118 µmol/L (45–90)
- Creatinine clearance (Cockcroft–Gault) 31 mL/min
- Liver function tests normal

After discussion, she agrees to start oral anticoagulation.

Which one of the following is the most appropriate regimen?','["Apixaban 2.5 mg orally twice daily", "Dabigatran 150 mg orally twice daily", "Apixaban 5 mg orally twice daily", "Rivaroxaban 20 mg orally once daily", "Aspirin 100 mg orally once daily"]'::jsonb,'C','CORRECT ANSWER

C. Apixaban 5 mg orally twice daily

---

Her CHA2DS2-VA score is 3: age 75 years or over scores 2 and hypertension scores 1. The 2018 NHFA/CSANZ Australian AF guideline dropped female sex from the score. A score of 2 or more means oral anticoagulation is recommended, and a NOAC (DOAC) is preferred over warfarin in non-valvular AF. The Australian product information says to reduce apixaban to 2.5 mg twice daily only when at least TWO of these apply: age 80 years or over, weight 60 kg or less, serum creatinine 133 µmol/L or more. She meets only one (weight 57 kg). She is 78, and her creatinine of 118 µmol/L is below 133. So the standard 5 mg twice daily is correct. Her creatinine clearance of 31 mL/min is above the level where the Australian PI contraindicates apixaban (below 25 mL/min).

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Use CHA2DS2-VA in Australia (no point for female sex). Reduce apixaban to 2.5 mg twice daily only if 2 of these 3 apply: age 80 or over, weight 60 kg or less, creatinine 133 µmol/L or more.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "NHFA/CSANZ Australian clinical guidelines for the diagnosis and management of atrial fibrillation 2018 (MJA)", "url": "https://www.mja.com.au/journal/2018/209/8/national-heart-foundation-australia-and-cardiac-society-australia-and-new"}
• {"name": "Eliquis (apixaban) Australian Product Information, sections 4.2 and 4.3", "url": "https://rss.medsinfo.com.au/bq/pi.cfm?product=bqpeliqu"}
• {"name": "Therapeutic Guidelines (eTG complete): Cardiovascular, atrial fibrillation (subscription)", "url": "https://www.tg.org.au"}

---

EDITED BY HEISENBERG','difficult','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "bank170", "review_status": "owner_approved_2026_09_29", "tags": ["ZQ-0001", "zyntra_bank", "pilot", "level_4", "task_management", "group_adult_medicine", "sys_cardiovascular", "approved_by_owner_2026_09_29"], "clinician_task": "management", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"NHFA/CSANZ Australian clinical guidelines for the diagnosis and management of atrial fibrillation 2018 (MJA)\", \"url\": \"https://www.mja.com.au/journal/2018/209/8/national-heart-foundation-australia-and-cardiac-society-australia-and-new\"}; {\"name\": \"Eliquis (apixaban) Australian Product Information, sections 4.2 and 4.3\", \"url\": \"https://rss.medsinfo.com.au/bq/pi.cfm?product=bqpeliqu\"}; {\"name\": \"Therapeutic Guidelines (eTG complete): Cardiovascular, atrial fibrillation (subscription)\", \"url\": \"https://www.tg.org.au\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "A common error: under-dosing because of low weight or reduced renal function alone. Only one dose-reduction criterion applies (weight 60 kg or less), and two are needed. Under-dosing apixaban gives less protection against stroke.", "B": "Dabigatran is contraindicated in Australia when CrCl is below 30 mL/min, and she is only just above that at 31 mL/min. It is also mostly cleared by the kidneys, and 110 mg twice daily is generally preferred at age 75 or over. 150 mg twice daily is not appropriate here.", "D": "Rivaroxaban is a reasonable drug, but the dose is wrong. With CrCl 30–49 mL/min the AF dose is 15 mg once daily, not 20 mg.", "E": "Aspirin is not recommended for stroke prevention in AF. It is much less effective than anticoagulation and its bleeding risk is not meaningfully lower in older people."}, "key_takeaways": ["Use CHA2DS2-VA in Australia (no point for female sex). Reduce apixaban to 2.5 mg twice daily only if 2 of these 3 apply: age 80 or over, weight 60 kg or less, creatinine 133 µmol/L or more."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,1.0,1.0,'tier_prior','tier=4 (bank170)'),
('b923ad31-b730-5772-93ed-6baafa8cb8cd'::uuid,'ZQ-0002','adult-medicine','respiratory','A 32-year-old woman comes to the emergency department of a Brisbane hospital with two days of left-sided chest pain. The pain is sharp, worse on deep inspiration and twisting, and started the morning after she moved house. She has no cough, haemoptysis, fever or leg symptoms. She takes a combined oral contraceptive pill and has no other medical history. She has not travelled, had surgery or been immobilised recently, and has no personal or family history of venous thromboembolism.

Her temperature is 36.8°C, pulse 92/min, blood pressure 124/78 mmHg, respiratory rate 16/min and oxygen saturation 98% on room air. Firm pressure over the left sixth costochondral junction reproduces part of her pain. Heart sounds are normal, the chest is clear, and there is no calf swelling or tenderness. ECG shows sinus rhythm with no abnormality, and a chest X-ray is normal. Urine β-hCG is negative. The treating team judges that a musculoskeletal cause is more likely than pulmonary embolism.

Which one of the following is the most appropriate next step?','["Discharge with no further testing for pulmonary embolism", "Measure a D-dimer level", "Arrange a CT pulmonary angiogram", "Arrange a ventilation–perfusion (V/Q) scan", "Arrange bilateral lower-limb venous duplex ultrasound"]'::jsonb,'B','CORRECT ANSWER

B. Measure a D-dimer level

---

Her pre-test probability is low. She scores 0 on the Wells score: no criteria are met, and PE is not the most likely diagnosis, so the +3 item does not apply. In a low-probability patient, the next question is whether she is PERC-negative, because a PERC-negative patient needs no further testing. PERC needs ALL eight criteria to be negative: age under 50, pulse under 100, SpO2 95% or more, no haemoptysis, no exogenous oestrogen, no previous VTE, no unilateral leg swelling, and no recent surgery or trauma. She uses the combined oral contraceptive pill (exogenous oestrogen), so she is PERC-positive. PE therefore cannot be ruled out clinically, and the next step is a D-dimer (age-adjusted where the local pathway uses this). A negative D-dimer excludes PE in a low-probability patient. A positive D-dimer leads to imaging.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
At low pre-test probability, PERC rules out PE only if all eight criteria are negative. Oestrogen use (e.g. the combined pill) makes her PERC-positive, so do a D-dimer.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "NSW Agency for Clinical Innovation (ECI): Pulmonary thromboembolism evaluation pathway (Wells, PERC, age-adjusted D-dimer)", "url": "https://aci.health.nsw.gov.au/networks/eci/clinical/tools/respiratory/pe/pulmonary-thromboembolism-pe---evaluation-pathway"}
• {"name": "Therapeutic Guidelines (eTG complete): Cardiovascular, venous thromboembolism (subscription)", "url": "https://www.tg.org.au"}

---

EDITED BY HEISENBERG','moderate','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "bank170", "review_status": "owner_approved_2026_09_29", "tags": ["ZQ-0002", "zyntra_bank", "pilot", "level_3", "task_data_gathering", "group_adult_medicine", "sys_respiratory", "approved_by_owner_2026_09_29"], "clinician_task": "data_gathering", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"NSW Agency for Clinical Innovation (ECI): Pulmonary thromboembolism evaluation pathway (Wells, PERC, age-adjusted D-dimer)\", \"url\": \"https://aci.health.nsw.gov.au/networks/eci/clinical/tools/respiratory/pe/pulmonary-thromboembolism-pe---evaluation-pathway\"}; {\"name\": \"Therapeutic Guidelines (eTG complete): Cardiovascular, venous thromboembolism (subscription)\", \"url\": \"https://www.tg.org.au\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "This would be right only if she were PERC-negative. Oestrogen-containing contraception is one of the eight PERC criteria, so she cannot be discharged without a D-dimer.", "C": "CTPA is for high (PE likely) pre-test probability or a positive D-dimer. Going straight to CTPA in a low-probability patient exposes her to unnecessary radiation, contrast and the risk of incidental findings.", "D": "A V/Q scan is an alternative to CTPA, for example in pregnancy, contrast allergy or renal impairment. It is still imaging, and imaging is not the next step before D-dimer at low pre-test probability.", "E": "Leg ultrasound can support a diagnosis of VTE when there are leg signs or CTPA is contraindicated. She has no leg symptoms, and a negative leg ultrasound would not exclude PE."}, "key_takeaways": ["At low pre-test probability, PERC rules out PE only if all eight criteria are negative. Oestrogen use (e.g. the combined pill) makes her PERC-positive, so do a D-dimer."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,0.0,1.0,'tier_prior','tier=3 (bank170)'),
('d3049df6-5c08-5899-a8c5-c3e359782aa6'::uuid,'ZQ-0003','adult-medicine','endocrinology','A 58-year-old man is on the surgical ward of a regional hospital in New South Wales, 36 hours after an emergency laparoscopic cholecystectomy for acute cholecystitis. He has type 2 diabetes treated with empagliflozin/metformin 12.5 mg/1000 mg twice daily. He took his usual morning dose on the day he was admitted and went to theatre that evening. Since surgery he has eaten very little because of nausea. The nurse calls you because he has vomited three times, feels exhausted and is breathing quickly.

His temperature is 37.4°C, pulse 112/min, blood pressure 118/72 mmHg, respiratory rate 28/min and oxygen saturation 97% on room air. His abdomen is soft, with appropriate port-site tenderness and no peritonism. His last HbA1c was 8.9% (74 mmol/mol).

Venous blood gas and biochemistry:
- pH 7.14 (7.35–7.45)
- pCO2 21 mmHg (35–45)
- Bicarbonate 8 mmol/L (22–32)
- Base excess −19 mmol/L (−3 to +3)
- Lactate 1.4 mmol/L (<2.0)
- Glucose 11.2 mmol/L
- Capillary β-hydroxybutyrate (ketones) 5.8 mmol/L (<0.6)
- Sodium 138 mmol/L (135–145)
- Chloride 104 mmol/L (95–110)
- Potassium 4.6 mmol/L (3.5–5.2)
- Creatinine 96 µmol/L (60–110)

Which one of the following is the most likely cause of his acid–base disturbance?','["Euglycaemic diabetic ketoacidosis", "Metformin-associated lactic acidosis", "Starvation ketosis after fasting", "Early postoperative sepsis with hypoperfusion", "Hyperchloraemic acidosis from intravenous 0.9% sodium chloride"]'::jsonb,'A','CORRECT ANSWER

A. Euglycaemic diabetic ketoacidosis

---

He has a high anion gap metabolic acidosis: anion gap 138 − (104 + 8) = 26. There is appropriate respiratory compensation (Winter''s formula predicts pCO2 of about 20 ± 2), with marked ketonaemia (5.8 mmol/L) and a normal lactate. His glucose is only 11.2 mmol/L. The Australian Diabetes Society periprocedural alert defines presumed DKA as ketones above 1.0 mmol/L with base excess below −5, and presumed euglycaemic DKA as the same with glucose below 14 mmol/L. SGLT2 inhibitors cause this, especially with fasting, surgery, intercurrent illness or relative insulin deficiency (HbA1c above 9% raises risk further). He could not stop empagliflozin 3 days before surgery because it was an emergency. A near-normal glucose does not exclude DKA. Management is DKA treatment: IV fluids, IV insulin with added IV glucose once BGL is below about 15 mmol/L, potassium replacement, stopping the SGLT2 inhibitor, and escalation to the rapid response team/ICU with endocrinology input.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
In a patient taking an SGLT2 inhibitor who is unwell, check blood ketones and a blood gas even if glucose is near-normal. Ketones above 1.0 with base excess below −5 means DKA. With glucose below 14, it is euglycaemic DKA.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "ADS/ADEA/ANZCA/NZSSD Alert Update (May 2023): Periprocedural DKA with SGLT2 inhibitor use in people with diabetes", "url": "https://www.diabetessociety.com.au/wp-content/uploads/2023/05/ADS-ADEA-ANZCA-NZSSD_DKA_SGLT2i_Alert_Ver-May-2023.pdf"}
• {"name": "ADS\u2013ANZCA Perioperative Diabetes and Hyperglycaemia Guidelines (Adults), Nov 2022", "url": "https://www.diabetessociety.com.au/wp-content/uploads/2023/03/ADS-ANZCA-Perioperative-Diabetes-and-Hyperglycaemia-Guidelines-Adults-November-2022-v2-Final-3.pdf"}

---

EDITED BY HEISENBERG','difficult','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "bank170", "review_status": "owner_approved_2026_09_29", "tags": ["ZQ-0003", "zyntra_bank", "pilot", "level_4", "task_data_interpretation_synthesis", "group_adult_medicine", "sys_endocrine", "approved_by_owner_2026_09_29"], "clinician_task": "data_interpretation_synthesis", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"ADS/ADEA/ANZCA/NZSSD Alert Update (May 2023): Periprocedural DKA with SGLT2 inhibitor use in people with diabetes\", \"url\": \"https://www.diabetessociety.com.au/wp-content/uploads/2023/05/ADS-ADEA-ANZCA-NZSSD_DKA_SGLT2i_Alert_Ver-May-2023.pdf\"}; {\"name\": \"ADS\\u2013ANZCA Perioperative Diabetes and Hyperglycaemia Guidelines (Adults), Nov 2022\", \"url\": \"https://www.diabetessociety.com.au/wp-content/uploads/2023/03/ADS-ANZCA-Perioperative-Diabetes-and-Hyperglycaemia-Guidelines-Adults-November-2022-v2-Final-3.pdf\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"B": "Metformin-associated lactic acidosis needs a raised lactate, usually with renal impairment or hypoperfusion. Lactate is 1.4 mmol/L and creatinine is normal. Ketones explain the anion gap.", "C": "Starvation ketosis gives mild ketonaemia (the ADS notes up to about 1.7 mmol/L) with little or no acidosis. A pH of 7.14, bicarbonate of 8 and ketones of 5.8 are far beyond simple starvation.", "D": "He is tachycardic, but lactate is normal, he is afebrile and the abdomen is benign. Sepsis would raise lactate and would not explain ketones of 5.8 mmol/L. Sepsis can trigger DKA, but it is not the direct cause of this acidosis.", "E": "Saline-induced hyperchloraemic acidosis has a NORMAL anion gap and a high chloride. His chloride is normal and his anion gap is 26."}, "key_takeaways": ["In a patient taking an SGLT2 inhibitor who is unwell, check blood ketones and a blood gas even if glucose is near-normal. Ketones above 1.0 with base excess below −5 means DKA. With glucose below 14, it is euglycaemic DKA."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,1.0,1.0,'tier_prior','tier=4 (bank170)'),
('cd43ed40-e50a-5747-8ef3-2bd1be5df68a'::uuid,'ZQ-0004','adult-medicine','infectious-diseases','A 52-year-old Aboriginal man is brought to Katherine Hospital in the Northern Territory in February with three days of fever, cough and increasing breathlessness. He works as a community ranger and has spent the past week clearing flood debris and mud after heavy wet-season rain. He has type 2 diabetes (last HbA1c 10.2% [88 mmol/mol]) and stage 3 chronic kidney disease, and attends his community clinic regularly for his diabetes and kidney care. He has not been in hospital or had antibiotics in the past three months. At his request, the hospital''s Aboriginal Liaison Officer is supporting him and has contacted his family.

His temperature is 39.4°C, pulse 124/min, blood pressure 84/50 mmHg after 1 L of intravenous crystalloid, respiratory rate 32/min and oxygen saturation 87% on room air. He is alert but anxious. There is bronchial breathing over the right upper zone. Chest X-ray shows dense right upper lobe consolidation. Lactate is 4.3 mmol/L and creatinine 162 µmol/L (60–110; his baseline is 140). Blood and sputum cultures have been collected. Retrieval to Royal Darwin Hospital ICU has been requested, and a noradrenaline infusion has been started.

Which one of the following is the most appropriate empirical antibiotic regimen?','["Intravenous ceftriaxone plus intravenous azithromycin", "Intravenous benzylpenicillin plus intravenous gentamicin", "Intravenous piperacillin–tazobactam plus intravenous vancomycin", "Intravenous meropenem plus intravenous azithromycin", "Intravenous amoxicillin–clavulanate plus oral doxycycline"]'::jsonb,'D','CORRECT ANSWER

D. Intravenous meropenem plus intravenous azithromycin

---

This is severe community-acquired pneumonia with septic shock and hypoxaemia in the tropical Top End during the wet season. He has major risk factors for melioidosis: diabetes, CKD, and heavy soil and water exposure after rain. Empirical therapy must cover Burkholderia pseudomallei as well as the usual CAP pathogens, including atypical organisms such as Legionella. The 2024 revised Darwin Melioidosis Treatment Guideline recommends meropenem (1 g IV 8-hourly, adjusted for renal function) for patients in ICU or with critical sepsis, and ceftazidime for less severe disease on the ward. In septic shock with augmented renal clearance it suggests 2 g 8-hourly as an extended infusion, and G-CSF may be considered. Therapeutic Guidelines pairs this with azithromycin for severe CAP in tropical northern Australia. If melioidosis is confirmed, the intensive IV phase lasts at least 14 days (at least 4 weeks for pneumonia needing ICU care). This is followed by at least 3 months of oral eradication therapy with trimethoprim–sulfamethoxazole plus folic acid. In remote NT clinics, the CARPA manual advises starting ceftriaxone or ceftazidime (with gentamicin) while retrieval is arranged, and the retrieval team gives meropenem. The very high burden of melioidosis in the Top End reflects environmental exposure and the high prevalence of diabetes and kidney disease. It is not explained by individual behaviour.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
In severe CAP in tropical northern Australia (especially in the wet season, with diabetes, CKD, hazardous alcohol use or soil and water exposure), start meropenem plus azithromycin to cover melioidosis. Use ceftazidime for less severe melioidosis on the ward.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "NT Health. Currie BJ et al. The 2024 revised Darwin Melioidosis Treatment Guideline. Northern Territory Disease Control Bulletin 2023;30(4)", "url": "https://digitallibrary.health.nt.gov.au/nthealthserver/api/core/bitstreams/88d2f610-e0e5-42c3-9770-a734f874ca98/content"}
• {"name": "Meumann EM, Currie BJ. Approach to melioidosis. CMI Communications 2024;1:100008 (Charles Darwin University repository)", "url": "https://ris.cdu.edu.au/ws/portalfiles/portal/112385432/1-s2.0-S2950590924000088-main.pdf"}
• {"name": "Remote Primary Health Care Manuals. CARPA Standard Treatment Manual, 8th edition (melioidosis / severe pneumonia)", "url": "https://remotephcmanuals.org.au/download/resources/manuals/CARPA_8th_ed_V1.03.pdf"}
