EDITED BY HEISENBERG','difficult','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "bank170", "review_status": "owner_approved_2026_09_29", "tags": ["ZQ-0004", "zyntra_bank", "pilot", "level_4", "task_management", "group_adult_medicine", "sys_infectious_disease", "approved_by_owner_2026_09_29"], "clinician_task": "management", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"NT Health. Currie BJ et al. The 2024 revised Darwin Melioidosis Treatment Guideline. Northern Territory Disease Control Bulletin 2023;30(4)\", \"url\": \"https://digitallibrary.health.nt.gov.au/nthealthserver/api/core/bitstreams/88d2f610-e0e5-42c3-9770-a734f874ca98/content\"}; {\"name\": \"Meumann EM, Currie BJ. Approach to melioidosis. CMI Communications 2024;1:100008 (Charles Darwin University repository)\", \"url\": \"https://ris.cdu.edu.au/ws/portalfiles/portal/112385432/1-s2.0-S2950590924000088-main.pdf\"}; {\"name\": \"Remote Primary Health Care Manuals. CARPA Standard Treatment Manual, 8th edition (melioidosis / severe pneumonia)\", \"url\": \"https://remotephcmanuals.org.au/download/resources/manuals/CARPA_8th_ed_V1.03.pdf\"}; {\"name\": \"Therapeutic Guidelines (eTG complete): Antibiotic, community-acquired pneumonia in adults, tropical Australia (subscription)\", \"url\": \"https://www.tg.org.au\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "This is the standard empirical regimen for severe CAP in temperate Australia. Ceftriaxone does not reliably treat B. pseudomallei, so in the tropical wet season with his risk factors it could miss a highly lethal infection.", "B": "An older severe-CAP combination for temperate regions. B. pseudomallei is intrinsically resistant to gentamicin and penicillin, and gentamicin is best avoided with his worsening CKD.", "C": "Broad cover for hospital-acquired or MRSA-risk pneumonia. Piperacillin–tazobactam is not a recommended melioidosis agent, and he has no hospital-acquired or MRSA risk factors that would make vancomycin the priority.", "E": "Amoxicillin–clavulanate is a second-line oral eradication option for melioidosis only. It is inadequate as initial therapy for septic shock, and oral doxycycline is unreliable in a shocked patient."}, "key_takeaways": ["In severe CAP in tropical northern Australia (especially in the wet season, with diabetes, CKD, hazardous alcohol use or soil and water exposure), start meropenem plus azithromycin to cover melioidosis. Use ceftazidime for less severe melioidosis on the ward."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,1.0,1.0,'tier_prior','tier=4 (bank170)'),
('1ee45298-f669-5273-9091-7745335a050c'::uuid,'ZQ-0005','adult-medicine','neurology','A 71-year-old man is brought by ambulance to a metropolitan stroke centre in Adelaide. At 0730 hours he suddenly developed right-sided weakness and difficulty speaking while eating breakfast; his wife had seen him well at 0725. He arrives at 0915. He has hypertension, treated with amlodipine, and no history of bleeding, recent surgery, stroke or anticoagulant use.

He is alert, with expressive dysphasia, right facial droop, and dense right arm and moderate right leg weakness (NIHSS 14). His pulse is 78/min and regular, blood pressure 204/112 mmHg on repeated measurement, and capillary blood glucose 7.1 mmol/L. He weighs 80 kg.

Non-contrast CT brain shows no haemorrhage and no established infarct. CT angiography shows an occlusion of the proximal left middle cerebral artery (M1). The stroke team plans intravenous thrombolysis, and the endovascular clot retrieval team has been activated.

Which one of the following is the most appropriate next step?','["Give tenecteplase 20 mg as an IV bolus now, then treat blood pressure after thrombolysis", "Give aspirin 300 mg rectally now and withhold thrombolysis", "Withhold thrombolysis because blood pressure exceeds 185/110 mmHg, and proceed to clot retrieval alone", "Lower systolic blood pressure below 140 mmHg with a glyceryl trinitrate infusion before any reperfusion therapy", "Give IV labetalol to bring blood pressure below 185/110 mmHg, then give tenecteplase"]'::jsonb,'E','CORRECT ANSWER

E. Give IV labetalol to bring blood pressure below 185/110 mmHg, then give tenecteplase

---

He has a disabling ischaemic stroke with large vessel occlusion, within 4.5 hours of last known well (about 1 h 50 min). He is eligible for IV thrombolysis and endovascular clot retrieval. The Australian and New Zealand Living Clinical Guidelines for Stroke Management recommend reducing blood pressure to below 185/110 mmHg BEFORE thrombolysis and keeping it there for the first 24 hours. The recommended agent is tenecteplase 0.25 mg/kg (maximum 25 mg), so 20 mg for 80 kg. Blood pressure above 185/110 is a reason to treat the blood pressure, not to abandon thrombolysis. Typical local protocols use IV labetalol 10–20 mg boluses, or alternatives such as hydralazine or a GTN infusion. If the target cannot be reached, thrombolysis is not given. Clot retrieval proceeds in parallel and should not be delayed.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Before thrombolysis for ischaemic stroke, bring blood pressure below 185/110 mmHg and keep it there for 24 hours. Tenecteplase 0.25 mg/kg (max 25 mg) is the preferred agent in the Australian living guideline.

---

