EDITED BY HEISENBERG','moderate','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "bank170", "review_status": "owner_approved_2026_09_29", "tags": ["ZQ-0005", "zyntra_bank", "pilot", "level_3", "task_management", "group_adult_medicine", "sys_neurology", "approved_by_owner_2026_09_29"], "clinician_task": "management", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"Stroke Foundation. Australian and New Zealand Living Clinical Guidelines for Stroke Management, Chapter 3: Acute medical and surgical management (MAGICapp)\", \"url\": \"https://files.magicapp.org/guideline/6908a2ac-9fbd-4501-811e-11bf06a29ba1/published_guideline_10631-12_0.pdf\"}; {\"name\": \"Stroke Foundation InformMe: Emergency department clinical guidelines summary (June 2025)\", \"url\": \"https://informme.org.au/media/rvroeucq/emergency-department-clinical-guidelines-summary_jun25.pdf\"}; {\"name\": \"SESLHD procedure: Thrombolysis in adult acute ischaemic stroke (example local BP protocol)\", \"url\": \"https://www.seslhd.health.nsw.gov.au/sites/default/files/documents/SESLHDPR236%20-%20Thrombolysis%20in%20Adult%20Acute%20Ischaemic%20Stroke.pdf\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "The dose is correct (0.25 mg/kg = 20 mg), but thrombolysing with blood pressure above 185/110 raises the risk of symptomatic intracranial haemorrhage. Blood pressure must be lowered first.", "B": "Aspirin is not given in the first 24 hours after thrombolysis, and choosing it instead of reperfusion withholds an effective treatment from an eligible patient.", "C": "Blood pressure that has NOT yet been treated is not a reason to withhold thrombolysis. He should get both thrombolysis and clot retrieval if blood pressure can be controlled.", "D": "Intensive lowering to systolic below 140 mmHg is not recommended in acute ischaemic stroke. It may reduce perfusion to the penumbra, and pursuing it delays reperfusion."}, "key_takeaways": ["Before thrombolysis for ischaemic stroke, bring blood pressure below 185/110 mmHg and keep it there for 24 hours. Tenecteplase 0.25 mg/kg (max 25 mg) is the preferred agent in the Australian living guideline."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,0.0,1.0,'tier_prior','tier=3 (bank170)'),
('8068c7e8-4186-500a-9fbb-6f2e6b0f293d'::uuid,'ZQ-0006','adult-medicine','gastroenterology','A 34-year-old woman who was born in Vietnam and moved to Australia six years ago attends a general practice in Footscray, Melbourne. Three years ago her antenatal screening showed she was hepatitis B surface antigen (HBsAg) positive, but she did not attend follow-up. She feels well, drinks no alcohol and takes no regular medicines. Her mother died of liver cancer at 68. Examination is normal, with no stigmata of chronic liver disease.

Investigations:
- HBsAg: positive
- HBeAg: negative
- Anti-HBe: positive
- Anti-HBc (total): positive
- IgM anti-HBc: negative
- HBV DNA: 850 IU/mL
- ALT: 16 U/L (female upper limit used by the laboratory: 19)
- Platelets 232 × 10^9/L; albumin and INR normal
- Liver transient elastography: 4.8 kPa
- Hepatitis C antibody, HIV and hepatitis D antibody: negative

Which one of the following best describes her current hepatitis B status?','["Resolved past hepatitis B infection", "HBeAg-negative chronic hepatitis (immune escape phase)", "HBeAg-negative chronic infection (immune control phase)", "HBeAg-positive chronic infection (immune tolerance phase)", "Acute hepatitis B infection in the window period"]'::jsonb,'C','CORRECT ANSWER

C. HBeAg-negative chronic infection (immune control phase)

---

HBsAg has been positive for more than 6 months (three years), so this is chronic infection. She is HBeAg-negative and anti-HBe-positive, with HBV DNA below 2,000 IU/mL, persistently normal ALT (using the lower female upper limit used in Australian guidance), and no fibrosis on elastography. That is the HBeAg-negative chronic infection (immune control, formerly ''inactive carrier'') phase. Antiviral treatment is not indicated now. She needs lifelong monitoring: ALT about every 6 months and HBV DNA periodically (the ASHM toolkit suggests yearly) to detect reactivation into the immune escape phase. She also needs household and sexual contact tracing and vaccination of contacts. HCC surveillance is not yet indicated. The 2023 NHMRC-approved Australian guideline suggests considering 6-monthly ultrasound from age 50 for women of Asian background with chronic HBV, and from 10 years before the earliest family case where there is a family history. Her mother was diagnosed at 68 (which points to 58), so the family history does not bring the start age forward from 50.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
HBsAg-positive for more than 6 months, HBeAg-negative, HBV DNA below 2,000 IU/mL and normal ALT means immune control. Monitor for life; treatment is not needed now.

---

