EDITED BY HEISENBERG','moderate','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "bank170", "review_status": "owner_approved_2026_09_29", "tags": ["ZQ-0006", "zyntra_bank", "pilot", "level_3", "task_data_interpretation_synthesis", "group_adult_medicine", "sys_gastroenterology", "approved_by_owner_2026_09_29"], "clinician_task": "data_interpretation_synthesis", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"ASHM. Decision Making in Hepatitis B toolkit (Aug 2024 update)\", \"url\": \"https://ashm.org.au/wp-content/uploads/2024/08/web-AUG2024-UPDATE_ASHM_Decision-Making-in-Hepatitis-B-Toolkit.pdf\"}; {\"name\": \"Lubel JS et al. Australian consensus recommendations for the management of hepatitis B. MJA 2022\", \"url\": \"https://www.mja.com.au/journal/2022/216/9/australian-consensus-recommendations-management-hepatitis-b\"}; {\"name\": \"George J et al. Clinical practice guidelines for HCC surveillance for people at high risk in Australia: summary of recommendations. MJA 2025\", \"url\": \"https://www.mja.com.au/journal/2025/223/8/clinical-practice-guidelines-hepatocellular-carcinoma-surveillance-people-high\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "Resolved infection is HBsAg NEGATIVE with anti-HBc positive (with or without anti-HBs). She is HBsAg-positive.", "B": "Immune escape (HBeAg-negative chronic hepatitis) has HBV DNA of 2,000 IU/mL or more and raised or fluctuating ALT, often with fibrosis. It usually needs treatment. Her DNA is below 2,000 and her ALT is normal.", "D": "Immune tolerance is HBeAg POSITIVE with very high HBV DNA, typically in younger people infected at birth. She is HBeAg-negative with low DNA.", "E": "Acute infection would be IgM anti-HBc positive, usually with raised ALT. She has been HBsAg-positive for three years and IgM anti-HBc is negative."}, "key_takeaways": ["HBsAg-positive for more than 6 months, HBeAg-negative, HBV DNA below 2,000 IU/mL and normal ALT means immune control. Monitor for life; treatment is not needed now."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,0.0,1.0,'tier_prior','tier=3 (bank170)'),
('32c8ae8a-c5c4-524e-b39d-871593a2d8ed'::uuid,'ZQ-0007','adult-surgery','vascular-surgery','A 76-year-old woman comes to the emergency department in Geelong, Victoria, with five hours of severe, constant periumbilical pain that started suddenly. She has vomited twice and had one loose bowel motion. She has atrial fibrillation, normally treated with apixaban, which she stopped six days ago before a colonoscopy and polypectomy. She has not restarted it. She also has hypertension and stage 3 chronic kidney disease.

She is distressed and needs repeated doses of IV fentanyl. Her temperature is 37.3°C, pulse 112/min and irregularly irregular, blood pressure 138/84 mmHg, and oxygen saturation 97% on room air. Her abdomen is soft and only mildly tender, with no guarding or rebound. Bowel sounds are present. There are no hernias.

Investigations:
- White cell count 17.8 × 10^9/L (4.0–11.0)
- Haemoglobin 131 g/L (115–160)
- Venous lactate 1.9 mmol/L (<2.0)
- Lipase 32 U/L (<60)
- Creatinine 128 µmol/L (45–90), eGFR 36 mL/min/1.73 m²
- Troponin not raised
- ECG: atrial fibrillation, rate 110/min, no acute ischaemic changes

Which one of the following is the most appropriate next investigation?','["Multiphase CT angiography of the abdomen", "Non-contrast CT of the abdomen and pelvis to avoid contrast nephropathy", "Erect chest X-ray and supine abdominal X-ray", "Repeat venous lactate in two hours before deciding on imaging", "Duplex ultrasound of the mesenteric arteries"]'::jsonb,'A','CORRECT ANSWER

A. Multiphase CT angiography of the abdomen

---

Sudden severe abdominal pain out of proportion to the examination, in a patient with AF whose anticoagulant has been stopped, suggests acute embolic superior mesenteric artery occlusion until proven otherwise. The leucocytosis supports this. Guidelines (e.g. ESVS 2025) recommend urgent multiphase CT angiography as the first-line test, with the suspicion of mesenteric ischaemia stated on the request. A normal lactate does NOT exclude acute mesenteric ischaemia: it rises late, once the bowel is infarcted, and single biomarkers should not be used to rule it out. Moderate renal impairment should not delay CTA in a condition where hours of delay mean bowel loss and death. The benefit far outweighs the small risk of contrast-associated kidney injury. Give IV hydration, start therapeutic heparin once the diagnosis is confirmed or strongly suspected, and refer urgently to vascular and general surgery.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Pain out of proportion to signs with AF (especially off anticoagulation) needs urgent CT angiography. Do not be reassured by a normal lactate, and do not delay for moderate CKD.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "ESVS 2025 Clinical Practice Guidelines on the Management of Diseases of the Mesenteric and Renal Arteries and Veins (Eur J Vasc Endovasc Surg)", "url": "https://www.ejves.com/article/S1078-5884(25)00516-7/fulltext"}

---

EDITED BY HEISENBERG','difficult','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "bank170", "review_status": "owner_approved_2026_09_29", "tags": ["ZQ-0007", "zyntra_bank", "pilot", "level_4", "task_data_gathering", "group_adult_surgery", "sys_vascular", "approved_by_owner_2026_09_29"], "clinician_task": "data_gathering", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"ESVS 2025 Clinical Practice Guidelines on the Management of Diseases of the Mesenteric and Renal Arteries and Veins (Eur J Vasc Endovasc Surg)\", \"url\": \"https://www.ejves.com/article/S1078-5884(25)00516-7/fulltext\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"B": "Non-contrast CT cannot show arterial occlusion and may look normal in early ischaemia. Avoiding contrast because of eGFR 36 would be a dangerous trade-off here.", "C": "Plain films are usually normal early and only show late signs (ileus, pneumatosis, free gas). They delay diagnosis.", "D": "Waiting for lactate to rise means waiting for bowel infarction. A normal lactate has poor negative predictive value in early mesenteric ischaemia.", "E": "Duplex ultrasound depends on the operator, is limited by bowel gas in the acute abdomen, and cannot assess bowel viability. It is used mainly for chronic mesenteric ischaemia."}, "key_takeaways": ["Pain out of proportion to signs with AF (especially off anticoagulation) needs urgent CT angiography. Do not be reassured by a normal lactate, and do not delay for moderate CKD."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,1.0,1.0,'tier_prior','tier=4 (bank170)'),
('f897568b-0262-5c84-b7c1-488f6e4fb37d'::uuid,'ZQ-0008','adult-surgery','orthopaedics','An 84-year-old woman is brought to the emergency department of a regional hospital in Queensland from her residential aged care facility after a fall. She was found on the floor with left hip pain and could not bear weight. She has moderate dementia (usually oriented to person only), hypertension, and stage 3b chronic kidney disease (baseline eGFR 38 mL/min/1.73 m²). She has no drug allergies and takes no anticoagulant or antiplatelet medicines.

Her left leg is shortened and externally rotated. The distal limb is neurovascularly intact. X-ray shows a displaced intracapsular fracture of the left femoral neck. The orthopaedic team plans a hemiarthroplasty on tomorrow''s list.

An hour ago she was given oral paracetamol 1 g and two 2.5 mg doses of IV morphine. She still grimaces and calls out whenever she is moved, and her PAINAD score is 7/10. She is now drowsy between episodes of pain, with a respiratory rate of 12/min.

Which one of the following is the most appropriate next step in managing her pain?','["Further IV morphine 2.5 mg every five minutes until she is settled", "Ultrasound-guided fascia iliaca block with local anaesthetic", "Oral ibuprofen 400 mg three times daily with regular paracetamol", "Apply skin traction to the left leg to reduce muscle spasm", "Intramuscular tramadol 100 mg once, with oral oxycodone as required"]'::jsonb,'B','CORRECT ANSWER

B. Ultrasound-guided fascia iliaca block with local anaesthetic

---

The ACSQHC Hip Fracture Clinical Care Standard (2023) expects early pain assessment (using a tool validated for cognitive impairment, such as PAINAD) and multimodal analgesia that includes a peripheral nerve block unless contraindicated. An ultrasound-guided fascia iliaca (or femoral nerve) block gives excellent analgesia for positioning and transfer and reduces opioid needs. That matters here: she is already drowsy with a respiratory rate of 12 after modest morphine doses, she has dementia (a delirium risk), and she has CKD (morphine metabolites accumulate). Surgery should ideally happen within 36 hours of presentation under the 2023 standard, and the block covers this period.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
The hip fracture standard calls for pain assessment with a dementia-appropriate tool and a nerve block (e.g. fascia iliaca) as part of multimodal analgesia, which limits opioid use in frail older people.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "ACSQHC. Hip Fracture Clinical Care Standard (2023)", "url": "https://www.safetyandquality.gov.au/standards/clinical-care-standards/hip-fracture-care-clinical-care-standard"}
