• {"name": "ANZ Hip Fracture Registry", "url": "https://anzhfr.org/"}

---

EDITED BY HEISENBERG','moderate','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "bank170", "review_status": "owner_approved_2026_09_29", "tags": ["ZQ-0008", "zyntra_bank", "pilot", "level_3", "task_management", "group_adult_surgery", "sys_orthopaedics", "approved_by_owner_2026_09_29"], "clinician_task": "management", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"ACSQHC. Hip Fracture Clinical Care Standard (2023)\", \"url\": \"https://www.safetyandquality.gov.au/standards/clinical-care-standards/hip-fracture-care-clinical-care-standard\"}; {\"name\": \"NSW ACI. Orthogeriatric hip fracture care: clinical practice guide\", \"url\": \"https://aci.health.nsw.gov.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0009/828531/ACI-Orthogeriatric-hip-fracture-care-clinical-practice-guide.pdf\"}; {\"name\": \"ANZ Hip Fracture Registry\", \"url\": \"https://anzhfr.org/\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "More rapid opioid titration in a drowsy 84-year-old with CKD and a respiratory rate of 12 risks respiratory depression and delirium. Opioids are part of multimodal analgesia but should not be the main strategy when a block is available.", "C": "NSAIDs are best avoided at eGFR 38 in an 84-year-old (risk of AKI and GI bleeding), and they would not give adequate analgesia on their own.", "D": "Routine preoperative skin traction is not recommended in hip fracture: it does not improve pain or outcomes and can cause skin injury.", "E": "Tramadol lowers the seizure threshold, is serotonergic and causes delirium in older people. The IM route is painful and absorption is unpredictable. It is not a substitute for a regional block."}, "key_takeaways": ["The hip fracture standard calls for pain assessment with a dementia-appropriate tool and a nerve block (e.g. fascia iliaca) as part of multimodal analgesia, which limits opioid use in frail older people."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,0.0,1.0,'tier_prior','tier=3 (bank170)'),
('3242058f-4dd6-52ce-b99a-a5de316f5650'::uuid,'ZQ-0009','adult-surgery','urology','A 46-year-old man comes to the emergency department in Townsville with two days of severe colicky left loin-to-groin pain. Since this morning he has had rigors and feels much worse. He has had kidney stones before.

His temperature is 39.2°C, pulse 118/min, blood pressure 96/58 mmHg after 1 L of IV crystalloid, and respiratory rate 22/min. He has marked left renal angle tenderness.

Investigations:
- White cell count 19.6 × 10^9/L (4.0–11.0)
- C-reactive protein 214 mg/L (<5)
- Creatinine 165 µmol/L (60–110; 85 six months ago)
- Lactate 2.8 mmol/L (<2.0)
- Urinalysis: leucocytes 3+, nitrites positive, blood 3+
- Non-contrast CT KUB: 7 mm calculus at the left vesicoureteric junction with moderate left hydronephrosis. Right kidney normal.

Blood and urine cultures have been taken, and IV antibiotics and further fluid resuscitation have started.

Which one of the following is the most appropriate next step?','["Tamsulosin 400 micrograms daily as medical expulsive therapy, with analgesia", "Extracorporeal shock wave lithotripsy to the stone within 24 hours", "Continue IV antibiotics for 48 hours, then repeat the CT KUB", "Urgent decompression with a ureteric stent or percutaneous nephrostomy", "Ureteroscopy with laser lithotripsy on the next elective list"]'::jsonb,'D','CORRECT ANSWER

D. Urgent decompression with a ureteric stent or percutaneous nephrostomy

---

This is an obstructed, infected kidney (pyonephrosis or obstructive pyelonephritis) with sepsis and AKI, a urological emergency. Antibiotics cannot sterilise an obstructed collecting system. Urgent drainage by retrograde ureteric stent or percutaneous nephrostomy is needed alongside antibiotics and resuscitation. EAU urolithiasis guidelines regard both methods as equally effective, and the choice depends on local expertise and the patient''s condition. Definitive stone treatment is DEFERRED until the sepsis has resolved and the course of antibiotics is complete.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
A fever with an obstructing stone is an emergency: decompress the kidney urgently (stent or nephrostomy) along with antibiotics, and delay definitive stone treatment.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "EAU Guidelines on Urolithiasis: decompression of the obstructed infected kidney", "url": "https://uroweb.org/guidelines/urolithiasis"}
• {"name": "Therapeutic Guidelines (eTG complete): Antibiotic, acute pyelonephritis; and Gastrointestinal/urological, renal colic (subscription)", "url": "https://www.tg.org.au"}

---

EDITED BY HEISENBERG','moderate','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "bank170", "review_status": "owner_approved_2026_09_29", "tags": ["ZQ-0009", "zyntra_bank", "pilot", "level_3", "task_management", "group_adult_surgery", "sys_urology", "approved_by_owner_2026_09_29"], "clinician_task": "management", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"EAU Guidelines on Urolithiasis: decompression of the obstructed infected kidney\", \"url\": \"https://uroweb.org/guidelines/urolithiasis\"}; {\"name\": \"Therapeutic Guidelines (eTG complete): Antibiotic, acute pyelonephritis; and Gastrointestinal/urological, renal colic (subscription)\", \"url\": \"https://www.tg.org.au\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "Medical expulsive therapy is reasonable for an uncomplicated distal stone of 10 mm or less. It is contraindicated as the sole treatment when there is infection and obstruction.", "B": "Shock wave lithotripsy in the presence of active infection and obstruction risks worsening sepsis, and does not give immediate drainage.", "C": "Waiting 48 hours with the kidney undrained risks septic shock, loss of the kidney and death. Antibiotics alone are inadequate when there is obstruction.", "E": "Definitive stone treatment is not done during active sepsis because of the risk of urosepsis. A delay to an elective list also leaves the kidney undrained."}, "key_takeaways": ["A fever with an obstructing stone is an emergency: decompress the kidney urgently (stent or nephrostomy) along with antibiotics, and delay definitive stone treatment."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,0.0,1.0,'tier_prior','tier=3 (bank170)'),
('f70ab3f3-930a-5df7-9d29-b6d71392b191'::uuid,'ZQ-0010','adult-surgery','ent-ophthalmology','A 67-year-old woman goes to a rural general practice in Mudgee, New South Wales, at 8 am. Since late last night she has had severe pain in her right eye with a right frontal headache, nausea and two episodes of vomiting. Before the pain started, she noticed coloured rings around the streetlights while driving home. She has always been long-sighted and wears reading glasses. Her medicines are amlodipine 5 mg daily, perindopril 4 mg daily and metformin 500 mg twice daily. Six days ago, she started oxybutynin 5 mg twice daily for urge incontinence. She has no drug allergies and no history of asthma or chronic lung disease.

Her right visual acuity is 6/60 and her left is 6/9. The right eye is red with injection most marked around the limbus. The cornea is hazy, and the pupil is mid-dilated, oval and does not react to light. The globe feels firm to gentle palpation through the closed lid. When a penlight is shone from the side, the left anterior chamber appears shallow. The practice has a tonometer: right intraocular pressure is 58 mmHg and left is 18 mmHg. The nearest ophthalmologist is at a regional base hospital about three hours'' drive away.

Which one of the following is the most appropriate immediate management?','["Dilate the right pupil with tropicamide to examine the optic disc, then decide on referral", "Stop oxybutynin, give analgesia and an antiemetic, and review her in the practice tomorrow", "Give intensive pilocarpine 2% drops to the right eye every 5 minutes as the sole treatment", "Give topical prednisolone 1% drops and arrange routine ophthalmology outpatient review", "Give acetazolamide, timolol and apraclonidine now, stop oxybutynin, and arrange same-day ophthalmology transfer"]'::jsonb,'E','CORRECT ANSWER

E. Give acetazolamide, timolol and apraclonidine now, stop oxybutynin, and arrange same-day ophthalmology transfer

---

She has acute primary angle closure (acute angle-closure glaucoma): a painful red eye with halos, vomiting, reduced vision, a hazy cornea, a fixed mid-dilated oval pupil and a very high IOP. The shallow anterior chamber in the other eye shows the anatomical predisposition. Hypermetropia and older age add to this, and the recently started anticholinergic (oxybutynin) is the likely trigger. This is sight-threatening, and lowering the IOP should start straight away, before and during transfer. RVEEH and NSW ACI guidance for non-ophthalmologists recommends: acetazolamide 500 mg (IV, or orally if she can keep it down), after checking for sulfonamide allergy and significant renal impairment; topical timolol 0.5% (check for asthma or COPD) and apraclonidine; pilocarpine 2% once the IOP starts to fall, because the ischaemic iris sphincter does not respond at very high pressure; lying supine; and analgesia and an antiemetic. Stop the precipitating drug. Refer urgently for same-day ophthalmology assessment and laser peripheral iridotomy. The fellow eye usually needs prophylactic iridotomy as well.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
In acute angle closure, start IOP-lowering treatment immediately (acetazolamide, topical beta-blocker and alpha-agonist, then pilocarpine once pressure falls), stop any anticholinergic trigger, never dilate the pupil, and arrange same-day ophthalmology for laser iridotomy.

---

