EDITED BY HEISENBERG','difficult','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "bank170", "review_status": "owner_approved_2026_09_29", "tags": ["ZQ-0011", "zyntra_bank", "pilot", "level_4", "task_management", "group_womens_health", "sys_obstetrics", "approved_by_owner_2026_09_29"], "clinician_task": "management", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"Queensland Clinical Guidelines. Hypertension and pregnancy; flowchart F21.13-1: Management of eclampsia\", \"url\": \"https://www.health.qld.gov.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0029/146387/f-hdp-eclampsia.pdf\"}; {\"name\": \"King Edward Memorial Hospital (WA). Hypertension in pregnancy clinical guideline\", \"url\": \"https://www.kemh.health.wa.gov.au/~/media/HSPs/NMHS/Hospitals/WNHS/Documents/Clinical-guidelines/Obs-Gyn-Guidelines/Hypertension-and-Pregnancy.pdf?thn=0\"}; {\"name\": \"SOMANZ Hypertension in Pregnancy Guideline 2023 (NHMRC-approved)\", \"url\": \"https://www.somanz.org/content/uploads/2024/01/SOMANZ_Hypertension_in_Pregnancy_Guideline_2023.pdf\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"B": "Phenytoin is less effective than magnesium sulfate for preventing and treating eclampsia and is not recommended for this purpose.", "C": "Her blood pressure is already within target after nifedipine. Lowering it further risks maternal hypotension and reduced placental perfusion. Antihypertensives are for sBP of 160 or more or dBP of 110 or more, or to keep her within target.", "D": "Antenatal corticosteroids are generally recommended for anticipated birth before 34+6 weeks. At 35+2 weeks they are not routinely indicated, and they do nothing to prevent eclampsia.", "E": "Birth is the definitive treatment, but the mother is stabilised first (blood pressure control and magnesium sulfate). Operating without seizure prophylaxis risks eclampsia around the time of birth. Mode of birth is decided separately."}, "key_takeaways": ["In severe pre-eclampsia, control blood pressure, then give MgSO4 4 g IV over 20 min followed by 1 g/h (continued 24 h after birth). Monitor reflexes, respiratory rate and urine output, and keep calcium gluconate ready."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,1.0,1.0,'tier_prior','tier=4 (bank170)'),
('3711709b-3515-5ebf-a47e-1e7752f7b490'::uuid,'ZQ-0012','womens-health','gynaecology','A 44-year-old woman sees her general practitioner in Hobart for the result of her cervical screening test. Eight years ago she had a kidney transplant for IgA nephropathy. She takes tacrolimus, mycophenolate mofetil and prednisolone 5 mg daily, and her graft function is stable. Her previous cervical screening test, three years ago, was clinician-collected, and oncogenic HPV was not detected. This time she chose to collect her own vaginal sample in the clinic.

The report states: ''Oncogenic HPV detected: HPV (not 16/18).''

She has no abnormal vaginal bleeding, postcoital bleeding or discharge. She is in a monogamous relationship and has two children.

Which one of the following is the most appropriate next step?','["Recall her for a clinician-collected cervical sample for liquid-based cytology", "Repeat a self-collected HPV test in 12 months", "Refer her directly for colposcopic assessment", "Repeat an HPV test in 3 years on the immune-deficient screening schedule", "Recall her in 6 weeks for a clinician-collected co-test (HPV and cytology)"]'::jsonb,'C','CORRECT ANSWER

C. Refer her directly for colposcopic assessment

---

A solid organ transplant recipient on immunosuppression counts as ''immune-deficient'' under the National Cervical Screening Program Guidelines (2025). For immune-deficient participants, detection of ANY oncogenic HPV type, including HPV (not 16/18), means direct referral for colposcopic assessment (REC 7.15). If the sample was self-collected, LBC is taken at colposcopy, so she does not need a separate clinician-collected sample first. This differs from the general population, where HPV (not 16/18) on a self-collected sample means returning (ideally within 6 weeks) for a clinician-collected sample for reflex LBC, which then decides the risk category.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Immune-deficient people (e.g. transplant recipients, people living with HIV) screen 3-yearly. ANY oncogenic HPV detected, including on self-collection, means direct referral for colposcopy.

---

REFERENCES
