splant recipients, people living with HIV) screen 3-yearly. ANY oncogenic HPV detected, including on self-collection, means direct referral for colposcopy."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,2.0,1.0,'tier_prior','tier=5 (bank170)'),
('8896aaf8-3c35-5b8a-93ab-d76f2a63c6c8'::uuid,'ZQ-0013','child-health','paediatric-medicine','A 3-year-old girl is admitted to the paediatric ward of a Melbourne hospital on the eighth day of fever (up to 39.8°C). Her general practitioner diagnosed a viral illness four days ago. Her parents describe her as the most irritable they have ever seen her. For the past five days her eyes have been red without discharge, and she has had a blotchy red rash on her trunk. She is fully immunised.

She is miserable but well perfused. Her temperature is 39.1°C, pulse 150/min, blood pressure 94/56 mmHg and respiratory rate 28/min. She has bilateral non-exudative conjunctival injection that spares the limbus, red cracked lips and a polymorphous macular rash on her trunk. There is no cervical lymphadenopathy, and her hands and feet are normal. Heart sounds are normal with no murmur. There is no hepatosplenomegaly or meningism.

Investigations:
- Haemoglobin 98 g/L (105–135)
- White cell count 17.4 × 10^9/L (5.0–15.0)
- Platelets 552 × 10^9/L (150–450)
- C-reactive protein 96 mg/L (<5)
- ESR 74 mm/h (<20)
- Albumin 27 g/L (35–50)
- ALT 86 U/L (<30)
- Urine microscopy: 25 white cells/high-power field, culture negative
- Throat swab: no group A streptococcus. Respiratory PCR: rhinovirus detected. Blood culture: no growth at 48 hours.

The first available echocardiogram is tomorrow afternoon.

Which one of the following is the most appropriate management?','["Await the echocardiogram before deciding whether to give IVIG", "Oral prednisolone 2 mg/kg daily alone, then echocardiography", "High-dose aspirin 80–100 mg/kg/day alone until the fever settles", "IV ceftriaxone for presumed occult bacterial infection, and review in 48 hours", "IV immunoglobulin 2 g/kg with aspirin 5 mg/kg daily, started today"]'::jsonb,'E','CORRECT ANSWER

E. IV immunoglobulin 2 g/kg with aspirin 5 mg/kg daily, started today

---

She has had fever for 4 days or more with only 3 of the 5 principal features (conjunctival injection, oral mucosal changes, rash), so this is suspected INCOMPLETE Kawasaki disease. Under the AHA algorithm adopted by the RCH guideline, CRP of 30 mg/L or more and/or ESR of 40 mm/h or more with 3 or more supplementary laboratory criteria justifies treatment even before echocardiography. The supplementary criteria are anaemia for age, platelets of 450 or more after day 7, albumin of 30 g/L or less, raised ALT, WCC of 15 or more, and urine with 10 or more white cells/hpf. She meets all six. The RCH guideline states that the availability of an echo should not delay treatment. First-line therapy is IVIG 2 g/kg as a single infusion over 8–12 hours plus aspirin 5 mg/kg once daily, continued until cardiology review at about 6 weeks. Treatment within 10 days of fever onset reduces the risk of coronary artery aneurysm. A positive respiratory virus PCR does not exclude KD: about one-third of children with KD have one.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
In incomplete KD, CRP of 30 or more / ESR of 40 or more plus 3 or more supplementary laboratory criteria means giving IVIG 2 g/kg plus aspirin now. Do not wait for the echo, and a positive viral PCR does not exclude KD.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "Royal Children''s Hospital Melbourne. Clinical Practice Guideline: Kawasaki disease (last updated Jan 2026)", "url": "https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/kawasaki_disease/"}
• {"name": "Jone P-N et al. Update on diagnosis and management of Kawasaki disease: AHA Scientific Statement. Circulation 2024;150(23)", "url": "https://doi.org/10.1161/CIR.0000000000001295"}

---

EDITED BY HEISENBERG','difficult','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "bank170", "review_status": "owner_approved_2026_09_29", "tags": ["ZQ-0013", "zyntra_bank", "pilot", "level_4", "task_management", "group_child_health", "sys_paediatric_medicine", "approved_by_owner_2026_09_29"], "clinician_task": "management", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"Royal Children''s Hospital Melbourne. Clinical Practice Guideline: Kawasaki disease (last updated Jan 2026)\", \"url\": \"https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/kawasaki_disease/\"}; {\"name\": \"Jone P-N et al. Update on diagnosis and management of Kawasaki disease: AHA Scientific Statement. Circulation 2024;150(23)\", \"url\": \"https://doi.org/10.1161/CIR.0000000000001295\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "A normal early echo does not exclude KD, and waiting risks losing the day 10 treatment window. Echo supports the diagnosis and monitors the coronaries but should not delay IVIG once the laboratory criteria are met.", "B": "Corticosteroids are an ADD-ON (primary intensification) for children at high risk of coronary aneurysm or with IVIG resistance. They do not replace IVIG.", "C": "Aspirin alone does not reduce aneurysm risk; IVIG does. The RCH guideline uses low-dose aspirin (5 mg/kg/day) because higher doses have not improved coronary outcomes.", "D": "A well-perfused child with negative cultures, sterile pyuria and 8 days of fever with mucocutaneous features needs KD treated. Antibiotics alone would miss the window for IVIG."}, "key_takeaways": ["In incomplete KD, CRP of 30 or more / ESR of 40 or more plus 3 or more supplementary laboratory criteria means giving IVIG 2 g/kg plus aspirin now. Do not wait for the echo, and a positive viral PCR does not exclude KD."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,1.0,1.0,'tier_prior','tier=4 (bank170)'),
('f9f43015-ebce-5061-835b-0abb0086403c'::uuid,'ZQ-0014','child-health','paediatric-medicine','An 8-year-old Aboriginal boy is brought to his community health clinic in Arnhem Land, Northern Territory, by his grandmother, who is one of his main carers. For three days his eyes have been puffy in the mornings and his urine has been ''dark like tea''. He also has a headache. Three weeks ago the clinic''s Aboriginal Health Practitioner treated him for infected skin sores on both legs and scabies as part of the community''s skin health program. He has not had a sore throat. There is no rash, joint pain or abdominal pain.

His blood pressure is 132/90 mmHg (above the 95th centile for his age and height). He has periorbital and ankle oedema and healing crusted lesions on both shins. There is no purpura. The rest of the examination is normal.

Urine dipstick: blood 3+, protein 2+. Microscopy at the regional laboratory shows dysmorphic red cells and red cell casts.

Blood results (from Royal Darwin Hospital):
- Creatinine 74 µmol/L (25–60)
- Albumin 33 g/L (35–50)
- C3 0.21 g/L (0.75–1.65)
- C4 0.24 g/L (0.14–0.54)
- Antistreptolysin O titre 150 IU/mL (<200)
- Anti-DNase B titre 1,150 U/mL (<240)
- Antinuclear antibody: negative

Which one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?','["IgA nephropathy", "Post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis", "Lupus nephritis", "IgA vasculitis (Henoch–Schönlein purpura) nephritis", "Minimal change disease"]'::jsonb,'B','CORRECT ANSWER

B. Post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis

---

He has nephritic syndrome: haematuria with red cell casts, hypertension, oedema and a mild rise in creatinine. It began about 3 weeks after group A streptococcal skin infection, which fits the typical 3–6 week latent period after impetigo (1–3 weeks after pharyngitis). Low C3 with normal C4 reflects alternative pathway activation, which is characteristic. A normal ASOT does NOT argue against the diagnosis: ASOT often does not rise after skin (as opposed to throat) infection, and anti-DNase B is the more useful marker here. In the NT most APSGN follows skin sores. The higher burden in some remote communities is driven by structural factors such as household crowding and limited access to working health hardware (washing facilities), not by individual or family behaviour. Management: supportive care (fluid and salt restriction, a loop diuretic for oedema or hypertension), benzathine benzylpenicillin for infected sores, public health notification with screening of household contacts, and follow-up C3/C4 at about 12 weeks to confirm normalisation. Plan care together with his grandmother and family and the Aboriginal Health Practitioner, who can support household skin checks and follow-up.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Nephritic syndrome 3–6 weeks after skin sores, with low C3 and normal C4, is APSGN. Anti-DNase B is more sensitive than ASOT after skin infection.

---

