EDITED BY HEISENBERG','moderate','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "bank170", "review_status": "owner_approved_2026_09_29", "tags": ["ZQ-0014", "zyntra_bank", "pilot", "level_3", "task_data_interpretation_synthesis", "group_child_health", "sys_paediatric_medicine", "approved_by_owner_2026_09_29"], "clinician_task": "data_interpretation_synthesis", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"NT Health. Public health management of acute post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis (APSGN) guideline\", \"url\": \"https://digitallibrary.health.nt.gov.au/nthealthserver/api/core/bitstreams/7b9ed73f-2cc1-48c9-8a60-bc327e7d5ae8/content\"}; {\"name\": \"NT Health. Acute post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis (notifiable disease information)\", \"url\": \"https://health.nt.gov.au/public-health-notifiable-diseases/apsgn\"}; {\"name\": \"Remote Primary Health Care Manuals. CARPA Standard Treatment Manual, 8th edition (APSGN section)\", \"url\": \"https://remotephcmanuals.org.au/download/resources/manuals/CARPA_8th_ed_V1.03.pdf\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "IgA nephropathy causes haematuria 1–2 days AFTER (or during) an upper respiratory infection (''synpharyngitic''). Complement is normal.", "C": "Lupus nephritis typically lowers BOTH C3 and C4 and is ANA-positive. It is rare at this age.", "D": "IgA vasculitis nephritis comes with palpable purpura (buttocks and legs), joint and abdominal pain, and normal complement.", "E": "Minimal change disease causes nephrotic syndrome (heavy proteinuria, albumin usually below 25 g/L, oedema) without red cell casts or hypertension, and complement is normal."}, "key_takeaways": ["Nephritic syndrome 3–6 weeks after skin sores, with low C3 and normal C4, is APSGN. Anti-DNase B is more sensitive than ASOT after skin infection."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,0.0,1.0,'tier_prior','tier=3 (bank170)'),
('2f6ceb6d-dc47-5e00-85ca-da712af139e0'::uuid,'ZQ-0015','child-health','neonatology','You are the junior doctor on the postnatal ward of a hospital in Wollongong, New South Wales. A midwife asks you to review a boy who is 16 hours old. He was born at 39 weeks and 1 day by normal vaginal birth, weighing 3,420 g, with Apgar scores of 9 and 9. His mother is 27 years old, and this is her second baby. Her blood group is O RhD-positive and her antenatal red cell antibody screen was negative. Her first child needed phototherapy on the first day of life.

He is breastfeeding every 2–3 hours and has had two wet nappies and passed meconium. He is alert and pink, with a temperature of 36.9°C. The midwife has noticed yellow skin on his face and upper chest. There is no cephalhaematoma or bruising. The rest of the examination is normal. A transcutaneous bilirubin meter reads 128 µmol/L, which is below the phototherapy line for his age on the nomogram.

Which one of the following is the most appropriate next step?','["Repeat the transcutaneous bilirubin in 6 hours and plot the trend", "Start phototherapy now and repeat the transcutaneous bilirubin in 6 hours", "Encourage more frequent breastfeeds and review at 24 hours of age", "Urgent total serum bilirubin with FBC, blood group and direct antiglobulin test", "Conjugated bilirubin, liver function tests and abdominal ultrasound"]'::jsonb,'D','CORRECT ANSWER

D. Urgent total serum bilirubin with FBC, blood group and direct antiglobulin test

---

Jaundice visible within the first 24 hours of life is pathological until proven otherwise, most often because of haemolysis. Here ABO incompatibility is likely: the mother is group O and her previous baby needed phototherapy on day 1. Queensland Clinical Guidelines (and other Australian neonatal guidelines) call for urgent measurement of total serum bilirubin (with conjugated and unconjugated fractions), plus FBC, the baby''s blood group and a direct antiglobulin test (DAT), with escalation to the medical team. Transcutaneous bilirubin is intended for babies over 24 hours old and 35 weeks or more gestation. It should not be relied on in the first 24 hours, when bilirubin may be rising quickly. Treatment decisions are based on the serum level plotted on the gestation-specific nomogram and on the rate of rise.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Visible jaundice under 24 hours of age means urgent serum bilirubin plus FBC, blood group and DAT. Do not rely on a transcutaneous reading at this age.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "Queensland Clinical Guidelines. Neonatal jaundice (guideline MN25.7)", "url": "https://www.health.qld.gov.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0018/142038/g-jaundice.pdf"}
• {"name": "Royal Children''s Hospital Melbourne. Clinical Practice Guideline: Jaundice in early infancy", "url": "https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/Jaundice_in_early_infancy/"}

---

EDITED BY HEISENBERG','moderate','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "bank170", "review_status": "owner_approved_2026_09_29", "tags": ["ZQ-0015", "zyntra_bank", "pilot", "level_3", "task_data_gathering", "group_child_health", "sys_neonatology", "approved_by_owner_2026_09_29"], "clinician_task": "data_gathering", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"Queensland Clinical Guidelines. Neonatal jaundice (guideline MN25.7)\", \"url\": \"https://www.health.qld.gov.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0018/142038/g-jaundice.pdf\"}; {\"name\": \"Royal Children''s Hospital Melbourne. Clinical Practice Guideline: Jaundice in early infancy\", \"url\": \"https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/Jaundice_in_early_infancy/\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "TcB is not recommended as the basis for decisions under 24 hours of age. Haemolytic jaundice can rise quickly, so waiting 6 hours for another estimate could miss a need for urgent treatment.", "B": "Phototherapy may well be needed, but without a serum bilirubin, DAT and FBC you cannot find the cause, set the threshold or assess the risk of exchange transfusion. TcB is also unreliable in the first 24 hours and once phototherapy has started.", "C": "Increasing feeds suits physiological or breastfeeding-related jaundice after 24 hours. Jaundice in the first 24 hours needs urgent investigation.", "E": "This is the work-up for conjugated hyperbilirubinaemia (e.g. biliary atresia), which is suspected in prolonged jaundice (beyond 14 days) or with pale stools and dark urine. The conjugated fraction is part of the urgent TSB request, but LFTs and imaging do not look for haemolysis, the likely problem here."}, "key_takeaways": ["Visible jaundice under 24 hours of age means urgent serum bilirubin plus FBC, blood group and DAT. Do not rely on a transcutaneous reading at this age."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,0.0,1.0,'tier_prior','tier=3 (bank170)'),
('8bf4b61d-3585-56e3-a067-d683c369ca98'::uuid,'ZQ-0016','mental-health','mood-disorders','A 44-year-old woman is on the orthopaedic ward of a Perth hospital, 40 hours after open reduction and internal fixation of an ankle fracture under general anaesthesia. The operation and anaesthetic were uneventful. She has major depressive disorder, well controlled for three years on sertraline 150 mg daily, which she has continued. Since surgery she has been charted tramadol 100 mg orally 6-hourly and ondansetron 4 mg as needed (four doses given), and she received a single dose of metoclopramide 10 mg yesterday. She drinks about two standard drinks a week.

Over the past six hours she has become restless, anxious and tremulous. She is sweating heavily and has had three episodes of diarrhoea. Her temperature is 38.4°C, pulse 122/min, blood pressure 162/94 mmHg and respiratory rate 20/min. She is alert and oriented. Her pupils are dilated and reactive, and her skin is flushed and moist. Bowel sounds are hyperactive. She has a coarse tremor and generalised hyperreflexia, more marked in the legs, with sustained inducible ankle clonus on both sides and intermittent horizontal ocular clonus. Tone is increased in the legs but normal in the arms. Creatine kinase is 380 U/L (<200).

Which one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?','["Serotonin toxicity", "Neuroleptic malignant syndrome", "Anticholinergic toxicity", "Alcohol withdrawal syndrome", "Malignant hyperthermia"]'::jsonb,'A','CORRECT ANSWER

A. Serotonin toxicity

---

She meets the Hunter serotonin toxicity criteria: she is taking serotonergic drugs (sertraline plus tramadol, and ondansetron to a lesser extent) and has inducible clonus with agitation and diaphoresis, ocular clonus, and tremor with hyperreflexia. Neuromuscular excitation worse in the lower limbs, rapid onset over hours, mydriasis, diarrhoea, hyperactive bowel sounds and autonomic instability all fit. Tramadol inhibits serotonin reuptake and is a common trigger when combined with an SSRI. Management: stop all serotonergic drugs (tramadol, ondansetron; review sertraline), give supportive care and benzodiazepines for agitation and neuromuscular hyperactivity, and use non-serotonergic analgesia. Consider cyproheptadine in moderate toxicity. Severe toxicity (temperature above 38.5°C with rising rigidity) needs ICU for cooling, sedation and paralysis.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Clonus (inducible, spontaneous or ocular) with hyperreflexia after adding a serotonergic drug such as tramadol to an SSRI is serotonin toxicity (Hunter criteria). NMS is slow, rigid and hyporeflexic.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "Isbister GK, Buckley NA, Whyte IM. Serotonin toxicity: a practical approach to diagnosis and treatment. Med J Aust 2007;187(6):361\u20135", "url": "https://www.mja.com.au/journal/2007/187/6/serotonin-toxicity-practical-approach-diagnosis-and-treatment"}
• {"name": "Therapeutic Guidelines (eTG complete): Toxicology and Toxinology, serotonin toxicity (subscription)", "url": "https://www.tg.org.au"}

---

EDITED BY HEISENBERG','moderate','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "bank170", "review_status": "owner_approved_2026_09_29", "tags": ["ZQ-0016", "zyntra_bank", "pilot", "level_3", "task_data_interpretation_synthesis", "group_mental_health", "sys_mood", "approved_by_owner_2026_09_29"], "clinician_task": "data_interpretation_synthesis", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"Isbister GK, Buckley NA, Whyte IM. Serotonin toxicity: a practical approach to diagnosis and treatment. Med J Aust 2007;187(6):361\\u20135\", \"url\": \"https://www.mja.com.au/journal/2007/187/6/serotonin-toxicity-practical-approach-diagnosis-and-treatment\"}; {\"name\": \"Therapeutic Guidelines (eTG complete): Toxicology and Toxinology, serotonin toxicity (subscription)\", \"url\": \"https://www.tg.org.au\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"B": "The single metoclopramide dose is a tempting clue, but NMS develops over days, with bradykinesia, generalised ''lead-pipe'' rigidity, REDUCED reflexes, no clonus, and often a much higher CK and altered consciousness.", "C": "Anticholinergic toxicity gives DRY, hot skin, urinary retention, reduced bowel sounds and delirium, without clonus. She is sweating, has diarrhoea and has clonus.", "D": "Her alcohol intake is minimal, and withdrawal would not explain inducible and ocular clonus. Tremor, sweating and tachycardia overlap, but the neuromuscular findings point to serotonin excess.", "E": "Malignant hyperthermia happens during or soon after exposure to volatile agents or suxamethonium, with rigidity, hypercarbia and rapidly rising temperature. It does not start 40 hours after an uneventful anaesthetic with clonus."}, "key_takeaways": ["Clonus (inducible, spontaneous or ocular) with hyperreflexia after adding a serotonergic drug such as tramadol to an SSRI is serotonin toxicity (Hunter criteria). NMS is slow, rigid and hyporeflexic."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,0.0,1.0,'tier_prior','tier=3 (bank170)'),
('d19e65de-69e5-598e-8fd0-b8703b7cf66d'::uuid,'ZQ-0017','mental-health','psychosis','A 27-year-old man with treatment-resistant schizophrenia is an inpatient in an acute mental health unit in Brisbane. He is on day 18 of clozapine titration and currently takes 200 mg daily. His baseline ECG, troponin and C-reactive protein were normal, as were his weekly full blood counts and the week 1 and week 2 troponin and CRP. His resting pulse has been 95–100/min since the dose was increased.

This morning he says he feels ''like I''ve got the flu'', with aching muscles, tiredness and a vague central chest discomfort. His temperature is 38.3°C, pulse 126/min, blood pressure 104/68 mmHg, respiratory rate 22/min and oxygen saturation 96% on room air. He is alert and oriented, with no rigidity or tremor. Heart sounds are normal, the chest is clear and the throat is not inflamed. A full blood count taken this morning shows a white cell count of 9.8 × 10^9/L and neutrophils of 6.4 × 10^9/L.

Which one of the following investigations is the most important to obtain now?','["Plasma clozapine concentration", "Respiratory virus PCR swab", "Troponin and C-reactive protein", "Serum creatine kinase level", "Chest X-ray (PA and lateral)"]'::jsonb,'C','CORRECT ANSWER

C. Troponin and C-reactive protein

---

Clozapine-induced myocarditis typically appears between days 14 and 21 of treatment. The prodrome is non-specific and flu-like, with fever, a rise in heart rate above the patient''s clozapine baseline, malaise, chest discomfort and breathlessness. The Queensland Health guideline for clozapine in mental health services (QH-GDL-437), which follows the Ronaldson protocol, requires baseline troponin and CRP, then weekly troponin and CRP for the first month (with ECG in weeks 1–4). Symptoms such as fever, chest pain, effort intolerance or a heart rate above 120/min (or 30/min above the patient''s usual rate) prompt urgent testing and medical review. A troponin more than twice the upper limit of normal or a CRP above 100 mg/L means withholding clozapine and arranging urgent cardiology review and echocardiography. Smaller rises lead to daily repeat testing. His neutrophil count is normal today, so agranulocytosis is already excluded as the cause of the fever. That leaves myocarditis as the life-threatening diagnosis to exclude immediately.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Fever, tachycardia and flu-like symptoms 2–3 weeks into clozapine treatment mean checking troponin and CRP to exclude myocarditis. Withhold clozapine and get urgent cardiology review if troponin is more than twice the ULN or CRP is above 100 mg/L.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "Queensland Health Guideline QH-GDL-437: Safe and quality use of clozapine in Mental Health Alcohol and Other Drugs Services (September 2021)", "url": "https://www.health.qld.gov.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0025/625354/qh-gdl-437.pdf"}
• {"name": "NSW Health Guideline GL2022_011: Monitoring clozapine-induced myocarditis (secondary, comparison)", "url": "https://www1.health.nsw.gov.au/pds/ActivePDSDocuments/GL2022_011.pdf"}
• {"name": "Ronaldson KJ et al. A new monitoring protocol for clozapine-induced myocarditis based on an analysis of 75 cases and 94 controls. Aust N Z J Psychiatry 2011;45(6):458\u201365", "url": "https://doi.org/10.3109/00048674.2011.572852"}

