EDITED BY HEISENBERG','difficult','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "bank170", "review_status": "owner_approved_2026_09_29", "tags": ["ZQ-0017", "zyntra_bank", "pilot", "level_4", "task_data_gathering", "group_mental_health", "sys_psychosis", "approved_by_owner_2026_09_29"], "clinician_task": "data_gathering", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"Queensland Health Guideline QH-GDL-437: Safe and quality use of clozapine in Mental Health Alcohol and Other Drugs Services (September 2021)\", \"url\": \"https://www.health.qld.gov.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0025/625354/qh-gdl-437.pdf\"}; {\"name\": \"NSW Health Guideline GL2022_011: Monitoring clozapine-induced myocarditis (secondary, comparison)\", \"url\": \"https://www1.health.nsw.gov.au/pds/ActivePDSDocuments/GL2022_011.pdf\"}; {\"name\": \"Ronaldson KJ et al. A new monitoring protocol for clozapine-induced myocarditis based on an analysis of 75 cases and 94 controls. Aust N Z J Psychiatry 2011;45(6):458\\u201365\", \"url\": \"https://doi.org/10.3109/00048674.2011.572852\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "A clozapine level helps assess toxicity, adherence or interactions (e.g. smoking changes, infection raising levels). It does not diagnose or exclude myocarditis.", "B": "A viral illness is possible, but a positive swab would not exclude clozapine myocarditis. Missing myocarditis is the more dangerous error.", "D": "CK is used when NMS is suspected. He has no rigidity, altered consciousness or autonomic storm pattern, and CK does not assess the myocardium specifically.", "E": "A chest X-ray may be done as part of a fever work-up and may show pulmonary oedema late, but it is insensitive for early myocarditis and his chest is clear."}, "key_takeaways": ["Fever, tachycardia and flu-like symptoms 2–3 weeks into clozapine treatment mean checking troponin and CRP to exclude myocarditis. Withhold clozapine and get urgent cardiology review if troponin is more than twice the ULN or CRP is above 100 mg/L."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,1.0,1.0,'tier_prior','tier=4 (bank170)'),
('6dafe6a7-6960-5aa8-bae9-0f326dd1c82e'::uuid,'ZQ-0018','population-health-ethics','screening','A 44-year-old man attends his general practitioner in Canberra to ask about bowel cancer screening. His mother was diagnosed with colon cancer at age 57. She was treated with surgery and is well six years later. Her tumour showed normal (proficient) mismatch repair protein expression. No other first- or second-degree relatives on either side of the family have had bowel, endometrial or other cancers.

He has no rectal bleeding, change in bowel habit, abdominal pain or weight loss. He has no personal history of polyps or inflammatory bowel disease. Examination is normal, and a recent full blood count and iron studies were normal.

Which one of the following is the most appropriate screening recommendation for him?','["Immunochemical FOBT every two years from age 45 through the NBCSP", "Colonoscopy every five years, starting at age 47", "Colonoscopy now, then every five years", "Colonoscopy every five years, starting at age 50", "Referral for germline genetic testing for Lynch syndrome"]'::jsonb,'B','CORRECT ANSWER

B. Colonoscopy every five years, starting at age 47

---

The 2023 NHMRC-approved Clinical practice guidelines for the prevention, early detection and management of colorectal cancer (family history chapter) moved the cut-off from 55 to 60 years. One first-degree relative diagnosed BEFORE age 60 now places a person in Category 2 (moderately increased risk, at least 2–4 times average). For Category 2 the guideline recommends offering colonoscopy every five years, starting 10 years younger than the earliest age of diagnosis in a first-degree relative or at age 50, whichever is EARLIER, until age 74. 57 − 10 = 47, which is earlier than 50, so colonoscopy starts at 47. Low-dose aspirin (100 mg daily) should also be considered from age 45 to 70 after discussing benefits and harms.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Under the 2023 Australian guideline, one first-degree relative diagnosed with CRC before 60 is Category 2: colonoscopy 5-yearly from 10 years before the relative''s age at diagnosis, or from 50 if that is earlier.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "Cancer Council Australia / NHMRC-approved. Clinical practice guidelines for the prevention, early detection and management of colorectal cancer: Risk and screening based on family history (2023), hosted by RACGP", "url": "https://www.racgp.org.au/getmedia/f448e12c-6e3f-4af2-8b4f-96fa678fc962/Family-history.pdf.aspx"}
• {"name": "RACGP Red Book (Guidelines for preventive activities in general practice): Colorectal cancer", "url": "https://www.racgp.org.au/wip-sites/preventive-activities-in-general-practice/cancer/colorectal-cancer"}
• {"name": "Australian Government Department of Health. National Bowel Cancer Screening Program: lowered eligible age (45) from 1 July 2024", "url": "https://www.health.gov.au/our-work/national-bowel-cancer-screening-program/about-the-national-bowel-cancer-screening-program/lowered-eligible-age"}

---

EDITED BY HEISENBERG','difficult','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "bank170", "review_status": "owner_approved_2026_09_29", "tags": ["ZQ-0018", "zyntra_bank", "pilot", "level_5", "task_data_gathering", "group_population_health_ethics", "sys_screening", "approved_by_owner_2026_09_29"], "clinician_task": "data_gathering", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"Cancer Council Australia / NHMRC-approved. Clinical practice guidelines for the prevention, early detection and management of colorectal cancer: Risk and screening based on family history (2023), hosted by RACGP\", \"url\": \"https://www.racgp.org.au/getmedia/f448e12c-6e3f-4af2-8b4f-96fa678fc962/Family-history.pdf.aspx\"}; {\"name\": \"RACGP Red Book (Guidelines for preventive activities in general practice): Colorectal cancer\", \"url\": \"https://www.racgp.org.au/wip-sites/preventive-activities-in-general-practice/cancer/colorectal-cancer\"}; {\"name\": \"Australian Government Department of Health. National Bowel Cancer Screening Program: lowered eligible age (45) from 1 July 2024\", \"url\": \"https://www.health.gov.au/our-work/national-bowel-cancer-screening-program/about-the-national-bowel-cancer-screening-program/lowered-eligible-age\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "This is the recommendation for average risk and for Category 1 (no family history, or one first-degree relative diagnosed at 60 or over). Under the older guideline (cut-off 55) his mother''s age of 57 would have been Category 1, which is why this is a common error.", "C": "Starting at 44 has no basis in the guideline. The start age is 10 years before the relative''s diagnosis (47), or 50 if that is earlier.", "D": "Age 50 applies only when 50 is earlier than ''relative''s age at diagnosis minus 10'', for example a relative diagnosed at 58–59. Here 47 is earlier.", "E": "Germline testing is considered for suspected Lynch syndrome (e.g. early-onset cases, multiple relatives with Lynch-associated cancers, or a mismatch-repair-deficient tumour). His mother''s tumour was mismatch repair proficient, and a single relative diagnosed at 57 does not meet criteria."}, "key_takeaways": ["Under the 2023 Australian guideline, one first-degree relative diagnosed with CRC before 60 is Category 2: colonoscopy 5-yearly from 10 years before the relative''s age at diagnosis, or from 50 if that is earlier."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,2.0,1.0,'tier_prior','tier=5 (bank170)'),
('a360b6d8-4301-50e5-bf94-d25859a251c6'::uuid,'ZQ-0019','population-health-ethics','ethics-law','A 36-year-old emergency physician sees her general practitioner in Melbourne. For three months she has had low mood, poor sleep, reduced enjoyment and difficulty concentrating after a relationship breakdown. She has had no suicidal thoughts and does not use alcohol or other drugs to excess. Her PHQ-9 score is 16. Two weeks ago, on her own initiative, she arranged four weeks of leave because she felt she was ''not at her best''. No clinical errors or complaints have been raised, and she describes no near-misses.

She is keen to start treatment. She agrees to a mental health treatment plan, psychology sessions and sertraline, and to review with the GP every two weeks. She says she will not return to work until she and her GP agree she is well enough. She has also contacted the Victorian Doctors'' Health Program. She asks whether the GP is obliged to report her to Ahpra.

Which one of the following is the most appropriate response?','["Make a mandatory notification to Ahpra because she has a mental illness that affects concentration", "Advise her to self-notify the Medical Board before starting treatment", "Contact her hospital''s director of medical services to arrange supervised practice", "Make a voluntary notification to Ahpra as a precaution while treatment starts", "Continue treatment and document the risk assessment; no notification is needed now"]'::jsonb,'E','CORRECT ANSWER

E. Continue treatment and document the risk assessment; no notification is needed now

---

Under the Health Practitioner Regulation National Law (as amended in 2020) and Ahpra''s mandatory notification guidelines, a TREATING practitioner must notify impairment only if they reasonably believe the practitioner-patient is placing the public at SUBSTANTIAL risk of harm. This is a deliberately high threshold, so that health practitioners seek care. The relevant factors are the nature and severity of the impairment, the patient''s insight and willingness to engage with treatment, the steps already taken to limit risk (here, voluntary leave and a return-to-work plan agreed with the GP), and the effectiveness of treatment. She has moderate depression, good insight, has removed herself from practice and is fully engaged with care, so the threshold is not met. The GP should keep her confidentiality, document the risk assessment, review regularly and reconsider if the risk changes (e.g. she returns to work while impaired or disengages from treatment).

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
A treating practitioner notifies Ahpra about a doctor-patient''s impairment only if there is a reasonable belief of SUBSTANTIAL risk of harm to the public. Insight, treatment and voluntary leave lower that risk.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "Ahpra and National Boards. Guidelines: Mandatory notifications about registered health practitioners (2020)", "url": "https://www.ahpra.gov.au/Notifications/mandatorynotifications/Mandatory-notifications.aspx"}
