• {"name": "Medical Board of Australia. Good medical practice: a code of conduct for doctors in Australia (confidentiality; doctors'' health)", "url": "https://www.medicalboard.gov.au/codes-guidelines-policies/code-of-conduct.aspx"}

---

EDITED BY HEISENBERG','difficult','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "bank170", "review_status": "owner_approved_2026_09_29", "tags": ["ZQ-0019", "zyntra_bank", "pilot", "level_4", "task_data_interpretation_synthesis", "group_population_health_ethics", "sys_ethics_law", "approved_by_owner_2026_09_29"], "clinician_task": "data_interpretation_synthesis", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"Ahpra and National Boards. Guidelines: Mandatory notifications about registered health practitioners (2020)\", \"url\": \"https://www.ahpra.gov.au/Notifications/mandatorynotifications/Mandatory-notifications.aspx\"}; {\"name\": \"Avant. Mandatory notifications for treating practitioners\", \"url\": \"https://avant.org.au/resources/mandatory-notifications-for-treating-practitioners\"}; {\"name\": \"Medical Board of Australia. Good medical practice: a code of conduct for doctors in Australia (confidentiality; doctors'' health)\", \"url\": \"https://www.medicalboard.gov.au/codes-guidelines-policies/code-of-conduct.aspx\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "Having a mental illness is not the test. The test is substantial risk of harm to the public because of impairment, and her insight, leave and engagement with treatment mitigate the risk.", "B": "There is no requirement to self-notify before seeking treatment. Practitioners declare impairment that affects their practice at registration renewal, but that is separate, and this advice would discourage doctors from seeking care.", "C": "Disclosing her health information to her employer without consent would breach confidentiality and is not justified when she has already stopped working.", "D": "Voluntary notifications are possible at a lower threshold, but making one without a genuine concern about risk breaches confidentiality and undermines the trust that lets doctors seek care."}, "key_takeaways": ["A treating practitioner notifies Ahpra about a doctor-patient''s impairment only if there is a reasonable belief of SUBSTANTIAL risk of harm to the public. Insight, treatment and voluntary leave lower that risk."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,1.0,1.0,'tier_prior','tier=4 (bank170)'),
('b230d73c-6e0a-5147-b6c2-cf2f164add6c'::uuid,'ZQ-0020','population-health-ethics','epidemiology','A 31-year-old woman who is 14 weeks pregnant in her first pregnancy sees her general practitioner in western Sydney. The local public health unit rang her this morning: four days (about 96 hours) ago she sat in an emergency department waiting room at the same time as a person with confirmed infectious measles. She was born in 1995 overseas and has no vaccination records. Her parents think she ''had all her needles''. Her antenatal bloods at 9 weeks showed she was rubella IgG-positive, hepatitis B surface antigen-negative, HIV-negative and syphilis-negative. Measles serology was not done. She is well, with no fever, cough, conjunctivitis or rash. She weighs 62 kg and is not immunocompromised. The public health unit is available to advise.

Which one of the following is the most appropriate management?','["Give MMR vaccine today", "Reassure her that rubella immunity implies measles immunity", "Advise no prophylaxis, as more than 72 hours have passed", "Urgent measles IgG, then NHIG 0.2 mL/kg IM if non-immune", "NHIG 0.5 mL/kg IM today without checking serology"]'::jsonb,'D','CORRECT ANSWER

D. Urgent measles IgG, then NHIG 0.2 mL/kg IM if non-immune

---

She has no documented evidence of measles immunity (no records of two MMR doses and no measles serology), and she was born after 1966. The Australian Immunisation Handbook (adapted from the CDNA measles guidelines) says that a susceptible pregnant contact should have measles IgG checked if time allows, and normal human immunoglobulin (NHIG) 0.2 mL/kg IM (maximum 15 mL) if not immune, within 6 days (144 hours) of exposure, in consultation with the public health unit. At 96 hours there is still time for an urgent IgG result before giving NHIG (62 kg × 0.2 = 12.4 mL). MMR is a live vaccine and is contraindicated in pregnancy. She should also avoid contact with other susceptible or vulnerable people and seek care (phoning ahead) if symptoms develop during the incubation period. MMR should be given after birth, and at least 5 months after NHIG.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Measles PEP: MMR within 72 h for non-pregnant, non-immune contacts. For pregnant non-immune contacts, give NHIG 0.2 mL/kg IM (max 15 mL) within 144 h, checking IgG first if time allows.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "Australian Immunisation Handbook. Measles (post-exposure prophylaxis)", "url": "https://immunisationhandbook.health.gov.au/contents/vaccine-preventable-diseases/measles"}
