• {"name": "Australian Immunisation Handbook. Table: Recommended intervals between immunoglobulins or blood products and MMR/MMRV/varicella vaccination", "url": "https://immunisationhandbook.health.gov.au/resources/tables/table-recommended-intervals-between-immunoglobulins-or-blood-products-and-measles-mumps-rubella-measles-mumps-rubella-varicella-or-varicella-vaccination"}

---

EDITED BY HEISENBERG','difficult','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "bank170", "review_status": "owner_approved_2026_09_29", "tags": ["ZQ-0020", "zyntra_bank", "pilot", "level_4", "task_management", "group_population_health_ethics", "sys_epidemiology", "approved_by_owner_2026_09_29"], "clinician_task": "management", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"Australian Immunisation Handbook. Measles (post-exposure prophylaxis)\", \"url\": \"https://immunisationhandbook.health.gov.au/contents/vaccine-preventable-diseases/measles\"}; {\"name\": \"Australian Immunisation Handbook. Table: Post-exposure prophylaxis needed within 6 days (144 hours) of 1st exposure for people exposed to measles\", \"url\": \"https://immunisationhandbook.health.gov.au/resources/tables/table-post-exposure-prophylaxis-needed-within-6-days-144-hours-of-1st-exposure-for-people-exposed-to-measles\"}; {\"name\": \"CDNA. Measles: National guidelines for public health units (2025)\", \"url\": \"https://www.cdc.gov.au/sites/default/files/2025-11/measles-cdna-national-guidelines-for-public-health-units_0.pdf\"}; {\"name\": \"Australian Immunisation Handbook. Table: Recommended intervals between immunoglobulins or blood products and MMR/MMRV/varicella vaccination\", \"url\": \"https://immunisationhandbook.health.gov.au/resources/tables/table-recommended-intervals-between-immunoglobulins-or-blood-products-and-measles-mumps-rubella-measles-mumps-rubella-varicella-or-varicella-vaccination\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "MMR is a live attenuated vaccine and is contraindicated in pregnancy. Even outside pregnancy, vaccine given more than 72 hours after exposure is not recommended as PEP.", "B": "Rubella immunity says nothing about measles immunity. The antenatal rubella test does not include measles, and she may have had a rubella-only or incomplete vaccination history.", "C": "The 72-hour limit applies to MMR vaccine. NHIG is effective up to 144 hours (6 days), so she is still within the window.", "E": "0.5 mL/kg is the dose for IMMUNOCOMPROMISED contacts. For pregnant contacts the Handbook recommends 0.2 mL/kg (max 15 mL), and checking IgG first when time allows avoids unnecessary blood product exposure."}, "key_takeaways": ["Measles PEP: MMR within 72 h for non-pregnant, non-immune contacts. For pregnant non-immune contacts, give NHIG 0.2 mL/kg IM (max 15 mL) within 144 h, checking IgG first if time allows."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,1.0,1.0,'tier_prior','tier=4 (bank170)'),
('331a1ef0-ca48-5138-8813-374fa976abef'::uuid,'ZQ-0101','adult-medicine','cardiology','A 64-year-old man is brought by ambulance to the emergency department of a regional hospital in Toowoomba, Queensland, with 90 minutes of central crushing chest pain and sweating. He has hypertension and type 2 diabetes and smokes 20 cigarettes a day. The paramedics gave aspirin 300 mg orally and one 400 microgram spray of sublingual glyceryl trinitrate 15 minutes ago. Soon after the spray he became light-headed and nauseated.

His pulse is 56/min and regular, blood pressure 78/50 mmHg, respiratory rate 20/min and oxygen saturation 97% on room air. He is pale and clammy but alert. The jugular venous pressure is raised at 6 cm above the sternal angle, the chest is clear to auscultation and there is no peripheral oedema.

The 12-lead ECG shows sinus rhythm with 3 mm ST elevation in leads II, III and aVF (greater in III than II) and ST depression in I and aVL. A right-sided lead V4R shows 1.5 mm ST elevation. The on-site cardiac catheterisation laboratory has been activated for primary percutaneous coronary intervention and the team will be ready in 20 minutes.

Which one of the following is the most appropriate immediate management of his hypotension?','["Furosemide 40 mg intravenously to relieve congestion", "Sodium chloride 0.9% 250 mL IV bolus, repeated if needed", "Glyceryl trinitrate IV infusion at 5 micrograms/min", "Metoprolol 5 mg intravenously over 2 minutes", "Morphine 2.5 mg intravenously for pain and anxiety"]'::jsonb,'B','CORRECT ANSWER

B. Sodium chloride 0.9% 250 mL IV bolus, repeated if needed

---

This is an inferior STEMI with right ventricular (RV) involvement. ST elevation greater in III than II, reciprocal change in I/aVL and ST elevation of 1 mm or more in V4R point to a proximal right coronary artery occlusion. The triad of hypotension, raised JVP and clear lung fields, made worse by a nitrate, is typical of RV infarction. The ischaemic RV depends on preload, so venodilators (nitrates, morphine) and diuretics can cause profound hypotension. While reperfusion by primary PCI is arranged, the immediate treatment is cautious IV crystalloid in small boluses (for example 250 mL of 0.9% sodium chloride), reassessing blood pressure, JVP and lungs after each bolus. If he stays hypotensive after adequate filling, inotropes are the next step. Bradycardia or heart block, common with inferior infarcts, may need atropine or pacing. Nitrates should be withheld.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
In inferior STEMI, record V4R. Hypotension with a raised JVP and clear lungs means RV infarction: give cautious fluid boluses, avoid nitrates, morphine and diuretics, and reperfuse urgently.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "NHFA/CSANZ Comprehensive Australian Clinical Guideline for Diagnosing and Managing Acute Coronary Syndromes 2025 (Heart Foundation)", "url": "https://www.heartfoundation.org.au/for-professionals/acs-guideline"}
• {"name": "2023 ESC Guidelines for the management of acute coronary syndromes (European Heart Journal)", "url": "https://academic.oup.com/eurheartj/article/44/38/3720/7243210"}

