EDITED BY HEISENBERG','moderate','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "bank170", "review_status": "owner_approved_2026_09_29", "tags": ["ZQ-0101", "zyntra_bank", "mock1", "level_3", "task_management", "group_adult_medicine", "sys_cardiovascular", "approved_by_owner_2026_09_29"], "clinician_task": "management", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"NHFA/CSANZ Comprehensive Australian Clinical Guideline for Diagnosing and Managing Acute Coronary Syndromes 2025 (Heart Foundation)\", \"url\": \"https://www.heartfoundation.org.au/for-professionals/acs-guideline\"}; {\"name\": \"2023 ESC Guidelines for the management of acute coronary syndromes (European Heart Journal)\", \"url\": \"https://academic.oup.com/eurheartj/article/44/38/3720/7243210\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "A raised JVP can tempt a clinician to diurese, but the lungs are clear. The JVP is high because the RV is failing, not because of fluid overload. A diuretic cuts preload further and will worsen the shock.", "C": "Nitrates are relatively contraindicated in RV infarction and in hypotension (systolic below 90 mmHg). The sublingual dose has probably already caused this deterioration.", "D": "Beta-blockers are avoided in the acute phase when there is hypotension, bradycardia or signs of low cardiac output. His pulse is 56/min and he is shocked.", "E": "Opioids venodilate and can drop blood pressure further in a preload-dependent patient. Analgesia matters, but it is not the treatment for his hypotension and would be given very cautiously, if at all, until the pressure improves."}, "key_takeaways": ["In inferior STEMI, record V4R. Hypotension with a raised JVP and clear lungs means RV infarction: give cautious fluid boluses, avoid nitrates, morphine and diuretics, and reperfuse urgently."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,0.0,1.0,'tier_prior','tier=3 (bank170)'),
('53045308-dc8f-5572-ad42-ea3afb7a2157'::uuid,'ZQ-0102','adult-medicine','cardiology','A 67-year-old woman attends the heart failure clinic at a Hobart hospital. Eight months ago she was diagnosed with heart failure with reduced ejection fraction due to non-ischaemic dilated cardiomyopathy. Her left ventricular ejection fraction on repeat echocardiography last week was 30%. She becomes breathless after walking about 200 m on the flat (NYHA class II–III) and has had no hospital admissions in the past 6 months.

Her medicines are perindopril 10 mg each morning at 0800 hours, bisoprolol 5 mg daily, spironolactone 25 mg daily, dapagliflozin 10 mg daily and furosemide 20 mg daily. She has no history of angioedema. Her pulse is 64/min and regular, and blood pressure 118/70 mmHg. There is no peripheral oedema and the chest is clear.

Investigations:
- Sodium 138 mmol/L (135–145)
- Potassium 4.6 mmol/L (3.5–5.2)
- Creatinine 88 µmol/L (45–90)
- eGFR 58 mL/min/1.73 m²

The cardiologist decides to replace perindopril with sacubitril/valsartan 49/51 mg twice daily. She took her perindopril at 0800 hours today, and the clinic appointment is at 1100 hours.

Which one of the following is the most appropriate instruction for starting sacubitril/valsartan?','["Stop perindopril now and take the first dose at 2000 hours tonight", "Stop perindopril now and take the first dose at 0800 hours tomorrow", "Halve perindopril to 5 mg daily and take both medicines together for 2 weeks", "Stop perindopril now and take the first dose at 2000 hours tomorrow", "Swap perindopril for candesartan for 4 weeks, then start the new medicine"]'::jsonb,'D','CORRECT ANSWER

D. Stop perindopril now and take the first dose at 2000 hours tomorrow

---

Sacubitril/valsartan (an angiotensin receptor–neprilysin inhibitor, ARNI) is recommended in place of an ACE inhibitor or ARB in patients with HFrEF who remain symptomatic on an ACE inhibitor (or ARB) and a beta-blocker. It lowers mortality and heart failure hospitalisation. Because combined ACE and neprilysin inhibition greatly increases the risk of angioedema, the Australian product information contraindicates co-administration and requires at least 36 hours between the last ACE inhibitor dose and the first sacubitril/valsartan dose. Her last perindopril was at 0800 today, so the earliest safe first dose is 2000 hours tomorrow (36 hours later). She should continue bisoprolol, spironolactone, dapagliflozin and furosemide. Check potassium, renal function and blood pressure 1–2 weeks after starting and after each up-titration.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
ACE inhibitor to sacubitril/valsartan: stop the ACE inhibitor and wait at least 36 hours before the first ARNI dose. No washout is needed when switching from an ARB.

---

