EDITED BY HEISENBERG','difficult','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "bank170", "review_status": "owner_approved_2026_09_29", "tags": ["ZQ-0102", "zyntra_bank", "mock1", "level_5", "task_management", "group_adult_medicine", "sys_cardiovascular", "approved_by_owner_2026_09_29"], "clinician_task": "management", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"Entresto (sacubitril/valsartan) Australian Product Information, sections 4.2\\u20134.5\", \"url\": \"https://medsinfo.com.au/api/documents/Entresto_PI?format=pdf\"}; {\"name\": \"Sindone AP et al. Consensus statement on the current pharmacological prevention and management of heart failure (MJA 2022)\", \"url\": \"https://www.mja.com.au/journal/2022/217/4/consensus-statement-current-pharmacological-prevention-and-management-heart\"}; {\"name\": \"Queensland Health: Sacubitril with valsartan (Entresto) fact sheet\", \"url\": \"https://www.health.qld.gov.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0028/675127/Sacubitril_w_Valsartan_fact_sheet.pdf\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "This is only 12 hours after her last perindopril dose. Starting an ARNI within 36 hours of an ACE inhibitor is contraindicated because of the risk of angioedema.", "B": "This is 24 hours after the last perindopril dose, still inside the 36-hour washout.", "C": "ACE inhibitors and sacubitril/valsartan must not be taken together at any dose, because the combination increases the risk of angioedema.", "E": "No washout is needed when switching from an ARB, but converting to an ARB first adds 4 weeks of avoidable delay to a therapy that reduces mortality. It is not required."}, "key_takeaways": ["ACE inhibitor to sacubitril/valsartan: stop the ACE inhibitor and wait at least 36 hours before the first ARNI dose. No washout is needed when switching from an ARB."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,2.0,1.0,'tier_prior','tier=5 (bank170)'),
('039131c7-14f9-5084-8655-b83a5fc2874e'::uuid,'ZQ-0103','adult-medicine','cardiology','A 19-year-old Aboriginal man from a remote community in the Kimberley, Western Australia, is flown to Broome Hospital with five days of a painful, swollen right knee. For the past two days he has also felt hot and unwell. He remembers a sore throat about three weeks ago and has had infected skin sores on his legs this year. He has no known history of acute rheumatic fever and is not on any regular medicines. He has not injured the knee and has no urethral discharge. An Aboriginal Liaison Officer is present, at his request.

His temperature is 38.3°C, pulse 104/min and blood pressure 122/70 mmHg. The right knee is warm and swollen with a moderate effusion and painful restriction of movement. No other joint is swollen or tender. There is no rash, no subcutaneous nodules and no abnormal movements. Heart sounds are normal with no murmur.

Investigations:
- Knee aspirate: turbid; white cells 16 × 10^9/L, mostly neutrophils; Gram stain negative; no crystals; culture negative at 48 hours
- C-reactive protein 72 mg/L (<5)
- ESR 58 mm/h (<15)
- Anti-streptolysin O titre 820 IU/mL (reference upper limit 200) and anti-DNase B raised
- ECG: sinus rhythm, PR interval 240 ms
- Echocardiogram (performed by an experienced sonographer and reviewed by a cardiologist): structurally normal heart, no pathological regurgitation

Which one of the following is the most appropriate interpretation of these findings?','["Definite acute rheumatic fever, first episode", "Probable acute rheumatic fever, as there is a single major manifestation", "Post-streptococcal reactive arthritis, not acute rheumatic fever", "Culture-negative septic arthritis until proven otherwise", "Possible acute rheumatic fever, as monoarthritis counts as a minor manifestation"]'::jsonb,'A','CORRECT ANSWER

A. Definite acute rheumatic fever, first episode

---

He belongs to a high-risk population: Aboriginal people living in rural or remote Australia. The Australian criteria (2020 guideline, retained in the 2025 third edition) use a different set of criteria for high-risk groups. In high-risk groups, aseptic monoarthritis is a MAJOR manifestation. The minor manifestations for high-risk groups include fever of 38°C or higher, ESR of 30 mm/h or more (or CRP of 30 mg/L or more), and a prolonged PR interval for age (when carditis is not already counted as major). An initial episode of ARF needs evidence of preceding group A streptococcal infection plus either two major manifestations, or one major and two minor. He has one major (aseptic monoarthritis), three minor (fever 38.3°C, raised ESR/CRP, PR 240 ms) and raised streptococcal serology. That meets the criteria for definite ARF. Management includes notification (ARF is notifiable in WA), registration with the jurisdictional RHD control program, benzathine benzylpenicillin G secondary prophylaxis, NSAIDs for the arthritis once the diagnosis is secure, and repeat echocardiography. This should be done in partnership with the patient, his family and Aboriginal health staff.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
In high-risk Australian populations, aseptic monoarthritis is a major ARF criterion, and fever of 38°C or more, ESR/CRP of 30 or more and a prolonged PR interval are minor criteria. One major plus two minor with streptococcal evidence equals definite ARF.

---

REFERENCES
