---

EDITED BY HEISENBERG','difficult','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "bank170", "review_status": "owner_approved_2026_09_29", "tags": ["ZQ-0103", "zyntra_bank", "mock1", "level_4", "task_data_interpretation_synthesis", "group_adult_medicine", "sys_cardiovascular", "approved_by_owner_2026_09_29"], "clinician_task": "data_interpretation_synthesis", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"Ralph AP, Noonan S, Wade V, Currie BJ. The 2020 Australian guideline for prevention, diagnosis and management of acute rheumatic fever and rheumatic heart disease (MJA 2021)\", \"url\": \"https://www.mja.com.au/journal/2021/214/5/2020-australian-guideline-prevention-diagnosis-and-management-acute-rheumatic\"}; {\"name\": \"RHDAustralia/CSANZ: Australian ARF/RHD Guideline, 3rd edition (2025)\", \"url\": \"https://www.csanz.edu.au/Common/Uploaded%20files/Smart%20Suite/Smart%20Library/862f5626-c013-4299-bf8b-dae2d8c7f554/Australian-ARF-RHD-Guideline-2025.pdf\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"B": "''Probable ARF'' is used when the criteria are not quite met (one minor manifestation short or no streptococcal evidence) but ARF is still the most likely diagnosis. He meets the full criteria, so ''probable'' under-calls the diagnosis and may lead to less certain follow-up.", "C": "Post-streptococcal reactive arthritis is a diagnosis to be very cautious about in high-risk Australian populations. The guideline advises that people in high-risk groups who meet the ARF criteria be diagnosed and managed as ARF, because missing ARF risks progressive rheumatic heart disease.", "D": "Septic arthritis must be considered in any acute monoarthritis, and it was appropriately excluded by aspiration (Gram stain and culture negative). With strong streptococcal serology and supporting criteria, ARF is the unifying diagnosis.", "E": "Monoarthritis is a MINOR manifestation only in low-risk populations. In high-risk populations it is a MAJOR manifestation, and monoarthralgia (pain without swelling) is the minor one."}, "key_takeaways": ["In high-risk Australian populations, aseptic monoarthritis is a major ARF criterion, and fever of 38°C or more, ESR/CRP of 30 or more and a prolonged PR interval are minor criteria. One major plus two minor with streptococcal evidence equals definite ARF."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,1.0,1.0,'tier_prior','tier=4 (bank170)'),
('c4aeb282-4955-592f-8401-db473717c2b1'::uuid,'ZQ-0104','adult-medicine','cardiology','A 58-year-old woman sees her general practitioner in Wollongong, New South Wales, for a cardiovascular risk assessment. She has chronic kidney disease secondary to childhood reflux nephropathy and is followed by a nephrologist. She does not have diabetes, has never smoked and does not identify as Aboriginal or Torres Strait Islander. There is no family history of premature cardiovascular disease and she has no history of atrial fibrillation. Her only medicine is irbesartan 150 mg daily.

Her blood pressure is 134/80 mmHg and her body mass index 26 kg/m².

Investigations:
- eGFR 43 mL/min/1.73 m² (five months ago: 41 mL/min/1.73 m²)
- Urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio 2.8 mg/mmol (<3.5 in women)
- Total cholesterol 5.6 mmol/L; HDL cholesterol 1.4 mmol/L; LDL cholesterol 3.5 mmol/L
- HbA1c 5.4% (36 mmol/mol)

The practice nurse has entered her details into the online Australian CVD risk calculator, which reports a 5-year cardiovascular risk of 3%.

Which one of the following best describes her cardiovascular risk?','["Low risk, based on the calculated 5-year risk of 3%", "Intermediate risk, after reclassifying upward for reduced eGFR", "Unable to be classified until the urine albumin is repeated twice", "Intermediate risk, because age over 55 with CKD adds 5%", "High risk, clinically determined without using the calculator"]'::jsonb,'E','CORRECT ANSWER

E. High risk, clinically determined without using the calculator

---

The 2023 Australian guideline for assessing and managing cardiovascular disease risk starts by identifying people at CLINICALLY DETERMINED high risk. For them the calculator is not used. Only two conditions now qualify: moderate-to-severe CKD and familial hypercholesterolaemia. Moderate-to-severe CKD is defined as a sustained eGFR below 45 mL/min/1.73 m², or a persistent uACR above 25 mg/mmol in men or above 35 mg/mmol in women. Her eGFR has been below 45 on two occasions more than 3 months apart, so she is automatically high risk (equivalent to 10% or more over 5 years). The calculator result of 3% is invalid for her. High risk means she should be offered lipid-lowering therapy (a statin) alongside her renin–angiotensin blockade and lifestyle advice, unless contraindicated or declined after shared decision-making.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
2023 Australian CVD risk guideline: sustained eGFR below 45, or uACR above 25 (men) / above 35 (women) mg/mmol, or familial hypercholesterolaemia means clinically determined high risk. Do not use the calculator for these people.

---

