---

EDITED BY HEISENBERG','difficult','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "bank170", "review_status": "owner_approved_2026_09_29", "tags": ["ZQ-0104", "zyntra_bank", "mock1", "level_4", "task_data_interpretation_synthesis", "group_adult_medicine", "sys_cardiovascular", "approved_by_owner_2026_09_29"], "clinician_task": "data_interpretation_synthesis", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"Heart Foundation/Australian Chronic Disease Prevention Alliance: Australian guideline and calculator for assessing and managing cardiovascular disease risk (2023)\", \"url\": \"https://www.cvdcheck.org.au/\"}; {\"name\": \"2023 Australian guideline for assessing and managing cardiovascular disease risk: summary (MJA)\", \"url\": \"https://cdn.craft.cloud/019efd17-dcc2-707f-8e07-011d00d1146a/assets/issues/mja252280.pdf\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "The calculator should not be used in people who already meet the criteria for clinically determined high risk (moderate-to-severe CKD or familial hypercholesterolaemia). Relying on the 3% figure would under-treat her.", "B": "Reclassification using eGFR/uACR applies to people whose calculated risk is near a threshold and who do NOT meet the moderate-to-severe CKD criteria. Her sustained eGFR below 45 puts her straight into the high-risk category.", "C": "The high-risk criteria are met by EITHER a sustained eGFR below 45 OR persistently raised albuminuria. Her eGFR alone is enough, so there is no need to wait for further albuminuria testing.", "D": "There is no rule that adds a fixed percentage for age plus CKD. This is a made-up adjustment."}, "key_takeaways": ["2023 Australian CVD risk guideline: sustained eGFR below 45, or uACR above 25 (men) / above 35 (women) mg/mmol, or familial hypercholesterolaemia means clinically determined high risk. Do not use the calculator for these people."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,1.0,1.0,'tier_prior','tier=4 (bank170)'),
('130df21d-c025-5091-b6f9-2c3a290f6fb9'::uuid,'ZQ-0105','adult-medicine','respiratory','A 27-year-old man sees a general practitioner in Canberra for a repeat prescription of salbutamol. He has had asthma since childhood. Over the past three months he has used salbutamol 2–3 times a week for wheeze and chest tightness, mostly when cycling in cold weather or during pollen season. He wakes with asthma symptoms about once a month. He has not needed oral corticosteroids or emergency care in the past two years. Two years ago he was prescribed a daily inhaled corticosteroid preventer, but he stopped it after a few weeks because he felt well and kept forgetting it. He says he is unlikely to take any inhaler every day.

He does not smoke or vape. His inhaler technique is good. Examination is normal. Spirometry today shows FEV1 88% predicted with a 9% (230 mL) improvement after bronchodilator.

Which one of the following is the most appropriate change to his treatment?','["Continue salbutamol as needed and add montelukast 10 mg nightly", "Continue salbutamol as needed with no additional treatment", "Replace salbutamol with budesonide/formoterol 200/6 microg, 1 inhalation as needed", "Budesonide/formoterol 200/6 microg, 2 inhalations twice daily plus as needed", "Fluticasone propionate 100 microg twice daily plus salbutamol as needed"]'::jsonb,'C','CORRECT ANSWER

C. Replace salbutamol with budesonide/formoterol 200/6 microg, 1 inhalation as needed

---

He has mild asthma: symptoms less than daily, night waking less than weekly and no recent flare-ups, but he still needs a treatment that reduces flare-up risk. The Australian Asthma Handbook no longer recommends SABA-only treatment for adults and adolescents, because relying on SABA alone increases the risk of severe flare-ups and asthma death. For adults and adolescents with mild asthma, as-needed low-dose budesonide–formoterol (anti-inflammatory reliever, ''AIR-only'') is a preferred option. It is as effective as a daily ICS for preventing severe flare-ups and much better than SABA alone. It suits people who will not use a daily preventer. He takes 1 inhalation of 200/6 microg whenever he has symptoms, with no more than 6 inhalations on one occasion and up to 12 in a day. His written asthma action plan should be updated and he should be reviewed in 1–3 months.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Adults with mild asthma should not be on SABA alone. As-needed budesonide–formoterol 200/6 (1 inhalation per dose, maximum 12 per day) is a preferred option, especially when daily preventer use is unlikely.

---

