• {"name": "Australian Commission on Safety and Quality in Health Care Medicine Finder: budesonide/formoterol (Rilast Turbuhaler) dosing", "url": "https://www.safetyandquality.gov.au/medicine-finder/rilast-turbuhaler"}

---

EDITED BY HEISENBERG','difficult','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "bank170", "review_status": "owner_approved_2026_09_29", "tags": ["ZQ-0105", "zyntra_bank", "mock1", "level_4", "task_management", "group_adult_medicine", "sys_respiratory", "approved_by_owner_2026_09_29"], "clinician_task": "management", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"National Asthma Council Australia: Australian Asthma Handbook (educating adults to self-manage; AIR-only dosing)\", \"url\": \"https://www.asthmahandbook.org.au/management/adults-and-adolescents/principles-of-management/educating-adults-and-adolescents-to-self-manage-asthma\"}; {\"name\": \"Australian Commission on Safety and Quality in Health Care Medicine Finder: budesonide/formoterol (Rilast Turbuhaler) dosing\", \"url\": \"https://www.safetyandquality.gov.au/medicine-finder/rilast-turbuhaler\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "Montelukast is less effective than ICS-containing treatment. The TGA has a boxed warning about neuropsychiatric adverse effects. It is not a preferred step for adults, and it leaves him relying on SABA.", "B": "SABA-only treatment is no longer recommended for adults and adolescents, even with infrequent symptoms, because it gives no anti-inflammatory protection and increases the risk of severe flare-ups.", "D": "This is maintenance-and-reliever therapy (MART) at a medium dose, a higher step suited to moderate–severe asthma. It is more treatment than he needs and requires the daily use he has said he will not do.", "E": "Daily low-dose ICS plus as-needed SABA is an acceptable regimen in general. However, it depends on daily adherence, which he has already shown he will not manage, and non-adherence leaves him on SABA alone. For him, AIR-only is the better choice."}, "key_takeaways": ["Adults with mild asthma should not be on SABA alone. As-needed budesonide–formoterol 200/6 (1 inhalation per dose, maximum 12 per day) is a preferred option, especially when daily preventer use is unlikely."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,1.0,1.0,'tier_prior','tier=4 (bank170)'),
('f34ced42-9f0c-5ee7-b090-767cdb83cbc7'::uuid,'ZQ-0106','adult-medicine','respiratory','A 76-year-old man is admitted to a Perth hospital with worsening breathlessness. He has heart failure with reduced ejection fraction (LVEF 25%) from ischaemic heart disease. For the first three days he received intravenous furosemide 80 mg twice daily and lost 4 kg. His orthopnoea and leg oedema have improved, but a chest X-ray still shows a moderate right pleural effusion and a small left effusion. He has no fever, cough, weight loss or chest pain, and he stopped smoking 20 years ago. A diagnostic pleural aspiration is done on day 4.

Investigations (day 4):
- Serum total protein 64 g/L; pleural fluid protein 33 g/L (ratio 0.52)
- Serum LDH 210 U/L (laboratory upper limit 250); pleural fluid LDH 115 U/L (ratio 0.55)
- Serum albumin 31 g/L; pleural fluid albumin 16 g/L
- Pleural fluid: straw-coloured; pH 7.46; glucose 6.8 mmol/L; white cells 0.6 × 10^9/L (80% lymphocytes); Gram stain negative
- NT-proBNP 6800 ng/L (on admission 11 200 ng/L)
- Pleural fluid cytology: pending

Which one of the following is the most likely explanation for his pleural effusion?','["Transudate from heart failure, altered by diuresis", "Uncomplicated parapneumonic exudate", "Malignant pleural effusion from occult lung cancer", "Tuberculous pleuritis with lymphocytic exudate", "Exudate caused by pulmonary embolism"]'::jsonb,'A','CORRECT ANSWER

A. Transudate from heart failure, altered by diuresis

---

By Light''s criteria this fluid is only just an exudate. The protein ratio is 0.52 (above 0.5). The LDH ratio is 0.55 (below 0.6), and pleural LDH of 115 U/L is below two-thirds of the serum upper limit (about 167 U/L). So only one criterion is met, and only barely. Light''s criteria are sensitive for exudates but misclassify about a quarter of transudates as exudates, especially after diuretics, which concentrate pleural protein. In this situation the serum-to-pleural albumin gradient helps. A gradient above 12 g/L suggests a transudate; here it is 31 − 16 = 15 g/L. (A serum-to-pleural protein gradient above 31 g/L is an alternative test; here it is exactly 31, which is borderline.) Together with bilateral effusions, a high NT-proBNP, lymphocyte-predominant low-cell fluid, normal pH and glucose, and recent intensive diuresis, this is a heart-failure transudate mislabelled as an exudate. Management is to optimise heart failure treatment. Cytology results should still be followed up.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Diuretics can make a heart-failure transudate meet Light''s criteria. If the fluid is only borderline exudative in a diuresed patient, a serum-to-pleural albumin gradient above 12 g/L supports a transudate.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "Light RW et al. Pleural effusions: the diagnostic separation of transudates and exudates (Ann Intern Med 1972), PubMed", "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/4642731/"}
• {"name": "British Thoracic Society Guideline for pleural disease 2023 (Thorax)", "url": "https://thorax.bmj.com/content/78/Suppl_3/s1"}

---

EDITED BY HEISENBERG','difficult','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "bank170", "review_status": "owner_approved_2026_09_29", "tags": ["ZQ-0106", "zyntra_bank", "mock1", "level_4", "task_data_interpretation_synthesis", "group_adult_medicine", "sys_respiratory", "approved_by_owner_2026_09_29"], "clinician_task": "data_interpretation_synthesis", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"Light RW et al. Pleural effusions: the diagnostic separation of transudates and exudates (Ann Intern Med 1972), PubMed\", \"url\": \"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/4642731/\"}; {\"name\": \"British Thoracic Society Guideline for pleural disease 2023 (Thorax)\", \"url\": \"https://thorax.bmj.com/content/78/Suppl_3/s1\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"B": "A parapneumonic effusion would usually come with fever, cough and consolidation, and the fluid would be neutrophil-predominant. He has none of these features, and the pH and glucose are normal.", "C": "Malignancy has to be considered in any unexplained exudate in an ex-smoker, and the cytology will be followed up. However, the albumin gradient shows a transudate, and bilateral effusions with a very high NT-proBNP fit heart failure better.", "D": "TB effusions are lymphocytic exudates, usually with a clearly high protein and high adenosine deaminase, often with fever and weight loss. The fluid here is only borderline exudative and the gradient points to a transudate.", "E": "PE-related effusions are usually small, unilateral and often bloodstained, with pleuritic pain or hypoxaemia. Nothing here points to PE."}, "key_takeaways": ["Diuretics can make a heart-failure transudate meet Light''s criteria. If the fluid is only borderline exudative in a diuresed patient, a serum-to-pleural albumin gradient above 12 g/L supports a transudate."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,1.0,1.0,'tier_prior','tier=4 (bank170)'),
('6b64c2e1-81f4-521e-be95-152b2f503ca3'::uuid,'ZQ-0107','adult-medicine','respiratory','A 62-year-old woman sees her general practitioner in Geelong, Victoria, because of breathlessness that has slowly worsened over 18 months. She now becomes breathless climbing one flight of stairs, and she has a cough with white sputum most mornings. She has smoked 20 cigarettes a day since she was 18 (about 44 pack-years). She had no asthma or hay fever as a child and has had no weight loss or haemoptysis. She was treated with antibiotics for a ''chest infection'' twice last winter.

She has scattered expiratory wheeze and a prolonged expiratory phase, but no clubbing. Oxygen saturation is 95% on room air. A chest X-ray shows hyperinflated lungs with no mass or consolidation.

At the practice, the nurse performs spirometry WITHOUT giving a bronchodilator. The results are FEV1 1.52 L (64% predicted), FVC 2.35 L (82% predicted) and FEV1/FVC 0.65. The report states that the manoeuvres were acceptable and repeatable.

Which one of the following is the most appropriate next step to establish the diagnosis?','["Diagnose COPD from these results and start a long-acting antimuscarinic", "Arrange high-resolution CT of the chest", "Arrange measurement of gas transfer (DLCO) and lung volumes", "Repeat spirometry before and after an inhaled bronchodilator", "Ask her to keep a twice-daily peak expiratory flow diary for 2 weeks"]'::jsonb,'D','CORRECT ANSWER

D. Repeat spirometry before and after an inhaled bronchodilator

---

COPD is diagnosed by showing persistent airflow limitation: a POST-bronchodilator FEV1/FVC below 0.7 in a person with suitable symptoms and exposure. A pre-bronchodilator ratio can overestimate obstruction. Part of the limitation may reverse (as in asthma or asthma–COPD overlap), and some people with a low pre-bronchodilator ratio have a normal post-bronchodilator ratio. COPD-X and GOLD therefore require post-bronchodilator spirometry to confirm the diagnosis and grade severity. Measuring the bronchodilator response also gives information about possible asthma. Once COPD is confirmed, she should get smoking cessation support (the most effective intervention), vaccination, pulmonary rehabilitation referral and inhaled bronchodilator therapy under the COPD-X stepwise plan.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
COPD needs a POST-bronchodilator FEV1/FVC below 0.7. Pre-bronchodilator spirometry alone cannot confirm the diagnosis.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "Lung Foundation Australia/TSANZ COPD-X Plan: C2.3 Spirometry", "url": "https://copdx.org.au/copd-x-plan/confirm-diagnosis/c2-diagnosis/c2-3-spirometry/"}
• {"name": "COPD-X Plan: C. Case finding and confirm diagnosis", "url": "https://copdx.org.au/copd-x-plan/confirm-diagnosis/"}

---

EDITED BY HEISENBERG','moderate','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "bank170", "review_status": "owner_approved_2026_09_29", "tags": ["ZQ-0107", "zyntra_bank", "mock1", "level_3", "task_data_gathering", "group_adult_medicine", "sys_respiratory", "approved_by_owner_2026_09_29"], "clinician_task": "data_gathering", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"Lung Foundation Australia/TSANZ COPD-X Plan: C2.3 Spirometry\", \"url\": \"https://copdx.org.au/copd-x-plan/confirm-diagnosis/c2-diagnosis/c2-3-spirometry/\"}; {\"name\": \"COPD-X Plan: C. Case finding and confirm diagnosis\", \"url\": \"https://copdx.org.au/copd-x-plan/confirm-diagnosis/\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "A pre-bronchodilator FEV1/FVC below 0.7 is not enough to diagnose COPD. Labelling and treating her now risks misdiagnosis, for example missing asthma, which is managed differently (ICS-containing therapy).", "B": "HRCT can show emphysema, bronchiectasis or interstitial lung disease, but it does not diagnose COPD. COPD is a physiological diagnosis made by spirometry. CT is kept for particular questions such as suspected bronchiectasis, ILD or lung cancer assessment.", "C": "Full lung function tests are useful when spirometry is uncertain or another disease is suspected. They are not the first step to confirm airflow limitation, which is done with post-bronchodilator spirometry.", "E": "PEF variability supports a diagnosis of asthma but cannot confirm or exclude COPD. PEF is effort-dependent and much less reliable than spirometry."}, "key_takeaways": ["COPD needs a POST-bronchodilator FEV1/FVC below 0.7. Pre-bronchodilator spirometry alone cannot confirm the diagnosis."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,0.0,1.0,'tier_prior','tier=3 (bank170)'),
('2a61d1ae-afc9-514d-8aec-9615457f03ad'::uuid,'ZQ-0108','adult-medicine','endocrinology','A 59-year-old Torres Strait Islander man sees his general practitioner at a community-controlled health service in Cairns, Queensland, for his diabetes review. He has had type 2 diabetes for 8 years. He takes metformin XR 1000 mg twice daily, perindopril 10 mg daily and atorvastatin 40 mg daily. He has no history of heart failure, myocardial infarction or stroke, and he has had no genital infections. He works as a boat mechanic.

His blood pressure is 128/78 mmHg and his body mass index 31 kg/m². Foot examination is normal.

Investigations:
- HbA1c 7.6% (60 mmol/mol)
- eGFR 52 mL/min/1.73 m² (stable over 12 months)
- Urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio 38 mg/mmol (on three samples over 6 months)
- Potassium 4.8 mmol/L (3.5–5.2)
- LDL cholesterol 1.7 mmol/L

Which one of the following is the most appropriate addition to his treatment?','["Gliclazide MR 30 mg daily", "Empagliflozin 10 mg daily", "Sitagliptin 50 mg daily", "Irbesartan 150 mg daily", "Insulin glargine 10 units at night"]'::jsonb,'B','CORRECT ANSWER

B. Empagliflozin 10 mg daily

---

He has type 2 diabetes with chronic kidney disease: eGFR 52 with persistent albuminuria (uACR 38 mg/mmol, which is macroalbuminuria). He is already on a maximum-dose ACE inhibitor. Australian guidance (the ADS type 2 diabetes algorithm and the Kidney Health Australia CKD guidance) recommends adding an SGLT2 inhibitor for people with type 2 diabetes and CKD, regardless of HbA1c. SGLT2 inhibitors slow the decline in eGFR, reduce progression to kidney failure, and lower heart failure and cardiovascular events. Empagliflozin can be started at an eGFR of 20 or more (dapagliflozin at 25 or more), although its glucose-lowering effect weakens below an eGFR of about 45. He should be counselled about sick-day rules (withhold during acute illness, fasting or surgery because of the risk of euglycaemic DKA), genital hygiene and hydration. A small initial dip in eGFR is expected. Metformin can continue at his eGFR, with dose review below 45.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
In type 2 diabetes with albuminuric CKD, add an SGLT2 inhibitor (on top of maximum-tolerated ACE inhibitor/ARB) for kidney and heart protection whatever the HbA1c. Empagliflozin can be started at an eGFR of 20 or more.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "Australian Diabetes Society: Australian Type 2 Diabetes Glycaemic Management Algorithm", "url": "https://www.diabetessociety.com.au/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/T2D-Treatment-Algorithm-24.02.2025.pdf"}
• {"name": "Medicine Today: Preventing diabetic kidney disease progression \u2013 a 2025 update", "url": "https://medicinetoday.com.au/mt/supplements/feature-article/preventing-diabetic-kidney-disease-progression-2025-update"}
