EDITED BY HEISENBERG','moderate','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "bank170", "review_status": "owner_approved_2026_09_29", "tags": ["ZQ-0108", "zyntra_bank", "mock1", "level_3", "task_management", "group_adult_medicine", "sys_endocrine", "approved_by_owner_2026_09_29"], "clinician_task": "management", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"Australian Diabetes Society: Australian Type 2 Diabetes Glycaemic Management Algorithm\", \"url\": \"https://www.diabetessociety.com.au/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/T2D-Treatment-Algorithm-24.02.2025.pdf\"}; {\"name\": \"Medicine Today: Preventing diabetic kidney disease progression \\u2013 a 2025 update\", \"url\": \"https://medicinetoday.com.au/mt/supplements/feature-article/preventing-diabetic-kidney-disease-progression-2025-update\"}; {\"name\": \"PBAC outcomes September 2025 (SGLT2 inhibitor CKD listings)\", \"url\": \"https://prod1.pbs.gov.au/industry/listing/elements/pbac-meetings/pbac-outcomes/2025-09/pbac-web-outcomes-09-2025v2.pdf\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "A sulfonylurea lowers glucose but has no kidney or cardiovascular benefit, and it causes weight gain and hypoglycaemia (a particular concern for someone working on boats).", "C": "DPP-4 inhibitors are weight-neutral and low-risk but have not been shown to protect the kidneys or heart. They are reserved for people who cannot use an SGLT2 inhibitor or GLP-1 receptor agonist.", "D": "Combining an ACE inhibitor and an ARB (dual RAS blockade) increases hyperkalaemia and acute kidney injury without improving kidney outcomes. It is not recommended.", "E": "His HbA1c is only modestly above target. Insulin does not protect the kidneys and brings a risk of hypoglycaemia and weight gain. It is not the next step."}, "key_takeaways": ["In type 2 diabetes with albuminuric CKD, add an SGLT2 inhibitor (on top of maximum-tolerated ACE inhibitor/ARB) for kidney and heart protection whatever the HbA1c. Empagliflozin can be started at an eGFR of 20 or more."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,0.0,1.0,'tier_prior','tier=3 (bank170)'),
('d8ec36d1-1aa0-5c77-afeb-5bfefebdbe8d'::uuid,'ZQ-0109','adult-medicine','endocrinology','A 41-year-old woman sees her general practitioner on the Sunshine Coast, Queensland, with two weeks of pain in the front of her neck that spreads to both ears and her jaw, and palpitations. Four weeks ago she had an upper respiratory tract infection. She has lost 2 kg. She last gave birth 3 years ago, is not breastfeeding, and has had no iodinated contrast in the past year. She takes no medicines or supplements.

Her temperature is 37.8°C, pulse 108/min and regular, and blood pressure 134/76 mmHg. The thyroid is firm, mildly enlarged and exquisitely tender, with no discrete nodule. There is a fine tremor. She has no eye signs or pretibial myxoedema.

Investigations:
- TSH <0.01 mIU/L (0.4–4.0)
- Free T4 38 pmol/L (10–20)
- Free T3 9.5 pmol/L (3.0–6.0)
- ESR 78 mm/h (<20); CRP 45 mg/L (<5)
- TSH-receptor antibody: negative
- White cell count 9.8 × 10^9/L (4.0–11.0)
- Technetium-99m thyroid scan: diffusely and markedly reduced uptake

Which one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?','["Graves disease (autoimmune hyperthyroidism)", "Toxic multinodular goitre (Plummer disease)", "Painless (silent) lymphocytic thyroiditis", "Acute suppurative thyroiditis", "Subacute (de Quervain) thyroiditis"]'::jsonb,'E','CORRECT ANSWER

E. Subacute (de Quervain) thyroiditis

---

She has thyrotoxicosis with a painful, tender goitre after a viral illness, a markedly raised ESR/CRP, negative TSH-receptor antibodies and LOW radionuclide uptake. Low uptake shows the thyrotoxicosis comes from leakage of preformed hormone from an inflamed gland, not from overproduction. Pain, tenderness and high inflammatory markers identify subacute granulomatous (de Quervain) thyroiditis. The typical course is thyrotoxic (weeks), then hypothyroid (weeks to months), then usually back to normal. Treatment is symptomatic: an NSAID for pain (prednisolone if severe or NSAID-resistant) and a beta-blocker for adrenergic symptoms. Antithyroid drugs (carbimazole, propylthiouracil) have no role because hormone synthesis is not increased. Check thyroid function every 4–6 weeks to detect the hypothyroid phase.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Thyrotoxicosis plus a tender thyroid, high ESR/CRP and low radionuclide uptake means subacute (de Quervain) thyroiditis. Treat with an NSAID (or prednisolone) and a beta-blocker, not carbimazole.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "Ross DS et al. 2016 American Thyroid Association Guidelines for diagnosis and management of hyperthyroidism and other causes of thyrotoxicosis (Thyroid)", "url": "https://www.liebertpub.com/doi/10.1089/thy.2016.0229"}
• {"name": "StatPearls: De Quervain thyroiditis (NCBI Bookshelf)", "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK526066/"}

---

EDITED BY HEISENBERG','difficult','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "bank170", "review_status": "owner_approved_2026_09_29", "tags": ["ZQ-0109", "zyntra_bank", "mock1", "level_4", "task_data_interpretation_synthesis", "group_adult_medicine", "sys_endocrine", "approved_by_owner_2026_09_29"], "clinician_task": "data_interpretation_synthesis", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"Ross DS et al. 2016 American Thyroid Association Guidelines for diagnosis and management of hyperthyroidism and other causes of thyrotoxicosis (Thyroid)\", \"url\": \"https://www.liebertpub.com/doi/10.1089/thy.2016.0229\"}; {\"name\": \"StatPearls: De Quervain thyroiditis (NCBI Bookshelf)\", \"url\": \"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK526066/\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "Graves disease causes a painless diffuse goitre with HIGH, diffuse uptake and usually positive TRAb. Neck pain, low uptake and very high inflammatory markers argue strongly against it.", "B": "Toxic multinodular goitre shows patchy INCREASED uptake in autonomous nodules, is painless and usually affects older people.", "C": "Painless (silent) thyroiditis also shows low uptake, but by definition the gland is NOT tender and inflammatory markers are normal or only mildly raised. It often occurs post partum (within 12 months of delivery), which does not apply here.", "D": "Acute suppurative thyroiditis is a bacterial infection with high fever, a very tender, often unilateral fluctuant mass, a raised white cell count, and usually NORMAL thyroid function. It is rare and often linked to a piriform sinus fistula."}, "key_takeaways": ["Thyrotoxicosis plus a tender thyroid, high ESR/CRP and low radionuclide uptake means subacute (de Quervain) thyroiditis. Treat with an NSAID (or prednisolone) and a beta-blocker, not carbimazole."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,1.0,1.0,'tier_prior','tier=4 (bank170)'),
('23c1f39f-481f-5c63-a0fa-4d1cbe408ec9'::uuid,'ZQ-0110','adult-medicine','endocrinology','A 44-year-old man sees his general practitioner in Adelaide, South Australia, after routine blood tests for a life-insurance application showed a high calcium. He feels well and has no history of kidney stones, fractures, abdominal pain, polyuria or mood change. He takes no medicines or supplements, including no lithium or thiazides. His 47-year-old sister was ''found to have a high calcium'' several years ago and had a neck exploration in Melbourne, but no parathyroid adenoma was found and her calcium stayed high afterwards.

Examination is normal. His blood pressure is 128/80 mmHg.

Investigations (repeated twice, fasting samples taken without prolonged tourniquet):
- Albumin-adjusted calcium 2.76 mmol/L and 2.78 mmol/L (2.10–2.60)
- Parathyroid hormone 7.4 pmol/L (1.6–6.9)
- Phosphate 0.82 mmol/L (0.75–1.50)
- Magnesium 1.02 mmol/L (0.70–1.10)
- 25-hydroxyvitamin D 72 nmol/L (>50)
- Creatinine 84 µmol/L; eGFR >90 mL/min/1.73 m²

Which one of the following is the most appropriate next investigation?','["Technetium-99m sestamibi scan to localise an adenoma", "Dual-energy X-ray absorptiometry (DXA) of spine, hip and forearm", "24-hour urine calcium with calcium/creatinine clearance ratio", "Ultrasound of the neck for parathyroid localisation", "Ultrasound of the renal tract for nephrocalcinosis"]'::jsonb,'C','CORRECT ANSWER

C. 24-hour urine calcium with calcium/creatinine clearance ratio

---

He has PTH-dependent hypercalcaemia (raised calcium with an inappropriately high PTH). The main differential is primary hyperparathyroidism (PHPT) versus familial hypocalciuric hypercalcaemia (FHH), usually due to an inactivating CASR variant. FHH is benign and does not need surgery, and parathyroidectomy does not cure it. His sister''s failed neck exploration with persistent hypercalcaemia is a strong clue to a familial condition. The Australian position statement on PHPT recommends measuring the calcium/creatinine clearance ratio (CCCR) from a 24-hour urine collection with paired serum values before localisation or surgery. A CCCR below 0.01 strongly suggests FHH; above 0.02 favours PHPT; 0.01–0.02 overlaps and needs CASR genetic testing. The test should be done when vitamin D is replete (as here, because vitamin D deficiency lowers urine calcium) and off thiazides or lithium. CASR (and AP2S1/GNA11) gene testing confirms FHH, and cascade testing can be offered to relatives.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Before localising a parathyroid adenoma, exclude FHH. A calcium/creatinine clearance ratio below 0.01 (vitamin D replete, off thiazides) suggests FHH, and a relative with a failed parathyroidectomy is a red flag.

---

REFERENCES
