• {"name": "Excluding familial hypocalciuric hypercalcaemia before surgery for primary hyperparathyroidism (PMC)", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9685926/"}

---

EDITED BY HEISENBERG','difficult','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "bank170", "review_status": "owner_approved_2026_09_29", "tags": ["ZQ-0110", "zyntra_bank", "mock1", "level_5", "task_data_gathering", "group_adult_medicine", "sys_endocrine", "approved_by_owner_2026_09_29"], "clinician_task": "data_gathering", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"Primary hyperparathyroidism in adults (Part I) assessment and medical management: Position statement of the Endocrine Society of Australia, ANZ Endocrine Surgeons and ANZ Bone and Mineral Society (WSLHD repository record)\", \"url\": \"https://wslhd.intersearch.com.au/wslhdjspui/handle/1/8977\"}; {\"name\": \"Excluding familial hypocalciuric hypercalcaemia before surgery for primary hyperparathyroidism (PMC)\", \"url\": \"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9685926/\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "Localisation imaging is only for planning surgery once PHPT is confirmed and surgery is intended. It does not diagnose PHPT, and a negative or false-positive scan could lead to an unnecessary operation, as happened to his sister.", "B": "DXA helps decide whether surgery is indicated once PHPT is confirmed (osteoporosis is a surgical criterion). It does not tell PHPT from FHH.", "D": "As for sestamibi, neck ultrasound is a localisation test for a confirmed biochemical diagnosis of PHPT. Localisation should not be used to make the diagnosis.", "E": "Renal imaging for silent stones or nephrocalcinosis is part of end-organ assessment in confirmed PHPT. It does not answer the key diagnostic question here."}, "key_takeaways": ["Before localising a parathyroid adenoma, exclude FHH. A calcium/creatinine clearance ratio below 0.01 (vitamin D replete, off thiazides) suggests FHH, and a relative with a failed parathyroidectomy is a red flag."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,2.0,1.0,'tier_prior','tier=5 (bank170)'),
('428c73ff-3414-596a-be4f-0082e0e0274e'::uuid,'ZQ-0111','adult-medicine','endocrinology','A 46-year-old man sees his general practitioner on the Gold Coast, Queensland, for blood pressure review. Hypertension was diagnosed 2 years ago and is not controlled despite amlodipine 10 mg daily and perindopril 10 mg daily. Home readings average 158/96 mmHg. He takes no diuretics, NSAIDs, liquorice or other supplements, drinks two standard drinks a week and does not snore. He has mild muscle cramps. His father had a stroke at 52.

His blood pressure is 162/98 mmHg in both arms and his body mass index 25 kg/m². There are no cushingoid features or renal bruits.

Investigations:
- Sodium 144 mmol/L (135–145)
- Potassium 3.1 mmol/L (3.5–5.2)
- Bicarbonate 31 mmol/L (22–32)
- Creatinine 82 µmol/L
- Fasting glucose 5.1 mmol/L
- ECG: left ventricular hypertrophy by voltage criteria

You suspect primary aldosteronism.

Which one of the following is the most appropriate next step in his investigation?','["CT of the adrenal glands with fine cuts", "Correct potassium, then measure aldosterone and renin on current medicines", "Arrange a seated saline suppression test", "Stop both antihypertensives for 4 weeks, then measure aldosterone and renin", "Collect a 24-hour urine for fractionated metanephrines"]'::jsonb,'B','CORRECT ANSWER

B. Correct potassium, then measure aldosterone and renin on current medicines

---

He has several reasons to screen for primary aldosteronism (PA): uncontrolled hypertension on two drugs, spontaneous hypokalaemia with metabolic alkalosis, early LVH and a family history of early stroke. The screening test is the plasma aldosterone concentration with renin (aldosterone-to-renin ratio, ARR). Hypokalaemia suppresses aldosterone secretion and can give a falsely negative result, so potassium should be corrected first. The 2025 Endocrine Society guideline (co-authored by the Australian PA expert Stowasser) supports initial screening while patients stay on most antihypertensives, including ACE inhibitors and dihydropyridine CCBs. These drugs tend to RAISE renin, so a positive screen on them strongly suggests PA; a negative result in a high-suspicion patient can be repeated after adjusting medicines. Blood should be taken mid-morning after at least 2 hours upright and 5–15 minutes seated. A positive screen leads to endocrinology referral for confirmatory testing, adrenal CT and adrenal venous sampling.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Screen for primary aldosteronism with the aldosterone–renin ratio after correcting hypokalaemia. Most antihypertensives can be continued for the initial screen; a positive result on an ACE inhibitor or CCB is strongly suggestive.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "Adler GK, Stowasser M et al. Primary aldosteronism: an Endocrine Society clinical practice guideline (2025): guideline page", "url": "https://www.endocrine.org/clinical-practice-guidelines/primary-aldosteronism-2"}
• {"name": "Cleveland Clinic Journal of Medicine: Interpreting the 2025 Endocrine Society primary aldosteronism guideline", "url": "https://www.ccjm.org/content/93/9/515"}

---

EDITED BY HEISENBERG','difficult','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "bank170", "review_status": "owner_approved_2026_09_29", "tags": ["ZQ-0111", "zyntra_bank", "mock1", "level_4", "task_data_gathering", "group_adult_medicine", "sys_endocrine", "approved_by_owner_2026_09_29"], "clinician_task": "data_gathering", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"Adler GK, Stowasser M et al. Primary aldosteronism: an Endocrine Society clinical practice guideline (2025): guideline page\", \"url\": \"https://www.endocrine.org/clinical-practice-guidelines/primary-aldosteronism-2\"}; {\"name\": \"Cleveland Clinic Journal of Medicine: Interpreting the 2025 Endocrine Society primary aldosteronism guideline\", \"url\": \"https://www.ccjm.org/content/93/9/515\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "Adrenal imaging comes AFTER biochemical confirmation. Non-functioning adrenal incidentalomas are common, and CT cannot tell unilateral from bilateral PA (that needs adrenal venous sampling).", "C": "Confirmatory (suppression) testing follows a positive screening ARR. It is not the first test, and the 2025 guideline allows it to be skipped when spontaneous hypokalaemia, suppressed renin and clearly raised aldosterone coexist.", "D": "Stopping both drugs would leave a man with LVH and BP of 160/98 exposed to dangerously uncontrolled hypertension. Full withdrawal is not needed for initial screening, and the hypokalaemia would still be uncorrected. If withdrawal is later needed, substitute drugs (for example verapamil SR, prazosin, hydralazine or moxonidine) are used.", "E": "Phaeochromocytoma screening is for paroxysmal symptoms, labile or crisis hypertension, or an adrenal incidentaloma. Hypokalaemic alkalosis with steady hypertension points to aldosterone excess."}, "key_takeaways": ["Screen for primary aldosteronism with the aldosterone–renin ratio after correcting hypokalaemia. Most antihypertensives can be continued for the initial screen; a positive result on an ACE inhibitor or CCB is strongly suggestive."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,1.0,1.0,'tier_prior','tier=4 (bank170)'),
('44475b56-aba5-5567-90a7-3a176f3ba9de'::uuid,'ZQ-0112','adult-medicine','endocrinology','A 72-year-old woman sees her general practitioner in Newcastle, New South Wales. Five years ago she was found to have osteoporosis (lumbar spine T-score −3.0) after a low-trauma wrist fracture. She has since had denosumab 60 mg subcutaneously every 6 months (10 injections), with calcium and vitamin D supplements. She has had no further fractures. Her last injection was exactly 6 months ago.

A DXA scan last week showed a lumbar spine T-score of −2.1 and a total hip T-score of −1.9. She has read about osteonecrosis of the jaw and, now that her ''bones are better'', wants to stop denosumab. After a full discussion of the benefits and risks, she has firmly decided to stop.

She has no dental problems and saw her dentist last month. There is no history of reflux or oesophageal disease.

Investigations:
- Albumin-adjusted calcium 2.34 mmol/L (2.10–2.60)
- 25-hydroxyvitamin D 84 nmol/L (>50)
- eGFR 68 mL/min/1.73 m²

Which one of the following is the most appropriate plan?','["Stop denosumab and give zoledronic acid 5 mg IV now, at the due date", "Stop denosumab and repeat DXA in 2 years as a planned drug holiday", "Delay the next denosumab injection by 6 months, then reassess her wishes", "Stop denosumab and continue calcium and vitamin D supplements alone", "Stop denosumab and start raloxifene 60 mg daily in 12 months"]'::jsonb,'A','CORRECT ANSWER

A. Stop denosumab and give zoledronic acid 5 mg IV now, at the due date

---

Denosumab''s effect wears off quickly. When it is stopped or delayed, bone turnover rebounds above baseline, the BMD gained is lost within about 12–24 months, and there is a well-documented risk of multiple vertebral fractures. The TGA has strengthened warnings about this, and the 2024 RACGP/Healthy Bones Australia guideline advises that denosumab should not be stopped without follow-on antiresorptive therapy. The recommended approach is to switch to a bisphosphonate, most often IV zoledronic acid, given about 6 months after the last denosumab dose (that is, when the next injection would be due). Continue calcium and vitamin D. Some specialists check bone turnover markers and repeat zoledronic acid if needed. Her eGFR of 68 and normal calcium and vitamin D make zoledronic acid suitable. A drug holiday is a bisphosphonate concept and does not apply to denosumab.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Do not stop denosumab without a transition plan: give a bisphosphonate (usually zoledronic acid) about 6 months after the last injection to prevent rebound bone loss and multiple vertebral fractures.

---

