EDITED BY HEISENBERG','difficult','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "bank170", "review_status": "owner_approved_2026_09_29", "tags": ["ZQ-0112", "zyntra_bank", "mock1", "level_5", "task_management", "group_adult_medicine", "sys_endocrine", "approved_by_owner_2026_09_29"], "clinician_task": "management", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"2024 RACGP and Healthy Bones Australia guideline for osteoporosis management and fracture prevention (MJA 2025)\", \"url\": \"https://www.mja.com.au/journal/2025/222/9/2024-royal-australian-college-general-practitioners-and-healthy-bones-australia\"}; {\"name\": \"TGA safety update: Strengthened warnings for fracture risk after discontinuation of denosumab (Prolia and biosimilars)\", \"url\": \"https://www.tga.gov.au/news/safety-updates/strengthened-warnings-fracture-risk-after-discontinuation-denosumab-prolia-and-biosimilars\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"B": "Unlike bisphosphonates, denosumab does not stay bound in bone. A ''holiday'' after denosumab leads to rapid bone loss and a high risk of multiple vertebral fractures.", "C": "Delaying a dose by more than about 4 weeks already starts the rebound. Waiting a further 6 months exposes her to the same fracture risk as stopping without cover.", "D": "Calcium and vitamin D alone do not prevent the rebound increase in bone resorption after denosumab.", "E": "Waiting 12 months leaves her unprotected during the period of highest rebound risk. Raloxifene is also a weak antiresorptive with little evidence for preventing post-denosumab bone loss, and it increases VTE risk."}, "key_takeaways": ["Do not stop denosumab without a transition plan: give a bisphosphonate (usually zoledronic acid) about 6 months after the last injection to prevent rebound bone loss and multiple vertebral fractures."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,2.0,1.0,'tier_prior','tier=5 (bank170)'),
('5c414fcb-5698-5d5b-aae9-08214e56d5ad'::uuid,'ZQ-0113','adult-medicine','renal','A 78-year-old man is admitted to a hospital in Bendigo, Victoria, with three days of diarrhoea and vomiting after a family gathering where several relatives also became unwell. He has hypertension and heart failure with preserved ejection fraction. His regular medicines are perindopril 5 mg daily and furosemide 40 mg daily. Ten days ago he started ibuprofen 400 mg three times daily, bought at a pharmacy, for knee osteoarthritis. He has kept taking all his medicines during the illness.

His pulse is 104/min, blood pressure 102/60 mmHg lying and 84/50 mmHg standing, and temperature 37.2°C. His mucous membranes are dry and the jugular venous pressure is not visible. There is no rash. A bladder scan after voiding shows 40 mL.

Investigations:
- Creatinine 245 µmol/L (60–110; baseline 105 two months ago)
- Urea 24.1 mmol/L (3.0–8.0)
- Potassium 5.6 mmol/L (3.5–5.2)
- Urine sodium 12 mmol/L; fractional excretion of sodium 0.6%
- Urinalysis: specific gravity 1.028; no blood, protein or leucocytes; microscopy shows occasional hyaline casts
- Renal ultrasound: normal-sized kidneys, no hydronephrosis

Which one of the following is the most likely mechanism of his acute kidney injury?','["Acute interstitial nephritis caused by ibuprofen", "Ischaemic acute tubular necrosis after dehydration", "Bilateral renal artery stenosis unmasked by perindopril", "Bladder outlet obstruction from prostatic enlargement", "Prerenal AKI from volume loss and the ''triple whammy''"]'::jsonb,'E','CORRECT ANSWER

E. Prerenal AKI from volume loss and the ''triple whammy''

---

This is haemodynamic (prerenal) AKI. Volume depletion from gastroenteritis was made much worse by the ''triple whammy'': an ACE inhibitor (efferent arteriolar dilatation), a diuretic (further volume loss) and an NSAID (afferent vasoconstriction by blocking prostaglandins). Together these remove the kidney''s ability to autoregulate GFR. The urine picture confirms intact tubular function: urine sodium below 20 mmol/L, FENa below 1%, concentrated urine, bland sediment with only hyaline casts, a raised urea-to-creatinine ratio, and postural hypotension. Management is to stop ibuprofen, withhold perindopril and furosemide during the acute illness (sick-day rules), give careful IV fluid while watching his HFpEF, treat the hyperkalaemia, and monitor creatinine. The medicines are reviewed on recovery, and he should be counselled to avoid NSAIDs.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
ACE inhibitor/ARB + diuretic + NSAID (''triple whammy'') plus volume depletion causes prerenal AKI with urine sodium below 20 and FENa below 1%. Stop the NSAID and follow sick-day rules for the others.

---

