EDITED BY HEISENBERG','moderate','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "bank170", "review_status": "owner_approved_2026_09_29", "tags": ["ZQ-0113", "zyntra_bank", "mock1", "level_3", "task_data_interpretation_synthesis", "group_adult_medicine", "sys_renal", "approved_by_owner_2026_09_29"], "clinician_task": "data_interpretation_synthesis", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"Kidney Health Australia: Chronic Kidney Disease Management in Primary Care handbook, 5th edition (2024), including medicines and sick-day guidance\", \"url\": \"https://kidney.org.au/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/KHA-CKD-Handbook-5th-Ed-July2024.pdf\"}; {\"name\": \"bpacnz: Avoiding the triple whammy in primary care (2018)\", \"url\": \"https://bpac.org.nz/2018/triple-whammy.aspx\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "NSAID-induced AIN usually causes sterile pyuria, white cell casts, sometimes eosinophiluria, and often proteinuria (NSAIDs can also cause minimal change disease). FENa is usually above 1%. The bland urine and low FENa here do not fit.", "B": "Established ATN causes loss of tubular sodium reabsorption: urine sodium above 40 mmol/L, FENa above 2%, isosthenuric urine and muddy brown granular casts. His tubules are still conserving sodium avidly.", "C": "Renovascular disease can cause AKI after starting an ACE inhibitor, but he has been on perindopril long-term with a stable baseline creatinine. The acute trigger is clearly the illness plus the new NSAID.", "D": "A post-void residual of 40 mL and no hydronephrosis exclude clinically significant obstruction."}, "key_takeaways": ["ACE inhibitor/ARB + diuretic + NSAID (''triple whammy'') plus volume depletion causes prerenal AKI with urine sodium below 20 and FENa below 1%. Stop the NSAID and follow sick-day rules for the others."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,0.0,1.0,'tier_prior','tier=3 (bank170)'),
('0f703cf8-4322-525c-9781-51692745cde0'::uuid,'ZQ-0114','adult-medicine','renal','A 69-year-old woman is admitted to a hospital in Mackay, Queensland, after her daughter noticed she had been confused and unsteady for two days. Three weeks ago her general practitioner started sertraline 50 mg daily for depression after her husband''s death. She also takes amlodipine 5 mg daily. She takes no diuretics. She has not had vomiting or diarrhoea, and she says she has been drinking ''plenty of water and tea'' because she read it is good for her.

Her pulse is 78/min, blood pressure 138/80 mmHg lying and 136/82 mmHg standing. Her mucous membranes are moist, the jugular venous pressure is normal, and there is no oedema or ascites. She is disoriented to time but has no focal neurological signs. She has not had a seizure.

Investigations:
- Sodium 121 mmol/L (135–145); one month ago 138 mmol/L
- Potassium 4.0 mmol/L
- Urea 2.8 mmol/L (3.0–8.0)
- Creatinine 58 µmol/L
- Glucose 5.6 mmol/L
- Serum osmolality 254 mOsm/kg (275–295)
- Uric acid 0.16 mmol/L (0.15–0.40)

Which one of the following is the most useful next investigation to establish the cause of her hyponatraemia?','["CT scan of the brain without contrast", "Serum lipids and total protein", "Plasma copeptin concentration", "Spot urine osmolality and urine sodium", "Fluid restriction to 1 L a day and repeat sodium at 48 hours"]'::jsonb,'D','CORRECT ANSWER

D. Spot urine osmolality and urine sodium

---

She has hypotonic hyponatraemia (serum osmolality 254) and is clinically euvolaemic. The key next step is to measure urine osmolality and urine sodium on a spot sample taken at the same time as the serum sample, before treatment. A urine osmolality below 100 mOsm/kg means maximally dilute urine, pointing to excess water intake (primary polydipsia) or low solute intake (''tea and toast''). Her reported heavy fluid intake makes this a real possibility. A urine osmolality above 100 mOsm/kg with urine sodium above 30 mmol/L in a euvolaemic patient not on diuretics fits SIADH, here plausibly caused by the SSRI. This distinction changes management: fluid restriction treats SIADH, whereas polydipsia responds to reduced intake and carries a high risk of rapid overcorrection once water diuresis begins. Hypothyroidism and adrenal insufficiency must also be excluded (TSH, morning cortisol) before SIADH is diagnosed. She has moderate symptoms, so correction should be monitored closely, with a limit of about 8–10 mmol/L in 24 hours to avoid osmotic demyelination.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
In hypotonic, euvolaemic hyponatraemia, measure paired urine osmolality and urine sodium before treatment. Urine osmolality below 100 suggests polydipsia or low solute intake; above 100 with urine sodium above 30 suggests SIADH (after excluding hypothyroidism and adrenal insufficiency).

---

REFERENCES
