EDITED BY HEISENBERG','difficult','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "bank170", "review_status": "owner_approved_2026_09_29", "tags": ["ZQ-0114", "zyntra_bank", "mock1", "level_4", "task_data_gathering", "group_adult_medicine", "sys_renal", "approved_by_owner_2026_09_29"], "clinician_task": "data_gathering", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"RACGP Australian Family Physician: The suspect \\u2013 SIADH (2017)\", \"url\": \"https://www.racgp.org.au/afp/2017/september/the-suspect-siadh\"}; {\"name\": \"Spasovski G et al. Clinical practice guideline on diagnosis and treatment of hyponatraemia (European Journal of Endocrinology 2014), PubMed\", \"url\": \"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/24569125/\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "Imaging may be needed if she has a fall or focal signs, but her confusion is explained by acute hyponatraemia. A CT will not identify its cause.", "B": "Lipids and protein are checked when pseudohyponatraemia is suspected, that is, when measured serum osmolality is NORMAL. Her osmolality is low, confirming true hypotonic hyponatraemia.", "C": "Copeptin is a specialist test with limited routine availability and uncertain value in the routine hyponatraemia work-up. It is not the next step.", "E": "Starting fluid restriction before the urine studies may be wrong (for example if urine osmolality is low) and changes the urine results, making them harder to interpret. Investigate first."}, "key_takeaways": ["In hypotonic, euvolaemic hyponatraemia, measure paired urine osmolality and urine sodium before treatment. Urine osmolality below 100 suggests polydipsia or low solute intake; above 100 with urine sodium above 30 suggests SIADH (after excluding hypothyroidism and adrenal insufficiency)."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,1.0,1.0,'tier_prior','tier=4 (bank170)'),
('1ac07a66-9f19-5b0a-9fcc-a7f81d7ccd4c'::uuid,'ZQ-0115','adult-medicine','renal','A 74-year-old man is brought to the emergency department of Rockhampton Hospital, Queensland, with tingling around his mouth and painful spasms of his hands. Over the past month he has had two episodes of palpitations. He has gastro-oesophageal reflux disease treated with esomeprazole 40 mg daily for 5 years, heart failure treated with furosemide 40 mg daily, and hypertension. He drinks one standard drink a day. He has had no neck surgery or radiation. Two weeks ago his general practitioner started oral potassium chloride and calcium carbonate supplements for low levels, but his symptoms have worsened.

He has a positive Trousseau sign. His pulse is 92/min and irregular, and blood pressure 132/78 mmHg.

Investigations:
- Albumin-adjusted calcium 1.84 mmol/L (2.10–2.60)
- Potassium 3.0 mmol/L (3.5–5.2)
- Magnesium 0.28 mmol/L (0.70–1.10)
- Phosphate 1.10 mmol/L (0.75–1.50)
- Parathyroid hormone 3.2 pmol/L (1.6–6.9)
- 25-hydroxyvitamin D 78 nmol/L (>50)
- Creatinine 96 µmol/L
- ECG: sinus rhythm with frequent ventricular ectopics; QTc 492 ms

Which one of the following best explains his biochemical abnormalities?','["Primary hypoparathyroidism", "Vitamin D–deficient osteomalacia", "Hypomagnesaemia from long-term proton pump inhibitor use", "Gitelman syndrome presenting late in life", "Hypokalaemia from furosemide causing secondary hypocalcaemia"]'::jsonb,'C','CORRECT ANSWER

C. Hypomagnesaemia from long-term proton pump inhibitor use

---

Severe hypomagnesaemia (0.28 mmol/L) is the unifying cause. Magnesium is needed for PTH secretion and for end-organ responsiveness to PTH, so severe deficiency causes hypocalcaemia with an inappropriately NORMAL or low PTH (functional hypoparathyroidism). Magnesium deficiency also causes renal potassium wasting through ROMK channels, which is why his potassium and calcium did not respond to oral replacement. Long-term PPI use (usually more than a year) is a recognised cause, through reduced intestinal magnesium absorption via TRPM6/7. The TGA and Australian Prescriber have highlighted it, and the loop diuretic makes it worse. Treatment is IV magnesium sulfate with cardiac monitoring (a typical adult regimen is about 10 mmol over 1–2 hours, then an infusion), followed by repletion of calcium and potassium, and review of the PPI indication (step down, H2 antagonist or stop if possible). Magnesium usually falls again if the PPI is continued.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Refractory hypokalaemia and hypocalcaemia with inappropriately normal PTH: check magnesium. Long-term PPIs (worse with diuretics) are a common cause. Replace magnesium first and review the PPI.

---

