EDITED BY HEISENBERG','difficult','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "bank170", "review_status": "owner_approved_2026_09_29", "tags": ["ZQ-0116", "zyntra_bank", "mock1", "level_4", "task_data_interpretation_synthesis", "group_adult_medicine", "sys_gastroenterology", "approved_by_owner_2026_09_29"], "clinician_task": "data_interpretation_synthesis", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"Gralnek IM et al. ESGE Guideline: Endoscopic diagnosis and management of nonvariceal upper GI haemorrhage \\u2013 update 2021 (Endoscopy), PubMed\", \"url\": \"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/33567467/\"}; {\"name\": \"Laine L et al. ACG Clinical Guideline: Upper Gastrointestinal and Ulcer Bleeding (2021)\", \"url\": \"https://www.darmzentrum-bern.ch/fileadmin/darmzentrum/Education/Bible_Class/2022/upper_GI_bleeding/ACG_-_Upper_GI_Bleed_2021.pdf\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"B": "This miscounts the urea, which scores 3 points in the 8.0–9.9 mmol/L band. Only a GBS of 0–1 is considered low risk.", "C": "A score of 0 needs a urea below 6.5 mmol/L and no melaena. Both are present here.", "D": "A score of 9 is not supported by his data. International guidelines also advise against very urgent (under 6 hours) endoscopy even in higher-risk patients once they are resuscitated; within 24 hours is standard.", "E": "The GBS is designed for use at presentation. A normal haemoglobin does not make it unreliable; the urea and melaena already carry the risk signal."}, "key_takeaways": ["Glasgow-Blatchford: urea is weighted heavily (6.5–7.9 = 2, 8.0–9.9 = 3, 10–24.9 = 4, 25 or more = 6) and melaena adds 1. Only a GBS of 0–1 is low risk for outpatient care, even if the patient looks well with a normal haemoglobin."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,1.0,1.0,'tier_prior','tier=4 (bank170)'),
('53d840a6-0e6a-572c-beee-fa710ed6287a'::uuid,'ZQ-0117','adult-medicine','gastroenterology','A 29-year-old woman sees her general practitioner in Albany, Western Australia, with tiredness. Blood tests show iron-deficiency anaemia (haemoglobin 104 g/L, ferritin 6 µg/L). For two years she had bloating and loose stools. Four months ago, after reading about gluten online, she started a strict gluten-free diet and her bowel symptoms settled. Her sister has type 1 diabetes. She has heavy periods only occasionally and eats red meat three times a week.

Last week her GP requested tissue transglutaminase IgA (tTG-IgA), which was negative, with a normal total IgA level. She is relieved, feels much better on the diet and says she is ''very reluctant'' to eat gluten again unless it is truly necessary. Examination is normal.

Which one of the following is the most appropriate next step to clarify whether she has coeliac disease?','["Accept the negative tTG-IgA as excluding coeliac disease", "Refer for duodenal biopsies while she stays gluten-free", "Measure deamidated gliadin peptide IgG antibodies now", "Measure faecal calprotectin to look for intestinal inflammation", "Arrange HLA-DQ2/DQ8 genotyping from a blood sample"]'::jsonb,'E','CORRECT ANSWER

E. Arrange HLA-DQ2/DQ8 genotyping from a blood sample

---

Coeliac serology and duodenal histology depend on gluten being eaten. After four months gluten-free, a negative tTG-IgA (even with normal total IgA) does not exclude coeliac disease, and biopsies may already have healed. HLA-DQ2/DQ8 genotyping is not affected by diet. About 99% of people with coeliac disease carry HLA-DQ2 and/or DQ8, so a NEGATIVE result effectively excludes it and spares her a gluten challenge. A positive result does not diagnose coeliac disease (about 30–40% of the population carry these genes). If she is positive, she would need a gluten challenge (Coeliac Australia suggests at least 3–6 g gluten a day, about 4 slices of wheat bread, for at least 6 weeks) followed by serology and gastroscopy with duodenal biopsies. Her unexplained iron deficiency also needs investigating in its own right, and a gastroenterologist may recommend endoscopy regardless of the coeliac result.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
If a patient is already gluten-free, serology and biopsy are unreliable. HLA-DQ2/DQ8 testing can exclude coeliac disease if negative; if positive, a gluten challenge (about 4 slices of bread daily for at least 6 weeks) is needed before testing.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "Coeliac Australia: Diagnosis (health professional and patient information)", "url": "https://coeliac.org.au/learn/diagnosis/"}
• {"name": "Coeliac Australia: Gluten challenge (health professionals)", "url": "https://coeliac.org.au/health-professionals/gluten-challenge/"}

