EDITED BY HEISENBERG','difficult','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "bank170", "review_status": "owner_approved_2026_09_29", "tags": ["ZQ-0117", "zyntra_bank", "mock1", "level_4", "task_data_gathering", "group_adult_medicine", "sys_gastroenterology", "approved_by_owner_2026_09_29"], "clinician_task": "data_gathering", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"Coeliac Australia: Diagnosis (health professional and patient information)\", \"url\": \"https://coeliac.org.au/learn/diagnosis/\"}; {\"name\": \"Coeliac Australia: Gluten challenge (health professionals)\", \"url\": \"https://coeliac.org.au/health-professionals/gluten-challenge/\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "Serology is unreliable once gluten has been removed; antibodies fall within weeks to months. A negative result on a gluten-free diet cannot exclude coeliac disease.", "B": "Villous atrophy heals on a gluten-free diet, so biopsies taken now may be falsely normal. Diagnostic biopsies need a gluten-containing diet.", "C": "DGP-IgG is mainly useful in IgA deficiency or young children. Like tTG, it depends on gluten intake, so it is also unreliable after 4 months gluten-free.", "D": "Calprotectin detects colonic inflammation such as IBD. It is not a test for coeliac disease and would not settle the question."}, "key_takeaways": ["If a patient is already gluten-free, serology and biopsy are unreliable. HLA-DQ2/DQ8 testing can exclude coeliac disease if negative; if positive, a gluten challenge (about 4 slices of bread daily for at least 6 weeks) is needed before testing."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,1.0,1.0,'tier_prior','tier=4 (bank170)'),
('437aa513-b6f3-5c20-bd7b-d27c0a59d7eb'::uuid,'ZQ-0118','adult-medicine','gastroenterology','A 56-year-old man with alcohol-related cirrhosis is brought to the emergency department of North West Regional Hospital, Burnie, Tasmania, by his partner. For two days he has had vague generalised abdominal discomfort and has been ''not himself'', sleeping during the day and muddled at night. He stopped drinking alcohol 8 months ago. His medicines are spironolactone 100 mg daily, furosemide 40 mg daily and propranolol 10 mg twice daily. He has no vomiting, haematemesis or melaena, and his bowels opened yesterday.

His temperature is 37.9°C, pulse 96/min and blood pressure 108/64 mmHg. He is jaundiced, drowsy but rousable and has asterixis. The abdomen is distended with shifting dullness and mild diffuse tenderness, without guarding or rigidity.

Investigations:
- Haemoglobin 118 g/L; white cells 11.8 × 10^9/L; platelets 72 × 10^9/L
- INR 1.7
- Bilirubin 64 µmol/L; albumin 26 g/L
- Sodium 131 mmol/L; creatinine 118 µmol/L (baseline 85)
- Urinalysis: no leucocytes or nitrites
- Chest X-ray: no consolidation

Which one of the following is the most important next investigation?','["CT of the abdomen and pelvis with contrast", "Diagnostic paracentesis for cell count and culture", "Serum ammonia concentration", "Abdominal ultrasound with Doppler of the portal vein", "Upper gastrointestinal endoscopy within 12 hours"]'::jsonb,'B','CORRECT ANSWER

B. Diagnostic paracentesis for cell count and culture

---

Any patient with cirrhosis and ascites admitted to hospital, especially with fever, abdominal pain, encephalopathy or worsening kidney function, needs a diagnostic paracentesis to look for spontaneous bacterial peritonitis (SBP). SBP is often subtle and has high mortality. An ascitic neutrophil count above 250 cells/mm³ confirms SBP and should be treated straight away with an IV third-generation cephalosporin (for example ceftriaxone), without waiting for culture. Ascitic fluid should be inoculated into blood culture bottles at the bedside to improve culture yield. The procedure is safe despite his INR and platelet count; routine correction of clotting is not recommended. Because his creatinine has risen, IV albumin (1.5 g/kg on day 1 and 1 g/kg on day 3) reduces the risk of hepatorenal syndrome and death. SBP is a common precipitant of his hepatic encephalopathy, which also needs treatment (lactulose).

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Cirrhosis with ascites and fever, abdominal pain, encephalopathy or AKI: do a diagnostic paracentesis. An ascitic neutrophil count above 250/mm³ means SBP; treat with a third-generation cephalosporin and give IV albumin if renal function is impaired.

---

