EDITED BY HEISENBERG','moderate','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "bank170", "review_status": "owner_approved_2026_09_29", "tags": ["ZQ-0118", "zyntra_bank", "mock1", "level_3", "task_data_gathering", "group_adult_medicine", "sys_gastroenterology", "approved_by_owner_2026_09_29"], "clinician_task": "data_gathering", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"EASL Clinical Practice Guidelines on the management of patients with decompensated cirrhosis (J Hepatol 2018), PubMed\", \"url\": \"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/29653741/\"}; {\"name\": \"MSD Manual Professional: Spontaneous bacterial peritonitis\", \"url\": \"https://www.msdmanuals.com/professional/hepatic-and-biliary-disorders/approach-to-the-patient-with-liver-disease/spontaneous-bacterial-peritonitis-sbp\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "CT may be needed if secondary (surgical) peritonitis is suspected, for example from a very high ascitic neutrophil count, multiple organisms or peritonism. It is not the first test, and IV contrast is best avoided with his rising creatinine.", "C": "Hepatic encephalopathy is a clinical diagnosis. A high ammonia supports it but does not change management or find the precipitant. A normal ammonia makes the diagnosis less likely.", "D": "Portal vein thrombosis can cause decompensation, and Doppler ultrasound is reasonable later. It does not address the most dangerous treatable cause of fever and encephalopathy here.", "E": "There is no evidence of GI bleeding (no haematemesis or melaena, stable haemoglobin). Endoscopy is not the priority."}, "key_takeaways": ["Cirrhosis with ascites and fever, abdominal pain, encephalopathy or AKI: do a diagnostic paracentesis. An ascitic neutrophil count above 250/mm³ means SBP; treat with a third-generation cephalosporin and give IV albumin if renal function is impaired."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,0.0,1.0,'tier_prior','tier=3 (bank170)'),
('92d328f1-cbb1-5f10-8d16-0982feb05884'::uuid,'ZQ-0119','adult-medicine','haematology-oncology','A 66-year-old woman with seropositive rheumatoid arthritis sees her rheumatologist in Toowong, Brisbane, because of increasing tiredness and breathlessness climbing stairs. Her arthritis has flared over the past 3 months, with swelling of several small joints of both hands. She takes methotrexate 20 mg weekly, folic acid 5 mg weekly and naproxen 500 mg twice daily. Her periods stopped at 51. She has no overt GI bleeding and eats a mixed diet including red meat.

Investigations:
- Haemoglobin 98 g/L (115–160)
- MCV 77 fL (80–100); MCH 24 pg (27–32)
- Platelets 468 × 10^9/L (150–400)
- White cells 7.2 × 10^9/L
- Ferritin 85 µg/L (30–300)
- Serum iron 5 µmol/L (10–30)
- Transferrin 3.4 g/L (2.0–3.6)
- Transferrin saturation 9% (15–45)
- C-reactive protein 38 mg/L (<5)
- Blood film: hypochromic microcytic red cells with pencil cells

Which one of the following is the most likely interpretation of her anaemia?','["Anaemia of inflammation alone, with adequate iron stores", "Beta-thalassaemia trait unmasked by active arthritis", "Methotrexate-induced marrow suppression", "Iron deficiency, with ferritin raised by inflammation", "Sideroblastic anaemia related to methotrexate and folate antagonism"]'::jsonb,'D','CORRECT ANSWER

D. Iron deficiency, with ferritin raised by inflammation

---

Ferritin is an acute-phase reactant. During active inflammation (CRP 38) it rises and can hide iron deficiency. A ferritin below 30 µg/L confirms iron deficiency, but with inflammation a ferritin up to about 100 µg/L can still be consistent with it, especially with a transferrin saturation below 20%. Other features point to true iron deficiency rather than anaemia of inflammation alone: marked microcytosis and hypochromia with pencil cells, reactive thrombocytosis, and a high-normal transferrin (in anaemia of inflammation transferrin is typically LOW). The likely cause is occult GI blood loss from a long-term NSAID. She needs GI evaluation (gastroscopy and colonoscopy, given her age and the coexisting iron deficiency), review of the NSAID, and iron replacement (IV iron is often preferred when inflammation reduces oral iron absorption through hepcidin). Her arthritis control also needs attention.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Ferritin is an acute-phase protein: with inflammation, a ferritin up to about 100 µg/L plus transferrin saturation below 20% still suggests iron deficiency. Look for a source (for example NSAID-related GI loss).

---

