EDITED BY HEISENBERG','difficult','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "bank170", "review_status": "owner_approved_2026_09_29", "tags": ["ZQ-0119", "zyntra_bank", "mock1", "level_4", "task_data_interpretation_synthesis", "group_adult_medicine", "sys_haematology_oncology", "approved_by_owner_2026_09_29"], "clinician_task": "data_interpretation_synthesis", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"Zhang GD et al. Updating the diagnosis and management of iron deficiency in the era of routine ferritin testing of blood donors by Australian Red Cross Lifeblood (MJA 2024)\", \"url\": \"https://www.mja.com.au/journal/2024/221/7/updating-diagnosis-and-management-iron-deficiency-era-routine-ferritin-testing\"}; {\"name\": \"Australian Red Cross Lifeblood: Diagnosis and investigation of iron deficiency anaemia\", \"url\": \"https://www.lifeblood.com.au/health-professionals/clinical-practice/clinical-indications/iron-deficiency-anaemia/diagnosis-investigation\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "In pure anaemia of inflammation, transferrin is usually low or low-normal, microcytosis is mild, and ferritin is often well above 100. A transferrin saturation of 9% with a high-normal transferrin and pencil cells points to coexisting absolute iron deficiency.", "B": "Thalassaemia trait causes marked microcytosis with a NORMAL or high red cell count and normal iron studies, and it does not cause a low transferrin saturation. It is also not ''unmasked'' by inflammation.", "C": "Methotrexate toxicity causes macrocytosis or pancytopenia (especially with renal impairment or interacting drugs), not isolated microcytic anaemia with thrombocytosis.", "E": "Sideroblastic anaemia (for example from alcohol, isoniazid, copper deficiency or MDS) gives high ferritin and HIGH transferrin saturation with ring sideroblasts in the marrow. Her saturation is low."}, "key_takeaways": ["Ferritin is an acute-phase protein: with inflammation, a ferritin up to about 100 µg/L plus transferrin saturation below 20% still suggests iron deficiency. Look for a source (for example NSAID-related GI loss)."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,1.0,1.0,'tier_prior','tier=4 (bank170)'),
('b42a6f43-1df0-5587-8d7c-18f0205ac12a'::uuid,'ZQ-0121','adult-medicine','haematology-oncology','A 38-year-old woman presents to the emergency department of Fiona Stanley Hospital, Perth, with 3 days of headache, tiredness and bruising on her legs. This morning her partner noticed she was briefly muddled and could not find words for about 20 minutes. She has no diarrhoea. She takes no regular medicines apart from the combined oral contraceptive pill. She is not pregnant.

Her temperature is 37.6°C, pulse 104/min and blood pressure 132/84 mmHg. She has scattered petechiae on both shins. Neurological examination is now normal.

Investigations:
- Haemoglobin 78 g/L (115–160)
- Platelets 14 × 10^9/L (150–400)
- Reticulocytes 6% (0.5–2.5)
- Blood film: numerous schistocytes
- Lactate dehydrogenase 1650 U/L (120–250)
- Bilirubin 42 µmol/L (unconjugated)
- Haptoglobin undetectable
- Direct antiglobulin test negative
- INR 1.1; APTT 30 s; fibrinogen 3.2 g/L
- Creatinine 96 µmol/L (45–90)
- Urine β-hCG negative

Which one of the following is the most likely diagnosis and immediate treatment?','["Evans syndrome; start prednisolone and IV immunoglobulin", "Immune TTP; start urgent plasma exchange and corticosteroids", "Disseminated intravascular coagulation; give platelets and cryoprecipitate", "Atypical HUS; give eculizumab and avoid plasma therapy", "Immune thrombocytopenia; give a platelet transfusion and IV immunoglobulin"]'::jsonb,'B','CORRECT ANSWER

B. Immune TTP; start urgent plasma exchange and corticosteroids

---

She has microangiopathic haemolytic anaemia (schistocytes, raised LDH, undetectable haptoglobin, negative DAT) with severe thrombocytopenia, normal coagulation, fluctuating neurological signs, and only mild renal impairment. This is typical of thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura (TTP), a medical emergency that is fatal in most untreated cases. Her PLASMIC score is high (platelets below 30, haemolysis, no active cancer, no transplant, MCV and INR not raised, creatinine below 177 µmol/L). An ADAMTS13 activity sample should be collected before plasma is given, but treatment must not wait for the result: urgent haematology referral, therapeutic plasma exchange, and corticosteroids. Caplacizumab and rituximab are usually added for immune TTP (ISTH 2020). Platelet transfusion should be avoided unless there is life-threatening bleeding, because it may worsen thrombosis.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
MAHA (schistocytes, high LDH, DAT negative) + severe thrombocytopenia + normal coagulation ± neurological signs = TTP until proven otherwise. Take an ADAMTS13 sample, then start plasma exchange and steroids urgently; avoid platelet transfusion.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "Zheng XL et al. ISTH guidelines for treatment of thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura (J Thromb Haemost 2020), PubMed", "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/32914526/"}
• {"name": "Zheng XL et al. ISTH good practice statements for the clinical care of patients with TTP (J Thromb Haemost 2020), PubMed", "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/32914535/"}

---

EDITED BY HEISENBERG','difficult','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "bank170", "review_status": "owner_approved_2026_09_29", "tags": ["ZQ-0121", "zyntra_bank", "mock1", "level_4", "task_data_interpretation_synthesis", "group_adult_medicine", "sys_haematology_oncology", "approved_by_owner_2026_09_29"], "clinician_task": "data_interpretation_synthesis", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"Zheng XL et al. ISTH guidelines for treatment of thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura (J Thromb Haemost 2020), PubMed\", \"url\": \"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/32914526/\"}; {\"name\": \"Zheng XL et al. ISTH good practice statements for the clinical care of patients with TTP (J Thromb Haemost 2020), PubMed\", \"url\": \"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/32914535/\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "Evans syndrome (autoimmune haemolysis with ITP) is DAT POSITIVE and has spherocytes rather than schistocytes.", "C": "DIC causes a prolonged INR/APTT and low fibrinogen, usually with an obvious trigger such as sepsis. Her coagulation is normal.", "D": "Atypical HUS is dominated by acute kidney injury and is usually diagnosed after TTP is excluded (ADAMTS13 above 10%). Plasma exchange is the right first step while results are pending.", "E": "ITP does not cause haemolysis or schistocytes. Platelet transfusion in TTP may worsen microvascular thrombosis."}, "key_takeaways": ["MAHA (schistocytes, high LDH, DAT negative) + severe thrombocytopenia + normal coagulation ± neurological signs = TTP until proven otherwise. Take an ADAMTS13 sample, then start plasma exchange and steroids urgently; avoid platelet transfusion."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,1.0,1.0,'tier_prior','tier=4 (bank170)'),
('561d9d3a-a29a-594e-8a36-f666f368e72f'::uuid,'ZQ-0122','adult-medicine','haematology-oncology','A 79-year-old man sees his general practitioner in Orange, New South Wales, for a routine INR check. He takes warfarin for non-valvular atrial fibrillation (CHA2DS2-VA score 4: age, hypertension and coronary disease). His doctors chose warfarin over a DOAC because of severe chronic kidney disease (creatinine clearance about 22 mL/min). He also takes aspirin 100 mg daily, prescribed by his cardiologist after a coronary stent 8 months ago, plus metoprolol, atorvastatin and pantoprazole. Five days ago he started a course of roxithromycin for a chest infection. His last INR, two weeks ago, was 2.6.

Today''s point-of-care INR is 7.4, confirmed at 7.3 on a venous sample. He has no bleeding, bruising, black stools or blood in his urine, and has had no falls. Haemoglobin is 128 g/L and platelets 190 × 10^9/L. He has no planned procedures. His pulse is 76/min and irregular, blood pressure 134/78 mmHg.

Which one of the following is the most appropriate management?','["Withhold one warfarin dose, then restart at a lower dose and recheck the INR in a week", "Continue warfarin at a reduced dose and recheck the INR in 3 days", "Withhold warfarin and give vitamin K 5–10 mg intravenously", "Withhold warfarin and give four-factor prothrombin complex concentrate", "Withhold warfarin, give vitamin K 1–2 mg orally, and recheck the INR in 24 hours"]'::jsonb,'E','CORRECT ANSWER

E. Withhold warfarin, give vitamin K 1–2 mg orally, and recheck the INR in 24 hours

---

Under the Australasian warfarin reversal consensus (Tran et al., MJA 2013; reaffirmed for non-bleeding patients in the 2025 update), an INR of 4.5–10 without bleeding is managed by stopping warfarin and restarting at a lower dose once the INR is in range. Vitamin K is usually unnecessary. However, if the patient has a HIGH bleeding risk, vitamin K 1–2 mg orally (or 0.5–1 mg IV) should be given and the INR checked within 24 hours. The consensus lists concurrent antiplatelet therapy as a high-risk feature, along with major bleeding within 4 weeks, major surgery within 2 weeks, thrombocytopenia (platelets below 50) and liver disease. He is on aspirin, so vitamin K is recommended. Low-dose vitamin K lowers the INR without causing warfarin resistance, which matters for his high stroke risk. The trigger for the rise (a macrolide antibiotic that reduces warfarin metabolism) should be noted, and closer INR monitoring arranged until the course ends. The need for aspirin alongside anticoagulation more than 6 months after a stent is also worth raising with his cardiologist.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
INR 4.5–10 without bleeding: cease warfarin; add vitamin K 1–2 mg orally (INR within 24 hours) only if bleeding risk is high. High risk includes concurrent antiplatelet therapy, recent major bleed or surgery, platelets below 50 and liver disease.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "Tran HA et al. An update of consensus guidelines for warfarin reversal (MJA 2013)", "url": "https://www.mja.com.au/journal/2013/198/4/update-consensus-guidelines-warfarin-reversal"}
