---

EDITED BY HEISENBERG','difficult','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "bank170", "review_status": "owner_approved_2026_09_29", "tags": ["ZQ-0122", "zyntra_bank", "mock1", "level_5", "task_management", "group_adult_medicine", "sys_haematology_oncology", "approved_by_owner_2026_09_29"], "clinician_task": "management", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"Tran HA et al. An update of consensus guidelines for warfarin reversal (MJA 2013)\", \"url\": \"https://www.mja.com.au/journal/2013/198/4/update-consensus-guidelines-warfarin-reversal\"}; {\"name\": \"Updated recommendations for warfarin reversal in the setting of four-factor prothrombin complex concentrate (MJA 2025)\", \"url\": \"https://www.mja.com.au/journal/2025/222/1/updated-recommendations-warfarin-reversal-setting-four-factor-prothrombin\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "This would suit an INR of 4.5–10 WITHOUT high bleeding risk (the consensus advises withholding warfarin until back in range). His concurrent aspirin puts him in the high-risk group, where low-dose vitamin K and an INR check within 24 hours are advised.", "B": "Dose reduction alone is appropriate for an INR above range but below 4.5 without bleeding. It is inadequate at an INR of 7.4 with an antiplatelet.", "C": "High-dose IV vitamin K (5–10 mg) is for clinically significant bleeding. In a non-bleeding patient it over-corrects and can cause warfarin resistance for days, leaving him unprotected against stroke.", "D": "The 2025 MJA update suggests 4F-PCC outside surgery only for an INR above 10 in high-risk patients, or for bleeding. It is not indicated at an INR of 7.4 without bleeding."}, "key_takeaways": ["INR 4.5–10 without bleeding: cease warfarin; add vitamin K 1–2 mg orally (INR within 24 hours) only if bleeding risk is high. High risk includes concurrent antiplatelet therapy, recent major bleed or surgery, platelets below 50 and liver disease."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,2.0,1.0,'tier_prior','tier=5 (bank170)'),
('92a3183c-d85d-5ccf-8aae-eeda155077ac'::uuid,'ZQ-0123','adult-medicine','haematology-oncology','A 74-year-old man with metastatic castration-sensitive prostate cancer, known to have bone metastases, presents to the emergency department of Wagga Wagga Base Hospital, New South Wales, at 2100 hours. For 3 weeks he has had worsening mid-back pain that is worse lying flat and keeps him awake. Over the last 2 days his legs have felt heavy and ''wobbly'', and today he stumbled twice. He has had no incontinence but has noticed some hesitancy passing urine. He is on androgen deprivation therapy with goserelin. He has no diabetes or history of peptic ulcer.

Examination shows tenderness over the T9–T10 spinous processes. Power is 4/5 in hip flexion bilaterally, with brisk knee reflexes and upgoing plantars. He has reduced pinprick sensation below the umbilicus. Anal tone is normal. Post-void bladder scan shows 150 mL. Oral morphine has been given for pain.

Which one of the following is the most appropriate immediate management?','["Give dexamethasone 16 mg now and arrange urgent whole-spine MRI", "Arrange thoracic spine X-ray and bone scan in the morning", "Give dexamethasone and arrange outpatient spinal radiotherapy", "Arrange CT of the thoracic spine and refer to physiotherapy", "Arrange MRI of the thoracic spine within the next week"]'::jsonb,'A','CORRECT ANSWER

A. Give dexamethasone 16 mg now and arrange urgent whole-spine MRI

---

Back pain in a patient with known bone metastases, plus progressive leg weakness, upper motor neuron signs and a sensory level around T10, is metastatic spinal cord compression (MSCC) until proven otherwise. It is an oncological emergency: neurological function at the time of treatment is the strongest predictor of whether he will walk again. eviQ recommends dexamethasone 16 mg (orally or IV) as soon as MSCC is suspected, with PPI cover, and an MRI of the WHOLE spine urgently (ideally within 24 hours, sooner with neurological deficit). The whole spine is imaged because compression is often multilevel. He should be nursed flat (spinal precautions) if there is pain or instability, and discussed urgently with radiation oncology and spinal surgery. Definitive treatment is radiotherapy and/or surgical decompression.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Suspected MSCC (known cancer + back pain + neurological signs): dexamethasone 16 mg immediately, urgent whole-spine MRI within 24 hours, and early oncology/spinal referral.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "eviQ: Spinal cord compression \u2013 palliative EBRT (protocol 296)", "url": "https://www.eviq.org.au/radiation-oncology/palliative/296-spinal-cord-compression-palliative-ebrt"}
• {"name": "NICE NG234: Spinal metastases and metastatic spinal cord compression (2023)", "url": "https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/ng234"}

---

EDITED BY HEISENBERG','moderate','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "bank170", "review_status": "owner_approved_2026_09_29", "tags": ["ZQ-0123", "zyntra_bank", "mock1", "level_3", "task_management", "group_adult_medicine", "sys_haematology_oncology", "approved_by_owner_2026_09_29"], "clinician_task": "management", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"eviQ: Spinal cord compression \\u2013 palliative EBRT (protocol 296)\", \"url\": \"https://www.eviq.org.au/radiation-oncology/palliative/296-spinal-cord-compression-palliative-ebrt\"}; {\"name\": \"NICE NG234: Spinal metastases and metastatic spinal cord compression (2023)\", \"url\": \"https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/ng234\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"B": "Plain X-ray and bone scan cannot show cord compression; delaying until morning risks irreversible paraplegia.", "C": "Steroids are right, but outpatient care is not. He needs urgent MRI to define the level and extent, and inpatient decision-making about radiotherapy or surgery.", "D": "CT is less sensitive than MRI for cord and soft-tissue compression and is used only if MRI is contraindicated. Physiotherapy does not address the emergency.", "E": "Imaging only the thoracic spine misses other levels, which are common, and a week''s delay is unsafe with evolving neurological deficit."}, "key_takeaways": ["Suspected MSCC (known cancer + back pain + neurological signs): dexamethasone 16 mg immediately, urgent whole-spine MRI within 24 hours, and early oncology/spinal referral."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,0.0,1.0,'tier_prior','tier=3 (bank170)'),
('69b27cab-6bc0-5d39-b3ec-6e3b6f829ae2'::uuid,'ZQ-0124','adult-medicine','haematology-oncology','A 58-year-old woman with stage IV melanoma presents to the emergency department of a Melbourne metropolitan hospital. She had her second cycle of combination ipilimumab and nivolumab 3 weeks ago. For 4 days she has had watery diarrhoea, now 8–9 times a day (her usual is once daily), with crampy lower abdominal pain and some nocturnal stools. There is no blood in the stool. She has not travelled, and nobody at home is unwell. She took loperamide at home without benefit.

Her temperature is 37.4°C, pulse 102/min and blood pressure 112/70 mmHg. The abdomen is soft with mild diffuse tenderness and no peritonism.

Investigations:
- White cells 10.2 × 10^9/L; haemoglobin 128 g/L
- C-reactive protein 64 mg/L
- Potassium 3.2 mmol/L; creatinine 88 µmol/L
- Stool: multiplex PCR and C. difficile toxin sent
- Abdominal X-ray: no colonic dilatation

Which one of the following is the most appropriate management?','["Continue immunotherapy, advise oral fluids and increase the loperamide dose", "Withhold immunotherapy and start oral budesonide 9 mg daily as an outpatient", "Start infliximab 5 mg/kg IV today, before any corticosteroid", "Withhold immunotherapy, admit and give IV methylprednisolone 1–2 mg/kg daily", "Start empirical oral metronidazole and review when stool results return"]'::jsonb,'D','CORRECT ANSWER

D. Withhold immunotherapy, admit and give IV methylprednisolone 1–2 mg/kg daily

---

Diarrhoea of 7 or more stools a day above baseline, with abdominal pain and interference with daily life, is grade 3 immune-related colitis (CTCAE). It is common with combined anti-CTLA-4 and anti-PD-1 therapy. eviQ guidance for grade 3–4 colitis: withhold the checkpoint inhibitor, admit, give IV fluids and electrolyte replacement, and start IV methylprednisolone 1–2 mg/kg/day (after sending stool tests for infection, but without waiting for results). Early gastroenterology input and flexible sigmoidoscopy are advised. If there is no improvement after about 48–72 hours of IV steroids, add infliximab 5 mg/kg (or vedolizumab). Steroids are tapered slowly over at least 4–6 weeks once symptoms improve. Antidiarrhoeals such as loperamide can mask worsening colitis and raise the risk of toxic megacolon, so they are avoided in moderate to severe ICI colitis.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
ICI colitis grade 3 (7 or more stools/day above baseline): withhold the ICI, admit, IV methylprednisolone 1–2 mg/kg/day; infliximab if no response in 48–72 hours. Avoid loperamide.

---

REFERENCES
