EDITED BY HEISENBERG','difficult','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "bank170", "review_status": "owner_approved_2026_09_29", "tags": ["ZQ-0124", "zyntra_bank", "mock1", "level_4", "task_management", "group_adult_medicine", "sys_haematology_oncology", "approved_by_owner_2026_09_29"], "clinician_task": "management", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"eviQ: Management of immune-related adverse events (irAEs)\", \"url\": \"https://www.eviq.org.au/clinical-resources/side-effect-and-toxicity-management/immunological/1993-management-of-immune-related-adverse-events\"}; {\"name\": \"Schneider BJ et al. Management of immune-related adverse events in patients treated with ICI therapy: ASCO guideline update (J Clin Oncol 2021), PubMed\", \"url\": \"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/34724392/\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "Continuing the checkpoint inhibitor and escalating loperamide is only acceptable for grade 1 diarrhoea. At grade 3, treatment must be stopped and loperamide may mask deterioration.", "B": "Budesonide may have a role in mild colitis, but grade 3 disease with tachycardia and a high CRP needs admission and IV corticosteroids.", "C": "Infliximab is second-line, used for steroid-refractory colitis (no response after 48–72 hours) or sometimes early in selected severe cases. It is not given instead of corticosteroids.", "E": "Infection should be excluded with stool tests, but empirical metronidazole with delayed review risks progression to perforation. Immune colitis is far more likely after ipilimumab plus nivolumab."}, "key_takeaways": ["ICI colitis grade 3 (7 or more stools/day above baseline): withhold the ICI, admit, IV methylprednisolone 1–2 mg/kg/day; infliximab if no response in 48–72 hours. Avoid loperamide."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,1.0,1.0,'tier_prior','tier=4 (bank170)'),
('d937e25c-b922-51f8-9389-a66595a7e2cc'::uuid,'ZQ-0125','adult-medicine','neurology','A 34-year-old accountant sees his general practitioner in Devonport, Tasmania, 10 days after an emergency department visit. At home, his wife saw him have a generalised tonic-clonic seizure lasting 2 minutes, followed by 20 minutes of confusion. He had slept normally the night before, drinks 2–3 standard drinks a week, uses no recreational drugs and takes no medicines. He has had no previous seizures, head injury or central nervous system infection. There is no family history of epilepsy.

In the emergency department his blood glucose, electrolytes, calcium and magnesium were normal. He has since had an MRI brain and a sleep-deprived EEG, both normal. The neurologist has not started antiseizure medication. He holds a private (car) licence only and drives to work daily. He asks when he can drive again.

Which one of the following is the most appropriate advice about driving?','["He may drive now, because his MRI and EEG are both normal", "He may drive after a 4-week stand-down if he remains well", "He should not drive until at least 6 months seizure-free", "He should not drive until at least 12 months seizure-free", "He should not drive until 5 years seizure-free, off medication"]'::jsonb,'C','CORRECT ANSWER

C. He should not drive until at least 6 months seizure-free

---

Under the Austroads ''Assessing Fitness to Drive'' standards, a person with a first unprovoked seizure should not drive a private vehicle for at least 6 months. A conditional licence may then be considered by the licensing authority on the advice of the treating doctor, provided there has been no further seizure. Normal investigations do not shorten this non-driving period for private drivers. Commercial drivers face much longer restrictions (generally at least 5 years seizure-free for a first unprovoked seizure, subject to specialist review). The doctor should document the advice. The legal responsibility to notify the driver licensing authority rests with the driver, but the doctor may report in the interests of public safety if the patient continues to drive against advice (in South Australia and the Northern Territory, health professionals have mandatory reporting obligations). Other safety advice includes supervision when swimming or bathing and care with heights and machinery.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Austroads: first unprovoked seizure means no private driving for at least 6 months seizure-free (conditional licence possible), and much longer (about 5 years) for commercial drivers. The patient is responsible for notifying the licensing authority.

---

