EDITED BY HEISENBERG','difficult','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "bank170", "review_status": "owner_approved_2026_09_29", "tags": ["ZQ-0127", "zyntra_bank", "mock1", "level_5", "task_data_gathering", "group_adult_medicine", "sys_neurology", "approved_by_owner_2026_09_29"], "clinician_task": "data_gathering", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"NSW Agency for Clinical Innovation (ECI): Subarachnoid haemorrhage \\u2013 clinical tool\", \"url\": \"https://aci.health.nsw.gov.au/networks/eci/clinical/tools/neurology/headache/subarachnoid-heamorrhage\"}; {\"name\": \"Perry JJ et al. Sensitivity of computed tomography performed within six hours of onset of headache for diagnosis of subarachnoid haemorrhage (BMJ 2011), PubMed\", \"url\": \"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/21768192/\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "The rule-out applies to CT within 6 hours of onset, not 12. At 10 hours, SAH is not excluded by a normal CT.", "C": "An LP before 12 hours may be falsely negative for xanthochromia, and red cells alone cannot distinguish SAH from a traumatic tap.", "D": "A delayed outpatient scan risks missing a sentinel bleed. Aneurysmal rebleeding carries high mortality and usually occurs early.", "E": "Repeat non-contrast CT becomes LESS sensitive with time as blood clears; it adds nothing here."}, "key_takeaways": ["Thunderclap headache: CT within 6 hours (neurologically intact, experienced reader) can rule out SAH. After 6 hours, a normal CT needs an LP at least 12 hours after onset for xanthochromia (or CTA as per local pathway)."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,2.0,1.0,'tier_prior','tier=5 (bank170)'),
('9706f947-a2ba-519b-9b36-7d6b6c58095b'::uuid,'ZQ-0128','adult-medicine','infectious-diseases','A 31-year-old man who has sex with men attends a sexual health clinic in Darlinghurst, Sydney, for his routine 3-monthly HIV pre-exposure prophylaxis (PrEP) review. He is well and has no rash, genital or anal ulcers, or neurological, visual or hearing symptoms. Two years ago he was treated for secondary syphilis with benzathine benzylpenicillin. Since then his RPR has fallen and stayed at 1:2 on 5 consecutive checks, most recently 3 months ago. He has had 4 new male partners in the past 3 months, with inconsistent condom use.

Examination, including the genitals, anus, mouth and skin, is normal.

Investigations today:
- Syphilis EIA (treponemal): reactive (as before)
- TPPA: reactive (as before)
- RPR: 1:32
- HIV antigen/antibody: negative
- Pharyngeal, rectal and urine gonorrhoea/chlamydia NAAT: pending

Which one of the following is the most appropriate interpretation and management?','["Reinfection; benzathine benzylpenicillin 2.4 million units IM, single dose", "Serofast state after treatment; no treatment, repeat serology in 3 months", "Likely false-positive RPR; repeat the RPR in 4 weeks before any treatment", "Tertiary syphilis; benzathine benzylpenicillin weekly for 3 weeks", "Neurosyphilis until proven otherwise; admit for IV benzylpenicillin"]'::jsonb,'A','CORRECT ANSWER

A. Reinfection; benzathine benzylpenicillin 2.4 million units IM, single dose

---

Treponemal tests (EIA, TPPA) usually stay reactive for life after syphilis, so they cannot distinguish past from new infection. The non-treponemal RPR titre is used to monitor treatment and detect reinfection. A sustained fourfold (two-dilution) or greater rise in RPR indicates reinfection or treatment failure. His RPR has risen from a stable 1:2 (including 3 months ago) to 1:32, a 16-fold rise, in the setting of ongoing risk. He has no signs, so this is early latent syphilis: a new infection acquired within the last 2 years (in fact within 3 months). The Australian STI Management Guidelines recommend benzathine benzylpenicillin 2.4 million units IM as a single dose for early syphilis (primary, secondary or early latent). Syphilis is notifiable, partner notification is needed for the past 3 months (plus symptom duration), and the RPR should be followed to confirm a fourfold fall. No neurological, ocular or otic symptoms means an LP is not routinely needed.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Treponemal tests stay positive for life; follow the RPR. A fourfold or greater rise after previous treatment means reinfection. Early (less than 2 years) syphilis: benzathine benzylpenicillin 2.4 MU IM once; late latent: weekly for 3 weeks.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "Australian STI Management Guidelines for Use in Primary Care (ASHM): Syphilis", "url": "https://sti.guidelines.org.au/sexually-transmissible-infections/syphilis/"}
• {"name": "NSW Health: Syphilis control guideline / fact sheet", "url": "https://www.health.nsw.gov.au/Infectious/factsheets/Pages/syphilis.aspx"}

---

EDITED BY HEISENBERG','difficult','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "bank170", "review_status": "owner_approved_2026_09_29", "tags": ["ZQ-0128", "zyntra_bank", "mock1", "level_4", "task_data_interpretation_synthesis", "group_adult_medicine", "sys_infectious_disease", "approved_by_owner_2026_09_29"], "clinician_task": "data_interpretation_synthesis", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"Australian STI Management Guidelines for Use in Primary Care (ASHM): Syphilis\", \"url\": \"https://sti.guidelines.org.au/sexually-transmissible-infections/syphilis/\"}; {\"name\": \"NSW Health: Syphilis control guideline / fact sheet\", \"url\": \"https://www.health.nsw.gov.au/Infectious/factsheets/Pages/syphilis.aspx\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"B": "A serofast state is a LOW, stable titre after treatment, which is what he had until now. A jump to 1:32 is not serofast.", "C": "False-positive RPRs are usually low titre with NEGATIVE treponemal tests. Delaying treatment of infectious syphilis risks onward transmission.", "D": "Weekly doses for 3 weeks are used for late latent syphilis (over 2 years or unknown duration). His infection is clearly recent, and he has no features of tertiary syphilis.", "E": "IV benzylpenicillin is for neurosyphilis or ocular/otic syphilis. He has no neurological, visual or hearing symptoms."}, "key_takeaways": ["Treponemal tests stay positive for life; follow the RPR. A fourfold or greater rise after previous treatment means reinfection. Early (less than 2 years) syphilis: benzathine benzylpenicillin 2.4 MU IM once; late latent: weekly for 3 weeks."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,1.0,1.0,'tier_prior','tier=4 (bank170)'),
('ba4a8fed-a20d-5083-9dbe-53f84fbc6d54'::uuid,'ZQ-0129','adult-medicine','infectious-diseases','A 27-year-old man presents to a general practice in Maryborough, Queensland, with 6 days of high fevers, sweats, severe frontal headache and aching muscles. For 2 days he has had a dry cough and mild breathlessness. He moved from New Zealand 5 months ago and works on the slaughter floor of a local abattoir processing cattle and goats. He has had no vaccines since arriving. He has not waded in floodwater or kept birds, has not travelled overseas recently, and drinks only pasteurised milk.

His temperature is 39.2°C, pulse 96/min, blood pressure 118/72 mmHg, respiratory rate 20/min and oxygen saturation 95% on room air. He has fine crackles at the left base, mild tender hepatomegaly, no rash, no conjunctival suffusion and no lymphadenopathy.

Investigations:
- White cells 6.8 × 10^9/L; platelets 96 × 10^9/L
- ALT 186 U/L; ALP 160 U/L; bilirubin 18 µmol/L
- Creatinine 84 µmol/L
- Chest X-ray: patchy left lower lobe consolidation

Which one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?','["Leptospirosis", "Brucellosis", "Psittacosis", "Q fever", "Legionnaires'' disease"]'::jsonb,'D','CORRECT ANSWER

D. Q fever

---

Q fever is caused by Coxiella burnetii, spread by inhaling contaminated dust or aerosols from cattle, sheep and goats (especially birth products). Abattoir workers are a classic high-risk group. His recent arrival from New Zealand, where Q fever is essentially absent, means he is likely to be non-immune and unvaccinated. Acute Q fever typically causes a flu-like illness with high fever, marked headache and myalgia, often with atypical pneumonia and/or granulomatous hepatitis, and thrombocytopenia. Diagnosis is by serology (phase II antibodies, repeated at 2–4 weeks) and PCR on early blood. Treatment should start on clinical suspicion: doxycycline 100 mg orally twice daily for 14 days. It is notifiable in Queensland. He should be followed up for chronic Q fever (especially endocarditis in people with valve disease) and post-Q fever fatigue syndrome. Abattoir co-workers need pre-vaccination screening (skin test and serology) and vaccination with Q-VAX.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Abattoir or farm worker (unvaccinated, often recently arrived) with fever, severe headache, atypical pneumonia, hepatitis and thrombocytopenia = acute Q fever. Treat with doxycycline 100 mg bd for 14 days; it is notifiable, and co-workers need screening and vaccination.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "Queensland Health: Q fever (disease control)", "url": "https://www.health.qld.gov.au/disease-control/conditions/q-fever"}
• {"name": "CDNA National Guidelines for Public Health Units: Q fever", "url": "https://www.cdc.gov.au/sites/default/files/2025-11/q-fever-cdna-national-guidelines-for-public-health-units_0.pdf"}
• {"name": "Australian Immunisation Handbook: Q fever", "url": "https://immunisationhandbook.health.gov.au/contents/vaccine-preventable-diseases/q-fever"}

---

