vaccination."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,1.0,1.0,'tier_prior','tier=4 (bank170)'),
('b8b62310-3fb9-50e3-8687-8733ba81c5c4'::uuid,'ZQ-0130','adult-medicine','infectious-diseases','A 63-year-old man is admitted to Geraldton Regional Hospital, Western Australia, with fever and rigors. He has type 2 diabetes and had an infected blister on his left heel 2 weeks ago. Two sets of blood cultures taken on admission, 48 hours ago, grew methicillin-susceptible Staphylococcus aureus (MSSA). He was started on IV flucloxacillin 2 g every 6 hours, and he is now afebrile.

He has no prosthetic valve, pacemaker or intravascular device, no previous endocarditis and no history of injecting drug use. Examination shows a healing heel ulcer with mild surrounding erythema, no heart murmur, and no embolic or vasculitic skin lesions. His ECG is normal (PR interval 160 ms).

Repeat blood cultures were collected this morning. X-ray of the heel shows no bone changes. The infectious diseases team is consulted by phone from Perth.

Which one of the following is the most appropriate next investigation?','["Transoesophageal echocardiography as the first cardiac imaging test", "Serum C-reactive protein repeated daily until normal", "Transthoracic echocardiography during this admission", "Whole-body PET-CT to look for a metastatic focus", "MRI of the lumbar spine to exclude discitis"]'::jsonb,'C','CORRECT ANSWER

C. Transthoracic echocardiography during this admission

---

Every patient with S. aureus bacteraemia (SAB) needs echocardiography, because infective endocarditis is common and changes the duration and route of therapy. Australian guidance (for example the WA Therapeutic Advisory Group and Hunter New England SAB guidelines) recommends transthoracic echocardiography (TTE) for all patients, with TOE when the TTE is non-diagnostic or abnormal or the risk is high. High-risk features include a prosthetic valve or cardiac device, previous endocarditis, injecting drug use, persistent bacteraemia (positive cultures after 72 hours of treatment), a new murmur, embolic phenomena, or a new conduction abnormality. He has none of these, and his repeat cultures are already collected. Other key SAB bundle elements are: a source search (the heel), infectious diseases consultation, IV flucloxacillin (or cefazolin), and at least 14 days of IV therapy for uncomplicated SAB from the first negative culture, longer if complicated.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
All S. aureus bacteraemia: repeat blood cultures, ID consult, source control and echocardiography. TTE for all; TOE if high risk (prosthetic valve/device, IVDU, persistent bacteraemia, emboli) or if the TTE is non-diagnostic.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "Hunter New England LHD: Staphylococcus aureus bacteraemia management guideline (2023)", "url": "https://aimed.net.au/wp-content/uploads/2024/02/hnelhd_cg_23_44_staphylococcus_aureus_bacteraemia_mgmt.pdf"}
• {"name": "WA Therapeutic Advisory Group: Staphylococcus aureus bacteraemia guideline", "url": "https://www.healthywa.wa.gov.au/~/media/Files/Corporate/general%20documents/WATAG/Staph-Aureus-Bacteraemia-guideline.pdf"}

---

EDITED BY HEISENBERG','difficult','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "bank170", "review_status": "owner_approved_2026_09_29", "tags": ["ZQ-0130", "zyntra_bank", "mock1", "level_4", "task_data_gathering", "group_adult_medicine", "sys_infectious_disease", "approved_by_owner_2026_09_29"], "clinician_task": "data_gathering", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"Hunter New England LHD: Staphylococcus aureus bacteraemia management guideline (2023)\", \"url\": \"https://aimed.net.au/wp-content/uploads/2024/02/hnelhd_cg_23_44_staphylococcus_aureus_bacteraemia_mgmt.pdf\"}; {\"name\": \"WA Therapeutic Advisory Group: Staphylococcus aureus bacteraemia guideline\", \"url\": \"https://www.healthywa.wa.gov.au/~/media/Files/Corporate/general%20documents/WATAG/Staph-Aureus-Bacteraemia-guideline.pdf\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "TOE is more sensitive, but local guidance reserves first-line TOE for high-risk patients (prosthetic valve, device, previous IE, IVDU, persistent bacteraemia, embolic signs), none of which he has. TTE comes first; TOE follows if it is inconclusive or abnormal.", "B": "CRP trends support monitoring but do not exclude endocarditis or change the need for echocardiography.", "D": "PET-CT may help find metastatic foci in persistent or complicated SAB. It is not first-line in a patient who is improving.", "E": "He has no back pain or neurological symptoms to suggest spinal infection."}, "key_takeaways": ["All S. aureus bacteraemia: repeat blood cultures, ID consult, source control and echocardiography. TTE for all; TOE if high risk (prosthetic valve/device, IVDU, persistent bacteraemia, emboli) or if the TTE is non-diagnostic."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,1.0,1.0,'tier_prior','tier=4 (bank170)'),
('d1469bae-97fe-5873-a4ca-5d53f8bdc60d'::uuid,'ZQ-0131','adult-medicine','infectious-diseases','A 26-year-old man presents to the emergency department of a Melbourne hospital at 1400 hours on a Sunday. About 30 hours ago he had receptive anal intercourse without a condom with a casual male partner he met through a dating app. He does not know the partner''s HIV status and cannot contact him. He is not on PrEP. He had a negative HIV test 5 months ago. He has no symptoms. He takes no medicines and has no known kidney or liver disease or hepatitis B.

Examination is normal. Rapid point-of-care HIV testing is not available; blood is taken for HIV antigen/antibody, hepatitis B and C serology, syphilis serology, creatinine and liver function tests. Urine, pharyngeal and rectal swabs are taken for gonorrhoea and chlamydia.

Which one of the following is the most appropriate management?','["Start tenofovir DF/emtricitabine plus dolutegravir for 28 days", "Start PrEP daily and continue indefinitely, without a PEP course", "Start tenofovir DF/emtricitabine once daily for 28 days", "Await the HIV result and start PEP if the test is negative", "No PEP; the risk is too low with a partner of unknown status"]'::jsonb,'C','CORRECT ANSWER

C. Start tenofovir DF/emtricitabine once daily for 28 days

---

He had receptive anal intercourse, the highest-risk sexual exposure, with a partner from a population with higher HIV prevalence (a man who has sex with men) whose status is unknown. He presented well within the 72-hour window. The ASHM Australian PEP guidelines recommend PEP in this situation, and for an unknown-status source a 2-drug regimen (tenofovir disoproxil 300 mg/emtricitabine 200 mg once daily) is recommended. A 3-drug regimen (adding dolutegravir 50 mg daily) is reserved for exposures where the source is KNOWN to have HIV with a detectable or unknown viral load, or where resistance is suspected. PEP should be started as soon as possible, ideally within 2 hours of assessment, without waiting for the baseline HIV result. It is taken for 28 days, with follow-up HIV testing at about 6 and 12 weeks. This is also a good opportunity to discuss transitioning to PrEP (''PEP-in-PrEP'') after completing the course if the HIV test at the end is negative.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
ASHM PEP: receptive anal sex with an unknown-status MSM partner within 72 hours needs 2-drug PEP (TDF/FTC daily for 28 days). Three drugs only if the source is known HIV-positive with a detectable or unknown viral load. Start without waiting for results.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "ASHM: National PEP Guidelines \u2013 When to prescribe PEP", "url": "https://hiv.guidelines.org.au/pep-guidelines/when-to-prescribe-pep/"}
