---

EDITED BY HEISENBERG','difficult','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "bank170", "review_status": "owner_approved_2026_09_29", "tags": ["ZQ-0131", "zyntra_bank", "mock1", "level_5", "task_management", "group_adult_medicine", "sys_infectious_disease", "approved_by_owner_2026_09_29"], "clinician_task": "management", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"ASHM: National PEP Guidelines \\u2013 When to prescribe PEP\", \"url\": \"https://hiv.guidelines.org.au/pep-guidelines/when-to-prescribe-pep/\"}; {\"name\": \"ASHM: Post-Exposure Prophylaxis after Non-Occupational and Occupational Exposure to HIV \\u2013 National Guidelines (2025, PDF)\", \"url\": \"https://hiv.guidelines.org.au/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/ASHM-PEP-guidelines-2025_FA_20251024.pdf\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "A 3-drug regimen is for a source known to have HIV with a detectable or unknown viral load, or suspected resistance. For an unknown-status partner, ASHM recommends 2 drugs; adding dolutegravir adds cost and side effects without clear benefit.", "B": "PrEP taken now does not treat an exposure 30 hours ago in the way a full PEP course does. The usual approach is a 28-day PEP course, then transition to PrEP.", "D": "Delaying PEP until results return wastes the time-critical window; PEP works best when started early. A baseline test is taken, but PEP starts immediately.", "E": "Receptive anal intercourse with an unknown-status MSM partner meets the ASHM threshold for recommending PEP."}, "key_takeaways": ["ASHM PEP: receptive anal sex with an unknown-status MSM partner within 72 hours needs 2-drug PEP (TDF/FTC daily for 28 days). Three drugs only if the source is known HIV-positive with a detectable or unknown viral load. Start without waiting for results."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,2.0,1.0,'tier_prior','tier=5 (bank170)'),
('0b7c7e5d-a7fc-5555-a632-dded735c9c33'::uuid,'ZQ-0132','adult-medicine','infectious-diseases','A 48-year-old market gardener presents to a general practice in Bunbury, Western Australia, 6 hours after stepping on a rusty garden fork. The tines went through his work boot, leaving a deep puncture wound in the sole of his right foot contaminated with soil and manure. He has HIV infection, diagnosed 9 years ago, and takes bictegravir/emtricitabine/tenofovir alafenamide. His viral load is undetectable and his CD4 count 3 months ago was 610 cells/µL.

His records show he completed a primary course of tetanus-containing vaccine as a child and had a booster (dTpa) 7 years ago. He has no allergies. The wound is irrigated and debrided and is not showing signs of infection.

In addition to wound care, which one of the following is the most appropriate tetanus prophylaxis?','["dTpa booster alone, as his last dose was over 5 years ago", "Oral metronidazole for 7 days instead of vaccine or immunoglobulin", "No vaccine or immunoglobulin, as his last dose was within 10 years", "Tetanus immunoglobulin 500 IU IM, without vaccine", "dTpa booster and tetanus immunoglobulin 250 IU IM"]'::jsonb,'E','CORRECT ANSWER

E. dTpa booster and tetanus immunoglobulin 250 IU IM

---

A deep puncture wound contaminated with soil and manure is tetanus-prone. According to the Australian Immunisation Handbook, a tetanus-prone wound in a person with at least 3 previous doses needs a tetanus-containing vaccine booster if the last dose was more than 5 years ago. He received his 7 years ago, so a booster is needed (dTpa or ADT). Tetanus immunoglobulin (TIG) is also recommended for tetanus-prone wounds in people with humoral immune deficiency, including HIV infection, regardless of the time since the last dose, because they may not mount an adequate antibody response. This applies even if HIV is well controlled. The TIG dose is 250 IU IM if given within 24 hours of injury (500 IU if more than 24 hours), given at a different site from the vaccine with a separate syringe. Antibiotics are used only for clinical wound infection, not to prevent tetanus.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Tetanus-prone wound: booster if last dose more than 5 years ago (3 or more prior doses). Add TIG (250 IU if within 24 hours, 500 IU if later) for fewer than 3 doses or unknown history, OR for any HIV/humoral immunodeficiency, regardless of time since last dose.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "Australian Immunisation Handbook: Guide to tetanus prophylaxis in wound management (table)", "url": "https://immunisationhandbook.health.gov.au/resources/tables/table-guide-to-tetanus-prophylaxis-in-wound-management"}
• {"name": "Australian Immunisation Handbook: Tetanus", "url": "https://immunisationhandbook.health.gov.au/contents/vaccine-preventable-diseases/tetanus"}

---

