('010ced59-c13d-541f-812c-343e48a0465d'::uuid,'ZQ-0133','adult-medicine','rheumatology','A 74-year-old woman sees her general practitioner in Coffs Harbour, New South Wales, on a Friday afternoon. For 3 weeks she has had a new, persistent right-sided temporal headache, scalp tenderness when brushing her hair, and aching and stiffness of her shoulders in the mornings. Over the past week her jaw has ached after chewing for a few minutes. She has lost 3 kg. She has no visual disturbance, double vision or transient loss of vision. She has type 2 diabetes managed with metformin, and osteopenia.

Her temperature is 37.5°C and blood pressure 138/82 mmHg. The right temporal artery is thickened and tender, with a reduced pulse. Visual acuity, visual fields and fundoscopy are normal. The shoulders are tender with painful movement.

Point-of-care and same-day laboratory results:
- C-reactive protein 86 mg/L
- ESR 78 mm/h
- Haemoglobin 112 g/L; platelets 486 × 10^9/L
- Blood glucose 8.4 mmol/L

Which one of the following is the most appropriate management?','["Arrange temporal artery biopsy first, then start prednisolone if giant cell arteritis is confirmed", "Start prednisolone 40–60 mg today, then arrange urgent temporal artery ultrasound or biopsy", "Start prednisolone 15 mg daily for likely polymyalgia rheumatica and review in a week", "Start IV methylprednisolone 1 g daily for 3 days, then arrange urgent temporal artery biopsy", "Arrange urgent MRI brain and a neurology review for a new-onset headache in an older adult"]'::jsonb,'B','CORRECT ANSWER

B. Start prednisolone 40–60 mg today, then arrange urgent temporal artery ultrasound or biopsy

---

She has typical features of giant cell arteritis (GCA): age over 50, new temporal headache, scalp tenderness, jaw claudication, an abnormal temporal artery, polymyalgic symptoms and raised inflammatory markers. Untreated GCA can cause sudden, permanent blindness. Guidelines (for example BSR 2020) advise starting high-dose glucocorticoids IMMEDIATELY on clinical suspicion, without waiting for confirmation: prednisolone 40–60 mg daily for GCA without visual or ischaemic symptoms. She should be referred urgently (same day or next day) to a rheumatology or fast-track GCA service for confirmation with temporal artery ultrasound and/or biopsy, ideally within a week of starting steroids. Diagnostic yield falls only slowly with treatment. Other steps: monitor and treat her glucose on steroids, bone protection (calcium, vitamin D and an antiresorptive given her osteopenia), and consider PPI cover. Low-dose aspirin is no longer routinely recommended.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Suspected GCA: start prednisolone 40–60 mg immediately (IV methylprednisolone if visual symptoms), then arrange urgent ultrasound/biopsy within a week. Do not wait for confirmation before treating.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "Mackie SL et al. British Society for Rheumatology guideline on diagnosis and treatment of giant cell arteritis (Rheumatology 2020)", "url": "https://academic.oup.com/rheumatology/article/59/3/e1/5714024"}
• {"name": "BSR 2020 GCA recommendations (summary PDF, hosted by PMRGCA Scotland)", "url": "https://pmrgcascotland.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/08/BSR-2020-Recommendations-for-the-management-of-GCA.pdf"}

---

EDITED BY HEISENBERG','moderate','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "bank170", "review_status": "owner_approved_2026_09_29", "tags": ["ZQ-0133", "zyntra_bank", "mock1", "level_3", "task_management", "group_adult_medicine", "sys_rheumatology", "approved_by_owner_2026_09_29"], "clinician_task": "management", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"Mackie SL et al. British Society for Rheumatology guideline on diagnosis and treatment of giant cell arteritis (Rheumatology 2020)\", \"url\": \"https://academic.oup.com/rheumatology/article/59/3/e1/5714024\"}; {\"name\": \"BSR 2020 GCA recommendations (summary PDF, hosted by PMRGCA Scotland)\", \"url\": \"https://pmrgcascotland.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/08/BSR-2020-Recommendations-for-the-management-of-GCA.pdf\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "Delaying steroids until biopsy risks irreversible visual loss. Biopsy remains diagnostic for 1–2 weeks after steroids are started.", "C": "Prednisolone 15 mg treats PMR alone. Jaw claudication, scalp tenderness and an abnormal temporal artery mean GCA, which needs 40–60 mg.", "D": "IV methylprednisolone pulses are recommended for GCA with visual loss or amaurosis fugax. She has no visual symptoms, so oral prednisolone is appropriate.", "E": "Her clinical picture is typical of GCA, not an intracranial lesion. Delaying steroids to pursue neuroimaging is inappropriate."}, "key_takeaways": ["Suspected GCA: start prednisolone 40–60 mg immediately (IV methylprednisolone if visual symptoms), then arrange urgent ultrasound/biopsy within a week. Do not wait for confirmation before treating."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,0.0,1.0,'tier_prior','tier=3 (bank170)'),
('a58415e9-246d-5da0-ab02-5991945d1629'::uuid,'ZQ-0134','adult-medicine','rheumatology','A 58-year-old man of Han Chinese ancestry sees his general practitioner in Chatswood, Sydney. He has had 4 attacks of gout in the past year, affecting his big toe and ankle, and has tophi on two fingers. His serum urate is 0.58 mmol/L. He has hypertension and stage 3a chronic kidney disease (eGFR 48 mL/min/1.73 m²). He takes amlodipine and irbesartan. He does not drink alcohol. His current attack settled a month ago on colchicine.

You agree that he needs long-term urate-lowering therapy with allopurinol, started at a low dose and gradually increased to a target serum urate below 0.30 mmol/L because of his tophi. He asks whether anything should be tested before he starts.

Which one of the following tests should be offered before starting allopurinol?','["HLA-B*58:01 genotyping", "HLA-B27 genotyping", "Thiopurine methyltransferase activity", "HLA-B*15:02 genotyping", "Glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase activity"]'::jsonb,'A','CORRECT ANSWER

A. HLA-B*58:01 genotyping

---

Allopurinol is a leading cause of severe cutaneous adverse reactions (SCAR), including drug reaction with eosinophilia and systemic symptoms (DRESS) and Stevens-Johnson syndrome/toxic epidermal necrolysis, which can be fatal. The HLA-B*58:01 allele greatly increases this risk. It is common in people of Han Chinese, Thai and Korean ancestry (especially with CKD). The RCPA and Australian Prescriber recommend HLA-B*58:01 testing before starting allopurinol in people from these higher-risk groups. If he carries HLA-B*58:01, allopurinol should be avoided and an alternative such as febuxostat considered. If negative, allopurinol can be started at a low dose (for example 50 mg daily with eGFR 48) and increased gradually, with anti-inflammatory prophylaxis (colchicine) for the first months. His CKD adds to the SCAR risk, but CKD alone is not a reason to avoid gradual dose titration to target.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Before allopurinol in people of Han Chinese, Thai or Korean ancestry (especially with CKD), test for HLA-B*58:01; if positive, avoid allopurinol. HLA-B*15:02 is for carbamazepine.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "RCPA: Pharmacogenomic indications in Australia \u2013 Allopurinol (HLA-B*58:01)", "url": "https://www.rcpa.edu.au/Library/Practising-Pathology/Pharmacogenomic-Indications-in-Australia/Recommended/Allopurinol"}
• {"name": "Australian Prescriber: Using pharmacogenomics to personalise drug therapy \u2013 which drugs, when and how", "url": "https://australianprescriber.tg.org.au/articles/using-pharmacogenomics-to-personalise-drug-therapy-which-drugs-when-and-how.html"}
• {"name": "MJA 2023: Avoiding severe drug hypersensitivity reactions \u2013 the case for HLA genotyping", "url": "https://www.mja.com.au/journal/2023/218/10/avoiding-severe-drug-hypersensitivity-reactions-case-hla-genotyping-risk"}

---

EDITED BY HEISENBERG','difficult','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "bank170", "review_status": "owner_approved_2026_09_29", "tags": ["ZQ-0134", "zyntra_bank", "mock1", "level_4", "task_data_gathering", "group_adult_medicine", "sys_rheumatology", "approved_by_owner_2026_09_29"], "clinician_task": "data_gathering", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"RCPA: Pharmacogenomic indications in Australia \\u2013 Allopurinol (HLA-B*58:01)\", \"url\": \"https://www.rcpa.edu.au/Library/Practising-Pathology/Pharmacogenomic-Indications-in-Australia/Recommended/Allopurinol\"}; {\"name\": \"Australian Prescriber: Using pharmacogenomics to personalise drug therapy \\u2013 which drugs, when and how\", \"url\": \"https://australianprescriber.tg.org.au/articles/using-pharmacogenomics-to-personalise-drug-therapy-which-drugs-when-and-how.html\"}; {\"name\": \"MJA 2023: Avoiding severe drug hypersensitivity reactions \\u2013 the case for HLA genotyping\", \"url\": \"https://www.mja.com.au/journal/2023/218/10/avoiding-severe-drug-hypersensitivity-reactions-case-hla-genotyping-risk\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"B": "HLA-B27 is associated with spondyloarthritis, not with drug hypersensitivity to allopurinol.", "C": "TPMT activity guides azathioprine and mercaptopurine dosing. It is relevant because allopurinol interacts with azathioprine, but it is not a pre-allopurinol screen.", "D": "HLA-B*15:02 predicts carbamazepine (and phenytoin/oxcarbazepine)-induced SJS/TEN, especially in South-East Asian ancestry. It is not the allele relevant to allopurinol.", "E": "G6PD deficiency matters for drugs like primaquine, dapsone and rasburicase. It does not predict allopurinol hypersensitivity."}, "key_takeaways": ["Before allopurinol in people of Han Chinese, Thai or Korean ancestry (especially with CKD), test for HLA-B*58:01; if positive, avoid allopurinol. HLA-B*15:02 is for carbamazepine."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,1.0,1.0,'tier_prior','tier=4 (bank170)'),
('5af5e7d0-f62a-51a2-9cc6-68849758ad93'::uuid,'ZQ-0135','adult-medicine','rheumatology','A 69-year-old man presents to the emergency department of Dubbo Base Hospital, New South Wales, with a 2-day history of a painful, swollen left knee. He cannot bear weight. He has had 3 previous attacks of gout in his big toes, the last a year ago, and takes hydrochlorothiazide for hypertension and metformin for type 2 diabetes. He is not on urate-lowering therapy. He had a left knee steroid injection for osteoarthritis 3 weeks ago. He has no rash, urethral discharge or recent diarrhoea.

His temperature is 38.6°C, pulse 104/min and blood pressure 128/76 mmHg. The left knee is hot, red and swollen with a large effusion, and any movement is very painful. There are no other swollen joints and no tophi.

Investigations:
- White cells 15.2 × 10^9/L (neutrophils 12.8)
- C-reactive protein 164 mg/L
- Serum urate 0.52 mmol/L (0.20–0.42)
- Creatinine 96 µmol/L
- Blood cultures: 2 sets taken
- X-ray of the knee: effusion, degenerative changes, no fracture

Which one of the following is the most appropriate next step?','["Start oral colchicine and review the knee in 24 hours", "Start IV flucloxacillin, then aspirate the knee tomorrow", "Arrange MRI of the knee to look for osteomyelitis", "Aspirate the knee for Gram stain, culture and crystals, then start IV antibiotics", "Give an intra-articular corticosteroid injection after aspirating the effusion"]'::jsonb,'D','CORRECT ANSWER

D. Aspirate the knee for Gram stain, culture and crystals, then start IV antibiotics

---

An acute hot, swollen joint with fever is septic arthritis until proven otherwise, even in someone with known gout. The two can coexist, and a raised serum urate does not prove gout (urate may be normal or high in either). His recent intra-articular steroid injection, diabetes, fever and high inflammatory markers all increase the likelihood of infection. The key investigation is urgent joint aspiration for synovial white cell count, Gram stain, culture (ideally also inoculated into blood culture bottles) and crystal microscopy. This should be done BEFORE antibiotics if it does not cause significant delay, because antibiotics reduce culture yield. Blood cultures are already taken. Empirical IV antibiotics (for example flucloxacillin, adjusted for local MRSA risk) should then start immediately, with orthopaedic review for washout if the aspirate suggests infection.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Hot swollen joint + fever = septic arthritis until proven otherwise, even with known gout. Aspirate for Gram stain, culture and crystals before antibiotics (if no delay), then start empirical IV antibiotics.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "Ravn C et al. Guideline for management of septic arthritis in native joints (SANJO), J Bone Jt Infect 2023, PMC", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9901514/"}
• {"name": "Healthdirect: Septic arthritis", "url": "https://www.healthdirect.gov.au/septic-arthritis"}

---

EDITED BY HEISENBERG','moderate','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "bank170", "review_status": "owner_approved_2026_09_29", "tags": ["ZQ-0135", "zyntra_bank", "mock1", "level_3", "task_data_gathering", "group_adult_medicine", "sys_rheumatology", "approved_by_owner_2026_09_29"], "clinician_task": "data_gathering", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"Ravn C et al. Guideline for management of septic arthritis in native joints (SANJO), J Bone Jt Infect 2023, PMC\", \"url\": \"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9901514/\"}; {\"name\": \"Healthdirect: Septic arthritis\", \"url\": \"https://www.healthdirect.gov.au/septic-arthritis\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "Treating as gout without aspiration risks missing septic arthritis, which can destroy the joint within days and cause sepsis.", "B": "Antibiotics are needed urgently, but aspirating first (without delay) gives the best chance of identifying the organism. Postponing aspiration to tomorrow is inappropriate.", "C": "MRI is not needed to diagnose septic arthritis and would delay aspiration and treatment.", "E": "Injecting a steroid into a possibly infected joint is dangerous. Infection must be excluded first."}, "key_takeaways": ["Hot swollen joint + fever = septic arthritis until proven otherwise, even with known gout. Aspirate for Gram stain, culture and crystals before antibiotics (if no delay), then start empirical IV antibiotics."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,0.0,1.0,'tier_prior','tier=3 (bank170)'),
('c40d8f6c-f6f6-50f4-9698-35c9ad54baa2'::uuid,'ZQ-0136','adult-medicine','rheumatology','A 24-year-old woman of Samoan heritage with systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE), diagnosed 2 years ago, sees her rheumatologist in Logan, Queensland. She takes hydroxychloroquine 300 mg daily but admits she stopped it 4 months ago because she felt well. For 3 weeks she has had ankle swelling, frothy urine, joint pains and mouth ulcers. She is not pregnant.

Her blood pressure is 158/96 mmHg. She has pitting oedema to the mid-shins, synovitis of several finger joints and two painless palatal ulcers.

Investigations:
- Creatinine 124 µmol/L (6 months ago: 68)
- Urine microscopy: >100 × 10^6/L dysmorphic red cells, red cell casts
- Urine protein:creatinine ratio 380 mg/mmol (<15)
- Albumin 28 g/L
- Anti-dsDNA 420 IU/mL (<10), up from 60
- C3 0.38 g/L (0.9–1.8); C4 0.04 g/L (0.1–0.4)
- Anti-phospholipid antibodies negative
- Renal ultrasound: normal-sized kidneys

Which one of the following is the most likely finding on renal biopsy?','["Minimal mesangial lupus nephritis (class I)", "Advanced sclerosing lupus nephritis (class VI)", "Membranous lupus nephritis (class V) alone", "Proliferative lupus nephritis (class III or IV)", "Thrombotic microangiopathy from antiphospholipid syndrome"]'::jsonb,'D','CORRECT ANSWER

D. Proliferative lupus nephritis (class III or IV)

---

She has an active nephritic picture: dysmorphic red cells with red cell casts, rising creatinine, hypertension and heavy proteinuria. This is accompanied by strongly positive and rising anti-dsDNA and very low C3 and C4, indicating immune-complex-driven disease. These features most strongly predict proliferative (focal class III or diffuse class IV) lupus nephritis, the most common and most serious class, with a high risk of progression to kidney failure. Renal biopsy is still needed to confirm the class and assess activity and chronicity before treatment. Treatment of class III/IV disease (KDIGO 2024) is glucocorticoids plus induction with mycophenolate or low-dose IV cyclophosphamide, often with belimumab or a calcineurin inhibitor, along with hydroxychloroquine, renin-angiotensin blockade and BP control. Stopping hydroxychloroquine is a common trigger for flares. Pacific Islander and Māori patients have higher rates of severe lupus nephritis.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
SLE with an active sediment (red cell casts), rising creatinine, heavy proteinuria, high dsDNA and low complement suggests proliferative (class III/IV) lupus nephritis. Biopsy confirms it before induction therapy.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "KDIGO 2024 Clinical Practice Guideline for the Management of Lupus Nephritis (Kidney International), PubMed", "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38182286/"}
• {"name": "Arthritis Australia / Lupus Australia: Lupus and your kidneys (patient information)", "url": "https://arthritisaustralia.com.au/types-of-arthritis/lupus/"}

---

EDITED BY HEISENBERG','difficult','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "bank170", "review_status": "owner_approved_2026_09_29", "tags": ["ZQ-0136", "zyntra_bank", "mock1", "level_4", "task_data_interpretation_synthesis", "group_adult_medicine", "sys_rheumatology", "approved_by_owner_2026_09_29"], "clinician_task": "data_interpretation_synthesis", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"KDIGO 2024 Clinical Practice Guideline for the Management of Lupus Nephritis (Kidney International), PubMed\", \"url\": \"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38182286/\"}; {\"name\": \"Arthritis Australia / Lupus Australia: Lupus and your kidneys (patient information)\", \"url\": \"https://arthritisaustralia.com.au/types-of-arthritis/lupus/\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "Class I has normal glomeruli by light microscopy with only mesangial deposits. It does not cause an active sediment, rising creatinine or nephrotic-range proteinuria.", "B": "Class VI (over 90% sclerosed glomeruli) causes chronic kidney failure with small kidneys and little active sediment. Her kidneys are normal in size and her decline is recent.", "C": "Pure membranous lupus nephritis presents mainly with nephrotic syndrome, often with a bland sediment, and anti-dsDNA and complement may be relatively normal. Red cell casts and rapidly rising creatinine favour proliferative disease.", "E": "TMA from APS causes haemolysis and thrombocytopenia with a bland sediment. Her antiphospholipid antibodies are negative."}, "key_takeaways": ["SLE with an active sediment (red cell casts), rising creatinine, heavy proteinuria, high dsDNA and low complement suggests proliferative (class III/IV) lupus nephritis. Biopsy confirms it before induction therapy."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,1.0,1.0,'tier_prior','tier=4 (bank170)'),
('75dbeb55-28c8-5ba6-835f-ddc677fdb0fd'::uuid,'ZQ-0137','adult-medicine','rheumatology','A 76-year-old woman with rheumatoid arthritis is brought to the emergency department of a hospital in Ballarat, Victoria, with 4 days of painful mouth ulcers, tiredness and a fever. She has taken methotrexate 15 mg once weekly (every Monday) with folic acid 5 mg weekly for 6 years, with stable blood tests. Ten days ago her GP started trimethoprim 300 mg daily for a urinary tract infection, which she is still taking because ''the chemist said to finish the box''. She also takes perindopril and paracetamol. She confirms she takes methotrexate once weekly only.

Her temperature is 38.2°C, pulse 108/min and blood pressure 110/68 mmHg. She has extensive oral ulceration and mucositis and a few petechiae on her legs.

Investigations:
- Haemoglobin 92 g/L (6 months ago 124); MCV 104 fL
- White cells 1.4 × 10^9/L; neutrophils 0.4 × 10^9/L
- Platelets 38 × 10^9/L
- Creatinine 98 µmol/L (eGFR 52 mL/min/1.73 m²; baseline eGFR 60)
- ALT 64 U/L
- Chest X-ray: clear

Which one of the following is the most likely explanation for her presentation?','["Methotrexate toxicity potentiated by trimethoprim", "Myelodysplastic syndrome unmasked by infection", "Vitamin B12 deficiency from long-term folate supplementation", "Drug-induced immune thrombocytopenia from trimethoprim", "Felty syndrome from long-standing rheumatoid arthritis"]'::jsonb,'A','CORRECT ANSWER

A. Methotrexate toxicity potentiated by trimethoprim

---

She has pancytopenia with macrocytosis, severe mucositis and neutropenic fever: the typical picture of methotrexate toxicity. The trigger is the recent addition of trimethoprim, which is an antifolate (dihydrofolate reductase inhibitor) and reduces renal excretion of methotrexate. This interaction is additive and well documented, and the product information warns against the combination. Older age and mildly reduced renal function (worsened by dehydration and an ACE inhibitor) add to the risk. Management: treat as febrile neutropenia (take cultures and give empirical IV broad-spectrum antibiotics immediately per sepsis guidelines), stop methotrexate and trimethoprim, give folinic acid (calcium folinate) rescue, provide hydration and mouth care, and consider G-CSF and transfusion as needed. Her methotrexate level may be measured, although it can be low after weekly dosing. Patients on methotrexate should avoid trimethoprim and trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole, and pharmacists and GPs should check for this interaction.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Methotrexate + trimethoprim (or co-trimoxazole) can cause life-threatening marrow suppression and mucositis, especially in older patients with reduced renal function. Stop both, give folinic acid, and treat febrile neutropenia.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "Resprim (trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole) Australian Product Information \u2013 interactions with methotrexate", "url": "https://apps.medicines.org.au/files/afprespr.pdf"}
• {"name": "Case series: Methotrexate and trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole interaction (PMC)", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3994806/"}
• {"name": "Australian Prescriber: Methotrexate (safety, interactions)", "url": "https://australianprescriber.tg.org.au/articles/methotrexate.html"}

---

EDITED BY HEISENBERG','difficult','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "bank170", "review_status": "owner_approved_2026_09_29", "tags": ["ZQ-0137", "zyntra_bank", "mock1", "level_4", "task_data_interpretation_synthesis", "group_adult_medicine", "sys_rheumatology", "approved_by_owner_2026_09_29"], "clinician_task": "data_interpretation_synthesis", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"Resprim (trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole) Australian Product Information \\u2013 interactions with methotrexate\", \"url\": \"https://apps.medicines.org.au/files/afprespr.pdf\"}; {\"name\": \"Case series: Methotrexate and trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole interaction (PMC)\", \"url\": \"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3994806/\"}; {\"name\": \"Australian Prescriber: Methotrexate (safety, interactions)\", \"url\": \"https://australianprescriber.tg.org.au/articles/methotrexate.html\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"B": "MDS can cause macrocytic pancytopenia in older adults, but it would not appear abruptly with mucositis after an interacting antifolate, with previously stable counts.", "C": "Folic acid does not cause B12 deficiency (it can mask the anaemia), and B12 deficiency does not cause acute mucositis with neutropenic fever.", "D": "Drug-induced ITP causes isolated thrombocytopenia, not pancytopenia with macrocytosis and mucositis.", "E": "Felty syndrome (RA, splenomegaly and neutropenia) develops gradually and does not explain the acute pancytopenia, macrocytosis and mucositis after a new antifolate."}, "key_takeaways": ["Methotrexate + trimethoprim (or co-trimoxazole) can cause life-threatening marrow suppression and mucositis, especially in older patients with reduced renal function. Stop both, give folinic acid, and treat febrile neutropenia."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,1.0,1.0,'tier_prior','tier=4 (bank170)'),
('8cd20231-b506-51ce-945e-56f15c351db8'::uuid,'ZQ-0138','adult-medicine','dermatology','A 52-year-old man sees his general practitioner in Mandurah, Western Australia, because his wife noticed a mole on his upper back had changed. He is a fair-skinned former surveyor with extensive past sun exposure. He has had two basal cell carcinomas removed. There is no family history of melanoma.

On examination there is a 7 mm pigmented lesion on the left upper back with an irregular border and uneven colours (brown, black and a grey area). His wife''s photographs from 6 months ago show a smaller, more uniform lesion. Dermoscopy shows an atypical pigment network, irregular dots and globules, and a blue-white veil. There is no palpable axillary or cervical lymphadenopathy. The lesion is on flat skin that can be closed easily.

Which one of the following is the most appropriate next step?','["Shave biopsy of the most raised part of the lesion", "Wide local excision with a 1 cm margin", "Excisional biopsy with a 2 mm clinical margin", "Punch biopsy of the darkest area and review", "Repeat photography and dermoscopy in 3 months"]'::jsonb,'C','CORRECT ANSWER

C. Excisional biopsy with a 2 mm clinical margin

---

The lesion is suspicious for melanoma: it is changing, asymmetric in colour and border, and shows several dermoscopic melanoma features. Australian melanoma guidelines (Cancer Council Australia) recommend complete EXCISIONAL biopsy with a 2 mm clinical margin and a cuff of subcutaneous fat as the preferred first step. This gives the pathologist the whole lesion to measure Breslow thickness accurately, which determines staging, re-excision margins and whether sentinel lymph node biopsy is discussed. Excising with a narrow margin, closed along the line of lymphatic drainage, preserves accurate lymphatic mapping if sentinel node biopsy is needed later. Once the diagnosis and thickness are known, definitive wide excision follows (for example 5–10 mm for in situ disease, 1 cm for melanoma 1 mm or less thick, 1–2 cm for more than 1 mm up to 4 mm, and 2 cm for more than 4 mm).

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Suspected melanoma: excisional biopsy with a 2 mm clinical margin (and some fat) for accurate Breslow thickness. Definitive wide excision margins are set after histology.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "Cancer Council Australia: Clinical practice guidelines for the diagnosis and management of melanoma \u2013 biopsy", "url": "https://www.cancer.org.au/clinical-guidelines/skin-cancer/melanoma"}
• {"name": "Cancer Council NSW: Melanoma skin cancer \u2013 guide for medical practitioners (2023, PDF)", "url": "https://www.cancercouncil.com.au/wp-content/uploads/2023/05/melanoma-skin-cancer-guide-for-medical-practitioners.pdf"}

---

EDITED BY HEISENBERG','moderate','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "bank170", "review_status": "owner_approved_2026_09_29", "tags": ["ZQ-0138", "zyntra_bank", "mock1", "level_3", "task_management", "group_adult_medicine", "sys_dermatology", "approved_by_owner_2026_09_29"], "clinician_task": "management", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"Cancer Council Australia: Clinical practice guidelines for the diagnosis and management of melanoma \\u2013 biopsy\", \"url\": \"https://www.cancer.org.au/clinical-guidelines/skin-cancer/melanoma\"}; {\"name\": \"Cancer Council NSW: Melanoma skin cancer \\u2013 guide for medical practitioners (2023, PDF)\", \"url\": \"https://www.cancercouncil.com.au/wp-content/uploads/2023/05/melanoma-skin-cancer-guide-for-medical-practitioners.pdf\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "Shave biopsies can transect the lesion and underestimate Breslow thickness, compromising staging.", "B": "Wide excision before diagnosis is not recommended. It may be unnecessary and can disrupt lymphatic drainage for later sentinel node mapping; margins are chosen after histology.", "D": "Partial (punch or incisional) biopsy risks sampling error and inaccurate thickness. It is reserved for large lesions or cosmetically sensitive sites where full excision is impractical.", "E": "A changing lesion with multiple dermoscopic melanoma features should not be monitored; it needs excision now."}, "key_takeaways": ["Suspected melanoma: excisional biopsy with a 2 mm clinical margin (and some fat) for accurate Breslow thickness. Definitive wide excision margins are set after histology."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,0.0,1.0,'tier_prior','tier=3 (bank170)'),
('851985eb-88da-562b-8285-31c215162af8'::uuid,'ZQ-0139','adult-medicine','dermatology','A 61-year-old man is being treated through Royal Darwin Hospital''s Hospital-in-the-Home service with IV vancomycin for MRSA vertebral osteomyelitis. He is in his fourth week of therapy, with vancomycin troughs in the target range. For 3 days he has had fever and an itchy rash that started on his face and upper trunk and has spread to most of his body. His face is swollen. He has no mouth, eye or genital soreness. He takes no other new medicines.

His temperature is 38.9°C, pulse 104/min and blood pressure 124/74 mmHg. He has a confluent, morbilliform red rash covering about 60% of his body surface area, with marked facial oedema. There are no blisters, pustules or skin detachment, and Nikolsky sign is negative. Mucous membranes are normal. He has tender cervical and axillary lymphadenopathy.

Investigations:
- White cells 16.4 × 10^9/L; eosinophils 2.8 × 10^9/L (<0.5)
- Blood film: atypical (reactive) lymphocytes
- ALT 412 U/L; ALP 210 U/L; bilirubin 22 µmol/L
- Creatinine 132 µmol/L (baseline 80)

Which one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?','["Stevens-Johnson syndrome (SJS)", "Toxic epidermal necrolysis (TEN)", "Acute generalised exanthematous pustulosis (AGEP)", "Vancomycin infusion reaction (vancomycin flushing syndrome)", "Drug reaction with eosinophilia and systemic symptoms (DRESS)"]'::jsonb,'E','CORRECT ANSWER

E. Drug reaction with eosinophilia and systemic symptoms (DRESS)

---

Drug reaction with eosinophilia and systemic symptoms (DRESS) is a severe delayed hypersensitivity reaction. It typically starts 2–8 weeks after a new drug and causes fever, a widespread morbilliform rash (often more than 50% of the body surface) with facial oedema, lymphadenopathy, eosinophilia and/or atypical lymphocytes, and internal organ involvement, most often the liver, then the kidneys. Vancomycin is one of the commonest causes of DRESS, along with allopurinol, anticonvulsants and sulfonamides. The RegiSCAR score helps confirm the diagnosis; he scores highly. Management: stop the culprit drug immediately (switch to a structurally unrelated anti-MRSA agent, for example daptomycin, with ID advice), admit, give supportive care, and start systemic corticosteroids for significant organ involvement. Document the allergy clearly as a severe reaction. Monitor for late complications, including thyroiditis and diabetes months later.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
DRESS: 2–8 weeks after a new drug, with fever, widespread rash and facial oedema, lymphadenopathy, eosinophilia/atypical lymphocytes and organ involvement (liver, kidney). Vancomycin is a common cause. Stop the drug and give steroids for organ involvement.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "DermNet: Drug reaction with eosinophilia and systemic symptoms (DRESS)", "url": "https://dermnetnz.org/topics/drug-hypersensitivity-syndrome"}
• {"name": "ASCIA: Drug allergy \u2013 severe cutaneous adverse reactions (health professional information)", "url": "https://www.allergy.org.au/hp/papers/drug-allergy"}

---

EDITED BY HEISENBERG','difficult','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "bank170", "review_status": "owner_approved_2026_09_29", "tags": ["ZQ-0139", "zyntra_bank", "mock1", "level_4", "task_data_interpretation_synthesis", "group_adult_medicine", "sys_dermatology", "approved_by_owner_2026_09_29"], "clinician_task": "data_interpretation_synthesis", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"DermNet: Drug reaction with eosinophilia and systemic symptoms (DRESS)\", \"url\": \"https://dermnetnz.org/topics/drug-hypersensitivity-syndrome\"}; {\"name\": \"ASCIA: Drug allergy \\u2013 severe cutaneous adverse reactions (health professional information)\", \"url\": \"https://www.allergy.org.au/hp/papers/drug-allergy\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "SJS causes painful mucosal erosions (two or more sites) and blistering with less than 10% skin detachment. He has no mucosal involvement or blisters.", "B": "TEN has more than 30% epidermal detachment, a positive Nikolsky sign and mucosal involvement. None of these is present.", "C": "AGEP appears within days of a new drug (often beta-lactams), with hundreds of small sterile pustules on a red background, and resolves rapidly after stopping the drug. It is not associated with weeks of latency and marked eosinophilia with hepatitis.", "D": "Vancomycin flushing syndrome (formerly ''red man syndrome'') occurs during or soon after the infusion, is rate-related, affects the face, neck and upper trunk, and does not cause fever, eosinophilia or organ injury."}, "key_takeaways": ["DRESS: 2–8 weeks after a new drug, with fever, widespread rash and facial oedema, lymphadenopathy, eosinophilia/atypical lymphocytes and organ involvement (liver, kidney). Vancomycin is a common cause. Stop the drug and give steroids for organ involvement."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,1.0,1.0,'tier_prior','tier=4 (bank170)'),
('1305868c-9a14-59b0-98e3-16032df2dac2'::uuid,'ZQ-0140','adult-medicine','dermatology','A 54-year-old Aboriginal woman from a remote community in Central Australia is admitted to Alice Springs Hospital, Northern Territory, with a 2-month history of thick, scaly, crusted skin on her hands, elbows, buttocks and feet. Her itch is mild. She has type 2 diabetes and stage 3 chronic kidney disease and had a fall at home 3 weeks ago. Her grandchildren, who live with her, have had itchy rashes and several have had skin sores treated at the community clinic.

On examination she has thick, grey-yellow, fissured crusts over the hands, wrists, elbows, buttocks and soles, with nail thickening. There are some areas of impetiginised skin. Skin scrapings show numerous Sarcoptes scabiei mites and eggs. Her temperature is 37.4°C. She is admitted with an Aboriginal Liaison Officer involved, and the treating team has graded her crusted scabies as grade 2 using the NT grading tool.

Which one of the following is the most appropriate treatment plan?','["Topical permethrin 5% repeated in 7 days, and discharge to community follow-up", "Multi-dose oral ivermectin 200 microgram/kg with permethrin and keratolytic cream", "A single dose of oral ivermectin 200 microgram/kg, and treat household contacts", "Oral prednisolone for eczema and flucloxacillin for skin infection, then review", "Topical benzyl benzoate 25% daily for 7 days, with no need for oral treatment"]'::jsonb,'B','CORRECT ANSWER

B. Multi-dose oral ivermectin 200 microgram/kg with permethrin and keratolytic cream

---

Crusted (Norwegian) scabies is a severe form with a massive mite burden. It is highly contagious and a reservoir for recurrent scabies, impetigo, and downstream complications (for example acute post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis and possibly rheumatic heart disease) in remote communities. Secondary bacterial sepsis carries significant mortality. NT Health guidance recommends admission and a combined approach: repeated oral ivermectin 200 microgram/kg (with food), with the number of doses set by the severity grade (grade 1: 3 doses on days 0, 1 and 7; grade 2: 5 doses adding days 8 and 14; grade 3: 7 doses adding days 21 and 28). This is combined with topical permethrin 5% and a keratolytic cream (for example urea with lactic acid) to break down crusts. Crusted scabies is notifiable in the NT. Household and community contacts need simultaneous treatment, with environmental measures (washing bedding and clothing). Care should be culturally safe and involve Aboriginal Health Practitioners or Liaison Officers, family and the community clinic. Her possible immune compromise (diabetes, CKD) should also be considered, and HTLV-1 serology is often checked in Central Australia.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Crusted scabies: admit; oral ivermectin 200 microgram/kg in 3, 5 or 7 doses by grade, plus permethrin 5% and a keratolytic. It is notifiable in the NT; treat contacts and involve Aboriginal health staff and the community clinic.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "NT Health: Scabies (public health, including crusted scabies)", "url": "https://health.nt.gov.au/public-health-notifiable-diseases/scabies"}
• {"name": "NT Health: Crusted scabies \u2013 clinical guideline (NT Health Digital Library)", "url": "https://digitallibrary.health.nt.gov.au/nthealthserver/api/core/bitstreams/c91ad290-ef58-4379-b428-48c4bb6297ad/content"}

---

EDITED BY HEISENBERG','difficult','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "bank170", "review_status": "owner_approved_2026_09_29", "tags": ["ZQ-0140", "zyntra_bank", "mock1", "level_4", "task_management", "group_adult_medicine", "sys_dermatology", "approved_by_owner_2026_09_29"], "clinician_task": "management", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"NT Health: Scabies (public health, including crusted scabies)\", \"url\": \"https://health.nt.gov.au/public-health-notifiable-diseases/scabies\"}; {\"name\": \"NT Health: Crusted scabies \\u2013 clinical guideline (NT Health Digital Library)\", \"url\": \"https://digitallibrary.health.nt.gov.au/nthealthserver/api/core/bitstreams/c91ad290-ef58-4379-b428-48c4bb6297ad/content\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "Topical treatment alone cannot penetrate thick crusts and fails in crusted scabies. Early discharge also risks ongoing community transmission.", "C": "A single ivermectin dose (repeated in 7–14 days) suits ordinary scabies. Crusted scabies needs multiple doses by grade plus topical and keratolytic therapy, and contacts need treatment too.", "D": "This is scabies, not eczema. Steroids would worsen infestation. The impetigo needs antibiotics, but the underlying infestation must be treated.", "E": "Topical therapy alone, without oral ivermectin and keratolytics, is inadequate for crusted scabies."}, "key_takeaways": ["Crusted scabies: admit; oral ivermectin 200 microgram/kg in 3, 5 or 7 doses by grade, plus permethrin 5% and a keratolytic. It is notifiable in the NT; treat contacts and involve Aboriginal health staff and the community clinic."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,1.0,1.0,'tier_prior','tier=4 (bank170)'),
('22882480-44c7-512c-b5b3-cc598da34ca9'::uuid,'ZQ-0141','adult-medicine','neurology','An 84-year-old man is in his third day on the medical ward at South West Healthcare, Warrnambool, Victoria, after admission with cellulitis of the left leg, now improving on IV cefazolin. Overnight he became agitated and disoriented, tried to climb out of bed, and pulled at his cannula. The day before, he was lucid. He has mild cognitive impairment and benign prostatic hyperplasia. His medicines include amitriptyline 25 mg at night (for nerve pain), oxycodone 5 mg as needed (taken 4 times in the last 24 hours), and paracetamol. Nursing staff report he has been passing small amounts of urine frequently and had no bowel action for 3 days.

His temperature is 37.3°C, pulse 94/min, blood pressure 142/84 mmHg and oxygen saturation 96% on room air. He is inattentive (cannot count backwards from 20) and restless, without focal neurological signs. The leg erythema is receding. He has lower abdominal fullness that is dull to percussion, and he groans when this is palpated.

Investigations this morning:
- White cells 9.1 × 10^9/L (admission 15.2)
- Sodium 138 mmol/L; creatinine 118 µmol/L (admission 92)
- Capillary glucose 7.2 mmol/L

Which one of the following is the most appropriate next step to identify the cause of his delirium?','["Non-contrast CT scan of the brain", "Urine dipstick and culture", "Bladder scan for residual urine volume", "Electroencephalogram (EEG) this morning", "Serum ammonia and thyroid function"]'::jsonb,'C','CORRECT ANSWER

C. Bladder scan for residual urine volume

---

He has new, fluctuating inattention and agitation, which is hyperactive delirium. The most likely precipitant is acute urinary retention: benign prostatic hyperplasia, an anticholinergic drug (amitriptyline), opioid use and constipation all predispose to it. Frequent small voids (overflow), a dull, tender suprapubic mass and a rising creatinine support this. The ACSQHC Delirium Clinical Care Standard asks clinicians to identify and treat the underlying causes. A bedside bladder scan is quick and non-invasive and immediately confirms retention, which should then be relieved by catheterisation. Other steps: review and deprescribe amitriptyline and minimise opioids, treat constipation, use non-drug strategies (orientation, family involvement, sleep hygiene), and avoid physical restraint and routine antipsychotics.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
In older inpatients with delirium, look for retention and constipation (especially with BPH, anticholinergics and opioids). A bedside bladder scan is a quick first step; avoid blaming a positive dipstick.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "Australian Commission on Safety and Quality in Health Care: Delirium Clinical Care Standard (2021)", "url": "https://www.safetyandquality.gov.au/standards/clinical-care-standards/delirium-clinical-care-standard"}
• {"name": "Queensland Health: Residential aged care clinical pathway \u2013 new or worsened confusion", "url": "https://www.health.qld.gov.au/clinical-practice/guidelines-procedures/clinical-pathways/residential-aged-care-clinical-pathways/all-pathways/new-or-worsened-confusion"}

---

EDITED BY HEISENBERG','moderate','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "bank170", "review_status": "owner_approved_2026_09_29", "tags": ["ZQ-0141", "zyntra_bank", "mock1", "level_3", "task_data_gathering", "group_adult_medicine", "sys_neurology", "approved_by_owner_2026_09_29"], "clinician_task": "data_gathering", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"Australian Commission on Safety and Quality in Health Care: Delirium Clinical Care Standard (2021)\", \"url\": \"https://www.safetyandquality.gov.au/standards/clinical-care-standards/delirium-clinical-care-standard\"}; {\"name\": \"Queensland Health: Residential aged care clinical pathway \\u2013 new or worsened confusion\", \"url\": \"https://www.health.qld.gov.au/clinical-practice/guidelines-procedures/clinical-pathways/residential-aged-care-clinical-pathways/all-pathways/new-or-worsened-confusion\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "He has no head injury or focal neurological signs and has a clear alternative cause. CT is not the first step here.", "B": "Dipstick testing in older adults is unreliable because asymptomatic bacteriuria is common; a positive result could lead to wrongly blaming a UTI and missing retention. Retention should be confirmed first.", "D": "EEG is reserved for suspected non-convulsive status epilepticus. It is not a first-line delirium investigation.", "E": "There is no liver disease or thyroid history to suggest these, and they would not explain his suprapubic findings."}, "key_takeaways": ["In older inpatients with delirium, look for retention and constipation (especially with BPH, anticholinergics and opioids). A bedside bladder scan is a quick first step; avoid blaming a positive dipstick."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,0.0,1.0,'tier_prior','tier=3 (bank170)'),
('470fc7a2-18a9-5d46-8ff7-03ac1cb3c18c'::uuid,'ZQ-0142','adult-medicine','renal','An 81-year-old man with end-stage kidney disease (eGFR 8 mL/min/1.73 m²) has chosen conservative (non-dialysis) kidney management. He is receiving end-of-life care at home in Launceston, Tasmania, supported by the community palliative care team. Five days ago, for painful bone metastases from prostate cancer, he was started on a continuous subcutaneous infusion of morphine 10 mg/24 hours via a syringe driver, with morphine 2.5 mg subcutaneously as needed (he has used 3 breakthrough doses a day).

His wife reports he has become increasingly drowsy and muddled over 2 days, sees ''insects on the walls'' and has jerking of his arms and legs. His pain is well controlled. On examination he is drowsy but rousable and oriented to person only, respiratory rate 14/min, pupils 3 mm, with multifocal myoclonus. He has no fever, and his bowels opened yesterday. His goals of care are comfort at home, and there is an advance care directive.

Which one of the following is the most appropriate management?','["Increase the morphine infusion to 15 mg/24 hours for better comfort", "Give naloxone 400 microgram IV bolus and stop the opioid", "Add subcutaneous midazolam and continue the morphine unchanged", "Switch to subcutaneous fentanyl at a reduced equianalgesic dose", "Change to oral tramadol, which is less sedating than morphine"]'::jsonb,'D','CORRECT ANSWER

D. Switch to subcutaneous fentanyl at a reduced equianalgesic dose

---

He has opioid-induced neurotoxicity: drowsiness, delirium with hallucinations, and myoclonus, with good pain control. In severe kidney impairment, morphine''s active metabolites (morphine-3-glucuronide and morphine-6-glucuronide) accumulate and cause these effects. Management is to rotate to an opioid that is safer in kidney failure. Fentanyl is metabolised in the liver to inactive metabolites and is preferred in end-stage kidney disease. A conservative starting dose (reduced from the calculated equianalgesic dose to allow for incomplete cross-tolerance) is used, with breakthrough fentanyl doses and advice from the palliative care team. Other supportive measures: hydration if consistent with goals of care, reviewing other contributing drugs, and treating reversible causes such as constipation and urinary retention. Midazolam can help distressing myoclonus but does not treat the cause.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Opioid neurotoxicity (drowsiness, hallucinations, myoclonus) on morphine in severe CKD: rotate to fentanyl (hepatic metabolism, no active metabolites) at a reduced equianalgesic dose. Avoid morphine, codeine and tramadol in ESKD.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "Eastern Palliative Care Victoria (EPCVic) / Palliative Care Victoria: Opioid Conversion Workbook (August 2024)", "url": "https://www.epcvic.org.au/uploads/778/64/Opioid-Conversion-Workbook-August-2024.pdf"}
