EDITED BY HEISENBERG','difficult','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "bank170", "review_status": "owner_approved_2026_09_29", "tags": ["ZQ-0142", "zyntra_bank", "mock1", "level_4", "task_management", "group_adult_medicine", "sys_renal", "approved_by_owner_2026_09_29"], "clinician_task": "management", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"Eastern Palliative Care Victoria (EPCVic) / Palliative Care Victoria: Opioid Conversion Workbook (August 2024)\", \"url\": \"https://www.epcvic.org.au/uploads/778/64/Opioid-Conversion-Workbook-August-2024.pdf\"}; {\"name\": \"WA Cancer and Palliative Care Network: Evidence-based clinical guidelines for adults in the terminal phase (2nd edition)\", \"url\": \"https://ahs.health.wa.gov.au/~/media/Files/Corporate/general-documents/Health-Networks/WA-Cancer-and-Palliative-Care/Palliative-care/Evidencebasedclinicalguidelinesforadultsintheterminalphase-2nd-Ed.pdf\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "Increasing morphine will worsen metabolite accumulation and neurotoxicity. His pain is already controlled.", "B": "His respiratory rate is 14/min, so there is no opioid-induced respiratory depression. A full 400 microgram bolus in an opioid-tolerant patient in pain would precipitate severe pain and withdrawal. If ever needed, naloxone is given in small titrated doses.", "C": "Midazolam may ease myoclonus symptomatically, but continuing morphine leaves the cause untreated and adds sedation.", "E": "Tramadol and its active metabolite accumulate in kidney failure and lower the seizure threshold. It is not recommended in ESKD, and the oral route may not be reliable at the end of life."}, "key_takeaways": ["Opioid neurotoxicity (drowsiness, hallucinations, myoclonus) on morphine in severe CKD: rotate to fentanyl (hepatic metabolism, no active metabolites) at a reduced equianalgesic dose. Avoid morphine, codeine and tramadol in ESKD."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,1.0,1.0,'tier_prior','tier=4 (bank170)'),
('86a46127-649d-504b-9335-ac7b6472e537'::uuid,'ZQ-0143','adult-medicine','gastroenterology','A 34-year-old man weighing 68 kg is brought to the emergency department of Cairns Hospital, Queensland, by ambulance. Two hours ago, after an argument, he swallowed 32 tablets of modified-release paracetamol 665 mg (for arthritis, from his mother''s supply). He took nothing else and has not drunk alcohol. He is alert, cooperative and not vomiting. He says he regrets taking them.

His observations are normal and GCS 15. Examination is normal. Baseline blood tests have been sent, including a paracetamol concentration, liver function tests, INR, electrolytes and creatinine. The mental health liaison team has been contacted, and he is under appropriate supervision.

Which one of the following is the most appropriate management of the overdose?','["Activated charcoal, then start acetylcysteine if the 4-hour level is above the nomogram line", "Activated charcoal, start acetylcysteine now and complete the full 20-hour course", "Start acetylcysteine now with the second bag doubled to 200 mg/kg over 16 hours", "No charcoal; take a 4-hour level and treat according to the nomogram line", "Start acetylcysteine now, then stop it if two levels 4 hours apart are below the line"]'::jsonb,'B','CORRECT ANSWER

B. Activated charcoal, start acetylcysteine now and complete the full 20-hour course

---

He has taken 32 × 665 mg = about 21.3 g of modified-release (MR) paracetamol, which is about 313 mg/kg. Under the 2020 ANZ paracetamol poisoning guidelines (Chiew et al., MJA), MR ingestions of 10 g or more, or 200 mg/kg or more (whichever is less), need specific management because absorption is delayed and prolonged: (1) Activated charcoal 50 g should be offered up to 4 hours after ingestion to a cooperative, awake patient. (2) Acetylcysteine should be started immediately, and a full 20-hour course given regardless of the serum paracetamol concentration. The nomogram cannot be used to decide on treatment after MR ingestion. (3) Two paracetamol concentrations 4 hours apart (the first at least 4 hours after ingestion) are used to guide dose and duration. The second bag is doubled (200 mg/kg over 16 hours) if either concentration is more than double the nomogram line, or if the ingestion is 30 g or more or 500 mg/kg or more. His 21 g (about 313 mg/kg) does not automatically qualify. ALT and paracetamol are rechecked near the end of the infusion. The standard two-bag regimen is 200 mg/kg over 4 hours, then 100 mg/kg over 16 hours, with adult doses based on actual weight rounded up to the nearest 10 kg (70 kg here). A psychosocial assessment is needed once he is medically stable.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
MR paracetamol of 10 g or more or 200 mg/kg or more: charcoal up to 4 hours, and start acetylcysteine immediately and complete the full 20-hour course regardless of levels. Double the second bag if 30 g or more/500 mg/kg or more, or a level above double the line.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "Chiew AL et al. Updated guidelines for the management of paracetamol poisoning in Australia and New Zealand (MJA 2020)", "url": "https://www.mja.com.au/journal/2020/212/4/updated-guidelines-management-paracetamol-poisoning-australia-and-new-zealand"}

---

EDITED BY HEISENBERG','difficult','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "bank170", "review_status": "owner_approved_2026_09_29", "tags": ["ZQ-0143", "zyntra_bank", "mock1", "level_5", "task_management", "group_adult_medicine", "sys_gastroenterology", "approved_by_owner_2026_09_29"], "clinician_task": "management", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"Chiew AL et al. Updated guidelines for the management of paracetamol poisoning in Australia and New Zealand (MJA 2020)\", \"url\": \"https://www.mja.com.au/journal/2020/212/4/updated-guidelines-management-paracetamol-poisoning-australia-and-new-zealand\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "Using the nomogram to decide on treatment applies only to immediate-release ingestions. Early MR levels may reflect only the immediate-release component (about 31%) and can be falsely reassuring.", "C": "A doubled second bag is reserved for MR ingestions of 30 g or more or 500 mg/kg or more, or concentrations above double the nomogram line. He has taken about 21 g (313 mg/kg), so he starts on the standard regimen and has levels monitored. Charcoal is also omitted.", "D": "Charcoal is indicated up to 4 hours for MR ingestions of 10 g or more, and the nomogram cannot rule out the need for treatment after MR paracetamol.", "E": "Stopping early on the basis of levels applies to MR ingestions below the toxic threshold (under 10 g and under 200 mg/kg). Above it, the full 20-hour course is given regardless of levels, and charcoal is omitted in this option."}, "key_takeaways": ["MR paracetamol of 10 g or more or 200 mg/kg or more: charcoal up to 4 hours, and start acetylcysteine immediately and complete the full 20-hour course regardless of levels. Double the second bag if 30 g or more/500 mg/kg or more, or a level above double the line."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,2.0,1.0,'tier_prior','tier=5 (bank170)'),
('b6c6b90c-6b85-5763-aec0-b1c8d33073fe'::uuid,'ZQ-0144','adult-medicine','haematology-oncology','A 39-year-old cotton farmer is brought to the emergency department of Moree District Hospital, New South Wales, at 1500 hours. At 1330 hours, while moving irrigation pipes, he felt a sharp pain on his left ankle and saw a brown snake moving away. His co-worker applied an elasticised pressure bandage over the whole leg and a splint, and the ambulance crew kept him still. He has had no headache, nausea, vomiting, abdominal pain, collapse, blurred vision or bleeding.

On arrival he is well, with pulse 78/min, blood pressure 128/80 mmHg and normal neurological examination (no ptosis, diplopia or weakness). The pressure bandage and splint are in place. There are two small scratch marks on the ankle without ongoing bleeding. The hospital stocks antivenom.

Baseline investigations (taken on arrival, 1500 hours):
- INR 1.0; APTT 29 s; fibrinogen 3.1 g/L; D-dimer normal
- CK 140 U/L
- Full blood count and creatinine normal

According to NSW guidance, which one of the following is the most appropriate next step?','["Remove the bandage in a critical care area; repeat bloods at 1 hour, then 6 and 12 hours", "Swab the bite site for a venom detection kit to choose the correct antivenom", "Give one vial of brown snake antivenom now, before removing the pressure bandage", "Leave the pressure bandage on until 12 hours after the bite, then repeat the bloods", "Remove the bandage, and discharge him if bloods are normal at 6 hours after the bite"]'::jsonb,'A','CORRECT ANSWER

A. Remove the bandage in a critical care area; repeat bloods at 1 hour, then 6 and 12 hours

---

The NSW Health Snake and Spider Bites Clinical Management guideline (GL2024_007) says that if baseline blood tests are normal and there is no clinical envenomation, the pressure bandage with immobilisation (PBI) can be removed in a critical care area under medical direction, with antivenom available. Envenomation usually becomes evident within about an hour of removal, because the bandage acts as a ''dam'' slowing lymphatic spread of venom. Serial coagulation studies (INR, APTT, fibrinogen, D-dimer) and CK are then repeated at 1 hour after PBI removal, 6 hours after the bite and 12 hours after the bite. All patients with suspected snakebite must be observed for 12 hours from the time of the bite. Discharge is appropriate only if tests at 6 and 12 hours are normal and the patient is well. Abnormal results (for example venom-induced consumption coagulopathy) or neurotoxicity at any stage mean he is envenomed and needs antivenom.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Suspected snakebite, normal baseline tests and no symptoms: remove the PBI in a critical care area, then take coagulation studies and CK at 1 hour after removal and at 6 and 12 hours after the bite. Observe for 12 hours; no venom detection kit.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "NSW Health Guideline GL2024_007: Snake and Spider Bites Clinical Management (June 2024)", "url": "https://www1.health.nsw.gov.au/pds/ActivePDSDocuments/GL2024_007.pdf"}

---

EDITED BY HEISENBERG','difficult','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "bank170", "review_status": "owner_approved_2026_09_29", "tags": ["ZQ-0144", "zyntra_bank", "mock1", "level_4", "task_data_gathering", "group_adult_medicine", "sys_haematology_oncology", "approved_by_owner_2026_09_29"], "clinician_task": "data_gathering", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"NSW Health Guideline GL2024_007: Snake and Spider Bites Clinical Management (June 2024)\", \"url\": \"https://www1.health.nsw.gov.au/pds/ActivePDSDocuments/GL2024_007.pdf\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"B": "NSW guidance states that venom detection kits are no longer recommended, and swabbing the wound is not advised. Antivenom choice relies on geography, clinical syndrome and toxicology advice.", "C": "Antivenom is given only for clinical or laboratory evidence of envenomation. Empirical antivenom in a well patient with normal tests exposes him to anaphylaxis risk without benefit.", "D": "Leaving the PBI on unnecessarily for 12 hours delays detection of envenomation and risks limb ischaemia and discomfort. It is removed once baseline tests are normal and the patient is well.", "E": "A single normal 6-hour set is insufficient; observation for 12 hours after the bite with a 12-hour set of bloods is required before discharge."}, "key_takeaways": ["Suspected snakebite, normal baseline tests and no symptoms: remove the PBI in a critical care area, then take coagulation studies and CK at 1 hour after removal and at 6 and 12 hours after the bite. Observe for 12 hours; no venom detection kit."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,1.0,1.0,'tier_prior','tier=4 (bank170)'),
('b0e1e557-50ce-5739-955f-984e75601a4b'::uuid,'ZQ-0145','adult-medicine','respiratory','A 45-year-old woman is brought to the emergency department of Hervey Bay Hospital, Queensland, 20 minutes after eating prawns at a restaurant. She developed generalised hives, lip swelling, wheeze, dizziness and vomiting within 10 minutes. She has no known allergies and takes no regular medicines, including no beta-blockers. Paramedics gave adrenaline 0.5 mg IM into the thigh, and a second 0.5 mg IM dose 5 minutes later. She has since received 2 L of IV 0.9% sodium chloride, lying flat, with high-flow oxygen.

Now her pulse is 128/min, blood pressure 74/40 mmHg, respiratory rate 26/min and oxygen saturation 93% on high-flow oxygen. She has widespread wheeze but is talking in phrases, with no stridor. She is alert. Cardiac monitoring shows sinus tachycardia. The emergency physician has called for senior and ICU help.

Which one of the following is the most appropriate next step?','["IV hydrocortisone 200 mg and IV promethazine 25 mg", "IV adrenaline 1 mg bolus, repeated every 3–5 minutes", "Nebulised salbutamol and observe for 30 minutes", "IV glucagon 1 mg bolus followed by an infusion", "IV adrenaline infusion with continuous monitoring"]'::jsonb,'E','CORRECT ANSWER

E. IV adrenaline infusion with continuous monitoring

---

She has refractory anaphylaxis: persisting hypotension after 2 appropriate doses of IM adrenaline and fluid resuscitation. ASCIA and the ANZCOR refractory anaphylaxis algorithm recommend starting an IV adrenaline infusion (by clinicians experienced in its use, with continuous cardiac and blood pressure monitoring) when there is inadequate response to 2 or more IM doses. IM adrenaline can be continued every 5 minutes while the infusion is set up. More fluid boluses (for example a further 20 mL/kg) should be given, with early ICU and airway expert involvement. If hypotension persists despite the adrenaline infusion, a second vasopressor (for example noradrenaline or vasopressin) may be added. Glucagon is considered for patients on beta-blockers. Corticosteroids and antihistamines are not first-line: they do not treat hypotension or airway obstruction.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Anaphylaxis still hypotensive after 2 IM adrenaline doses plus fluids: start an IV adrenaline infusion with monitoring and call ICU. No IV 1 mg boluses unless in cardiac arrest; glucagon only with beta-blockers.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "ASCIA: Acute management of anaphylaxis guidelines", "url": "https://www.allergy.org.au/hp/papers/acute-management-of-anaphylaxis-guidelines"}
• {"name": "ANZCOR Guideline 11.10: Refractory anaphylaxis algorithm (July 2026)", "url": "https://www.anzcor.org/assets/ANZCOR-Guidelines-/GL-11/GL-11-10-Flowcharts/GL-11.10-Refractory-anaphylaxis-algorithm-July-2026.pdf"}

---

EDITED BY HEISENBERG','difficult','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "bank170", "review_status": "owner_approved_2026_09_29", "tags": ["ZQ-0145", "zyntra_bank", "mock1", "level_4", "task_management", "group_adult_medicine", "sys_respiratory", "approved_by_owner_2026_09_29"], "clinician_task": "management", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"ASCIA: Acute management of anaphylaxis guidelines\", \"url\": \"https://www.allergy.org.au/hp/papers/acute-management-of-anaphylaxis-guidelines\"}; {\"name\": \"ANZCOR Guideline 11.10: Refractory anaphylaxis algorithm (July 2026)\", \"url\": \"https://www.anzcor.org/assets/ANZCOR-Guidelines-/GL-11/GL-11-10-Flowcharts/GL-11.10-Refractory-anaphylaxis-algorithm-July-2026.pdf\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "Steroids and antihistamines have no role in reversing shock. Promethazine is also a sedating antihistamine that can worsen hypotension; ASCIA does not recommend it in anaphylaxis.", "B": "A 1 mg IV bolus is a cardiac arrest dose. In a patient with a pulse it risks severe hypertension, arrhythmia and myocardial ischaemia. IV adrenaline in anaphylaxis with a pulse is given as a titrated infusion.", "C": "Salbutamol may help bronchospasm as an add-on, but it does not treat shock. Observation alone would be unsafe.", "D": "Glucagon is reserved for refractory anaphylaxis in patients taking beta-blockers. She takes none, and adrenaline has not yet been given IV."}, "key_takeaways": ["Anaphylaxis still hypotensive after 2 IM adrenaline doses plus fluids: start an IV adrenaline infusion with monitoring and call ICU. No IV 1 mg boluses unless in cardiac arrest; glucagon only with beta-blockers."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,1.0,1.0,'tier_prior','tier=4 (bank170)'),
('5f1df062-8c67-5e29-9122-eb50e42ed5fa'::uuid,'ZQ-0146','adult-surgery','general-surgery','A 64-year-old woman comes to the emergency department of a hospital in Wagga Wagga, New South Wales, with two days of colicky central abdominal pain, abdominal distension and vomiting. She has not passed flatus for 24 hours. She had an open appendicectomy at the age of 19 and an abdominal hysterectomy at 45. She has hypertension treated with amlodipine.

Her temperature is 37.2°C, pulse 96/min, blood pressure 132/78 mmHg and respiratory rate 18/min. Her abdomen is distended and tympanic, with mild diffuse tenderness but no guarding or rebound. There is no hernia, and bowel sounds are high-pitched.

Investigations:
- White cell count 10.8 × 10^9/L (4.0–11.0)
- C-reactive protein 18 mg/L (<5)
- Venous lactate 1.3 mmol/L (<2.0)
- Sodium 134 mmol/L (135–145)
- Potassium 3.4 mmol/L (3.5–5.2)
- Creatinine 96 µmol/L (45–90)
- CT abdomen and pelvis with IV contrast: dilated small bowel loops up to 38 mm with a single transition point in the right lower quadrant and collapsed distal ileum. No closed loop, normal bowel wall enhancement, no mesenteric oedema, no free fluid and no free gas.

A nasogastric tube has drained 900 mL of bilious fluid. IV fluids with potassium replacement have been started, and the surgical registrar has reviewed her.

Which one of the following is the most appropriate next step in management?','["Emergency midline laparotomy and adhesiolysis today", "Laparoscopic adhesiolysis on the next available emergency list", "Water-soluble oral contrast via the nasogastric tube, then an abdominal X-ray", "Continue nasogastric decompression and IV fluids for 5 days before reassessing", "Barium small bowel follow-through to define the level of obstruction"]'::jsonb,'C','CORRECT ANSWER

C. Water-soluble oral contrast via the nasogastric tube, then an abdominal X-ray

---

This is adhesive small bowel obstruction (ASBO) without CT signs of strangulation or ischaemia (no closed loop, normal wall enhancement, no free fluid, normal lactate) and without peritonism, so non-operative management is appropriate. The WSES Bologna guidelines (2017 update) recommend a water-soluble contrast agent (e.g. diatrizoate/Gastrografin), given orally or via the nasogastric tube, followed by an abdominal X-ray, usually at up to 24 hours. Contrast in the colon predicts resolution, so the patient can be fed. If contrast has not reached the colon by 24 hours, non-operative management is likely to fail and surgery should be considered. The contrast is also mildly therapeutic: it shortens time to resolution and hospital stay. Non-operative management should not usually continue beyond about 72 hours without resolution. Surgery is needed at any point if signs of strangulation, peritonitis or clinical deterioration appear.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
In adhesive SBO without CT or clinical signs of strangulation, give water-soluble contrast and X-ray at up to 24 hours. Contrast in the colon predicts resolution; its absence predicts failure of non-operative care.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "ten Broek RPG et al. Bologna guidelines for diagnosis and management of adhesive small bowel obstruction (ASBO): 2017 update of the WSES guidelines. World J Emerg Surg 2018;13:24", "url": "https://wjes.biomedcentral.com/counter/pdf/10.1186/s13017-018-0185-2.pdf"}

---

EDITED BY HEISENBERG','difficult','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "bank170", "review_status": "owner_approved_2026_09_29", "tags": ["ZQ-0146", "zyntra_bank", "mock1", "level_4", "task_management", "group_adult_surgery", "sys_general_surgery", "approved_by_owner_2026_09_29"], "clinician_task": "management", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"ten Broek RPG et al. Bologna guidelines for diagnosis and management of adhesive small bowel obstruction (ASBO): 2017 update of the WSES guidelines. World J Emerg Surg 2018;13:24\", \"url\": \"https://wjes.biomedcentral.com/counter/pdf/10.1186/s13017-018-0185-2.pdf\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "Immediate laparotomy is for strangulation, peritonitis, closed-loop obstruction or ischaemia on CT. None is present, and most ASBO settles without surgery.", "B": "Laparoscopic adhesiolysis is an option in selected patients who need surgery, but she has not yet had a trial of non-operative management, which is first line here.", "D": "The Bologna guidelines advise against prolonged non-operative management: failure to resolve within about 72 hours should prompt surgery. Leaving out the contrast challenge also loses its diagnostic and therapeutic value.", "E": "Barium should be avoided in suspected obstruction because it can inspissate and cause barium peritonitis if there is a perforation. CT has already defined the level."}, "key_takeaways": ["In adhesive SBO without CT or clinical signs of strangulation, give water-soluble contrast and X-ray at up to 24 hours. Contrast in the colon predicts resolution; its absence predicts failure of non-operative care."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,1.0,1.0,'tier_prior','tier=4 (bank170)'),
('6894a8ff-a693-5f79-9851-02759973d1fb'::uuid,'ZQ-0147','adult-surgery','general-surgery','A 47-year-old woman was admitted to a public hospital in Hobart three days ago with severe epigastric pain radiating to her back and vomiting. Her lipase on admission was 3800 U/L (<60). She has no history of alcohol use disorder and takes no regular medicines.

Admission ultrasound showed multiple small gallstones, a gallbladder wall of normal thickness, and a common bile duct of 5 mm with no duct stone seen. She received IV fluids and analgesia and did not need oxygen, vasopressors or renal support. Her pain has now settled and she is eating a low-fat diet without pain.

Today her temperature is 36.9°C, pulse 76/min and blood pressure 124/78 mmHg. Her abdomen is soft with mild epigastric tenderness.

Investigations (admission → today):
- Bilirubin 28 → 12 µmol/L (<20)
- ALT 310 → 84 U/L (<35)
- ALP 160 → 118 U/L (30–110)
- White cell count 13.1 → 8.2 × 10^9/L (4.0–11.0)
- Creatinine 70 → 62 µmol/L (45–90)
- C-reactive protein 95 → 60 mg/L (<5)

Which one of the following is the most appropriate management?','["Laparoscopic cholecystectomy during this admission", "Discharge and arrange elective cholecystectomy in 6 to 8 weeks", "ERCP with sphincterotomy before discharge", "Discharge with ursodeoxycholic acid and a low-fat diet", "CT abdomen at 72 hours to exclude necrosis before any surgery"]'::jsonb,'A','CORRECT ANSWER

A. Laparoscopic cholecystectomy during this admission

---

She has mild acute biliary pancreatitis by the revised Atlanta classification: no organ failure and no local or systemic complications, with clinical resolution. International guidelines (IAP/APA, and the 2023 MANCTRA acute biliary pancreatitis bundle) recommend cholecystectomy during the index admission for mild biliary pancreatitis. The PONCHO trial showed that same-admission cholecystectomy roughly halved readmission for recurrent gallstone-related complications (recurrent pancreatitis, cholangitis, colic) compared with interval surgery, with no increase in operative difficulty. Her liver tests are falling, the duct is not dilated and there is no cholangitis, so routine ERCP is not indicated. Intraoperative cholangiography can assess the duct at surgery if needed. Delayed cholecystectomy is reserved for severe pancreatitis with peripancreatic collections, when surgery waits until inflammation settles or collections resolve (commonly after about 6 weeks).

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Mild gallstone pancreatitis: cholecystectomy in the same admission. Delay surgery only for severe pancreatitis with collections. ERCP only for cholangitis or persistent obstruction.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "Podda M et al. The 2023 MANCTRA Acute Biliary Pancreatitis Care Bundle. Ann Surg 2023 (open access, PMC)", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10782931/"}
• {"name": "AGA: Diagnosis and management of acute pancreatitis (Gastroenterology, 2024)", "url": "https://www.gastrojournal.org/article/S0016-5085(24)04916-3/fulltext"}

---

EDITED BY HEISENBERG','difficult','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "bank170", "review_status": "owner_approved_2026_09_29", "tags": ["ZQ-0147", "zyntra_bank", "mock1", "level_4", "task_management", "group_adult_surgery", "sys_general_surgery", "approved_by_owner_2026_09_29"], "clinician_task": "management", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"Podda M et al. The 2023 MANCTRA Acute Biliary Pancreatitis Care Bundle. Ann Surg 2023 (open access, PMC)\", \"url\": \"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10782931/\"}; {\"name\": \"AGA: Diagnosis and management of acute pancreatitis (Gastroenterology, 2024)\", \"url\": \"https://www.gastrojournal.org/article/S0016-5085(24)04916-3/fulltext\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"B": "Interval cholecystectomy exposes her to a significant risk of a recurrent biliary event while waiting. It is the strategy for severe pancreatitis, not mild disease.", "C": "ERCP is for cholangitis or persistent biliary obstruction. It does not prevent recurrent pancreatitis as well as cholecystectomy, and it carries a risk of post-ERCP pancreatitis.", "D": "Ursodeoxycholic acid does not reliably prevent recurrent gallstone pancreatitis and is not a substitute for cholecystectomy in a fit patient.", "E": "Routine CT is not needed in mild pancreatitis that is resolving clinically. CT is for diagnostic doubt or failure to improve (usually after 72–96 hours)."}, "key_takeaways": ["Mild gallstone pancreatitis: cholecystectomy in the same admission. Delay surgery only for severe pancreatitis with collections. ERCP only for cholangitis or persistent obstruction."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,1.0,1.0,'tier_prior','tier=4 (bank170)'),
('2b093a2e-6234-5db1-9e30-e8bfd33f5603'::uuid,'ZQ-0148','adult-surgery','general-surgery','A 58-year-old man is admitted under the general surgical team at a hospital in Ipswich, Queensland, with 18 hours of constant right upper quadrant pain after a fatty meal. He has had three similar but shorter episodes in the past year. He has no fever or rigors, and his urine is a normal colour. He has type 2 diabetes treated with metformin.

His temperature is 37.1°C, pulse 88/min and blood pressure 138/84 mmHg. He is not jaundiced. He is tender in the right upper quadrant with a negative Murphy''s sign.

Investigations:
- White cell count 9.6 × 10^9/L (4.0–11.0)
- Bilirubin 42 µmol/L (<20)
- ALT 220 U/L (<40)
- GGT 310 U/L (<60)
- ALP 190 U/L (30–110)
- Lipase 45 U/L (<60)
- Abdominal ultrasound: multiple gallstones, gallbladder wall 2 mm, no pericholecystic fluid. Common bile duct 8 mm. The distal duct is obscured by bowel gas and no duct stone is seen.

Which one of the following is the most appropriate next investigation?','["Endoscopic retrograde cholangiopancreatography (ERCP)", "Hepatobiliary iminodiacetic acid (HIDA) scan", "Contrast-enhanced CT of the abdomen", "Repeat liver function tests in 6 weeks", "Magnetic resonance cholangiopancreatography (MRCP)"]'::jsonb,'E','CORRECT ANSWER

E. Magnetic resonance cholangiopancreatography (MRCP)

---

The ASGE 2019 guideline stratifies suspected choledocholithiasis. HIGH risk (go straight to ERCP) means any of: a duct stone seen on ultrasound or CT, clinical ascending cholangitis, or bilirubin above 4 mg/dL (about 68 µmol/L) together with a dilated duct. INTERMEDIATE risk means abnormal liver tests, age over 55, or a dilated duct on ultrasound without high-risk features. He has a dilated duct (8 mm), abnormal liver tests and is over 55, but his bilirubin is 42 µmol/L (about 2.5 mg/dL), no stone is seen and he has no cholangitis. So he is intermediate risk. Intermediate-risk patients should have confirmatory imaging with MRCP or EUS (or intraoperative cholangiography/laparoscopic ultrasound at cholecystectomy) before ERCP. This avoids diagnostic ERCP and its risks (pancreatitis, bleeding, perforation) in the many patients whose stone has already passed.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
ASGE 2019: stone on imaging, cholangitis, or bilirubin >4 mg/dL (~68 µmol/L) with a dilated duct → ERCP. Otherwise abnormal LFTs, age >55 or dilated duct → MRCP/EUS first.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "ASGE guideline on the role of endoscopy in the evaluation and management of choledocholithiasis (Gastrointest Endosc 2019)", "url": "https://www.asge.org/docs/default-source/guidelines/asge-guideline-on-the-role-of-endoscopy-in-the-evaluation-and-management-of-choledocholithiasis-2019-june-gie.pdf"}

---

EDITED BY HEISENBERG','difficult','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "bank170", "review_status": "owner_approved_2026_09_29", "tags": ["ZQ-0148", "zyntra_bank", "mock1", "level_4", "task_data_gathering", "group_adult_surgery", "sys_general_surgery", "approved_by_owner_2026_09_29"], "clinician_task": "data_gathering", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"ASGE guideline on the role of endoscopy in the evaluation and management of choledocholithiasis (Gastrointest Endosc 2019)\", \"url\": \"https://www.asge.org/docs/default-source/guidelines/asge-guideline-on-the-role-of-endoscopy-in-the-evaluation-and-management-of-choledocholithiasis-2019-june-gie.pdf\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "ERCP is for high-risk patients. Using it diagnostically in intermediate-risk patients exposes many people with no duct stone to post-ERCP pancreatitis and other complications.", "B": "A HIDA scan assesses cystic duct patency in suspected acute cholecystitis. It does not show duct stones or anatomy well enough to plan ERCP.", "C": "CT has poor sensitivity for non-calcified duct stones and adds radiation. It is not the preferred confirmatory test.", "D": "He has a dilated duct and obstructive liver tests now. Waiting 6 weeks risks cholangitis or pancreatitis from a retained stone."}, "key_takeaways": ["ASGE 2019: stone on imaging, cholangitis, or bilirubin >4 mg/dL (~68 µmol/L) with a dilated duct → ERCP. Otherwise abnormal LFTs, age >55 or dilated duct → MRCP/EUS first."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,1.0,1.0,'tier_prior','tier=4 (bank170)'),
('b888527b-2b5a-592b-9943-3d533173daaa'::uuid,'ZQ-0150','adult-surgery','general-surgery','A 66-year-old man is on the colorectal ward of a tertiary hospital in Perth, five days after a laparoscopic high anterior resection with a primary stapled anastomosis and no defunctioning stoma, for a sigmoid adenocarcinoma. He was eating a light diet on day 3 but since yesterday has felt bloated, has vomited once and has vague lower abdominal pain. This morning the nurse noticed a fast, irregular pulse. He has no chest pain, cough or diarrhoea. He has no previous cardiac history.

His temperature is 37.9°C, pulse 128/min and irregularly irregular, blood pressure 112/70 mmHg, respiratory rate 22/min and oxygen saturation 95% on room air. His abdomen is mildly distended with tenderness in the left iliac fossa and suprapubic region, but no generalised guarding. Port sites look clean. Bowel sounds are sparse. The chest is clear.

Investigations:
- ECG: atrial fibrillation, ventricular rate 125/min, no ischaemic change
- White cell count 15.9 × 10^9/L (4.0–11.0)
- C-reactive protein: day 3, 72 mg/L; today (day 5), 246 mg/L (<5)
- Lactate 2.1 mmol/L (<2.0)
- Creatinine 104 µmol/L (baseline 80)
- Potassium 4.1 mmol/L, magnesium 0.8 mmol/L
- Chest X-ray: clear lungs, no free subdiaphragmatic gas

Which one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?','["Prolonged postoperative ileus", "Clostridioides difficile colitis", "Primary postoperative atrial fibrillation", "Colorectal anastomotic leak", "Hospital-acquired pneumonia"]'::jsonb,'D','CORRECT ANSWER

D. Colorectal anastomotic leak

---

An anastomotic leak typically presents around days 3–7 after colorectal resection, often subtly: return of ileus after initial recovery, fever, tachycardia, localised tenderness near the anastomosis, rising inflammatory markers and early organ dysfunction. New atrial fibrillation after colorectal surgery is a recognised early warning sign of an intra-abdominal septic complication, especially a leak, and should not be treated as a primary cardiac event. A CRP that rises between day 3 and day 5 (here 72 to 246 mg/L) is a strong signal: a low or falling CRP on day 3–4 has high negative predictive value for a leak, whereas a rising CRP should prompt imaging. Free gas on a chest X-ray is not expected and cannot exclude a leak after laparoscopy. The next steps are resuscitation, IV broad-spectrum antibiotics, rate control and urgent CT abdomen and pelvis with IV (and ideally rectal) contrast, with early senior surgical review.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Days 3–7 after colorectal anastomosis: new AF, return of ileus, fever or a rising CRP means anastomotic leak until proven otherwise. Get an urgent contrast CT.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "Diagnostic value of CT scan findings for anastomotic leakage after low anterior resection (Sci Rep 2025)", "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-025-06712-4"}
• {"name": "Singh PP et al. Systematic review and meta-analysis of serum C-reactive protein to predict anastomotic leak after colorectal surgery. Colorectal Dis 2014 (PubMed 24311257)", "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/24311257/"}

---

EDITED BY HEISENBERG','difficult','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "bank170", "review_status": "owner_approved_2026_09_29", "tags": ["ZQ-0150", "zyntra_bank", "mock1", "level_4", "task_data_interpretation_synthesis", "group_adult_surgery", "sys_general_surgery", "approved_by_owner_2026_09_29"], "clinician_task": "data_interpretation_synthesis", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"Diagnostic value of CT scan findings for anastomotic leakage after low anterior resection (Sci Rep 2025)\", \"url\": \"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-025-06712-4\"}; {\"name\": \"Singh PP et al. Systematic review and meta-analysis of serum C-reactive protein to predict anastomotic leak after colorectal surgery. Colorectal Dis 2014 (PubMed 24311257)\", \"url\": \"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/24311257/\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "Ileus alone does not explain fever, leucocytosis, localised tenderness, rising CRP, a rising creatinine and new AF. Recurrent ileus after initial recovery is itself a warning sign of a leak.", "B": "C. difficile usually causes diarrhoea. He has none, and colitis would not explain localised pelvic tenderness near the anastomosis.", "C": "Postoperative AF does occur, but in a patient with fever, rising CRP and abdominal signs after an anastomosis, AF is a marker of sepsis until proven otherwise. Treating it as a primary arrhythmia risks missing the leak.", "E": "The chest is clear clinically and on X-ray. Pneumonia would not explain the abdominal findings."}, "key_takeaways": ["Days 3–7 after colorectal anastomosis: new AF, return of ileus, fever or a rising CRP means anastomotic leak until proven otherwise. Get an urgent contrast CT."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,1.0,1.0,'tier_prior','tier=4 (bank170)'),
('84317f40-918c-5cec-a261-25e5044b8106'::uuid,'ZQ-0151','adult-surgery','general-surgery','A 68-year-old man is referred to the emergency department of Mackay Base Hospital, Queensland, by his GP. For three weeks he has felt full after a few mouthfuls and has vomited large volumes of partly digested food, often several hours after eating, sometimes food he ate the day before. The vomit is not bile-stained. He has lost 8 kg. He has taken naproxen for back pain for several years and had a ''stomach ulcer'' treated 5 years ago. He takes no diuretics or other regular medicines.

He looks dehydrated. His pulse is 108/min, blood pressure 104/66 mmHg lying and 88/58 mmHg sitting, and respiratory rate 12/min. The upper abdomen is distended, and a succussion splash is heard when the abdomen is shaken. There is no peritonism.

Investigations:
- Venous blood gas: pH 7.54, pCO2 50 mmHg, bicarbonate 42 mmol/L
- Sodium 131 mmol/L (135–145)
- Potassium 2.6 mmol/L (3.5–5.2)
- Chloride 78 mmol/L (95–110)
- Urea 14.2 mmol/L (2.5–8.0)
- Creatinine 132 µmol/L (60–110; baseline 85)
- Glucose 5.8 mmol/L
- Spot urine: pH 5.0, sodium 12 mmol/L, chloride under 10 mmol/L

Which one of the following best explains his acid–base and urine findings?','["Gastric acid loss causing chloride-responsive metabolic alkalosis, with paradoxical aciduria", "Mineralocorticoid excess causing chloride-resistant metabolic alkalosis with renal chloride wasting", "Chronic respiratory acidosis from hypoventilation, with appropriate renal compensation", "Metabolic alkalosis combined with a high anion gap acidosis from starvation ketosis", "Occult diuretic use causing metabolic alkalosis with inappropriate renal chloride loss"]'::jsonb,'A','CORRECT ANSWER

A. Gastric acid loss causing chloride-responsive metabolic alkalosis, with paradoxical aciduria

---

Vomiting of old food, early satiety, weight loss and a succussion splash in a man with long-term NSAID use and previous peptic ulcer indicate gastric outlet obstruction (from a pyloric/duodenal ulcer stricture, or gastric cancer, which must be excluded). Loss of gastric HCl causes a metabolic alkalosis (pH 7.54, bicarbonate 42) with appropriate respiratory compensation (expected pCO2 ≈ 0.7 × 42 + 20 ≈ 49 mmHg). The anion gap is normal: 131 − (78 + 42) = 11. Volume depletion activates aldosterone, so the kidney avidly reabsorbs sodium. With low chloride available and marked potassium depletion, sodium is reabsorbed in exchange for H+ as well as K+, so the urine is inappropriately ACIDIC (paradoxical aciduria) despite a systemic alkalosis. A urine chloride below about 20 mmol/L identifies a chloride-responsive (saline-responsive) alkalosis; urine sodium can be misleading in alkalosis, which is why urine chloride is the better marker. Treatment is IV 0.9% sodium chloride with generous potassium replacement, nasogastric decompression, IV proton pump inhibitor, and endoscopy with biopsy once resuscitated.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Gastric outlet obstruction: hypochloraemic hypokalaemic metabolic alkalosis. Low urine chloride = saline-responsive; acidic urine = paradoxical aciduria from volume and K+ depletion. Treat with saline and potassium.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "StatPearls. Gastric Outlet Obstruction (NCBI Bookshelf)", "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK557826/"}
• {"name": "Metabolic alkalosis pathogenesis, diagnosis, and treatment: Core Curriculum 2022. Am J Kidney Dis (PMC)", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10947768/"}

---

