('3808f231-c152-583e-8cde-b38c8c49cb70'::uuid,'ZQ-0152','adult-surgery','general-surgery','A 52-year-old man is brought by ambulance to the emergency department of a hospital in Geelong, Victoria, at 2 am. After a night of heavy drinking at a wedding, he vomited forcefully several times and then developed sudden, severe pain behind the lower sternum radiating to his back and left shoulder. The pain is worse on swallowing and deep breathing. He has not vomited blood. He has no cardiac history and takes no regular medicines.

He is sweaty and distressed. His temperature is 37.8°C, pulse 118/min, blood pressure 106/68 mmHg, respiratory rate 26/min and oxygen saturation 94% on room air. There is crepitus over the left supraclavicular fossa. Breath sounds are reduced at the left base. The upper abdomen is tender with some guarding.

Investigations:
- ECG: sinus tachycardia, no ST-segment changes
- High-sensitivity troponin 6 ng/L (<14)
- Lipase 40 U/L (<60)
- White cell count 16.2 × 10^9/L (4.0–11.0)
- Erect chest X-ray: small left pleural effusion and a thin lucent line along the left heart border; no free subdiaphragmatic gas

IV fluids, analgesia and oxygen have been started and he has been made nil by mouth.

Which one of the following is the most appropriate next investigation?','["Urgent flexible upper gastrointestinal endoscopy", "Barium swallow", "CT pulmonary angiogram", "CT chest and abdomen with oral water-soluble contrast", "Transthoracic echocardiogram"]'::jsonb,'D','CORRECT ANSWER

D. CT chest and abdomen with oral water-soluble contrast

---

Forceful vomiting followed by severe retrosternal pain, subcutaneous emphysema (Mackler''s triad: vomiting, chest pain, subcutaneous emphysema), a left effusion and probable pneumomediastinum on chest X-ray indicate spontaneous oesophageal rupture (Boerhaave syndrome). The typical site is the left posterolateral wall of the distal oesophagus. Mortality rises steeply with delay, so rapid confirmation is essential. The WSES 2019 guideline on oesophageal emergencies recommends CT (ideally with oral water-soluble contrast, i.e. CT oesophagography) as the investigation of choice: it confirms extraluminal gas or contrast leak, shows mediastinal and pleural contamination, and helps plan management. At the same time he needs broad-spectrum IV antibiotics and antifungal cover, nil by mouth, and urgent upper GI/thoracic surgical referral.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Vomiting then chest pain and subcutaneous emphysema = Boerhaave until proven otherwise. Confirm with CT using oral water-soluble contrast; avoid barium and avoid endoscopy as the first test.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "Chirica M et al. Esophageal emergencies: WSES guidelines. World J Emerg Surg 2019;14:26", "url": "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s13017-019-0245-2"}

---

EDITED BY HEISENBERG','difficult','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "bank170", "review_status": "owner_approved_2026_09_29", "tags": ["ZQ-0152", "zyntra_bank", "mock1", "level_4", "task_data_gathering", "group_adult_surgery", "sys_general_surgery", "approved_by_owner_2026_09_29"], "clinician_task": "data_gathering", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"Chirica M et al. Esophageal emergencies: WSES guidelines. World J Emerg Surg 2019;14:26\", \"url\": \"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s13017-019-0245-2\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "Endoscopy can miss small tears and insufflation may enlarge a perforation and worsen mediastinal contamination. It is not the first diagnostic test, although it may be used later by experienced teams (e.g. for stenting).", "B": "Barium causes a severe inflammatory mediastinitis if it leaks. When a contrast swallow is used, a water-soluble agent is used first, and CT has largely replaced fluoroscopic studies.", "C": "A CTPA is timed for the pulmonary arteries and is not designed to detect a leak of oral contrast. The history, crepitus and chest X-ray point to the oesophagus, not pulmonary embolism.", "E": "There is no clinical or ECG evidence of pericardial or cardiac disease, and echocardiography does not assess the oesophagus."}, "key_takeaways": ["Vomiting then chest pain and subcutaneous emphysema = Boerhaave until proven otherwise. Confirm with CT using oral water-soluble contrast; avoid barium and avoid endoscopy as the first test."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,1.0,1.0,'tier_prior','tier=4 (bank170)'),
('728c3c47-7c5f-5501-9b15-787496f20a18'::uuid,'ZQ-0153','adult-surgery','general-surgery','A 29-year-old woman sees her general practitioner in Fremantle, Western Australia, because she found a lump in her left breast in the shower three weeks ago. It is not painful and has not changed with her menstrual cycle. She has no nipple discharge or skin changes. She has never been pregnant and takes a combined oral contraceptive pill. Her maternal aunt had breast cancer at the age of 61. No other relatives have had breast or ovarian cancer, and she has no Ashkenazi Jewish ancestry.

On examination there is a 2 cm smooth, firm, rubbery and very mobile lump in the upper outer quadrant of the left breast. There is no skin tethering, nipple change or axillary or supraclavicular lymphadenopathy. The right breast is normal.

Which one of the following is the most appropriate next investigation?','["Bilateral diagnostic mammography", "Ultrasound of the left breast and axilla", "MRI of both breasts", "Core needle biopsy of the lump before any imaging", "Referral to a familial cancer clinic for BRCA testing"]'::jsonb,'B','CORRECT ANSWER

B. Ultrasound of the left breast and axilla

---

Every new breast lump needs the triple test: clinical examination, imaging and non-surgical biopsy. The lump is likely to be a fibroadenoma, but that cannot be assumed. Cancer Australia''s guide to investigating a new breast symptom recommends ultrasound as the first imaging test for women under 35, because young breast tissue is dense and mammography is less sensitive. Mammography is added in younger women if clinical or ultrasound findings are suspicious or indeterminate. If ultrasound shows a solid lesion, a core biopsy (usually ultrasound-guided) completes the triple test. The triple test is only negative if all three components are benign; any suspicious or indeterminate component needs specialist referral.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
New breast lump: triple test. Under 35, start with ultrasound; add mammography if findings are suspicious or indeterminate.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "Cancer Australia. The investigation of a new breast symptom: a guide for general practitioners", "url": "https://www.canceraustralia.gov.au/sites/default/files/migrated-files/publications/investigation-new-breast-symptom-guide-general-practitioners/pdf/investigation-of-a-new-breast-symptom-a-guide-for-general-practitioners.pdf"}
• {"name": "RACGP. Breast imaging in general practice (AJGP/AFP)", "url": "https://www.racgp.org.au/getattachment/926f9784-b6d8-44bb-8afd-cbeced101935/attachment.aspx"}

---

EDITED BY HEISENBERG','moderate','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "bank170", "review_status": "owner_approved_2026_09_29", "tags": ["ZQ-0153", "zyntra_bank", "mock1", "level_3", "task_data_gathering", "group_adult_surgery", "sys_general_surgery", "approved_by_owner_2026_09_29"], "clinician_task": "data_gathering", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"Cancer Australia. The investigation of a new breast symptom: a guide for general practitioners\", \"url\": \"https://www.canceraustralia.gov.au/sites/default/files/migrated-files/publications/investigation-new-breast-symptom-guide-general-practitioners/pdf/investigation-of-a-new-breast-symptom-a-guide-for-general-practitioners.pdf\"}; {\"name\": \"RACGP. Breast imaging in general practice (AJGP/AFP)\", \"url\": \"https://www.racgp.org.au/getattachment/926f9784-b6d8-44bb-8afd-cbeced101935/attachment.aspx\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "Mammography is less sensitive in dense breast tissue under 35 and exposes her to radiation. It is used in this age group only after suspicious or indeterminate clinical or ultrasound findings.", "C": "MRI is not a first-line test for a palpable lump. It is used for specific indications such as high-risk screening, occult primary or assessing extent of known cancer.", "D": "Biopsy is usually done under imaging guidance after imaging, so that the lesion is characterised and sampled accurately. Blind biopsy before imaging is not recommended.", "E": "One second-degree relative with postmenopausal breast cancer does not put her at high familial risk. Genetic referral does not investigate the lump."}, "key_takeaways": ["New breast lump: triple test. Under 35, start with ultrasound; add mammography if findings are suspicious or indeterminate."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,0.0,1.0,'tier_prior','tier=3 (bank170)'),
('b24a9d3e-411c-5adb-a2da-86914ba2650f'::uuid,'ZQ-0154','adult-surgery','general-surgery','A 31-year-old woman comes to the emergency department of a hospital in Wollongong, New South Wales, five weeks after the vaginal birth of her first baby, whom she is exclusively breastfeeding. Six days ago she developed a painful red area in her right breast with fever. Her GP diagnosed lactational mastitis and started oral flucloxacillin 500 mg four times daily. She has taken it as prescribed for 4 days. The redness has become more localised, but she now has a very tender swelling and her fever has returned. She has no drug allergies.

Her temperature is 38.3°C, pulse 104/min and blood pressure 118/72 mmHg. In the upper outer quadrant of the right breast there is a 3 cm tender, fluctuant swelling with overlying erythema. The skin is intact and not necrotic. There is tender right axillary lymphadenopathy.

Bedside ultrasound shows a 3.2 cm unilocular fluid collection. White cell count is 14.2 × 10^9/L (4.0–11.0).

Which one of the following is the most appropriate management?','["Incision and drainage under general anaesthesia, with breastfeeding stopped on the right side", "Continue oral flucloxacillin alone and review in one week", "Stop breastfeeding and start cabergoline to suppress lactation", "Change to IV vancomycin and avoid any drainage procedure", "Ultrasound-guided needle aspiration, continued antibiotics and continued breastfeeding"]'::jsonb,'E','CORRECT ANSWER

E. Ultrasound-guided needle aspiration, continued antibiotics and continued breastfeeding

---

Worsening localised pain, a fluctuant mass and recurrent fever despite antibiotics suggest a breast abscess, confirmed on ultrasound. First-line treatment is ultrasound-guided needle aspiration (repeated if needed) or small-incision drainage, together with antibiotics active against Staphylococcus aureus, guided by the culture of the aspirate. Aspiration has better cosmetic results than open incision and makes it easier to keep breastfeeding. Australian guidance (RACGP, Australian Breastfeeding Association) is to continue breastfeeding or expressing from the affected breast: effective milk removal helps resolution, and the milk is safe for a healthy term baby. Open incision and drainage is kept for large or multiloculated abscesses, abscesses that fail repeated aspiration, or skin necrosis.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Lactational breast abscess: ultrasound-guided aspiration plus anti-staphylococcal antibiotics, and keep breastfeeding or expressing from the affected side.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "RACGP (AJGP). Lactational mastitis and breast abscess: diagnosis and management in general practice", "url": "https://www.racgp.org.au/getattachment/ccf54e89-7e44-409a-afa9-c14f1bb40639/Lactational-mastitis-and-breast-abscess.aspx"}
• {"name": "Australian Breastfeeding Association. Breast abscess", "url": "https://www.breastfeeding.asn.au/resources/breast-abscess"}

---

EDITED BY HEISENBERG','moderate','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "bank170", "review_status": "owner_approved_2026_09_29", "tags": ["ZQ-0154", "zyntra_bank", "mock1", "level_3", "task_management", "group_adult_surgery", "sys_general_surgery", "approved_by_owner_2026_09_29"], "clinician_task": "management", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"RACGP (AJGP). Lactational mastitis and breast abscess: diagnosis and management in general practice\", \"url\": \"https://www.racgp.org.au/getattachment/ccf54e89-7e44-409a-afa9-c14f1bb40639/Lactational-mastitis-and-breast-abscess.aspx\"}; {\"name\": \"Australian Breastfeeding Association. Breast abscess\", \"url\": \"https://www.breastfeeding.asn.au/resources/breast-abscess\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "Open drainage is not first line for a small unilocular abscess with intact skin, and there is no need to stop breastfeeding from the affected side.", "B": "An established abscess rarely resolves with antibiotics alone. It needs drainage.", "C": "Stopping breastfeeding abruptly worsens milk stasis and is not needed. Lactation suppression is not part of abscess treatment.", "D": "IV antibiotics may be needed if she is systemically unwell or MRSA is suspected, but antibiotics without drainage will not treat the abscess."}, "key_takeaways": ["Lactational breast abscess: ultrasound-guided aspiration plus anti-staphylococcal antibiotics, and keep breastfeeding or expressing from the affected side."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,0.0,1.0,'tier_prior','tier=3 (bank170)'),
('ff8040fa-8c1d-5e29-8630-e7f2d7fb1ffe'::uuid,'ZQ-0155','adult-surgery','vascular-surgery','A 72-year-old woman sees her general practitioner in Toowoomba, Queensland, for the result of her surveillance ultrasound for an infrarenal abdominal aortic aneurysm found incidentally four years ago. She has no abdominal, back or groin pain. She is an ex-smoker (30 pack-years, stopped 6 years ago) with hypertension, hypercholesterolaemia and stage 2 chronic kidney disease. She takes perindopril, atorvastatin and aspirin. She walks 5 km most days, cares for her grandchildren and has no symptoms of heart failure or angina.

Her blood pressure is 128/76 mmHg. There is a pulsatile, non-tender abdominal mass.

Serial maximum anteroposterior aortic diameters (same accredited vascular laboratory):
- 4 years ago: 4.2 cm
- 2 years ago: 4.5 cm
- 12 months ago: 4.8 cm
- This week: 5.1 cm

The report says ''Growth 0.3 cm in 12 months. Recommend clinical correlation.''

Which one of the following is the most appropriate interpretation of this result?','["Below the repair threshold; continue 6-monthly ultrasound until it reaches 5.5 cm", "Growth of 0.3 cm/year is rapid and is the main reason to refer for repair", "At the diameter where elective repair should be considered in a woman; refer to vascular surgery", "Below the repair threshold; repeat the ultrasound in 12 months", "Refer for repair when symptoms develop, whatever the diameter"]'::jsonb,'C','CORRECT ANSWER

C. At the diameter where elective repair should be considered in a woman; refer to vascular surgery

---

Thresholds for elective AAA repair are lower in women. The ESVS 2024 guideline suggests considering elective repair at a maximum diameter of 5.5 cm or more in men and 5.0 cm or more in women (and advises against elective repair below these sizes). Women have smaller aortas, rupture at smaller diameters, and have a higher rupture risk at any given diameter, so applying the male 5.5 cm threshold to her would leave her at unnecessary risk. She is 5.1 cm, asymptomatic, active and apparently fit, so she should be referred to a vascular surgeon to assess for elective repair (CT angiography for anatomy and EVAR suitability, cardiorespiratory and renal assessment, and shared decision-making). The other indication for referral regardless of diameter is growth of 1 cm or more per year, or symptoms (pain or tenderness), which need urgent assessment.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Elective AAA repair is considered at 5.5 cm in men but 5.0 cm in women (ESVS 2024), or with growth of 1 cm/year or more, or symptoms.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "Wanhainen A et al. ESVS 2024 Clinical Practice Guidelines on the Management of Abdominal Aorto-Iliac Artery Aneurysms. Eur J Vasc Endovasc Surg 2024", "url": "https://www.ejves.com/article/S1078-5884(23)00889-4/fulltext"}
• {"name": "Vascular News. ESVS 2024 AAA guidelines: what''s new?", "url": "https://vascularnews.com/esvs-2024-clinical-practice-guidelines-on-the-management-of-abdominal-aortoiliac-artery-aneurysms-whats-new/"}

---

EDITED BY HEISENBERG','difficult','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "bank170", "review_status": "owner_approved_2026_09_29", "tags": ["ZQ-0155", "zyntra_bank", "mock1", "level_5", "task_data_interpretation_synthesis", "group_adult_surgery", "sys_vascular", "approved_by_owner_2026_09_29"], "clinician_task": "data_interpretation_synthesis", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"Wanhainen A et al. ESVS 2024 Clinical Practice Guidelines on the Management of Abdominal Aorto-Iliac Artery Aneurysms. Eur J Vasc Endovasc Surg 2024\", \"url\": \"https://www.ejves.com/article/S1078-5884(23)00889-4/fulltext\"}; {\"name\": \"Vascular News. ESVS 2024 AAA guidelines: what''s new?\", \"url\": \"https://vascularnews.com/esvs-2024-clinical-practice-guidelines-on-the-management-of-abdominal-aortoiliac-artery-aneurysms-whats-new/\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "This applies the male threshold of 5.5 cm. A woman at 5.1 cm has already reached the size at which repair should be considered.", "B": "Growth of 0.3 cm in a year is not rapid. The growth-based referral trigger is 1 cm or more per year. The reason to refer is her diameter, given her sex.", "D": "Annual scans suit small aneurysms (around 3.0–3.9 cm) in most schedules. At 5.1 cm in a woman, she needs referral, not routine surveillance.", "E": "Symptoms (pain, tenderness) mean urgent assessment for possible impending rupture. But elective repair of asymptomatic aneurysms is based on diameter and growth, not symptoms."}, "key_takeaways": ["Elective AAA repair is considered at 5.5 cm in men but 5.0 cm in women (ESVS 2024), or with growth of 1 cm/year or more, or symptoms."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,2.0,1.0,'tier_prior','tier=5 (bank170)'),
('f2d49a54-5240-56eb-8e09-f8878fab86e9'::uuid,'ZQ-0156','adult-surgery','vascular-surgery','A 61-year-old Aboriginal man attends an Aboriginal Community Controlled Health Service in Mparntwe (Alice Springs), Northern Territory, with the Aboriginal Health Practitioner who has been dressing his foot. He has had an ulcer on the tip of his left great toe for six weeks that is not healing despite offloading footwear and regular dressings. He has had type 2 diabetes for 18 years (HbA1c 8.6% [70 mmol/mol]), stage 4 chronic kidney disease, and peripheral neuropathy. He smoked until 3 years ago. He walks only short distances because of knee pain and has not noticed calf pain.

The ulcer is 1 cm across with a pale, sloughy base and no probe-to-bone contact. There is no spreading erythema, warmth or discharge. His feet are cool with shiny, hairless skin. Dorsalis pedis and posterior tibial pulses cannot be felt on either side. Monofilament sensation is absent to mid-shin.

A handheld Doppler shows monophasic signals at both left ankle arteries. The cuff could not compress the ankle arteries at 240 mmHg, and the ankle-brachial index is recorded as 1.45 on the left.

Which one of the following is the most appropriate next investigation to assess perfusion of his left foot?','["Repeat ankle-brachial index in 3 months", "Toe systolic pressure and toe-brachial index", "Digital subtraction angiography of the left leg", "Treadmill exercise ankle-brachial index", "Plain X-ray of the left foot"]'::jsonb,'B','CORRECT ANSWER

B. Toe systolic pressure and toe-brachial index

---

In diabetes and CKD, medial arterial calcification makes ankle arteries incompressible and gives a falsely high or normal ankle-brachial index (ABI). An ABI above 1.3–1.4 (or incompressible arteries) is not reassuring. His non-healing ulcer, absent pulses and monophasic Doppler signals strongly suggest peripheral artery disease. The 2021 Australian evidence-based guideline on PAD in diabetes-related foot disease recommends combining pedal Doppler waveforms with ABI AND toe-brachial index (TBI), as no single bedside test excludes PAD. Digital arteries are usually spared from calcification, so toe pressure and TBI are more reliable here. A TBI below 0.70 suggests PAD, and a low toe pressure (e.g. below about 30 mmHg) predicts poor healing and supports urgent vascular imaging and referral for revascularisation. In this setting, telehealth and outreach vascular review with the ACCHS team, and planning around renal care and travel to Adelaide or Darwin, are important to culturally safe care.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
In diabetes or CKD, an ABI above ~1.3 or incompressible arteries is unreliable. Use pedal Doppler waveforms plus toe pressure and toe-brachial index (TBI <0.70 suggests PAD).

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "Chuter V et al. Australian guideline on diagnosis and management of peripheral artery disease: part of the 2021 Australian evidence-based guidelines for diabetes-related foot disease (J Foot Ankle Res 2022)", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9254685/"}

---

EDITED BY HEISENBERG','difficult','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "bank170", "review_status": "owner_approved_2026_09_29", "tags": ["ZQ-0156", "zyntra_bank", "mock1", "level_4", "task_data_gathering", "group_adult_surgery", "sys_vascular", "approved_by_owner_2026_09_29"], "clinician_task": "data_gathering", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"Chuter V et al. Australian guideline on diagnosis and management of peripheral artery disease: part of the 2021 Australian evidence-based guidelines for diabetes-related foot disease (J Foot Ankle Res 2022)\", \"url\": \"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9254685/\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "The ABI is invalid here because the arteries are incompressible. Repeating it will not help, and a 3-month delay risks tissue loss.", "C": "Angiography may be needed to plan revascularisation, but it is invasive, uses contrast in a man with advanced CKD, and is not the next bedside test to establish perfusion.", "D": "Exercise ABI helps when resting ABI is normal in someone with claudication. It still depends on compressible ankle arteries, and he cannot walk far.", "E": "An X-ray looks for osteomyelitis, gas or foreign body. The question asks about perfusion, and the ulcer does not probe to bone."}, "key_takeaways": ["In diabetes or CKD, an ABI above ~1.3 or incompressible arteries is unreliable. Use pedal Doppler waveforms plus toe pressure and toe-brachial index (TBI <0.70 suggests PAD)."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,1.0,1.0,'tier_prior','tier=4 (bank170)'),
('107a7abc-5f54-574b-9f10-a73502dfebf9'::uuid,'ZQ-0157','adult-surgery','vascular-surgery','A 68-year-old man sees his general practitioner in Orange, New South Wales, four days after an episode in which ''a curtain came down'' over the vision of his left eye for about five minutes and then fully cleared. He had no weakness, speech disturbance or headache. He has hypertension, type 2 diabetes and hypercholesterolaemia, and smokes 10 cigarettes a day. He was already taking aspirin 100 mg, atorvastatin 40 mg, metformin and ramipril.

The GP arranged same-week investigations through the local TIA pathway. His blood pressure is 142/84 mmHg and pulse 72/min and regular. Neurological and fundal examination are normal.

Investigations:
- ECG and 24-hour Holter: sinus rhythm throughout
- MRI brain: no infarct
- Carotid duplex ultrasound: 80–99% stenosis of the left internal carotid artery; less than 50% on the right
- CT angiography: confirms about 85% stenosis at the origin of the left internal carotid artery, with no tandem lesion

He is independent, walks daily and has no symptoms of angina or heart failure. He is keen to reduce his risk of stroke.

In addition to optimising medical therapy and smoking cessation, which one of the following is the most appropriate next step?','["Continue medical therapy alone and repeat carotid duplex in 6 months", "Replace aspirin with warfarin", "Elective carotid endarterectomy in 3 months once risk factors are controlled", "Carotid artery stenting as the first-choice procedure", "Urgent vascular referral for carotid endarterectomy, ideally within 2 weeks of symptoms"]'::jsonb,'E','CORRECT ANSWER

E. Urgent vascular referral for carotid endarterectomy, ideally within 2 weeks of symptoms

---

Amaurosis fugax is a retinal TIA and counts as a symptomatic event in the territory of the left internal carotid artery. He has a symptomatic 70–99% stenosis. The Australian and New Zealand Living Clinical Guidelines for Stroke Management recommend carotid endarterectomy for recent non-disabling stroke or TIA with ipsilateral 70–99% stenosis, performed as soon as possible and ideally within 2 weeks of the event, by a surgical team with audited low complication rates. The benefit of surgery is greatest in the first two weeks, when the risk of recurrent stroke is highest, and falls away quickly with delay. Medical therapy (antiplatelet, high-intensity statin, blood pressure and diabetes control, stopping smoking) is continued alongside surgery.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Symptomatic (including amaurosis fugax) 70–99% carotid stenosis: carotid endarterectomy, ideally within 2 weeks of the event, plus best medical therapy.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "Stroke Foundation. Australian and New Zealand Living Clinical Guidelines for Stroke Management, Chapter 4 Secondary prevention (MAGICapp)", "url": "https://files.magicapp.org/guideline/4663aeab-b6f8-4d90-8063-0a3073ef90ed/published_guideline_7904-10_0.pdf"}
• {"name": "Stroke Foundation InformMe: Living Clinical Guidelines for Stroke Management", "url": "https://informme.org.au/guidelines/living-clinical-guidelines-for-stroke-management"}

---

EDITED BY HEISENBERG','moderate','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "bank170", "review_status": "owner_approved_2026_09_29", "tags": ["ZQ-0157", "zyntra_bank", "mock1", "level_3", "task_management", "group_adult_surgery", "sys_vascular", "approved_by_owner_2026_09_29"], "clinician_task": "management", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"Stroke Foundation. Australian and New Zealand Living Clinical Guidelines for Stroke Management, Chapter 4 Secondary prevention (MAGICapp)\", \"url\": \"https://files.magicapp.org/guideline/4663aeab-b6f8-4d90-8063-0a3073ef90ed/published_guideline_7904-10_0.pdf\"}; {\"name\": \"Stroke Foundation InformMe: Living Clinical Guidelines for Stroke Management\", \"url\": \"https://informme.org.au/guidelines/living-clinical-guidelines-for-stroke-management\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "Medical therapy alone is not enough for symptomatic 70–99% stenosis in a fit patient. Surveillance suits asymptomatic disease.", "B": "Anticoagulation is for cardioembolic sources such as AF. He is in sinus rhythm, and warfarin does not treat carotid atherosclerosis.", "C": "Delaying surgery for 3 months wastes most of its benefit, because recurrent stroke risk is concentrated in the first weeks after the event.", "D": "Stenting is an alternative in selected patients (e.g. hostile neck, surgically inaccessible lesion, high surgical risk), but endarterectomy is preferred for most symptomatic patients, particularly those over 70, because of a lower periprocedural stroke risk."}, "key_takeaways": ["Symptomatic (including amaurosis fugax) 70–99% carotid stenosis: carotid endarterectomy, ideally within 2 weeks of the event, plus best medical therapy."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,0.0,1.0,'tier_prior','tier=3 (bank170)'),
('038dfbdd-1afa-5432-9502-2798393f3e96'::uuid,'ZQ-0158','adult-surgery','urology','A 19-year-old man comes to the emergency department of Dubbo Base Hospital, New South Wales, at 11 pm. Three hours ago he was woken by sudden, severe pain in his left testicle, which has spread to the left lower abdomen. He has vomited twice. He has no urinary symptoms, urethral discharge or recent trauma, and he has not been sexually active for six months. He had a similar but brief episode of pain a month ago that settled on its own.

His temperature is 37.1°C, pulse 104/min and blood pressure 132/78 mmHg. The left testis is swollen, exquisitely tender, high-riding and lies horizontally. The left cremasteric reflex is absent. Lifting the testis does not relieve the pain. The right testis is normal. Urinalysis shows no leucocytes or nitrites.

The on-call general surgeon is in the hospital and an operating theatre is available. Formal ultrasound is not available until the morning.

Which one of the following is the most appropriate next step?','["Immediate scrotal exploration in theatre", "Transfer to a tertiary centre for urgent colour Doppler ultrasound", "Manual detorsion in the emergency department and discharge if pain settles", "Ceftriaxone and doxycycline for epididymo-orchitis, with urine NAAT", "Admit for analgesia and formal ultrasound in the morning"]'::jsonb,'A','CORRECT ANSWER

A. Immediate scrotal exploration in theatre

---

Sudden severe testicular pain with vomiting, a high-riding horizontal testis, an absent cremasteric reflex and a previous self-resolving episode (suggesting intermittent torsion) make torsion highly likely. Testicular salvage depends on time: it is high if detorted within about 6 hours and falls sharply after that. The RACS 2022 guideline on acute scrotal pain and suspected testicular torsion states that suspected torsion is a surgical emergency and that imaging should not delay exploration, because ultrasound can be falsely reassuring. The surgeon on site should explore the scrotum immediately, detort, assess viability, and fix the testis (orchidopexy), with fixation of the contralateral testis because the ''bell-clapper'' anomaly is often bilateral. A non-viable testis is removed.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Suspected testicular torsion is a clinical diagnosis: explore immediately. Do not let ultrasound or transfer delay surgery when a surgeon can operate.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "Royal Australasian College of Surgeons. Acute scrotal pain and suspected testicular torsion guidelines (2022)", "url": "https://www.surgeons.org/about-racs/position-papers/acute-scrotal-pain-and-suspected-testicular-torsion-guidelines-2022"}
• {"name": "NSW Agency for Clinical Innovation (ECI). Acute scrotum", "url": "https://aci.health.nsw.gov.au/networks/eci/clinical/tools/acute-scrotum"}

---

EDITED BY HEISENBERG','moderate','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "bank170", "review_status": "owner_approved_2026_09_29", "tags": ["ZQ-0158", "zyntra_bank", "mock1", "level_3", "task_management", "group_adult_surgery", "sys_urology", "approved_by_owner_2026_09_29"], "clinician_task": "management", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"Royal Australasian College of Surgeons. Acute scrotal pain and suspected testicular torsion guidelines (2022)\", \"url\": \"https://www.surgeons.org/about-racs/position-papers/acute-scrotal-pain-and-suspected-testicular-torsion-guidelines-2022\"}; {\"name\": \"NSW Agency for Clinical Innovation (ECI). Acute scrotum\", \"url\": \"https://aci.health.nsw.gov.au/networks/eci/clinical/tools/acute-scrotum\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"B": "Transfer for imaging would add hours and risk losing the testis. Doppler ultrasound can miss partial or intermittent torsion and should not delay surgery when the clinical picture is typical.", "C": "Manual detorsion can be a temporising measure when surgery will be delayed, but it is not definitive. He still needs exploration and fixation, and residual torsion is common.", "D": "Epididymo-orchitis is less likely: onset is sudden, urinalysis is normal and there is no recent sexual exposure. Treating it here risks missing torsion.", "E": "Waiting until morning would take him well beyond the 6-hour window for salvage."}, "key_takeaways": ["Suspected testicular torsion is a clinical diagnosis: explore immediately. Do not let ultrasound or transfer delay surgery when a surgeon can operate."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,0.0,1.0,'tier_prior','tier=3 (bank170)'),
('087e2845-afb5-5ac2-b742-3f3eed8ac28a'::uuid,'ZQ-0159','adult-surgery','urology','A 67-year-old man sees his general practitioner in Mandurah, Western Australia, because he has twice noticed red urine over the past fortnight, each time lasting a day. He has no dysuria, frequency, loin pain or fever. He has no recent trauma or vigorous exercise. He smoked 25 cigarettes a day for 40 years and stopped 5 years ago. He worked as a painter and decorator. He has hypertension treated with amlodipine and takes no anticoagulant or antiplatelet medicine.

His blood pressure is 138/82 mmHg. Abdominal and genital examination is normal. Digital rectal examination shows a smooth, mildly enlarged prostate.

Investigations:
- Urine dipstick today: blood 2+, protein trace, leucocytes negative, nitrites negative
- Urine microscopy: red cells 150 × 10^6/L (<13), no casts or dysmorphic red cells; culture: no growth
- Creatinine 84 µmol/L (60–110), eGFR 82 mL/min/1.73 m²
- Full blood count normal

Which one of the following is the most appropriate next step in investigation?','["Repeat urinalysis in 3 months and investigate if haematuria persists", "Urine cytology alone", "Serum prostate-specific antigen and review", "CT urogram and referral for flexible cystoscopy", "Renal tract ultrasound alone"]'::jsonb,'D','CORRECT ANSWER

D. CT urogram and referral for flexible cystoscopy

---

Painless visible (macroscopic) haematuria in an adult, once infection is excluded, needs investigation for urothelial and renal cancer, even if it is intermittent. His risk is high: age over 60, a heavy smoking history, and occupational exposure (painters are exposed to aromatic amines and solvents). Standard practice in Australia, following guidelines endorsed by USANZ (such as the EAU guidelines) and the Cancer Council optimal care pathway for bladder cancer, is to evaluate both the upper tract and the bladder: CT urography (the most sensitive imaging test for upper tract urothelial cancer and renal masses) and cystoscopy (the standard for detecting bladder cancer, which imaging can miss). Refer to urology. Glomerular causes are unlikely: renal function is normal and there are no casts, dysmorphic red cells or significant proteinuria.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Painless visible haematuria in a high-risk adult (age, smoking, occupation): CT urogram plus cystoscopy. Intermittent bleeding is not reassuring.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "Urological Society of Australia and New Zealand (USANZ). Position statements and guidelines", "url": "https://usanz.org.au/info-resources/position-statements-guidelines"}
• {"name": "RACGP (AFP/AJGP). Macroscopic haematuria", "url": "https://www.racgp.org.au/getattachment/2713edc1-efd1-4a74-b9d1-6e8963e86f3d/Macroscopic-haematuria.aspx"}

---

EDITED BY HEISENBERG','moderate','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "bank170", "review_status": "owner_approved_2026_09_29", "tags": ["ZQ-0159", "zyntra_bank", "mock1", "level_3", "task_data_gathering", "group_adult_surgery", "sys_urology", "approved_by_owner_2026_09_29"], "clinician_task": "data_gathering", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"Urological Society of Australia and New Zealand (USANZ). Position statements and guidelines\", \"url\": \"https://usanz.org.au/info-resources/position-statements-guidelines\"}; {\"name\": \"RACGP (AFP/AJGP). Macroscopic haematuria\", \"url\": \"https://www.racgp.org.au/getattachment/2713edc1-efd1-4a74-b9d1-6e8963e86f3d/Macroscopic-haematuria.aspx\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "Visible haematuria that stops is still significant. Waiting 3 months could delay diagnosis of bladder cancer.", "B": "Cytology has poor sensitivity for low-grade urothelial tumours. It can complement but not replace cystoscopy and imaging.", "C": "A mildly enlarged prostate can bleed, but BPH is a diagnosis of exclusion in visible haematuria. PSA does not evaluate the urinary tract for urothelial cancer.", "E": "Ultrasound may be used in lower-risk patients or when CT contrast is contraindicated, but it misses small upper tract urothelial tumours, and on its own it leaves out cystoscopy."}, "key_takeaways": ["Painless visible haematuria in a high-risk adult (age, smoking, occupation): CT urogram plus cystoscopy. Intermittent bleeding is not reassuring."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,0.0,1.0,'tier_prior','tier=3 (bank170)'),
('fc9b647e-267d-5035-b7db-43f8011b86e7'::uuid,'ZQ-0160','adult-surgery','urology','A 74-year-old man is admitted under urology at a hospital in Rockhampton, Queensland, with increasing confusion, lower abdominal distension and dribbling incontinence for several weeks. He has benign prostatic hyperplasia but stopped his tamsulosin a year ago. A urethral catheter was inserted in the emergency department at 1400 and drained 1.8 L of urine. His creatinine was 312 µmol/L (baseline 95 µmol/L six months ago), and renal ultrasound showed bilateral hydronephrosis with a thick-walled bladder.

He is now more alert and drinking to thirst. He has had no IV fluids and no diuretics. His hourly urine output since the initial drainage has been:
- 1500–1600: 320 mL
- 1600–1700: 350 mL
- 1700–1800: 310 mL
- 1800–1900: 290 mL

At 1900 his pulse is 90/min, blood pressure 124/74 mmHg lying and 112/68 mmHg sitting, and his mucous membranes are slightly dry. The urine is clear.

Investigations at 1900:
- Sodium 142 mmol/L (135–145)
- Potassium 4.8 mmol/L (3.5–5.2)
- Creatinine 268 µmol/L
- Glucose 6.2 mmol/L
- Urine osmolality 290 mOsm/kg

Which one of the following is the most appropriate interpretation and plan?','["Central diabetes insipidus; give desmopressin and restrict fluids", "Hyperglycaemic osmotic diuresis; start an insulin infusion", "Post-obstructive diuresis; monitor closely and replace part of the output", "Post-obstructive diuresis; replace every millilitre of urine output with IV 0.9% sodium chloride", "Normal physiological response; no monitoring beyond standard ward observations is needed"]'::jsonb,'C','CORRECT ANSWER

C. Post-obstructive diuresis; monitor closely and replace part of the output

---

Post-obstructive diuresis is usually defined as urine output above 200 mL/h for 2 consecutive hours, or more than 3 L in 24 hours, after relief of obstruction. It is most common after relief of high-pressure chronic retention with renal impairment, as here. It has a physiological component (excretion of retained urea, salt and water) and sometimes a pathological component (a tubular concentrating defect), which can cause dehydration, hypotension and electrolyte disturbance. His near-isotonic urine (290 mOsm/kg), normal glucose and high-normal sodium fit this picture, and he is starting to show a postural drop. Management: hourly urine output and fluid balance, daily or twice-daily weights, 12-hourly (or more frequent) electrolytes, encourage oral intake if he can, and if IV fluids are needed replace a proportion (commonly about 50–75%) of the previous hour''s output, rather than chasing the output mL for mL, which can perpetuate the diuresis. The catheter should not be clamped.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
After relieving high-pressure chronic retention, >200 mL/h for 2 hours = post-obstructive diuresis. Monitor closely; replace part of the output (not mL for mL); do not clamp the catheter.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "StatPearls. Postobstructive Diuresis (NCBI Bookshelf)", "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK459387/"}
• {"name": "LITFL. Post-obstructive diuresis", "url": "https://litfl.com/post-obstructive-diuresis/"}

---

EDITED BY HEISENBERG','difficult','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "bank170", "review_status": "owner_approved_2026_09_29", "tags": ["ZQ-0160", "zyntra_bank", "mock1", "level_4", "task_data_interpretation_synthesis", "group_adult_surgery", "sys_urology", "approved_by_owner_2026_09_29"], "clinician_task": "data_interpretation_synthesis", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"StatPearls. Postobstructive Diuresis (NCBI Bookshelf)\", \"url\": \"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK459387/\"}; {\"name\": \"LITFL. Post-obstructive diuresis\", \"url\": \"https://litfl.com/post-obstructive-diuresis/\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "Central diabetes insipidus produces dilute urine (low osmolality) with a high sodium. His urine is near-isotonic and there is an obvious cause for the diuresis. Desmopressin and fluid restriction could cause harm.", "B": "His glucose is 6.2 mmol/L, which cannot drive an osmotic diuresis.", "D": "Replacing all output tends to keep the diuresis going and can lead to fluid and sodium overload. Partial replacement, guided by haemodynamics and electrolytes, is preferred.", "E": "Part of the diuresis is physiological, but he already has a postural drop and dry mucous membranes. He needs close monitoring of fluid balance and electrolytes."}, "key_takeaways": ["After relieving high-pressure chronic retention, >200 mL/h for 2 hours = post-obstructive diuresis. Monitor closely; replace part of the output (not mL for mL); do not clamp the catheter."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,1.0,1.0,'tier_prior','tier=4 (bank170)'),
('2341176e-8a52-5a0d-b8ba-6176aff695a0'::uuid,'ZQ-0161','adult-surgery','orthopaedics','A 44-year-old man comes to a general practice in Canberra the morning after playing indoor netball. As he pushed off to run, he felt as if someone had kicked him hard in the back of his right lower leg, and he heard a snap. He could not continue playing and has been walking with a limp since. He has no previous injury to this leg. He had a course of ciprofloxacin for prostatitis two months ago.

He walks with a flat-footed gait and cannot stand on tiptoe on the right. Lying prone, he can plantarflex his right ankle against gravity, although more weakly than on the left. There is swelling and tenderness about 5 cm above the right heel. A gap is difficult to feel because of swelling. With both knees flexed to 90 degrees, the right foot hangs in slightly more dorsiflexion than the left. The ankle is not tender over the malleoli, navicular or base of the fifth metatarsal.

Which one of the following examination findings would most strongly support the suspected diagnosis?','["Active plantarflexion against resistance is preserved", "Positive anterior drawer test of the right ankle", "Pain when the Ottawa ankle criteria are applied", "Tenderness over the posterior edge of the lateral malleolus", "No plantarflexion of the foot when the calf is squeezed"]'::jsonb,'E','CORRECT ANSWER

E. No plantarflexion of the foot when the calf is squeezed

---

The history (sudden ''kick'' or snap on push-off, a middle-aged recreational athlete, recent fluoroquinolone use) strongly suggests an acute Achilles tendon rupture. The Simmonds (Thompson) calf squeeze test is the key clinical test: with the patient prone and the feet over the end of the bed, squeezing the calf normally causes passive plantarflexion. No movement indicates a complete rupture. Other supportive findings are an increased resting angle of dorsiflexion with knees flexed (a lost ''angle of declination'', as here) and a palpable gap. Diagnosis is clinical; ultrasound is used if in doubt. Many Achilles ruptures are missed because the patient can still actively plantarflex using tibialis posterior, the long toe flexors and the peroneal muscles.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Suspected Achilles rupture: perform the Simmonds (Thompson) calf squeeze test and check the angle of declination. Preserved active plantarflexion does not exclude rupture.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "British Orthopaedic Foot & Ankle Society (BOFAS) Hyperbook: Achilles tendon acute rupture", "url": "https://www.bofas.org.uk/hyperbook/trauma/achilles-tendon-acute-rupture"}
• {"name": "Achilles tendon rupture: how to avoid missing the diagnosis. Br J Gen Pract 2015;65:668", "url": "https://bjgp.org/content/65/641/668"}

