EDITED BY HEISENBERG','moderate','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "bank170", "review_status": "owner_approved_2026_09_29", "tags": ["ZQ-0161", "zyntra_bank", "mock1", "level_3", "task_data_gathering", "group_adult_surgery", "sys_orthopaedics", "approved_by_owner_2026_09_29"], "clinician_task": "data_gathering", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"British Orthopaedic Foot & Ankle Society (BOFAS) Hyperbook: Achilles tendon acute rupture\", \"url\": \"https://www.bofas.org.uk/hyperbook/trauma/achilles-tendon-acute-rupture\"}; {\"name\": \"Achilles tendon rupture: how to avoid missing the diagnosis. Br J Gen Pract 2015;65:668\", \"url\": \"https://bjgp.org/content/65/641/668\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "Preserved active plantarflexion is a classic reason for missing the diagnosis. The other plantarflexors can still move the ankle, so this does not exclude rupture.", "B": "The anterior drawer test assesses the anterior talofibular ligament in lateral ankle sprain. It does not assess the Achilles tendon.", "C": "The Ottawa ankle rules decide whether an ankle X-ray is needed to exclude fracture. They are not designed to diagnose tendon rupture.", "D": "Tenderness at the posterior edge of the lateral malleolus is an Ottawa criterion for fracture. His tenderness is 5 cm above the heel, over the tendon."}, "key_takeaways": ["Suspected Achilles rupture: perform the Simmonds (Thompson) calf squeeze test and check the angle of declination. Preserved active plantarflexion does not exclude rupture."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,0.0,1.0,'tier_prior','tier=3 (bank170)'),
('4bf33957-6ba2-5392-a8f4-f76fd898bc76'::uuid,'ZQ-0162','adult-surgery','orthopaedics','A 23-year-old man is on the orthopaedic ward of a hospital in Newcastle, New South Wales, eight hours after admission with a closed, displaced fracture of the right tibial shaft from a motorbike crash. His leg was placed in an above-knee plaster backslab, elevated on two pillows, and he is waiting for intramedullary nailing on tomorrow''s trauma list. He has a morphine patient-controlled analgesia pump.

The nurse calls you because his pain has become much worse over the past two hours despite frequent PCA use. He says his leg feels ''tight'' and ''like it''s bursting''. His pulse is 108/min and blood pressure 126/70 mmHg. When the bandage over the backslab is loosened, the anterior leg is tense and swollen. Passive plantarflexion of the toes causes severe pain. He reports reduced sensation between the first and second toes. The dorsalis pedis and posterior tibial pulses are palpable and capillary refill in the toes is 2 seconds.

The orthopaedic registrar measures anterior compartment pressure at 52 mmHg.

Which one of the following is the most appropriate management?','["Raise the leg higher to reduce swelling and reassess in one hour", "Increase the PCA bolus dose and repeat the compartment pressure in two hours", "Split dressings to skin and arrange emergency four-compartment fasciotomy", "Arrange urgent CT angiography of the right leg", "Proceed to intramedullary nailing first, then reassess compartments afterwards"]'::jsonb,'C','CORRECT ANSWER

C. Split dressings to skin and arrange emergency four-compartment fasciotomy

---

He has acute compartment syndrome: escalating pain out of proportion to the injury despite opioids, pain on passive stretch of the anterior compartment muscles, a tense compartment and early deep peroneal nerve dysfunction (first web-space numbness). The measured pressure supports it: his delta pressure (diastolic BP minus compartment pressure) is 70 − 52 = 18 mmHg, and a delta below 30 mmHg indicates inadequate perfusion. An absolute pressure above 40 mmHg is also concerning. Palpable pulses do NOT exclude compartment syndrome: arterial pressure far exceeds compartment pressure until very late. The BOAST guideline (2025) says that once compartment syndrome is diagnosed, all circumferential dressings and casts must be split to skin, the limb kept at heart level, and decompression by fasciotomy performed as an emergency (target within 1 hour of the decision). For the leg, this means two-incision fasciotomy of all four compartments.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Compartment syndrome is clinical: pain out of proportion and on passive stretch. Pulses are usually present. Delta pressure <30 mmHg supports it. Split dressings to skin, limb at heart level, emergency fasciotomy.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "British Orthopaedic Association. BOAST: Diagnosis and management of compartment syndrome of the limbs (September 2025)", "url": "https://www.boa.ac.uk/static/d0432d40-5d99-4b64-9952f6eb1b60c166/BOASTCompartmentSyndromeSeptember-2025.pdf"}

---

EDITED BY HEISENBERG','difficult','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "bank170", "review_status": "owner_approved_2026_09_29", "tags": ["ZQ-0162", "zyntra_bank", "mock1", "level_4", "task_management", "group_adult_surgery", "sys_orthopaedics", "approved_by_owner_2026_09_29"], "clinician_task": "management", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"British Orthopaedic Association. BOAST: Diagnosis and management of compartment syndrome of the limbs (September 2025)\", \"url\": \"https://www.boa.ac.uk/static/d0432d40-5d99-4b64-9952f6eb1b60c166/BOASTCompartmentSyndromeSeptember-2025.pdf\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "Elevating the limb above heart level lowers arterial inflow pressure and worsens compartment perfusion. The limb should be kept at heart level.", "B": "Increasing analgesia masks the main symptom, and waiting two hours with established compartment syndrome risks irreversible muscle and nerve necrosis.", "D": "The pulses and capillary refill are normal, so there is no sign of an arterial injury. Imaging would delay decompression.", "E": "Fracture fixation is done at the same operation, but fasciotomy is the priority. Nailing first without decompression would worsen compartment pressure."}, "key_takeaways": ["Compartment syndrome is clinical: pain out of proportion and on passive stretch. Pulses are usually present. Delta pressure <30 mmHg supports it. Split dressings to skin, limb at heart level, emergency fasciotomy."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,1.0,1.0,'tier_prior','tier=4 (bank170)'),
('f91a727e-13e2-583f-88f2-eec3458e46f7'::uuid,'ZQ-0163','adult-surgery','orthopaedics','A 42-year-old woman comes to the emergency department of Townsville University Hospital, Queensland, with worsening low back pain and pain down both legs for three days. She has had intermittent back pain for years, and an MRI two years ago showed an L4/5 disc bulge. Since yesterday she has noticed numbness ''down below'' when she wipes after going to the toilet, and she has had to strain to pass urine this morning. She has no fever, weight loss, cancer history or injecting drug use.

Her temperature is 36.8°C. Straight leg raising is limited to 40 degrees on both sides. Ankle reflexes are reduced bilaterally. Power is 5/5 except for 4/5 plantarflexion on the left. There is reduced pinprick sensation over the perianal area and the posterior upper thighs. A bladder scan after she tries to void shows a residual volume of 420 mL.

Which one of the following is the most appropriate next investigation?','["Emergency MRI of the lumbosacral spine", "CT of the lumbar spine without contrast", "Plain X-ray of the lumbar spine", "Urgent outpatient neurosurgical review with MRI within 2 weeks", "Nerve conduction studies and electromyography"]'::jsonb,'A','CORRECT ANSWER

A. Emergency MRI of the lumbosacral spine

---

She has red flags for cauda equina syndrome (CES): bilateral sciatica, saddle (perianal) numbness, new urinary hesitancy and a raised post-void residual volume, on a background of a known L4/5 disc. She has incomplete CES with retention developing (CES-I/early CES-R). Outcomes, especially bladder and sexual function, depend on decompression before the syndrome is complete. The investigation is an emergency MRI of the lumbosacral spine. Pathways such as the national suspected CES pathway (GIRFT, England) set a target of MRI within about 4 hours of request, at the presenting hospital, without waiting for a spinal surgical opinion first. If MRI confirms compression, she needs urgent referral to spinal surgery for decompression.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Bilateral sciatica, saddle anaesthesia and bladder symptoms or a raised post-void residual = suspected cauda equina: emergency MRI the same day, then urgent spinal decompression.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "GIRFT (NHS England). National Suspected Cauda Equina Syndrome Pathway (updated March 2026)", "url": "https://gettingitrightfirsttime.co.uk/wp-content/uploads/2026/04/National-Suspected-Cauda-Equina-Pathway-March-2026.pdf"}

---

EDITED BY HEISENBERG','moderate','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "bank170", "review_status": "owner_approved_2026_09_29", "tags": ["ZQ-0163", "zyntra_bank", "mock1", "level_3", "task_data_gathering", "group_adult_surgery", "sys_orthopaedics", "approved_by_owner_2026_09_29"], "clinician_task": "data_gathering", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"GIRFT (NHS England). National Suspected Cauda Equina Syndrome Pathway (updated March 2026)\", \"url\": \"https://gettingitrightfirsttime.co.uk/wp-content/uploads/2026/04/National-Suspected-Cauda-Equina-Pathway-March-2026.pdf\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"B": "CT is less sensitive for disc herniation and neural compression. It is used only when MRI is contraindicated or truly unavailable, often with myelography.", "C": "X-rays do not show the disc or cauda equina and would only delay diagnosis.", "D": "Suspected CES needs emergency imaging. A 2-week delay risks permanent bladder, bowel and sexual dysfunction.", "E": "Neurophysiology is not used to diagnose acute cord or cauda equina compression and would delay treatment."}, "key_takeaways": ["Bilateral sciatica, saddle anaesthesia and bladder symptoms or a raised post-void residual = suspected cauda equina: emergency MRI the same day, then urgent spinal decompression."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,0.0,1.0,'tier_prior','tier=3 (bank170)'),
('3285cf96-7f44-55c9-bdc1-163a42c3ad47'::uuid,'ZQ-0164','adult-surgery','orthopaedics','A 24-year-old man is on the orthopaedic ward of Royal Hobart Hospital, Tasmania, 30 hours after a motorbike crash in which he sustained a closed, displaced fracture of the left femoral shaft. He is in skin traction awaiting intramedullary nailing, which was postponed because of emergency theatre demand. He has had no other injuries, no surgery and no blood transfusion. He is taking oxycodone for pain and enoxaparin prophylaxis was started 12 hours ago.

The nurse calls because he has become restless and confused over the past 2 hours. His temperature is 38.3°C, pulse 128/min, blood pressure 118/70 mmHg, respiratory rate 32/min and oxygen saturation 85% on room air, rising to 93% on 15 L/min by non-rebreather mask. His GCS is 13 (E3 V4 M6), with no focal neurological signs and pupils 4 mm, equal and reactive. There are fine petechiae in both axillae, across the upper chest and in the conjunctivae. Chest auscultation reveals scattered fine crackles.

Investigations:
- Haemoglobin 108 g/L (admission 139 g/L)
- Platelets 92 × 10^9/L (admission 240)
- White cell count 13.1 × 10^9/L
- Arterial blood gas on oxygen: pH 7.47, pCO2 30 mmHg, pO2 68 mmHg
- Chest X-ray: bilateral diffuse patchy opacities
- CT brain: normal
- CT pulmonary angiogram: no filling defects; diffuse bilateral ground-glass opacities

Which one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?','["Pulmonary thromboembolism", "Fat embolism syndrome", "Hospital-acquired pneumonia with sepsis", "Transfusion-related acute lung injury", "Opioid-induced respiratory depression"]'::jsonb,'B','CORRECT ANSWER

B. Fat embolism syndrome

---

Fat embolism syndrome (FES) typically appears 24–72 hours after a long bone (especially femoral) or pelvic fracture, more often when fixation is delayed. The classic triad is respiratory insufficiency (hypoxaemia, diffuse bilateral opacities), cerebral involvement (confusion, reduced GCS) and a petechial rash in non-dependent areas (axillae, upper chest, conjunctivae, oral mucosa), which is the most specific sign. He meets Gurd and Wilson''s criteria: major criteria (petechiae, respiratory insufficiency, cerebral involvement) and minor criteria (tachycardia, fever, unexplained fall in haemoglobin, thrombocytopenia). A CTPA without filling defects and a normal CT brain support the diagnosis (MRI brain may show a ''starfield'' pattern of small lesions). Treatment is supportive: oxygen and ventilatory support as needed, ICU review, and early fracture stabilisation, which also reduces the risk of FES.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
24–72 hours after a femoral fracture: hypoxaemia, confusion and petechiae in the axillae/conjunctivae = fat embolism syndrome. Support and fix the fracture early.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "StatPearls. Fat Embolism (NCBI Bookshelf)", "url": "https://ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK499885/"}
• {"name": "Radiopaedia. Fat embolism syndrome", "url": "https://radiopaedia.org/articles/fat-embolism-syndrome"}

---

EDITED BY HEISENBERG','difficult','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "bank170", "review_status": "owner_approved_2026_09_29", "tags": ["ZQ-0164", "zyntra_bank", "mock1", "level_4", "task_data_interpretation_synthesis", "group_adult_surgery", "sys_orthopaedics", "approved_by_owner_2026_09_29"], "clinician_task": "data_interpretation_synthesis", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"StatPearls. Fat Embolism (NCBI Bookshelf)\", \"url\": \"https://ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK499885/\"}; {\"name\": \"Radiopaedia. Fat embolism syndrome\", \"url\": \"https://radiopaedia.org/articles/fat-embolism-syndrome\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "PE is a key differential in an immobile trauma patient, but the CTPA shows no filling defects. PE would not explain petechiae, thrombocytopenia or a fall in haemoglobin.", "C": "Pneumonia with sepsis could cause fever, hypoxia and confusion, but not an abrupt onset at 30 hours with axillary and conjunctival petechiae and diffuse ground-glass change.", "D": "TRALI occurs within 6 hours of transfusion of a blood product. He has not been transfused.", "E": "Opioid toxicity causes a LOW respiratory rate, a raised pCO2 and small pupils. He has tachypnoea, a low pCO2 and normal pupils."}, "key_takeaways": ["24–72 hours after a femoral fracture: hypoxaemia, confusion and petechiae in the axillae/conjunctivae = fat embolism syndrome. Support and fix the fracture early."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,1.0,1.0,'tier_prior','tier=4 (bank170)'),
('8964c658-9f50-555e-adb2-89c549ffc5de'::uuid,'ZQ-0165','adult-surgery','orthopaedics','A 36-year-old man with epilepsy is brought to the emergency department of a hospital in Darwin, Northern Territory, by his partner. Overnight he had a generalised tonic-clonic seizure in bed. He had run out of levetiracetam two days earlier. This morning he has severe pain in his right shoulder and cannot use his arm. There was no fall.

He is alert and oriented. He holds his right arm against his body in internal rotation. The front of the shoulder looks flattened and the coracoid process is prominent. He cannot externally rotate the arm, even passively. Sensation over the lateral upper arm is normal, and radial pulse and hand function are normal.

A single anteroposterior shoulder X-ray has been reported by the overnight junior doctor as ''no fracture, joint congruent''. On review, the humeral head looks symmetrically rounded, and the normal overlap of the humeral head with the posterior glenoid rim is lost.

Which one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?','["Adhesive capsulitis (frozen shoulder)", "Acute rotator cuff tear", "Anterior glenohumeral dislocation", "Posterior glenohumeral dislocation", "Acromioclavicular joint dislocation"]'::jsonb,'D','CORRECT ANSWER

D. Posterior glenohumeral dislocation

---

Seizures (and electric shocks) are the classic causes of posterior shoulder dislocation, because the powerful internal rotators overpower the external rotators. The clinical signs are an arm locked in adduction and internal rotation with a block to external rotation, flattening of the anterior shoulder and a prominent coracoid. On an AP view the internally rotated humeral head looks symmetrical (the ''light bulb'' sign), and the normal half-moon overlap with the glenoid is lost; the AP film can look almost normal, which is why 50% or more of these injuries are missed at first presentation. An axillary or scapular Y view (or CT) confirms the diagnosis and shows any reverse Hill-Sachs lesion or fracture. It needs reduction and orthopaedic review, and his epilepsy management needs attention.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
After a seizure, a shoulder locked in internal rotation with no external rotation is a posterior dislocation until proven otherwise. The AP film can look nearly normal: get an axillary or Y view.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "Posterior dislocation of the shoulder: the light-bulb sign (PMC)", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10679799/"}
• {"name": "Neglected posterior dislocation of the shoulder: a systematic literature review (PMC)", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5982360/"}

---

EDITED BY HEISENBERG','moderate','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "bank170", "review_status": "owner_approved_2026_09_29", "tags": ["ZQ-0165", "zyntra_bank", "mock1", "level_3", "task_data_interpretation_synthesis", "group_adult_surgery", "sys_orthopaedics", "approved_by_owner_2026_09_29"], "clinician_task": "data_interpretation_synthesis", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"Posterior dislocation of the shoulder: the light-bulb sign (PMC)\", \"url\": \"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10679799/\"}; {\"name\": \"Neglected posterior dislocation of the shoulder: a systematic literature review (PMC)\", \"url\": \"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5982360/\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "Frozen shoulder develops over weeks to months with global loss of movement. It does not start suddenly after a seizure with abnormal X-ray signs.", "B": "A cuff tear causes weakness and painful active movement, but passive movement is usually preserved. A mechanical block to passive external rotation suggests dislocation.", "C": "Anterior dislocation (the commonest type) holds the arm in slight abduction and external rotation, with a squared-off shoulder and a humeral head that lies below the coracoid on the AP view.", "E": "AC joint injury gives a step at the top of the shoulder and localised tenderness, usually after a fall onto the shoulder. It does not lock the arm in internal rotation."}, "key_takeaways": ["After a seizure, a shoulder locked in internal rotation with no external rotation is a posterior dislocation until proven otherwise. The AP film can look nearly normal: get an axillary or Y view."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,0.0,1.0,'tier_prior','tier=3 (bank170)'),
('ede45f55-9369-509d-b6e6-f8eeb15ab10e'::uuid,'ZQ-0166','adult-surgery','trauma','An 82-year-old woman is brought to the emergency department of John Hunter Hospital, Newcastle, New South Wales, by her daughter two hours after tripping on a rug at home and hitting her forehead on a door frame. She did not lose consciousness, and she remembers the whole event. She has not vomited and has only a mild headache. She has atrial fibrillation treated with apixaban 5 mg twice daily (last dose 3 hours ago), hypertension and osteoarthritis. She lives independently.

Her GCS is 15. Her pulse is 78/min and irregularly irregular, and blood pressure 148/84 mmHg. There is a 3 cm bruise and swelling over the right forehead without laceration. There are no signs of a base-of-skull fracture. Pupils are equal and reactive, and there is no focal neurological deficit. There is no neck pain or midline cervical tenderness, and she moves her neck freely.

Which one of the following is the most appropriate next step?','["Non-contrast CT of the brain now", "Observe for 4 hours in the emergency department, with CT if she deteriorates", "Discharge home with written head injury advice and her daughter", "Skull X-ray to exclude a fracture", "Withhold apixaban for 48 hours and arrange GP review, without imaging"]'::jsonb,'A','CORRECT ANSWER

A. Non-contrast CT of the brain now

---

She has a mild head injury (GCS 15, no loss of consciousness or amnesia) but is taking an anticoagulant and is over 65. The NSW Health guideline on initial management of closed head injury in adults classes anticoagulation or known coagulopathy as a high-risk feature that needs early CT brain, even with a GCS of 15 and no other symptoms. Anticoagulated older patients have a substantially higher risk of intracranial haemorrhage, and early symptoms can be minimal. If CT shows haemorrhage, apixaban should be reversed (e.g. andexanet alfa where available or prothrombin complex concentrate, according to local protocol) and neurosurgery consulted. If CT is normal, a period of observation is usual; local pathways vary on whether a repeat CT is needed.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Head injury in a patient taking an anticoagulant (including a DOAC) needs early CT brain, even with GCS 15 and no symptoms.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "NSW Health / ACI. Initial management of closed head injury in adults, 2nd edition (clinical practice guideline)", "url": "https://aci.health.nsw.gov.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/195150/NSW-Health-Initial-management-closed-head-injuries-full.pdf"}
• {"name": "NSW Health Policy Directive PD2012_013: Closed Head Injury in Adults \u2013 Initial Management", "url": "https://www1.health.nsw.gov.au/pds/ActivePDSDocuments/PD2012_013.pdf"}

---

EDITED BY HEISENBERG','moderate','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "bank170", "review_status": "owner_approved_2026_09_29", "tags": ["ZQ-0166", "zyntra_bank", "mock1", "level_3", "task_data_gathering", "group_adult_surgery", "sys_trauma", "approved_by_owner_2026_09_29"], "clinician_task": "data_gathering", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"NSW Health / ACI. Initial management of closed head injury in adults, 2nd edition (clinical practice guideline)\", \"url\": \"https://aci.health.nsw.gov.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0003/195150/NSW-Health-Initial-management-closed-head-injuries-full.pdf\"}; {\"name\": \"NSW Health Policy Directive PD2012_013: Closed Head Injury in Adults \\u2013 Initial Management\", \"url\": \"https://www1.health.nsw.gov.au/pds/ActivePDSDocuments/PD2012_013.pdf\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"B": "Observation without imaging is inappropriate for an anticoagulated patient. Deterioration from an expanding haematoma may be late and catastrophic.", "C": "Discharge without imaging ignores the anticoagulant, which is a high-risk feature on all major head injury rules.", "D": "Skull X-rays have no role in adult head injury assessment. A normal X-ray does not exclude intracranial bleeding.", "E": "Stopping apixaban without imaging leaves her unprotected from stroke and does not exclude an existing haemorrhage."}, "key_takeaways": ["Head injury in a patient taking an anticoagulant (including a DOAC) needs early CT brain, even with GCS 15 and no symptoms."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,0.0,1.0,'tier_prior','tier=3 (bank170)'),
('fd59b48f-c946-5b01-9b7d-9e50f05fe816'::uuid,'ZQ-0167','adult-surgery','trauma','A 38-year-old woman is brought to the emergency department of The Alfred, Melbourne, after a high-speed car crash on the freeway. She was the restrained driver, the airbags deployed, and she was able to get out of the car herself. She complains of neck pain. She has no other medical history.

Her primary survey is normal: GCS 15, pulse 88/min, blood pressure 132/80 mmHg, respiratory rate 16/min and oxygen saturation 99% on room air. She has a seatbelt abrasion across the left chest and lower abdomen, but not the neck. She has midline tenderness at the mid-cervical spine. Neurological examination of the limbs and cranial nerves is normal. There is no Horner syndrome, no carotid bruit, no neck haematoma and no epistaxis.

Trauma CT (non-contrast head, cervical spine; contrast-enhanced chest, abdomen and pelvis):
- CT brain: normal
- CT cervical spine: undisplaced fracture of the right C4 lateral mass extending into the right transverse foramen; alignment is normal, with no subluxation
- CT chest, abdomen and pelvis: no injury

She is comfortable in a cervical collar, and the spinal team plans non-operative management.

Which one of the following is the most appropriate next investigation?','["MRI of the cervical spine to assess the ligaments", "CT angiography of the head and neck vessels", "Flexion–extension X-rays of the cervical spine", "Duplex ultrasound of the carotid and vertebral arteries", "No further imaging; discharge in a hard collar"]'::jsonb,'B','CORRECT ANSWER

B. CT angiography of the head and neck vessels

---

A cervical spine fracture extending into the transverse foramen puts the vertebral artery (which runs through the foramina from C6 to C1) at risk of blunt cerebrovascular injury (BCVI): dissection, intimal flap, pseudoaneurysm or occlusion. BCVI is often asymptomatic at first, and presents hours to days later with posterior circulation stroke. Screening finds it before a stroke, so that antithrombotic therapy can be started (in discussion with the trauma and spinal teams). The EAST 2020 practice management guideline strongly recommends CT angiography screening for patients with high-risk cervical spine injuries, specifically including fractures into the transverse foramen, subluxation and C1–C3 fractures. These are also expanded Denver criteria. A normal neurological examination does not remove the indication, which is why this is a common miss.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Cervical fracture into the transverse foramen, subluxation or C1–C3 fracture = high risk of blunt cerebrovascular injury: screen with CT angiography even if neurologically intact.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "EAST Practice Management Guideline: Evaluation and management of blunt cerebrovascular injury (2020)", "url": "https://www.east.org/Content/documents/practicemanagementguidelines/evaluation_and_management_of_blunt_cerebrovascular_injury.pdf"}
• {"name": "Radiopaedia. Denver criteria for blunt cerebrovascular injury", "url": "https://radiopaedia.org/articles/denver-criteria-for-blunt-cerebrovascular-injury-1"}

---

EDITED BY HEISENBERG','difficult','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "bank170", "review_status": "owner_approved_2026_09_29", "tags": ["ZQ-0167", "zyntra_bank", "mock1", "level_5", "task_data_gathering", "group_adult_surgery", "sys_trauma", "approved_by_owner_2026_09_29"], "clinician_task": "data_gathering", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"EAST Practice Management Guideline: Evaluation and management of blunt cerebrovascular injury (2020)\", \"url\": \"https://www.east.org/Content/documents/practicemanagementguidelines/evaluation_and_management_of_blunt_cerebrovascular_injury.pdf\"}; {\"name\": \"Radiopaedia. Denver criteria for blunt cerebrovascular injury\", \"url\": \"https://radiopaedia.org/articles/denver-criteria-for-blunt-cerebrovascular-injury-1\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "MRI assesses ligaments, discs and cord. The CT already shows the fracture with normal alignment, and MRI does not answer the vascular question (MR angiography is a less preferred alternative).", "C": "Flexion–extension films are not recommended in the acute phase and do not assess the vertebral artery.", "D": "Duplex ultrasound cannot image the vertebral artery reliably within the bony foramina, and misses many BCVIs. It is not an adequate screening test.", "E": "Discharging her without vascular screening leaves a possible vertebral artery injury undetected, with a risk of delayed stroke."}, "key_takeaways": ["Cervical fracture into the transverse foramen, subluxation or C1–C3 fracture = high risk of blunt cerebrovascular injury: screen with CT angiography even if neurologically intact."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,2.0,1.0,'tier_prior','tier=5 (bank170)'),
('3c851b60-63ca-5709-82a8-d0cf537d82a8'::uuid,'ZQ-0168','adult-surgery','ent-ophthalmology','A 48-year-old man sees his general practitioner in Brisbane because he woke two days ago with his left ear feeling ''blocked'' and muffled, with a high-pitched ringing. He has no ear pain, discharge, vertigo or recent upper respiratory infection, and no history of noise exposure, head injury or ear surgery. He has hypertension treated with candesartan.

Otoscopy shows normal, mobile tympanic membranes on both sides with no wax. Cranial nerve examination is otherwise normal, and there is no nystagmus or ataxia.

Tuning fork tests (512 Hz):
- Rinne test: air conduction louder than bone conduction on both sides
- Weber test: the sound is heard in the right ear

A whispered voice test shows reduced hearing on the left.

Which one of the following is the most appropriate interpretation of these findings?','["Left conductive hearing loss from otitis media with effusion", "Right conductive hearing loss", "Right sensorineural hearing loss", "Left sensorineural hearing loss needing same-day or next-day ENT assessment", "Normal hearing with left Eustachian tube dysfunction"]'::jsonb,'D','CORRECT ANSWER

D. Left sensorineural hearing loss needing same-day or next-day ENT assessment

---

In a unilateral sensorineural loss, Rinne is positive (air louder than bone) on both sides, because the affected cochlea hears both air and bone conduction less, in the normal ratio. Weber lateralises to the BETTER (unaffected) ear. In a conductive loss, Rinne is negative on the affected side and Weber lateralises TO the affected ear. So a Weber heard on the right with bilateral positive Rinne and poor left hearing means a left sensorineural loss. Sudden onset over less than 72 hours with no obvious cause is sudden sensorineural hearing loss (SSNHL), which is an otological emergency. The AAO-HNS 2019 guideline recommends audiometry as soon as possible to confirm it, offering systemic corticosteroids within 2 weeks of onset (earlier is better), and MRI of the internal auditory meati to exclude retrocochlear causes such as vestibular schwannoma. Many patients describe SSNHL as a ''blocked ear'' and are wrongly treated for wax or congestion.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Weber goes TO a conductive loss and AWAY from a sensorineural loss. A sudden unilateral sensorineural loss needs urgent audiometry, steroids within 2 weeks and later MRI.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "Chandrasekhar SS et al. Clinical Practice Guideline: Sudden Hearing Loss (Update). Otolaryngol Head Neck Surg 2019 (AAO-HNS)", "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/31369359"}

---

EDITED BY HEISENBERG','difficult','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "bank170", "review_status": "owner_approved_2026_09_29", "tags": ["ZQ-0168", "zyntra_bank", "mock1", "level_4", "task_data_interpretation_synthesis", "group_adult_surgery", "sys_ent_ophthalmology", "approved_by_owner_2026_09_29"], "clinician_task": "data_interpretation_synthesis", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"Chandrasekhar SS et al. Clinical Practice Guideline: Sudden Hearing Loss (Update). Otolaryngol Head Neck Surg 2019 (AAO-HNS)\", \"url\": \"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/31369359\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "Middle ear effusion would give a negative Rinne on the left, Weber lateralising to the left, and usually an abnormal tympanic membrane. His drums are normal.", "B": "A right conductive loss would also lateralise Weber to the right, but it would give a negative Rinne on the right and poor hearing on the right. His hearing is worse on the left.", "C": "Weber lateralises away from a sensorineural loss. A right sensorineural loss would send Weber to the left.", "E": "Eustachian tube dysfunction usually causes a retracted drum or conductive features. It does not explain objective left-sided hearing loss with Weber lateralising away from that ear."}, "key_takeaways": ["Weber goes TO a conductive loss and AWAY from a sensorineural loss. A sudden unilateral sensorineural loss needs urgent audiometry, steroids within 2 weeks and later MRI."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,1.0,1.0,'tier_prior','tier=4 (bank170)'),
('29b1a709-aceb-50d3-9791-63ad7dcdbed8'::uuid,'ZQ-0169','adult-surgery','ent-ophthalmology','A 34-year-old concreter is brought by a workmate to a general practice in Katherine, Northern Territory, ten minutes after wet cement splashed into his right eye while he was pouring a slab. He rinsed it briefly with a water bottle at the site. He has severe pain, watering and cannot open the eye. He wears no contact lenses.

He is distressed and holding his eye closed. The eyelids are red and there is grey cement paste visible in the lower fornix. The left eye is normal. The practice has sterile saline, Hartmann''s solution, topical anaesthetic drops, litmus (pH) paper, fluorescein and a Snellen chart. The nearest ophthalmologist is in Darwin, over 300 km away.

Which one of the following is the most appropriate first step?','["Measure visual acuity in both eyes to document a baseline", "Instil fluorescein and examine the cornea with a blue light", "Instil chloramphenicol ointment and apply an eye pad", "Check the pH of the tears with litmus paper to confirm an alkali", "Instil topical anaesthetic and irrigate the eye copiously straight away"]'::jsonb,'E','CORRECT ANSWER

E. Instil topical anaesthetic and irrigate the eye copiously straight away

---

Cement and lime are alkalis. Alkalis saponify cell membranes and penetrate deeply into the cornea and anterior chamber, causing limbal ischaemia, corneal opacification and blindness. Damage continues until the chemical is removed. Immediate, copious irrigation takes priority over everything else, including visual acuity, history and detailed examination. This is the one situation in ophthalmology where acuity is measured after treatment. The Royal Victorian Eye and Ear Hospital referral guidelines advise irrigating immediately with saline, Hartmann''s or water. Topical anaesthetic helps the patient open the eye. Evert the lids and sweep the fornices to remove particles of cement. Check the pH about 5 minutes after pausing irrigation, and continue until it is neutral (about 7–7.5). Then assess acuity and the cornea with fluorescein and arrange urgent ophthalmology review (via telehealth and transfer to Darwin as needed).

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Chemical eye injury, especially alkali (cement, lime): irrigate immediately and copiously, before acuity or examination, and continue until the pH is neutral. Remove particles from the fornices.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "Royal Victorian Eye and Ear Hospital. Primary Care Referral Guidelines \u2013 Ophthalmology (July 2026)", "url": "https://eyeandear.org.au/wp-content/uploads/2026/07/Primary-Care-Referral-Guidelines-Ophthalmology-July-2026.pdf"}
• {"name": "AJGP 2024 (RACGP). Chemical eye injury in remote and urgent care clinics", "url": "https://www1.racgp.org.au/ajgp/2024/supplement-december/chemical-eye-injury-in-remote-and-urgent-care-clin"}

---

EDITED BY HEISENBERG','moderate','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "bank170", "review_status": "owner_approved_2026_09_29", "tags": ["ZQ-0169", "zyntra_bank", "mock1", "level_3", "task_management", "group_adult_surgery", "sys_ent_ophthalmology", "approved_by_owner_2026_09_29"], "clinician_task": "management", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"Royal Victorian Eye and Ear Hospital. Primary Care Referral Guidelines \\u2013 Ophthalmology (July 2026)\", \"url\": \"https://eyeandear.org.au/wp-content/uploads/2026/07/Primary-Care-Referral-Guidelines-Ophthalmology-July-2026.pdf\"}; {\"name\": \"AJGP 2024 (RACGP). Chemical eye injury in remote and urgent care clinics\", \"url\": \"https://www1.racgp.org.au/ajgp/2024/supplement-december/chemical-eye-injury-in-remote-and-urgent-care-clin\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "Measuring acuity first is the usual rule in eye injuries, but chemical burns are the exception. Every minute of delay allows more alkali penetration.", "B": "Fluorescein examination comes after irrigation, once the pH is neutral.", "C": "Covering the eye traps the alkali against the cornea. Antibiotic ointment is not first-line treatment.", "D": "pH testing guides how long to irrigate. It is done after irrigation starts, not before, and the history already tells you it is an alkali."}, "key_takeaways": ["Chemical eye injury, especially alkali (cement, lime): irrigate immediately and copiously, before acuity or examination, and continue until the pH is neutral. Remove particles from the fornices."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,0.0,1.0,'tier_prior','tier=3 (bank170)'),
('651fa9ea-242f-598d-b483-c5f31fcc76b4'::uuid,'ZQ-0170','adult-surgery','ent-ophthalmology','A 27-year-old woman comes to the emergency department of University Hospital Geelong, Victoria. She has had a blocked nose, facial pressure and purulent nasal discharge for 6 days. For the past 2 days her right upper and lower eyelids have become increasingly swollen and red, and since this morning she has had double vision and pain when she moves her right eye. She has no history of trauma, insect bite or skin lesions around the eye. She is otherwise well and takes no regular medicines.

Her temperature is 38.7°C, pulse 102/min and blood pressure 118/74 mmHg. The right eyelids are swollen, tense and red, and she can open the eye only with help. The right eye is displaced forward compared with the left. Abduction and upgaze of the right eye are restricted and painful. Visual acuity is 6/9 right and 6/6 left. Pupils are equal and reactive, with no relative afferent pupillary defect. Sensation over the forehead and cheeks is normal. The left eye is normal. She has no neck stiffness and is alert.

Which one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?','["Preseptal (periorbital) cellulitis", "Acute dacryocystitis", "Orbital (post-septal) cellulitis", "Cavernous sinus thrombosis", "Allergic angioedema"]'::jsonb,'C','CORRECT ANSWER

C. Orbital (post-septal) cellulitis

---

Features that point to infection behind the orbital septum are proptosis, painful or restricted eye movements (ophthalmoplegia), diplopia, reduced visual acuity or colour vision, and a relative afferent pupillary defect. She has proptosis, restricted and painful movements with diplopia, and slightly reduced acuity, after several days of sinusitis (ethmoid sinusitis is the usual source). This is orbital cellulitis. It needs urgent contrast-enhanced CT of the orbits and sinuses (to look for a subperiosteal or orbital abscess), IV antibiotics, and urgent ophthalmology and ENT review. Vision, pupils and colour vision should be checked frequently, because an abscess or optic nerve compression may need surgical drainage.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Eyelid swelling plus proptosis, painful or restricted eye movements, or reduced vision = orbital cellulitis: urgent CT orbits/sinuses, IV antibiotics, ophthalmology and ENT.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "StatPearls. Orbital Cellulitis (NCBI Bookshelf)", "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK507901/"}
• {"name": "Royal Children''s Hospital Melbourne. Clinical Practice Guideline: Periorbital and orbital cellulitis (paediatric; distinguishing features apply to adults)", "url": "https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/Periorbital_and_orbital_cellulitis/"}

---

EDITED BY HEISENBERG','moderate','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "bank170", "review_status": "owner_approved_2026_09_29", "tags": ["ZQ-0170", "zyntra_bank", "mock1", "level_3", "task_data_interpretation_synthesis", "group_adult_surgery", "sys_ent_ophthalmology", "approved_by_owner_2026_09_29"], "clinician_task": "data_interpretation_synthesis", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"StatPearls. Orbital Cellulitis (NCBI Bookshelf)\", \"url\": \"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK507901/\"}; {\"name\": \"Royal Children''s Hospital Melbourne. Clinical Practice Guideline: Periorbital and orbital cellulitis (paediatric; distinguishing features apply to adults)\", \"url\": \"https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/Periorbital_and_orbital_cellulitis/\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "Preseptal cellulitis causes lid swelling and redness, but eye movements are full and painless, the eye is not proptosed and vision is normal.", "B": "Dacryocystitis causes a tender swelling at the medial canthus over the lacrimal sac, not proptosis with ophthalmoplegia.", "D": "Cavernous sinus thrombosis is a rarer, more severe complication. It often becomes bilateral, with multiple cranial nerve palsies (III, IV, VI), reduced sensation in V1/V2, and a very unwell patient. She has unilateral signs and normal facial sensation.", "E": "Angioedema is usually afebrile, itchy or painless, and does not cause proptosis, ophthalmoplegia or reduced vision."}, "key_takeaways": ["Eyelid swelling plus proptosis, painful or restricted eye movements, or reduced vision = orbital cellulitis: urgent CT orbits/sinuses, IV antibiotics, ophthalmology and ENT."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,0.0,1.0,'tier_prior','tier=3 (bank170)'),
('08a0eeb8-4984-51b2-91d0-dbbee1c5a623'::uuid,'ZQ-0171','adult-surgery','trauma','A 34-year-old man weighing 70 kg is brought to the emergency department of Fiona Stanley Hospital, Perth, after a gas barbecue exploded at 1400. He arrives at 1600. Paramedics cooled the burns with running water for 20 minutes and gave him 1000 mL of Hartmann''s solution through two large-bore cannulas on the way.

He is alert and talking in full sentences with no hoarseness, stridor, carbonaceous sputum or singed nasal hair. His pulse is 118/min, blood pressure 124/76 mmHg, respiratory rate 20/min and oxygen saturation 98% on room air. Carboxyhaemoglobin is 2%.

The burns have been mapped on a Lund and Browder chart:
- Deep dermal and full-thickness burns to the front of the trunk and both arms: 30% of total body surface area (TBSA)
- Erythema only (superficial burn) over the face and neck: 5% TBSA

A urinary catheter has been inserted. The team plans fluid resuscitation using the Emergency Management of Severe Burns (EMSB) formula.

At what hourly rate should Hartmann''s solution be infused from 1600 until 2200?','["358 mL/h", "394 mL/h", "446 mL/h", "525 mL/h", "533 mL/h"]'::jsonb,'A','CORRECT ANSWER

A. 358 mL/h

---

The ANZBA EMSB course uses the modified Parkland formula for adults with burns over 20% TBSA: 3 mL Hartmann''s × body weight (kg) × %TBSA burn over 24 hours, timed from the time of the BURN, not from arrival. Erythema (superficial epidermal burn) is NOT counted in the %TBSA. So %TBSA = 30. Total for 24 hours = 3 × 70 × 30 = 6300 mL. Half (3150 mL) is given in the first 8 hours after the burn (1400–2200). He has already received 1000 mL, so 2150 mL remains for the 6 hours from 1600 to 2200: 2150 ÷ 6 ≈ 358 mL/h. The remaining 3150 mL is given over the next 16 hours. The formula is only a starting point: titrate to a urine output of about 0.5 mL/kg/h in adults (about 35 mL/h here) to avoid both under-resuscitation and ''fluid creep''. He meets ANZBA criteria for transfer to a burns unit.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
EMSB adult burns >20% TBSA: 3 mL × kg × %TBSA (exclude erythema), half in the first 8 hours from the time of burn, minus fluid already given. Titrate to urine output 0.5 mL/kg/h.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "Tasmanian Health Service. Tasmanian Burns Service \u2013 Fluid Resuscitation (modified Parkland formula, 3 mL/kg/%TBSA)", "url": "https://www.health.tas.gov.au/sites/default/files/2021-12/Fluid_Resuscitation_DoHTasmania2021.pdf"}
• {"name": "ANZBA/British Burn Association. EMSB Course Manual, 19th edition (2024, UK edition of the ANZBA course)", "url": "https://www.britishburnassociation.org/wp-content/uploads/2024/12/2024-EMSB-Manual-UK_19ed.pdf"}
• {"name": "ANZBA. Management of burns and referral criteria", "url": "https://www.anzba.org/quality-care/management-of-burns/"}

