('5afdbd41-05cf-5205-8575-391b9760c579'::uuid,'ZQ-0172','adult-surgery','general-surgery','A 22-year-old man is having an elective arthroscopic anterior cruciate ligament reconstruction at a private hospital in Adelaide. He is fit and well, takes no regular medicines and has had no previous anaesthetic. His family history is not known because he was adopted. Anaesthesia was induced with propofol, fentanyl and rocuronium, and maintained with sevoflurane in oxygen and air through a circle system with a fresh CO2 absorbent.

Forty minutes into the operation, the anaesthetist notices that end-tidal CO2 has risen from 38 to 64 mmHg despite doubling the minute ventilation. Inspired CO2 is 0 mmHg. His heart rate has risen from 70 to 132/min with occasional ventricular ectopics, and blood pressure is 150/90 mmHg. His nasopharyngeal temperature has risen from 36.4°C to 38.4°C over 15 minutes, and his jaw and limbs are rigid. The absorbent canister feels hot.

Arterial blood gas:
- pH 7.16 (7.35–7.45)
- pCO2 72 mmHg (35–45)
- pO2 210 mmHg (FiO2 0.5)
- Bicarbonate 20 mmol/L (22–32)
- Lactate 4.2 mmol/L (<2.0)
- Potassium 6.0 mmol/L (3.5–5.2)

Which one of the following is the most likely cause?','["Sepsis from contamination of the surgical field", "Thyroid storm", "Inadequate depth of anaesthesia", "Exhausted CO2 absorbent causing rebreathing", "Malignant hyperthermia"]'::jsonb,'E','CORRECT ANSWER

E. Malignant hyperthermia

---

A rapidly rising end-tidal CO2 that does not respond to increased ventilation is the most sensitive early sign of malignant hyperthermia (MH), a pharmacogenetic hypermetabolic crisis triggered by volatile anaesthetics and suxamethonium in people with susceptibility variants (usually RYR1). Other features here are tachycardia with arrhythmia, muscle rigidity, rising temperature (often a later sign), a hot absorbent (from increased CO2 production), and a mixed respiratory and metabolic acidosis with hyperkalaemia. A normal previous anaesthetic history is uninformative, as this is his first. The MHANZ (Malignant Hyperthermia Australia and New Zealand) resource kit steps are: call for help and the MH kit, stop the volatile agent, hyperventilate with 100% oxygen at high flow, give dantrolene 2.5 mg/kg IV (repeated until the hypermetabolism resolves), switch to TIVA, cool actively, treat hyperkalaemia and arrhythmias, and finish or abandon surgery quickly. He and his relatives will later need referral to an MH diagnostic unit.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Unexplained rising end-tidal CO2 despite increased ventilation under volatile anaesthesia = malignant hyperthermia until proven otherwise. Stop the trigger, 100% O2, dantrolene 2.5 mg/kg.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "MHANZ. Malignant Hyperthermia Resource Kit 2025 (v3, Jan 2026)", "url": "https://malignanthyperthermia.org.au/sites/default/files/2026-03/MALIGNANT-HYPERTHERMIA-RESOURCE-KIT-2025%20V3%2014.1.26.pdf"}
• {"name": "European Malignant Hyperthermia Group. Recognising and managing a malignant hyperthermia crisis (v2024)", "url": "https://www.emhg.org/recommendations-1/2024/11/4/recognising-and-managing-a-malignant-hyperthermia-crisis-v2024"}

---

EDITED BY HEISENBERG','difficult','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "bank170", "review_status": "owner_approved_2026_09_29", "tags": ["ZQ-0172", "zyntra_bank", "mock1", "level_4", "task_data_interpretation_synthesis", "group_adult_surgery", "sys_general_surgery", "approved_by_owner_2026_09_29"], "clinician_task": "data_interpretation_synthesis", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"MHANZ. Malignant Hyperthermia Resource Kit 2025 (v3, Jan 2026)\", \"url\": \"https://malignanthyperthermia.org.au/sites/default/files/2026-03/MALIGNANT-HYPERTHERMIA-RESOURCE-KIT-2025%20V3%2014.1.26.pdf\"}; {\"name\": \"European Malignant Hyperthermia Group. Recognising and managing a malignant hyperthermia crisis (v2024)\", \"url\": \"https://www.emhg.org/recommendations-1/2024/11/4/recognising-and-managing-a-malignant-hyperthermia-crisis-v2024\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "Sepsis does not start this suddenly 40 minutes into a clean elective procedure, and it does not cause muscle rigidity or a steep rise in CO2 production.", "B": "Thyroid storm can cause hyperthermia and tachycardia, but it is rare without known thyroid disease, and it does not cause rigidity, hyperkalaemia or such a fast rise in CO2.", "C": "Light anaesthesia causes tachycardia and hypertension, but not a rising end-tidal CO2 despite doubled ventilation, rigidity, hyperthermia or a metabolic acidosis with hyperkalaemia.", "D": "An exhausted absorbent causes rebreathing, so INSPIRED CO2 rises above zero. His inspired CO2 is 0 and the absorbent is new. Rebreathing also does not explain rigidity, hyperthermia or hyperkalaemia."}, "key_takeaways": ["Unexplained rising end-tidal CO2 despite increased ventilation under volatile anaesthesia = malignant hyperthermia until proven otherwise. Stop the trigger, 100% O2, dantrolene 2.5 mg/kg."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,1.0,1.0,'tier_prior','tier=4 (bank170)'),
('38e84db4-f275-5a35-b4e1-8b7fd2a545a2'::uuid,'ZQ-0173','adult-surgery','trauma','A 46-year-old man is brought to the emergency department of Toowoomba Hospital, Queensland, after his motorbike hit a kangaroo at 90 km/h. He was wearing a helmet. The trauma team is carrying out the primary survey.

- Airway: talking, cervical collar in place
- Breathing: respiratory rate 26/min, oxygen saturation 97% on high-flow oxygen, equal air entry, eFAST shows no pneumothorax
- Circulation: pulse 132/min, blood pressure 80/50 mmHg after 1 L of warmed crystalloid; peripheries cold; no external bleeding; eFAST shows no free fluid in the abdomen or pericardium
- Disability: GCS 14 (E3 V5 M6), pupils equal and reactive
- Exposure: bruising over the lower abdomen, perineum and scrotum; no long bone deformity; blood at the urethral meatus

Chest X-ray is normal. Pelvic X-ray shows widening of the pubic symphysis to 4 cm with disruption of the right sacroiliac joint. The massive haemorrhage protocol has been activated, two units of O-negative red cells are running, and 1 g of IV tranexamic acid has been given.

Which one of the following is the most appropriate next step?','["Emergency laparotomy to control intra-abdominal bleeding", "Apply a pelvic binder centred over the greater trochanters", "Transfer to CT for contrast-enhanced imaging of the abdomen and pelvis", "Apply a pelvic binder centred over the iliac crests", "Give a further 2 L crystalloid bolus and reassess the blood pressure"]'::jsonb,'B','CORRECT ANSWER

B. Apply a pelvic binder centred over the greater trochanters

---

He has haemorrhagic shock (tachycardia, hypotension, cold peripheries, poor response to fluid) with an open-book pelvic fracture (symphysis diastasis and sacroiliac disruption). eFAST is negative for abdominal free fluid and the chest is clear, so the pelvis is the likely source. Pelvic bleeding is mostly venous and from fracture surfaces. In EMST/ATLS teaching, the immediate step in circulation (C) is mechanical stabilisation with a pelvic binder, which reduces pelvic volume, tamponades venous bleeding and stabilises clot. The binder must be centred over the greater trochanters (at the level of the symphysis), not the iliac crests. This goes alongside damage control resuscitation (blood products in balanced ratios, tranexamic acid, permissive hypotension, avoiding large crystalloid volumes). If he remains unstable, definitive haemorrhage control is by angioembolisation and/or preperitoneal packing and external fixation, depending on local resources, with early discussion with the retrieval service and a major trauma centre. Blood at the meatus means no urethral catheter until the urethra is imaged.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Shock with an open-book pelvic fracture and negative eFAST: apply a pelvic binder over the greater trochanters, resuscitate with blood and TXA, then arrange angioembolisation or packing.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "Royal Children''s Hospital Melbourne. Trauma \u2013 Pelvic injury (binder placement over the greater trochanters)", "url": "https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/Trauma_-_Pelvic_injury/"}
• {"name": "Sydney HEMS / NSW Ambulance. Clinical practice standard: Pelvic trauma", "url": "https://sydneyhems.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/12/ao.cli_.-07-pelvic-trauma.pdf"}

---

EDITED BY HEISENBERG','difficult','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "bank170", "review_status": "owner_approved_2026_09_29", "tags": ["ZQ-0173", "zyntra_bank", "mock1", "level_4", "task_management", "group_adult_surgery", "sys_trauma", "approved_by_owner_2026_09_29"], "clinician_task": "management", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"Royal Children''s Hospital Melbourne. Trauma \\u2013 Pelvic injury (binder placement over the greater trochanters)\", \"url\": \"https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/Trauma_-_Pelvic_injury/\"}; {\"name\": \"Sydney HEMS / NSW Ambulance. Clinical practice standard: Pelvic trauma\", \"url\": \"https://sydneyhems.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/12/ao.cli_.-07-pelvic-trauma.pdf\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "Laparotomy is for intra-abdominal bleeding. eFAST shows no free fluid, and opening the abdomen can release the tamponade of a pelvic haematoma and worsen bleeding.", "C": "A patient in shock who is not responding to resuscitation should not go to CT before bleeding is controlled. CT is for patients who have been stabilised.", "D": "A binder over the iliac crests is too high. It does not close the pelvic ring effectively and can even widen some fractures.", "E": "Large-volume crystalloid dilutes clotting factors, worsens acidosis and hypothermia, and raises blood pressure enough to disrupt clot. Resuscitation should be with blood products."}, "key_takeaways": ["Shock with an open-book pelvic fracture and negative eFAST: apply a pelvic binder over the greater trochanters, resuscitate with blood and TXA, then arrange angioembolisation or packing."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,1.0,1.0,'tier_prior','tier=4 (bank170)'),
('676b51fb-2c53-5d63-a4e5-bba240a0b9bf'::uuid,'ZQ-0174','adult-surgery','trauma','A 24-year-old man comes to the emergency department of a hospital in Cairns, Queensland, with a painful, swollen right hand. Two days ago he punched another man in the mouth during an argument. He has a small cut over his knuckle that he cleaned himself. Since yesterday the hand has become more swollen and he cannot make a full fist. He is right-handed and works as a chef. His tetanus vaccination is up to date. He has no drug allergies.

His temperature is 37.9°C and pulse 96/min. There is a 5 mm ragged wound over the dorsum of the right fourth metacarpophalangeal (MCP) joint, with a little cloudy discharge. The dorsum of the hand is swollen and red, with erythema extending 3 cm proximally. Movement of the fourth MCP joint is very painful, and he cannot fully flex or extend the ring finger. Sensation and capillary refill are normal.

X-ray of the hand shows no fracture and no radio-opaque foreign body. White cell count is 13.8 × 10^9/L and CRP 64 mg/L.

Which one of the following is the most appropriate management?','["Primary closure of the wound and oral amoxicillin–clavulanate", "Oral flucloxacillin and review in the fracture clinic in 1 week", "Oral amoxicillin–clavulanate at home with review in 48 hours", "Admission for IV antibiotics and urgent surgical exploration and washout", "Wound dressing and tetanus booster, with no antibiotics"]'::jsonb,'D','CORRECT ANSWER

D. Admission for IV antibiotics and urgent surgical exploration and washout

---

A wound over the MCP joint after punching someone in the mouth is a clenched-fist (fight bite) injury until proven otherwise. With the fist clenched, a tooth can penetrate skin, extensor tendon and joint capsule; when the fingers straighten, the tendon slides and seals the tract, so the wound looks trivial. Mouth flora (streptococci, Staphylococcus aureus, anaerobes and Eikenella corrodens) cause septic arthritis, tenosynovitis and osteomyelitis. He presented late with signs of established infection: fever, discharge, spreading cellulitis, painful joint movement and raised inflammatory markers. NSW ACI guidance on human bites recommends surgical consultation for suspected joint or tendon involvement, and admission for IV antibiotics for infected hand bites. He needs admission, elevation, IV antibiotics covering mouth flora (according to local guidance), and urgent hand surgery review for exploration, joint washout and debridement. The wound should be left open.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Knuckle wound after punching a mouth = fight bite. If infected or over the joint, admit for IV antibiotics and hand-surgical exploration and washout; never close primarily.

---

