EDITED BY HEISENBERG','difficult','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "bank170", "review_status": "owner_approved_2026_09_29", "tags": ["ZQ-0175", "zyntra_bank", "mock1", "level_5", "task_management", "group_adult_surgery", "sys_general_surgery", "approved_by_owner_2026_09_29"], "clinician_task": "management", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"ANZCA. Clinical practice recommendations regarding patients taking GLP-1 receptor agonists and dual GLP-1/GIP receptor co-agonists (updated 2025)\", \"url\": \"https://www.anzca.edu.au/safety-and-advocacy/standards-of-practice/clinical-practice-recommendations-regarding-patients-taking-glp-1\"}; {\"name\": \"ADS/ANZCA/GESA/NACOS/ANZMOSS Clinical Practice Recommendations: periprocedural GLP-1RA use (April 2025, PDF)\", \"url\": \"https://www.anzca.edu.au/getContentAsset/0f35028e-e371-4220-a49a-ddee877051c8/80feb437-d24d-46b8-a858-4a2a28b9b970/Clinical-Practice-Recommendations-Periprocedural-GLP-1RA-use-Apr-2025.pdf\"}; {\"name\": \"RACS Surgical News 2025. Updated guidance for patients using GLP-1/GIP receptor agonists\", \"url\": \"https://www.surgeons.org/surgicalnews/Articles/2025/Volume-26/Issue-4/GLP\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "Stopping for 1 week reflects older overseas advice (e.g. 2023 US anaesthesia guidance for weekly agents). Current ANZ recommendations advise against elective cessation, and a week may not normalise gastric emptying anyway.", "B": "There is no established cessation period that restores gastric emptying, and stopping for 3 weeks with insulin bridging adds risk and loses weight and glycaemic control. This is not recommended.", "D": "This is the trap. The recommendations state that absence of GI symptoms cannot be used for risk stratification, and a standard fast alone does not address residual gastric contents.", "E": "Omitting one dose was part of some earlier interim advice, but it is not what the 2025 ANZ recommendations advise, and it does not replace the 24-hour clear fluid diet."}, "key_takeaways": ["ANZ 2025: continue GLP-1 RAs (and GLP-1/GIP agonists) before procedures; 24-hour clear fluid diet the day before, then a standard 6-hour fast. GI symptoms do not stratify risk."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,2.0,1.0,'tier_prior','tier=5 (bank170)'),
('6685ef24-60b1-5236-88bc-866c85711f0b'::uuid,'ZQ-0176','womens-health','obstetrics','A 27-year-old woman in her first pregnancy sees her general practitioner in Cabramatta, Sydney, for the results of her first antenatal blood tests at 9 weeks'' gestation. She was born in Vietnam and moved to Australia at the age of 12. Her partner, whose family is from southern China, is well. She feels well apart from mild nausea. Her diet includes red meat several times a week.

Investigations:
- Haemoglobin 112 g/L (115–160)
- Mean cell volume (MCV) 68 fL (80–100)
- Mean cell haemoglobin (MCH) 21 pg (27–33)
- Ferritin 85 µg/L (30–200)
- Haemoglobin HPLC: HbA2 2.6% (2.0–3.5), HbF 0.4% (<1.0), no variant haemoglobin detected
- Blood group O Rh D positive, antibody screen negative
- Rubella immune; HIV, hepatitis B, hepatitis C and syphilis serology negative

Which one of the following is the most appropriate next step?','["Start oral iron and repeat the full blood count in 6 weeks", "Reassure her that the normal HbA2 excludes a clinically important haemoglobinopathy", "Arrange full blood count, ferritin and haemoglobin studies for her partner now", "Offer chorionic villus sampling at 11–13 weeks", "Offer non-invasive prenatal testing (cfDNA) to screen the fetus for thalassaemia"]'::jsonb,'C','CORRECT ANSWER

C. Arrange full blood count, ferritin and haemoglobin studies for her partner now

---

She has microcytosis (MCV below 80 fL, MCH below 27 pg) with a normal ferritin, so iron deficiency does not explain it. A normal HbA2 makes beta-thalassaemia trait unlikely but does NOT exclude alpha-thalassaemia trait, which is common in people of Southeast Asian and southern Chinese background and cannot be diagnosed by HPLC. The key question in pregnancy is whether the couple is at risk of a severe fetal haemoglobinopathy, in particular haemoglobin Bart''s hydrops fetalis (when both partners carry alpha-zero deletions such as --SEA, which is common in both their populations). RACGP genomics guidance on haemoglobinopathies advises that when a pregnant woman has a low MCV/MCH with normal iron studies, her partner should be tested promptly, and DNA testing for alpha-thalassaemia should be arranged for both partners when it cannot be excluded. Testing the partner early is time-critical so that an at-risk couple can be referred to genetics/fetal medicine and offered prenatal diagnosis (CVS at 11–13 weeks) in time.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Low MCV/MCH with normal ferritin in pregnancy: test the partner now. A normal HbA2 does not exclude alpha-thalassaemia; DNA testing is needed if it cannot be excluded.

---

REFERENCES
