• {"name": "O&G Magazine (RANZCOG). Thalassaemia and haemoglobinopathy screening in pregnancy (2024)", "url": "https://www.ogmagazine.org.au/24/1-24/thalassaemia-and-haemoglobinopathy-screening-in-pregnancy/"}

---

EDITED BY HEISENBERG','difficult','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "bank170", "review_status": "owner_approved_2026_09_29", "tags": ["ZQ-0176", "zyntra_bank", "mock1", "level_4", "task_data_gathering", "group_womens_health", "sys_obstetrics", "approved_by_owner_2026_09_29"], "clinician_task": "data_gathering", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"RACGP. Genomics in general practice: Haemoglobinopathies\", \"url\": \"https://www.racgp.org.au/clinical-resources/clinical-guidelines/key-racgp-guidelines/view-all-racgp-guidelines/genomics-in-general-practice/disease-specific-topics/haemoglobinopathies\"}; {\"name\": \"O&G Magazine (RANZCOG). Thalassaemia and haemoglobinopathy screening in pregnancy (2024)\", \"url\": \"https://www.ogmagazine.org.au/24/1-24/thalassaemia-and-haemoglobinopathy-screening-in-pregnancy/\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "Her ferritin is 85 µg/L, so she is not iron deficient. Giving iron and waiting 6 weeks wastes time for prenatal diagnosis if the couple is at risk.", "B": "A normal HbA2 does not exclude alpha-thalassaemia trait, which is likely given her indices and ancestry.", "D": "CVS is offered only once the couple is shown to be at risk. It carries a small miscarriage risk and is premature before partner testing.", "E": "Standard cfDNA screening is for chromosomal aneuploidy. It is not a routine screening test for fetal thalassaemia."}, "key_takeaways": ["Low MCV/MCH with normal ferritin in pregnancy: test the partner now. A normal HbA2 does not exclude alpha-thalassaemia; DNA testing is needed if it cannot be excluded."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,1.0,1.0,'tier_prior','tier=4 (bank170)'),
('9d771427-fef3-5965-82cb-3b432be85332'::uuid,'ZQ-0177','womens-health','gynaecology','A 29-year-old woman comes to the emergency department of Mount Isa Hospital, Queensland, with 2 days of left iliac fossa pain and light vaginal bleeding. Her last menstrual period was 7 weeks ago. She works as a cook on a cattle station 380 km away, reached by an unsealed road that often becomes impassable after rain. There is no mobile phone reception at the station. She plans to return there in two days. She has had one previous vaginal birth and has no medical problems. She is not breastfeeding.

Her temperature is 36.8°C, pulse 82/min and blood pressure 118/74 mmHg. She has mild left iliac fossa tenderness without guarding.

Investigations:
- Serum β-hCG 1800 IU/L; repeat at 48 hours 1950 IU/L
- Haemoglobin 128 g/L
- Liver and renal function normal
- Blood group A Rh D positive
- Transvaginal ultrasound: empty uterus; 2.4 cm inhomogeneous left adnexal mass separate from the ovary, with no yolk sac or fetal heart; small amount of free fluid in the pouch of Douglas; right tube and ovary normal

She would like to have more children in future.

Which one of the following is the most appropriate management?','["Intramuscular methotrexate 50 mg/m² and β-hCG on days 4 and 7", "Expectant management with β-hCG every 48 hours", "Intravenous methotrexate infusion because β-hCG is rising", "Laparoscopic left salpingectomy", "Laparoscopic left salpingostomy"]'::jsonb,'D','CORRECT ANSWER

D. Laparoscopic left salpingectomy

---

She has a stable, unruptured left tubal ectopic pregnancy with a plateauing β-hCG below 3000 IU/L, a mass under 3.5 cm and no fetal heart activity. On biochemical and ultrasound grounds she would be a candidate for methotrexate. But medical management needs reliable follow-up for weeks (serial β-hCG until it is undetectable, often 4–6 weeks or longer, with ready access to emergency care in case of rupture). The Queensland Clinical Guideline on early pregnancy loss lists geographic isolation and inability to take part in prolonged follow-up as contraindications to methotrexate. Rupture 380 km from hospital without phone access could be fatal. So laparoscopic surgery is safest. With a healthy contralateral tube, salpingectomy is preferred to salpingostomy: subsequent intrauterine pregnancy rates are similar, and salpingectomy avoids persistent trophoblast and the need for β-hCG follow-up.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Methotrexate for ectopic pregnancy needs reliable follow-up. Geographic isolation or inability to attend is a contraindication: offer laparoscopic salpingectomy if the other tube is healthy.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "Queensland Clinical Guidelines. Early pregnancy loss (guideline and flowchart)", "url": "https://www.health.qld.gov.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0033/139947/g-epl.pdf"}
• {"name": "RANZCOG. Miscarriage, recurrent miscarriage and ectopic pregnancy (C-Gyn 38)", "url": "https://www.ranzcog.edu.au/wp-content/uploads/Miscarriage-Ectopic-Pregnancy.pdf"}

---

EDITED BY HEISENBERG','difficult','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "bank170", "review_status": "owner_approved_2026_09_29", "tags": ["ZQ-0177", "zyntra_bank", "mock1", "level_4", "task_management", "group_womens_health", "sys_gynaecology", "approved_by_owner_2026_09_29"], "clinician_task": "management", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"Queensland Clinical Guidelines. Early pregnancy loss (guideline and flowchart)\", \"url\": \"https://www.health.qld.gov.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0033/139947/g-epl.pdf\"}; {\"name\": \"RANZCOG. Miscarriage, recurrent miscarriage and ectopic pregnancy (C-Gyn 38)\", \"url\": \"https://www.ranzcog.edu.au/wp-content/uploads/Miscarriage-Ectopic-Pregnancy.pdf\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "Her β-hCG, mass size and lack of fetal heart would suit IM methotrexate, but follow-up is not feasible and she lives far from emergency care. This is exactly the situation the guideline warns against.", "B": "Expectant management is for a low and falling β-hCG (e.g. below 1500 IU/L at presentation) in a woman who can attend follow-up. Her β-hCG is above that and not falling, and follow-up is not possible.", "C": "The Queensland protocol uses IV methotrexate when β-hCG is above 3000 IU/L. Hers is below that, and the same follow-up problem applies whatever the route.", "E": "Salpingostomy conserves the tube but needs weekly β-hCG follow-up for persistent trophoblast (about 1 in 10–20). It is considered when the other tube is damaged. Her other tube is normal and follow-up is the problem."}, "key_takeaways": ["Methotrexate for ectopic pregnancy needs reliable follow-up. Geographic isolation or inability to attend is a contraindication: offer laparoscopic salpingectomy if the other tube is healthy."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,1.0,1.0,'tier_prior','tier=4 (bank170)'),
('23138b6a-e44a-54f9-b9af-e4c36a14618a'::uuid,'ZQ-0179','womens-health','obstetrics','A 30-year-old woman in her third pregnancy presents to the maternity unit of Broken Hill Hospital, New South Wales, at 31 weeks'' gestation with a sudden episode of painless, bright red vaginal bleeding that woke her from sleep. She estimates about 200 mL of blood loss, which has now slowed to light spotting. She has had two previous caesarean births. She booked late at 26 weeks after moving from interstate and missed her morphology ultrasound, so she has not yet had a scan in this pregnancy. She reports normal fetal movements. She has no pain, contractions or leakage of fluid, and no recent intercourse or trauma.

Her pulse is 92/min and blood pressure 116/72 mmHg. Her abdomen is soft and non-tender between palpations, the uterus is not irritable, fundal height is 31 cm, and the fetus is in a transverse lie. CTG shows a baseline of 140 bpm with normal variability, accelerations and no decelerations. Blood group is O Rh D positive. IV access has been obtained and blood sent for full blood count, coagulation and group and hold.

Which one of the following is the most appropriate next step in assessment?','["Digital vaginal examination to assess the cervix", "Ultrasound to locate the placenta before any vaginal examination", "Kleihauer test to quantify fetomaternal haemorrhage", "Discharge home if the CTG remains normal for a further 2 hours", "Immediate caesarean section under general anaesthesia"]'::jsonb,'B','CORRECT ANSWER

B. Ultrasound to locate the placenta before any vaginal examination

---

Painless bright red bleeding in the third trimester with a non-tender uterus and an abnormal lie (transverse) suggests placenta praevia until proven otherwise. Her two previous caesareans increase the risk of both praevia and placenta accreta spectrum. Her placental site is unknown because she has had no scan. A digital vaginal examination must not be done until placenta praevia has been excluded, because it can provoke catastrophic haemorrhage (RCOG Green-top 63 and Australian local APH guidelines such as SESLHD). The next step is ultrasound to locate the placenta. Transvaginal ultrasound is safe in suspected praevia and is more accurate than transabdominal scanning. A gentle speculum examination can then be used to assess the cervix and bleeding. She will also need antenatal corticosteroids (at 31 weeks, with risk of preterm birth), admission, and plans for transfer to a tertiary unit with accreta expertise if praevia is confirmed.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
APH with unknown placental site: no digital vaginal examination until ultrasound excludes placenta praevia. Transvaginal ultrasound is safe and more accurate.

---

