---

EDITED BY HEISENBERG','moderate','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "bank170", "review_status": "owner_approved_2026_09_29", "tags": ["ZQ-0179", "zyntra_bank", "mock1", "level_3", "task_data_gathering", "group_womens_health", "sys_obstetrics", "approved_by_owner_2026_09_29"], "clinician_task": "data_gathering", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"RCOG Green-top Guideline No. 63. Antepartum Haemorrhage\", \"url\": \"https://www.rcog.org.uk/media/pwdi1tef/gtg_63.pdf\"}; {\"name\": \"South Eastern Sydney LHD. Antepartum haemorrhage (APH) clinical guideline (2020)\", \"url\": \"https://www.seslhd.health.nsw.gov.au/sites/default/files/documents/antepartumhaemorrhage-aph2020.pdf\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "Digital examination is contraindicated until praevia is excluded. It may cause torrential bleeding.", "C": "She is Rh D positive, so a Kleihauer test to guide Rh D immunoglobulin dosing is not needed. It does not diagnose the cause of bleeding.", "D": "A normal CTG does not exclude praevia. Any APH with an unknown placental site at 31 weeks needs admission and imaging, and praevia bleeds often recur.", "E": "She and the fetus are currently stable and bleeding has slowed. Emergency caesarean is for heavy ongoing bleeding or fetal compromise. Delivery at 31 weeks has important risks and should be planned if possible."}, "key_takeaways": ["APH with unknown placental site: no digital vaginal examination until ultrasound excludes placenta praevia. Transvaginal ultrasound is safe and more accurate."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,0.0,1.0,'tier_prior','tier=3 (bank170)'),
('6a3aa535-e672-568e-bcac-58453f39275c'::uuid,'ZQ-0180','womens-health','obstetrics','A 34-year-old woman gave birth vaginally 20 minutes ago at a hospital in Bundaberg, Queensland, after an induction of labour at 38 weeks for pre-eclampsia. Her blood pressure has been controlled with oral labetalol. She has moderate asthma treated with budesonide/formoterol. She received oxytocin 10 IU IM with delivery of the anterior shoulder, and the placenta and membranes were delivered complete 10 minutes later.

She is now bleeding heavily, with an estimated blood loss of 1100 mL. Her pulse is 116/min and blood pressure 108/66 mmHg (it was 152/98 mmHg before birth). The uterus is soft and boggy and rises above the umbilicus; it firms briefly with fundal massage and bimanual compression. The bladder has been emptied by catheter. A rapid inspection shows no significant genital tract trauma. The PPH emergency has been called. Two large-bore cannulas are in place, bloods (including fibrinogen) have been sent, and she has been given oxytocin 5 IU IV twice, 5 minutes apart. An oxytocin infusion has been started. Bleeding continues.

Which one of the following is the most appropriate medication to give now?','["Ergometrine 250 micrograms IV", "Carboprost 250 micrograms IM", "Recombinant activated factor VII IV", "Misoprostol 200 micrograms orally", "Tranexamic acid 1 g IV over 10 minutes"]'::jsonb,'E','CORRECT ANSWER

E. Tranexamic acid 1 g IV over 10 minutes

---

This is a major primary PPH from uterine atony (tone), continuing despite oxytocin boluses, an infusion and uterine massage. The Queensland Clinical Guideline on primary PPH recommends tranexamic acid 1 g IV over 10 minutes as soon as possible after the onset of PPH, ideally within 3 hours, in addition to uterotonics. The WOMAN trial showed that early TXA reduces death from bleeding, with no increase in thromboembolic events. A second 1 g dose can be given if bleeding continues after 30 minutes. Her other uterotonic options are limited: ergometrine is contraindicated in pre-eclampsia and severe hypertension, and carboprost (a prostaglandin F2α) can cause bronchospasm and should be avoided or used with great caution in asthma. Alongside TXA, she needs continued bimanual compression, blood products and fibrinogen replacement guided by results, and early transfer to theatre for balloon tamponade or surgical measures if bleeding continues.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
In PPH, give tranexamic acid 1 g IV early (within 3 hours) alongside uterotonics. Ergometrine is contraindicated in pre-eclampsia or hypertension; carboprost is a problem in asthma.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "Queensland Clinical Guidelines. Primary postpartum haemorrhage (PPH): guideline, flowchart and Table 17 Tranexamic acid", "url": "https://www.health.qld.gov.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0015/140136/g-pph.pdf"}
• {"name": "RANZCOG. Management of postpartum haemorrhage (C-Obs 43)", "url": "https://www.ranzcog.edu.au/wp-content/uploads/Management-Postpartum-Haemorrhage.pdf"}

---

EDITED BY HEISENBERG','difficult','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "bank170", "review_status": "owner_approved_2026_09_29", "tags": ["ZQ-0180", "zyntra_bank", "mock1", "level_4", "task_management", "group_womens_health", "sys_obstetrics", "approved_by_owner_2026_09_29"], "clinician_task": "management", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"Queensland Clinical Guidelines. Primary postpartum haemorrhage (PPH): guideline, flowchart and Table 17 Tranexamic acid\", \"url\": \"https://www.health.qld.gov.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0015/140136/g-pph.pdf\"}; {\"name\": \"RANZCOG. Management of postpartum haemorrhage (C-Obs 43)\", \"url\": \"https://www.ranzcog.edu.au/wp-content/uploads/Management-Postpartum-Haemorrhage.pdf\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "Ergometrine is a first-line uterotonic in the Queensland algorithm, but it is contraindicated in pre-eclampsia and severe hypertension because it can cause severe hypertension, stroke and seizures.", "B": "Carboprost is a second-line uterotonic and can cause severe bronchospasm in asthma. It is not the best next drug in a woman with moderate asthma.", "C": "Recombinant factor VIIa is a last-resort measure for refractory haemorrhage after other medical and surgical measures and fibrinogen/coagulation correction. It is not used at this stage.", "D": "Misoprostol is an option when other uterotonics are unavailable or contraindicated, but its onset is slow and the recommended treatment dose is higher (800–1000 micrograms, usually PR). A 200 microgram oral dose is inadequate."}, "key_takeaways": ["In PPH, give tranexamic acid 1 g IV early (within 3 hours) alongside uterotonics. Ergometrine is contraindicated in pre-eclampsia or hypertension; carboprost is a problem in asthma."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,1.0,1.0,'tier_prior','tier=4 (bank170)'),
('22315aa3-fb32-5e7c-953d-40814c58cd1a'::uuid,'ZQ-0181','womens-health','obstetrics','A 33-year-old woman in her second pregnancy is in spontaneous labour at 39 weeks and 4 days at a hospital in Ballarat, Victoria. Her first baby was born by lower segment caesarean section for breech presentation 3 years ago, and she has chosen a planned vaginal birth after caesarean. Labour was augmented with an oxytocin infusion 4 hours ago because of slow progress, and she has an effective epidural. Continuous CTG monitoring is in place. At the last examination, 1 hour ago, the cervix was 8 cm dilated with the vertex at station 0.

She now calls the midwife because of sudden, severe, constant lower abdominal pain that is breaking through her epidural, with pain in her right shoulder. There is fresh vaginal bleeding of about 150 mL. Her pulse has risen from 88 to 124/min and her blood pressure has fallen from 124/76 to 96/58 mmHg. The CTG shows a prolonged deceleration to 70 bpm lasting 4 minutes, and the tocograph shows that contractions have become weak and irregular. On vaginal examination the vertex is now at station −3 and easily pushed up.

Which one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?','["Uterine rupture", "Placental abruption", "Uterine tachysystole caused by oxytocin", "Umbilical cord prolapse", "Ruptured vasa praevia"]'::jsonb,'A','CORRECT ANSWER

A. Uterine rupture

---

Uterine rupture is the key risk of labour after caesarean (about 0.5–0.7% after one lower segment incision, higher with induction or augmentation). The most common and often earliest sign is an abnormal fetal heart rate pattern, especially a prolonged deceleration or bradycardia. Other features are new constant abdominal pain (including pain breaking through an epidural), shoulder-tip pain from haemoperitoneum, vaginal bleeding, maternal tachycardia and hypotension, a change in the contraction pattern (contractions weaken or stop), and loss of station of the presenting part as the fetus moves out of the uterus. She has all of these. Management is immediate: stop the oxytocin, call for help, resuscitate, and perform a category 1 caesarean (laparotomy) with neonatal team present. RANZCOG C-Obs 38 (Birth after previous caesarean) advises continuous fetal monitoring in labour for this reason.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
In labour after caesarean: prolonged deceleration or bradycardia with new constant pain, shoulder-tip pain, bleeding, maternal shock, weaker contractions and loss of station = uterine rupture. Immediate laparotomy.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "RANZCOG. Birth After Caesarean (C-Obs 38), updated 2025", "url": "https://ranzcog.edu.au/wp-content/uploads/Birth-After-Caesarean.pdf"}
• {"name": "StatPearls. Uterine Rupture (NCBI Bookshelf)", "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK559209/"}

---

EDITED BY HEISENBERG','moderate','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "bank170", "review_status": "owner_approved_2026_09_29", "tags": ["ZQ-0181", "zyntra_bank", "mock1", "level_3", "task_data_interpretation_synthesis", "group_womens_health", "sys_obstetrics", "approved_by_owner_2026_09_29"], "clinician_task": "data_interpretation_synthesis", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"RANZCOG. Birth After Caesarean (C-Obs 38), updated 2025\", \"url\": \"https://ranzcog.edu.au/wp-content/uploads/Birth-After-Caesarean.pdf\"}; {\"name\": \"StatPearls. Uterine Rupture (NCBI Bookshelf)\", \"url\": \"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK559209/\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"B": "Abruption also causes pain, bleeding and fetal compromise, but the uterus becomes tense and hypertonic with frequent contractions. It does not cause loss of station, and contractions do not fade.", "C": "Tachysystole means more than 5 contractions in 10 minutes. Her contractions have become weaker, and tachysystole does not cause shoulder-tip pain, maternal shock or loss of station.", "D": "Cord prolapse causes fetal bradycardia, but it needs ruptured membranes with a cord felt or seen. It does not cause maternal pain, bleeding or shock.", "E": "Vasa praevia bleeding is fetal blood, typically at membrane rupture, causing a sinusoidal pattern or bradycardia with a well mother. It does not cause maternal shock, pain or loss of station."}, "key_takeaways": ["In labour after caesarean: prolonged deceleration or bradycardia with new constant pain, shoulder-tip pain, bleeding, maternal shock, weaker contractions and loss of station = uterine rupture. Immediate laparotomy."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,0.0,1.0,'tier_prior','tier=3 (bank170)'),
('7f9a465c-5774-58bf-b02f-80c4eb712498'::uuid,'ZQ-0182','womens-health','obstetrics','A 31-year-old woman in her first pregnancy sees her general practitioner in Geelong, Victoria, at 27 weeks'' gestation for the result of her routine 75 g pregnancy oral glucose tolerance test, done at 26 weeks. She is of South Asian background, her BMI before pregnancy was 27 kg/m², and her mother has type 2 diabetes. Her first-trimester HbA1c was 5.4% (36 mmol/mol). The pregnancy has been uncomplicated, and fundal height is 27 cm. Her previous GP had told her she was ''likely to get gestational diabetes'' because of her risk factors.

75 g pregnancy OGTT (venous plasma glucose), taken in September 2026:
- Fasting 5.2 mmol/L
- 1 hour 10.3 mmol/L
- 2 hours 8.8 mmol/L

Which one of the following is the most accurate interpretation of this result?','["Gestational diabetes on each of the three values; refer for diabetes education and monitoring", "Gestational diabetes on the fasting value alone", "Overt diabetes in pregnancy; start insulin", "Does not meet the criteria for gestational diabetes; continue routine antenatal care", "Borderline result; repeat the OGTT at 32 weeks"]'::jsonb,'D','CORRECT ANSWER

D. Does not meet the criteria for gestational diabetes; continue routine antenatal care

---

In 2025 the Australasian Diabetes in Pregnancy Society (ADIPS) raised the diagnostic thresholds for gestational diabetes on the 75 g pregnancy OGTT. GDM is now diagnosed if ANY of: fasting 5.3–6.9 mmol/L, 1-hour 10.6 mmol/L or more, or 2-hour 9.0–11.0 mmol/L. The previous (IADPSG/WHO 2013-based) thresholds were 5.1, 10.0 and 8.5 mmol/L. Her values (5.2, 10.3, 8.8) would have met all three OLD criteria but meet NONE of the new ones, so she does not have GDM. Victorian public and private pathology services changed to the new criteria on 4 August 2025. Overt diabetes in pregnancy is fasting 7.0 or more, 2-hour 11.1 or more, or HbA1c 6.5% or more. She should continue routine care with healthy eating and activity advice, and a repeat OGTT is not routinely recommended without a new clinical indication.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
ADIPS 2025 GDM thresholds (75 g POGTT): fasting ≥5.3, 1-hour ≥10.6, 2-hour ≥9.0 mmol/L. The old 5.1 / 10.0 / 8.5 cut-offs are superseded.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "ADIPS 2025 consensus recommendations for the screening, diagnosis and classification of gestational diabetes. Med J Aust 2025;223(3)", "url": "https://www.mja.com.au/journal/2025/223/3/australasian-diabetes-pregnancy-society-adips-2025-consensus-recommendations"}
• {"name": "Safer Care Victoria. New gestational diabetes guideline from ADIPS (16 Sep 2025)", "url": "https://www.safercare.vic.gov.au/news/new-gestational-diabetes-guideline-from-the-australian-diabetes-in-pregnancy-society-adips"}
• {"name": "ADIPS. Frequently asked questions for health professionals (June 2025)", "url": "https://www.adips.org/FAQ_for%20health%20professionals%2020250623.pdf"}

---

EDITED BY HEISENBERG','difficult','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "bank170", "review_status": "owner_approved_2026_09_29", "tags": ["ZQ-0182", "zyntra_bank", "mock1", "level_5", "task_data_interpretation_synthesis", "group_womens_health", "sys_obstetrics", "approved_by_owner_2026_09_29"], "clinician_task": "data_interpretation_synthesis", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"ADIPS 2025 consensus recommendations for the screening, diagnosis and classification of gestational diabetes. Med J Aust 2025;223(3)\", \"url\": \"https://www.mja.com.au/journal/2025/223/3/australasian-diabetes-pregnancy-society-adips-2025-consensus-recommendations\"}; {\"name\": \"Safer Care Victoria. New gestational diabetes guideline from ADIPS (16 Sep 2025)\", \"url\": \"https://www.safercare.vic.gov.au/news/new-gestational-diabetes-guideline-from-the-australian-diabetes-in-pregnancy-society-adips\"}; {\"name\": \"ADIPS. Frequently asked questions for health professionals (June 2025)\", \"url\": \"https://www.adips.org/FAQ_for%20health%20professionals%2020250623.pdf\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "This applies the superseded thresholds (5.1 / 10.0 / 8.5). Under ADIPS 2025 none of her values reach the diagnostic cut-offs.", "B": "The fasting threshold is now 5.3 mmol/L, so 5.2 is below it.", "C": "Overt diabetes needs fasting 7.0 or more, 2-hour 11.1 or more, or HbA1c 6.5% or more. She meets none.", "E": "ADIPS 2025 does not define a ''borderline'' category that needs a routine repeat OGTT at 32 weeks. Repeat testing depends on new clinical concerns (e.g. fetal overgrowth or polyhydramnios)."}, "key_takeaways": ["ADIPS 2025 GDM thresholds (75 g POGTT): fasting ≥5.3, 1-hour ≥10.6, 2-hour ≥9.0 mmol/L. The old 5.1 / 10.0 / 8.5 cut-offs are superseded."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,2.0,1.0,'tier_prior','tier=5 (bank170)'),
('715623c1-0407-50c9-92d1-3639e9438aa6'::uuid,'ZQ-0183','womens-health','obstetrics','A 36-year-old woman sees her general practitioner in Darwin at 11 weeks and 4 days of her second pregnancy, confirmed by a dating scan. She has had essential hypertension for 4 years, treated with perindopril 5 mg daily, and her blood pressure has been well controlled. She did not plan the pregnancy. Her first pregnancy, 5 years ago, was complicated by pre-eclampsia at 35 weeks. She takes no other medicines, does not smoke, and eats little dairy (she estimates under 1 serve a day).

Her blood pressure is 134/86 mmHg on repeated measurement. Her BMI is 31 kg/m². Urinalysis shows no proteinuria. Renal function, liver function, platelets and urine protein:creatinine ratio are normal.

Which one of the following is the most appropriate management?','["Continue perindopril until 20 weeks, then change to labetalol", "Change perindopril to labetalol and start aspirin 150 mg daily now", "Change perindopril to methyldopa and start aspirin 100 mg daily now", "Change perindopril to labetalol and start aspirin 150 mg daily from 20 weeks", "Stop perindopril without a replacement, as blood pressure falls in the second trimester"]'::jsonb,'B','CORRECT ANSWER

B. Change perindopril to labetalol and start aspirin 150 mg daily now

---

ACE inhibitors (and ARBs) should be stopped as soon as pregnancy is confirmed, because they are fetotoxic in the second and third trimesters (renal dysgenesis, oligohydramnios, skull hypoplasia). They should be replaced with a pregnancy-compatible drug such as labetalol, nifedipine or methyldopa. She is at high risk of pre-eclampsia (chronic hypertension and previous pre-eclampsia, plus age and BMI). The SOMANZ 2023 hypertension in pregnancy guideline strongly recommends aspirin 150 mg daily for women at high risk, started before 16 weeks and stopped between 34 weeks and birth. Because her dietary calcium intake is low (under 1 g/day), SOMANZ also recommends calcium supplementation (e.g. 1–1.5 g/day). Her BP target on treatment is around 110–140/80–85 mmHg; stopping all treatment would risk uncontrolled hypertension.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Chronic hypertension in pregnancy: stop ACE inhibitors/ARBs now, switch to labetalol, nifedipine or methyldopa. High pre-eclampsia risk: aspirin 150 mg daily started before 16 weeks, plus calcium if intake is low (SOMANZ 2023).

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "SOMANZ Hypertension in Pregnancy Guideline 2023", "url": "https://www.somanz.org/content/uploads/2023/06/SOMANZ_Hypertension_in_Pregnancy_Guideline_2023_Updated_30.6.23.pdf"}
• {"name": "A summary of the 2023 SOMANZ hypertension in pregnancy guideline. Med J Aust 2024;220(11)", "url": "https://www.mja.com.au/journal/2024/220/11/summary-2023-society-obstetric-medicine-australia-and-new-zealand-somanz"}

---

EDITED BY HEISENBERG','difficult','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "bank170", "review_status": "owner_approved_2026_09_29", "tags": ["ZQ-0183", "zyntra_bank", "mock1", "level_4", "task_management", "group_womens_health", "sys_obstetrics", "approved_by_owner_2026_09_29"], "clinician_task": "management", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"SOMANZ Hypertension in Pregnancy Guideline 2023\", \"url\": \"https://www.somanz.org/content/uploads/2023/06/SOMANZ_Hypertension_in_Pregnancy_Guideline_2023_Updated_30.6.23.pdf\"}; {\"name\": \"A summary of the 2023 SOMANZ hypertension in pregnancy guideline. Med J Aust 2024;220(11)\", \"url\": \"https://www.mja.com.au/journal/2024/220/11/summary-2023-society-obstetric-medicine-australia-and-new-zealand-somanz\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "ACE inhibitors are fetotoxic from the second trimester. Continuing to 20 weeks is unsafe; they should be changed now.", "C": "Methyldopa is an acceptable antihypertensive in pregnancy, but SOMANZ 2023 recommends 150 mg of aspirin, not 100 mg, for high-risk women. This is the trap.", "D": "The dose is right but the timing is wrong. Aspirin is most effective started before 16 weeks, and has little benefit started at 20 weeks.", "E": "BP often falls in mid-pregnancy, but she has established hypertension and a history of pre-eclampsia. Stopping treatment altogether risks severe hypertension, and she still needs aspirin."}, "key_takeaways": ["Chronic hypertension in pregnancy: stop ACE inhibitors/ARBs now, switch to labetalol, nifedipine or methyldopa. High pre-eclampsia risk: aspirin 150 mg daily started before 16 weeks, plus calcium if intake is low (SOMANZ 2023)."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,1.0,1.0,'tier_prior','tier=4 (bank170)'),
('daac648d-266f-5764-811c-1cae45fade4f'::uuid,'ZQ-0184','womens-health','gynaecology','A 24-year-old woman sees a pharmacist-referred general practitioner in Bunbury, Western Australia, on a Friday afternoon. She had unprotected vaginal intercourse on Monday night, about 90 hours ago, when a condom broke. Her last menstrual period started 13 days ago, and her cycles are regular at 28 days. This was the only episode of unprotected sex this cycle. She wants emergency contraception and would also like to start the combined oral contraceptive pill for ongoing contraception. She has a strong fear of pelvic procedures and declines an intrauterine device after discussion.

She weighs 92 kg (BMI 33 kg/m²) and her blood pressure is 118/74 mmHg. She has no migraine, no personal or family history of venous thromboembolism, does not smoke and takes no other medicines. A urine pregnancy test is negative.

Which one of the following is the most appropriate management?','["Ulipristal acetate 30 mg now and start the combined pill today", "Levonorgestrel 1.5 mg now and start the combined pill today", "Ulipristal acetate 30 mg now and start the combined pill 5 days later, with condoms meanwhile", "Levonorgestrel 3 mg now and start the combined pill today", "No emergency contraception needed, as ovulation has probably already occurred"]'::jsonb,'C','CORRECT ANSWER

C. Ulipristal acetate 30 mg now and start the combined pill 5 days later, with condoms meanwhile

---

The copper IUD is the most effective emergency contraception (up to 120 hours, unaffected by weight), but she has declined it. Of the oral options, ulipristal acetate (UPA) is more effective than levonorgestrel (LNG), particularly beyond 72 hours and in women with a higher BMI. LNG is licensed in Australia up to 72 hours (with some efficacy to 96 hours), while UPA is licensed up to 120 hours. She is at 90 hours, mid-cycle, with a BMI of 33, so UPA is the best oral choice. UPA is a progesterone receptor modulator: progestogens taken in the following days can reduce its ability to delay ovulation. Australian guidance (e.g. Australian Prescriber; Sexual Health and Family Planning Australia) is to wait 5 days after UPA before starting hormonal contraception, using condoms or abstinence in the meantime and then for the first 7 days of active combined pills. A pregnancy test is advised 3–4 weeks after UPSI if the next period is delayed or unusual.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Emergency contraception: copper IUD is most effective. If declined, UPA beats LNG beyond 72 hours and at higher BMI. After UPA, wait 5 days before starting hormonal contraception.

---

REFERENCES
