• {"name": "RACGP (AFP 2017). Emergency contraception: oral and intrauterine options", "url": "https://www.racgp.org.au/afp/2017/october/emergency-contraception-oral-and-intrauterine-opti"}

---

EDITED BY HEISENBERG','difficult','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "bank170", "review_status": "owner_approved_2026_09_29", "tags": ["ZQ-0184", "zyntra_bank", "mock1", "level_4", "task_management", "group_womens_health", "sys_gynaecology", "approved_by_owner_2026_09_29"], "clinician_task": "management", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"Australian Prescriber. Emergency contraception: an overview\", \"url\": \"https://australianprescriber.tg.org.au/articles/emergency-contraception-an-overview.html\"}; {\"name\": \"Sexual Health and Family Planning Australia. Statement on emergency contraception\", \"url\": \"https://www.fpnsw.org.au/sites/default/files/assets/shfpa_ec.pdf\"}; {\"name\": \"RACGP (AFP 2017). Emergency contraception: oral and intrauterine options\", \"url\": \"https://www.racgp.org.au/afp/2017/october/emergency-contraception-oral-and-intrauterine-opti\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "Starting a progestogen-containing pill straight after UPA may reduce UPA''s effectiveness. The pill should be delayed 5 days.", "B": "LNG can be started with the pill immediately, but at 90 hours it is outside its licensed window and less effective than UPA, especially at a BMI of 33.", "D": "Some guidelines suggest a double LNG dose for higher weight, but this is off-label, the evidence is weak, and at 90 hours UPA is more effective anyway.", "E": "She is on day 13 of a 28-day cycle, the time of highest pregnancy risk. Ovulation may not have occurred, and emergency contraception is clearly indicated."}, "key_takeaways": ["Emergency contraception: copper IUD is most effective. If declined, UPA beats LNG beyond 72 hours and at higher BMI. After UPA, wait 5 days before starting hormonal contraception."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,1.0,1.0,'tier_prior','tier=4 (bank170)'),
('601ef0a9-7076-5b9a-a010-566fc3daa90a'::uuid,'ZQ-0185','womens-health','gynaecology','A 26-year-old woman sees her general practitioner in Wollongong, New South Wales, to start contraception. She has been using condoms but would like something more reliable and has read about the combined oral contraceptive pill. She has ''sick headaches'' three or four times a year that last most of a day and make her lie down in a dark room. She takes ibuprofen for them. She has no other medical history and takes no regular medicines. She has no personal or family history of venous thromboembolism, and her BMI is 23 kg/m². Her blood pressure is 114/70 mmHg. She had a normal cervical screening test 2 years ago.

Before deciding whether the combined pill is suitable, which one of the following is the most important additional information to obtain?','["Whether she smokes cigarettes", "Whether any relative has had breast cancer", "Whether she has ever had a sexually transmitted infection", "Whether she has had a previous ectopic pregnancy", "Whether her headaches are preceded by visual or sensory symptoms"]'::jsonb,'E','CORRECT ANSWER

E. Whether her headaches are preceded by visual or sensory symptoms

---

Her headaches sound like migraine. The key question is whether she has migraine WITH aura (e.g. spreading zig-zag lines, scotoma, unilateral tingling or numbness developing over minutes before the headache). Migraine with aura is UKMEC category 4 (unacceptable health risk) for combined hormonal contraception at any age, because oestrogen further increases the already raised risk of ischaemic stroke. Migraine without aura is category 2 for starting the pill under 35. This single question changes her options most. If she has aura, suitable alternatives include progestogen-only methods (implant, LNG-IUD, progestogen-only pill, DMPA) and the copper IUD. Australian clinicians commonly use the UK Medical Eligibility Criteria (UKMEC), as recommended in Australian contraception resources.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Before prescribing combined hormonal contraception to a woman with migraine, ask about aura. Migraine with aura is UKMEC 4 at any age: offer progestogen-only or copper IUD methods.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "Medicine Today (2021). Contraception choice dilemma: a woman with migraine with aura", "url": "https://medicinetoday.com.au/mt/2021/november/regular-series/contraception-choice-dilemma-woman-migraine-aura"}
• {"name": "Australian Prescriber. Choosing a combined oral contraceptive pill", "url": "https://australianprescriber.tg.org.au/articles/choosing-a-combined-oral-contraceptive-pill.html"}
• {"name": "RACGP (AFP 2017). Oral hormonal contraception in special circumstances", "url": "https://www.racgp.org.au/afp/2017/october/oral-hormonal-contraception-in-special-circumstanc"}

---

EDITED BY HEISENBERG','moderate','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "bank170", "review_status": "owner_approved_2026_09_29", "tags": ["ZQ-0185", "zyntra_bank", "mock1", "level_3", "task_data_gathering", "group_womens_health", "sys_gynaecology", "approved_by_owner_2026_09_29"], "clinician_task": "data_gathering", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"Medicine Today (2021). Contraception choice dilemma: a woman with migraine with aura\", \"url\": \"https://medicinetoday.com.au/mt/2021/november/regular-series/contraception-choice-dilemma-woman-migraine-aura\"}; {\"name\": \"Australian Prescriber. Choosing a combined oral contraceptive pill\", \"url\": \"https://australianprescriber.tg.org.au/articles/choosing-a-combined-oral-contraceptive-pill.html\"}; {\"name\": \"RACGP (AFP 2017). Oral hormonal contraception in special circumstances\", \"url\": \"https://www.racgp.org.au/afp/2017/october/oral-hormonal-contraception-in-special-circumstanc\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "Smoking matters for the combined pill from age 35 (category 3–4 depending on amount). At 26, smoking is category 2 and would not usually prevent use.", "B": "A family history of breast cancer is UKMEC category 1 for the combined pill. It does not change eligibility.", "C": "STI history is important for sexual health and IUD decisions, but it does not affect eligibility for the combined pill.", "D": "Previous ectopic pregnancy is category 1 for the combined pill (it is relevant for progestogen-only pill counselling). It does not change the decision."}, "key_takeaways": ["Before prescribing combined hormonal contraception to a woman with migraine, ask about aura. Migraine with aura is UKMEC 4 at any age: offer progestogen-only or copper IUD methods."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,0.0,1.0,'tier_prior','tier=3 (bank170)'),
('a496ac21-144b-5e96-b142-aa7b7a60ab0f'::uuid,'ZQ-0186','womens-health','gynaecology','A 23-year-old woman sees her general practitioner in Hobart because of heavy periods. Her periods have been heavy since they started at 12: she bleeds for 8 days, changes a super tampon and pad every 1–2 hours on the heaviest days, passes large clots and has had to leave work because of flooding. Her cycles are regular at 28 days. She has frequent nosebleeds that sometimes last 20 minutes, bruises easily, and needed a return visit to the dentist for bleeding after a wisdom tooth extraction. Her mother had very heavy periods and a hysterectomy at 38. She is not sexually active and has never been pregnant. She takes no medicines, including no NSAIDs or supplements.

Examination shows scattered bruises on her shins. Abdominal examination is normal. She declines a pelvic examination.

Investigations:
- Haemoglobin 104 g/L (115–160), MCV 74 fL (80–100)
- Platelets 268 × 10^9/L (150–400)
- Ferritin 6 µg/L (30–200)
- TSH normal
- Urine β-hCG negative

Which one of the following is the most appropriate next investigation?','["VWF antigen and activity, factor VIII and coagulation studies", "Transvaginal pelvic ultrasound on days 5–10 of the cycle", "Endometrial biopsy", "Serum FSH, LH and oestradiol", "Cervical screening test"]'::jsonb,'A','CORRECT ANSWER

A. VWF antigen and activity, factor VIII and coagulation studies

---

She has heavy menstrual bleeding since menarche with iron deficiency anaemia, AND a personal bleeding history (prolonged nosebleeds, easy bruising, bleeding after dental extraction) and a family history suggestive of heavy bleeding. This strongly suggests an inherited bleeding disorder, most often von Willebrand disease, which is present in a meaningful proportion of women with HMB and is the most common inherited bleeding disorder. The Heavy Menstrual Bleeding Clinical Care Standard (ACSQHC 2024) says testing for bleeding disorders, thyroid function, cervical screening and ultrasound are not routine for everyone, but should be arranged based on history and presentation. Here, the history makes bleeding disorder testing the priority: von Willebrand factor antigen and activity (e.g. VWF:GP1bM or ristocetin cofactor), factor VIII, full blood count and a coagulation screen, ideally with haematology input. She also needs iron replacement (probably IV given her ferritin and symptoms) and treatment for the bleeding (e.g. tranexamic acid or hormonal options) while results are awaited.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
HMB since menarche plus a personal or family bleeding history: test for von Willebrand disease and other bleeding disorders. Ultrasound and other tests are not routine; choose them by history.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "Australian Commission on Safety and Quality in Health Care. Heavy Menstrual Bleeding Clinical Care Standard 2024 (PDF copy hosted by ACN)", "url": "https://www.acn.edu.au/wp-content/uploads/heavy-menstrual-bleeding-clinical-care-standard-2024.pdf"}
• {"name": "METEOR (AIHW). Heavy Menstrual Bleeding Clinical Care Standard 2024 indicators", "url": "https://meteor.aihw.gov.au/content/788514"}

---

EDITED BY HEISENBERG','difficult','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "bank170", "review_status": "owner_approved_2026_09_29", "tags": ["ZQ-0186", "zyntra_bank", "mock1", "level_4", "task_data_gathering", "group_womens_health", "sys_gynaecology", "approved_by_owner_2026_09_29"], "clinician_task": "data_gathering", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"Australian Commission on Safety and Quality in Health Care. Heavy Menstrual Bleeding Clinical Care Standard 2024 (PDF copy hosted by ACN)\", \"url\": \"https://www.acn.edu.au/wp-content/uploads/heavy-menstrual-bleeding-clinical-care-standard-2024.pdf\"}; {\"name\": \"METEOR (AIHW). Heavy Menstrual Bleeding Clinical Care Standard 2024 indicators\", \"url\": \"https://meteor.aihw.gov.au/content/788514\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"B": "The standard recommends transvaginal ultrasound on days 5–10 WHEN ultrasound is needed. Structural causes are unlikely in a 23-year-old with HMB since menarche and a bleeding history, and she is not sexually active.", "C": "Endometrial sampling is for women with risk factors for endometrial hyperplasia or cancer (e.g. older age, obesity, persistent intermenstrual bleeding, failed treatment). She has none.", "D": "She has regular ovulatory cycles, so ovarian hormone testing is not informative for HMB.", "E": "Cervical screening starts at 25, and HMB is not a symptom of cervical cancer (it originates in the uterus)."}, "key_takeaways": ["HMB since menarche plus a personal or family bleeding history: test for von Willebrand disease and other bleeding disorders. Ultrasound and other tests are not routine; choose them by history."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,1.0,1.0,'tier_prior','tier=4 (bank170)'),
('6ebef92d-d8e7-5e8e-b248-cb84d1f8db07'::uuid,'ZQ-0187','womens-health','gynaecology','A 52-year-old woman sees her general practitioner in Cairns, Queensland, because of severe hot flushes and night sweats for a year, waking her four or five times a night. Her last menstrual period was 14 months ago. She has tried layered clothing, reducing alcohol and cognitive behavioural therapy with little benefit, and would like hormone therapy. She has not had a hysterectomy.

She has obesity (BMI 36 kg/m²), migraine without aura and hypertriglyceridaemia. She has no personal or family history of venous thromboembolism, breast cancer, stroke or heart disease, and she does not smoke. Her blood pressure is 128/80 mmHg. Breast examination is normal and her screening mammogram 8 months ago was normal. Cervical screening is up to date.

Fasting lipids: total cholesterol 5.4 mmol/L, triglycerides 3.2 mmol/L (<2.0), HDL 1.1 mmol/L, LDL 2.8 mmol/L.

Which one of the following is the most appropriate regimen?','["Oral conjugated oestrogens with oral medroxyprogesterone acetate daily", "Transdermal oestradiol alone", "Oral oestradiol with oral dydrogesterone daily", "Transdermal oestradiol with oral micronised progesterone 100 mg nightly", "Vaginal oestriol cream alone"]'::jsonb,'D','CORRECT ANSWER

D. Transdermal oestradiol with oral micronised progesterone 100 mg nightly

---

She is under 60 and within 10 years of menopause with troublesome vasomotor symptoms, so MHT is appropriate and its benefits are likely to outweigh the risks. Because she has a uterus, she needs a progestogen to protect the endometrium. Being more than 12 months after her last period, a continuous combined regimen is appropriate (e.g. micronised progesterone 100 mg nightly). Transdermal oestrogen is preferred in her case: the Australasian Menopause Society notes that transdermal oestradiol at standard doses does not increase venous thromboembolism risk (important with obesity), avoids the first-pass hepatic effect that raises triglycerides with oral oestrogen, and is generally preferred in migraine. Micronised progesterone (or dydrogesterone) appears to carry less breast cancer and VTE risk than older synthetic progestogens such as medroxyprogesterone acetate. Micronised progesterone should be taken orally; transdermal progesterone creams do not protect the endometrium.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
MHT with a uterus needs a progestogen. Choose transdermal oestradiol in obesity/VTE risk, hypertriglyceridaemia or migraine, and prefer oral micronised progesterone (continuous if >12 months after the last period).

---

