EDITED BY HEISENBERG','difficult','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "bank170", "review_status": "owner_approved_2026_09_29", "tags": ["ZQ-0187", "zyntra_bank", "mock1", "level_4", "task_management", "group_womens_health", "sys_gynaecology", "approved_by_owner_2026_09_29"], "clinician_task": "management", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"Australasian Menopause Society. Information sheet: Combined Menopausal Hormone Therapy (MHT)\", \"url\": \"https://hub.menopause.org.au/Play?pId=f9c7390f-ae66-4058-b0e0-a017a3ecf4c4\"}; {\"name\": \"Australasian Menopause Society. Information sheet: Risks and benefits of MHT\", \"url\": \"https://hub.menopause.org.au/Play?pId=d68b3007-6518-43ac-bc8b-bea2f66418d6\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "Oral oestrogen raises VTE risk and triglycerides, and MPA is associated with higher breast cancer and VTE risk than micronised progesterone. This is the least suitable combination for her.", "B": "Unopposed oestrogen in a woman with a uterus causes endometrial hyperplasia and cancer. A progestogen is needed.", "C": "Dydrogesterone is a reasonable progestogen, but oral oestradiol increases VTE risk and triglycerides, which matters with her obesity and hypertriglyceridaemia.", "E": "Vaginal oestrogen treats genitourinary symptoms but has too little systemic effect to control severe vasomotor symptoms."}, "key_takeaways": ["MHT with a uterus needs a progestogen. Choose transdermal oestradiol in obesity/VTE risk, hypertriglyceridaemia or migraine, and prefer oral micronised progesterone (continuous if >12 months after the last period)."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,1.0,1.0,'tier_prior','tier=4 (bank170)'),
('47a67e66-16ad-5378-9af3-8bd6a67621e4'::uuid,'ZQ-0188','womens-health','gynaecology','A 29-year-old woman and her 31-year-old husband see a general practitioner in Parramatta, Sydney, because they have not conceived after 14 months of regular unprotected intercourse. She has had irregular periods since her teens, with cycles of 45–90 days, and she has excess hair on her chin and abdomen. She has no pelvic pain, no history of pelvic infection or abdominal surgery, and has never been pregnant. She does not smoke and drinks rarely. Her BMI is 27 kg/m² and blood pressure 118/76 mmHg.

Investigations:
- Pelvic ultrasound: more than 20 follicles per ovary, ovarian volume 12 mL bilaterally, normal uterus
- Total testosterone 2.4 nmol/L (<1.7), SHBG low
- TSH and prolactin normal
- 75 g OGTT: normal
- Rubella immune, cervical screening up to date, folic acid started
- Husband''s semen analysis: normal by WHO criteria

They would like to start treatment.

Which one of the following is the most appropriate first-line pharmacological treatment?','["Clomiphene citrate", "Letrozole", "Metformin alone", "Gonadotrophin injections", "Referral for in vitro fertilisation"]'::jsonb,'B','CORRECT ANSWER

B. Letrozole

---

She meets the Rotterdam criteria for PCOS (oligo-ovulation, clinical and biochemical hyperandrogenism, polycystic ovarian morphology), and her infertility is anovulatory, with a normal semen analysis and no risk factors for tubal disease. The 2023 International Evidence-based Guideline for PCOS (led from Australia by Monash University) recommends letrozole as the first-line pharmacological treatment for ovulation induction in anovulatory infertility with PCOS and no other infertility factors, because it gives higher ovulation, pregnancy and live birth rates than clomiphene. Letrozole is used off-label for this indication in Australia, which should be discussed with the couple. Lifestyle measures and preconception care continue alongside. Cycle monitoring (ultrasound) is advised to reduce the risk of multiple pregnancy.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Anovulatory infertility with PCOS: letrozole is first-line (2023 international guideline), off-label in Australia. Clomiphene and metformin are alternatives; gonadotrophins and IVF come later.

---

