EDITED BY HEISENBERG','moderate','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "bank170", "review_status": "owner_approved_2026_09_29", "tags": ["ZQ-0188", "zyntra_bank", "mock1", "level_3", "task_management", "group_womens_health", "sys_gynaecology", "approved_by_owner_2026_09_29"], "clinician_task": "management", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"Summary of the 2023 international evidence-based guideline for the assessment and management of PCOS: an Australian perspective. Med J Aust 2024;221(7)\", \"url\": \"https://www.mja.com.au/journal/2024/221/7/summary-2023-international-evidence-based-guideline-assessment-and-management\"}; {\"name\": \"ASRM. Recommendations from the 2023 International Evidence-based Guideline for PCOS\", \"url\": \"https://www.asrm.org/practice-guidance/practice-committee-documents/recommendations-from-the-2023-international-evidence-based-guideline-for-the-assessment-and-management-of-polycystic-ovary-syndrome/\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "Clomiphene is an alternative, but it is less effective than letrozole for live birth in PCOS and is now second choice.", "C": "Metformin alone improves ovulation modestly, but it is less effective than letrozole for live birth. It may be used alone or added in some situations (e.g. in combination, or with impaired glucose tolerance).", "D": "Gonadotrophins are second-line after oral agents fail, and they carry higher risks of multiple pregnancy and ovarian hyperstimulation.", "E": "IVF is third-line for anovulatory PCOS without other infertility factors, after failed ovulation induction."}, "key_takeaways": ["Anovulatory infertility with PCOS: letrozole is first-line (2023 international guideline), off-label in Australia. Clomiphene and metformin are alternatives; gonadotrophins and IVF come later."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,0.0,1.0,'tier_prior','tier=3 (bank170)'),
('7eb60a00-4b9b-5689-83c7-2d87055e5e49'::uuid,'ZQ-0189','womens-health','gynaecology','A 58-year-old woman sees her general practitioner in Ballarat, Victoria, because of 3 months of abdominal bloating, early satiety and needing to pass urine more often. Her last period was at 51. She has not used hormone therapy. She has no family history of ovarian, breast or bowel cancer. Abdominal examination shows mild fullness in the lower abdomen, with no palpable mass and no shifting dullness.

Investigations:
- Transvaginal ultrasound: 7 cm left adnexal cystic mass with multiple locules (thick septations) and a 1.5 cm solid area with internal blood flow; right ovary normal; no ascites; no peritoneal deposits seen
- CA-125 30 U/mL (<35)
- Full blood count, liver and renal function normal

Which one of the following is the most accurate interpretation?','["RMI 90: low risk; repeat the ultrasound in 6 weeks", "RMI 30: low risk; reassure her because CA-125 is normal", "RMI 270: high risk; refer urgently to a gynaecological oncologist", "RMI cannot be used because CA-125 is within the normal range", "RMI 270, but laparoscopic cystectomy by a general gynaecologist is appropriate"]'::jsonb,'C','CORRECT ANSWER

C. RMI 270: high risk; refer urgently to a gynaecological oncologist

---

The risk of malignancy index (RMI) = U × M × CA-125 (U/mL). U (ultrasound score) is 0 for no features, 1 for one feature and 3 for two to five features, where the features are: multilocular cyst, solid areas, metastases, ascites and bilateral lesions. She has two (multilocular and solid areas), so U = 3. M (menopausal status) is 3 for postmenopausal women and 1 for premenopausal women, so M = 3. RMI = 3 × 3 × 30 = 270. An RMI above 200 is high risk (sensitivity about 87%, specificity about 97% for ovarian cancer in the RACGP summary) and needs urgent referral to a gynaecological oncologist, who will usually arrange CT staging and plan surgery. A normal CA-125 does not exclude ovarian cancer: about half of early-stage cancers have a normal CA-125. Her symptoms (bloating, early satiety, urinary frequency) are also typical of ovarian cancer.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
RMI = U (0/1/3) × M (1 premenopausal, 3 postmenopausal) × CA-125. RMI above 200 = high risk: urgent gynaecological oncology referral. A normal CA-125 does not exclude cancer.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "RACGP (AFP 2015). Investigation and management of an ovarian mass", "url": "https://www.racgp.org.au/afp/2015/january-february/investigation-and-management-of-an-ovarian-mass"}
