---

EDITED BY HEISENBERG','difficult','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "bank170", "review_status": "owner_approved_2026_09_29", "tags": ["ZQ-0189", "zyntra_bank", "mock1", "level_4", "task_data_interpretation_synthesis", "group_womens_health", "sys_gynaecology", "approved_by_owner_2026_09_29"], "clinician_task": "data_interpretation_synthesis", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"RACGP (AFP 2015). Investigation and management of an ovarian mass\", \"url\": \"https://www.racgp.org.au/afp/2015/january-february/investigation-and-management-of-an-ovarian-mass\"}; {\"name\": \"Cancer Australia. Appropriate referral of women with suspected ovarian cancer (GP card)\", \"url\": \"https://www.canceraustralia.gov.au/sites/default/files/migrated-files/publications/appropriate-referral-women-suspected-ovarian-cancer/pdf/ocarw_approp_refferal_gp_card_0.pdf\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "RMI 90 would result from scoring U as 1 (counting only one feature). She has two features, so U = 3.", "B": "RMI 30 comes from scoring both U and M as 1. She is postmenopausal (M = 3) with two ultrasound features (U = 3).", "D": "The RMI uses the actual CA-125 value, whatever it is. A normal CA-125 still contributes to the score.", "E": "An RMI above 200 means a high risk of cancer. Surgery should be done by a gynaecological oncologist (usually oophorectomy with staging, not cystectomy, to avoid rupture and spill)."}, "key_takeaways": ["RMI = U (0/1/3) × M (1 premenopausal, 3 postmenopausal) × CA-125. RMI above 200 = high risk: urgent gynaecological oncology referral. A normal CA-125 does not exclude cancer."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,1.0,1.0,'tier_prior','tier=4 (bank170)'),
('6cefb7d6-7f9f-504e-a20e-0dfaec13a8eb'::uuid,'ZQ-0190','womens-health','gynaecology','A 57-year-old woman sees her general practitioner in Adelaide because she has had two episodes of light vaginal bleeding over the past month. Her last natural period was at 52. Four years ago she had a wide local excision and radiotherapy for an oestrogen receptor-positive left breast cancer. She started an aromatase inhibitor but stopped it after 6 months because of severe joint pain, and has taken tamoxifen 20 mg daily for the past 3 years. She has no other medical history, and her BMI is 29 kg/m². Her cervical screening test 2 years ago was negative.

Speculum examination shows a healthy cervix with a small amount of blood from the os. Bimanual examination is normal.

Transvaginal ultrasound: uterus normal size; endometrial thickness 7 mm with multiple small subendometrial cystic spaces, described as ''consistent with tamoxifen effect''; no focal lesion seen; ovaries normal.

Which one of the following is the most appropriate next investigation?','["Repeat transvaginal ultrasound in 3 months", "Stop tamoxifen and observe for further bleeding", "Reassure her that the ultrasound appearance is typical of tamoxifen", "Hysteroscopy with directed endometrial biopsy", "CA-125 and MRI of the pelvis"]'::jsonb,'D','CORRECT ANSWER

D. Hysteroscopy with directed endometrial biopsy

---

Any postmenopausal bleeding needs investigation for endometrial cancer, and tamoxifen increases the risk of endometrial hyperplasia, polyps and cancer (a partial oestrogen agonist on the endometrium). In women taking tamoxifen, transvaginal ultrasound is unreliable: tamoxifen causes subendometrial cystic change and apparent thickening that make the endometrial thickness hard to interpret, so a reassuring-looking scan does not exclude pathology. RANZCOG''s statement on tamoxifen and the endometrium and Cancer Australia''s abnormal vaginal bleeding guide advise that women on tamoxifen with abnormal bleeding should be referred promptly for hysteroscopy and endometrial sampling. Hysteroscopy allows direct visualisation and targeted biopsy of polyps and focal lesions, which blind (Pipelle) sampling can miss. Routine ultrasound screening of asymptomatic women taking tamoxifen is not recommended.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Postmenopausal bleeding on tamoxifen: ultrasound is unreliable because of tamoxifen-related endometrial changes. Refer for hysteroscopy and directed biopsy.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "RANZCOG. Tamoxifen and the endometrium (College statement)", "url": "https://ranzcog.edu.au/wp-content/uploads/Tamoxifen-and-the-Endometrium.pdf"}
