• {"name": "ACOG Committee Opinion. Tamoxifen and uterine cancer", "url": "https://www.acog.org/clinical/clinical-guidance/committee-opinion/articles/2014/06/tamoxifen-and-uterine-cancer"}

---

EDITED BY HEISENBERG','difficult','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "bank170", "review_status": "owner_approved_2026_09_29", "tags": ["ZQ-0190", "zyntra_bank", "mock1", "level_4", "task_data_gathering", "group_womens_health", "sys_gynaecology", "approved_by_owner_2026_09_29"], "clinician_task": "data_gathering", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"RANZCOG. Tamoxifen and the endometrium (College statement)\", \"url\": \"https://ranzcog.edu.au/wp-content/uploads/Tamoxifen-and-the-Endometrium.pdf\"}; {\"name\": \"Cancer Australia. Abnormal vaginal bleeding in pre-, peri- and post-menopausal women: diagnostic guide for GPs (chart)\", \"url\": \"https://www.canceraustralia.gov.au/sites/default/files/migrated-files/publications/abnormal-vaginal-bleeding-pre-peri-and-post-menopausal-women-diagnostic-guide-general-practitioners/pdf/ncgc_a3_menopause_chart_june_2012_final.pdf\"}; {\"name\": \"ACOG Committee Opinion. Tamoxifen and uterine cancer\", \"url\": \"https://www.acog.org/clinical/clinical-guidance/committee-opinion/articles/2014/06/tamoxifen-and-uterine-cancer\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "Repeating an unreliable test delays diagnosis. Bleeding on tamoxifen needs tissue diagnosis now.", "B": "Stopping tamoxifen without her oncologist''s input compromises breast cancer treatment, and it does not investigate the bleeding.", "C": "This is the trap. The typical tamoxifen appearance makes ultrasound unreliable; it does not make it reassuring.", "E": "CA-125 and MRI do not diagnose endometrial pathology. The key test is histology from hysteroscopy and biopsy."}, "key_takeaways": ["Postmenopausal bleeding on tamoxifen: ultrasound is unreliable because of tamoxifen-related endometrial changes. Refer for hysteroscopy and directed biopsy."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,1.0,1.0,'tier_prior','tier=4 (bank170)'),
('f991c933-8d55-56ae-b56f-bfa1234e2764'::uuid,'ZQ-0191','womens-health','gynaecology','A 20-year-old woman sees her general practitioner on the Sunshine Coast, Queensland, for review. Two days ago she presented with 5 days of lower abdominal pain, deep dyspareunia and spotting after sex. She has had a new male partner for 2 months and they do not use condoms; she takes the combined pill. At that visit her temperature was 37.7°C, and there was cervical motion tenderness, bilateral adnexal tenderness and mucopurulent cervical discharge. A pregnancy test was negative. Swabs were taken, and she was treated with ceftriaxone 500 mg IM and started on oral doxycycline and metronidazole for 14 days.

Today her pain is improving and her temperature is 37.1°C. She still has mild adnexal tenderness. She wants to stop the tablets because ''the tests showed I don''t have an STI''.

Results:
- Chlamydia trachomatis NAAT (endocervical): not detected
- Neisseria gonorrhoeae NAAT: not detected
- Trichomonas vaginalis NAAT: not detected
- Mycoplasma genitalium NAAT: pending
- Pelvic ultrasound: normal uterus and ovaries, no adnexal collection
- C-reactive protein 24 mg/L (<5); urinalysis normal

Which one of the following is the most accurate interpretation?','["Negative chlamydia and gonorrhoea tests exclude PID, so antibiotics can be stopped", "PID remains the most likely diagnosis; she should complete the 14-day course", "A tubo-ovarian abscess is likely and needs drainage", "Acute appendicitis is more likely and needs surgical referral", "Ectopic pregnancy has not been excluded and needs serial β-hCG"]'::jsonb,'B','CORRECT ANSWER

B. PID remains the most likely diagnosis; she should complete the 14-day course

---

PID is a clinical diagnosis. The Australian STI Management Guidelines (ASHM) advise a low threshold for diagnosis and empirical treatment in a sexually active young woman with pelvic pain and cervical motion, uterine or adnexal tenderness, because delayed treatment increases the risk of tubal infertility, ectopic pregnancy and chronic pelvic pain. Chlamydia and gonorrhoea are found in only a minority of cases; most PID is polymicrobial (including anaerobes and bacterial vaginosis-associated organisms, and sometimes M. genitalium). Negative NAATs therefore do not exclude PID or justify stopping treatment. She is improving clinically at 48–72 hours, which supports the diagnosis and outpatient management. She should complete the recommended regimen (ceftriaxone plus 14 days of doxycycline and metronidazole), and the pending M. genitalium result may change therapy if positive. Her partner(s) from the past 6 months should be tested and treated empirically, and she should avoid sex until both have finished treatment.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
PID is a clinical diagnosis. Negative chlamydia and gonorrhoea NAATs do not exclude it: complete the full course (ceftriaxone plus 14 days of doxycycline and metronidazole) and treat partners.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "ASHM. Australian STI Management Guidelines: Pelvic inflammatory disease (PID)", "url": "https://sti.guidelines.org.au/syndromes/pelvic-inflammatory-diseases-pid/"}
• {"name": "ASHM. Australasian Contact Tracing Guidelines: PID", "url": "https://contacttracing.ashm.org.au/pelvic-inflammatory-disease-pid/"}

---

EDITED BY HEISENBERG','difficult','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "bank170", "review_status": "owner_approved_2026_09_29", "tags": ["ZQ-0191", "zyntra_bank", "mock1", "level_4", "task_data_interpretation_synthesis", "group_womens_health", "sys_gynaecology", "approved_by_owner_2026_09_29"], "clinician_task": "data_interpretation_synthesis", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"ASHM. Australian STI Management Guidelines: Pelvic inflammatory disease (PID)\", \"url\": \"https://sti.guidelines.org.au/syndromes/pelvic-inflammatory-diseases-pid/\"}; {\"name\": \"ASHM. Australasian Contact Tracing Guidelines: PID\", \"url\": \"https://contacttracing.ashm.org.au/pelvic-inflammatory-disease-pid/\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "This is the trap. Negative STI tests do not exclude PID, which is often polymicrobial. Stopping early risks relapse and tubal damage.", "C": "She is improving, afebrile and the ultrasound shows no collection. A tubo-ovarian abscess is not supported.", "D": "Bilateral adnexal and cervical motion tenderness with mucopurulent discharge and improvement on PID treatment point away from appendicitis.", "E": "Her pregnancy test was negative, which excludes ectopic pregnancy as the explanation for these symptoms."}, "key_takeaways": ["PID is a clinical diagnosis. Negative chlamydia and gonorrhoea NAATs do not exclude it: complete the full course (ceftriaxone plus 14 days of doxycycline and metronidazole) and treat partners."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,1.0,1.0,'tier_prior','tier=4 (bank170)'),
('f6be3601-96dc-567c-8f59-b0c5e0f57bb5'::uuid,'ZQ-0192','womens-health','gynaecology','A 34-year-old woman returns to her general practitioner in Newcastle, New South Wales, because of ongoing bleeding after sex. It started 5 months ago and happens after most episodes of intercourse. Six weeks ago, at her first visit about this, the GP took a clinician-collected cervical sample for a co-test and tested for chlamydia and gonorrhoea. Results: oncogenic HPV not detected; liquid-based cytology negative; chlamydia and gonorrhoea NAAT negative. Her previous routine cervical screening test, 3 years ago, was also negative for HPV. She has one long-term male partner and uses a copper IUD, inserted 2 years ago, and her periods are regular and unchanged. She has no intermenstrual bleeding, pelvic pain or discharge.

Speculum examination today shows IUD threads visible at a cervix that looks normal to the GP, with no ectropion, polyp or contact bleeding seen. Bimanual examination is normal. A urine pregnancy test is negative.

Which one of the following is the most appropriate next step?','["Reassure her and return to routine 5-yearly cervical screening", "Repeat the co-test in 12 months", "Offer a self-collected HPV test in 6 months", "Remove the copper IUD and review in 3 months", "Refer to a gynaecologist for colposcopy and consider transvaginal ultrasound"]'::jsonb,'E','CORRECT ANSWER

E. Refer to a gynaecologist for colposcopy and consider transvaginal ultrasound

---

Postcoital bleeding is a symptom, not a diagnosis, and can be caused by cervical or other genital tract cancer. Symptomatic people are managed on a diagnostic pathway, not the screening pathway. The National Cervical Screening Program Guidelines (2025) recommend a clinician-collected co-test (REC 10.1), which she has had. After a SINGLE episode of postcoital bleeding with a normal-looking cervix, a negative co-test means colposcopy is not needed (REC 10.2). But for RECURRENT or PERSISTENT postcoital bleeding, even with HPV not detected and negative cytology, she should be referred to a gynaecologist for assessment including colposcopy, and upper genital tract imaging (usually transvaginal ultrasound) should be considered (REC 10.3). This is because rare cancers (e.g. HPV-independent adenocarcinoma) and non-cervical causes can be missed by HPV testing and cytology.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Postcoital bleeding: clinician-collected co-test. If bleeding is recurrent or persistent, refer to a gynaecologist for colposcopy even when the co-test is negative (NCSP 2025, REC 10.3).

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "Cancer Council Australia / NCSP. National Cervical Screening Program Guidelines (2025), Chapter 10: REC 10.1\u201310.4", "url": "https://files.magicapp.org/guideline/fd994396-78f1-417b-98a6-384b0a1efa25/published_guideline_9714-2_9.pdf"}
• {"name": "Australian Government. NCSP: a summary guide for healthcare providers (2025)", "url": "https://www.health.gov.au/sites/default/files/2025-02/national-cervical-screening-program-a-summary-guide-for-healthcare-providers_0.pdf"}

---

EDITED BY HEISENBERG','difficult','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "bank170", "review_status": "owner_approved_2026_09_29", "tags": ["ZQ-0192", "zyntra_bank", "mock1", "level_4", "task_data_gathering", "group_womens_health", "sys_gynaecology", "approved_by_owner_2026_09_29"], "clinician_task": "data_gathering", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"Cancer Council Australia / NCSP. National Cervical Screening Program Guidelines (2025), Chapter 10: REC 10.1\\u201310.4\", \"url\": \"https://files.magicapp.org/guideline/fd994396-78f1-417b-98a6-384b0a1efa25/published_guideline_9714-2_9.pdf\"}; {\"name\": \"Australian Government. NCSP: a summary guide for healthcare providers (2025)\", \"url\": \"https://www.health.gov.au/sites/default/files/2025-02/national-cervical-screening-program-a-summary-guide-for-healthcare-providers_0.pdf\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "Returning to routine screening would be appropriate after a single episode with a negative co-test and normal cervix, but not with persistent symptoms.", "B": "A 12-month repeat co-test is part of the intermediate-risk screening pathway. It leaves persistent symptoms uninvestigated.", "C": "Self-collection is not appropriate for symptomatic people, because it cannot provide cytology and the guideline pathway for symptoms is clinician-led.", "D": "A copper IUD more often causes heavier or longer periods than postcoital bleeding. Attributing her symptom to the IUD without further assessment risks missing pathology."}, "key_takeaways": ["Postcoital bleeding: clinician-collected co-test. If bleeding is recurrent or persistent, refer to a gynaecologist for colposcopy even when the co-test is negative (NCSP 2025, REC 10.3)."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,1.0,1.0,'tier_prior','tier=4 (bank170)'),
('9ae644c5-4e94-5ad4-bc03-6d58a23fe3e8'::uuid,'ZQ-0193','womens-health','gynaecology','A 36-year-old woman sees her general practitioner in Perth because her periods stopped 7 months ago. Before that her cycles had become irregular over a year. She has hot flushes, night sweats and vaginal dryness. She has one child, aged 6, and she and her partner hoped to have another. She stopped the combined pill 2 years ago. She has had no chemotherapy, radiotherapy or pelvic surgery, and no weight loss, excessive exercise or galactorrhoea. Her mother went through menopause at 41. Her BMI is 23 kg/m² and examination is normal.

Investigations (random day, as she has no cycle):
- Urine and serum β-hCG negative
- FSH 68 IU/L
- Oestradiol 55 pmol/L (low)
- Prolactin and TSH normal

Which one of the following is the most accurate interpretation?','["This confirms premature ovarian insufficiency; a repeat FSH is not required", "Premature ovarian insufficiency cannot be diagnosed until FSH is repeated 4–6 weeks later", "Hypothalamic amenorrhoea is the most likely cause", "This is early normal perimenopause and no specific follow-up is needed", "An AMH level is needed as the primary diagnostic test"]'::jsonb,'A','CORRECT ANSWER

A. This confirms premature ovarian insufficiency; a repeat FSH is not required

---

Premature ovarian insufficiency (POI) is loss of ovarian function before 40. The 2024 ESHRE/ASRM/CRE/IMS international evidence-based guideline (developed with the Australian Centre for Research Excellence in Women''s Health in Reproductive Life) changed the diagnostic criteria. POI is now diagnosed by disordered cycles (amenorrhoea or irregular cycles) for at least 4 months plus ONE elevated FSH above 25 IU/L. A repeat FSH after 4–6 weeks is needed only if there is diagnostic uncertainty. The 2015/16 guideline required two elevated FSH levels. She has 7 months of amenorrhoea, oestrogen deficiency symptoms, FSH of 68 IU/L with low oestradiol, and no hormonal contraception confounding the result, so there is no uncertainty. She should be counselled (including about the small chance of spontaneous pregnancy and options such as donor eggs), have karyotype and FMR1 premutation testing (and consider autoimmune screening), baseline bone density, and hormone therapy until at least the average age of menopause, unless contraindicated.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
POI (2024 guideline): at least 4 months of disordered cycles plus a single FSH >25 IU/L. Repeat FSH only if the diagnosis is uncertain. AMH is not the primary test.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "ESHRE/ASRM/CRE/IMS. Evidence-based guideline: Premature Ovarian Insufficiency (2024), full guideline PDF", "url": "https://www.eshre.eu/-/media/sitecore-files/Guidelines/POI/2024/INTERNATIONAL-GUIDELINE-ON-POI_2024_2.pdf"}
• {"name": "Evidence-based guideline: premature ovarian insufficiency (Hum Reprod Open / Climacteric 2024; PubMed 39647506)", "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/39647506/"}

---

EDITED BY HEISENBERG','difficult','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "bank170", "review_status": "owner_approved_2026_09_29", "tags": ["ZQ-0193", "zyntra_bank", "mock1", "level_5", "task_data_interpretation_synthesis", "group_womens_health", "sys_gynaecology", "approved_by_owner_2026_09_29"], "clinician_task": "data_interpretation_synthesis", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"ESHRE/ASRM/CRE/IMS. Evidence-based guideline: Premature Ovarian Insufficiency (2024), full guideline PDF\", \"url\": \"https://www.eshre.eu/-/media/sitecore-files/Guidelines/POI/2024/INTERNATIONAL-GUIDELINE-ON-POI_2024_2.pdf\"}; {\"name\": \"Evidence-based guideline: premature ovarian insufficiency (Hum Reprod Open / Climacteric 2024; PubMed 39647506)\", \"url\": \"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/39647506/\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"B": "This was the 2015/16 ESHRE rule. The 2024 guideline requires a repeat only when there is diagnostic uncertainty, which is not the case here.", "C": "Hypothalamic amenorrhoea has a LOW or normal FSH with low oestradiol, usually with weight loss, stress or exercise. Her FSH is very high.", "D": "Menopause before 40 is not normal perimenopause. POI has long-term consequences for bone, cardiovascular health and fertility, and needs specific care and hormone therapy.", "E": "The 2024 guideline says AMH should not be used as the primary diagnostic test; it can help when FSH results are inconclusive."}, "key_takeaways": ["POI (2024 guideline): at least 4 months of disordered cycles plus a single FSH >25 IU/L. Repeat FSH only if the diagnosis is uncertain. AMH is not the primary test."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,2.0,1.0,'tier_prior','tier=5 (bank170)'),
('362da8c3-0206-5dff-b86e-7e4dacb0e2bb'::uuid,'ZQ-0194','womens-health','gynaecology','A 32-year-old woman and her partner see their general practitioner in Townsville, Queensland, because they have not conceived after 13 months of regular unprotected intercourse. Her periods have always been regular, every 35 days (range 34–36 days), with 5 days of bleeding. She has no hirsutism, acne, galactorrhoea, pelvic pain or history of pelvic infection. Her BMI is 22 kg/m². Her partner''s semen analysis is normal.

The GP requested a ''day 21 progesterone''. Blood was taken on day 21 of her cycle (counting the first day of bleeding as day 1). Her next period started on day 35 as expected.

Investigations:
- Serum progesterone (day 21): 6 nmol/L (a level above about 30 nmol/L in the mid-luteal phase usually indicates ovulation)
- FSH (day 3) 6 IU/L, LH 5 IU/L, TSH and prolactin normal
- Rubella immune, cervical screening up to date

Which one of the following is the most appropriate interpretation?','["Anovulatory cycles; start letrozole", "Luteal phase defect; start vaginal progesterone after ovulation", "The sample was taken too early; repeat progesterone on about day 28", "Diminished ovarian reserve; refer urgently for IVF", "Hyperprolactinaemia is likely despite a normal prolactin"]'::jsonb,'C','CORRECT ANSWER

C. The sample was taken too early; repeat progesterone on about day 28

---

Progesterone should be measured in the mid-luteal phase, about 7 days BEFORE the expected next period. The ''day 21'' test only fits a 28-day cycle. In a regular 35-day cycle, ovulation occurs around day 21 (the luteal phase is fairly constant at about 14 days), so a day 21 sample is taken at or before ovulation, when progesterone is expected to be low. The mid-luteal sample for her should be taken around day 28 (35 − 7). Regular cycles of 34–36 days are very likely to be ovulatory, and a single low result at the wrong time does not show anovulation. Repeating a correctly timed progesterone (or using ovulation tracking) avoids unnecessary ovulation induction. Investigation of the couple should continue in parallel (e.g. tubal patency assessment), as they have been trying for more than 12 months.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Measure progesterone 7 days before the expected period, not automatically on day 21. In a 35-day cycle, test around day 28.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "Lab Tests Online UK. Progesterone test (timing for ovulation confirmation)", "url": "https://www.labtestsonline.org.uk/tests/progesterone-test"}
• {"name": "GPnotebook. Progesterone (ovulatory test)", "url": "https://gpnotebook.com/en-GB/pages/gynaecology/progesterone-ovulatory-test"}

---

EDITED BY HEISENBERG','difficult','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "bank170", "review_status": "owner_approved_2026_09_29", "tags": ["ZQ-0194", "zyntra_bank", "mock1", "level_4", "task_data_interpretation_synthesis", "group_womens_health", "sys_gynaecology", "approved_by_owner_2026_09_29"], "clinician_task": "data_interpretation_synthesis", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"Lab Tests Online UK. Progesterone test (timing for ovulation confirmation)\", \"url\": \"https://www.labtestsonline.org.uk/tests/progesterone-test\"}; {\"name\": \"GPnotebook. Progesterone (ovulatory test)\", \"url\": \"https://gpnotebook.com/en-GB/pages/gynaecology/progesterone-ovulatory-test\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "Starting ovulation induction on the basis of a mistimed result is inappropriate. Her regular cycles strongly suggest she ovulates.", "B": "''Luteal phase defect'' is not a reliable diagnosis, and progesterone supplementation has no proven benefit for natural conception. The result is explained by timing.", "D": "Her early follicular FSH is normal. Nothing here suggests diminished ovarian reserve, and IVF is not the first step.", "E": "Her prolactin is normal and she has regular cycles without galactorrhoea. There is no basis for this."}, "key_takeaways": ["Measure progesterone 7 days before the expected period, not automatically on day 21. In a 35-day cycle, test around day 28."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,1.0,1.0,'tier_prior','tier=4 (bank170)'),
('a578347a-e755-5e2f-9879-cb24cc077fe3'::uuid,'ZQ-0195','child-health','neonatology','You are the paediatric registrar at a regional hospital in Rockhampton, Queensland. A midwife asks you to review a male infant who is 2 hours old. He was born at 38 weeks and 3 days by planned caesarean section. His mother has type 2 diabetes that needed insulin from 20 weeks of gestation. Birth weight was 4180 g, and Apgar scores were 8 and 9.

He had his first breastfeed at 40 minutes of age, and a pre-feed heel-prick blood glucose at 2 hours is 2.0 mmol/L. It is 1.9 mmol/L on a repeat sample sent to the laboratory. He is pink and alert, with normal tone. He is not jittery or lethargic and had no apnoea. His temperature is 36.8 °C, heart rate 138/min and respiratory rate 44/min. He attaches well at the breast, and his mother is keen to keep breastfeeding and to keep him in the room with her.

Which one of the following is the most appropriate next step?','["Give a 10% glucose bolus of 2 mL/kg IV and then start an IV glucose infusion", "Reassure that this is normal transition and recheck the glucose before the next feed in 3 hours", "Give 40% glucose gel 0.5 mL/kg to the buccal mucosa, feed, and recheck glucose in 30 minutes", "Admit him to the special care nursery for continuous IV glucose infusion at 60 mL/kg/day", "Give 30 mL/kg of infant formula by bottle instead of breastfeeding and recheck in 1 hour"]'::jsonb,'C','CORRECT ANSWER

C. Give 40% glucose gel 0.5 mL/kg to the buccal mucosa, feed, and recheck glucose in 30 minutes

---

This infant is at risk of hypoglycaemia: his mother has insulin-treated diabetes and he is macrosomic. He is well, ≥35 weeks, under 48 hours old and feeding, and his blood glucose is 1.5–2.5 mmol/L without symptoms. Queensland Clinical Guidelines recommend 40% glucose gel 0.5 mL/kg (200 mg/kg) massaged into the buccal mucosa, followed by a feed (breastfeed, or expressed breast milk or formula if needed), and a repeat BGL about 30 minutes after the gel. Gel can be repeated (commonly up to a total of about six doses in 48 hours). Escalate to IV glucose if the BGL stays low despite gel and feeds, falls below 1.5 mmol/L, or if he develops symptoms. Gel plus feeding keeps the mother and baby together, supports breastfeeding and reduces NICU admission for hypoglycaemia.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Well at-risk newborn (≥35 wk, <48 h), BGL 1.5–2.5 mmol/L without symptoms: give 40% glucose gel 0.5 mL/kg buccally plus a feed, and recheck in 30 min. Go to IV glucose for symptoms, BGL <1.5, or failure of gel and feeds.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "Queensland Clinical Guidelines. Newborn hypoglycaemia (guideline g-hypogly)", "url": "https://www.health.qld.gov.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0043/881899/g-hypogly.pdf"}
• {"name": "The Royal Children''s Hospital Melbourne. Clinical Practice Guideline: Hypoglycaemia", "url": "https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/Hypoglycaemia/"}

---

EDITED BY HEISENBERG','difficult','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "bank170", "review_status": "owner_approved_2026_09_29", "tags": ["ZQ-0195", "zyntra_bank", "mock1", "level_4", "task_management", "group_child_health", "sys_neonatology", "approved_by_owner_2026_09_29"], "clinician_task": "management", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"Queensland Clinical Guidelines. Newborn hypoglycaemia (guideline g-hypogly)\", \"url\": \"https://www.health.qld.gov.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0043/881899/g-hypogly.pdf\"}; {\"name\": \"The Royal Children''s Hospital Melbourne. Clinical Practice Guideline: Hypoglycaemia\", \"url\": \"https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/Hypoglycaemia/\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "IV bolus and infusion are for symptomatic hypoglycaemia (for example seizures or reduced consciousness), a BGL under about 1.5 mmol/L, or failure of gel and feeds. They are not first-line for a well infant at 2.0 mmol/L.", "B": "Transitional low glucose is common, but a BGL under 2.6 mmol/L in an at-risk infant needs treatment and a repeat within about 30 minutes. Waiting 3 hours is unsafe.", "D": "Separating mother and baby for IV glucose is unnecessary at this stage and undermines breastfeeding. It is the escalation step if gel and feeding fail.", "E": "Replacing breastfeeding with a large formula volume is not recommended. Supplementary feeds are given as part of the gel pathway if needed, in physiological volumes, while breastfeeding continues."}, "key_takeaways": ["Well at-risk newborn (≥35 wk, <48 h), BGL 1.5–2.5 mmol/L without symptoms: give 40% glucose gel 0.5 mL/kg buccally plus a feed, and recheck in 30 min. Go to IV glucose for symptoms, BGL <1.5, or failure of gel and feeds."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,1.0,1.0,'tier_prior','tier=4 (bank170)'),
('be7920c1-9cf9-569f-adbb-aae4106f3908'::uuid,'ZQ-0196','child-health','neonatology','A 5-day-old girl is brought to the emergency department of a regional hospital in Townsville, Queensland, because she has vomited three times in the last 4 hours. Her mother says the last two vomits were ''bright green''. She was born at term by normal vaginal birth after an uncomplicated pregnancy, passed meconium on day 1 and has been breastfeeding well until today.

She is alert and pink. Her heart rate is 168/min, respiratory rate 40/min, capillary refill time 2 seconds and temperature 36.9 °C. Her abdomen is soft and not distended, and there are no palpable masses. The anus is normally placed, and there is yellow stool in her nappy. A plain abdominal X-ray shows a non-specific bowel gas pattern with some gas in the distal bowel.

A nasogastric tube has been placed on free drainage, IV fluids have been started and she is nil by mouth. The on-call paediatric surgeon has been contacted.

Which one of the following investigations is most appropriate to establish the diagnosis?','["Urgent upper gastrointestinal contrast study", "Abdominal ultrasound for pyloric muscle thickness", "Contrast enema to look for a microcolon", "Repeat abdominal X-ray in 6 hours to look for obstruction", "Blood culture, urine culture and lumbar puncture"]'::jsonb,'A','CORRECT ANSWER

A. Urgent upper gastrointestinal contrast study

---

Bilious vomiting in a neonate is due to intestinal obstruction until proven otherwise, and malrotation with midgut volvulus is the diagnosis that must not be missed, because the midgut can become ischaemic within hours. A soft abdomen, a well-looking baby, passed stool and a non-specific X-ray do NOT exclude volvulus; early volvulus often looks exactly like this. The investigation of choice in a stable infant is an urgent upper GI contrast study to show the position of the duodenojejunal flexure (normally left of the spine at the level of the pylorus) and any ''corkscrew'' duodenum. If she is unstable or peritonitic, she goes straight to theatre (Ladd procedure) without imaging. The RCH guideline says bilious vomiting needs urgent surgical referral.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Green vomiting in a neonate means malrotation with volvulus until proven otherwise: NBM, NG drainage, IV fluids, urgent surgical referral and an urgent upper GI contrast study (straight to theatre if unstable).

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "The Royal Children''s Hospital Melbourne. Clinical Practice Guideline: Vomiting (last updated Sept 2025)", "url": "https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/Vomiting/"}

---

EDITED BY HEISENBERG','moderate','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "bank170", "review_status": "owner_approved_2026_09_29", "tags": ["ZQ-0196", "zyntra_bank", "mock1", "level_3", "task_data_gathering", "group_child_health", "sys_neonatology", "approved_by_owner_2026_09_29"], "clinician_task": "data_gathering", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"The Royal Children''s Hospital Melbourne. Clinical Practice Guideline: Vomiting (last updated Sept 2025)\", \"url\": \"https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/Vomiting/\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"B": "Pyloric stenosis causes NON-bilious projectile vomiting, usually at 2–8 weeks of age. Green vomit points to obstruction beyond the ampulla.", "C": "A contrast enema helps in distal obstruction (for example Hirschsprung disease or meconium ileus), where there is distension and failure to pass meconium. She has passed stool and her abdomen is not distended.", "D": "Waiting for the X-ray to become ''obstructive'' risks loss of the midgut. A normal or non-specific film does not exclude volvulus.", "E": "Sepsis can cause vomiting in a neonate and can be considered, but bilious vomiting in a well baby needs urgent exclusion of a surgical cause first. A septic screen does not establish that diagnosis."}, "key_takeaways": ["Green vomiting in a neonate means malrotation with volvulus until proven otherwise: NBM, NG drainage, IV fluids, urgent surgical referral and an urgent upper GI contrast study (straight to theatre if unstable)."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,0.0,1.0,'tier_prior','tier=3 (bank170)'),
('8bd2dd16-d37b-54c7-9294-3d08d9595e4b'::uuid,'ZQ-0197','child-health','child-development','A 4-year-old boy is brought to his general practitioner in Ballarat, Victoria, because his kindergarten teacher has noticed he cannot keep up with other children, falls often and cannot jump with both feet together. He walked independently at 19 months. His speech was a little late, but he now speaks in sentences. He was born at term, and there were no concerns in the neonatal period. His maternal uncle ''had problems with his muscles'' and used a wheelchair from his teens, but the family knows few details.

On examination he waddles when he walks and runs awkwardly. When getting up from the floor he rolls prone and pushes on his thighs with his hands to stand. His calves are firm and look bulky. Tone is normal, reflexes are present but reduced at the knees, and sensation appears normal. Hearing and vision were normal at his 4-year health check.

Which one of the following is the most appropriate first investigation?','["MRI of the brain and whole spine", "Nerve conduction studies and electromyography", "Thyroid function tests and vitamin D level", "Serum creatine kinase level", "Quadriceps muscle biopsy for dystrophin staining"]'::jsonb,'D','CORRECT ANSWER

D. Serum creatine kinase level

---

This boy has a proximal myopathic pattern (waddling gait, Gowers manoeuvre), calf pseudohypertrophy, early motor delay and a family history in a maternal male relative, consistent with X-linked Duchenne muscular dystrophy. The first test is serum creatine kinase, which is markedly raised in DMD (often 10–100 times the upper limit). A high CK leads to genetic testing of the DMD gene (deletion/duplication analysis, then sequencing), which confirms the diagnosis in most boys and usually removes the need for biopsy. Early diagnosis allows referral to a neuromuscular service, genetic counselling for the family (including the mother''s carrier status), and timely corticosteroid treatment and cardiac and respiratory surveillance.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Boy with late walking, Gowers sign and calf hypertrophy: check CK first. A markedly raised CK leads to DMD gene testing and neuromuscular referral.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "Birnkrant DJ et al. Diagnosis and management of Duchenne muscular dystrophy, part 1. Lancet Neurol 2018;17:251\u2013267", "url": "https://doi.org/10.1016/S1474-4422(18)30024-3"}

---

EDITED BY HEISENBERG','moderate','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "bank170", "review_status": "owner_approved_2026_09_29", "tags": ["ZQ-0197", "zyntra_bank", "mock1", "level_3", "task_data_gathering", "group_child_health", "sys_development", "approved_by_owner_2026_09_29"], "clinician_task": "data_gathering", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"Birnkrant DJ et al. Diagnosis and management of Duchenne muscular dystrophy, part 1. Lancet Neurol 2018;17:251\\u2013267\", \"url\": \"https://doi.org/10.1016/S1474-4422(18)30024-3\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "Neuroimaging is for upper motor neurone signs or regression. This child has normal tone and reduced reflexes with a myopathic pattern.", "B": "NCS/EMG can show a myopathic pattern but is uncomfortable, non-specific and is no longer routine in suspected DMD when CK and genetic testing are available.", "C": "Hypothyroid myopathy and rickets can cause weakness, but they do not explain calf pseudohypertrophy with a matching family history. They are not the best first test here.", "E": "Muscle biopsy is invasive and is now reserved for cases where genetic testing is non-diagnostic. It is not the first investigation."}, "key_takeaways": ["Boy with late walking, Gowers sign and calf hypertrophy: check CK first. A markedly raised CK leads to DMD gene testing and neuromuscular referral."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,0.0,1.0,'tier_prior','tier=3 (bank170)'),
('73317325-4e8c-5a90-8871-ae6bff458eae'::uuid,'ZQ-0198','child-health','child-development','A 6-week-old Aboriginal girl is brought by her mother and grandmother to the Aboriginal Community Controlled Health Service in Wagga Wagga, New South Wales, for her first scheduled immunisations. She was born at 39 weeks, weighs 4.6 kg and is breastfeeding well. She received hepatitis B vaccine at birth. There is no family history of immune deficiency, and she has no medical conditions. Her mother had dTpa, influenza and RSV vaccines during pregnancy.

Her mother says she wants ''everything she is due'', but has heard that ''one of the needles makes babies really hot'' and asks whether it should be given on a different day.

Which one of the following is the most appropriate plan for today?','["Give DTPa-hepB-IPV-Hib, 20vPCV and rotavirus today, and give MenB alone at 8 weeks", "Give DTPa-hepB-IPV-Hib, 20vPCV, rotavirus and MenB today, with paracetamol for 3 doses", "Give DTPa-hepB-IPV-Hib, 20vPCV, rotavirus and MenB today, with paracetamol if fever develops", "Give DTPa-hepB-IPV-Hib, 20vPCV and rotavirus today; MenB is not funded without a risk condition", "Give DTPa-hepB-IPV-Hib, 20vPCV, rotavirus and BCG today, and offer MenB from 6 months of age"]'::jsonb,'B','CORRECT ANSWER

B. Give DTPa-hepB-IPV-Hib, 20vPCV, rotavirus and MenB today, with paracetamol for 3 doses

---

Under the National Immunisation Program, Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander infants receive meningococcal B vaccine (Bexsero) at 2 and 4 months, with a booster at 12 months (plus an extra dose at 6 months if they have a specified risk condition). It is given with the routine 2-month vaccines: DTPa-hepB-IPV-Hib, pneumococcal conjugate vaccine (20vPCV) and oral rotavirus. The Handbook recommends 20vPCV at 2, 4, 6 and 12 months for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander children. MenB commonly causes fever in infants, especially when given with other vaccines, so prophylactic paracetamol (15 mg/kg) is recommended for children under 2 years: the first dose within 30 minutes before vaccination (or as soon as possible after), then two more doses 6 hours apart, whether or not fever develops. MenB can be given on a separate day, but this is not necessary. Co-administration with prophylactic paracetamol is the standard approach and avoids extra visits and delayed protection.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander infants get MenB at 2, 4 and 12 months with the routine vaccines (20vPCV at 2, 4, 6 and 12 months). Give prophylactic paracetamol 15 mg/kg: first dose around vaccination, then 2 more doses 6 h apart, regardless of fever.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "Australian Immunisation Handbook. Meningococcal disease", "url": "https://immunisationhandbook.health.gov.au/contents/vaccine-preventable-diseases/meningococcal-disease"}
• {"name": "Australian Immunisation Handbook. Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander children are recommended to receive pneumococcal conjugate vaccine (updated June 2026)", "url": "https://immunisationhandbook.health.gov.au/recommendations/aboriginal-and-torres-strait-islander-children-are-recommended-to-receive-pneumococcal-conjugate-vaccine"}

---

EDITED BY HEISENBERG','difficult','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "bank170", "review_status": "owner_approved_2026_09_29", "tags": ["ZQ-0198", "zyntra_bank", "mock1", "level_5", "task_management", "group_child_health", "sys_development", "approved_by_owner_2026_09_29"], "clinician_task": "management", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"Australian Immunisation Handbook. Meningococcal disease\", \"url\": \"https://immunisationhandbook.health.gov.au/contents/vaccine-preventable-diseases/meningococcal-disease\"}; {\"name\": \"Australian Immunisation Handbook. Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander children are recommended to receive pneumococcal conjugate vaccine (updated June 2026)\", \"url\": \"https://immunisationhandbook.health.gov.au/recommendations/aboriginal-and-torres-strait-islander-children-are-recommended-to-receive-pneumococcal-conjugate-vaccine\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "Separating MenB from the other vaccines is permitted (the Handbook allows an interval of at least 3 days to reduce fever), but it is not required and adds a visit and delays protection. Prophylactic paracetamol is the standard way to manage the fever risk.", "C": "The paracetamol recommendation with MenB is prophylactic: three doses starting around the time of vaccination regardless of temperature, not ''as needed'' after fever.", "D": "MenB is funded under the NIP for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander children under 2 years, whether or not they have a risk condition.", "E": "BCG is not routine at 6 weeks; it is recommended for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander neonates only in regions of high TB incidence, as directed by state/territory TB services. MenB for infants starts at 6 weeks to 2 months, not 6 months."}, "key_takeaways": ["Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander infants get MenB at 2, 4 and 12 months with the routine vaccines (20vPCV at 2, 4, 6 and 12 months). Give prophylactic paracetamol 15 mg/kg: first dose around vaccination, then 2 more doses 6 h apart, regardless of fever."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,2.0,1.0,'tier_prior','tier=5 (bank170)'),
('1853f429-654d-59b7-a0e8-66edc3d098ea'::uuid,'ZQ-0199','child-health','neonatology','An 18-day-old boy is brought to the emergency department of a metropolitan hospital in Perth, Western Australia, because he felt hot and has been feeding a little less for 6 hours. His 3-year-old brother has had a runny nose and cough for 4 days. He was born at term by vaginal birth. His mother''s group B streptococcus screen was negative, and membranes ruptured 2 hours before birth.

His rectal temperature is 38.4 °C, heart rate 164/min, respiratory rate 48/min and oxygen saturation 98% on room air. Capillary refill time is 2 seconds. He is alert, handles well and has a soft anterior fontanelle, clear nasal discharge and no rash. Chest and abdominal examinations are normal.

A rapid nasopharyngeal PCR done at triage is positive for respiratory syncytial virus. His parents ask whether he can go home now that ''they know it is just a virus''.

Which one of the following is the most appropriate approach to investigation?','["No further tests are needed because RSV explains the fever; arrange review in 24 hours", "Bag urine for microscopy and culture, and discharge home if the result is negative", "Full blood count, CRP and blood culture, with admission for observation off antibiotics", "Chest X-ray and urine by catheter, deferring lumbar puncture unless he becomes unwell", "Bloods with blood culture, catheter or suprapubic urine and lumbar puncture, then IV antibiotics"]'::jsonb,'E','CORRECT ANSWER

E. Bloods with blood culture, catheter or suprapubic urine and lumbar puncture, then IV antibiotics

---

RCH guidance for febrile infants ≤28 days old recommends a full septic work-up and empirical IV antibiotics regardless of how well they appear: FBE, CRP, blood culture, urine by catheter or suprapubic aspirate, and lumbar puncture (chest X-ray if respiratory signs). Clinical appearance and a positive viral test do not reliably exclude serious bacterial infection (bacteraemia, UTI or meningitis) in this age group, and concurrent bacterial infection with RSV is well described in young infants. Empirical treatment at this age covers group B streptococcus, E. coli and Listeria (for example benzylpenicillin or amoxicillin plus cefotaxime), and aciclovir is added if HSV is possible.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Any febrile infant aged 28 days or less gets a full septic screen (bloods, blood culture, catheter/SPA urine, LP) and empirical IV antibiotics, even if well-appearing and virus-positive.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "The Royal Children''s Hospital Melbourne. Clinical Practice Guideline: Febrile child", "url": "https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/Febrile_child/"}

---

EDITED BY HEISENBERG','difficult','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "bank170", "review_status": "owner_approved_2026_09_29", "tags": ["ZQ-0199", "zyntra_bank", "mock1", "level_4", "task_data_gathering", "group_child_health", "sys_neonatology", "approved_by_owner_2026_09_29"], "clinician_task": "data_gathering", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"The Royal Children''s Hospital Melbourne. Clinical Practice Guideline: Febrile child\", \"url\": \"https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/Febrile_child/\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "A proven viral infection lowers but does not remove the risk of serious bacterial infection in neonates. Guidelines do not use viral test results to avoid a full work-up at ≤28 days.", "B": "Bag urine is not suitable for culture because of high contamination rates, and a urine test alone is incomplete for a febrile neonate.", "C": "Omitting urine and CSF samples and withholding antibiotics is not recommended at ≤28 days. Observation off antibiotics is considered only in older, well infants with low-risk results under local pathways.", "D": "Deferring the LP until he ''becomes unwell'' risks missing early meningitis. LP is part of the initial work-up in febrile neonates unless contraindicated."}, "key_takeaways": ["Any febrile infant aged 28 days or less gets a full septic screen (bloods, blood culture, catheter/SPA urine, LP) and empirical IV antibiotics, even if well-appearing and virus-positive."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,1.0,1.0,'tier_prior','tier=4 (bank170)'),
('c710a217-1dd1-5ed9-95a7-91882bd80b35'::uuid,'ZQ-0200','child-health','paediatric-medicine','A 4-month-old boy is admitted to the paediatric ward of a regional hospital in Bendigo, Victoria, on day 3 of an illness with rhinorrhoea, cough and increased work of breathing. He was born at term and has no medical history. His 2-year-old sister attends childcare.

He is taking about 40% of his usual breastfeeds, and his mother reports three wet nappies in the past 24 hours. His temperature is 37.8 °C, heart rate 158/min and respiratory rate 58/min, with moderate subcostal recession and widespread fine crackles and wheeze. Oxygen saturation is 91–93% on room air, dipping to 89% for a few seconds during feeds and recovering quickly. He is alert and has no apnoeas. The mucous membranes are slightly dry, and capillary refill time is 2 seconds.

The resident asks what to do about feeding and oxygen.

Which one of the following is the most appropriate management?','["Start nasal high-flow therapy at 2 L/kg/min and give IV fluids at full maintenance", "Give a trial of nebulised salbutamol and continue if his work of breathing improves", "Start nasogastric feeds at two-thirds maintenance rate and do not give supplemental oxygen", "Give supplemental oxygen to keep saturations above 95% and start nebulised 3% saline", "Start IV fluids at two-thirds maintenance and give oxygen by nasal prongs at 1 L/min"]'::jsonb,'C','CORRECT ANSWER

C. Start nasogastric feeds at two-thirds maintenance rate and do not give supplemental oxygen

---

The RCH Bronchiolitis guideline advises that oxygen is not needed while saturations are persistently 90% or above; brief self-correcting dips during feeds or sleep do not need oxygen. He needs non-oral hydration because he is taking less than 50% of feeds. The nasogastric route is preferred over IV (fewer complications, supports nutrition) and is given at two-thirds of the calculated maintenance, because of the risk of fluid retention from ADH secretion. IV fluids are reserved for infants who cannot tolerate NG feeds, or who are severely ill or approaching respiratory failure. Salbutamol, nebulised hypertonic saline, adrenaline, corticosteroids and antibiotics are not recommended in bronchiolitis, and routine chest X-ray and blood tests are not needed.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Bronchiolitis: oxygen only if SpO2 is persistently <90%. If intake is <50%, use NG fluids at two-thirds maintenance (IV only if NG is not possible). No salbutamol, steroids, hypertonic saline or antibiotics.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "The Royal Children''s Hospital Melbourne. Clinical Practice Guideline: Bronchiolitis (Nov 2023)", "url": "https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/Bronchiolitis/"}

---

EDITED BY HEISENBERG','difficult','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "bank170", "review_status": "owner_approved_2026_09_29", "tags": ["ZQ-0200", "zyntra_bank", "mock1", "level_4", "task_management", "group_child_health", "sys_paediatric_medicine", "approved_by_owner_2026_09_29"], "clinician_task": "management", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"The Royal Children''s Hospital Melbourne. Clinical Practice Guideline: Bronchiolitis (Nov 2023)\", \"url\": \"https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/Bronchiolitis/\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "High-flow therapy is for infants with hypoxia despite standard oxygen or severe work of breathing, not for this infant. Full maintenance IV fluid is also inappropriate because fluids are restricted to about two-thirds maintenance.", "B": "Bronchodilators do not improve outcomes in bronchiolitis, and a ''trial'' of salbutamol is specifically not recommended under 12 months of age.", "D": "Targeting saturations above 95% prolongs admission without benefit; the threshold is persistently below 90%. Hypertonic saline is not recommended.", "E": "NG hydration is preferred over IV when he can tolerate it. Oxygen is not indicated at saturations of 91–93% with brief self-correcting dips."}, "key_takeaways": ["Bronchiolitis: oxygen only if SpO2 is persistently <90%. If intake is <50%, use NG fluids at two-thirds maintenance (IV only if NG is not possible). No salbutamol, steroids, hypertonic saline or antibiotics."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,1.0,1.0,'tier_prior','tier=4 (bank170)'),
('831e8a55-9fe8-5ce7-8a8b-5767f731f91e'::uuid,'ZQ-0201','child-health','paediatric-medicine','A 3-year-old boy is brought to a rural emergency department in Dubbo, New South Wales, at 2 am. For 2 days he has had a runny nose and a barking cough, and his general practitioner diagnosed croup yesterday. Tonight he suddenly became much worse, with a temperature of 39.9 °C and noisy breathing. His immunisations are up to date.

He is flushed, looks toxic and is sitting up. There is loud inspiratory and expiratory stridor, marked sternal recession and a harsh cough producing thick yellow secretions. He is not drooling and is able to speak a few words. His heart rate is 178/min, respiratory rate 44/min and oxygen saturation 92% on room air. The front of his neck is tender over the trachea.

He was given oral dexamethasone 0.6 mg/kg and two doses of nebulised adrenaline 30 minutes apart. The stridor improved only slightly and briefly each time. The white cell count is 22 × 10^9/L with neutrophilia, and CRP is 168 mg/L (<5).

Which one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?','["Acute epiglottitis", "Bacterial tracheitis", "Severe viral laryngotracheobronchitis", "Retropharyngeal abscess", "Inhaled foreign body in the trachea"]'::jsonb,'B','CORRECT ANSWER

B. Bacterial tracheitis

---

Bacterial tracheitis (usually Staphylococcus aureus, Streptococcus pyogenes or Moraxella) typically follows a viral croup-like prodrome and then deteriorates quickly with high fever, toxicity, a productive cough with thick purulent secretions, tracheal tenderness and poor or short-lived response to nebulised adrenaline. RCH lists these features in its Acute upper airway obstruction guideline. Management is a senior airway response: anaesthetic and ENT involvement, controlled intubation in theatre (secretions and sloughed membrane can block the tube), and IV antibiotics covering S. aureus.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
A ''croup'' that turns toxic, with high fever, purulent secretions, tracheal tenderness and poor response to adrenaline, is bacterial tracheitis: get senior airway help and give IV anti-staphylococcal antibiotics.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "The Royal Children''s Hospital Melbourne. Clinical Practice Guideline: Acute upper airway obstruction", "url": "https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/Acute_upper_airway_obstruction/"}
• {"name": "The Royal Children''s Hospital Melbourne. Clinical Practice Guideline: Croup (laryngotracheobronchitis) (Sept 2024)", "url": "https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/Croup_Laryngotracheobronchitis/"}

---

EDITED BY HEISENBERG','difficult','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "bank170", "review_status": "owner_approved_2026_09_29", "tags": ["ZQ-0201", "zyntra_bank", "mock1", "level_4", "task_data_interpretation_synthesis", "group_child_health", "sys_paediatric_medicine", "approved_by_owner_2026_09_29"], "clinician_task": "data_interpretation_synthesis", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"The Royal Children''s Hospital Melbourne. Clinical Practice Guideline: Acute upper airway obstruction\", \"url\": \"https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/Acute_upper_airway_obstruction/\"}; {\"name\": \"The Royal Children''s Hospital Melbourne. Clinical Practice Guideline: Croup (laryngotracheobronchitis) (Sept 2024)\", \"url\": \"https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/Croup_Laryngotracheobronchitis/\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "Epiglottitis presents with drooling, a muffled voice, a preference to sit forward and usually NO barking cough. It is now rare in immunised children. This child has a harsh cough with purulent sputum after a croup prodrome.", "C": "Viral croup usually responds well to adrenaline and steroids and rarely causes high fever, toxicity, very high inflammatory markers or thick secretions.", "D": "Retropharyngeal abscess causes neck stiffness or reduced neck extension, drooling, dysphagia and a muffled voice. A croup prodrome with tracheal tenderness and purulent sputum is more typical of tracheitis.", "E": "A foreign body causes sudden choking and stridor, often without fever or a viral prodrome. It does not explain the toxicity and high inflammatory markers."}, "key_takeaways": ["A ''croup'' that turns toxic, with high fever, purulent secretions, tracheal tenderness and poor response to adrenaline, is bacterial tracheitis: get senior airway help and give IV anti-staphylococcal antibiotics."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,1.0,1.0,'tier_prior','tier=4 (bank170)'),
('09c601d6-f9e7-521c-909d-2910c7b337c3'::uuid,'ZQ-0202','child-health','paediatric-medicine','An 8-year-old girl with known asthma is brought to the emergency department of a metropolitan hospital in Adelaide, South Australia, with 6 hours of worsening wheeze after a cold. She uses an inhaled corticosteroid preventer irregularly.

On arrival she could speak only single words, was using her accessory muscles and had oxygen saturation of 89% on room air. She has had one hour of continuous nebulised salbutamol driven by oxygen, three doses of nebulised ipratropium bromide 20 minutes apart, and oral prednisolone 2 mg/kg.

She is now sitting forward, still speaking in short phrases, with marked subcostal and intercostal recession. Her heart rate is 150/min, respiratory rate 40/min and oxygen saturation 93% on 6 L/min oxygen by mask. There is widespread wheeze with reduced air entry at both bases. She is alert and not drowsy. A capillary blood glucose is 9.4 mmol/L. IV access has been obtained.

Which one of the following is the most appropriate next step?','["IV magnesium sulfate 50 mg/kg over 20 minutes", "IV aminophylline loading dose over 1 hour", "Chest X-ray before any further treatment is given", "Capillary blood gas to guide the need for intubation", "IV hydrocortisone 4 mg/kg in addition to the prednisolone"]'::jsonb,'A','CORRECT ANSWER

A. IV magnesium sulfate 50 mg/kg over 20 minutes

---

She has severe acute asthma that has not responded to first-line treatment (continuous salbutamol, ipratropium and systemic corticosteroid). The RCH Acute asthma guideline recommends IV magnesium sulfate 50 mg/kg (0.2 mmol/kg, maximum 2 g/8 mmol) over 20 minutes as the next add-on, while salbutamol continues and senior help is involved. Further escalation (IV salbutamol, aminophylline, non-invasive ventilation or intubation) is considered after magnesium, with ICU involvement. A chest X-ray and blood gas are not routinely needed and must not delay treatment; a rising CO2, drowsiness or a silent chest are ominous signs.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Severe paediatric asthma not responding to continuous salbutamol, ipratropium and steroid: give IV magnesium sulfate 50 mg/kg over 20 min before aminophylline or IV salbutamol. No routine CXR or blood gas.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "The Royal Children''s Hospital Melbourne. Clinical Practice Guideline: Acute asthma (Jul 2023)", "url": "https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/Asthma_acute/"}

---

EDITED BY HEISENBERG','difficult','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "bank170", "review_status": "owner_approved_2026_09_29", "tags": ["ZQ-0202", "zyntra_bank", "mock1", "level_4", "task_management", "group_child_health", "sys_paediatric_medicine", "approved_by_owner_2026_09_29"], "clinician_task": "management", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"The Royal Children''s Hospital Melbourne. Clinical Practice Guideline: Acute asthma (Jul 2023)\", \"url\": \"https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/Asthma_acute/\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"B": "Aminophylline is a later-line option in critical asthma because it has a narrow therapeutic index and frequent side effects. Magnesium comes first.", "C": "A chest X-ray is needed only if pneumothorax, collapse or another diagnosis is suspected. Reduced air entry at both bases is expected in severe asthma and is not an indication.", "D": "Blood gases are not routinely needed and should not delay treatment. The decision to intubate is clinical (fatigue, drowsiness, rising CO2 despite maximal therapy).", "E": "She has already had an adequate systemic corticosteroid. IV hydrocortisone is used if she cannot take oral medicines and gives no additional benefit here."}, "key_takeaways": ["Severe paediatric asthma not responding to continuous salbutamol, ipratropium and steroid: give IV magnesium sulfate 50 mg/kg over 20 min before aminophylline or IV salbutamol. No routine CXR or blood gas."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,1.0,1.0,'tier_prior','tier=4 (bank170)'),
('17009316-9fc2-5f35-890a-3e293f51e95a'::uuid,'ZQ-0203','child-health','paediatric-medicine','A 3-year-old girl is brought to the emergency department in Geelong, Victoria, because she has been pale and very tired for a day and has passed very little urine since yesterday. Seven days ago, two days after the family visited a petting farm during the school holidays, she developed crampy abdominal pain and diarrhoea that became bloody on day 2. The diarrhoea has now settled. She has not had antibiotics.

She is pale and lethargic. Her temperature is 37.2 °C, heart rate 136/min and blood pressure 118/76 mmHg (above the 95th centile for her age and height). There are a few petechiae on her legs and mild periorbital oedema.

Investigations:
- Haemoglobin 74 g/L (105–135)
- Platelets 38 × 10^9/L (150–400)
- White cell count 14.2 × 10^9/L (5.0–15.0)
- Blood film: numerous fragmented red cells (schistocytes)
- Reticulocytes 6% (0.5–2.5)
- Lactate dehydrogenase 2150 U/L (<500)
- Direct antiglobulin (Coombs) test: negative
- Urea 24 mmol/L (2.5–6.0)
- Creatinine 188 µmol/L (20–40)
- Coagulation studies (PT, APTT, fibrinogen): normal
- Urinalysis: blood 3+, protein 2+

Which one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?','["Immune thrombocytopenic purpura", "Disseminated intravascular coagulation from sepsis", "Prerenal acute kidney injury from dehydration", "Haemolytic uraemic syndrome", "Warm autoimmune haemolytic anaemia"]'::jsonb,'D','CORRECT ANSWER

D. Haemolytic uraemic syndrome

---

She has the triad of microangiopathic haemolytic anaemia (anaemia, schistocytes, high LDH and reticulocytes, negative Coombs test), thrombocytopenia and acute kidney injury about a week after bloody diarrhoea. This is typical of haemolytic uraemic syndrome due to Shiga toxin-producing E. coli (e.g. O157), and animal contact at a petting farm is a classic exposure. Normal coagulation studies separate HUS from DIC. Management is supportive: careful fluid balance, avoiding antibiotics and antimotility agents in suspected STEC (they may increase toxin release/HUS risk), avoiding nephrotoxins, managing hypertension and electrolytes, transfusing red cells as needed (platelets only for bleeding or procedures), and early referral to paediatric nephrology for possible dialysis. Send stool for culture/PCR and notify public health.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Bloody diarrhoea followed a week later by MAHA (Coombs-negative, schistocytes), low platelets and AKI with normal coagulation is STEC-HUS. Give supportive care, avoid antibiotics and antimotility drugs, and refer to nephrology early.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "The Royal Children''s Hospital Melbourne. Clinical Practice Guideline: Gastroenteritis (Aug 2025)", "url": "https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/Gastroenteritis/"}

---

EDITED BY HEISENBERG','moderate','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "bank170", "review_status": "owner_approved_2026_09_29", "tags": ["ZQ-0203", "zyntra_bank", "mock1", "level_3", "task_data_interpretation_synthesis", "group_child_health", "sys_paediatric_medicine", "approved_by_owner_2026_09_29"], "clinician_task": "data_interpretation_synthesis", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"The Royal Children''s Hospital Melbourne. Clinical Practice Guideline: Gastroenteritis (Aug 2025)\", \"url\": \"https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/Gastroenteritis/\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "ITP causes isolated thrombocytopenia with normal haemoglobin and kidney function. It does not explain the schistocytes or AKI.", "B": "DIC causes prolonged PT/APTT and low fibrinogen. Her coagulation studies are normal, and she is not septic.", "C": "Dehydration can raise urea and creatinine, but it does not cause haemolysis, schistocytes and thrombocytopenia. Her hypertension and oedema also argue against volume depletion.", "E": "Autoimmune haemolysis is Coombs-positive and does not cause thrombocytopenia with AKI or red cell fragments."}, "key_takeaways": ["Bloody diarrhoea followed a week later by MAHA (Coombs-negative, schistocytes), low platelets and AKI with normal coagulation is STEC-HUS. Give supportive care, avoid antibiotics and antimotility drugs, and refer to nephrology early."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,0.0,1.0,'tier_prior','tier=3 (bank170)'),
('e31b259d-6e4f-582e-9c2a-b3988ce05101'::uuid,'ZQ-0204','child-health','paediatric-medicine','A 4-month-old boy is brought by his father to a general practice in Frankston, Victoria, for a routine visit about eczema. While examining his skin you notice a 1.5 cm purplish bruise over the left cheek and a smaller bruise on the outer ear. The father says the boy''s 3-year-old sister ''hit him with a plastic toy'' two days ago. The boy cannot roll over yet. He was born at term, is thriving along the 50th centile and has had all his scheduled immunisations.

He is alert and smiles, with a soft anterior fontanelle. There are no other bruises, petechiae or mouth injuries, and his limbs move normally without obvious pain. There is no family history of bleeding disorders. The father is calm and cooperative and says the mother is at work.

Which one of the following is the most appropriate next step?','["Accept the explanation, document the bruise and review the boy in one week", "Arrange a full blood count and coagulation studies and review the results tomorrow", "Refer to an ophthalmologist as an outpatient to check for retinal haemorrhages", "Report to Child Protection and wait for their assessment before any investigations", "Arrange same-day hospital assessment including skeletal survey, bloods and a report"]'::jsonb,'E','CORRECT ANSWER

E. Arrange same-day hospital assessment including skeletal survey, bloods and a report

---

Any bruise in a non-mobile infant (not yet cruising) is a sentinel sign that needs assessment for physical abuse: ''those who don''t cruise rarely bruise''. Bruising of the face or ear in an infant is a recognised high-risk pattern (TEN-4-FACESp: torso, ears, neck; frenulum, angle of jaw, cheeks, eyelids, subconjunctivae; or ANY bruise in a child 4 months or younger). The explanation that a toddler caused it is plausible but does not remove the need for work-up. The Victorian Forensic Paediatric Medical Service advises same-day paediatric/forensic assessment with a skeletal survey (particularly for infants under 6 months with suspected physical abuse), FBE and coagulation screen, consideration of neuroimaging and ophthalmology, and a report to Child Protection. Doctors in Victoria are mandatory reporters when they form a belief on reasonable grounds that a child needs protection because of physical injury. Assessment and reporting happen in parallel and are handled non-judgementally with the family.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Any bruise in a non-mobile infant, especially to the face, ears or neck, needs same-day paediatric/forensic assessment: skeletal survey, FBE and coagulation, consideration of neuroimaging, and a child protection report.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "Victorian Forensic Paediatric Medical Service (RCH). Bruising guideline", "url": "https://www.rch.org.au/vfpms/guidelines/Bruising/"}
• {"name": "Victorian Forensic Paediatric Medical Service (RCH). Fractures guideline", "url": "https://www.rch.org.au/vfpms/guidelines/Fractures/"}
• {"name": "The Royal Children''s Hospital Melbourne. Clinical Practice Guideline: Child abuse", "url": "https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/Child_abuse/"}

---

EDITED BY HEISENBERG','difficult','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "bank170", "review_status": "owner_approved_2026_09_29", "tags": ["ZQ-0204", "zyntra_bank", "mock1", "level_4", "task_data_gathering", "group_child_health", "sys_paediatric_medicine", "approved_by_owner_2026_09_29"], "clinician_task": "data_gathering", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"Victorian Forensic Paediatric Medical Service (RCH). Bruising guideline\", \"url\": \"https://www.rch.org.au/vfpms/guidelines/Bruising/\"}; {\"name\": \"Victorian Forensic Paediatric Medical Service (RCH). Fractures guideline\", \"url\": \"https://www.rch.org.au/vfpms/guidelines/Fractures/\"}; {\"name\": \"The Royal Children''s Hospital Melbourne. Clinical Practice Guideline: Child abuse\", \"url\": \"https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/Child_abuse/\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "Accepting a plausible story without assessment risks missing abuse. Sentinel injuries often come before more serious injuries.", "B": "Blood tests to exclude a bleeding disorder are part of the work-up, but alone they are incomplete. Occult fractures and head injury need to be looked for, and assessment should not be delayed.", "C": "Retinal examination may be part of the assessment, but outpatient ophthalmology alone is neither timely nor complete.", "D": "A report is appropriate, but medical assessment should not wait for Child Protection. The doctor arranges urgent medical evaluation, including looking for occult injury, in parallel."}, "key_takeaways": ["Any bruise in a non-mobile infant, especially to the face, ears or neck, needs same-day paediatric/forensic assessment: skeletal survey, FBE and coagulation, consideration of neuroimaging, and a child protection report."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,1.0,1.0,'tier_prior','tier=4 (bank170)'),
('18473f80-2b00-5d97-ba55-108da106f416'::uuid,'ZQ-0205','child-health','child-development','A 15-year-old girl is brought by her mother to a general practice in Launceston, Tasmania, because of headaches on most days for the past 3 months and missing about two school days a week. The headaches are frontal and band-like, are worse in the afternoon, are not present on waking and are not associated with vomiting or visual symptoms. Paracetamol helps a little. Her mother says she ''spends all night on her phone'' and has recently dropped out of her netball team. The girl sits with her arms folded and lets her mother answer most questions.

Her blood pressure is 108/66 mmHg, and her neurological examination, including fundoscopy, is normal. Her height and weight are on the 50th centile. She has no past medical history and takes no regular medicines.

Which one of the following is the most appropriate next step in the assessment?','["Arrange a CT scan of the brain to reassure the family that there is no tumour", "Explain confidentiality and its limits, then talk with her alone using HEEADSSS", "Ask the mother to stay so that she can provide the most accurate history", "Give her a headache diary and review her in 3 months with her mother", "Refer her to a paediatric neurologist for assessment of chronic daily headache"]'::jsonb,'B','CORRECT ANSWER

B. Explain confidentiality and its limits, then talk with her alone using HEEADSSS

---

Her headaches have features of tension-type headache with no red flags, and her normal neurological examination and fundoscopy make a structural cause unlikely. The school absence, withdrawal from sport, late-night phone use and reluctance to speak suggest a psychosocial driver (for example bullying, low mood, anxiety, family conflict, substance use or abuse) that will be missed if only her mother speaks. The RCH guideline recommends explaining confidentiality (and its limits: risk of serious harm to self or others, or abuse) and completing a HEEADSSS psychosocial assessment (Home, Education/Employment, Eating/Exercise, Activities, Drugs/alcohol, Sexuality, Suicide/depression, Safety) with the adolescent alone for at least part of the consultation.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
With adolescents, explain confidentiality and its limits, then spend time with them alone using HEEADSSS. Psychosocial issues often drive somatic presentations such as headache and school refusal.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "The Royal Children''s Hospital Melbourne. Clinical Practice Guideline: Engaging with and assessing the adolescent patient (May 2025)", "url": "https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/Engaging_with_and_assessing_the_adolescent_patient/"}

