EDITED BY HEISENBERG','moderate','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "bank170", "review_status": "owner_approved_2026_09_29", "tags": ["ZQ-0205", "zyntra_bank", "mock1", "level_3", "task_data_gathering", "group_child_health", "sys_development", "approved_by_owner_2026_09_29"], "clinician_task": "data_gathering", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"The Royal Children''s Hospital Melbourne. Clinical Practice Guideline: Engaging with and assessing the adolescent patient (May 2025)\", \"url\": \"https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/Engaging_with_and_assessing_the_adolescent_patient/\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "Imaging is not indicated without red flags (such as morning vomiting, papilloedema, focal signs or a change in pattern). It exposes her to radiation and does not address the likely cause.", "C": "Parents are valuable informants, but adolescents often do not disclose sensitive information in front of them. Time alone is recommended practice.", "D": "A diary may help later, but deferring for 3 months misses a chance to identify psychosocial problems or risk now.", "E": "Specialist referral is not needed for tension-type headache without red flags. The key next step is a psychosocial assessment."}, "key_takeaways": ["With adolescents, explain confidentiality and its limits, then spend time with them alone using HEEADSSS. Psychosocial issues often drive somatic presentations such as headache and school refusal."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,0.0,1.0,'tier_prior','tier=3 (bank170)'),
('6a366d69-d327-57ac-9f50-24c6edb27392'::uuid,'ZQ-0206','child-health','neonatology','A 4-day-old boy is brought to the emergency department of a regional hospital in Wagga Wagga, New South Wales, because over the past 6 hours he has become grey, breathless and uninterested in feeds. He was born at term after an uncomplicated pregnancy with a normal 20-week morphology scan, and he was discharged home on day 2 after a normal newborn examination.

He is mottled and grey, with a heart rate of 196/min, respiratory rate 72/min and capillary refill time of 5 seconds. Brachial pulses are weak, and femoral pulses cannot be felt. The liver edge is 4 cm below the costal margin. Oxygen saturation is 90% in the right hand and 84% in the foot. There is a soft systolic murmur.

Investigations:
- Venous pH 7.04 (7.35–7.45)
- Lactate 9.2 mmol/L (<2.0)
- Glucose 4.1 mmol/L

A 10 mL/kg bolus of 0.9% sodium chloride has been given with no improvement. IV access is in place, and the newborn emergency transport service has been called.

Which one of the following is the most appropriate immediate treatment?','["Start an IV alprostadil (prostaglandin E1) infusion", "Give IV indomethacin to reduce ductal shunting", "Give 100% oxygen by mask and further 20 mL/kg saline boluses", "Give IV furosemide 1 mg/kg to treat heart failure", "Give IV antibiotics and await an urgent echocardiogram"]'::jsonb,'A','CORRECT ANSWER

A. Start an IV alprostadil (prostaglandin E1) infusion

---

A previously well neonate who collapses in the first days to weeks of life with shock, absent or weak femoral pulses, hepatomegaly, a differential in saturations and severe lactic acidosis most likely has a duct-dependent systemic circulation (for example critical coarctation, interrupted aortic arch or hypoplastic left heart syndrome) with closure of the ductus arteriosus. A normal antenatal scan does not exclude it. The immediate life-saving treatment is an IV prostaglandin E1 (alprostadil) infusion to reopen the duct, with the dose set on specialist/retrieval advice (higher starting doses in shock). Be ready to support the airway, because apnoea is a common dose-related adverse effect. Other care runs in parallel: cautious fluids, inotropes, correction of acidosis and glucose, and empirical antibiotics because sepsis cannot be excluded, with urgent echocardiography and retrieval.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Neonate in shock at days 2–14 with poor femoral pulses, hepatomegaly and pre/post-ductal saturation difference: start a prostaglandin E1 infusion empirically (watch for apnoea) while arranging echo and retrieval.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "The Royal Children''s Hospital Melbourne. Paediatric Injectable Guidelines: Alprostadil", "url": "https://pig.rch.org.au/pages/alprostadil"}
• {"name": "Child and Adolescent Health Service WA, Newborn Emergency Transport Service. Congenital heart disease guideline", "url": "https://cahs.health.wa.gov.au/~/media/HSPs/CAHS/Documents/Health-Professionals/NETS/Congenital-Heart-Disease-CHD.pdf"}

---

EDITED BY HEISENBERG','difficult','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "bank170", "review_status": "owner_approved_2026_09_29", "tags": ["ZQ-0206", "zyntra_bank", "mock1", "level_4", "task_management", "group_child_health", "sys_neonatology", "approved_by_owner_2026_09_29"], "clinician_task": "management", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"The Royal Children''s Hospital Melbourne. Paediatric Injectable Guidelines: Alprostadil\", \"url\": \"https://pig.rch.org.au/pages/alprostadil\"}; {\"name\": \"Child and Adolescent Health Service WA, Newborn Emergency Transport Service. Congenital heart disease guideline\", \"url\": \"https://cahs.health.wa.gov.au/~/media/HSPs/CAHS/Documents/Health-Professionals/NETS/Congenital-Heart-Disease-CHD.pdf\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"B": "Indomethacin closes the ductus arteriosus. In duct-dependent lesions this would be fatal.", "C": "High oxygen concentrations promote ductal closure, and repeated large boluses may worsen heart failure (he has hepatomegaly). Neither reopens the duct.", "D": "Furosemide does not treat the underlying cause (loss of systemic blood flow through the duct) and may worsen shock.", "E": "Antibiotics are appropriate in parallel because sepsis is a differential, but withholding prostaglandin until an echo is done risks death. Start PGE1 empirically when duct-dependent disease is suspected."}, "key_takeaways": ["Neonate in shock at days 2–14 with poor femoral pulses, hepatomegaly and pre/post-ductal saturation difference: start a prostaglandin E1 infusion empirically (watch for apnoea) while arranging echo and retrieval."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,1.0,1.0,'tier_prior','tier=4 (bank170)'),
('342b44ee-2979-50ca-8b4a-e3d66a42297f'::uuid,'ZQ-0207','child-health','paediatric-medicine','A 2-year-old girl is brought to the emergency department of a regional hospital in Cairns, Queensland, 50 minutes after her father found her chewing a TV remote control with the battery cover open. One 20 mm lithium coin battery is missing and cannot be found in the room. Nobody saw her swallow anything.

She is playing happily and eating a biscuit. She has no drooling, coughing, vomiting, stridor or chest pain. Her heart rate is 118/min, respiratory rate 26/min and oxygen saturation 99% on room air. Examination of the mouth, chest and abdomen is normal. She weighs 12 kg and has no allergies. Her father asks whether they can go home and ''watch her nappies''.

Which one of the following is the most appropriate next step?','["Discharge with advice to check her stools, because she has no symptoms", "Arrange an abdominal X-ray, because most batteries pass beyond the stomach", "Arrange an urgent X-ray from neck to abdomen, giving honey while it is organised", "Observe her in the department for 6 hours and X-ray her if symptoms develop", "Give a nasogastric laxative preparation to hasten passage of the battery"]'::jsonb,'C','CORRECT ANSWER

C. Arrange an urgent X-ray from neck to abdomen, giving honey while it is organised

---

Any suspected button battery ingestion is an emergency, even when the child is asymptomatic and the ingestion was not witnessed. A lithium coin cell of 20 mm or more can lodge in the oesophagus and cause severe caustic injury within 2 hours (perforation, tracheo-oesophageal or aorto-oesophageal fistula). RCH and Queensland (CHQ) guidelines recommend urgent X-ray covering the neck, chest and abdomen (a battery shows a ''halo''/double-ring sign). A battery in the oesophagus needs endoscopic removal as soon as possible (ideally within 2 hours). For children over 1 year within 12 hours of ingestion, honey 10 mL every 10 minutes (up to 6 doses) can be given while arranging imaging or transfer, but it must not delay them. Batteries beyond the oesophagus are managed according to size, age, symptoms and time, often with repeat imaging. The Poisons Information Centre (13 11 26) can advise.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Suspected button battery ingestion: urgent X-ray from neck to abdomen, even if asymptomatic. An oesophageal battery needs emergency removal within 2 h. Honey 10 mL every 10 min (up to 6 doses) if over 12 months and within 12 h, without delaying imaging or transfer.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "The Royal Children''s Hospital Melbourne. Clinical Practice Guideline: Foreign body ingestion", "url": "https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/Foreign_body_ingestion/"}
• {"name": "Children''s Health Queensland. Queensland Paediatric Clinical Guideline: Foreign body ingested", "url": "https://www.childrens.health.qld.gov.au/for-health-professionals/queensland-paediatric-emergency-care-qpec/queensland-paediatric-clinical-guidelines/foreign-body-ingested"}

---

EDITED BY HEISENBERG','difficult','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "bank170", "review_status": "owner_approved_2026_09_29", "tags": ["ZQ-0207", "zyntra_bank", "mock1", "level_4", "task_data_gathering", "group_child_health", "sys_paediatric_medicine", "approved_by_owner_2026_09_29"], "clinician_task": "data_gathering", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"The Royal Children''s Hospital Melbourne. Clinical Practice Guideline: Foreign body ingestion\", \"url\": \"https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/Foreign_body_ingestion/\"}; {\"name\": \"Children''s Health Queensland. Queensland Paediatric Clinical Guideline: Foreign body ingested\", \"url\": \"https://www.childrens.health.qld.gov.au/for-health-professionals/queensland-paediatric-emergency-care-qpec/queensland-paediatric-clinical-guidelines/foreign-body-ingested\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "Absence of symptoms does not exclude an oesophageal battery. Serious injury can develop silently over hours.", "B": "An abdominal film can miss a battery lodged in the upper oesophagus. The film has to include the neck and chest.", "D": "Waiting for symptoms delays diagnosis while caustic injury progresses. Imaging is urgent.", "E": "Laxatives do not remove an oesophageal battery and delay the key step: urgent imaging to locate it."}, "key_takeaways": ["Suspected button battery ingestion: urgent X-ray from neck to abdomen, even if asymptomatic. An oesophageal battery needs emergency removal within 2 h. Honey 10 mL every 10 min (up to 6 doses) if over 12 months and within 12 h, without delaying imaging or transfer."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,1.0,1.0,'tier_prior','tier=4 (bank170)'),
('27ed669f-2806-5115-a9a7-a0643ec9b27b'::uuid,'ZQ-0208','child-health','paediatric-medicine','A 10-year-old Torres Strait Islander girl is brought to the primary health care centre on Thursday Island, Queensland, by her mother and grandmother. For 3 weeks her teachers have noticed that her handwriting has become messy, she drops things and she ''can''t sit still''. Her mother says she has become tearful and irritable, which is unlike her. She had no sore throat or skin sores that the family can recall in the past 3 months. She is otherwise well, and her immunisations are up to date. The clinic''s Torres Strait Islander Health Practitioner, who knows the family, is present at their request.

She has irregular, non-rhythmic, jerky movements of her face, hands and feet that are worse when she is anxious. When asked to squeeze the examiner''s fingers her grip waxes and wanes, and she cannot keep her tongue protruded for more than a few seconds. She can walk and feed herself, but more slowly than usual. Her temperature is 36.8 °C, and her heart rate is 88/min. There is no murmur, joint swelling or rash. She weighs 31 kg.

Investigations:
- ESR 12 mm/h (<20)
- CRP 3 mg/L (<5)
- Anti-streptolysin O and anti-DNase B titres: within the normal range for age
- Full blood count, electrolytes, calcium, glucose, thyroid function and copper studies: normal
- ECG: normal PR interval

The paediatrician on telehealth has reviewed a video of the movements and agrees they are choreiform. Other causes of chorea have been excluded as far as possible, and no medicines or toxins are involved.

Which one of the following is the most appropriate management?','["Explain it is not rheumatic fever because the streptococcal titres are normal, and refer to neurology", "Start oral haloperidol for the movements and repeat the streptococcal titres in 2 weeks", "Treat as a tic disorder with behavioural therapy and review her again in 3 months", "Treat as acute rheumatic fever: benzathine benzylpenicillin G, echocardiogram, and consider carbamazepine", "Give a 10-day course of oral penicillin and review whether prophylaxis is needed in 1 month"]'::jsonb,'D','CORRECT ANSWER

D. Treat as acute rheumatic fever: benzathine benzylpenicillin G, echocardiogram, and consider carbamazepine

---

Sydenham chorea is a major manifestation of acute rheumatic fever. It often appears weeks to months after the streptococcal infection, when inflammatory markers and streptococcal serology may already have returned to normal. Under the 2020 Australian guideline (Edition 3.3), Sydenham chorea ALONE is enough to diagnose ARF, provided other causes of chorea are excluded. Other manifestations and evidence of preceding Strep A infection are not required. Management: give benzathine benzylpenicillin G (to treat any residual Strep A and begin secondary prophylaxis; 1,200,000 units for her weight of 20 kg or more), notify and register her with the state RHD program, and arrange echocardiography, which is essential for all patients with chorea because subclinical carditis is common. Her chorea is moderate (motor impersistence, functional slowing, mood change), so the guideline supports an anticonvulsant such as carbamazepine (or sodium valproate if there is no pregnancy risk), with supportive measures and a calm environment. Haloperidol is now largely discouraged. Care is planned with her family and the Torres Strait Islander Health Practitioner, including a culturally safe, pain-minimising approach to regular BPG injections.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Sydenham chorea alone (other causes excluded) is enough to diagnose ARF, even with normal ESR/CRP and streptococcal titres. Start BPG secondary prophylaxis, get an echo, notify, and use carbamazepine or valproate for moderate chorea (not haloperidol).

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "RHDAustralia. The 2020 Australian guideline for prevention, diagnosis and management of ARF and RHD (Edition 3.3, 2025), Key information", "url": "https://rhdaustralia.org.au/wp-content/uploads/2025/08/Key-Information-2025_August.pdf"}

---

EDITED BY HEISENBERG','difficult','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "bank170", "review_status": "owner_approved_2026_09_29", "tags": ["ZQ-0208", "zyntra_bank", "mock1", "level_5", "task_management", "group_child_health", "sys_paediatric_medicine", "approved_by_owner_2026_09_29"], "clinician_task": "management", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"RHDAustralia. The 2020 Australian guideline for prevention, diagnosis and management of ARF and RHD (Edition 3.3, 2025), Key information\", \"url\": \"https://rhdaustralia.org.au/wp-content/uploads/2025/08/Key-Information-2025_August.pdf\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "Chorea is the one manifestation of ARF that does not need evidence of preceding Strep A infection. Normal titres are expected in late-onset chorea and do not exclude ARF.", "B": "Haloperidol is largely discouraged because of sedation and extrapyramidal effects. Delaying the diagnosis and penicillin prophylaxis while waiting for titres is inappropriate.", "C": "New-onset chorea with motor impersistence and emotional lability in a child from a high-risk setting is Sydenham chorea until proven otherwise, not a tic disorder. Delay risks missed carditis and recurrence.", "E": "A short oral course does not provide secondary prophylaxis. Once ARF is diagnosed, regular BPG is started, and the need for it is not ''reviewed'' after a month."}, "key_takeaways": ["Sydenham chorea alone (other causes excluded) is enough to diagnose ARF, even with normal ESR/CRP and streptococcal titres. Start BPG secondary prophylaxis, get an echo, notify, and use carbamazepine or valproate for moderate chorea (not haloperidol)."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,2.0,1.0,'tier_prior','tier=5 (bank170)'),
('c4a9c921-8e9e-5ce4-87bb-7634622efbd6'::uuid,'ZQ-0209','child-health','neonatology','You are the resident attending an emergency caesarean section at 39 weeks of gestation at a hospital in Mackay, Queensland, for a pathological cardiotocograph. The liquor is clear.

The baby girl is floppy and pale at birth and is placed on the resuscitaire under a radiant warmer. She is dried, the wet towel is removed, and her head is placed in a neutral position. She is gently stimulated. At 30 seconds of age she is still not breathing, and her heart rate by auscultation is 80/min. A pulse oximeter probe is being placed on her right wrist. The neonatal team has been called and is 3 minutes away.

Which one of the following is the most appropriate next step?','["Start chest compressions at a 3:1 ratio with ventilation", "Give positive-pressure ventilation by mask using air (21% oxygen)", "Give positive-pressure ventilation by mask using 100% oxygen", "Intubate and suction the trachea before starting any ventilation", "Give adrenaline 10 micrograms/kg via an umbilical venous catheter"]'::jsonb,'B','CORRECT ANSWER

B. Give positive-pressure ventilation by mask using air (21% oxygen)

---

An apnoeic newborn (or one with a heart rate below 100/min) after drying, warming, airway positioning and stimulation needs positive-pressure ventilation by face mask within the first minute. ANZCOR recommends starting resuscitation of infants ≥35 weeks with air (21% oxygen), then titrating oxygen to pre-ductal SpO2 targets. Effective ventilation is the most important intervention in newborn resuscitation; most babies'' heart rates rise once the lungs are aerated. Chest compressions (3:1) start only if the heart rate stays below 60/min despite 30 seconds of effective ventilation.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Newborn apnoeic or HR <100 after initial steps: PPV by mask within the first minute, starting with air (21%) at ≥35 weeks. Compressions only if HR stays <60 despite 30 s of effective ventilation.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "ANZCOR Guideline 13.4: Airway management and mask ventilation of the newborn", "url": "https://www.anzcor.org/home/neonatal-resuscitation/guideline-13-4-airway-management-and-mask-ventilation-of-the-newborn"}

---

EDITED BY HEISENBERG','moderate','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "bank170", "review_status": "owner_approved_2026_09_29", "tags": ["ZQ-0209", "zyntra_bank", "mock1", "level_3", "task_management", "group_child_health", "sys_neonatology", "approved_by_owner_2026_09_29"], "clinician_task": "management", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"ANZCOR Guideline 13.4: Airway management and mask ventilation of the newborn\", \"url\": \"https://www.anzcor.org/home/neonatal-resuscitation/guideline-13-4-airway-management-and-mask-ventilation-of-the-newborn\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "Compressions are indicated for a heart rate below 60/min after 30 seconds of effective ventilation, not for 80/min before any ventilation.", "C": "Starting with 100% oxygen in term or near-term infants is associated with worse outcomes than air. Oxygen is increased if saturations stay below targets or compressions are needed.", "D": "Routine tracheal suction is not recommended (even with meconium-stained liquor, which is absent here) and delays ventilation.", "E": "Adrenaline is for a heart rate below 60/min despite effective ventilation and compressions."}, "key_takeaways": ["Newborn apnoeic or HR <100 after initial steps: PPV by mask within the first minute, starting with air (21%) at ≥35 weeks. Compressions only if HR stays <60 despite 30 s of effective ventilation."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,0.0,1.0,'tier_prior','tier=3 (bank170)'),
('b7c103d8-28c9-5899-a082-16903e7a8255'::uuid,'ZQ-0210','child-health','paediatric-medicine','A 6-year-old boy with no previous seizures is brought by ambulance to the emergency department of a hospital in Hobart, Tasmania. His mother found him having a generalised tonic-clonic seizure at home 25 minutes ago. Paramedics gave intramuscular midazolam 0.15 mg/kg 15 minutes ago with no effect. On arrival he is still having generalised clonic movements.

He weighs 20 kg. His temperature is 38.1 °C, heart rate 150/min, blood pressure 108/68 mmHg and oxygen saturation 95% on 15 L/min oxygen by non-rebreather mask with an airway manoeuvre. Capillary blood glucose is 6.2 mmol/L. IV access is obtained, and he is given IV midazolam 0.15 mg/kg. Five minutes later he is still seizing.

Which one of the following is the most appropriate next step?','["Give a third benzodiazepine dose of IV diazepam 0.2 mg/kg", "Perform rapid sequence intubation with thiopentone now", "Give IV pyridoxine 100 mg for possible pyridoxine dependency", "Give rectal paraldehyde 0.4 mL/kg and wait 10 minutes", "Give IV levetiracetam 40–60 mg/kg over 5 minutes"]'::jsonb,'E','CORRECT ANSWER

E. Give IV levetiracetam 40–60 mg/kg over 5 minutes

---

He is in convulsive status epilepticus and has already had two benzodiazepine doses (one pre-hospital, one in hospital). The RCH Seizures – acute management guideline (June 2025) limits benzodiazepines to a MAXIMUM of two doses, including any given pre-hospital, because further doses add respiratory depression with little additional benefit. The next step is a second-line agent: levetiracetam 40–60 mg/kg IV/IO (max 4.5 g) over 5 minutes, or phenytoin 20 mg/kg as an alternative. If seizures continue after the second-line agent, a further second-line drug is given and anaesthetic/ICU help is called for rapid sequence induction. Look for and treat causes in parallel (glucose, electrolytes, infection, head injury).

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Paediatric status epilepticus: a maximum of two benzodiazepine doses (count pre-hospital doses), then a second-line agent such as levetiracetam 40–60 mg/kg IV over 5 min (or phenytoin 20 mg/kg). Escalate to RSI if still seizing.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "The Royal Children''s Hospital Melbourne. Clinical Practice Guideline: Seizures \u2013 acute management (June 2025)", "url": "https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/Seizures_-_acute_management/"}

---

EDITED BY HEISENBERG','difficult','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "bank170", "review_status": "owner_approved_2026_09_29", "tags": ["ZQ-0210", "zyntra_bank", "mock1", "level_4", "task_management", "group_child_health", "sys_paediatric_medicine", "approved_by_owner_2026_09_29"], "clinician_task": "management", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"The Royal Children''s Hospital Melbourne. Clinical Practice Guideline: Seizures \\u2013 acute management (June 2025)\", \"url\": \"https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/Seizures_-_acute_management/\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "A third benzodiazepine dose is not recommended. It increases the risk of apnoea and hypotension and rarely stops seizures that have not responded to two doses.", "B": "RSI/anaesthesia is for refractory status after second-line agents, or for airway compromise. He is oxygenating with airway support.", "C": "Pyridoxine is considered in infants (typically under 18 months) with refractory seizures. It is not the next step in a 6-year-old.", "D": "Paraldehyde is an older option that is not a standard step in current RCH guidance, and waiting prolongs status epilepticus."}, "key_takeaways": ["Paediatric status epilepticus: a maximum of two benzodiazepine doses (count pre-hospital doses), then a second-line agent such as levetiracetam 40–60 mg/kg IV over 5 min (or phenytoin 20 mg/kg). Escalate to RSI if still seizing."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,1.0,1.0,'tier_prior','tier=4 (bank170)'),
('8cd41fda-eebb-585b-824e-8797c06e8340'::uuid,'ZQ-0211','child-health','child-development','A 14-year-6-month-old boy is brought by his mother to a general practice in Darwin, Northern Territory, because he is the shortest boy in his year and is being teased. He is otherwise well, plays soccer and is doing well at school. His sense of smell is normal. His father recalls that he ''shot up at 17'' and started shaving at university. His mother had her first period at 13.

His height is 148 cm (below the 3rd centile), and his weight is on the 10th centile. His growth record from the school nurse and previous GP visits shows height tracking just below and parallel to the 3rd centile since age 4, with a growth velocity of 4.8 cm in the last year. His mid-parental target height is on the 50th centile. Testicular volumes are 4 mL bilaterally (Tanner stage 2 genitalia), and there is no pubic hair. He has no dysmorphic features, and the rest of the examination is normal.

Investigations:
- Bone age (Greulich and Pyle): 12 years
- TSH 2.1 mU/L (0.5–4.0)
- Free T4 14 pmol/L (10–20)
- Coeliac serology: negative
- Full blood count, ESR, electrolytes and creatinine: normal

Which one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?','["Familial short stature", "Growth hormone deficiency", "Constitutional delay of growth and puberty", "Hypogonadotropic hypogonadism (Kallmann syndrome)", "Primary hypothyroidism"]'::jsonb,'C','CORRECT ANSWER

C. Constitutional delay of growth and puberty

---

Constitutional delay of growth and puberty is the most common cause of short stature with late puberty in boys. Features: normal height velocity for bone age, height tracking parallel to a low centile, a delayed bone age (here 2.5 years behind), a family history of late puberty (''late bloomer'' father), a normal examination and normal screening tests. Testicular volume of 4 mL shows puberty has only just started at 14.5 years, which argues against hypogonadism. Final adult height is usually close to the genetic target. Management is reassurance and monitoring; a short course of low-dose testosterone can be offered by a paediatric endocrinologist for psychosocial distress.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Short boy growing parallel to a low centile with delayed bone age, late puberty and a ''late bloomer'' parent has constitutional delay. Familial short stature has normal bone age; GH deficiency and hypothyroidism cause falling velocity.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "Palmert MR, Dunkel L. Delayed puberty. N Engl J Med 2012;366:443\u2013453", "url": "https://doi.org/10.1056/NEJMcp1109290"}

