EDITED BY HEISENBERG','difficult','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "bank170", "review_status": "owner_approved_2026_09_29", "tags": ["ZQ-0211", "zyntra_bank", "mock1", "level_4", "task_data_interpretation_synthesis", "group_child_health", "sys_development", "approved_by_owner_2026_09_29"], "clinician_task": "data_interpretation_synthesis", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"Palmert MR, Dunkel L. Delayed puberty. N Engl J Med 2012;366:443\\u2013453\", \"url\": \"https://doi.org/10.1056/NEJMcp1109290\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "In familial short stature, bone age matches chronological age, puberty occurs at a normal time and parents are short. His mid-parental height is on the 50th centile.", "B": "GH deficiency causes growth velocity that falls away (crossing centiles downwards), often with increased truncal adiposity. He is growing in parallel to the centile at a normal prepubertal velocity.", "D": "Kallmann syndrome causes absent puberty, often with anosmia. He has a normal sense of smell and 4 mL testes (early puberty), and his father''s history suggests constitutional delay.", "E": "His thyroid function is normal. Hypothyroidism also causes falling height velocity and weight gain."}, "key_takeaways": ["Short boy growing parallel to a low centile with delayed bone age, late puberty and a ''late bloomer'' parent has constitutional delay. Familial short stature has normal bone age; GH deficiency and hypothyroidism cause falling velocity."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,1.0,1.0,'tier_prior','tier=4 (bank170)'),
('8c867f9b-9236-5efa-a115-29885f9b1e7e'::uuid,'ZQ-0212','child-health','paediatric-medicine','A 9-year-old girl with newly diagnosed type 1 diabetes is being treated for severe diabetic ketoacidosis in the emergency department of a regional hospital in Ballarat, Victoria. At presentation her venous pH was 6.98, bicarbonate 4 mmol/L, glucose 32 mmol/L, sodium 131 mmol/L and urea 11 mmol/L. She is receiving IV 0.9% sodium chloride with potassium at the calculated rate and an insulin infusion at 0.05 units/kg/hour, started one hour after fluids.

Four hours into treatment she complains of a severe headache and vomits. She is now confused and opens her eyes only to voice; her GCS has fallen from 15 to 11. Her heart rate has fallen from 124/min to 68/min, and her blood pressure has risen from 104/60 to 132/84 mmHg. Pupils are equal and reactive. Capillary glucose is 17 mmol/L. The paediatric retrieval service has been called.

Which one of the following is the most appropriate immediate management?','["Give mannitol 0.5 g/kg IV over 20 minutes and reduce the fluid rate by one-third", "Arrange an urgent CT scan of the brain and treat if oedema is confirmed", "Increase the IV fluid rate, because the bradycardia suggests hypovolaemia", "Give sodium bicarbonate to correct the acidosis more rapidly", "Stop the insulin infusion and give a 5 mL/kg bolus of 10% glucose"]'::jsonb,'A','CORRECT ANSWER

A. Give mannitol 0.5 g/kg IV over 20 minutes and reduce the fluid rate by one-third

---

New headache, vomiting and falling GCS, with bradycardia and rising blood pressure (Cushing response), during DKA treatment strongly suggest DKA-related cerebral injury (cerebral oedema). This is most common in young children with new-onset diabetes and severe acidosis, often 4–12 hours into treatment. The RCH DKA guideline says to treat immediately, without waiting for imaging: hyperosmolar therapy with mannitol 20% 0.5 g/kg IV over 20 minutes OR 3% hypertonic saline 3 mL/kg over 15 minutes, reduce IV fluids by one-third, elevate the head of the bed, and involve ICU/retrieval and senior staff. CT can be done after the child is stabilised, to exclude other causes such as thrombosis or haemorrhage.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
DKA with headache, falling GCS, bradycardia and hypertension is cerebral injury: give mannitol 0.5 g/kg or 3% saline 3 mL/kg now (no CT first), cut fluids by one-third, head up, and call ICU.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "The Royal Children''s Hospital Melbourne. Clinical Practice Guideline: Diabetic ketoacidosis (Jul 2025)", "url": "https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/Diabetic_Ketoacidosis/"}

---

EDITED BY HEISENBERG','difficult','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "bank170", "review_status": "owner_approved_2026_09_29", "tags": ["ZQ-0212", "zyntra_bank", "mock1", "level_4", "task_management", "group_child_health", "sys_paediatric_medicine", "approved_by_owner_2026_09_29"], "clinician_task": "management", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"The Royal Children''s Hospital Melbourne. Clinical Practice Guideline: Diabetic ketoacidosis (Jul 2025)\", \"url\": \"https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/Diabetic_Ketoacidosis/\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"B": "Imaging should not delay treatment. Cerebral injury is a clinical diagnosis, and early CT can be normal.", "C": "Bradycardia with hypertension here reflects raised intracranial pressure, not hypovolaemia. Increasing fluids may worsen the oedema.", "D": "Bicarbonate is not recommended in paediatric DKA except in specific life-threatening situations, and it is associated with cerebral injury.", "E": "Stopping insulin prolongs ketoacidosis. A glucose of 17 does not call for a glucose bolus; glucose is added to fluids when glucose falls, as per protocol."}, "key_takeaways": ["DKA with headache, falling GCS, bradycardia and hypertension is cerebral injury: give mannitol 0.5 g/kg or 3% saline 3 mL/kg now (no CT first), cut fluids by one-third, head up, and call ICU."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,1.0,1.0,'tier_prior','tier=4 (bank170)'),
('a6c0ab74-3164-5201-a824-82f669f2b3fe'::uuid,'ZQ-0214','mental-health','mood-disorders','A 27-year-old graphic designer sees a general practitioner in Adelaide, South Australia, with 5 weeks of low mood, loss of interest, sleeping 11 hours a night, increased appetite, poor concentration and feelings of worthlessness. She has no thoughts of self-harm. This is her third episode like this since age 19; the previous two got better after several months, and she was never treated.

On careful questioning, she describes at least three periods, each lasting 5–6 days, when she felt ''switched on'': she needed only 4 hours of sleep without feeling tired, talked quickly, started several design projects at once and spent more than usual on clothes. Her colleagues noticed she was ''unusually chatty''. She kept working and was never admitted to hospital, and she never lost touch with reality. She drinks alcohol socially and does not use other drugs. Thyroid function is normal.

Her mother has had treatment for ''depression and mood swings''.

Which one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?','["Bipolar I disorder, current episode depressed", "Bipolar II disorder, current episode depressed", "Cyclothymic disorder", "Recurrent major depressive disorder with atypical features", "Borderline personality disorder"]'::jsonb,'B','CORRECT ANSWER

B. Bipolar II disorder, current episode depressed

---

She has had recurrent major depressive episodes and at least one hypomanic episode: an elevated mood with decreased need for sleep, pressured speech and increased goal-directed activity lasting at least 4 consecutive days, noticed by others, without marked impairment, hospitalisation or psychosis. Major depression plus hypomania (never mania) is bipolar II disorder. Recognising it matters because antidepressant monotherapy may destabilise mood, and treatment follows bipolar depression guidance (for example quetiapine, lamotrigine or lithium, with psychoeducation), as in the RANZCP mood disorders guideline. Features that should prompt screening for bipolarity in depression include early onset, atypical features (hypersomnia, hyperphagia), recurrence and a family history.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Depression plus at least one hypomanic episode (≥4 days, noticed by others, no marked impairment or psychosis) is bipolar II. Always ask about past elevated mood before starting an antidepressant.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "RANZCP. Clinical practice guidelines for mood disorders (Malhi et al., 2020)", "url": "https://www.ranzcp.org/getmedia/a8c79505-c0b5-46b4-9f27-67f79e047674/Mood-disorders-clinical-practice-guideline-2020.pdf"}

---

EDITED BY HEISENBERG','difficult','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "bank170", "review_status": "owner_approved_2026_09_29", "tags": ["ZQ-0214", "zyntra_bank", "mock1", "level_4", "task_data_interpretation_synthesis", "group_mental_health", "sys_mood", "approved_by_owner_2026_09_29"], "clinician_task": "data_interpretation_synthesis", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"RANZCP. Clinical practice guidelines for mood disorders (Malhi et al., 2020)\", \"url\": \"https://www.ranzcp.org/getmedia/a8c79505-c0b5-46b4-9f27-67f79e047674/Mood-disorders-clinical-practice-guideline-2020.pdf\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "Bipolar I needs at least one MANIC episode (lasting 7 days or needing hospitalisation, or with marked impairment or psychosis). Her episodes did not impair function and had no psychosis.", "C": "Cyclothymia involves 2 years of numerous hypomanic and depressive symptoms that never meet criteria for a hypomanic or major depressive episode. She has had full major depressive episodes.", "D": "She does have atypical features, but her history of hypomanic episodes rules out unipolar depression.", "E": "Mood changes in BPD are brief (hours to days), reactive to interpersonal stress and occur with identity disturbance and impulsivity. Her episodes are sustained, distinct changes from her baseline."}, "key_takeaways": ["Depression plus at least one hypomanic episode (≥4 days, noticed by others, no marked impairment or psychosis) is bipolar II. Always ask about past elevated mood before starting an antidepressant."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,1.0,1.0,'tier_prior','tier=4 (bank170)'),
('41a59c26-3672-5067-8794-37629979132b'::uuid,'ZQ-0215','mental-health','mood-disorders','A 45-year-old man sees his general practitioner in Perth, Western Australia, for review. Seven months ago he had his first episode of major depression after being made redundant, with low mood, anhedonia, insomnia, weight loss and passive thoughts of death. He started sertraline, increased to 100 mg daily at week 4, and attended six sessions of CBT with a psychologist under a Mental Health Treatment Plan.

He has felt ''completely back to normal'' for the past 3 months. He has a new job, is sleeping well, exercises three times a week and has a PHQ-9 score of 3. He has no side effects apart from mildly delayed ejaculation. He has no history of mania, and there is no family history of mood disorder. He asks whether he can stop the tablets now ''because I feel fine''.

Which one of the following is the most appropriate advice?','["Stop sertraline now, because he has been in remission for 3 months", "Reduce sertraline to 50 mg daily as a maintenance dose for 2 years", "Taper sertraline over the next 2 weeks and restart it if symptoms recur", "Continue sertraline 100 mg daily for a further 3–9 months before a slow taper", "Continue sertraline 100 mg daily indefinitely because of the risk of relapse"]'::jsonb,'D','CORRECT ANSWER

D. Continue sertraline 100 mg daily for a further 3–9 months before a slow taper

---

After a first episode of major depression treated to remission, antidepressants should be continued at the dose that achieved remission for at least 6–12 months after remission, to reduce the high risk of early relapse. The RANZCP mood disorders guideline supports continuing effective treatment beyond remission, then withdrawing gradually (over weeks to months) with monitoring for relapse and discontinuation symptoms. He has been well for 3 months, so a further 3–9 months at the same dose (total 6–12 months) is appropriate. The side effect can be discussed, including strategies such as timing of the dose, rather than stopping early. Longer-term maintenance is considered for recurrent episodes or other risk factors.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
First episode of depression: continue the antidepressant at the full effective dose for 6–12 months after remission, then taper slowly. Do not reduce the dose during continuation.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "RANZCP. Clinical practice guidelines for mood disorders (Malhi et al., 2020)", "url": "https://www.ranzcp.org/getmedia/a8c79505-c0b5-46b4-9f27-67f79e047674/Mood-disorders-clinical-practice-guideline-2020.pdf"}
• {"name": "Malhi GS et al. The 2020 RANZCP clinical practice guidelines for mood disorders: major depression summary. Aust N Z J Psychiatry 2021", "url": "https://doi.org/10.1177/0004867420979353"}

---

EDITED BY HEISENBERG','moderate','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "bank170", "review_status": "owner_approved_2026_09_29", "tags": ["ZQ-0215", "zyntra_bank", "mock1", "level_3", "task_management", "group_mental_health", "sys_mood", "approved_by_owner_2026_09_29"], "clinician_task": "management", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"RANZCP. Clinical practice guidelines for mood disorders (Malhi et al., 2020)\", \"url\": \"https://www.ranzcp.org/getmedia/a8c79505-c0b5-46b4-9f27-67f79e047674/Mood-disorders-clinical-practice-guideline-2020.pdf\"}; {\"name\": \"Malhi GS et al. The 2020 RANZCP clinical practice guidelines for mood disorders: major depression summary. Aust N Z J Psychiatry 2021\", \"url\": \"https://doi.org/10.1177/0004867420979353\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "Stopping at 3 months of remission carries a high relapse risk; abrupt cessation of sertraline also causes discontinuation symptoms.", "B": "Reducing the dose during continuation increases relapse risk. The dose that achieved remission is continued. Two years of maintenance is not routinely needed after a single uncomplicated episode.", "C": "A 2-week taper at 3 months of remission is too early and too fast.", "E": "Indefinite maintenance is considered for recurrent (for example three or more) episodes or high-risk features, not after a single episode with full remission."}, "key_takeaways": ["First episode of depression: continue the antidepressant at the full effective dose for 6–12 months after remission, then taper slowly. Do not reduce the dose during continuation."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,0.0,1.0,'tier_prior','tier=3 (bank170)'),
('c3b7d06e-b5bd-51a9-b806-99b21fe36efd'::uuid,'ZQ-0216','mental-health','anxiety-disorders','A 34-year-old woman sees her general practitioner in Mackay, Queensland, 4 months after a crash on the Bruce Highway in which her car was hit by a truck and the other driver died at the scene. Her physical injuries have healed.

Since the crash she has had nightmares about the collision several times a week, intrusive images when she hears truck horns, and she avoids driving, taking a longer bus route to work. She feels ''on edge'', startles easily, sleeps 4–5 hours a night and feels guilty that she ''could have swerved''. She has had to reduce her hours at work. She has no thoughts of self-harm, drinks two standard drinks on weekends and does not use drugs. Her PCL-5 score is 52.

She asks what treatment is most likely to help.

Which one of the following is the most appropriate first-line treatment?','["Trauma-focused CBT such as cognitive processing therapy", "Sertraline 50 mg daily, increasing according to response", "Diazepam 5 mg at night for 4 weeks to improve her sleep", "A single session of psychological debriefing about the crash", "Quetiapine 25 mg at night for nightmares and hyperarousal"]'::jsonb,'A','CORRECT ANSWER

A. Trauma-focused CBT such as cognitive processing therapy

---

She meets criteria for PTSD more than a month after a traumatic event (intrusions, avoidance, negative changes in mood and cognition such as guilt, and hyperarousal, with functional impairment). The Phoenix Australia Australian PTSD Guidelines (NHMRC-approved) strongly recommend trauma-focused psychological therapies (cognitive processing therapy, cognitive therapy for PTSD, prolonged exposure, trauma-focused CBT or EMDR) as first-line treatment for adults. Medicines (SSRIs such as sertraline, paroxetine or fluoxetine, or venlafaxine) are recommended when trauma-focused therapy is not available, not preferred or has not helped enough. Benzodiazepines are not recommended. A GP Mental Health Treatment Plan can support referral, and a road-crash compensation scheme may fund treatment in some states.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
PTSD in adults: trauma-focused psychological therapy (CPT, CT-PTSD, PE, TF-CBT, EMDR) is first line. SSRIs or venlafaxine if therapy is unavailable or not preferred. Avoid benzodiazepines and debriefing.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "Phoenix Australia. Australian Guidelines for the Prevention and Treatment of Acute Stress Disorder, PTSD and Complex PTSD", "url": "https://www.phoenixaustralia.org/australian-guidelines-for-ptsd/"}
• {"name": "Phoenix Australia. PTSD Guidelines Chapter 6: Treatment recommendations", "url": "https://www.phoenixaustralia.org/wp-content/uploads/2022/11/PTSD-Guidelines-Chapter-6-Treatment-recommendations.pdf"}

---

EDITED BY HEISENBERG','moderate','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "bank170", "review_status": "owner_approved_2026_09_29", "tags": ["ZQ-0216", "zyntra_bank", "mock1", "level_3", "task_management", "group_mental_health", "sys_anxiety", "approved_by_owner_2026_09_29"], "clinician_task": "management", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"Phoenix Australia. Australian Guidelines for the Prevention and Treatment of Acute Stress Disorder, PTSD and Complex PTSD\", \"url\": \"https://www.phoenixaustralia.org/australian-guidelines-for-ptsd/\"}; {\"name\": \"Phoenix Australia. PTSD Guidelines Chapter 6: Treatment recommendations\", \"url\": \"https://www.phoenixaustralia.org/wp-content/uploads/2022/11/PTSD-Guidelines-Chapter-6-Treatment-recommendations.pdf\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"B": "An SSRI is a reasonable second-line option (or first choice if she prefers medicine or therapy is unavailable), but trauma-focused psychological therapy has stronger evidence and is first line.", "C": "Benzodiazepines do not treat PTSD, may interfere with recovery and carry dependence risk. They are not recommended.", "D": "Single-session psychological debriefing is not recommended; it does not prevent PTSD and may cause harm. She has established PTSD, which needs treatment.", "E": "Antipsychotics are not first-line treatment for PTSD; they are sometimes used as specialist adjuncts."}, "key_takeaways": ["PTSD in adults: trauma-focused psychological therapy (CPT, CT-PTSD, PE, TF-CBT, EMDR) is first line. SSRIs or venlafaxine if therapy is unavailable or not preferred. Avoid benzodiazepines and debriefing."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,0.0,1.0,'tier_prior','tier=3 (bank170)'),
('4530cbad-cc34-56b7-a9b5-a987b5cacbe4'::uuid,'ZQ-0217','mental-health','anxiety-disorders','A 34-year-old accountant sees a general practitioner in Canberra, Australian Capital Territory, because for about 9 months she has been worrying ''about everything'': her work, her parents'' health, money and her children''s safety. She finds it hard to stop worrying, feels tense, has neck and shoulder aches, tires easily, has trouble falling asleep and gets irritable with her family. She has had no panic attacks. She drinks one to two glasses of wine most nights ''to switch off'' and does not use other drugs. Caffeine intake is two coffees a day.

Physical examination, thyroid function and full blood count are normal. Her GAD-7 score is 15, and her PHQ-9 score is 8, with no thoughts of self-harm. She says a friend ''got Valium and it worked straight away'' and asks for the same. She is open to other options and has a smartphone and reliable internet.

Which one of the following is the most appropriate initial management?','["Regular diazepam 5 mg three times daily, reviewed in 4 weeks", "Low-dose quetiapine 25 mg at night to help her worry and sleep", "Cognitive behavioural therapy, face to face or internet-delivered", "Regular propranolol 40 mg twice daily for the physical symptoms", "Pregabalin 75 mg twice daily as the first-line medicine"]'::jsonb,'C','CORRECT ANSWER

C. Cognitive behavioural therapy, face to face or internet-delivered

---

She has generalised anxiety disorder (more than 6 months of excessive, hard-to-control worry with muscle tension, fatigue, sleep disturbance and irritability, causing distress). First-line treatment is CBT, which can be delivered face to face (e.g. with a psychologist through a Mental Health Treatment Plan) or through evidence-based internet-delivered programs such as MindSpot (free, national) or THIS WAY UP. If medicine is chosen or therapy is not enough, an SSRI (e.g. escitalopram or sertraline) or an SNRI is the first-line medicine. Benzodiazepines are not recommended for ongoing treatment because of dependence, cognitive effects and interaction with her nightly alcohol use. Her drinking should be discussed as part of the plan.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
GAD: offer CBT first (face to face or internet-delivered, e.g. MindSpot). If medicine is needed, use an SSRI or SNRI. Avoid ongoing benzodiazepines.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "Andrews G et al. RANZCP clinical practice guidelines for the treatment of panic disorder, social anxiety disorder and generalised anxiety disorder. Aust N Z J Psychiatry 2018", "url": "https://doi.org/10.1177/0004867418799453"}
• {"name": "MindSpot (free national digital mental health service, Macquarie University)", "url": "https://mindspot.org.au/"}

---

EDITED BY HEISENBERG','moderate','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "bank170", "review_status": "owner_approved_2026_09_29", "tags": ["ZQ-0217", "zyntra_bank", "mock1", "level_3", "task_management", "group_mental_health", "sys_anxiety", "approved_by_owner_2026_09_29"], "clinician_task": "management", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"Andrews G et al. RANZCP clinical practice guidelines for the treatment of panic disorder, social anxiety disorder and generalised anxiety disorder. Aust N Z J Psychiatry 2018\", \"url\": \"https://doi.org/10.1177/0004867418799453\"}; {\"name\": \"MindSpot (free national digital mental health service, Macquarie University)\", \"url\": \"https://mindspot.org.au/\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "Regular benzodiazepines are not recommended for GAD because of tolerance, dependence and withdrawal risk, and they are especially risky with daily alcohol.", "B": "Quetiapine has some evidence in GAD but causes metabolic adverse effects and sedation. It is not a first-line option in primary care.", "D": "Beta-blockers may reduce tremor or palpitations in performance anxiety but do not treat the worry of GAD.", "E": "Pregabalin is a second-line option in some guidelines; it has misuse potential and is not first line ahead of CBT or an SSRI/SNRI."}, "key_takeaways": ["GAD: offer CBT first (face to face or internet-delivered, e.g. MindSpot). If medicine is needed, use an SSRI or SNRI. Avoid ongoing benzodiazepines."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,0.0,1.0,'tier_prior','tier=3 (bank170)'),
('26eea36c-4c8f-5040-825d-db2ecb6f4400'::uuid,'ZQ-0218','mental-health','psychosis','A 22-year-old university student is brought to the emergency department of a tertiary hospital in Brisbane, Queensland, by her flatmates. Two weeks ago she had a flu-like illness with headache. Over the past 10 days she became anxious, stopped sleeping, and then began saying that her flatmates were ''recording her thoughts''. She has been hearing voices commenting on her actions. She has no previous psychiatric history, does not use drugs according to her flatmates, and her urine drug screen is negative.

In the department she is disorientated, with periods of staring and reduced responsiveness alternating with agitation. She makes repetitive chewing and lip-smacking movements, and her left arm occasionally takes up an abnormal posture. Her temperature is 37.9 °C, heart rate varies between 58 and 132/min and blood pressure between 104/60 and 168/98 mmHg. There is no neck stiffness or rash. Twenty minutes after arrival she has a generalised tonic-clonic seizure lasting 90 seconds.

Full blood count, electrolytes, glucose, calcium, liver function, thyroid function and a CT scan of the brain are normal.

Which one of the following is the most appropriate next step in establishing the diagnosis?','["Admit to the mental health unit and start oral olanzapine, reviewing in 1 week", "Serum prolactin level to confirm that the seizure was epileptic", "Repeat urine drug screen with an extended synthetic cannabinoid panel", "HIV and syphilis serology, then outpatient neurology review", "MRI brain, EEG, and lumbar puncture with CSF and serum NMDA receptor antibodies"]'::jsonb,'E','CORRECT ANSWER

E. MRI brain, EEG, and lumbar puncture with CSF and serum NMDA receptor antibodies

---

A young woman with a viral-like prodrome followed by rapidly progressive psychiatric symptoms, then reduced consciousness, orofacial dyskinesias, abnormal postures, autonomic instability and a seizure, has probable autoimmune encephalitis. Anti-NMDA receptor encephalitis is the most common type in this group. This is not a primary psychotic disorder. Seizures, dyskinesias, autonomic instability and fluctuating consciousness are ''red flags'' for an organic cause of first-episode psychosis. The work-up is MRI brain (often normal or showing medial temporal changes), EEG (slowing, epileptiform activity or ''extreme delta brush''), and lumbar puncture for CSF cell count, protein, oligoclonal bands, HSV PCR (with empirical aciclovir until HSV encephalitis is excluded) and CSF NMDAR antibodies (more sensitive than serum). Screen for an ovarian teratoma (pelvic ultrasound/MRI). Early immunotherapy (steroids, IVIG or plasma exchange) and tumour removal improve outcomes. Antipsychotics are poorly tolerated in these patients and may worsen dyskinesias or precipitate an NMS-like state.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
First-episode psychosis with seizures, dyskinesias, autonomic instability or fluctuating consciousness needs an encephalitis work-up (MRI, EEG, LP with CSF NMDAR antibodies, HSV PCR) and a teratoma screen, not antipsychotics alone.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "Graus F et al. A clinical approach to diagnosis of autoimmune encephalitis. Lancet Neurol 2016;15:391\u2013404", "url": "https://doi.org/10.1016/S1474-4422(15)00401-9"}

---

EDITED BY HEISENBERG','difficult','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "bank170", "review_status": "owner_approved_2026_09_29", "tags": ["ZQ-0218", "zyntra_bank", "mock1", "level_5", "task_data_gathering", "group_mental_health", "sys_psychosis", "approved_by_owner_2026_09_29"], "clinician_task": "data_gathering", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"Graus F et al. A clinical approach to diagnosis of autoimmune encephalitis. Lancet Neurol 2016;15:391\\u2013404\", \"url\": \"https://doi.org/10.1016/S1474-4422(15)00401-9\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "Treating this as primary psychosis would miss a treatable encephalitis. These patients are prone to severe antipsychotic adverse effects.", "B": "Prolactin testing after a seizure has limited value and does not address the underlying cause.", "C": "A negative screen with the neurological signs and autonomic features makes drug-induced psychosis less likely. An extended panel would not explain a seizure with dyskinesia and fluctuating consciousness here.", "D": "HIV and syphilis testing is reasonable in first-episode psychosis, but outpatient review is unsafe for a patient with encephalopathy and seizures."}, "key_takeaways": ["First-episode psychosis with seizures, dyskinesias, autonomic instability or fluctuating consciousness needs an encephalitis work-up (MRI, EEG, LP with CSF NMDAR antibodies, HSV PCR) and a teratoma screen, not antipsychotics alone."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,2.0,1.0,'tier_prior','tier=5 (bank170)'),
('e2ca5145-9315-514b-a7c7-bfbf2c9b6bf0'::uuid,'ZQ-0219','mental-health','psychosis','A 24-year-old man is an inpatient in the acute mental health unit of a hospital in Newcastle, New South Wales, with first-episode schizophrenia. Aripiprazole was started 10 days ago and increased to 15 mg daily 4 days ago. His auditory hallucinations are becoming less intrusive.

Over the past 3 days nursing staff report that he paces the corridor for hours, cannot sit through meals or groups and repeatedly shifts his weight from foot to foot when standing. He says he feels ''like I''m going to jump out of my skin'' and that ''my legs won''t stop'', and he became tearful and said, ''I can''t live like this.'' The night nurse has asked for PRN olanzapine ''for agitation''.

His temperature is 36.8 °C, heart rate 88/min and blood pressure 124/78 mmHg. There is no rigidity, tremor or abnormal posturing, and his conscious state is normal. He is not responding to hallucinations on the ward. Creatine kinase is 140 U/L (<200).

Which one of the following is the most likely explanation for his current symptoms?','["Antipsychotic-induced akathisia", "Worsening psychotic agitation", "Neuroleptic malignant syndrome", "Acute dystonic reaction", "Tardive dyskinesia"]'::jsonb,'A','CORRECT ANSWER

A. Antipsychotic-induced akathisia

---

Akathisia is a subjective sense of inner restlessness with objective motor restlessness (pacing, rocking, shifting weight, inability to sit). It usually appears days to weeks after starting or increasing an antipsychotic. Aripiprazole is a common cause despite its otherwise favourable side-effect profile. It is often mistaken for psychotic agitation or anxiety, which leads to more antipsychotic and worse symptoms. It is distressing and associated with suicidal thoughts and impulsivity, so his comment needs a risk assessment. Management: reduce the dose or switch to an antipsychotic less likely to cause akathisia, and consider propranolol (low dose, titrated) or a short benzodiazepine course, while avoiding extra antipsychotic PRNs.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Inner restlessness, pacing and inability to sit days after starting or increasing an antipsychotic is akathisia, not agitation. Reduce or switch the drug, consider propranolol, and assess suicide risk.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "Australian Family Physician (RACGP), May 2017. Beyond anxiety and agitation: a clinical approach to akathisia", "url": "https://www.racgp.org.au/afp/2017/may/beyond-anxiety-and-agitation-a-clinical-approach-t"}

---

EDITED BY HEISENBERG','difficult','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "bank170", "review_status": "owner_approved_2026_09_29", "tags": ["ZQ-0219", "zyntra_bank", "mock1", "level_4", "task_data_interpretation_synthesis", "group_mental_health", "sys_psychosis", "approved_by_owner_2026_09_29"], "clinician_task": "data_interpretation_synthesis", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"Australian Family Physician (RACGP), May 2017. Beyond anxiety and agitation: a clinical approach to akathisia\", \"url\": \"https://www.racgp.org.au/afp/2017/may/beyond-anxiety-and-agitation-a-clinical-approach-t\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"B": "His psychotic symptoms are improving, and his restlessness is focused on the legs and inner tension, not on psychotic experiences. Giving more antipsychotic would likely worsen akathisia.", "C": "NMS causes fever, rigidity, altered consciousness, autonomic instability and a raised CK. None of these are present.", "D": "Acute dystonia is a sustained involuntary muscle contraction (e.g. torticollis, oculogyric crisis), usually within the first days, not motor restlessness.", "E": "Tardive dyskinesia appears after months to years of treatment, with involuntary choreiform or orofacial movements. The patient is often unaware of it and does not feel restless."}, "key_takeaways": ["Inner restlessness, pacing and inability to sit days after starting or increasing an antipsychotic is akathisia, not agitation. Reduce or switch the drug, consider propranolol, and assess suicide risk."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,1.0,1.0,'tier_prior','tier=4 (bank170)'),
('a61228b8-b89f-5b21-96f2-008fad43ad03'::uuid,'ZQ-0220','mental-health','mood-disorders','A 52-year-old sheep farmer from near Horsham in western Victoria sees a general practitioner at the local clinic because his wife made the appointment for ''his sleep''. After three years of drought he has sold most of his flock, and his bank has refused to extend his loan. His wife has recently moved to their daughter''s house in Ballarat.

He says he has been waking at 3 am, has lost 6 kg, has stopped going to the football club and is drinking four to six beers most nights. He says he feels ''useless'' and adds, ''Everyone would be better off without me around; the insurance would at least clear the debt.'' He denies having made any attempt. He has no previous psychiatric history. He is calm and makes limited eye contact, and there is no evidence of psychosis.

He agrees to keep talking but says he needs to be home before dark to feed the dogs.

Which one of the following is the most important question to ask him next?','["Whether anyone in his family has a history of depression", "How many hours of sleep he is getting each night", "Whether he has thought about how he would end his life and has access to firearms", "His score on a structured suicide risk scale such as SAD PERSONS", "Whether he would accept a referral to a psychologist in the next month"]'::jsonb,'C','CORRECT ANSWER

C. Whether he has thought about how he would end his life and has access to firearms

---

He has disclosed suicidal thinking with a possible motive (insurance), multiple risk factors (male, middle-aged, rural, financial loss, relationship separation, social withdrawal, increasing alcohol use, depressive symptoms) and likely access to lethal means: firearms are common on farms and are a leading method of suicide in rural men. The priority is to ask directly and specifically about suicidal intent, plans, preparations and access to means, especially firearms. Asking about suicide does not increase risk. This leads to a collaborative safety plan: means restriction (for example firearms stored off-site with a trusted person or the police, with his agreement), reducing alcohol, involving supports with consent, and a same-day or next-day review or urgent mental health triage depending on intent. The RANZCP recommends against using risk-scoring tools to classify people as low or high risk; assessment should be a comprehensive, needs-based clinical formulation.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
When someone discloses suicidal thoughts, ask directly about intent, plans, preparations and access to means (especially firearms in rural men), then make a collaborative safety plan. Do not rely on risk-scoring tools.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "RACGP. Guidelines for preventive activities in general practice (Red Book): Suicide", "url": "https://www.racgp.org.au/clinical-resources/clinical-guidelines/key-racgp-guidelines/view-all-racgp-guidelines/preventive-activities-in-general-practice/mental-health/suicide"}
• {"name": "RANZCP. Suicide prevention \u2013 the role of psychiatry (position statement)", "url": "https://www.ranzcp.org/clinical-guidelines-publications/clinical-guidelines-publications-library/suicide-prevention-the-role-of-psychiatry"}

---

EDITED BY HEISENBERG','difficult','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "bank170", "review_status": "owner_approved_2026_09_29", "tags": ["ZQ-0220", "zyntra_bank", "mock1", "level_4", "task_data_gathering", "group_mental_health", "sys_mood", "approved_by_owner_2026_09_29"], "clinician_task": "data_gathering", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"RACGP. Guidelines for preventive activities in general practice (Red Book): Suicide\", \"url\": \"https://www.racgp.org.au/clinical-resources/clinical-guidelines/key-racgp-guidelines/view-all-racgp-guidelines/preventive-activities-in-general-practice/mental-health/suicide\"}; {\"name\": \"RANZCP. Suicide prevention \\u2013 the role of psychiatry (position statement)\", \"url\": \"https://www.ranzcp.org/clinical-guidelines-publications/clinical-guidelines-publications-library/suicide-prevention-the-role-of-psychiatry\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "Family history is a relevant background factor, but it is less urgent than establishing current intent, plans and means after he has expressed suicidal thoughts.", "B": "Sleep is part of the depression assessment but does not address immediate safety.", "D": "Risk scales such as SAD PERSONS have poor predictive value and are not recommended for deciding care. They should not replace a direct inquiry about intent, plans and means.", "E": "Referral is part of management, but a routine referral within a month does not address immediate risk. Safety assessment and planning come first."}, "key_takeaways": ["When someone discloses suicidal thoughts, ask directly about intent, plans, preparations and access to means (especially firearms in rural men), then make a collaborative safety plan. Do not rely on risk-scoring tools."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,1.0,1.0,'tier_prior','tier=4 (bank170)'),
('ad164e4d-b957-5828-917c-dfb85692b3e6'::uuid,'ZQ-0221','mental-health','anxiety-disorders','A 15-year-old girl is brought by her parents to the emergency department of a children''s hospital in Perth, Western Australia, after she fainted at school. Over the past 8 months she has lost 11 kg (2 kg in the last 2 weeks) by restricting food and running for 2 hours a day. Her periods stopped 5 months ago. She says she ''feels fat'' and is frightened of gaining weight. She denies vomiting or laxative use.

Her weight is 38 kg and height 163 cm (BMI 14.3 kg/m², about 70% of the median BMI for her age and sex). Her tympanic temperature is 35.4 °C. Heart rate is 44/min lying (awake) and 98/min standing. Blood pressure is 86/52 mmHg lying and 64/40 mmHg standing. Her hands and feet are cold, with lanugo hair on her arms.

Investigations:
- Sodium 136 mmol/L (135–145)
- Potassium 3.4 mmol/L (3.5–5.0)
- Phosphate 0.85 mmol/L (1.0–1.8)
- Magnesium 0.72 mmol/L (0.7–1.0)
- Glucose 3.6 mmol/L
- ECG: sinus bradycardia 44/min, QTc 440 ms

Which one of the following is the most appropriate management?','["Discharge with urgent family-based treatment in the community and weekly GP weighing", "Admit to a paediatric medical ward for monitoring and supervised nutritional rehabilitation", "Admit to an adult mental health unit under the Mental Health Act for psychiatric treatment", "Start olanzapine 2.5 mg daily to reduce her fear of weight gain before any refeeding", "Give a 10 mL/kg bolus of 10% glucose and then discharge her once the heart rate is above 50"]'::jsonb,'B','CORRECT ANSWER

B. Admit to a paediatric medical ward for monitoring and supervised nutritional rehabilitation

---

She meets several criteria for medical instability that require admission in the RCH emergency department eating disorder guideline: awake heart rate below 50/min, postural heart rate rise above 30–50/min (here 54), systolic BP below 80 (standing) and a postural drop above 20 mmHg, temperature below 35.5 °C, BMI about 70% of median, rapid weight loss (more than 1 kg/week), low phosphate and syncope. She needs admission to a paediatric medical bed (with mental health and dietetic input) for cardiac monitoring, electrolyte monitoring and a structured refeeding plan. Nutrition is started early with close monitoring for refeeding syndrome: phosphate, potassium and magnesium are checked and replaced, and thiamine is given. Family-based treatment remains the main evidence-based therapy once she is medically stable.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Adolescent anorexia nervosa with awake HR <50, postural instability, hypothermia, low phosphate, syncope or rapid weight loss needs admission to a paediatric medical bed for monitoring and supervised refeeding (with phosphate and thiamine).

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "The Royal Children''s Hospital Melbourne. Clinical Practice Guideline: Management of eating disorders in the emergency department (Oct 2025)", "url": "https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/Management_of_Eating_Disorders_in_the_Emergency_Department/"}

---

EDITED BY HEISENBERG','difficult','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "bank170", "review_status": "owner_approved_2026_09_29", "tags": ["ZQ-0221", "zyntra_bank", "mock1", "level_4", "task_management", "group_mental_health", "sys_anxiety", "approved_by_owner_2026_09_29"], "clinician_task": "management", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"The Royal Children''s Hospital Melbourne. Clinical Practice Guideline: Management of eating disorders in the emergency department (Oct 2025)\", \"url\": \"https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/Management_of_Eating_Disorders_in_the_Emergency_Department/\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "Outpatient care is not safe while she meets medical instability criteria (bradycardia, hypotension, hypothermia, postural changes, syncope).", "C": "Her immediate risk is medical. An adult mental health unit is inappropriate for a 15-year-old and cannot provide cardiac monitoring and refeeding; mental health legislation is considered only if she refuses life-saving treatment and other options fail.", "D": "Medicines have a limited role in anorexia nervosa, and delaying nutrition while waiting for a medicine is unsafe. Olanzapine is a specialist adjunct once she is medically stable.", "E": "Hypertonic glucose boluses risk refeeding complications and do not treat the underlying instability. Discharging her is unsafe."}, "key_takeaways": ["Adolescent anorexia nervosa with awake HR <50, postural instability, hypothermia, low phosphate, syncope or rapid weight loss needs admission to a paediatric medical bed for monitoring and supervised refeeding (with phosphate and thiamine)."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,1.0,1.0,'tier_prior','tier=4 (bank170)'),
('8b189c25-bf22-589f-bb25-b31c2d403d27'::uuid,'ZQ-0222','mental-health','substance-use','A 34-year-old man attends a community drug and alcohol clinic in Parramatta, New South Wales, asking to start opioid treatment. He has injected heroin daily for 3 years, about 0.5 g a day, and last injected 4 hours ago, before catching the train to the clinic. He has not used methadone or other opioids in the past month. He also drinks 2–3 standard drinks a day and uses cannabis occasionally. He wants to start buprenorphine today because he ''can''t keep doing this''.

He is alert and cooperative, with a heart rate of 84/min and blood pressure 126/80 mmHg. His pupils are 3 mm. There is no yawning, sweating, rhinorrhoea, piloerection or tremor, and he feels ''okay for now''. There are healed injection sites on both forearms. The nurse practitioner has prepared a sublingual buprenorphine-naloxone 8 mg/2 mg film.

Before the first dose of buprenorphine is given, which one of the following assessments is most important?','["A urine drug screen confirming opioid use", "Liver function tests and hepatitis C serology", "A 12-lead ECG to measure the corrected QT interval", "Objective withdrawal severity using a scale such as COWS", "A breath alcohol reading below 0.05%"]'::jsonb,'D','CORRECT ANSWER

D. Objective withdrawal severity using a scale such as COWS

---

Buprenorphine is a high-affinity partial agonist. If it is given while a full agonist such as heroin still occupies the receptors, it displaces the heroin and causes precipitated withdrawal, which is severe and discourages patients from continuing treatment. NSW guidelines advise waiting until the patient has objective signs of at least moderate withdrawal before the first dose, measured with a validated scale such as the Clinical Opiate Withdrawal Scale (COWS), commonly a score of 8 to 12 or more. This is typically at least 8–12 hours after short-acting opioids such as heroin (longer after methadone). He used 4 hours ago and has no withdrawal signs, so dosing now risks precipitated withdrawal. He can wait in the clinic or return later, or a low-dose (''micro-dosing'') induction can be considered under specialist guidance. Once withdrawal is confirmed, a typical first dose is 2–4 mg, then further doses are titrated.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Start buprenorphine only when objective opioid withdrawal is at least moderate (e.g. COWS ≥8–12; usually ≥8–12 h after heroin). Dosing too early causes precipitated withdrawal.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "NSW Health. NSW Clinical Guidelines: Treatment of Opioid Dependence (GL2018_018)", "url": "https://www1.health.nsw.gov.au/pds/ActivePDSDocuments/GL2018_018.pdf"}

---

EDITED BY HEISENBERG','difficult','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "bank170", "review_status": "owner_approved_2026_09_29", "tags": ["ZQ-0222", "zyntra_bank", "mock1", "level_4", "task_data_gathering", "group_mental_health", "sys_substance_use", "approved_by_owner_2026_09_29"], "clinician_task": "data_gathering", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"NSW Health. NSW Clinical Guidelines: Treatment of Opioid Dependence (GL2018_018)\", \"url\": \"https://www1.health.nsw.gov.au/pds/ActivePDSDocuments/GL2018_018.pdf\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "A urine screen supports the history but does not tell you whether it is safe to dose now; heroin metabolites stay positive for days. Treatment should not be delayed waiting for results.", "B": "Baseline LFTs and blood-borne virus screening are part of comprehensive assessment but do not determine the timing of the first dose.", "C": "ECG monitoring for QT prolongation matters more for methadone; buprenorphine has minimal QT effect.", "E": "Intoxication with alcohol or sedatives is a reason to delay dosing, but his reported intake is modest and he is not intoxicated. The key issue is opioid withdrawal status."}, "key_takeaways": ["Start buprenorphine only when objective opioid withdrawal is at least moderate (e.g. COWS ≥8–12; usually ≥8–12 h after heroin). Dosing too early causes precipitated withdrawal."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,1.0,1.0,'tier_prior','tier=4 (bank170)'),
('15873942-a015-5a13-8209-873f24cce5c1'::uuid,'ZQ-0223','mental-health','substance-use','A 48-year-old man is brought to the emergency department of a regional hospital in Mildura, Victoria, by a friend who found him confused at home. He has drunk about 1.5 litres of wine a day for years and has eaten very little for the past month. His last drink was the previous evening.

He is disorientated to time and place and cannot recall why he is in hospital, but he is not hallucinating and not agitated. His temperature is 36.6 °C, heart rate 92/min and blood pressure 128/80 mmHg. He is not sweating, and there is no tremor. He has horizontal nystagmus and cannot fully abduct his right eye. When he tries to walk he has a wide-based, unsteady gait and needs support. There is no asterixis, jaundice or ascites. He has reduced vibration sense in both feet.

Investigations:
- Capillary glucose 5.4 mmol/L
- Sodium 136 mmol/L (135–145)
- Bilirubin 18 µmol/L (<20)
- ALT 64 U/L (<40)
- GGT 280 U/L (<60)
- Albumin 34 g/L (35–50)
- INR 1.1
- CT brain: mild generalised atrophy, no bleed

Which one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?','["Korsakoff syndrome", "Delirium tremens", "Hepatic encephalopathy", "Alcoholic cerebellar degeneration", "Wernicke encephalopathy"]'::jsonb,'E','CORRECT ANSWER

E. Wernicke encephalopathy

---

He has acute confusion, ophthalmoplegia/nystagmus and ataxia in the setting of long-term heavy drinking and poor nutrition. This is Wernicke encephalopathy from thiamine deficiency. The full triad is present in only a minority, so any one feature in an at-risk person should prompt treatment. Treatment is urgent high-dose parenteral thiamine (Australian alcohol guidelines recommend doses such as 300–500 mg IV three times daily for suspected WE), given before or with glucose-containing fluids but without delaying treatment of hypoglycaemia. Magnesium is corrected too. Without adequate treatment it can progress to Korsakoff syndrome (permanent amnesia with confabulation).

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Confusion, ophthalmoplegia/nystagmus or ataxia in a person with alcohol dependence or malnutrition is Wernicke encephalopathy until proven otherwise: give high-dose parenteral thiamine immediately.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "Australian Government Department of Health. Quick reference guide to the treatment of alcohol problems (companion to the 2021 Guidelines for the Treatment of Alcohol Problems)", "url": "https://www.health.gov.au/sites/default/files/quick-reference-guide-to-the-treatment-of-alcohol-problems_0.pdf"}

---

EDITED BY HEISENBERG','moderate','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "bank170", "review_status": "owner_approved_2026_09_29", "tags": ["ZQ-0223", "zyntra_bank", "mock1", "level_3", "task_data_interpretation_synthesis", "group_mental_health", "sys_substance_use", "approved_by_owner_2026_09_29"], "clinician_task": "data_interpretation_synthesis", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"Australian Government Department of Health. Quick reference guide to the treatment of alcohol problems (companion to the 2021 Guidelines for the Treatment of Alcohol Problems)\", \"url\": \"https://www.health.gov.au/sites/default/files/quick-reference-guide-to-the-treatment-of-alcohol-problems_0.pdf\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "Korsakoff syndrome is a chronic amnestic state with confabulation and a relatively clear sensorium, usually after WE. It does not explain the acute eye signs and ataxia.", "B": "Delirium tremens occurs 48–72 hours after the last drink, with agitation, hallucinations, tremor, fever and autonomic hyperactivity. He last drank the previous evening and is calm, afebrile and not tremulous.", "C": "Hepatic encephalopathy causes confusion with asterixis, usually with signs of liver failure (jaundice, ascites, raised INR). His liver synthetic function is preserved.", "D": "Alcoholic cerebellar degeneration causes chronic truncal and gait ataxia but not acute confusion or ophthalmoplegia."}, "key_takeaways": ["Confusion, ophthalmoplegia/nystagmus or ataxia in a person with alcohol dependence or malnutrition is Wernicke encephalopathy until proven otherwise: give high-dose parenteral thiamine immediately."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,0.0,1.0,'tier_prior','tier=3 (bank170)'),
('9c91de7f-b045-5448-b0b5-018c3bf02229'::uuid,'ZQ-0224','mental-health','mood-disorders','A 31-year-old woman brings her 3-week-old son to a general practice in Toowoomba, Queensland, for a weight check. She had an emergency caesarean section after a long labour, and breastfeeding has been difficult. Her partner went back to work, driving trucks, a week after the birth. As part of routine postnatal care, the practice nurse asked her to complete the Edinburgh Postnatal Depression Scale in the waiting room.

Her total EPDS score is 11. For question 10 (''The thought of harming myself has occurred to me'') she ticked ''Sometimes''. In the consultation she is tired but engages well and smiles at her baby. She says she is ''just exhausted, like every new mum''. The baby is gaining weight appropriately.

Which one of the following is the most appropriate next step?','["Ask her directly about thoughts of self-harm or suicide and assess her safety today", "Repeat the EPDS in 2–4 weeks, because her total score is below 13", "Reassure her that this is likely to be the ''baby blues'' and review at the 6-week check", "Start sertraline 50 mg daily, because it is compatible with breastfeeding", "Give her a referral to a perinatal psychologist with a routine appointment"]'::jsonb,'A','CORRECT ANSWER

A. Ask her directly about thoughts of self-harm or suicide and assess her safety today

---

Any positive response to EPDS item 10 (thoughts of self-harm) needs an immediate, direct assessment of risk, regardless of the total score. The COPE Perinatal Mental Health Guideline (2023) uses a total of 13 or more to prompt further assessment and 10–12 for monitoring and repeat screening, but item 10 is the exception. The GP should ask about the nature, frequency and intent of the thoughts, any plans, thoughts of harm to the baby, psychotic symptoms and supports, then make a collaborative safety plan and arrange referral (e.g. perinatal mental health service, or urgent mental health triage if risk is high). The ''baby blues'' peak at days 3–5 and settle by about 2 weeks. Symptoms continuing at 3 weeks, with self-harm thoughts, need assessment for perinatal depression.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
EPDS: ≥13 = further assessment, 10–12 = monitor and repeat, BUT any positive answer to item 10 needs an immediate direct risk assessment regardless of the total.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "COPE (Centre of Perinatal Excellence). Mental Health Care in the Perinatal Period: Australian Clinical Practice Guideline (2023)", "url": "https://www.cope.org.au/uploads/images/Health-professionals/COPE_2023_Perinatal_Mental_Health_Practice_Guideline.pdf"}

---

EDITED BY HEISENBERG','difficult','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "bank170", "review_status": "owner_approved_2026_09_29", "tags": ["ZQ-0224", "zyntra_bank", "mock1", "level_4", "task_data_gathering", "group_mental_health", "sys_mood", "approved_by_owner_2026_09_29"], "clinician_task": "data_gathering", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"COPE (Centre of Perinatal Excellence). Mental Health Care in the Perinatal Period: Australian Clinical Practice Guideline (2023)\", \"url\": \"https://www.cope.org.au/uploads/images/Health-professionals/COPE_2023_Perinatal_Mental_Health_Practice_Guideline.pdf\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"B": "Repeat screening applies to a score of 10–12 WITHOUT a positive item 10. A positive item 10 needs same-day assessment.", "C": "Baby blues settle within about 2 weeks and do not include thoughts of self-harm. Waiting 3 weeks risks missing serious illness.", "D": "Sertraline is compatible with breastfeeding and may be appropriate after assessment, but medicine is not started before the risk and diagnosis are assessed.", "E": "Referral may be appropriate, but a routine appointment does not address the possible immediate risk, which must be assessed first."}, "key_takeaways": ["EPDS: ≥13 = further assessment, 10–12 = monitor and repeat, BUT any positive answer to item 10 needs an immediate direct risk assessment regardless of the total."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,1.0,1.0,'tier_prior','tier=4 (bank170)'),
('ecfef364-c22b-5d4b-a62a-41a6d5df1b96'::uuid,'ZQ-0225','mental-health','mood-disorders','A 16-year-old boy is reviewed by his general practitioner in Wollongong, New South Wales, with his consent and his mother present for part of the visit. For 4 months he has had low mood, irritability, loss of interest in basketball and gaming, poor sleep, falling school grades and social withdrawal.

Over the past 10 weeks he has attended eight sessions of CBT at a headspace centre and has engaged well. His sleep has improved slightly, but he still feels ''flat most days'', has stopped seeing friends and scores 18 on the PHQ-A. He has passive thoughts that ''it wouldn''t matter if I didn''t wake up'', but no plans or intent and no self-harm. He does not use alcohol or other drugs. There is no personal or family history of bipolar disorder. He and his mother would like to discuss medicine, and his psychologist agrees.

Which one of the following is the most appropriate management?','["Stop CBT and start venlafaxine, because a dual-action drug will work faster", "Continue CBT and start sertraline 100 mg daily, reviewing in 8 weeks", "Continue CBT and add fluoxetine 10 mg daily, with weekly review early on", "Continue CBT and add paroxetine 10 mg daily, with monthly review", "Continue CBT alone for another 6 months before considering any medicine"]'::jsonb,'C','CORRECT ANSWER

C. Continue CBT and add fluoxetine 10 mg daily, with weekly review early on

---

He has moderate-to-severe depression with only a partial response to an adequate trial of psychological therapy. Adding an antidepressant alongside continued therapy is appropriate. In young people, fluoxetine has the best evidence of benefit and is the first-choice antidepressant (its use under 18 years is off-label in Australia). It is started at a low dose (e.g. 10 mg, increasing to 20 mg after 1–2 weeks if tolerated). Because antidepressants can increase suicidal thinking and agitation in people under 25, especially in the first weeks, close monitoring (about weekly for the first 4 weeks), a safety plan, and involving family with his consent are recommended. Psychological therapy continues.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Adolescent depression: psychological therapy first. If moderate-to-severe or only partly responding, add fluoxetine (off-label, start low) with weekly monitoring for the first 4 weeks for suicidality. Avoid paroxetine and venlafaxine.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "Australian Prescriber. Antidepressants in adolescence", "url": "https://australianprescriber.tg.org.au/articles/antidepressants-in-adolescence.html"}
• {"name": "RANZCP. Clinical practice guidelines for mood disorders (Malhi et al., 2020)", "url": "https://www.ranzcp.org/getmedia/a8c79505-c0b5-46b4-9f27-67f79e047674/Mood-disorders-clinical-practice-guideline-2020.pdf"}

---

EDITED BY HEISENBERG','difficult','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "bank170", "review_status": "owner_approved_2026_09_29", "tags": ["ZQ-0225", "zyntra_bank", "mock1", "level_4", "task_management", "group_mental_health", "sys_mood", "approved_by_owner_2026_09_29"], "clinician_task": "management", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"Australian Prescriber. Antidepressants in adolescence\", \"url\": \"https://australianprescriber.tg.org.au/articles/antidepressants-in-adolescence.html\"}; {\"name\": \"RANZCP. Clinical practice guidelines for mood disorders (Malhi et al., 2020)\", \"url\": \"https://www.ranzcp.org/getmedia/a8c79505-c0b5-46b4-9f27-67f79e047674/Mood-disorders-clinical-practice-guideline-2020.pdf\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "Venlafaxine is not recommended in young people because of a higher risk of suicidal ideation and no evidence of efficacy. Stopping effective engagement in therapy is also unhelpful.", "B": "Sertraline (or escitalopram) is a second-line alternative if fluoxetine is unsuitable. Starting at 100 mg and waiting 8 weeks to review is too high and too slow.", "D": "Paroxetine is not recommended in adolescents (poor efficacy and higher suicidality risk), and monthly review is not close enough early on.", "E": "With moderate-to-severe depression and only partial response after an adequate CBT trial, waiting 6 more months prolongs illness and risk."}, "key_takeaways": ["Adolescent depression: psychological therapy first. If moderate-to-severe or only partly responding, add fluoxetine (off-label, start low) with weekly monitoring for the first 4 weeks for suicidality. Avoid paroxetine and venlafaxine."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,1.0,1.0,'tier_prior','tier=4 (bank170)'),
('a2717db1-6368-52cb-b560-111f10aff3ba'::uuid,'ZQ-0226','mental-health','psychosis','An 83-year-old woman living in a residential aged care facility in Toowoomba, Queensland, is reviewed by her general practitioner because staff say she has been ''not herself'' for 3 days. She has mild Alzheimer disease (MMSE 24/30 six months ago), osteoarthritis and hypertension. Five days ago she was started on trimethoprim for a urinary tract infection and on oxycodone 5 mg twice daily for knee pain.

Staff report that she is staying in bed, eating little, dozing through meals and has stopped joining activities. Her daughter thinks she is ''depressed because her friend in the next room died last month''. When examined she is drowsy but rousable, gives short answers and drifts off mid-sentence. She cannot say the months of the year backwards and is unsure where she is. Staff say she was ''much brighter'' yesterday afternoon. Her temperature is 37.4 °C, heart rate 90/min, blood pressure 118/64 mmHg and oxygen saturation 95% on room air. There are no focal neurological signs, and her bladder is not palpable. Her 4AT score is 7.

Which one of the following is the most likely explanation for her change?','["Major depressive episode triggered by bereavement", "Progression of her Alzheimer disease", "Dementia with Lewy bodies", "Hypoactive delirium", "Late-onset schizophrenia-like psychosis"]'::jsonb,'D','CORRECT ANSWER

D. Hypoactive delirium

---

She has an acute onset (days) of fluctuating inattention (cannot do the months backwards, drifts off mid-sentence), altered alertness and disorientation, with likely precipitants: infection, a new opioid and underlying dementia. This is hypoactive delirium. A 4AT score of 4 or more suggests delirium. The hypoactive form is the most commonly missed and is often mistaken for depression or ''tiredness'', and it has a worse prognosis. The ACSQHC Delirium Clinical Care Standard asks for early identification using a validated tool, a search for and treatment of causes (here: review the oxycodone, check bowel and bladder function, reassess the infection, bloods), non-drug strategies, and involving the family. Antipsychotics are not first line.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
An acute, fluctuating change in attention and alertness in an older person, especially with new drugs or infection, is delirium (often hypoactive). Screen with the 4AT and find and treat the cause.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "Australian Commission on Safety and Quality in Health Care. Delirium Clinical Care Standard (2021)", "url": "https://www.safetyandquality.gov.au/standards/clinical-care-standards/delirium-clinical-care-standard"}

---

EDITED BY HEISENBERG','difficult','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "bank170", "review_status": "owner_approved_2026_09_29", "tags": ["ZQ-0226", "zyntra_bank", "mock1", "level_4", "task_data_interpretation_synthesis", "group_mental_health", "sys_psychosis", "approved_by_owner_2026_09_29"], "clinician_task": "data_interpretation_synthesis", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"Australian Commission on Safety and Quality in Health Care. Delirium Clinical Care Standard (2021)\", \"url\": \"https://www.safetyandquality.gov.au/standards/clinical-care-standards/delirium-clinical-care-standard\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "Depression develops over weeks, with low mood; it does not usually cause fluctuating attention and drowsiness. Bereavement is a distractor here.", "B": "Alzheimer disease progresses gradually over months to years. A change over 3 days with fluctuating alertness suggests delirium superimposed on dementia.", "C": "DLB causes fluctuating cognition, but over a longer course with visual hallucinations, parkinsonism and REM sleep behaviour disorder. This acute change after new precipitants is delirium.", "E": "There are no hallucinations or delusions, and the presentation is acute with impaired attention and alertness, which points to delirium."}, "key_takeaways": ["An acute, fluctuating change in attention and alertness in an older person, especially with new drugs or infection, is delirium (often hypoactive). Screen with the 4AT and find and treat the cause."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,1.0,1.0,'tier_prior','tier=4 (bank170)'),
('820e7ee5-e564-563d-bf1d-1d656a372dbb'::uuid,'ZQ-0227','mental-health','psychosis','A 74-year-old retired electrician was admitted to a hospital in Launceston, Tasmania, 3 days ago with a urinary tract infection. On the first night he was frightened and trying to leave, saying there were ''children standing at the end of the bed''. He was given haloperidol 1 mg orally twice.

The next day he became very stiff, fell twice and was much more confused and drowsy. He is afebrile, and his creatine kinase is normal. The UTI has been treated.

His wife says that for about a year before admission his memory and concentration have ''come and gone'': some days he is almost normal, and on others he stares into space for long periods. He often sees small children or animals in the house and is not frightened by them. For several years he has shouted and punched out in his sleep, ''acting out his dreams''. In the past 6 months he has become slower and shuffles when he walks. On examination (off haloperidol for 48 hours) he has symmetrical bradykinesia and mild rigidity, and no tremor. His MoCA is 17/30, with marked impairment of attention and visuospatial function and relatively preserved recall.

Which one of the following is the most likely underlying diagnosis?','["Alzheimer disease", "Dementia with Lewy bodies", "Parkinson disease dementia", "Vascular dementia", "Behavioural-variant frontotemporal dementia"]'::jsonb,'B','CORRECT ANSWER

B. Dementia with Lewy bodies

---

He has the core clinical features of dementia with Lewy bodies: fluctuating cognition and alertness, recurrent well-formed visual hallucinations, REM sleep behaviour disorder and parkinsonism. Cognitive testing shows the typical profile of attention, executive and visuospatial deficits with relatively preserved memory early on. Severe sensitivity to antipsychotics (marked worsening of parkinsonism, confusion and falls after haloperidol) is a supportive feature and a major safety issue. Haloperidol and other D2 blockers should be avoided. Cholinesterase inhibitors (e.g. rivastigmine or donepezil) can help cognition and hallucinations. By the ''1-year rule'', dementia starting before or within a year of parkinsonism is called DLB; dementia arising in established Parkinson disease is PDD.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Fluctuating cognition, well-formed visual hallucinations, REM sleep behaviour disorder and parkinsonism suggest dementia with Lewy bodies. These patients can react severely to antipsychotics, so avoid haloperidol.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "McKeith IG et al. Diagnosis and management of dementia with Lewy bodies: fourth consensus report of the DLB Consortium. Neurology 2017;89:88\u2013100", "url": "https://doi.org/10.1212/WNL.0000000000004058"}

---

EDITED BY HEISENBERG','difficult','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "bank170", "review_status": "owner_approved_2026_09_29", "tags": ["ZQ-0227", "zyntra_bank", "mock1", "level_4", "task_data_interpretation_synthesis", "group_mental_health", "sys_psychosis", "approved_by_owner_2026_09_29"], "clinician_task": "data_interpretation_synthesis", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"McKeith IG et al. Diagnosis and management of dementia with Lewy bodies: fourth consensus report of the DLB Consortium. Neurology 2017;89:88\\u2013100\", \"url\": \"https://doi.org/10.1212/WNL.0000000000004058\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "Alzheimer disease typically begins with prominent episodic memory loss. Early visual hallucinations, RBD, fluctuations and parkinsonism point to DLB.", "C": "PDD is diagnosed when dementia develops in the context of well-established Parkinson disease (more than 1 year of motor symptoms first). His cognitive fluctuations and hallucinations came before the parkinsonism.", "D": "Vascular dementia usually has a stepwise course with focal neurological signs and vascular risk factors, not RBD and well-formed hallucinations.", "E": "bvFTD presents with early disinhibition, apathy, loss of empathy and compulsive behaviour, usually in younger people, without RBD or visual hallucinations."}, "key_takeaways": ["Fluctuating cognition, well-formed visual hallucinations, REM sleep behaviour disorder and parkinsonism suggest dementia with Lewy bodies. These patients can react severely to antipsychotics, so avoid haloperidol."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,1.0,1.0,'tier_prior','tier=4 (bank170)'),
('6ec0fa86-6628-526b-b285-0b1882669214'::uuid,'ZQ-0228','mental-health','mood-disorders','A 23-year-old hospitality worker sees her general practitioner in Hobart, Tasmania, a week after an emergency department visit for superficial cuts to her forearms after an argument with her partner. She has self-harmed by cutting at times of distress since age 15. She describes intense, rapidly changing emotions that last hours, chronic feelings of emptiness, a strong fear of being abandoned, a series of intense but unstable relationships, and impulsive spending and binge drinking. She has had two brief overdoses of paracetamol in the past 3 years, both after relationship break-ups.

She has previously been prescribed sertraline, quetiapine and diazepam by different doctors, ''none of which helped''. She is not psychotic, and she has no current plan or intent to end her life. She has agreed a safety plan with the ED mental health team. She asks, ''Is there anything that actually works for people like me?''

Which one of the following is the most appropriate longer-term management?','["Start lamotrigine as a mood stabiliser and titrate over 6 weeks", "Restart quetiapine at 100 mg at night to reduce emotional instability", "Arrange a planned 4-week inpatient psychiatric admission for stabilisation", "Prescribe regular diazepam for use at times of intense distress", "Refer for a structured therapy such as dialectical behaviour therapy"]'::jsonb,'E','CORRECT ANSWER

E. Refer for a structured therapy such as dialectical behaviour therapy

---

She meets criteria for borderline personality disorder (affective instability, fear of abandonment, unstable relationships, identity disturbance and emptiness, impulsivity, recurrent self-harm). The NHMRC Clinical Practice Guideline for the Management of BPD (2012) recommends structured psychological therapies designed for BPD (for example DBT or mentalisation-based therapy) as the main treatment. Medicines are NOT recommended as primary therapy because they do not change the course of the disorder. They may be used short term as adjuncts for specific symptoms, with attention to overdose risk. The GP''s role includes giving a diagnosis with hope (many people improve substantially), a consistent treating relationship, crisis/safety planning, and managing comorbidities such as alcohol use.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
BPD: structured, BPD-specific psychotherapy (e.g. DBT, MBT) is the main treatment. Medicines are adjuncts only (not primary), benzodiazepines are best avoided, and admissions should be brief and goal-directed.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "NHMRC. Clinical Practice Guideline for the Management of Borderline Personality Disorder (2012)", "url": "https://www.nhmrc.gov.au/sites/default/files/2022-10/mh25-bpd-guideline.pdf"}

---

EDITED BY HEISENBERG','moderate','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "bank170", "review_status": "owner_approved_2026_09_29", "tags": ["ZQ-0228", "zyntra_bank", "mock1", "level_3", "task_management", "group_mental_health", "sys_mood", "approved_by_owner_2026_09_29"], "clinician_task": "management", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"NHMRC. Clinical Practice Guideline for the Management of Borderline Personality Disorder (2012)\", \"url\": \"https://www.nhmrc.gov.au/sites/default/files/2022-10/mh25-bpd-guideline.pdf\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "Mood stabilisers have not been shown to change the overall course of BPD and are not recommended as primary treatment. Lamotrigine also has serious rash risks.", "B": "Antipsychotics are not recommended as primary therapy in BPD. They add metabolic risk and overdose potential.", "C": "Long admissions can reinforce dependence and self-harm. The guideline supports only brief admissions for crisis with clear, agreed goals.", "D": "Benzodiazepines increase disinhibition, impulsivity and overdose risk in BPD and should be avoided."}, "key_takeaways": ["BPD: structured, BPD-specific psychotherapy (e.g. DBT, MBT) is the main treatment. Medicines are adjuncts only (not primary), benzodiazepines are best avoided, and admissions should be brief and goal-directed."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,0.0,1.0,'tier_prior','tier=3 (bank170)'),
('dda60a56-a9b0-546a-817e-fc45ba19bb95'::uuid,'ZQ-0229','mental-health','psychosis','A 29-year-old man is brought to a general practice in Bendigo, Victoria, by his parents, with whom he lives. Over 3 weeks he has stopped sleeping, barricaded his bedroom window and says that neighbours are ''pumping gas through the vents'' to kill him. Two days ago he threatened a neighbour with a garden spade. He has a history of schizophrenia and stopped his depot antipsychotic 4 months ago. He is not currently under a treatment order.

The general practitioner examines him now. He is suspicious and guarded, describes persecutory delusions and hears voices telling him to ''defend the house''. He has no insight and refuses any treatment or referral, saying ''there''s nothing wrong with me''. He is not intoxicated and is physically well. The local area mental health service cannot send a community team to assess him today, and his parents are frightened to take him home.

Which one of the following is the most appropriate action for the general practitioner?','["Make a temporary treatment order so that he can be given his depot injection today", "Apply to the Mental Health Tribunal for an order requiring him to be assessed", "Make an inpatient assessment order and arrange his transport to a designated service", "Ask his parents to take him home and phone police if he becomes violent again", "Arrange two medical recommendations so he can be detained under the Act"]'::jsonb,'C','CORRECT ANSWER

C. Make an inpatient assessment order and arrange his transport to a designated service

---

Under the Victorian Mental Health and Wellbeing Act 2022, a registered medical practitioner (including a GP) who has examined the person may make an assessment order if the criteria appear to be met: the person appears to have mental illness; because of it, needs immediate treatment to prevent serious deterioration in mental or physical health or serious harm to themselves or another person; an assessment order would enable an assessment; and there is no less restrictive means reasonably available. An inpatient assessment order is made only if the assessment cannot occur in the community, which is the case here. It allows the person to be taken to (and detained in) a designated mental health service for examination by an authorised psychiatrist, who decides whether to make a temporary treatment order. Only an authorised psychiatrist can make a temporary treatment order. Transport can be arranged under the Act (ambulance and/or police). The GP should explain the order and his rights to him and give him the statement of rights.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Victoria (MHW Act 2022): a GP who has examined the person can make an assessment order (inpatient if community assessment is not possible). Only an authorised psychiatrist can make a temporary treatment order.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "Victorian Department of Health. Mental Health and Wellbeing Act 2022 handbook: Assessment orders (updated Jan 2025)", "url": "https://www.health.vic.gov.au/mental-health-and-wellbeing-act-handbook/treatments-and-interventions/assessment-orders"}

---

EDITED BY HEISENBERG','difficult','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "bank170", "review_status": "owner_approved_2026_09_29", "tags": ["ZQ-0229", "zyntra_bank", "mock1", "level_5", "task_management", "group_mental_health", "sys_psychosis", "approved_by_owner_2026_09_29"], "clinician_task": "management", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"Victorian Department of Health. Mental Health and Wellbeing Act 2022 handbook: Assessment orders (updated Jan 2025)\", \"url\": \"https://www.health.vic.gov.au/mental-health-and-wellbeing-act-handbook/treatments-and-interventions/assessment-orders\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "A temporary treatment order can be made only by an authorised psychiatrist after examination at a designated service, not by a GP.", "B": "The Mental Health Tribunal hears applications about treatment orders and reviews; it does not make assessment orders. A GP can make an assessment order directly.", "D": "He appears to meet the criteria, poses a risk of serious harm and cannot be assessed in the community today. Sending him home transfers the risk to his frightened family.", "E": "Victoria does not use a ''two medical recommendations'' model (that is UK-style legislation). A single assessment order by a medical practitioner is the pathway."}, "key_takeaways": ["Victoria (MHW Act 2022): a GP who has examined the person can make an assessment order (inpatient if community assessment is not possible). Only an authorised psychiatrist can make a temporary treatment order."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,2.0,1.0,'tier_prior','tier=5 (bank170)'),
('cd990377-29c5-51a3-a33e-c97b1f85bb06'::uuid,'ZQ-0230','mental-health','mood-disorders','A 70-year-old woman with bipolar I disorder, stable for 15 years on lithium carbonate 750 mg daily, is brought to the emergency department of a hospital in Hobart, Tasmania, by her husband. Three weeks ago her GP started ramipril for hypertension, and she has been taking ibuprofen for a painful shoulder. For 4 days she has had diarrhoea and vomiting and has been eating and drinking little.

She is drowsy, opening her eyes to voice, and confused (GCS 12). She has a coarse tremor of both hands, multifocal myoclonic jerks, dysarthria and ataxia. Her temperature is 37.1 °C, heart rate 64/min, blood pressure 104/62 mmHg and oxygen saturation 96% on room air. There is no evidence of an overdose, and her husband keeps her tablets in a dosette box.

Investigations:
- Serum lithium 2.4 mmol/L (therapeutic 0.6–1.0); sample taken 12 hours after her last dose, which she took the evening before admission
- Sodium 138 mmol/L (135–145)
- Potassium 4.8 mmol/L (3.5–5.2)
- Creatinine 196 µmol/L (45–90; baseline 88 three months ago)
- eGFR 22 mL/min/1.73 m²
- ECG: sinus rhythm 64/min, QTc 470 ms, T-wave flattening

In addition to stopping lithium, ramipril and ibuprofen, which one of the following is the most appropriate management?','["IV 0.9% sodium chloride and urgent haemodialysis", "IV 0.9% sodium chloride and repeat the lithium level in 6 hours", "Activated charcoal 50 g orally, with IV fluids", "Whole bowel irrigation with polyethylene glycol, with IV fluids", "IV furosemide with IV fluids to increase lithium excretion"]'::jsonb,'A','CORRECT ANSWER

A. IV 0.9% sodium chloride and urgent haemodialysis

---

She has chronic lithium toxicity, precipitated by dehydration, an ACE inhibitor and an NSAID (all reduce renal lithium clearance), with acute kidney injury. In chronic toxicity, neurological toxicity correlates poorly with the serum level; a level of 2.4 mmol/L with a reduced level of consciousness is serious. The EXTRIP workgroup recommends extracorporeal treatment (haemodialysis) in lithium poisoning when there is a decreased level of consciousness, seizures or life-threatening dysrhythmias IRRESPECTIVE of the lithium concentration, or when the level is above 4.0 mmol/L with impaired kidney function. It also suggests dialysis when the level is above 5.0, with significant confusion, or when the expected time to reach a level below 1.0 mmol/L with optimal management is more than 36 hours. She meets the ''recommended'' criterion (reduced consciousness), and her AKI will also slow elimination. IV isotonic saline to restore volume is given too. Discuss early with a clinical toxicologist (Poisons Information Centre 13 11 26) and nephrology.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Lithium toxicity: reduced consciousness, seizures or dysrhythmia means haemodialysis regardless of the level (EXTRIP). So does a level >4.0 with impaired kidney function. Charcoal does not bind lithium.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "Decker BS et al. Extracorporeal treatment for lithium poisoning: systematic review and recommendations from the EXTRIP workgroup. Clin J Am Soc Nephrol 2015;10:875\u2013887", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4422246/"}

---

EDITED BY HEISENBERG','difficult','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "bank170", "review_status": "owner_approved_2026_09_29", "tags": ["ZQ-0230", "zyntra_bank", "mock1", "level_5", "task_management", "group_mental_health", "sys_mood", "approved_by_owner_2026_09_29"], "clinician_task": "management", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"Decker BS et al. Extracorporeal treatment for lithium poisoning: systematic review and recommendations from the EXTRIP workgroup. Clin J Am Soc Nephrol 2015;10:875\\u2013887\", \"url\": \"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4422246/\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"B": "Fluids alone might be adequate in mild chronic toxicity with normal consciousness, but with reduced GCS and AKI, delaying dialysis risks permanent neurological injury (SILENT syndrome).", "C": "Activated charcoal does not bind lithium (or other metal ions) and is not indicated.", "D": "Whole bowel irrigation is considered only after a large ingestion of a sustained-release lithium preparation. This is chronic toxicity without an overdose.", "E": "Forced diuresis with loop diuretics does not increase lithium clearance meaningfully and worsens volume depletion and kidney function."}, "key_takeaways": ["Lithium toxicity: reduced consciousness, seizures or dysrhythmia means haemodialysis regardless of the level (EXTRIP). So does a level >4.0 with impaired kidney function. Charcoal does not bind lithium."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,2.0,1.0,'tier_prior','tier=5 (bank170)'),
('f40fd91d-84d6-558d-8351-e948bb7a17a1'::uuid,'ZQ-0231','mental-health','psychosis','A 26-year-old woman with schizophrenia sees her general practitioner in Geraldton, Western Australia, for review. Her illness has been stable for 6 months on risperidone 4 mg daily, prescribed through her community mental health team. She says her periods, which used to be regular, stopped 3 months ago, and she has noticed a milky discharge from both nipples. She has a new partner and says they ''mostly'' use condoms. She has no headaches or visual symptoms. She takes no other medicines.

Her BMI is 29 kg/m², up from 26 a year ago. Blood pressure is 118/76 mmHg. Visual fields are full to confrontation. Expressible milky discharge is noted from both breasts, with no breast lumps. She is worried that ''the tablets have ruined my fertility'' and wants to stop them.

Which one of the following is the most appropriate initial investigation?','["Serum prolactin level", "MRI of the pituitary gland", "Thyroid-stimulating hormone", "Serum or urine β-hCG", "Serum FSH, LH and oestradiol"]'::jsonb,'D','CORRECT ANSWER

D. Serum or urine β-hCG

---

Risperidone is one of the antipsychotics most likely to raise prolactin, and amenorrhoea with galactorrhoea is a typical adverse effect. However, pregnancy is the commonest cause of secondary amenorrhoea in a sexually active woman, and it also raises prolactin and can cause breast secretion. Her contraception is inconsistent, so a pregnancy test is the first investigation. It changes management immediately (antenatal care, medication review and planning with her psychiatrist) and must be known before interpreting a prolactin level or arranging imaging. If she is not pregnant, check prolactin (plus TSH and renal function), then consider switching to a prolactin-sparing antipsychotic or adding aripiprazole in liaison with her mental health team. She should not stop risperidone abruptly because of relapse risk. Discuss reliable contraception, because fertility may return when prolactin falls.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Amenorrhoea in a woman taking a prolactin-raising antipsychotic: do a pregnancy test first, then prolactin. Manage hyperprolactinaemia with her psychiatrist (switch or add aripiprazole) and discuss contraception.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "Galletly C et al. RANZCP clinical practice guidelines for the management of schizophrenia and related disorders (2016), MJA summary", "url": "https://cdn.craft.cloud/019efd17-dcc2-707f-8e07-011d00d1146a/assets/issues/10.5694mja16.01159.pdf"}

---

EDITED BY HEISENBERG','difficult','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "bank170", "review_status": "owner_approved_2026_09_29", "tags": ["ZQ-0231", "zyntra_bank", "mock1", "level_4", "task_data_gathering", "group_mental_health", "sys_psychosis", "approved_by_owner_2026_09_29"], "clinician_task": "data_gathering", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"Galletly C et al. RANZCP clinical practice guidelines for the management of schizophrenia and related disorders (2016), MJA summary\", \"url\": \"https://cdn.craft.cloud/019efd17-dcc2-707f-8e07-011d00d1146a/assets/issues/10.5694mja16.01159.pdf\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "Prolactin is very likely raised and will be checked, but pregnancy also raises prolactin and must be excluded first.", "B": "Pituitary imaging is considered only if prolactin is markedly raised without another explanation, or if there are headaches or visual field defects. It is not the first step.", "C": "Hypothyroidism can raise prolactin and is part of the work-up, but pregnancy is far more likely and more urgent to exclude.", "E": "Gonadotropins help classify amenorrhoea after pregnancy and hyperprolactinaemia have been assessed. They are not the first test."}, "key_takeaways": ["Amenorrhoea in a woman taking a prolactin-raising antipsychotic: do a pregnancy test first, then prolactin. Manage hyperprolactinaemia with her psychiatrist (switch or add aripiprazole) and discuss contraception."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,1.0,1.0,'tier_prior','tier=4 (bank170)'),
('abc2092c-b699-58b6-a97d-71f75c052033'::uuid,'ZQ-0232','mental-health','substance-use','A 52-year-old man is admitted to the medical ward of a regional hospital in Bunbury, Western Australia, with community-acquired pneumonia. He expects to stay in hospital for at least 4 days. He tells the admitting resident that he drinks ''a fair bit'': about 10–12 standard drinks every day, mostly beer, for the past 15 years. His last drink was 3 hours ago.

He currently feels well apart from his cough. His temperature is 38.0 °C, heart rate 98/min, blood pressure 138/86 mmHg and oxygen saturation 94% on room air. He is not tremulous or sweaty, and he is fully orientated. Blood tests show a GGT of 240 U/L (<60), MCV of 102 fL (80–100), platelets 168 × 10^9/L (150–400), potassium 3.8 mmol/L and a normal INR.

The resident is planning alcohol withdrawal monitoring with a withdrawal scale and wants to know how likely he is to develop severe or complicated withdrawal.

Which one of the following additional pieces of history is most important to obtain to anticipate complicated withdrawal?','["The age at which he first started drinking alcohol", "Whether he has had withdrawal seizures or delirium tremens before", "Whether he prefers beer, wine or spirits", "Whether he has a family history of alcohol dependence", "Whether he has ever attended Alcoholics Anonymous"]'::jsonb,'B','CORRECT ANSWER

B. Whether he has had withdrawal seizures or delirium tremens before

---

The strongest clinical predictor of severe or complicated alcohol withdrawal (seizures, delirium tremens) is a past history of these complications. Other predictors include heavy daily consumption, concurrent medical illness (such as his pneumonia), older age, other substance dependence (especially benzodiazepines), and markers such as thrombocytopenia, hypokalaemia and a high initial withdrawal score. NSW Health withdrawal guidance identifies a history of withdrawal seizures or delirium tremens as high risk, which should prompt early prophylactic benzodiazepine dosing (typically diazepam, with dose adjusted for liver function), close monitoring and thiamine, rather than symptom-triggered treatment alone. All patients with heavy alcohol use should receive thiamine, and withdrawal is monitored with a validated scale (e.g. AWS or CIWA-Ar).

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
A past history of withdrawal seizures or delirium tremens is the strongest predictor of complicated alcohol withdrawal; it calls for prophylactic benzodiazepines, close monitoring and thiamine.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "NSW Health. Management of Withdrawal from Alcohol and Other Drugs \u2013 Clinical Guidance", "url": "https://www.health.nsw.gov.au/aod/professionals/Publications/clinical-guidance-withdrawal-alcohol-and-other-drugs.pdf"}
• {"name": "Goodson CM et al. Predictors of severe alcohol withdrawal syndrome: a systematic review and meta-analysis. Alcohol Clin Exp Res 2014", "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/25346507/"}

---

EDITED BY HEISENBERG','moderate','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "bank170", "review_status": "owner_approved_2026_09_29", "tags": ["ZQ-0232", "zyntra_bank", "mock1", "level_3", "task_data_gathering", "group_mental_health", "sys_substance_use", "approved_by_owner_2026_09_29"], "clinician_task": "data_gathering", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"NSW Health. Management of Withdrawal from Alcohol and Other Drugs \\u2013 Clinical Guidance\", \"url\": \"https://www.health.nsw.gov.au/aod/professionals/Publications/clinical-guidance-withdrawal-alcohol-and-other-drugs.pdf\"}; {\"name\": \"Goodson CM et al. Predictors of severe alcohol withdrawal syndrome: a systematic review and meta-analysis. Alcohol Clin Exp Res 2014\", \"url\": \"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/25346507/\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "Age at first drinking is relevant to the history of dependence but does not predict complicated withdrawal as well as a past history of withdrawal complications.", "C": "The type of drink matters less than the quantity (standard drinks per day) and past withdrawal history.", "D": "Family history contributes to the risk of developing alcohol use disorder but does not predict the severity of withdrawal in this admission.", "E": "Past engagement with mutual-help groups is useful for planning long-term recovery support but does not predict withdrawal severity."}, "key_takeaways": ["A past history of withdrawal seizures or delirium tremens is the strongest predictor of complicated alcohol withdrawal; it calls for prophylactic benzodiazepines, close monitoring and thiamine."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,0.0,1.0,'tier_prior','tier=3 (bank170)'),
('085f0d02-8577-58b9-8cb7-1d1183148c36'::uuid,'ZQ-0233','population-health-ethics','screening','A 63-year-old woman sees her general practitioner in Wollongong, New South Wales, after seeing an advertisement for the National Lung Cancer Screening Program. She asks for ''the free lung scan''.

She smoked 15 cigarettes a day from age 18 until she quit at age 54, 9 years ago. She has not smoked since and does not vape. She has no cough, haemoptysis, breathlessness, weight loss or chest pain. She had a normal chest X-ray 2 years ago for a pre-operative assessment before a knee replacement. She has hypertension treated with perindopril. Her father died of lung cancer at 70. Her examination is normal, and her oxygen saturation is 98% on room air.

Which one of the following is the most appropriate response to her request?','["Refer her for a low-dose CT scan through the national program now", "Enrol her in the program once she reaches 65 years of age", "Arrange a chest X-ray now and repeat it every two years", "Explain that she does not meet the program''s smoking-history criterion", "Refer her through the program after spirometry confirms COPD"]'::jsonb,'D','CORRECT ANSWER

D. Explain that she does not meet the program''s smoking-history criterion

---

The National Lung Cancer Screening Program (started 1 July 2025) offers low-dose CT every 2 years to people who are aged 50–70 years, have no signs or symptoms suggesting lung cancer, currently smoke or quit within the past 10 years, and have at least a 30 pack-year smoking history. Pack-years = (cigarettes per day ÷ 20) × years smoked = (15 ÷ 20) × 36 = 27 pack-years. She meets the age, symptom and quit-time criteria (quit 9 years ago) but not the 30 pack-year threshold, so she is not eligible. The GP should explain this, discuss her risk honestly (family history is not an eligibility criterion), reinforce that she should present promptly with any symptoms, and continue other preventive care. A chest X-ray is not a screening test for lung cancer.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
NLCSP: age 50–70, asymptomatic, currently smoking or quit within 10 years, and ≥30 pack-years (cigarettes/day ÷ 20 × years) means 2-yearly low-dose CT. Calculate pack-years carefully.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "Australian Government Department of Health, Disability and Ageing. How the National Lung Cancer Screening Program works", "url": "https://www.health.gov.au/our-work/nlcsp/how-it-works"}
• {"name": "National Lung Cancer Screening Program \u2013 Guidelines (Apr 2025)", "url": "https://www.health.gov.au/sites/default/files/2025-04/national-lung-cancer-screening-program-guidelines.pdf"}

---

EDITED BY HEISENBERG','difficult','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "bank170", "review_status": "owner_approved_2026_09_29", "tags": ["ZQ-0233", "zyntra_bank", "mock1", "level_5", "task_management", "group_population_health_ethics", "sys_screening", "approved_by_owner_2026_09_29"], "clinician_task": "management", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"Australian Government Department of Health, Disability and Ageing. How the National Lung Cancer Screening Program works\", \"url\": \"https://www.health.gov.au/our-work/nlcsp/how-it-works\"}; {\"name\": \"National Lung Cancer Screening Program \\u2013 Guidelines (Apr 2025)\", \"url\": \"https://www.health.gov.au/sites/default/files/2025-04/national-lung-cancer-screening-program-guidelines.pdf\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "She falls short of the 30 pack-year criterion (27 pack-years), so she is not eligible for program screening despite meeting the other criteria.", "B": "The program''s age range is 50–70 years; turning 65 does not change eligibility. Her pack-years stay at 27 because she has quit.", "C": "Chest X-ray screening does not reduce lung cancer mortality and is not recommended.", "E": "COPD is not an eligibility criterion for the program, and spirometry would not change her pack-year history."}, "key_takeaways": ["NLCSP: age 50–70, asymptomatic, currently smoking or quit within 10 years, and ≥30 pack-years (cigarettes/day ÷ 20 × years) means 2-yearly low-dose CT. Calculate pack-years carefully."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,2.0,1.0,'tier_prior','tier=5 (bank170)'),
('67961d2c-5175-584c-9239-b1e4f8708c57'::uuid,'ZQ-0234','population-health-ethics','epidemiology','A randomised controlled trial in Queensland assigned 40 000 adults aged 55–74 years to annual screening with a new blood test for a particular cancer or to usual care, and followed them for 12 years.

Results:
- Cumulative incidence of the cancer: 1.9% in the screened group and 1.8% in the usual-care group
- Proportion diagnosed at an early stage: 58% screened vs 31% usual care
- 5-year survival from the date of diagnosis: 71% screened vs 49% usual care
- Mean age at diagnosis: 66.1 years screened vs 68.3 years usual care
- Mean age at death from the cancer: 72.4 years screened vs 72.5 years usual care
- Cancer-specific mortality: 41 per 100 000 person-years in both groups

A newspaper headline reports that ''the new test improves cancer survival by 45%''.

Which one of the following best explains the difference in 5-year survival between the groups?','["Length-time bias", "Lead-time bias", "Overdiagnosis", "Healthy volunteer (selection) bias", "Recall bias"]'::jsonb,'B','CORRECT ANSWER

B. Lead-time bias

---

Lead-time bias occurs when screening moves the date of diagnosis earlier without delaying death. Survival measured from diagnosis looks longer even though patients do not live any longer. Here screening brought diagnosis forward by about 2 years (mean age 66.1 vs 68.3), but age at death (72.4 vs 72.5) and cancer-specific mortality were unchanged. The apparent survival gain is therefore an artefact of the earlier start. Randomisation makes selection bias unlikely, and similar cumulative incidence argues against substantial overdiagnosis. The valid measure of screening benefit is a reduction in disease-specific (and ideally all-cause) mortality in a randomised comparison, not survival from diagnosis.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Longer survival from diagnosis with unchanged mortality and age at death means lead-time bias. Judge screening by mortality reduction in randomised trials.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "Australian Government Department of Health. Population Based Screening Framework (2018)", "url": "https://www.health.gov.au/resources/publications/population-based-screening-framework"}

---

EDITED BY HEISENBERG','difficult','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "bank170", "review_status": "owner_approved_2026_09_29", "tags": ["ZQ-0234", "zyntra_bank", "mock1", "level_4", "task_data_interpretation_synthesis", "group_population_health_ethics", "sys_epidemiology", "approved_by_owner_2026_09_29"], "clinician_task": "data_interpretation_synthesis", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"Australian Government Department of Health. Population Based Screening Framework (2018)\", \"url\": \"https://www.health.gov.au/resources/publications/population-based-screening-framework\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "Length-time bias arises because screening preferentially detects slow-growing tumours with a better prognosis. It is a concern when comparing screen-detected with symptomatic cancers, but here the key finding is an earlier diagnosis with unchanged age at death.", "C": "Overdiagnosis (detecting cancers that would never cause harm) would usually increase incidence in the screened group. Incidence was almost the same.", "D": "Healthy volunteer bias affects observational comparisons of people who choose screening. This was a randomised trial.", "E": "Recall bias affects case-control studies that rely on participants'' memory of exposures. It does not apply to survival data."}, "key_takeaways": ["Longer survival from diagnosis with unchanged mortality and age at death means lead-time bias. Judge screening by mortality reduction in randomised trials."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,1.0,1.0,'tier_prior','tier=4 (bank170)'),
('5dc67a29-f054-5dd0-ad1f-8f01a5e0b456'::uuid,'ZQ-0235','population-health-ethics','epidemiology','A youth health service in regional South Australia is considering a new point-of-care test for chlamydia for asymptomatic 16–29-year-olds attending for other reasons. In this population the prevalence of chlamydia is estimated at 2%. The manufacturer reports that the test has a sensitivity of 90% and a specificity of 95% compared with nucleic acid amplification testing.

The service director asks you to estimate, for a young person in this setting who tests positive on the new test, how likely it is that they actually have chlamydia.

Which one of the following is closest to the probability that a person with a positive result has chlamydia?','["90%", "95%", "50%", "99.8%", "27%"]'::jsonb,'E','CORRECT ANSWER

E. 27%

---

This is the positive predictive value (PPV), which depends on prevalence as well as on test accuracy. Take 10 000 people: 200 have chlamydia (2%) and 9800 do not. True positives = 90% of 200 = 180. False positives = 5% of 9800 = 490. PPV = 180 ÷ (180 + 490) = 180 ÷ 670 ≈ 27%. So about three in four positive results would be false positives. In a low-prevalence population even a fairly specific test gives a low PPV, which is why confirmatory NAAT testing (or a more specific test) would be needed before treatment and partner notification. The negative predictive value is very high: 9310 ÷ 9330 ≈ 99.8%.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
PPV falls as prevalence falls. At 2% prevalence, a test with 90% sensitivity and 95% specificity has a PPV of only about 27%, so confirm positives with a more specific test.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "Australian Government Department of Health. Population Based Screening Framework (2018)", "url": "https://www.health.gov.au/resources/publications/population-based-screening-framework"}

---

EDITED BY HEISENBERG','moderate','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "bank170", "review_status": "owner_approved_2026_09_29", "tags": ["ZQ-0235", "zyntra_bank", "mock1", "level_3", "task_data_interpretation_synthesis", "group_population_health_ethics", "sys_epidemiology", "approved_by_owner_2026_09_29"], "clinician_task": "data_interpretation_synthesis", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"Australian Government Department of Health. Population Based Screening Framework (2018)\", \"url\": \"https://www.health.gov.au/resources/publications/population-based-screening-framework\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "90% is the sensitivity: the proportion of people WITH chlamydia who test positive. It is not the probability of disease given a positive test.", "B": "95% is the specificity: the proportion of people WITHOUT chlamydia who test negative.", "C": "50% has no basis in these figures. PPV has to be calculated from prevalence, sensitivity and specificity.", "D": "99.8% is the negative predictive value (the probability of NOT having chlamydia after a negative result)."}, "key_takeaways": ["PPV falls as prevalence falls. At 2% prevalence, a test with 90% sensitivity and 95% specificity has a PPV of only about 27%, so confirm positives with a more specific test."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,0.0,1.0,'tier_prior','tier=3 (bank170)'),
('dd836e4a-070f-5017-bfbd-2238cd3188ef'::uuid,'ZQ-0236','population-health-ethics','epidemiology','A general practitioner in Brisbane is reviewing a randomised, placebo-controlled trial of a new once-yearly injection to prevent hip fracture in community-dwelling women aged over 70 years with osteopenia. The trial enrolled 8000 women and followed them for 3 years.

Results:
- Hip fracture over 3 years: 4.0% in the placebo group and 2.5% in the treatment group
- The difference was statistically significant (p = 0.002)
- Serious adverse events: similar in both groups

A patient asks how many women like her would need to have the injection for one of them to avoid a hip fracture.

Which one of the following is the number needed to treat for 3 years to prevent one hip fracture?','["67", "3", "25", "40", "150"]'::jsonb,'A','CORRECT ANSWER

A. 67

---

Absolute risk reduction (ARR) = 4.0% − 2.5% = 1.5% (0.015) over 3 years. NNT = 1 ÷ ARR = 1 ÷ 0.015 ≈ 66.7, which rounds UP to 67 women treated for 3 years to prevent one hip fracture. The relative risk reduction (RRR) is 1.5 ÷ 4.0 = 37.5%, which sounds much more impressive. Presenting absolute rather than relative effects, with the time frame, helps patients make informed decisions. The NNT depends on baseline risk: the same RRR gives a smaller NNT in higher-risk women.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
NNT = 1 ÷ absolute risk reduction (round up) and always has a time frame. Here ARR 1.5% gives NNT 67 over 3 years, while the RRR is 37.5%.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "Australian Prescriber. Interpreting risks and ratios in therapy trials", "url": "https://australianprescriber.tg.org.au/articles/interpreting-risks-and-ratios-in-therapy-trials.html"}

---

EDITED BY HEISENBERG','moderate','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "bank170", "review_status": "owner_approved_2026_09_29", "tags": ["ZQ-0236", "zyntra_bank", "mock1", "level_3", "task_data_interpretation_synthesis", "group_population_health_ethics", "sys_epidemiology", "approved_by_owner_2026_09_29"], "clinician_task": "data_interpretation_synthesis", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"Australian Prescriber. Interpreting risks and ratios in therapy trials\", \"url\": \"https://australianprescriber.tg.org.au/articles/interpreting-risks-and-ratios-in-therapy-trials.html\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"B": "3 is roughly 1 ÷ RRR (1 ÷ 0.375). Using the relative risk reduction instead of the absolute risk reduction is a common error.", "C": "25 is 1 ÷ 0.04, the reciprocal of the placebo event rate, not of the risk difference.", "D": "40 is 1 ÷ 0.025, the reciprocal of the treatment event rate.", "E": "150 comes from a decimal error (treating 1.5% as 0.0067 or similar). The correct ARR is 0.015."}, "key_takeaways": ["NNT = 1 ÷ absolute risk reduction (round up) and always has a time frame. Here ARR 1.5% gives NNT 67 over 3 years, while the RRR is 37.5%."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,0.0,1.0,'tier_prior','tier=3 (bank170)'),
('71dee3f1-61b7-569a-a842-0070cae37968'::uuid,'ZQ-0237','population-health-ethics','epidemiology','Clinicians at a tertiary maternity and neonatal service in Melbourne have noticed several infants born with a rare abdominal wall defect (about 1 in 3000 births) whose mothers reported using a particular over-the-counter decongestant in early pregnancy. The state health department wants to know, within about 12 months and with a limited budget, whether there is an association between early-pregnancy use of the decongestant and the defect.

State perinatal data collections can identify all affected infants born over the past 8 years, and a random sample of unaffected births from the same hospitals and years can be selected. Mothers can be interviewed, and pharmacy records are available for some.

Which one of the following study designs is most appropriate to answer this question?','["Prospective cohort study of women exposed and unexposed in pregnancy", "Randomised controlled trial of the decongestant in early pregnancy", "Case-control study comparing past exposure in cases and controls", "Cross-sectional survey of current decongestant use among pregnant women", "Ecological study correlating sales data with birth defect rates"]'::jsonb,'C','CORRECT ANSWER

C. Case-control study comparing past exposure in cases and controls

---

For a rare outcome, a case-control study is the most efficient design: start with people who have the outcome (cases) and a comparable group without it (controls), then compare how often each group was exposed. It can be done quickly and cheaply using existing records and interviews, and it gives an odds ratio. Its main weaknesses are recall bias (mothers of affected infants may remember exposures more thoroughly; pharmacy records help) and selection of appropriate controls. Confounders such as maternal age, smoking and fever should be measured and adjusted for.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Rare outcome with a suspected earlier exposure: use a case-control study (efficient, quick, gives an odds ratio; watch for recall and selection bias). Cohorts suit rare exposures or common outcomes.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "NHMRC. Guidelines for Guidelines handbook (evidence and study design)", "url": "https://www.nhmrc.gov.au/guidelinesforguidelines"}

---

EDITED BY HEISENBERG','moderate','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "bank170", "review_status": "owner_approved_2026_09_29", "tags": ["ZQ-0237", "zyntra_bank", "mock1", "level_3", "task_data_gathering", "group_population_health_ethics", "sys_epidemiology", "approved_by_owner_2026_09_29"], "clinician_task": "data_gathering", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"NHMRC. Guidelines for Guidelines handbook (evidence and study design)\", \"url\": \"https://www.nhmrc.gov.au/guidelinesforguidelines\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "A prospective cohort would need to follow tens of thousands of pregnancies to observe enough cases of a 1-in-3000 outcome, and would take years. It is best suited to common outcomes or rare exposures.", "B": "Randomly assigning pregnant women to a drug suspected of causing birth defects would be unethical, and an RCT would be enormous and slow for a rare outcome.", "D": "A cross-sectional survey measures exposure and outcome at one time. It cannot establish that the exposure came before the outcome, and it would capture very few cases.", "E": "Ecological studies use group-level data and are prone to the ecological fallacy. They can generate hypotheses but cannot link exposure to outcome in individuals."}, "key_takeaways": ["Rare outcome with a suspected earlier exposure: use a case-control study (efficient, quick, gives an odds ratio; watch for recall and selection bias). Cohorts suit rare exposures or common outcomes."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,0.0,1.0,'tier_prior','tier=3 (bank170)'),
('37d67b72-9857-5d7b-9877-ef974e41ff73'::uuid,'ZQ-0238','population-health-ethics','screening','A 26-year-old cook at a busy café in Hobart, Tasmania, sees his general practitioner with 5 days of fatigue, nausea, anorexia and dark urine, and yellow eyes that he noticed 2 days ago. He returned from a 3-week trip to South-East Asia a month ago and had not been vaccinated against hepatitis A. He lives with his partner and has worked shifts preparing food, including salads and sandwiches, up until yesterday.

He is jaundiced and has mild tender hepatomegaly. He is alert, with no asterixis.

Investigations:
- ALT 2140 U/L (<40)
- Bilirubin 96 µmol/L (<20)
- INR 1.1
- Hepatitis A IgM antibody: positive
- HBsAg: negative; HCV antibody: negative

He asks when he can return to work, because he cannot afford to take much time off.

In addition to notifying the public health unit, which one of the following is the most appropriate advice about work?','["He can return to work as soon as he feels well enough, with careful hand hygiene", "Exclude him from food handling until 7 days after the onset of jaundice", "Exclude him from food handling until his liver function tests are normal", "Exclude him from food handling until 2 weeks after his last day of work", "Exclude him from food handling until his hepatitis A IgM is negative"]'::jsonb,'B','CORRECT ANSWER

B. Exclude him from food handling until 7 days after the onset of jaundice

---

Hepatitis A is notifiable in all Australian jurisdictions, and a food handler with hepatitis A is a public health priority because of the risk of foodborne outbreaks. People with hepatitis A are most infectious in the 2 weeks before symptoms start, and infectivity falls quickly after jaundice appears. The CDNA national guidelines and state control guidelines recommend that food handlers are excluded from work until at least 7 days after the onset of jaundice (or 2 weeks after the onset of first symptoms if there is no jaundice). The public health unit will assess the risk to café patrons and co-workers. Close contacts (such as his partner) should be offered post-exposure prophylaxis (hepatitis A vaccine, or normal human immunoglobulin for some groups) as soon as possible and within 2 weeks of last exposure.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Hepatitis A in a food handler: notify, and exclude from food handling until 7 days after jaundice onset (or 2 weeks after first symptoms if no jaundice). Offer contacts vaccine or NHIG within 2 weeks of exposure.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "CDNA. Hepatitis A: National Guidelines for Public Health Units (2026)", "url": "https://www.cdc.gov.au/sites/default/files/2026-07/hepatitis-a-cdna-national-guidelines-for-public-health-units.pdf"}
• {"name": "NSW Health. Hepatitis A control guideline", "url": "https://www.health.nsw.gov.au/Infectious/controlguideline/Pages/hepa.aspx"}

---

EDITED BY HEISENBERG','difficult','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "bank170", "review_status": "owner_approved_2026_09_29", "tags": ["ZQ-0238", "zyntra_bank", "mock1", "level_4", "task_management", "group_population_health_ethics", "sys_screening", "approved_by_owner_2026_09_29"], "clinician_task": "management", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"CDNA. Hepatitis A: National Guidelines for Public Health Units (2026)\", \"url\": \"https://www.cdc.gov.au/sites/default/files/2026-07/hepatitis-a-cdna-national-guidelines-for-public-health-units.pdf\"}; {\"name\": \"NSW Health. Hepatitis A control guideline\", \"url\": \"https://www.health.nsw.gov.au/Infectious/controlguideline/Pages/hepa.aspx\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "Feeling well is not the criterion. He may still be shedding virus, and handling ready-to-eat food risks an outbreak.", "C": "Transaminases can take weeks to normalise, long after infectivity has fallen. LFTs are not the exclusion criterion.", "D": "The exclusion period is measured from symptom (jaundice) onset, not from his last shift.", "E": "HAV IgM stays positive for months after infection and does not indicate ongoing infectivity."}, "key_takeaways": ["Hepatitis A in a food handler: notify, and exclude from food handling until 7 days after jaundice onset (or 2 weeks after first symptoms if no jaundice). Offer contacts vaccine or NHIG within 2 weeks of exposure."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,1.0,1.0,'tier_prior','tier=4 (bank170)'),
('917a9431-15ee-5501-bbaa-77ffcea58b53'::uuid,'ZQ-0239','population-health-ethics','screening','A 19-year-old university student in Perth, Western Australia, was admitted to hospital this morning with invasive meningococcal disease (Neisseria meningitidis confirmed on blood PCR). His symptoms began yesterday afternoon, and he received IV ceftriaxone on arrival in the emergency department. The public health unit asks the treating team to help identify contacts. The following people are identified:

- A lecturer who taught him in a 150-seat lecture theatre 3 days ago
- His flatmate, who has been overseas for the past 10 days
- The emergency department nurse who took his blood samples while wearing a surgical mask
- His girlfriend, who kissed him and stayed at his flat 2 nights before his symptoms began
- The rideshare driver who drove him to hospital, a 20-minute trip

Which one of the following people should be offered clearance antibiotics?','["The lecturer", "The flatmate", "The emergency department nurse", "The girlfriend", "The rideshare driver"]'::jsonb,'D','CORRECT ANSWER

D. The girlfriend

---

Clearance antibiotics are for close contacts: people who had close, prolonged contact with the case during the 7 days before symptom onset, until 24 hours after the case started effective antibiotics. This includes household or household-like contacts (e.g. staying overnight), intimate (kissing) contacts, childcare contacts with household-equivalent exposure, healthcare workers with unprotected close airway exposure (e.g. intubation or suctioning without a mask), and passengers seated next to the case for more than 8 hours. His girlfriend had intimate contact and stayed overnight within the 7-day window. Options are single-dose ciprofloxacin (ceftriaxone in pregnancy; rifampicin is contraindicated in pregnancy), given as soon as possible, ideally within 24 hours of the diagnosis. The case also needs clearance if the treatment antibiotic does not eliminate carriage (ceftriaxone does). Close contacts are offered vaccination if the serogroup is vaccine-preventable.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
IMD clearance antibiotics: household or household-like, intimate (kissing), childcare-equivalent, unprotected airway-exposed HCWs, and adjacent passengers >8 h, within 7 days before onset. Ciprofloxacin single dose (ceftriaxone in pregnancy).

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "The Royal Children''s Hospital Melbourne. Clinical Practice Guideline: Contact prophylaxis for invasive meningococcal or Hib disease (Apr 2026)", "url": "https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/Meningococcal_prophylaxis/"}
• {"name": "CDNA. Invasive Meningococcal Disease: National Guidelines for Public Health Units", "url": "https://www.cdc.gov.au/sites/default/files/2025-11/invasive-meningococcal-disease-cdna-national-guidelines-for-public-health-units_0.pdf"}

---

EDITED BY HEISENBERG','difficult','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "bank170", "review_status": "owner_approved_2026_09_29", "tags": ["ZQ-0239", "zyntra_bank", "mock1", "level_4", "task_data_interpretation_synthesis", "group_population_health_ethics", "sys_screening", "approved_by_owner_2026_09_29"], "clinician_task": "data_interpretation_synthesis", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"The Royal Children''s Hospital Melbourne. Clinical Practice Guideline: Contact prophylaxis for invasive meningococcal or Hib disease (Apr 2026)\", \"url\": \"https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/Meningococcal_prophylaxis/\"}; {\"name\": \"CDNA. Invasive Meningococcal Disease: National Guidelines for Public Health Units\", \"url\": \"https://www.cdc.gov.au/sites/default/files/2025-11/invasive-meningococcal-disease-cdna-national-guidelines-for-public-health-units_0.pdf\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "Classroom or lecture-theatre contact is not close, prolonged contact and does not warrant prophylaxis.", "B": "Household contacts qualify only if the exposure occurred in the 7 days before symptom onset. He has been away for 10 days.", "C": "Healthcare workers need prophylaxis only after unprotected exposure to respiratory secretions during procedures such as intubation or suctioning. Venepuncture while wearing a mask is not such an exposure.", "E": "A short car trip is not close, prolonged contact. Passenger prophylaxis applies to people seated adjacent for more than 8 hours."}, "key_takeaways": ["IMD clearance antibiotics: household or household-like, intimate (kissing), childcare-equivalent, unprotected airway-exposed HCWs, and adjacent passengers >8 h, within 7 days before onset. Ciprofloxacin single dose (ceftriaxone in pregnancy)."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,1.0,1.0,'tier_prior','tier=4 (bank170)'),
('b9632b4d-0c6d-55c6-b4bc-1166cb3a7181'::uuid,'ZQ-0240','population-health-ethics','screening','A 30-year-old woman in her second pregnancy moves from overseas to Melbourne, Victoria, and attends her first antenatal visit at a general practice at 37 weeks and 2 days of gestation. The pregnancy has been uncomplicated. She had an influenza vaccine through her workplace in April this year, before she became aware of the pregnancy''s dates. She has not had any other vaccines during this pregnancy. Her first child, now 3, is fully immunised.

She has read about whooping cough and RSV and asks whether it is ''too late'' for any vaccines. She has no allergies and is well today.

Which one of the following is the most appropriate advice?','["Give RSV vaccine and dTpa today; the baby needs nirsevimab if born within 2 weeks", "Give dTpa today; RSV vaccine is not recommended after 36 weeks of gestation", "Give no vaccines now; plan nirsevimab for the baby and give her dTpa after birth", "Give RSV vaccine today and dTpa in 2 weeks, as the two should be spaced apart", "Give dTpa today and give the RSV vaccine to her in the first week after birth"]'::jsonb,'A','CORRECT ANSWER

A. Give RSV vaccine and dTpa today; the baby needs nirsevimab if born within 2 weeks

---

The Australian Immunisation Handbook recommends a single dose of the maternal RSV vaccine (Abrysvo) in each pregnancy from 28 weeks'' gestation. A woman who has not been vaccinated by 36 weeks should receive it as soon as possible. Antibody transfer needs at least 2 weeks before birth for adequate protection, so if the baby is born less than 2 weeks after maternal vaccination, the infant is recommended to receive nirsevimab. Pertussis vaccine (dTpa) is recommended in every pregnancy, ideally at 20–32 weeks, but if it was missed it should be given as soon as possible, at any time up to delivery. RSV vaccine can be given at the same visit as dTpa, influenza or COVID-19 vaccines. Her influenza vaccine this year covers the current season.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Maternal RSV vaccine (Abrysvo) is recommended from 28 weeks; if missed, give it as soon as possible (no 36-week cut-off). Infants born <2 weeks after maternal vaccination get nirsevimab. dTpa is ideally given at 20–32 weeks but can be given up to delivery, and can be co-administered.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "Australian Immunisation Handbook. Pregnant women are recommended to receive an RSV vaccine during each pregnancy to protect their infant (updated 12 May 2026)", "url": "https://immunisationhandbook.health.gov.au/recommendations/pregnant-women-are-recommended-to-receive-an-rsv-vaccine-during-each-pregnancy-to-protect-their-infant"}
• {"name": "Australian Immunisation Handbook. Pregnant women are recommended to receive a single dose of pertussis-containing vaccine in each pregnancy", "url": "https://immunisationhandbook.health.gov.au/recommendations/pregnant-women-are-recommended-to-receive-a-single-dose-of-pertussis-containing-vaccine-in-each-pregnancy"}

---

EDITED BY HEISENBERG','difficult','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "bank170", "review_status": "owner_approved_2026_09_29", "tags": ["ZQ-0240", "zyntra_bank", "mock1", "level_5", "task_management", "group_population_health_ethics", "sys_screening", "approved_by_owner_2026_09_29"], "clinician_task": "management", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"Australian Immunisation Handbook. Pregnant women are recommended to receive an RSV vaccine during each pregnancy to protect their infant (updated 12 May 2026)\", \"url\": \"https://immunisationhandbook.health.gov.au/recommendations/pregnant-women-are-recommended-to-receive-an-rsv-vaccine-during-each-pregnancy-to-protect-their-infant\"}; {\"name\": \"Australian Immunisation Handbook. Pregnant women are recommended to receive a single dose of pertussis-containing vaccine in each pregnancy\", \"url\": \"https://immunisationhandbook.health.gov.au/recommendations/pregnant-women-are-recommended-to-receive-a-single-dose-of-pertussis-containing-vaccine-in-each-pregnancy\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"B": "Current Handbook advice is that a woman not vaccinated by the end of 36 weeks should receive the RSV vaccine as soon as possible. There is no 36-week cut-off for the recommendation.", "C": "Delaying both vaccines loses the benefit of transplacental antibody transfer. Postpartum dTpa does not protect the newborn as well as antenatal vaccination.", "D": "RSV vaccine and dTpa can be given at the same visit; spacing them only delays protection at this late gestation.", "E": "RSV vaccination of the mother after birth does not protect the infant (breastfeeding alone is not expected to give protection). Infant protection after birth comes from nirsevimab."}, "key_takeaways": ["Maternal RSV vaccine (Abrysvo) is recommended from 28 weeks; if missed, give it as soon as possible (no 36-week cut-off). Infants born <2 weeks after maternal vaccination get nirsevimab. dTpa is ideally given at 20–32 weeks but can be given up to delivery, and can be co-administered."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,2.0,1.0,'tier_prior','tier=5 (bank170)'),
('ef93d11d-492c-5c45-97fc-76671bf3c500'::uuid,'ZQ-0241','population-health-ethics','ethics-law','A 58-year-old man with type 2 diabetes and long-standing schizophrenia is an inpatient at a hospital in Newcastle, New South Wales, with a spreading infection and wet gangrene of his right forefoot. The vascular surgeon recommends a below-knee amputation within 24 hours and explains that without it he will probably develop worsening sepsis and may die. He refuses, saying, ''I''ve had this leg for 58 years and I''m going out with it. I know I might die.''

His schizophrenia has been stable for 10 years on a monthly depot antipsychotic. He lives alone in a public housing unit, manages his own finances and attends his community mental health appointments. He is not delirious. He has no current hallucinations and expresses no delusions about his leg or the surgery. His sister, who is his closest relative, strongly wants the surgery to go ahead. A nurse suggests that ''he can''t consent because he has schizophrenia''.

Which one of the following is most important to establish before accepting or overriding his refusal?','["Whether he currently meets diagnostic criteria for schizophrenia", "Whether his score on a cognitive screening test is in the normal range", "Whether his family members agree with the decision he has made", "Whether his decision is one most people in his situation would make", "Whether he can understand, weigh and communicate this specific decision"]'::jsonb,'E','CORRECT ANSWER

E. Whether he can understand, weigh and communicate this specific decision

---

Adults are presumed to have capacity. Capacity is decision-specific and is assessed functionally: can the person understand the information relevant to the decision (including the consequences of refusing), retain it long enough, use or weigh it to reach a decision, and communicate that decision? A diagnosis of mental illness does not in itself mean that a person lacks capacity, and an unwise or unusual decision does not prove incapacity. If he has capacity, his refusal must be respected even if it leads to death. The team should explore his reasons, correct misunderstandings, offer alternatives and support, involve his treating psychiatrist and document carefully. If he lacks capacity for this decision, NSW law provides for substitute decision-making (a person responsible or guardian under the Guardianship Act 1987, or the Tribunal), guided by his known wishes; emergency treatment provisions apply if needed to save life.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Capacity is presumed, decision-specific and functional (understand, retain, weigh, communicate). Mental illness or an unwise choice does not by itself mean incapacity.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "NSW Health. Consent to Medical and Healthcare Treatment Manual", "url": "https://www.health.nsw.gov.au/policies/manuals/Pages/consent-manual.aspx"}
• {"name": "Medical Board of Australia. Good medical practice: a code of conduct for doctors in Australia", "url": "https://www.medicalboard.gov.au/Codes-Guidelines-Policies/Code-of-conduct.aspx"}

---

EDITED BY HEISENBERG','difficult','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "bank170", "review_status": "owner_approved_2026_09_29", "tags": ["ZQ-0241", "zyntra_bank", "mock1", "level_4", "task_data_gathering", "group_population_health_ethics", "sys_ethics_law", "approved_by_owner_2026_09_29"], "clinician_task": "data_gathering", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"NSW Health. Consent to Medical and Healthcare Treatment Manual\", \"url\": \"https://www.health.nsw.gov.au/policies/manuals/Pages/consent-manual.aspx\"}; {\"name\": \"Medical Board of Australia. Good medical practice: a code of conduct for doctors in Australia\", \"url\": \"https://www.medicalboard.gov.au/Codes-Guidelines-Policies/Code-of-conduct.aspx\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "A diagnosis of schizophrenia does not determine capacity. The question is his functional ability to make this decision.", "B": "Cognitive screening scores do not equal decision-making capacity. People with low scores may have capacity for some decisions, and vice versa.", "C": "Family views are important but do not determine the capacity of a competent adult, and they cannot override his decision if he has capacity.", "D": "Capacity is not judged by whether the decision is ''reasonable'' or common. Competent adults may make decisions others disagree with."}, "key_takeaways": ["Capacity is presumed, decision-specific and functional (understand, retain, weigh, communicate). Mental illness or an unwise choice does not by itself mean incapacity."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,1.0,1.0,'tier_prior','tier=4 (bank170)'),
('da3957d1-39fe-5cd5-826f-53cadb496869'::uuid,'ZQ-0242','population-health-ethics','ethics-law','A 44-year-old interstate B-double truck driver from Shepparton, Victoria, sees his general practitioner for a repeat prescription of levetiracetam. A neurologist diagnosed epilepsy 2 months ago after two generalised seizures in 6 weeks, one of them at a truck stop. He had a further seizure at home 10 days ago. The neurologist and the GP have both told him that he is not fit to drive any vehicle and that he must tell VicRoads. His letter from the neurologist confirms this advice.

He says he has not told VicRoads or his employer and is still driving his usual Melbourne–Brisbane route ''because I''ll lose my job and the house''. He becomes angry when the GP raises it again and says, ''What I tell you is confidential.'' He leaves for a run to Brisbane tomorrow. The GP has discussed the risks with him, offered a letter to support a change of duties, and documented the advice.

Which one of the following is the most appropriate next step for the GP?','["Maintain confidentiality, because telling VicRoads is legally his duty alone", "Notify his employer directly so he can be taken off driving duties", "Tell him that you intend to notify VicRoads, and then notify VicRoads", "Notify VicRoads, because Victorian law makes medical reporting mandatory", "Report him to Victoria Police so that his licence is cancelled at once"]'::jsonb,'C','CORRECT ANSWER

C. Tell him that you intend to notify VicRoads, and then notify VicRoads

---

Under Austroads Assessing Fitness to Drive, drivers are legally responsible for reporting conditions that affect their driving to the licensing authority. Health professionals advise patients about their fitness to drive and document it. If a patient continues to drive despite advice and poses a risk to public safety, a health professional may report directly to the driver licensing authority. In most jurisdictions, including Victoria, this is voluntary (not mandatory), and doctors who report in good faith are protected. Only South Australia and the Northern Territory have mandatory reporting by health professionals. Confidentiality is not absolute. Disclosure is justified here because the risk of serious harm to the public (a heavy vehicle driver with recent seizures) outweighs his privacy, and the Medical Board code supports disclosure when there is a serious risk to others. Good practice is to tell the patient of the intention to report (unless this would increase risk), report to VicRoads with the minimum necessary information, and document everything.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Fitness to drive: the patient must self-report. If a patient keeps driving unsafely, a doctor may report to the licensing authority (voluntary and legally protected in most states; mandatory in SA and NT). Tell the patient and document.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "Austroads. Assessing Fitness to Drive: roles and responsibilities of health professionals", "url": "https://austroads.gov.au/publications/assessing-fitness-to-drive/ap-g56/roles-and-responsibilities/roles-and-responsibilities-of-health-professionals"}
• {"name": "Medical Board of Australia. Good medical practice: a code of conduct for doctors in Australia", "url": "https://www.medicalboard.gov.au/Codes-Guidelines-Policies/Code-of-conduct.aspx"}

---

EDITED BY HEISENBERG','difficult','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "bank170", "review_status": "owner_approved_2026_09_29", "tags": ["ZQ-0242", "zyntra_bank", "mock1", "level_5", "task_management", "group_population_health_ethics", "sys_ethics_law", "approved_by_owner_2026_09_29"], "clinician_task": "management", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"Austroads. Assessing Fitness to Drive: roles and responsibilities of health professionals\", \"url\": \"https://austroads.gov.au/publications/assessing-fitness-to-drive/ap-g56/roles-and-responsibilities/roles-and-responsibilities-of-health-professionals\"}; {\"name\": \"Medical Board of Australia. Good medical practice: a code of conduct for doctors in Australia\", \"url\": \"https://www.medicalboard.gov.au/Codes-Guidelines-Policies/Code-of-conduct.aspx\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "The driver has the primary duty, but that does not prevent the GP from reporting when a patient ignores advice and endangers the public.", "B": "Disclosure to the employer is not the appropriate channel and discloses more health information than necessary. The licensing authority is the appropriate body.", "D": "Reporting is appropriate, but the stated reason is wrong: in Victoria reporting by doctors is permitted and protected, not mandatory. Mandatory reporting applies in SA and the NT.", "E": "Police are not the usual reporting pathway for fitness-to-drive concerns. The licensing authority assesses fitness and licence conditions."}, "key_takeaways": ["Fitness to drive: the patient must self-report. If a patient keeps driving unsafely, a doctor may report to the licensing authority (voluntary and legally protected in most states; mandatory in SA and NT). Tell the patient and document."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,2.0,1.0,'tier_prior','tier=5 (bank170)'),
('4dc65714-ad02-52f6-b512-7eb5bec9ea17'::uuid,'ZQ-0243','population-health-ethics','ethics-law','A 76-year-old woman is an inpatient on a medical ward at a hospital in Townsville, Queensland, after an elective knee replacement. Her creatinine clearance is 25 mL/min. Because of a prescribing error, she was given enoxaparin 1.5 mg/kg daily (a treatment dose not adjusted for her kidney function) instead of the intended prophylactic dose for 3 days. Last night she developed a large thigh haematoma and a haemoglobin drop from 118 to 76 g/L, needing a two-unit red cell transfusion. She is now stable.

The error was identified this morning and has been entered into the hospital''s incident management system. The consultant has asked you, the ward resident who is part of the treating team, to help plan what to tell the patient and her son, who is asking ''what went wrong''.

Which one of the following is the most appropriate approach?','["Wait until the incident review is complete so that accurate information can be given", "Meet them soon, say sorry, explain the known facts and outline the next steps", "Explain what happened but avoid the word ''sorry'', as it could be an admission of liability", "Ask the nurse who gave the doses to explain the error to the family on her own", "Contact the hospital''s medical indemnity insurer before speaking to the family"]'::jsonb,'B','CORRECT ANSWER

B. Meet them soon, say sorry, explain the known facts and outline the next steps

---

The Australian Open Disclosure Framework (ACSQHC) requires timely, open communication with patients and their support people when an adverse event causes harm. Key elements: an apology or expression of regret that includes the words ''I am sorry'' or ''we are sorry''; a factual explanation of what is known (without speculation or blame); the opportunity for the patient and family to tell their story and ask questions; an explanation of the steps being taken to manage the event and prevent recurrence; and a plan for ongoing communication and support. Disclosure starts early (ideally within 24 hours) and continues as the review progresses; it is led by a senior clinician, with other team members as appropriate. All Australian jurisdictions have apology provisions protecting an expression of regret from being treated as an admission of liability.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Open disclosure: prompt, senior-led, includes saying ''sorry'', gives the known facts without speculation or blame, listens to the patient and family, and explains the next steps and follow-up. Apology laws protect expressions of regret.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "Australian Commission on Safety and Quality in Health Care. Open disclosure (Australian Open Disclosure Framework)", "url": "https://www.safetyandquality.gov.au/open-disclosure"}
• {"name": "ACSQHC. Open disclosure: principles, elements and process", "url": "https://www.safetyandquality.gov.au/sites/default/files/2021-09/open-disclosure-principles-elements-and-process_9.pdf"}

---

EDITED BY HEISENBERG','moderate','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "bank170", "review_status": "owner_approved_2026_09_29", "tags": ["ZQ-0243", "zyntra_bank", "mock1", "level_3", "task_management", "group_population_health_ethics", "sys_ethics_law", "approved_by_owner_2026_09_29"], "clinician_task": "management", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"Australian Commission on Safety and Quality in Health Care. Open disclosure (Australian Open Disclosure Framework)\", \"url\": \"https://www.safetyandquality.gov.au/open-disclosure\"}; {\"name\": \"ACSQHC. Open disclosure: principles, elements and process\", \"url\": \"https://www.safetyandquality.gov.au/sites/default/files/2021-09/open-disclosure-principles-elements-and-process_9.pdf\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "Open disclosure should begin promptly with the facts that are known. Waiting for the final review damages trust. Further information is shared as it becomes available.", "C": "The framework specifically includes saying ''sorry''. Apology laws protect expressions of regret, and not saying it undermines trust.", "D": "Disclosure is a team and organisational responsibility, led by a senior clinician. Leaving an individual staff member to disclose alone is inappropriate and does not support staff.", "E": "Doctors may seek advice, but contacting an insurer is not a prerequisite for open disclosure, and it should not delay communication with the patient."}, "key_takeaways": ["Open disclosure: prompt, senior-led, includes saying ''sorry'', gives the known facts without speculation or blame, listens to the patient and family, and explains the next steps and follow-up. Apology laws protect expressions of regret."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,0.0,1.0,'tier_prior','tier=3 (bank170)'),
('b9d756a8-e9d9-560c-9a4d-190031dd6dfd'::uuid,'ZQ-0244','population-health-ethics','ethics-law','An 81-year-old man with severe COPD (FEV1 28% predicted, home oxygen) is admitted to a hospital in Geelong, Victoria, with pneumonia and hypercapnic respiratory failure. He is on non-invasive ventilation and IV antibiotics. Over the past 6 hours he has become drowsy and no longer has capacity to make treatment decisions.

A year ago, after an ICU admission, he completed an advance care directive with his GP. It is signed and properly witnessed. Its instructional directive states: ''I refuse intubation and mechanical ventilation and I refuse CPR. I consent to antibiotics, oxygen and mask ventilation (NIV) for a chest infection.'' His values directive says that being ''kept alive on machines'' is unacceptable to him. His daughter is his appointed medical treatment decision maker. She is distressed and asks the ICU registrar to intubate him, saying ''he didn''t really mean it, and I''m the one who decides now''. The ICU consultant agrees that intubation is a clinically reasonable option.

Which one of the following is the most appropriate course of action?','["Intubate him, because his appointed decision maker has consented to it", "Apply to VCAT and intubate him while waiting for its decision", "Intubate him for a 48-hour time-limited trial, then review the directive", "Follow the instructional directive: continue NIV and antibiotics without intubation", "Intubate him, because the directive was made before this illness began"]'::jsonb,'D','CORRECT ANSWER

D. Follow the instructional directive: continue NIV and antibiotics without intubation

---

Under the Victorian Medical Treatment Planning and Decisions Act 2016, an instructional directive is a legally binding statement of consent to, or refusal of, specified future medical treatment. It takes effect as if the person had made the decision themselves when they lack capacity. A medical treatment decision maker cannot override a relevant instructional directive; their role applies to decisions the directive does not cover, guided by the person''s values and preferences. His directive explicitly refuses intubation in exactly the kind of situation he anticipated (a chest infection with COPD). Health practitioners should therefore continue the treatments he consented to (NIV, antibiotics, oxygen) and provide comfort-focused care, while supporting his daughter compassionately. The Act allows a practitioner to not follow an instructional directive only in narrow circumstances (e.g. circumstances have changed so much that the person would not have wanted it to apply), and then an application can be made to VCAT. Those circumstances do not apply here.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Victoria: a valid instructional directive is legally binding; the medical treatment decision maker cannot override it. They decide only matters the directive does not cover, guided by the person''s values.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "Office of the Public Advocate (Victoria). Interpreting an instructional directive", "url": "https://www.publicadvocate.vic.gov.au/medical-treatment/patient-consent/interpreting-an-instructional-directive"}
• {"name": "Victorian Department of Health. Medical Treatment Planning and Decisions Act 2016", "url": "https://www.health.vic.gov.au/advance-care-planning/medical-treatment-planning-and-decisions-act-2016"}

---

EDITED BY HEISENBERG','difficult','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "bank170", "review_status": "owner_approved_2026_09_29", "tags": ["ZQ-0244", "zyntra_bank", "mock1", "level_4", "task_management", "group_population_health_ethics", "sys_ethics_law", "approved_by_owner_2026_09_29"], "clinician_task": "management", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"Office of the Public Advocate (Victoria). Interpreting an instructional directive\", \"url\": \"https://www.publicadvocate.vic.gov.au/medical-treatment/patient-consent/interpreting-an-instructional-directive\"}; {\"name\": \"Victorian Department of Health. Medical Treatment Planning and Decisions Act 2016\", \"url\": \"https://www.health.vic.gov.au/advance-care-planning/medical-treatment-planning-and-decisions-act-2016\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "A medical treatment decision maker cannot consent to treatment that the person has refused in a valid instructional directive.", "B": "VCAT can be asked to review a directive in limited circumstances, but there is no reason to doubt this one, and giving the refused treatment while waiting would override it.", "C": "A ''trial'' of intubation is still intubation, which he has specifically refused.", "E": "Advance care directives are made precisely to cover future illness. This situation is the one he anticipated."}, "key_takeaways": ["Victoria: a valid instructional directive is legally binding; the medical treatment decision maker cannot override it. They decide only matters the directive does not cover, guided by the person''s values."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,1.0,1.0,'tier_prior','tier=4 (bank170)'),
('c3556df6-03c6-5ca8-b78d-ed0147ff49c2'::uuid,'ZQ-0245','population-health-ethics','ethics-law','A general practice in Adelaide, South Australia, is reviewing why very few of its patients are recorded as Aboriginal or Torres Strait Islander, even though the local population includes a sizeable Aboriginal community. The audit finds that the Indigenous status field is blank for 38% of patients. Reception staff explain that they ''only ask people who look Aboriginal'', because they worry that asking everyone might offend people. One of the GPs says she usually works it out from a patient''s surname, family connections or appearance, and records it herself.

As a result, eligible patients are missing out on annual Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander health assessments, follow-up services and other programs linked to Indigenous status. The practice manager asks you, the GP registrar, to recommend a change to practice.

Which one of the following is the most appropriate way for the practice to collect this information?','["Ask every patient the standard Indigenous status question and record the answer they give", "Ask the question of patients whose appearance or surname suggests an Aboriginal background", "Have the GP record Indigenous status from family knowledge, to avoid offending patients", "Ask for a confirmation of Aboriginality letter before recording a patient as Aboriginal", "Record Indigenous status as ''not stated'' unless the patient raises it themselves"]'::jsonb,'A','CORRECT ANSWER

A. Ask every patient the standard Indigenous status question and record the answer they give

---

The AIHW National best practice guidelines for collecting Indigenous status in health data sets recommend that ALL patients are asked the standard question, ''Are you of Aboriginal or Torres Strait Islander origin?'', with the response options No / Yes, Aboriginal / Yes, Torres Strait Islander / Yes, both Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander. Staff should never guess or assume based on appearance, surname, country of birth or family knowledge. Self-identification is what is recorded, and people can choose not to answer. Explaining why the question is asked (to provide appropriate care and access to services) makes it more acceptable. Accurate identification improves access to culturally appropriate care, including Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander health assessments and follow-up, and improves population health data for planning. It is part of culturally safe practice under the Medical Board code and the RACGP/NACCHO National Guide.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Ask every patient ''Are you of Aboriginal or Torres Strait Islander origin?'' and record their self-identified answer. Never assume from appearance or surname.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "AIHW. National best practice guidelines for collecting Indigenous status in health data sets", "url": "https://www.aihw.gov.au/getmedia/ad54c4a7-4e03-4604-a0f3-ccb13c6d4260/11052.pdf"}
• {"name": "AIHW METEOR. Person\u2014Indigenous status, code N", "url": "https://meteor.aihw.gov.au/content/291036"}

---

EDITED BY HEISENBERG','difficult','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "bank170", "review_status": "owner_approved_2026_09_29", "tags": ["ZQ-0245", "zyntra_bank", "mock1", "level_4", "task_data_gathering", "group_population_health_ethics", "sys_ethics_law", "approved_by_owner_2026_09_29"], "clinician_task": "data_gathering", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"AIHW. National best practice guidelines for collecting Indigenous status in health data sets\", \"url\": \"https://www.aihw.gov.au/getmedia/ad54c4a7-4e03-4604-a0f3-ccb13c6d4260/11052.pdf\"}; {\"name\": \"AIHW METEOR. Person\\u2014Indigenous status, code N\", \"url\": \"https://meteor.aihw.gov.au/content/291036\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"B": "Selective asking based on appearance or surname is inaccurate (many Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people are missed) and can be experienced as discriminatory.", "C": "Clinicians should not assign Indigenous status on a patient''s behalf. It is based on self-identification.", "D": "Confirmation of Aboriginality documents are used for some specific purposes (such as some jobs or programs); they are not needed to record Indigenous status in health care, and requiring them is a barrier to care.", "E": "Waiting for patients to volunteer the information leads to under-identification and missed care."}, "key_takeaways": ["Ask every patient ''Are you of Aboriginal or Torres Strait Islander origin?'' and record their self-identified answer. Never assume from appearance or surname."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,1.0,1.0,'tier_prior','tier=4 (bank170)'),
('5b7bebbc-85c1-53a6-a7b3-9a3f8aa8120e'::uuid,'ZQ-0246','population-health-ethics','ethics-law','A 32-year-old woman sees her general practitioner in Orange, New South Wales, with bruising on her upper arms and neck. After gentle questioning she says that her partner ''grabbed me by the throat and squeezed until I nearly blacked out'' during an argument 4 days ago. Their children, aged 4 and 7, were in the room and screamed at him to stop. She says he has pushed her before, has been drinking more since losing his job, and last night threatened that she would ''never see the kids again'' if she left.

She does not want the police involved and asks the GP not to tell anyone, because she fears it will ''make things worse''. She plans to stay with her sister for a few nights. The children are at school today. The GP has assessed her injuries, documented them and discussed safety options and 1800RESPECT.

Which one of the following best describes the GP''s obligation regarding the children?','["Keep the disclosure confidential, because the adult victim has refused consent to share it", "Notify police rather than child protection, because the violence was directed at her", "Report to the Child Protection Helpline if the Mandatory Reporter Guide indicates risk of significant harm", "Report to child protection if she gives written consent, because otherwise it breaches her privacy", "Advise her to leave the relationship and review the children at the next scheduled visit"]'::jsonb,'C','CORRECT ANSWER

C. Report to the Child Protection Helpline if the Mandatory Reporter Guide indicates risk of significant harm

---

In NSW, doctors are mandatory reporters under the Children and Young Persons (Care and Protection) Act 1998. They must report to the Child Protection Helpline (13 21 11), or through the online ChildStory Reporter, when they have reasonable grounds to suspect a child is at risk of significant harm (ROSH). Exposure to domestic and family violence that causes or is likely to cause serious physical or psychological harm is one of the ROSH circumstances. The Mandatory Reporter Guide helps decide whether the threshold is met and what to do if it is not (e.g. referral to a Child Wellbeing Unit or support services). Non-fatal strangulation is a strong predictor of future lethal violence, the children witnessed it, and there are escalating threats, so a report is very likely to be required. Consent is not needed for a mandatory report. Where it is safe, the GP should tell her about the report and involve her in safety planning, and should call police (000) if anyone is in immediate danger.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
NSW: doctors must report reasonable suspicion of risk of significant harm (including DFV exposure) to the Child Protection Helpline (13 21 11); use the Mandatory Reporter Guide. Consent is not required; strangulation is a major lethality red flag.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "NSW Department of Communities and Justice. Mandatory reporting (NSW Interagency Guidelines)", "url": "https://dcj.nsw.gov.au/service-providers/deliver-services-to-children-and-families/nsw-interagency-guidelines-for-practitioners/reporting-responding-wellbeing-and-safety-concerns/mandatory-reporting.html"}
• {"name": "Children and Young Persons (Care and Protection) Act 1998 (NSW)", "url": "https://legislation.nsw.gov.au/view/whole/html/inforce/current/act-1998-157"}

---

EDITED BY HEISENBERG','difficult','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "bank170", "review_status": "owner_approved_2026_09_29", "tags": ["ZQ-0246", "zyntra_bank", "mock1", "level_4", "task_data_interpretation_synthesis", "group_population_health_ethics", "sys_ethics_law", "approved_by_owner_2026_09_29"], "clinician_task": "data_interpretation_synthesis", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"NSW Department of Communities and Justice. Mandatory reporting (NSW Interagency Guidelines)\", \"url\": \"https://dcj.nsw.gov.au/service-providers/deliver-services-to-children-and-families/nsw-interagency-guidelines-for-practitioners/reporting-responding-wellbeing-and-safety-concerns/mandatory-reporting.html\"}; {\"name\": \"Children and Young Persons (Care and Protection) Act 1998 (NSW)\", \"url\": \"https://legislation.nsw.gov.au/view/whole/html/inforce/current/act-1998-157\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "The mother''s wishes carry weight, but they do not override the mandatory reporting obligation for children at ROSH. Confidentiality is legally limited here.", "B": "Police may need to be involved if there is immediate danger, but the obligation regarding the children is a report to child protection. Children exposed to DFV are themselves at risk of harm.", "D": "Mandatory reports do not need the parent''s consent, and reporters acting in good faith are legally protected.", "E": "Advising her to leave does not meet the legal obligation, and separation can be a time of increased danger. Waiting until the next visit is unsafe."}, "key_takeaways": ["NSW: doctors must report reasonable suspicion of risk of significant harm (including DFV exposure) to the Child Protection Helpline (13 21 11); use the Mandatory Reporter Guide. Consent is not required; strangulation is a major lethality red flag."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,1.0,1.0,'tier_prior','tier=4 (bank170)'),
('30cc00b4-4a12-5cb2-bc50-352429b5ac04'::uuid,'ZQ-0247','population-health-ethics','ethics-law','A 36-year-old single general practitioner works in one of two practices in Esperance, Western Australia. For 2 years she has treated a 38-year-old man, also single, for depression and anxiety, including regular reviews under a Mental Health Treatment Plan. His mood has been stable for 6 months. At the end of his latest review he thanks her for ''getting him through the worst year of his life'' and asks whether she would like to go out for dinner. She finds him attractive and has run into him socially at community events.

She is unsure how to respond.

Which one of the following is the most appropriate response?','["Accept, because they are consenting adults in a small town who meet socially anyway", "Transfer his care to the other practice first, and then accept the invitation", "Accept once 6 months have passed since his last consultation with her", "Continue as his GP and see him socially, keeping it separate from his care", "Decline kindly, keep the professional relationship, and document and seek advice"]'::jsonb,'E','CORRECT ANSWER

E. Decline kindly, keep the professional relationship, and document and seek advice

---

The Medical Board of Australia''s guidelines ''Sexual boundaries in the doctor–patient relationship'' state that it is never appropriate for a doctor to engage in a sexual relationship with a current patient. Ending the professional relationship in order to start a relationship is not acceptable. For former patients, a relationship may still be unethical depending on the power imbalance, the patient''s vulnerability, the nature and length of care, and the use of knowledge gained in the doctor–patient relationship. Long-term psychological care creates a lasting power imbalance, so a future relationship would be very likely to breach the guidelines. She should decline kindly and clearly, maintain professional care if it remains appropriate, document the interaction, and seek advice from a colleague or her medical defence organisation. She should consider transferring his care if she cannot keep objectivity. Small rural communities need extra attention to boundaries, but the obligations are the same.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Sexual relationships with current patients are prohibited. Ending care to start a relationship is not acceptable. Relationships with former patients (especially after psychological care) are often unethical, because of lasting power imbalance.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "Medical Board of Australia. Guidelines: Sexual boundaries in the doctor-patient relationship (2018)", "url": "https://www.medicalboard.gov.au/Codes-Guidelines-Policies/Sexual-boundaries-guidelines"}

---

EDITED BY HEISENBERG','moderate','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "bank170", "review_status": "owner_approved_2026_09_29", "tags": ["ZQ-0247", "zyntra_bank", "mock1", "level_3", "task_management", "group_population_health_ethics", "sys_ethics_law", "approved_by_owner_2026_09_29"], "clinician_task": "management", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"Medical Board of Australia. Guidelines: Sexual boundaries in the doctor-patient relationship (2018)\", \"url\": \"https://www.medicalboard.gov.au/Codes-Guidelines-Policies/Sexual-boundaries-guidelines\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "A relationship with a current patient is prohibited regardless of consent or community size.", "B": "Ending care in order to start a relationship is specifically identified as inappropriate. The power imbalance from 2 years of mental health care persists.", "C": "There is no ''safe'' waiting period. Time alone does not remove the power imbalance, especially after psychological care.", "D": "A romantic relationship alongside ongoing care is a clear boundary violation."}, "key_takeaways": ["Sexual relationships with current patients are prohibited. Ending care to start a relationship is not acceptable. Relationships with former patients (especially after psychological care) are often unethical, because of lasting power imbalance."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,0.0,1.0,'tier_prior','tier=3 (bank170)'),
('a0676179-45b1-5047-b021-c0933043f888'::uuid,'ZQ-0248','population-health-ethics','ethics-law','Dr Tran is a general practitioner in Brisbane, Queensland. During a busy month the following situations arise:

- His brother, who lives nearby, asks him for a repeat prescription for temazepam because he ''can''t get in to see his own GP''.
- At a family picnic in a park, his 9-year-old niece, who has a known peanut allergy, develops lip swelling, wheeze and hypotension after eating a biscuit. Her parents are frozen, and the ambulance is 15 minutes away.
- He notices he feels tired, orders his own thyroid function tests through his practice, and increases his own thyroxine dose.
- His wife asks him to excise a changing mole from her back in his rooms after hours to save her a wait.
- His 24-year-old son asks for a medical certificate to cover two days off work for ''a bad cold''.

Which one of the following actions is consistent with the Medical Board of Australia''s code of conduct?','["Writing a repeat temazepam prescription for his brother", "Giving his niece her adrenaline autoinjector and starting first aid", "Ordering his own thyroid tests and adjusting his own thyroxine", "Excising the mole from his wife''s back in his rooms after hours", "Writing a medical certificate for his son''s sick leave"]'::jsonb,'B','CORRECT ANSWER

B. Giving his niece her adrenaline autoinjector and starting first aid

---

''Good medical practice: a code of conduct for doctors in Australia'' advises doctors to avoid providing medical care to themselves and to anyone with whom they have a close personal relationship. Exceptions are minor or emergency situations where no other care is available, and then care should be handed to another practitioner as soon as possible. The code specifically says doctors should not prescribe Schedule 8, psychotropic and/or drugs of dependence, or perform elective surgery, for themselves or people close to them. Giving adrenaline to a child with anaphylaxis while awaiting an ambulance is emergency care where no other practitioner is available, and it is expected of any doctor. Doctors should also have their own GP rather than self-treat, and certificates for family members compromise objectivity.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
MBA code: avoid treating yourself and people close to you. Never prescribe S8/psychotropic drugs or do elective procedures for them. Emergency care when no one else is available is the exception.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "Medical Board of Australia. Good medical practice: a code of conduct for doctors in Australia (2020)", "url": "https://www.medicalboard.gov.au/Codes-Guidelines-Policies/Code-of-conduct.aspx"}

---

EDITED BY HEISENBERG','difficult','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "bank170", "review_status": "owner_approved_2026_09_29", "tags": ["ZQ-0248", "zyntra_bank", "mock1", "level_4", "task_data_interpretation_synthesis", "group_population_health_ethics", "sys_ethics_law", "approved_by_owner_2026_09_29"], "clinician_task": "data_interpretation_synthesis", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"Medical Board of Australia. Good medical practice: a code of conduct for doctors in Australia (2020)\", \"url\": \"https://www.medicalboard.gov.au/Codes-Guidelines-Policies/Code-of-conduct.aspx\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "Temazepam is a psychotropic drug of dependence. The code advises against prescribing such drugs for people in a close personal relationship.", "C": "Self-treatment, including ordering one''s own tests and adjusting one''s own medicines, is discouraged. Doctors should have their own independent GP.", "D": "Elective procedures on close family members are specifically discouraged. Objectivity, consent and follow-up are compromised.", "E": "Certifying a family member''s illness lacks objectivity and is inappropriate. His son should see his own doctor."}, "key_takeaways": ["MBA code: avoid treating yourself and people close to you. Never prescribe S8/psychotropic drugs or do elective procedures for them. Emergency care when no one else is available is the exception."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,1.0,1.0,'tier_prior','tier=4 (bank170)'),
('1a1d93c4-645b-5ee5-8e20-30e6878ef3be'::uuid,'ZQ-0249','population-health-ethics','epidemiology','Researchers in New South Wales conducted a case-control study of solarium (sunbed) use and melanoma in adults aged 18–40 years. They recruited 200 people with newly diagnosed melanoma from the state cancer registry and 400 controls without melanoma, frequency-matched for age and sex, from the electoral roll. Participants completed a telephone interview about past sunbed use.

Results:
- Cases: 80 of 200 (40%) reported ever using a sunbed
- Controls: 80 of 400 (20%) reported ever using a sunbed
- Crude odds ratio 2.67 (95% confidence interval 1.83–3.88)

The analysis was not adjusted for skin type, number of naevi or history of sunburn.

Which one of the following is the most accurate interpretation of these results?','["The odds of sunbed use were about 2.7 times higher in cases; confounding is possible", "Sunbed use causes melanoma, because the confidence interval does not include 1", "The result is not statistically significant, because the confidence interval is wide", "The incidence of melanoma among sunbed users in this study was 40%", "The relative risk of melanoma calculated directly from these data is 2.0"]'::jsonb,'A','CORRECT ANSWER

A. The odds of sunbed use were about 2.7 times higher in cases; confounding is possible

---

OR = (80 × 320) ÷ (120 × 80) = 25 600 ÷ 9600 ≈ 2.67. In a case-control study the odds ratio compares the odds of exposure in cases with the odds in controls; mathematically this equals the odds ratio for disease. Because melanoma is relatively rare, it approximates the relative risk. The 95% CI (1.83–3.88) excludes 1, so the association is statistically significant at the 5% level. However, an association does not prove causation. This crude estimate was not adjusted for important potential confounders (fair skin, naevi, sunburn history, which are linked to both sunbed use and melanoma), and case-control studies are prone to recall and selection bias. Incidence and relative risk cannot be calculated directly from a case-control design, because the investigators fixed the numbers of cases and controls.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Case-control studies give odds ratios (≈ RR if the disease is rare), not incidence or RR. A CI excluding 1 means statistical significance, not causation; consider confounding and bias.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "Australian Prescriber. Interpreting risks and ratios in therapy trials", "url": "https://australianprescriber.tg.org.au/articles/interpreting-risks-and-ratios-in-therapy-trials.html"}

---

EDITED BY HEISENBERG','difficult','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "bank170", "review_status": "owner_approved_2026_09_29", "tags": ["ZQ-0249", "zyntra_bank", "mock1", "level_4", "task_data_interpretation_synthesis", "group_population_health_ethics", "sys_epidemiology", "approved_by_owner_2026_09_29"], "clinician_task": "data_interpretation_synthesis", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"Australian Prescriber. Interpreting risks and ratios in therapy trials\", \"url\": \"https://australianprescriber.tg.org.au/articles/interpreting-risks-and-ratios-in-therapy-trials.html\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"B": "Statistical significance shows the association is unlikely to be due to chance alone; it does not establish causation, particularly with unadjusted confounding and possible recall bias.", "C": "The CI excludes 1, so the result is statistically significant. Width reflects precision, not significance.", "D": "40% is the proportion of CASES who used sunbeds, not the incidence of melanoma among users. Incidence cannot be derived from a case-control study.", "E": "Risks are not directly calculable in case-control studies. 2.0 is just the ratio of the exposure proportions (40% ÷ 20%), not a relative risk of disease."}, "key_takeaways": ["Case-control studies give odds ratios (≈ RR if the disease is rare), not incidence or RR. A CI excluding 1 means statistical significance, not causation; consider confounding and bias."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,1.0,1.0,'tier_prior','tier=4 (bank170)'),
('d12b55d9-1193-5c3c-9fd9-a091f4fed795'::uuid,'ZQ-0250','population-health-ethics','screening','A 29-year-old registered nurse at a hospital in Darwin, Northern Territory, sustains a deep needlestick injury with a hollow-bore needle while taking blood from a patient known to have chronic hepatitis B (HBsAg positive, HBeAg negative). The source patient is HIV and hepatitis C antibody negative on testing done this week. She washed the site immediately with soap and water and reported to the staff health service within 30 minutes.

Her staff health record shows she completed a three-dose hepatitis B vaccine course 9 years ago as a nursing student, and her anti-HBs level measured 6 weeks after the third dose was 184 mIU/mL. A rapid anti-HBs test done today is below 10 mIU/mL. She is well, is not pregnant and has no other medical conditions. She is anxious and asks whether she needs ''the injection and another vaccine''.

Which one of the following best interprets her hepatitis B immune status and need for prophylaxis?','["Immunity has been lost; she needs immunoglobulin and a booster dose", "She is partially immune; she needs a booster dose but no immunoglobulin", "She is immune; no immunoglobulin or booster dose is needed", "She is a vaccine non-responder; she needs a repeat full vaccine course", "Her status is uncertain until anti-HBs is rechecked in 1 month"]'::jsonb,'C','CORRECT ANSWER

C. She is immune; no immunoglobulin or booster dose is needed

---

According to the Australian Immunisation Handbook, a person who has had a documented anti-HBs level of 10 mIU/mL or more at any time after completing a primary hepatitis B vaccine course is considered immune and does not need hepatitis B immunoglobulin, booster doses or further hepatitis B testing after an exposure, even if the current anti-HBs level has fallen below 10. Immune memory persists and gives protection despite waning antibody levels. She had a documented response (184 mIU/mL), so no hepatitis B post-exposure prophylaxis is needed. The rest of the occupational exposure pathway continues: baseline testing, counselling, and assessment for HIV and HCV risk according to the local protocol (here the source is negative for both), incident reporting and follow-up.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Hepatitis B exposure: a documented anti-HBs ≥10 mIU/mL at any time after a primary course means immune, so no HBIG, booster or HBV testing is needed, even if the current level is low.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "Australian Immunisation Handbook. Hepatitis B", "url": "https://immunisationhandbook.health.gov.au/contents/vaccine-preventable-diseases/hepatitis-b"}
• {"name": "Queensland Health. Management of occupational exposure to blood and body fluids (guideline)", "url": "https://www.health.qld.gov.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0032/1398191/occ-exposure-blood-body-fluids.pdf"}

---

EDITED BY HEISENBERG','difficult','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "bank170", "review_status": "owner_approved_2026_09_29", "tags": ["ZQ-0250", "zyntra_bank", "mock1", "level_4", "task_data_interpretation_synthesis", "group_population_health_ethics", "sys_screening", "approved_by_owner_2026_09_29"], "clinician_task": "data_interpretation_synthesis", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"Australian Immunisation Handbook. Hepatitis B\", \"url\": \"https://immunisationhandbook.health.gov.au/contents/vaccine-preventable-diseases/hepatitis-b\"}; {\"name\": \"Queensland Health. Management of occupational exposure to blood and body fluids (guideline)\", \"url\": \"https://www.health.qld.gov.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0032/1398191/occ-exposure-blood-body-fluids.pdf\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "A fall in anti-HBs below 10 years after a documented response does not mean lost immunity; immune memory persists. HBIG plus vaccine is for people who are non-immune or whose response is unknown.", "B": "There is no ''partial immunity'' category requiring a booster after a documented response. Boosters are not recommended for documented responders.", "D": "A non-responder is someone whose anti-HBs was <10 mIU/mL after a primary course. She had 184 mIU/mL, so she is a responder.", "E": "Her documented post-vaccination response already establishes immunity. Rechecking anti-HBs adds nothing and would not change management."}, "key_takeaways": ["Hepatitis B exposure: a documented anti-HBs ≥10 mIU/mL at any time after a primary course means immune, so no HBIG, booster or HBV testing is needed, even if the current level is low."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,1.0,1.0,'tier_prior','tier=4 (bank170)'),
('69cb5c87-3aa1-5d19-a078-b4e380c87add'::uuid,'ZQ-0251','adult-medicine','renal','A 71-year-old man with stage 4 chronic kidney disease is brought to the emergency department in Wollongong, NSW, with generalised weakness for one day. He recently started spironolactone for heart failure and also takes ramipril. He is alert. His pulse is 52/min and regular and his blood pressure is 118/70 mmHg.

Investigations:
- Serum potassium 7.4 mmol/L (3.5–5.2)
- Serum creatinine 310 µmol/L (60–110)
- Venous pH 7.31

A 12-lead ECG shows tall peaked T waves, flattened P waves and a QRS duration of 150 ms. Spironolactone and ramipril are withheld.

Which one of the following is the most appropriate immediate treatment?','["Intravenous calcium gluconate 10%", "Intravenous insulin with glucose alone", "Oral sodium zirconium cyclosilicate", "Intravenous sodium bicarbonate 8.4%", "Intravenous furosemide"]'::jsonb,'A','CORRECT ANSWER

A. Intravenous calcium gluconate 10%

---

With ECG changes (peaked T waves, loss of P waves, broad QRS), the first priority is to stabilise the myocardial membrane with IV calcium (calcium gluconate 10% or calcium chloride). It acts within minutes but does not lower potassium, so it is followed straight away by insulin with glucose (with or without nebulised salbutamol) to shift potassium into cells, then measures to remove potassium (binders, dialysis). Repeat the ECG after calcium; the dose can be repeated if changes persist.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Hyperkalaemia with ECG changes: IV calcium first to stabilise the myocardium, then insulin–glucose to shift potassium, then remove it.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "Therapeutic Guidelines (eTG complete)", "url": "https://www.tg.org.au"}','easy','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "batch01", "review_status": "draft_pending_owner_review", "tags": ["ZQ-0251", "zyntra_bank", "batch01", "level_2", "task_management", "group_adult_medicine", "sys_renal", "irtb_-1.30", "pending_owner_review"], "clinician_task": "management", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"Therapeutic Guidelines (eTG complete)\", \"url\": \"https://www.tg.org.au\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"B": "Insulin–glucose shifts potassium into cells within 15–30 minutes and should be given next, but it does not protect the heart immediately. With a broad QRS, calcium comes first.", "C": "A potassium binder lowers total body potassium over hours. Too slow for life-threatening ECG changes.", "D": "Bicarbonate has a weak, unreliable effect on potassium and is not first-line, even with mild acidosis.", "E": "A loop diuretic can increase potassium excretion when kidney function allows, but it is slow and unreliable in stage 4 CKD."}, "key_takeaways": ["Hyperkalaemia with ECG changes: IV calcium first to stabilise the myocardium, then insulin–glucose to shift potassium, then remove it."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,-1.3,0.5,'expert_prior','author_estimate (batch01)'),
('0ddad721-2b7e-555d-bde3-b480d964bca5'::uuid,'ZQ-0252','adult-medicine','endocrinology','A 34-year-old woman is brought to a regional emergency department in Dubbo, NSW, with 3 days of vomiting, abdominal pain and dizziness. Over the past 4 months she has lost 6 kg and felt tired, and her partner says her skin has darkened even though it is winter. She has hypothyroidism treated with levothyroxine and takes no other medicines.

Her pulse is 118/min and her blood pressure is 82/50 mmHg lying. She has hyperpigmented palmar creases and buccal mucosa.

Investigations:
- Serum sodium 124 mmol/L (135–145)
- Serum potassium 5.9 mmol/L (3.5–5.2)
- Blood glucose 3.4 mmol/L
- Urea 12.1 mmol/L (2.5–8.0)

Two litres of 0.9% sodium chloride have been started. Blood has been taken for cortisol and ACTH.

Which one of the following is the most appropriate next step?','["Perform a short Synacthen (250 µg tetracosactide) test before giving steroids", "Give fludrocortisone 100 micrograms orally", "Give dexamethasone 4 mg intravenously as the only steroid", "Give hydrocortisone 100 mg intravenously now", "Arrange an urgent CT scan of the adrenal glands"]'::jsonb,'D','CORRECT ANSWER

D. Give hydrocortisone 100 mg intravenously now

---

This is an adrenal crisis from probable primary adrenal insufficiency (autoimmune Addison disease, with another autoimmune condition): hypotension, hyponatraemia, hyperkalaemia, hypoglycaemia and hyperpigmentation. Treatment must not wait for confirmation. Once blood for cortisol and ACTH has been taken, give hydrocortisone 100 mg IV immediately, with IV 0.9% saline and glucose, then hydrocortisone 50 mg 6-hourly (or by infusion). At this dose hydrocortisone has enough mineralocorticoid effect, so fludrocortisone is not needed acutely. The diagnosis can be confirmed later.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Suspected adrenal crisis: take blood for cortisol/ACTH, then give IV hydrocortisone 100 mg immediately. Never delay for a Synacthen test.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "Therapeutic Guidelines (eTG complete)", "url": "https://www.tg.org.au"}
• {"name": "Endocrine Society. Diagnosis and treatment of primary adrenal insufficiency (Bornstein et al., J Clin Endocrinol Metab 2016)", "url": "https://doi.org/10.1210/jc.2015-1710"}','difficult','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "batch01", "review_status": "draft_pending_owner_review", "tags": ["ZQ-0252", "zyntra_bank", "batch01", "level_4", "task_management", "group_adult_medicine", "sys_endocrine", "irtb_+1.20", "pending_owner_review"], "clinician_task": "management", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"Therapeutic Guidelines (eTG complete)\", \"url\": \"https://www.tg.org.au\"}; {\"name\": \"Endocrine Society. Diagnosis and treatment of primary adrenal insufficiency (Bornstein et al., J Clin Endocrinol Metab 2016)\", \"url\": \"https://doi.org/10.1210/jc.2015-1710\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "The Synacthen test confirms adrenal insufficiency, but delaying treatment in a crisis can be fatal. Treat first; the random cortisol and ACTH are already taken and confirmation can follow.", "B": "Fludrocortisone is long-term mineralocorticoid replacement. It is not needed acutely because high-dose hydrocortisone provides mineralocorticoid activity, and she is vomiting.", "C": "Dexamethasone has no mineralocorticoid activity and is not the preferred treatment of adrenal crisis with hyperkalaemia and hyponatraemia.", "E": "Imaging may be done later to look for a cause but has no role while she is in shock."}, "key_takeaways": ["Suspected adrenal crisis: take blood for cortisol/ACTH, then give IV hydrocortisone 100 mg immediately. Never delay for a Synacthen test."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,1.2,0.5,'expert_prior','author_estimate (batch01)'),
('a412e958-7677-5308-88fc-b45e4b21fb16'::uuid,'ZQ-0253','adult-medicine','endocrinology','A 29-year-old woman sees her general practitioner in Hobart, Tasmania, because she has gained 14 kg over 18 months. She has new purple abdominal striae up to 1.5 cm wide, easy bruising and difficulty climbing stairs. She has taken a combined oral contraceptive pill containing ethinylestradiol for 8 years. She uses no topical, inhaled or oral glucocorticoids.

Her blood pressure is 154/96 mmHg and BMI 33 kg/m². She has facial plethora and proximal muscle weakness. Her HbA1c is 6.6%.

The GP suspects Cushing syndrome. She does not want to stop her pill.

Which one of the following is the most appropriate initial investigation?','["Overnight 1 mg dexamethasone suppression test", "Random morning serum cortisol", "Plasma ACTH", "Late-night salivary cortisol measured on two separate nights", "High-dose (8 mg) dexamethasone suppression test"]'::jsonb,'D','CORRECT ANSWER

D. Late-night salivary cortisol measured on two separate nights

---

Wide purple striae, easy bruising, proximal myopathy, plethora, new hypertension and dysglycaemia make Cushing syndrome a real possibility. First-line screening tests are the overnight 1 mg dexamethasone suppression test, late-night salivary cortisol (at least two) or 24-hour urinary free cortisol (at least two). Oral oestrogen raises cortisol-binding globulin, which raises total serum cortisol and causes false-positive dexamethasone suppression tests in a large proportion of women. Salivary cortisol measures free cortisol, is not affected by CBG, and is the best choice when she will not stop the pill (ideally the pill is stopped for about 6 weeks before a DST).

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Screen for Cushing syndrome with late-night salivary cortisol, 24-hour UFC or a 1 mg DST. Oral oestrogen causes false-positive DSTs by raising CBG.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "Endocrine Society. The diagnosis of Cushing''s syndrome (Nieman et al., J Clin Endocrinol Metab 2008)", "url": "https://doi.org/10.1210/jc.2008-0125"}
• {"name": "Therapeutic Guidelines (eTG complete)", "url": "https://www.tg.org.au"}','difficult','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "batch01", "review_status": "draft_pending_owner_review", "tags": ["ZQ-0253", "zyntra_bank", "batch01", "level_5", "task_data_gathering", "group_adult_medicine", "sys_endocrine", "irtb_+1.50", "pending_owner_review"], "clinician_task": "data_gathering", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"Endocrine Society. The diagnosis of Cushing''s syndrome (Nieman et al., J Clin Endocrinol Metab 2008)\", \"url\": \"https://doi.org/10.1210/jc.2008-0125\"}; {\"name\": \"Therapeutic Guidelines (eTG complete)\", \"url\": \"https://www.tg.org.au\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "Valid in most people, but oral oestrogen raises CBG and total serum cortisol, so false positives are common in women on the combined pill.", "B": "A single serum cortisol cannot separate Cushing syndrome from normal, because of diurnal variation and stress. It is not a screening test.", "C": "ACTH is used after hypercortisolism is confirmed, to separate ACTH-dependent from ACTH-independent causes.", "E": "This was used to localise the source in confirmed ACTH-dependent disease. It has no role in initial screening."}, "key_takeaways": ["Screen for Cushing syndrome with late-night salivary cortisol, 24-hour UFC or a 1 mg DST. Oral oestrogen causes false-positive DSTs by raising CBG."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,1.5,0.5,'expert_prior','author_estimate (batch01)'),
('c6399733-6ff8-5799-b10c-5254614d56a2'::uuid,'ZQ-0254','adult-medicine','gastroenterology','A 52-year-old woman sees her general practitioner in Toowoomba, Queensland, with 6 months of itch, worse at night, and tiredness. She has no rash, takes no medicines or supplements and rarely drinks alcohol. She has no inflammatory bowel disease. Examination shows excoriations but no jaundice or signs of chronic liver disease.

Investigations:
- ALP 412 U/L (30–110)
- GGT 380 U/L (<38)
- ALT 58 U/L (<35)
- Bilirubin 15 µmol/L (<20)
- Albumin 39 g/L (35–50)

Abdominal ultrasound shows normal liver texture, a normal gallbladder and no intrahepatic or extrahepatic bile duct dilatation.

Which one of the following tests is most likely to confirm the diagnosis?','["Serum anti-smooth muscle antibody", "Magnetic resonance cholangiopancreatography", "Serum ceruloplasmin", "Liver biopsy", "Serum antimitochondrial antibody"]'::jsonb,'E','CORRECT ANSWER

E. Serum antimitochondrial antibody

---

Pruritus and fatigue with a cholestatic pattern (ALP and GGT raised far more than ALT) and non-dilated ducts in a middle-aged woman suggests primary biliary cholangitis. A positive antimitochondrial antibody (M2) with a raised ALP is diagnostic in most cases without liver biopsy. Treatment is ursodeoxycholic acid.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Middle-aged woman, itch, cholestatic LFTs, normal ducts on ultrasound: test AMA for primary biliary cholangitis.

---

REFERENCES
