('9775aed9-fe9a-55b0-8c6c-b6f27b8bc5cf'::uuid,'ZQ-0255','adult-medicine','haematology-oncology','Two men see the same general practitioner in Bendigo, Victoria, about a raised ferritin found on routine blood tests. Neither has symptoms, and both drink fewer than 10 standard drinks a week.

Man 1 is 46, with a BMI of 33 kg/m², type 2 diabetes and fatty liver on ultrasound.
Man 2 is 44, with a BMI of 24 kg/m²; his father had ''liver trouble''.

Investigations (fasting):
| | Man 1 | Man 2 |
|---|---|---|
| Ferritin (30–500 µg/L) | 780 | 760 |
| Transferrin saturation (15–45%) | 28 | 71 |
| ALT (<40 U/L) | 64 | 52 |
| CRP (<5 mg/L) | 6 | 2 |

Which one of the following is the most appropriate interpretation?','["Both men most likely have haemochromatosis and should have HFE genotyping", "Man 1 most likely has haemochromatosis because he has diabetes and a raised ALT", "Man 2 most likely has HFE haemochromatosis and should have HFE genotyping", "Neither man needs further testing because their ferritin is below 1000 µg/L", "Both men should start venesection now and then be genotyped"]'::jsonb,'C','CORRECT ANSWER

C. Man 2 most likely has HFE haemochromatosis and should have HFE genotyping

---

Ferritin rises with iron overload but also with inflammation, alcohol, fatty liver and metabolic syndrome. Transferrin saturation separates these. A fasting transferrin saturation above 45% with a raised ferritin suggests iron overload, and HFE genotyping (C282Y, H63D) is the next step; Man 2 fits, with a possible family history. Man 1 has a normal transferrin saturation, so his ferritin most likely reflects metabolic dysfunction-associated fatty liver disease (dysmetabolic hyperferritinaemia). He does not need HFE testing or venesection; he needs management of weight and metabolic risk.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Raised ferritin: check fasting transferrin saturation. TSAT >45% suggests iron overload (HFE genotyping); normal TSAT points to inflammation, alcohol or fatty liver.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "Gastroenterological Society of Australia. Haemochromatosis clinical update", "url": "https://www.gesa.org.au/"}
• {"name": "Haemochromatosis Australia: information for health professionals", "url": "https://haemochromatosis.org.au/"}','difficult','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "batch01", "review_status": "draft_pending_owner_review", "tags": ["ZQ-0255", "zyntra_bank", "batch01", "level_4", "task_data_interpretation_synthesis", "group_adult_medicine", "sys_haematology_oncology", "irtb_+1.00", "pending_owner_review"], "clinician_task": "data_interpretation_synthesis", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"Gastroenterological Society of Australia. Haemochromatosis clinical update\", \"url\": \"https://www.gesa.org.au/\"}; {\"name\": \"Haemochromatosis Australia: information for health professionals\", \"url\": \"https://haemochromatosis.org.au/\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "A ferritin above 500 µg/L alone does not mean iron overload. Man 1''s normal transferrin saturation points away from haemochromatosis.", "B": "Diabetes and a raised ALT are better explained by fatty liver; his transferrin saturation is normal.", "D": "Ferritin below 1000 µg/L lowers the risk of advanced fibrosis in haemochromatosis but does not exclude it. Man 2 still needs genotyping.", "E": "Venesection follows a diagnosis of iron overload. Man 1 has no evidence of iron overload."}, "key_takeaways": ["Raised ferritin: check fasting transferrin saturation. TSAT >45% suggests iron overload (HFE genotyping); normal TSAT points to inflammation, alcohol or fatty liver."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,1.0,0.5,'expert_prior','author_estimate (batch01)'),
('6ae5f9a8-2662-564a-8401-7d4d58d75450'::uuid,'ZQ-0256','adult-medicine','infectious-diseases','A 58-year-old man sees his general practitioner in Mandurah, Western Australia, with 2 days of spreading redness and pain over his left shin after a gardening scratch. He feels well. He has type 2 diabetes treated with metformin. As a child he developed a mild itchy rash 4 days into a course of amoxicillin; it settled without treatment, with no blistering, mucosal involvement or organ involvement. He has had no penicillins since.

His temperature is 37.8 °C, pulse 84/min and blood pressure 134/82 mmHg. There is a 10 × 8 cm area of warm, tender, non-fluctuant erythema on the shin, with no crepitus or bullae, and pain in proportion to the signs. He has interdigital tinea of the left foot.

Which one of the following is the most appropriate antibiotic?','["Oral clindamycin", "Oral flucloxacillin", "Oral amoxicillin–clavulanate", "Intravenous cefazolin in hospital", "Oral cefalexin"]'::jsonb,'E','CORRECT ANSWER

E. Oral cefalexin

---

This is non-purulent cellulitis without systemic toxicity, suitable for oral treatment. First-line therapy targets streptococci and Staphylococcus aureus (flucloxacillin or cefalexin). His past reaction was a mild, delayed, non-severe rash; eTG advises that patients with a non-severe delayed penicillin reaction can usually be given a cephalosporin such as cefalexin. Clindamycin (or trimethoprim–sulfamethoxazole) is kept for immediate (IgE-mediated) or severe delayed reactions. The tinea should be treated as a portal of entry, the leg elevated, and penicillin allergy delabelling considered later.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Cellulitis with a past mild delayed penicillin rash: oral cefalexin. Reserve clindamycin for immediate or severe reactions.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "Therapeutic Guidelines (eTG complete)", "url": "https://www.tg.org.au"}
• {"name": "ASCIA. Penicillin allergy guide for health professionals", "url": "https://www.allergy.org.au/hp/papers/penicillin-allergy-guide"}','moderate','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "batch01", "review_status": "draft_pending_owner_review", "tags": ["ZQ-0256", "zyntra_bank", "batch01", "level_3", "task_management", "group_adult_medicine", "sys_infectious_disease", "irtb_+0.00", "pending_owner_review"], "clinician_task": "management", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"Therapeutic Guidelines (eTG complete)\", \"url\": \"https://www.tg.org.au\"}; {\"name\": \"ASCIA. Penicillin allergy guide for health professionals\", \"url\": \"https://www.allergy.org.au/hp/papers/penicillin-allergy-guide\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "Clindamycin is for immediate or severe penicillin hypersensitivity; it has more adverse effects, including C. difficile infection.", "B": "First-line without allergy, but with a documented penicillin reaction a cephalosporin is used for a non-severe delayed reaction unless he has been formally delabelled.", "C": "This is a penicillin and gives broader cover than cellulitis needs; it is used for bites and some diabetic foot infections.", "D": "He has no systemic toxicity, rapid spread or high-risk features, so oral treatment in the community is appropriate."}, "key_takeaways": ["Cellulitis with a past mild delayed penicillin rash: oral cefalexin. Reserve clindamycin for immediate or severe reactions."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,0.0,0.5,'expert_prior','author_estimate (batch01)'),
('0e18d78b-0f40-5fb2-81b6-f87e5a2f9601'::uuid,'ZQ-0257','adult-medicine','respiratory','A 31-year-old woman who moved from Sweden to Perth 2 years ago sees her general practitioner with 10 days of fever, painful swollen ankles and tender red lumps on both shins. She has no cough, weight loss or night sweats, no recent travel and no known tuberculosis contacts. She takes no medicines.

Her temperature is 37.9 °C. Both ankles are swollen with periarticular tenderness. There are several tender, raised, red-purple nodules 2–4 cm across on both anterior shins. Chest examination is normal, with no peripheral lymphadenopathy or hepatosplenomegaly.

Investigations:
- CRP 48 mg/L (<5)
- Corrected calcium 2.48 mmol/L (2.10–2.60)
- Chest X-ray: bilateral symmetrical hilar lymphadenopathy; clear lung fields
- Interferon-gamma release assay negative

Which one of the following is the most appropriate interpretation?','["Lymphoma; she needs an urgent biopsy of a mediastinal node", "Primary tuberculosis; she should start four-drug treatment", "Chronic pulmonary sarcoidosis needing high-dose oral prednisolone", "Löfgren syndrome (acute sarcoidosis); a biopsy is not needed and the outlook is good", "Reactive arthritis after a gastrointestinal infection"]'::jsonb,'D','CORRECT ANSWER

D. Löfgren syndrome (acute sarcoidosis); a biopsy is not needed and the outlook is good

---

Erythema nodosum, bilateral hilar lymphadenopathy, fever and ankle periarthritis is the classic picture of Löfgren syndrome, an acute form of sarcoidosis that is more common in women of Scandinavian descent. When the presentation is typical and there are no atypical features (weight loss, asymmetric or bulky nodes, a positive TB test), the combination is specific enough that biopsy is generally not needed. Most cases resolve spontaneously within 1–2 years. Treatment is supportive with NSAIDs; corticosteroids are rarely needed. A repeat chest X-ray confirms resolution.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Erythema nodosum + bilateral hilar nodes + fever + ankle periarthritis = Löfgren syndrome. A typical case needs no biopsy; NSAIDs and follow-up.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "ATS clinical practice guideline: diagnosis and detection of sarcoidosis (Crouser et al., Am J Respir Crit Care Med 2020)", "url": "https://doi.org/10.1164/rccm.202002-0251ST"}
• {"name": "ERS clinical practice guidelines on treatment of sarcoidosis (Baughman et al., Eur Respir J 2021)", "url": "https://doi.org/10.1183/13993003.04079-2020"}','difficult','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "batch01", "review_status": "draft_pending_owner_review", "tags": ["ZQ-0257", "zyntra_bank", "batch01", "level_4", "task_data_interpretation_synthesis", "group_adult_medicine", "sys_respiratory", "irtb_+1.30", "pending_owner_review"], "clinician_task": "data_interpretation_synthesis", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"ATS clinical practice guideline: diagnosis and detection of sarcoidosis (Crouser et al., Am J Respir Crit Care Med 2020)\", \"url\": \"https://doi.org/10.1164/rccm.202002-0251ST\"}; {\"name\": \"ERS clinical practice guidelines on treatment of sarcoidosis (Baughman et al., Eur Respir J 2021)\", \"url\": \"https://doi.org/10.1183/13993003.04079-2020\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "Lymphoma rarely causes this acute triad and usually gives asymmetric or bulky nodes and B symptoms.", "B": "She has no TB contacts, no respiratory symptoms and a negative IGRA. The full picture favours Löfgren syndrome.", "C": "Löfgren syndrome is acute and self-limiting with clear lungs; steroids are not routinely needed.", "E": "Reactive arthritis does not cause hilar lymphadenopathy, and there was no preceding diarrhoea or urethritis."}, "key_takeaways": ["Erythema nodosum + bilateral hilar nodes + fever + ankle periarthritis = Löfgren syndrome. A typical case needs no biopsy; NSAIDs and follow-up."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,1.3,0.5,'expert_prior','author_estimate (batch01)'),
('d29288b2-12eb-5777-90b4-1d156abe6599'::uuid,'ZQ-0258','adult-medicine','respiratory','A 69-year-old retired carpenter sees his general practitioner in Launceston, Tasmania, with 9 months of gradually worsening exertional breathlessness and a dry cough. He smoked 20 cigarettes a day for 25 years and stopped 15 years ago. He has no joint symptoms, rash, pets or birds, and takes no regular medicines.

His SpO2 is 95% at rest on room air. He has digital clubbing and fine ''velcro-like'' inspiratory crackles at both lung bases. There is no ankle oedema.

Investigations:
- Spirometry: FEV1 78% predicted, FVC 70% predicted, FEV1/FVC 0.84
- DLCO 52% predicted
- Chest X-ray: reticular shadowing at both bases

Which one of the following is the most appropriate next investigation?','["Surgical lung biopsy", "CT pulmonary angiogram", "High-resolution CT scan of the chest", "Bronchoscopy with bronchoalveolar lavage", "Repeat spirometry after a bronchodilator"]'::jsonb,'C','CORRECT ANSWER

C. High-resolution CT scan of the chest

---

Progressive exertional breathlessness, dry cough, clubbing, bibasal velcro crackles, a restrictive pattern (preserved FEV1/FVC with reduced FVC) and a low DLCO strongly suggest interstitial lung disease, most likely idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis in an older male ex-smoker. HRCT is the key investigation: a usual interstitial pneumonia pattern (subpleural basal reticulation, honeycombing, traction bronchiectasis) in the right clinical context, after excluding other causes, allows diagnosis without biopsy. He should be referred to a respiratory physician for multidisciplinary discussion and consideration of antifibrotic therapy.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Velcro crackles, clubbing, restriction and low DLCO suggest pulmonary fibrosis: arrange HRCT chest. A typical UIP pattern may avoid biopsy.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "ATS/ERS/JRS/ALAT guideline: Idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis (an update) and progressive pulmonary fibrosis in adults (Raghu et al., 2022)", "url": "https://doi.org/10.1164/rccm.202202-0399ST"}
• {"name": "Lung Foundation Australia: health professional resources", "url": "https://lungfoundation.com.au/health-professionals/"}','moderate','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "batch01", "review_status": "draft_pending_owner_review", "tags": ["ZQ-0258", "zyntra_bank", "batch01", "level_3", "task_data_gathering", "group_adult_medicine", "sys_respiratory", "irtb_+0.30", "pending_owner_review"], "clinician_task": "data_gathering", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"ATS/ERS/JRS/ALAT guideline: Idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis (an update) and progressive pulmonary fibrosis in adults (Raghu et al., 2022)\", \"url\": \"https://doi.org/10.1164/rccm.202202-0399ST\"}; {\"name\": \"Lung Foundation Australia: health professional resources\", \"url\": \"https://lungfoundation.com.au/health-professionals/\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "Biopsy is reserved for when HRCT and multidisciplinary review are not diagnostic; it carries real risk in IPF.", "B": "CTPA looks for pulmonary embolism, which does not explain months of progressive symptoms, crackles, clubbing and restriction.", "D": "BAL can help in some ILDs (such as hypersensitivity pneumonitis) but HRCT comes first.", "E": "Bronchodilator testing looks for reversible obstruction; his pattern is restrictive."}, "key_takeaways": ["Velcro crackles, clubbing, restriction and low DLCO suggest pulmonary fibrosis: arrange HRCT chest. A typical UIP pattern may avoid biopsy."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,0.3,0.5,'expert_prior','author_estimate (batch01)'),
('7ed1a6b5-0989-5adb-a983-838243299bb6'::uuid,'ZQ-0259','adult-medicine','cardiology','A 19-year-old university footballer sees a sports physician in Adelaide after feeling lightheaded during a sprint at training last week. He did not lose consciousness. His uncle died suddenly aged 32. He takes no medicines or supplements.

His pulse is 64/min and regular and blood pressure 122/74 mmHg. He has a grade 3/6 harsh ejection systolic murmur, loudest at the left lower sternal edge, that does not radiate to the carotids. His carotid pulse is brisk. An echocardiogram will be arranged, but the physician first examines the murmur further at the bedside.

Which one of the following findings would most support the likely diagnosis?','["The murmur becomes louder when he squats from standing", "The murmur becomes louder during the strain phase of a Valsalva manoeuvre", "The murmur becomes louder with sustained isometric handgrip", "The murmur radiates to both carotids with a slow-rising pulse", "The murmur becomes softer when he stands up from squatting"]'::jsonb,'B','CORRECT ANSWER

B. The murmur becomes louder during the strain phase of a Valsalva manoeuvre

---

Exertional presyncope, a family history of sudden death and a harsh systolic murmur at the left sternal edge with a brisk carotid pulse suggest hypertrophic cardiomyopathy with left ventricular outflow tract obstruction. The obstruction is dynamic: reducing preload (Valsalva strain, standing from squatting) makes the ventricle smaller and the murmur louder; increasing preload or afterload (squatting, passive leg raise, handgrip) makes it softer. This is the opposite of aortic stenosis. He should stop competitive sport until assessed and have an echocardiogram, ECG and cardiology review.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
HCM murmur: louder with less preload (Valsalva strain, standing), softer with more preload or afterload (squatting, handgrip). Opposite to aortic stenosis.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "2024 AHA/ACC/AMSSM/HRS/PACES/SCMR guideline for the management of hypertrophic cardiomyopathy (Circulation 2024)", "url": "https://doi.org/10.1161/CIR.0000000000001250"}
• {"name": "Heart Foundation (Australia): health professional resources", "url": "https://www.heartfoundation.org.au/for-professionals"}','moderate','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "batch01", "review_status": "draft_pending_owner_review", "tags": ["ZQ-0259", "zyntra_bank", "batch01", "level_3", "task_data_gathering", "group_adult_medicine", "sys_cardiovascular", "irtb_+0.40", "pending_owner_review"], "clinician_task": "data_gathering", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"2024 AHA/ACC/AMSSM/HRS/PACES/SCMR guideline for the management of hypertrophic cardiomyopathy (Circulation 2024)\", \"url\": \"https://doi.org/10.1161/CIR.0000000000001250\"}; {\"name\": \"Heart Foundation (Australia): health professional resources\", \"url\": \"https://www.heartfoundation.org.au/for-professionals\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "Squatting increases venous return and afterload, reducing obstruction, so the HCM murmur softens.", "C": "Handgrip raises afterload, which reduces the outflow gradient; it makes mitral regurgitation louder, not HCM.", "D": "A slow-rising pulse with carotid radiation suggests aortic stenosis. HCM gives a brisk, sometimes bifid, pulse.", "E": "Standing suddenly reduces preload and worsens obstruction, so the HCM murmur gets louder."}, "key_takeaways": ["HCM murmur: louder with less preload (Valsalva strain, standing), softer with more preload or afterload (squatting, handgrip). Opposite to aortic stenosis."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,0.4,0.5,'expert_prior','author_estimate (batch01)'),
('4326511d-41c9-5099-9ba4-4e45e2187447'::uuid,'ZQ-0260','adult-medicine','cardiology','A 26-year-old man presents to an emergency department in Canberra with 2 days of sharp central chest pain, worse lying flat and on deep breathing, and eased by sitting forward. He had a sore throat and runny nose a week ago. He has no past medical history.

His temperature is 37.6 °C, pulse 96/min and blood pressure 126/78 mmHg. A scratchy sound is heard at the left sternal edge with him leaning forward. His chest is clear.

The ECG shows concave ST elevation in I, II, aVL, aVF and V2–V6, with PR depression in the same leads and PR elevation in aVR, and no reciprocal ST depression. High-sensitivity troponin is normal on two samples.

Which one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?','["Acute anterior ST-elevation myocardial infarction", "Acute pericarditis", "Acute myocarditis", "Pulmonary embolism", "Benign early repolarisation"]'::jsonb,'B','CORRECT ANSWER

B. Acute pericarditis

---

Pleuritic, positional chest pain relieved by sitting forward, a friction rub, a preceding viral illness and widespread concave ST elevation with PR depression (PR elevation in aVR) without reciprocal change is typical of acute pericarditis. A normal troponin makes significant myocardial involvement unlikely. An echocardiogram looks for an effusion. First-line treatment is an NSAID (or aspirin) plus colchicine for 3 months, which reduces recurrence.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Pleuritic pain eased by sitting forward + rub + diffuse saddle ST elevation with PR depression = acute pericarditis. NSAID plus colchicine.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "2015 ESC Guidelines for the diagnosis and management of pericardial diseases (Eur Heart J 2015)", "url": "https://doi.org/10.1093/eurheartj/ehv318"}
• {"name": "Therapeutic Guidelines (eTG complete)", "url": "https://www.tg.org.au"}','easy','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "batch01", "review_status": "draft_pending_owner_review", "tags": ["ZQ-0260", "zyntra_bank", "batch01", "level_2", "task_data_interpretation_synthesis", "group_adult_medicine", "sys_cardiovascular", "irtb_-1.00", "pending_owner_review"], "clinician_task": "data_interpretation_synthesis", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"2015 ESC Guidelines for the diagnosis and management of pericardial diseases (Eur Heart J 2015)\", \"url\": \"https://doi.org/10.1093/eurheartj/ehv318\"}; {\"name\": \"Therapeutic Guidelines (eTG complete)\", \"url\": \"https://www.tg.org.au\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "STEMI gives convex ST elevation in a coronary territory with reciprocal depression and rising troponin.", "C": "Myocarditis would usually raise troponin and may impair ventricular function.", "D": "PE can cause pleuritic pain and tachycardia but not a rub, positional relief or diffuse ST elevation with PR depression.", "E": "Early repolarisation occurs without symptoms, rub or PR depression, and does not evolve."}, "key_takeaways": ["Pleuritic pain eased by sitting forward + rub + diffuse saddle ST elevation with PR depression = acute pericarditis. NSAID plus colchicine."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,-1.0,0.5,'expert_prior','author_estimate (batch01)'),
('9a3f9ec1-ef4e-5609-a93d-26d072a1b550'::uuid,'ZQ-0261','adult-medicine','neurology','A 63-year-old man sees his general practitioner in Cairns, Queensland, because the right side of his face has drooped since he woke this morning. For 3 days he has had severe burning pain deep in his right ear. Food tastes strange and loud sounds are uncomfortable in his right ear. He has type 2 diabetes.

He cannot wrinkle his right forehead, close his right eye fully or raise the right corner of his mouth. There are small clustered vesicles on an erythematous base in the right external auditory canal and concha. The rest of the neurological examination is normal.

Which one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?','["Ramsay Hunt syndrome (herpes zoster oticus)", "Bell palsy", "Acute ischaemic stroke", "Acute otitis media with facial nerve involvement", "Parotid gland tumour"]'::jsonb,'A','CORRECT ANSWER

A. Ramsay Hunt syndrome (herpes zoster oticus)

---

A lower motor neurone facial palsy (forehead involved) with ear pain and vesicles in the canal and concha is Ramsay Hunt syndrome, from varicella-zoster reactivation in the geniculate ganglion. Altered taste and hyperacusis reflect chorda tympani and stapedius nerve involvement. Compared with Bell palsy it is more painful, more often complete, and has a poorer chance of full recovery; it may also cause hearing loss or vertigo. Treatment is prednisolone plus an antiviral (such as valaciclovir) started early, with eye protection.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Facial palsy (forehead involved) + ear pain + vesicles in the canal or concha = Ramsay Hunt syndrome. Prednisolone plus antiviral early.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "Therapeutic Guidelines (eTG complete)", "url": "https://www.tg.org.au"}
• {"name": "Australian Immunisation Handbook. Zoster (herpes zoster)", "url": "https://immunisationhandbook.health.gov.au/contents/vaccine-preventable-diseases/zoster-herpes-zoster"}','moderate','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "batch01", "review_status": "draft_pending_owner_review", "tags": ["ZQ-0261", "zyntra_bank", "batch01", "level_3", "task_data_interpretation_synthesis", "group_adult_medicine", "sys_neurology", "irtb_+0.20", "pending_owner_review"], "clinician_task": "data_interpretation_synthesis", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"Therapeutic Guidelines (eTG complete)\", \"url\": \"https://www.tg.org.au\"}; {\"name\": \"Australian Immunisation Handbook. Zoster (herpes zoster)\", \"url\": \"https://immunisationhandbook.health.gov.au/contents/vaccine-preventable-diseases/zoster-herpes-zoster\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"B": "Bell palsy is a diagnosis of exclusion; the vesicles indicate VZV, which changes treatment (antiviral) and prognosis.", "C": "Stroke causes upper motor neurone facial weakness that spares the forehead, usually with other signs.", "D": "Otitis media affects the middle ear (bulging drum) and does not cause canal or concha vesicles.", "E": "A parotid malignancy causes gradual palsy with a parotid mass, not vesicles."}, "key_takeaways": ["Facial palsy (forehead involved) + ear pain + vesicles in the canal or concha = Ramsay Hunt syndrome. Prednisolone plus antiviral early."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,0.2,0.5,'expert_prior','author_estimate (batch01)'),
('53ccf2f9-730c-5728-95a3-e6368cb1f8d3'::uuid,'ZQ-0262','adult-medicine','neurology','A 27-year-old woman presents to an emergency department in Newcastle, NSW, with 6 weeks of daily headache, worse lying down and on waking, and brief episodes of her vision ''greying out'' when she stands. She hears a pulsing ''whooshing'' sound in both ears. She started the combined oral contraceptive pill 4 months ago and has gained 8 kg in the past year. Her BMI is 36 kg/m².

She is afebrile and her blood pressure is 124/80 mmHg. Fundoscopy shows bilateral optic disc swelling. Visual acuity is 6/6 in both eyes. She has a mild right sixth nerve palsy; the rest of the neurological examination is normal. A non-contrast CT brain is normal.

Which one of the following is the most appropriate next investigation?','["Lumbar puncture with opening pressure", "ESR and CRP", "MRI brain with MR venography", "Formal visual field testing alone with GP follow-up", "Carotid duplex ultrasound"]'::jsonb,'C','CORRECT ANSWER

C. MRI brain with MR venography

---

Headache with papilloedema means raised intracranial pressure. Idiopathic intracranial hypertension is likely in an obese young woman (a sixth nerve palsy is a non-localising sign), but it is a diagnosis of exclusion. Cerebral venous sinus thrombosis can look identical, and she has a prothrombotic risk factor (the combined pill). A non-contrast CT can miss venous sinus thrombosis. MRI with MR venography (or CT venography) should come before lumbar puncture. If imaging is normal, LP with opening pressure (lateral decubitus) and CSF analysis confirms IIH. She also needs formal visual fields and ophthalmology review.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Papilloedema with a normal CT: MRI with MR venography to exclude venous sinus thrombosis before LP. IIH is a diagnosis of exclusion.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "Idiopathic intracranial hypertension: consensus guidelines on management (Mollan et al., J Neurol Neurosurg Psychiatry 2018)", "url": "https://doi.org/10.1136/jnnp-2017-317440"}
