('4512e937-91fe-58b1-a2f4-ec7c6f2a4cc8'::uuid,'ZQ-0263','adult-medicine','haematology-oncology','A 61-year-old man sees his general practitioner in Geelong, Victoria, with 3 months of headaches and intense itch after hot showers. He has never smoked, does not snore and takes no medicines, including testosterone. SpO2 is 98% on room air. He has a ruddy complexion and the spleen tip is palpable 2 cm below the costal margin.

Investigations:
- Haemoglobin 198 g/L (130–180)
- Haematocrit 0.59 (0.40–0.52)
- White cell count 13.1 × 10^9/L (4.0–11.0)
- Platelets 520 × 10^9/L (150–400)

Which one of the following results would most strongly confirm the likely diagnosis?','["Raised serum erythropoietin with a normal JAK2 result", "JAK2 V617F mutation detected, with a low serum erythropoietin", "BCR-ABL1 fusion gene detected", "Raised ferritin with a raised transferrin saturation", "Low saturation on overnight oximetry"]'::jsonb,'B','CORRECT ANSWER

B. JAK2 V617F mutation detected, with a low serum erythropoietin

---

Raised haemoglobin and haematocrit with leucocytosis, thrombocytosis, splenomegaly and aquagenic pruritus, without hypoxia, smoking or testosterone, strongly suggests polycythaemia vera. A JAK2 mutation (V617F or exon 12) is present in about 95% of cases and is a major WHO criterion; a low serum EPO is the minor criterion and reflects EPO-independent erythropoiesis (secondary polycythaemia has normal or high EPO). Management is haematology referral, venesection to a haematocrit below 0.45 and low-dose aspirin, with cytoreduction for high-risk patients.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Raised Hb + splenomegaly + aquagenic itch (± high WCC/platelets): JAK2 V617F with low EPO = polycythaemia vera.

---

REFERENCES
