• {"name": "Therapeutic Guidelines (eTG complete)", "url": "https://www.tg.org.au"}','moderate','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "batch01", "review_status": "draft_pending_owner_review", "tags": ["ZQ-0263", "zyntra_bank", "batch01", "level_3", "task_data_interpretation_synthesis", "group_adult_medicine", "sys_haematology_oncology", "irtb_+0.60", "pending_owner_review"], "clinician_task": "data_interpretation_synthesis", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"WHO Classification of Haematolymphoid Tumours, 5th edition: myeloproliferative neoplasms\", \"url\": \"https://tumourclassification.iarc.who.int/\"}; {\"name\": \"Therapeutic Guidelines (eTG complete)\", \"url\": \"https://www.tg.org.au\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "This suggests secondary polycythaemia (hypoxia, renal tumour), not PV.", "C": "BCR-ABL1 defines chronic myeloid leukaemia, which does not raise haemoglobin.", "D": "This suggests iron overload; PV patients are often iron deficient.", "E": "Nocturnal hypoxia causes secondary polycythaemia, arguing against PV."}, "key_takeaways": ["Raised Hb + splenomegaly + aquagenic itch (± high WCC/platelets): JAK2 V617F with low EPO = polycythaemia vera."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,0.6,0.5,'expert_prior','author_estimate (batch01)'),
('c1377eba-a5c1-5ba0-b3f2-a622f94ce748'::uuid,'ZQ-0264','adult-medicine','rheumatology','A 72-year-old woman sees her general practitioner in Albury, NSW, with 5 weeks of aching and stiffness of both shoulders and hips. The stiffness lasts about 2 hours each morning and she struggles to get out of bed and comb her hair. She has no headache, scalp tenderness, jaw pain on chewing or visual symptoms, and no weight loss, joint swelling or rash.

Active shoulder movement is limited by pain, but strength is normal. Her temporal arteries are non-tender with normal pulses. There is no synovitis.

Investigations:
- ESR 64 mm/h (<30)
- CRP 42 mg/L (<5)
- Creatine kinase normal
- Rheumatoid factor and anti-CCP negative
- FBC, calcium, TSH, electrolytes and LFTs normal

Which one of the following is the most appropriate management?','["Oral prednisolone 15 mg daily, with review of the response in 1–2 weeks", "Oral prednisolone 60 mg daily and urgent temporal artery biopsy", "Regular oral NSAIDs and review in 4 weeks", "Methotrexate as first-line monotherapy", "Intramuscular methylprednisolone 120 mg weekly"]'::jsonb,'A','CORRECT ANSWER

A. Oral prednisolone 15 mg daily, with review of the response in 1–2 weeks

---

Bilateral shoulder and hip girdle aching with prolonged morning stiffness, raised inflammatory markers, a normal CK and no features of another rheumatic disease or giant cell arteritis fits polymyalgia rheumatica. Treatment is oral prednisolone 12.5–25 mg daily (commonly 15 mg). A rapid, marked response supports the diagnosis; a poor response should prompt a search for another cause. The dose is tapered slowly over 1–2 years. Consider bone protection, and tell her to report GCA symptoms urgently.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
PMR without GCA features: prednisolone 12.5–25 mg daily (often 15 mg). Expect a dramatic response; if not, reconsider the diagnosis.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "2015 EULAR/ACR recommendations for the management of polymyalgia rheumatica (Dejaco et al., Ann Rheum Dis 2015)", "url": "https://doi.org/10.1136/annrheumdis-2015-207492"}
• {"name": "Therapeutic Guidelines (eTG complete)", "url": "https://www.tg.org.au"}','moderate','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "batch01", "review_status": "draft_pending_owner_review", "tags": ["ZQ-0264", "zyntra_bank", "batch01", "level_3", "task_management", "group_adult_medicine", "sys_rheumatology", "irtb_-0.20", "pending_owner_review"], "clinician_task": "management", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"2015 EULAR/ACR recommendations for the management of polymyalgia rheumatica (Dejaco et al., Ann Rheum Dis 2015)\", \"url\": \"https://doi.org/10.1136/annrheumdis-2015-207492\"}; {\"name\": \"Therapeutic Guidelines (eTG complete)\", \"url\": \"https://www.tg.org.au\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"B": "This dose is for suspected giant cell arteritis; she has no headache, jaw claudication, scalp tenderness or visual symptoms.", "C": "NSAIDs give little benefit in PMR, carry more risk in older people and delay effective treatment.", "D": "Methotrexate is a steroid-sparing add-on for relapsing disease or high steroid risk, not first-line monotherapy.", "E": "IM methylprednisolone is an alternative in some guidelines, but not as a weekly regimen; oral prednisolone is standard."}, "key_takeaways": ["PMR without GCA features: prednisolone 12.5–25 mg daily (often 15 mg). Expect a dramatic response; if not, reconsider the diagnosis."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,-0.2,0.5,'expert_prior','author_estimate (batch01)'),
('9d24fe00-c905-5cd8-ac2c-b9954f46c1b1'::uuid,'ZQ-0265','adult-medicine','renal','A 45-year-old man is brought to an emergency department in Ipswich, Queensland, by his housemate, who found him confused and vomiting in the garage near empty containers. He has alcohol use disorder. He is drowsy but rousable, with no focal neurological signs; pupils and fundi are normal. Pulse is 112/min, blood pressure 138/84 mmHg and respiratory rate 30/min.

Investigations:
- Venous pH 7.08; bicarbonate 7 mmol/L
- Sodium 140 mmol/L; chloride 101 mmol/L; potassium 5.0 mmol/L
- Glucose 5.0 mmol/L; urea 6.0 mmol/L
- Creatinine 190 µmol/L (60–110)
- Lactate 2.6 mmol/L (<2.0)
- Measured serum osmolality 335 mOsm/kg
- Blood ethanol undetectable
- Urine microscopy: many envelope-shaped and needle-shaped crystals

Which one of the following is the most likely cause of his presentation?','["Methanol poisoning", "Alcoholic ketoacidosis", "Isopropyl alcohol poisoning", "Salicylate poisoning", "Ethylene glycol poisoning"]'::jsonb,'E','CORRECT ANSWER

E. Ethylene glycol poisoning

---

Anion gap = Na − (Cl + HCO3) = 140 − (101 + 7) = 32 mmol/L, very high and not explained by a lactate of 2.6. Calculated osmolality = 2 × Na + glucose + urea = 280 + 5 + 6 = 291, so the osmolar gap is 335 − 291 = 44 mOsm/kg (normal below about 10) with no ethanol to explain it. A high anion gap acidosis with a high osmolar gap means a toxic alcohol. Calcium oxalate crystals (envelope-shaped dihydrate, needle-shaped monohydrate) and acute kidney injury point to ethylene glycol (antifreeze, coolant). Treatment is fomepizole (or ethanol) to block alcohol dehydrogenase, haemodialysis and correction of acidosis, with advice from the Poisons Information Centre (13 11 26).

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
High anion gap acidosis + high osmolar gap = toxic alcohol. Oxalate crystals + AKI = ethylene glycol; visual disturbance = methanol. Fomepizole and dialysis.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "Therapeutic Guidelines (eTG complete)", "url": "https://www.tg.org.au"}
• {"name": "NSW Poisons Information Centre (13 11 26)", "url": "https://www.poisonsinfo.nsw.gov.au/"}','difficult','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "batch01", "review_status": "draft_pending_owner_review", "tags": ["ZQ-0265", "zyntra_bank", "batch01", "level_5", "task_data_interpretation_synthesis", "group_adult_medicine", "sys_renal", "irtb_+2.00", "pending_owner_review"], "clinician_task": "data_interpretation_synthesis", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"Therapeutic Guidelines (eTG complete)\", \"url\": \"https://www.tg.org.au\"}; {\"name\": \"NSW Poisons Information Centre (13 11 26)\", \"url\": \"https://www.poisonsinfo.nsw.gov.au/\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "Methanol also raises both gaps, but typically causes visual symptoms and disc changes, not oxalate crystals and prominent AKI.", "B": "AKA causes a high anion gap acidosis but not a large osmolar gap, oxalate crystals or this degree of AKI.", "C": "Isopropyl alcohol raises the osmolar gap but is metabolised to acetone, causing ketosis without significant acidosis.", "D": "Salicylates cause a high anion gap acidosis, often with respiratory alkalosis and tinnitus, but not a large osmolar gap or oxalate crystals."}, "key_takeaways": ["High anion gap acidosis + high osmolar gap = toxic alcohol. Oxalate crystals + AKI = ethylene glycol; visual disturbance = methanol. Fomepizole and dialysis."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,2.0,0.5,'expert_prior','author_estimate (batch01)'),
('d47f2298-8314-5334-bca1-b3a095ea938b'::uuid,'ZQ-0266','adult-surgery','vascular-surgery','A 76-year-old woman presents to an emergency department in Rockhampton, Queensland, 3 hours after the sudden onset of pain and numbness in her right leg while watching television. She has atrial fibrillation but stopped her apixaban 2 months ago because of bruising. She has no history of claudication.

Her pulse is 104/min and irregularly irregular, blood pressure 150/88 mmHg. The right leg is pale and cool from mid-calf down. The right femoral pulse is present but the popliteal and foot pulses are absent. Light touch is mildly reduced over the toes; toe movement is normal. The left leg has normal pulses. Handheld Doppler signals are absent in the right foot arteries but present in the veins.

Which one of the following is the most appropriate immediate management?','["Restart apixaban and arrange an outpatient arterial duplex scan", "Give aspirin 300 mg and arrange vascular review the next morning", "Arrange an immediate below-knee amputation", "Give an intravenous bolus of unfractionated heparin and arrange urgent vascular surgical review", "Elevate the leg and apply a warm blanket"]'::jsonb,'D','CORRECT ANSWER

D. Give an intravenous bolus of unfractionated heparin and arrange urgent vascular surgical review

---

Sudden onset of a cold, painful, pulseless leg in a woman with AF off anticoagulation, normal contralateral pulses and no claudication history is acute embolic limb ischaemia (probably popliteal). Mild sensory loss with preserved movement and audible venous but not arterial Doppler signals is Rutherford IIa (marginally threatened). Immediate treatment is an IV unfractionated heparin bolus then infusion to stop clot propagation, analgesia, and urgent vascular surgery for revascularisation (embolectomy or catheter-directed therapy) within hours.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Acute limb ischaemia with a salvageable limb: IV heparin bolus, analgesia and urgent vascular surgery for revascularisation.

---

REFERENCES
