• {"name": "Therapeutic Guidelines (eTG complete)", "url": "https://www.tg.org.au"}','moderate','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "batch01", "review_status": "draft_pending_owner_review", "tags": ["ZQ-0266", "zyntra_bank", "batch01", "level_3", "task_management", "group_adult_surgery", "sys_vascular", "irtb_+0.10", "pending_owner_review"], "clinician_task": "management", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"ESVS 2020 clinical practice guidelines on the management of acute limb ischaemia (Eur J Vasc Endovasc Surg 2020)\", \"url\": \"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ejvs.2019.09.006\"}; {\"name\": \"Therapeutic Guidelines (eTG complete)\", \"url\": \"https://www.tg.org.au\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "Acute limb ischaemia is a surgical emergency; delay risks limb loss.", "B": "Aspirin does not stop clot propagation adequately, and waiting risks irreversible ischaemia.", "C": "Amputation is for an irreversible (class III) limb with profound sensory loss, paralysis and no arterial or venous Doppler signals.", "E": "Elevation reduces perfusion pressure and warming increases metabolic demand; keep the leg level or slightly dependent."}, "key_takeaways": ["Acute limb ischaemia with a salvageable limb: IV heparin bolus, analgesia and urgent vascular surgery for revascularisation."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,0.1,0.5,'expert_prior','author_estimate (batch01)'),
('deec622c-b4c8-5ec5-aded-bdfcd29b620a'::uuid,'ZQ-0267','adult-surgery','general-surgery','A 38-year-old man sees his general practitioner in Bunbury, Western Australia, with 4 days of worsening constant throbbing pain beside his anus; sitting is painful. He has had no change in bowel habit or rectal bleeding, and has no inflammatory bowel disease, diabetes or immune compromise.

His temperature is 37.9 °C. There is a 3 cm tender, red, fluctuant swelling 2 cm from the anal verge at the 4 o''clock position. Digital rectal examination is too painful to complete.

Which one of the following is the most appropriate management?','["Oral amoxicillin–clavulanate and review in 1 week", "Sitz baths, stool softeners and topical glyceryl trinitrate", "Rubber band ligation", "Same-day referral for incision and drainage", "Elective colonoscopy"]'::jsonb,'D','CORRECT ANSWER

D. Same-day referral for incision and drainage

---

A tender fluctuant perianal swelling with fever is a perianal abscess. Definitive treatment is prompt surgical incision and drainage, usually under anaesthesia so that examination under anaesthesia can also look for a fistula. Antibiotics alone do not cure an established abscess; they are added for spreading cellulitis, systemic sepsis, diabetes or immune compromise. Delay risks spread to deeper spaces.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Perianal abscess: urgent incision and drainage. Antibiotics alone are not enough.

---

