('137090d1-8c6f-5d48-b233-3593c5d5808a'::uuid,'ZQ-0268','adult-surgery','general-surgery','A 64-year-old man presents to an emergency department in Ballarat, Victoria, with 2 days of constant left iliac fossa pain and loose stools. He has never had a colonoscopy; his last screening iFOBT 4 years ago was negative.

His temperature is 38.1 °C, pulse 98/min and blood pressure 132/80 mmHg. There is localised left iliac fossa tenderness and guarding, without generalised peritonism. Bowel sounds are present.

Investigations:
- White cell count 14.2 × 10^9/L (4.0–11.0)
- CRP 128 mg/L (<5)
- Creatinine and liver function normal

Which one of the following is the most appropriate investigation to confirm the diagnosis?','["Colonoscopy during this admission", "Plain abdominal X-ray", "CT scan of the abdomen and pelvis with intravenous contrast", "Barium enema", "Faecal calprotectin"]'::jsonb,'C','CORRECT ANSWER

C. CT scan of the abdomen and pelvis with intravenous contrast

---

Left iliac fossa pain, fever and raised inflammatory markers in an older adult suggest acute diverticulitis. With no previous episode, imaging is needed to confirm it, to separate uncomplicated from complicated disease (abscess, perforation, fistula, obstruction) and to exclude other causes; contrast CT is preferred. Uncomplicated disease in a well, immunocompetent patient may be managed without antibiotics; an abscess may need drainage. Colonoscopy is usually done 6–8 weeks after recovery to exclude cancer if the colon has not been recently assessed.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Suspected first diverticulitis: CT abdomen/pelvis with IV contrast. Defer colonoscopy for 6–8 weeks.

---

