• {"name": "ASCRS clinical practice guidelines for the management of left-sided colonic diverticulitis (Dis Colon Rectum 2020)", "url": "https://doi.org/10.1097/DCR.0000000000001679"}','moderate','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "batch01", "review_status": "draft_pending_owner_review", "tags": ["ZQ-0268", "zyntra_bank", "batch01", "level_3", "task_data_gathering", "group_adult_surgery", "sys_general_surgery", "irtb_-0.10", "pending_owner_review"], "clinician_task": "data_gathering", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"Therapeutic Guidelines (eTG complete)\", \"url\": \"https://www.tg.org.au\"}; {\"name\": \"ASCRS clinical practice guidelines for the management of left-sided colonic diverticulitis (Dis Colon Rectum 2020)\", \"url\": \"https://doi.org/10.1097/DCR.0000000000001679\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "Colonoscopy in acute diverticulitis risks perforation and is deferred for about 6–8 weeks.", "B": "A plain film may show free gas or obstruction but cannot diagnose or stage diverticulitis.", "D": "Contrast enema has no acute role, and barium can cause severe peritonitis if perforated.", "E": "Calprotectin helps separate IBD from functional disorders; it does not confirm diverticulitis or its complications."}, "key_takeaways": ["Suspected first diverticulitis: CT abdomen/pelvis with IV contrast. Defer colonoscopy for 6–8 weeks."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,-0.1,0.5,'expert_prior','author_estimate (batch01)'),
('a8594bfc-dc58-50c5-9a97-d0086dd3d036'::uuid,'ZQ-0269','adult-surgery','general-surgery','An 82-year-old man from a residential aged care facility in Mildura, Victoria, is brought to the emergency department with 2 days of increasing abdominal distension, colicky pain and no passage of stool or flatus. He has Parkinson disease and chronic constipation, and takes regular opioids for back pain.

He is afebrile, pulse 96/min, blood pressure 128/76 mmHg. His abdomen is grossly distended and tympanic, with mild generalised tenderness but no guarding. Bowel sounds are high-pitched. The rectum is empty.

A supine abdominal X-ray shows a hugely dilated inverted-U loop of large bowel without haustra (''coffee bean''), arising from the left lower quadrant and pointing to the right upper quadrant. There is no free gas on the erect chest X-ray.

Which one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?','["Caecal volvulus", "Sigmoid volvulus", "Acute colonic pseudo-obstruction (Ogilvie syndrome)", "Obstructing sigmoid carcinoma", "Adhesive small bowel obstruction"]'::jsonb,'B','CORRECT ANSWER

B. Sigmoid volvulus

---

An older institutionalised man with neurological disease, chronic constipation and opioid use is the typical patient. The ''coffee bean'' (bent inner tube) sign, a hugely dilated haustra-free loop arising from the pelvis and pointing towards the right upper quadrant, is characteristic; CT can confirm it (whirl sign). Without ischaemia or perforation, initial treatment is endoscopic decompression (flexible sigmoidoscopy) with a flatus tube. Recurrence is common, so elective sigmoid colectomy during the same admission is usually advised if he is fit.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
''Coffee bean'' loop from pelvis to RUQ in an older constipated patient = sigmoid volvulus. Endoscopic decompression if no ischaemia, then consider colectomy.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "ASCRS clinical practice guidelines for the management of colonic volvulus and acute colonic pseudo-obstruction (Dis Colon Rectum 2021)", "url": "https://doi.org/10.1097/DCR.0000000000002165"}','moderate','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "batch01", "review_status": "draft_pending_owner_review", "tags": ["ZQ-0269", "zyntra_bank", "batch01", "level_3", "task_data_interpretation_synthesis", "group_adult_surgery", "sys_general_surgery", "irtb_+0.30", "pending_owner_review"], "clinician_task": "data_interpretation_synthesis", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"ASCRS clinical practice guidelines for the management of colonic volvulus and acute colonic pseudo-obstruction (Dis Colon Rectum 2021)\", \"url\": \"https://doi.org/10.1097/DCR.0000000000002165\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "Caecal volvulus gives a dilated loop in the left upper quadrant with small bowel dilatation, usually in younger patients.", "C": "Pseudo-obstruction shows diffuse colonic dilatation (often caecal), not a single twisted loop.", "D": "A tumour causes proximal colonic dilatation with preserved haustra, not a single haustra-free twisted loop.", "E": "Small bowel loops are central with valvulae conniventes; this is a large bowel loop and he has no surgical history."}, "key_takeaways": ["''Coffee bean'' loop from pelvis to RUQ in an older constipated patient = sigmoid volvulus. Endoscopic decompression if no ischaemia, then consider colectomy."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,0.3,0.5,'expert_prior','author_estimate (batch01)'),
('dd9d6365-c551-5e41-bd91-b7d6a79ab02f'::uuid,'ZQ-0271','adult-surgery','orthopaedics','A 34-year-old woman presents to an emergency department in Darwin, Northern Territory, 2 hours after rolling her right ankle inwards stepping off a kerb. She has pain over the outside of the ankle.

There is swelling and bruising below and in front of the lateral malleolus. The doctor applies the Ottawa ankle rules to decide whether she needs an X-ray.

Which one of the following findings would indicate that an ankle X-ray is needed?','["Swelling and bruising over the lateral ankle", "Tenderness over the anterior talofibular ligament", "Pain when walking, although she can take four steps", "Age over 30 years", "Bone tenderness at the posterior edge of the distal 6 cm of the lateral malleolus"]'::jsonb,'E','CORRECT ANSWER

E. Bone tenderness at the posterior edge of the distal 6 cm of the lateral malleolus

---

The Ottawa ankle rules say an ankle X-ray is needed if there is malleolar-zone pain and any of: bone tenderness along the posterior edge or tip of the distal 6 cm of the lateral malleolus; the same at the medial malleolus; or inability to bear weight for four steps both immediately and in the ED. A foot X-ray is needed for midfoot pain with tenderness at the base of the fifth metatarsal or the navicular, or inability to bear weight. The rules are close to 100% sensitive for clinically significant fractures and reduce unnecessary imaging.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Ottawa ankle rules: X-ray if bone tenderness at the posterior edge or tip of the distal 6 cm of either malleolus, or inability to take 4 steps at injury and in the ED.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "Stiell IG et al. Decision rules for the use of radiography in acute ankle injuries (JAMA 1993)", "url": "https://doi.org/10.1001/jama.1993.03500080039030"}','easy','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "batch01", "review_status": "draft_pending_owner_review", "tags": ["ZQ-0271", "zyntra_bank", "batch01", "level_2", "task_data_gathering", "group_adult_surgery", "sys_orthopaedics", "irtb_-1.40", "pending_owner_review"], "clinician_task": "data_gathering", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"Stiell IG et al. Decision rules for the use of radiography in acute ankle injuries (JAMA 1993)\", \"url\": \"https://doi.org/10.1001/jama.1993.03500080039030\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "Swelling and bruising are common in sprains and are not part of the rules.", "B": "ATFL soft-tissue tenderness indicates a sprain; it is not an X-ray criterion.", "C": "Taking four steps, even with a limp, does not meet the weight-bearing criterion.", "D": "Age is not a criterion in the Ottawa ankle rules."}, "key_takeaways": ["Ottawa ankle rules: X-ray if bone tenderness at the posterior edge or tip of the distal 6 cm of either malleolus, or inability to take 4 steps at injury and in the ED."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,-1.4,0.5,'expert_prior','author_estimate (batch01)'),
('bde17ae8-fdae-51fb-a97b-1c44d54eb12d'::uuid,'ZQ-0272','adult-surgery','trauma','A 24-year-old man arrives by ambulance at a major trauma centre in Brisbane 25 minutes after being stabbed once in the left chest. He is anxious and pale.

His airway is patent and he is talking. Respiratory rate is 26/min and SpO2 97% on 15 L/min via non-rebreather. Breath sounds are equal, the trachea is central and percussion is resonant bilaterally. Pulse is 128/min and blood pressure 82/60 mmHg, falling by 15 mmHg systolic on inspiration. His neck veins are distended and heart sounds are quiet. There is a 2 cm wound just left of the sternum in the 4th intercostal space, and no other injury.

Two large-bore cannulas are in place and an eFAST is being performed.

Which one of the following is the most likely cause of his shock?','["Tension pneumothorax", "Massive haemothorax", "Cardiac tamponade", "Hypovolaemic shock from an intercostal artery injury", "Blunt cardiac injury with cardiogenic shock"]'::jsonb,'C','CORRECT ANSWER

C. Cardiac tamponade

---

A stab wound in the ''cardiac box'' with shock, distended neck veins and quiet heart sounds (Beck''s triad) plus pulsus paradoxus (inspiratory systolic fall above 10 mmHg) indicates obstructive shock from cardiac tamponade. Equal breath sounds, a central trachea and resonant percussion make tension pneumothorax and massive haemothorax unlikely. eFAST will show pericardial fluid. Management is immediate surgical decompression (thoracotomy or sternotomy by the trauma team); pericardiocentesis is only a temporising measure when surgery is unavailable.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Penetrating ''cardiac box'' wound + shock + raised JVP + quiet heart sounds + pulsus paradoxus = tamponade. Urgent surgical decompression.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "RACS Early Management of Severe Trauma (EMST/ATLS) course manual", "url": "https://www.surgeons.org/en/education/skills-training-courses/emst"}','difficult','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "batch01", "review_status": "draft_pending_owner_review", "tags": ["ZQ-0272", "zyntra_bank", "batch01", "level_4", "task_data_interpretation_synthesis", "group_adult_surgery", "sys_trauma", "irtb_+1.10", "pending_owner_review"], "clinician_task": "data_interpretation_synthesis", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"RACS Early Management of Severe Trauma (EMST/ATLS) course manual\", \"url\": \"https://www.surgeons.org/en/education/skills-training-courses/emst\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "Also causes shock and distended neck veins, but with absent breath sounds and hyper-resonance on the affected side.", "B": "Causes hypovolaemic shock with flat neck veins, reduced breath sounds and dullness on the injured side.", "D": "Hypovolaemia gives collapsed neck veins and does not explain pulsus paradoxus or quiet heart sounds.", "E": "This follows blunt trauma, with arrhythmia and raised troponin; he has a penetrating wound with signs of a pericardial collection."}, "key_takeaways": ["Penetrating ''cardiac box'' wound + shock + raised JVP + quiet heart sounds + pulsus paradoxus = tamponade. Urgent surgical decompression."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,1.1,0.5,'expert_prior','author_estimate (batch01)'),
('aa6821e4-6ceb-5556-83ee-4edeb022b9cd'::uuid,'ZQ-0273','adult-surgery','urology','A 41-year-old man presents to an emergency department in Townsville, Queensland, with sudden severe colicky right loin-to-groin pain that began 6 hours ago. He has no fever or rigors. His pain is now controlled after an NSAID, and his observations are normal.

Investigations:
- Urinalysis: blood 3+, nitrites and leucocytes negative
- White cell count 8.2 × 10^9/L (4.0–11.0)
- Creatinine 82 µmol/L (60–110)
- Non-contrast CT KUB: a single 6 mm stone in the right distal ureter 2 cm above the vesicoureteric junction, with mild right hydronephrosis; normal left kidney

Which one of the following is the most appropriate management?','["Discharge with an NSAID, tamsulosin, and urology follow-up with repeat imaging", "Urgent ureteric stent or nephrostomy", "Admit for emergency ureteroscopy tonight", "Oral potassium citrate and review in 3 months", "Oral antibiotics for 7 days and discharge without follow-up"]'::jsonb,'A','CORRECT ANSWER

A. Discharge with an NSAID, tamsulosin, and urology follow-up with repeat imaging

---

This is an uncomplicated stone: no infection, normal renal function, two kidneys and controlled pain. Stones of 5–7 mm pass with conservative management in about 60% of cases, and the RACGP review advises NSAIDs as the most effective analgesia and routine tamsulosin 400 µg daily in conservatively managed renal colic (off-label, and not PBS-listed for urolithiasis). Tamsulosin''s benefit is mainly for distal stones over 5 mm. He should strain his urine, have follow-up imaging at about 4 weeks (unless the stone is retrieved), and see a urologist if the stone has not passed by about 3 weeks or pain is not controlled. He should return immediately for fever or uncontrolled pain. Urgent drainage is for infection, a single kidney, bilateral obstruction, anuria or poor renal reserve.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Uncomplicated distal ureteric stone 5–10 mm: NSAID, tamsulosin and follow-up imaging. Drain urgently only if infected, AKI, solitary kidney or uncontrolled pain.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "Sewell J et al. Urolithiasis – ten things every general practitioner should know. Aust Fam Physician 2017;46(9) (RACGP)", "url": "https://www.racgp.org.au/afp/2017/september/urolithiasis"}
• {"name": "EAU Guidelines on Urolithiasis: medical expulsive therapy", "url": "https://uroweb.org/guidelines/urolithiasis/chapter/guidelines"}','moderate','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "batch01", "review_status": "draft_pending_owner_review", "tags": ["ZQ-0273", "zyntra_bank", "batch01", "level_3", "task_management", "group_adult_surgery", "sys_urology", "irtb_-0.30", "pending_owner_review"], "clinician_task": "management", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"Sewell J et al. Urolithiasis – ten things every general practitioner should know. Aust Fam Physician 2017;46(9) (RACGP)\", \"url\": \"https://www.racgp.org.au/afp/2017/september/urolithiasis\"}; {\"name\": \"EAU Guidelines on Urolithiasis: medical expulsive therapy\", \"url\": \"https://uroweb.org/guidelines/urolithiasis/chapter/guidelines\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"B": "Urgent decompression is for an obstructed infected kidney, AKI, a solitary kidney or uncontrolled pain.", "C": "Emergency intervention is not needed for an uncomplicated 6 mm distal stone.", "D": "Citrate helps prevent some recurrences; it does not help an acute stone pass, and 3 months is too long.", "E": "He has no infection and needs follow-up to confirm stone passage."}, "key_takeaways": ["Uncomplicated distal ureteric stone 5–10 mm: NSAID, tamsulosin and follow-up imaging. Drain urgently only if infected, AKI, solitary kidney or uncontrolled pain."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,-0.3,0.5,'expert_prior','author_estimate (batch01)'),
('a52f6041-cda1-5dba-9a68-0bc630ca7438'::uuid,'ZQ-0274','adult-surgery','trauma','A 19-year-old man is brought to an emergency department in Orange, NSW, after being struck on the right side of the head by a cricket ball 2 hours ago. He was unconscious for about a minute, then talked normally and walked to the car. Over the last 30 minutes he has become increasingly drowsy and has vomited twice.

His GCS is now 11 (E3 V3 M5). The right pupil is 5 mm and sluggish; the left is 3 mm and brisk. There is a boggy right temporal swelling. Pulse is 58/min and blood pressure 168/92 mmHg.

Non-contrast CT brain shows a right temporoparietal lens-shaped hyperdense collection that does not cross suture lines, with 6 mm midline shift and an overlying skull fracture.

Which one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?','["Acute subdural haematoma", "Traumatic subarachnoid haemorrhage", "Acute extradural haematoma", "Diffuse axonal injury", "Cerebral contusion"]'::jsonb,'C','CORRECT ANSWER

C. Acute extradural haematoma

---

A temporal blow with skull fracture, brief loss of consciousness, a lucid interval and then rapid decline is the classic picture of an extradural haematoma, usually from middle meningeal artery injury. On CT it is biconvex because blood strips dura from bone, and it is limited by sutures where dura is firmly attached. An ipsilateral dilating pupil (uncal herniation) and Cushing''s response (bradycardia, hypertension) mean emergency neurosurgical evacuation; in a rural hospital, immediate discussion with the neurosurgical centre and retrieval, with airway protection and measures to reduce ICP meanwhile.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Lucid interval + temporal fracture + biconvex collection limited by sutures = extradural haematoma. A blown pupil means emergency evacuation.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "RACS Early Management of Severe Trauma (EMST/ATLS) course manual", "url": "https://www.surgeons.org/en/education/skills-training-courses/emst"}
• {"name": "NSW Agency for Clinical Innovation. Adult trauma clinical practice guidelines: initial management of closed head injury in adults", "url": "https://aci.health.nsw.gov.au/networks/institute-of-trauma-and-injury-management"}','moderate','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "batch01", "review_status": "draft_pending_owner_review", "tags": ["ZQ-0274", "zyntra_bank", "batch01", "level_3", "task_data_interpretation_synthesis", "group_adult_surgery", "sys_trauma", "irtb_+0.50", "pending_owner_review"], "clinician_task": "data_interpretation_synthesis", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"RACS Early Management of Severe Trauma (EMST/ATLS) course manual\", \"url\": \"https://www.surgeons.org/en/education/skills-training-courses/emst\"}; {\"name\": \"NSW Agency for Clinical Innovation. Adult trauma clinical practice guidelines: initial management of closed head injury in adults\", \"url\": \"https://aci.health.nsw.gov.au/networks/institute-of-trauma-and-injury-management\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "Subdural blood is crescentic, crosses sutures (not the falx), and usually comes from bridging veins in older people or high-energy injury.", "B": "Subarachnoid blood fills sulci and cisterns, not a lens-shaped collection with mass effect.", "D": "DAI causes immediate, persistent coma with little on CT, not a lucid interval.", "E": "A contusion is intraparenchymal haemorrhage, not a collection between skull and dura."}, "key_takeaways": ["Lucid interval + temporal fracture + biconvex collection limited by sutures = extradural haematoma. A blown pupil means emergency evacuation."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,0.5,0.5,'expert_prior','author_estimate (batch01)'),
('96f47e21-b7a5-5397-8a7c-f5a93b5eaa72'::uuid,'ZQ-0275','adult-surgery','ent-ophthalmology','A 48-year-old woman had a total thyroidectomy for multinodular goitre 5 hours ago at a private hospital in Adelaide. The nurse calls you, the surgical resident, because she has become anxious and breathless and says her neck feels tight.

She has audible inspiratory stridor, respiratory rate 30/min and SpO2 91% on 6 L/min via face mask. Pulse is 116/min and blood pressure 158/92 mmHg. The neck is visibly swollen and tense under the dressing, and the drain contains little blood. She can speak in short phrases.

The anaesthetist and surgeon have been called and are 10 minutes away.

Which one of the following is the most appropriate immediate action?','["Give nebulised adrenaline and IV dexamethasone and observe closely", "Perform an emergency needle cricothyroidotomy", "Attempt rapid sequence intubation on the ward", "Arrange an urgent neck ultrasound", "Remove the dressing, cut the skin sutures and open the wound, including the strap muscle layer, at the bedside"]'::jsonb,'E','CORRECT ANSWER

E. Remove the dressing, cut the skin sutures and open the wound, including the strap muscle layer, at the bedside

---

Increasing neck swelling with stridor and hypoxia after thyroidectomy is a compressive haematoma, which can obstruct the airway within minutes through venous and lymphatic congestion and laryngeal oedema. A blocked or poorly draining drain does not exclude it. The emergency treatment is immediate bedside wound opening (the ''SCOOP'' approach: Skin exposure, Cut sutures, Open skin, Open muscles, Pack) to release the clot, with high-flow oxygen and help called. She then returns to theatre for haemostasis, with the airway managed by an experienced anaesthetist. Decompression comes before attempts at intubation, which are very difficult through a distorted, oedematous airway.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Post-thyroidectomy haematoma with airway compromise: open the wound at the bedside (SCOOP) immediately, then return to theatre.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "Difficult Airway Society. Management of haematoma after thyroid surgery: systematic review and multidisciplinary consensus guidelines (Iliff et al., Anaesthesia 2022)", "url": "https://doi.org/10.1111/anae.15585"}','difficult','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "batch01", "review_status": "draft_pending_owner_review", "tags": ["ZQ-0275", "zyntra_bank", "batch01", "level_4", "task_management", "group_adult_surgery", "sys_ent_ophthalmology", "irtb_+1.40", "pending_owner_review"], "clinician_task": "management", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"Difficult Airway Society. Management of haematoma after thyroid surgery: systematic review and multidisciplinary consensus guidelines (Iliff et al., Anaesthesia 2022)\", \"url\": \"https://doi.org/10.1111/anae.15585\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "These may reduce laryngeal oedema but do not relieve compression by the haematoma.", "B": "A front-of-neck airway is a last resort; opening the wound is the first step and may relieve the obstruction.", "C": "Intubation is likely to be very difficult and should not be attempted on the ward before decompression.", "D": "Imaging delays treatment of a clinical emergency."}, "key_takeaways": ["Post-thyroidectomy haematoma with airway compromise: open the wound at the bedside (SCOOP) immediately, then return to theatre."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,1.4,0.5,'expert_prior','author_estimate (batch01)'),
('495e583c-97d8-55d8-9858-a765691677fd'::uuid,'ZQ-0276','womens-health','obstetrics','A 30-year-old woman, gravida 2 para 1, at 39 weeks'' gestation presents in spontaneous labour to a maternity unit in Wagga Wagga, NSW. Her rectovaginal swab at 36 weeks grew group B streptococcus. Her membranes are intact, she is afebrile and her cervix is 5 cm dilated.

As a child she developed a mild, itchy, non-blistering rash on day 5 of a course of amoxicillin, which resolved by itself. She has never had hives, swelling, breathing difficulty or a severe skin reaction with any medicine.

Which one of the following is the most appropriate intrapartum management?','["Intravenous benzylpenicillin until birth", "Intravenous cefazolin until birth", "Intravenous clindamycin until birth", "No antibiotics, because her membranes are intact", "Oral amoxicillin when she reaches full dilatation"]'::jsonb,'B','CORRECT ANSWER

B. Intravenous cefazolin until birth

---

She is GBS-colonised, so intrapartum antibiotic prophylaxis is recommended to reduce early-onset neonatal GBS disease, ideally starting at least 4 hours before birth. The usual drug is IV benzylpenicillin. For a non-severe, delayed (non-immediate) penicillin reaction, eTG and state maternity guidelines recommend IV cefazolin. Clindamycin is reserved for immediate or severe hypersensitivity and depends on susceptibility, as clindamycin resistance in GBS is increasing.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
GBS-positive in labour: IV benzylpenicillin; non-severe delayed penicillin reaction: IV cefazolin; immediate or severe reaction: clindamycin if susceptible.

---

