• {"name": "RANZCOG Statements and Guidelines directory", "url": "https://ranzcog.edu.au/resources/statements-and-guidelines-directory/"}','moderate','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "batch01", "review_status": "draft_pending_owner_review", "tags": ["ZQ-0276", "zyntra_bank", "batch01", "level_3", "task_management", "group_womens_health", "sys_obstetrics", "irtb_+0.20", "pending_owner_review"], "clinician_task": "management", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"Therapeutic Guidelines (eTG complete)\", \"url\": \"https://www.tg.org.au\"}; {\"name\": \"Queensland Clinical Guidelines. Early onset Group B streptococcal disease\", \"url\": \"https://www.health.qld.gov.au/qcg\"}; {\"name\": \"RANZCOG Statements and Guidelines directory\", \"url\": \"https://ranzcog.edu.au/resources/statements-and-guidelines-directory/\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "First-line without allergy; with a documented non-severe delayed reaction, cefazolin is used.", "C": "Reserved for immediate or severe penicillin hypersensitivity, and only if the isolate is susceptible.", "D": "GBS colonisation is an indication for prophylaxis regardless of membrane status.", "E": "Oral therapy does not reach adequate levels quickly, amoxicillin is a penicillin, and starting late gives too little time."}, "key_takeaways": ["GBS-positive in labour: IV benzylpenicillin; non-severe delayed penicillin reaction: IV cefazolin; immediate or severe reaction: clindamycin if susceptible."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,0.2,0.5,'expert_prior','author_estimate (batch01)'),
('ee86893b-f0bf-5883-a0ca-38aa4c34da41'::uuid,'ZQ-0277','womens-health','obstetrics','A 33-year-old primigravida with insulin-treated gestational diabetes is in the second stage of labour at 40 weeks in a regional hospital in Lismore, NSW. After the head is delivered it retracts against the perineum (the ''turtle sign'') and does not restitute. Gentle axial traction fails to deliver the anterior shoulder.

The emergency bell has been pressed, the time of head delivery noted, and the obstetric registrar, a second midwife and the paediatric team are in the room. She is lying flat on the bed.

Which one of the following is the most appropriate next step?','["Apply fundal pressure", "Apply strong downward traction to the head", "Replace the head and perform a caesarean section (Zavanelli manoeuvre)", "Perform an episiotomy as the first step", "Hyperflex and abduct her hips onto her abdomen (McRoberts manoeuvre)"]'::jsonb,'E','CORRECT ANSWER

E. Hyperflex and abduct her hips onto her abdomen (McRoberts manoeuvre)

---

This is shoulder dystocia. After calling for help, the first manoeuvre is McRoberts: with the woman flat, her hips are hyperflexed and abducted onto her abdomen, which flattens the sacral promontory and rotates the symphysis upwards; it resolves a large proportion of cases. It is usually combined with suprapubic pressure from the side of the fetal back. If these fail, internal manoeuvres (posterior arm delivery, rotational manoeuvres) or the all-fours position follow. Fundal pressure and strong lateral traction must be avoided.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Shoulder dystocia: call for help, then McRoberts plus suprapubic pressure. Avoid fundal pressure and excessive traction.

---

REFERENCES
