• {"name": "Queensland Clinical Guidelines. Shoulder dystocia", "url": "https://www.health.qld.gov.au/qcg"}','easy','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "batch01", "review_status": "draft_pending_owner_review", "tags": ["ZQ-0277", "zyntra_bank", "batch01", "level_2", "task_management", "group_womens_health", "sys_obstetrics", "irtb_-0.80", "pending_owner_review"], "clinician_task": "management", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"RCOG Green-top Guideline No. 42: Shoulder dystocia\", \"url\": \"https://www.rcog.org.uk/guidance/browse-all-guidance/green-top-guidelines/shoulder-dystocia-green-top-guideline-no-42/\"}; {\"name\": \"Queensland Clinical Guidelines. Shoulder dystocia\", \"url\": \"https://www.health.qld.gov.au/qcg\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "Fundal pressure further impacts the shoulder and increases the risk of uterine rupture and brachial plexus injury.", "B": "Excessive traction greatly increases brachial plexus injury; only gentle axial traction is used.", "C": "This is a last resort after all other manoeuvres fail.", "D": "Shoulder dystocia is a bony problem; episiotomy may help access for internal manoeuvres but does not release the shoulder."}, "key_takeaways": ["Shoulder dystocia: call for help, then McRoberts plus suprapubic pressure. Avoid fundal pressure and excessive traction."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,-0.8,0.5,'expert_prior','author_estimate (batch01)'),
('2813186f-b1f0-559e-a3dc-2a67d93e15ed'::uuid,'ZQ-0278','womens-health','obstetrics','A 32-year-old woman, gravida 1 para 0, at 33 weeks'' gestation sees her general practitioner in Shepparton, Victoria, with 10 days of intense itch, worst on her palms and soles and at night. She has no rash apart from scratch marks, and no abdominal pain, headache or visual disturbance. She takes only a pregnancy multivitamin.

Her blood pressure is 118/72 mmHg, urinalysis shows no protein and fetal movements are normal. The skin shows excoriations only.

Which one of the following is the most appropriate initial investigation?','["Skin biopsy with immunofluorescence", "Serum IgE and skin-prick testing", "Gallbladder ultrasound", "Serum bile acids and liver function tests", "Hepatitis A IgM"]'::jsonb,'D','CORRECT ANSWER

D. Serum bile acids and liver function tests

---

Itch without a primary rash, worst on the palms and soles and at night, in the third trimester suggests intrahepatic cholestasis of pregnancy. Diagnosis rests on raised serum (total) bile acids, often with raised transaminases. The bile acid level helps estimate stillbirth risk (highest at 100 µmol/L or more) and guides timing of birth. If tests are normal but the itch persists, repeat them every 1–2 weeks, as itch can precede biochemical change. Pre-eclampsia and HELLP should be considered, but her BP and urine are normal.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Third-trimester itch of palms and soles without a rash: serum bile acids and LFTs for intrahepatic cholestasis of pregnancy; repeat if normal and itch persists.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "Queensland Clinical Guidelines. Intrahepatic cholestasis of pregnancy", "url": "https://www.health.qld.gov.au/qcg"}
• {"name": "RANZCOG Statements and Guidelines directory", "url": "https://ranzcog.edu.au/resources/statements-and-guidelines-directory/"}','moderate','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "batch01", "review_status": "draft_pending_owner_review", "tags": ["ZQ-0278", "zyntra_bank", "batch01", "level_3", "task_data_gathering", "group_womens_health", "sys_obstetrics", "irtb_+0.00", "pending_owner_review"], "clinician_task": "data_gathering", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"Queensland Clinical Guidelines. Intrahepatic cholestasis of pregnancy\", \"url\": \"https://www.health.qld.gov.au/qcg\"}; {\"name\": \"RANZCOG Statements and Guidelines directory\", \"url\": \"https://ranzcog.edu.au/resources/statements-and-guidelines-directory/\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "This is for pemphigoid gestationis, which causes a blistering rash often starting periumbilically.", "B": "Allergy testing does not help pregnancy itch without a rash.", "C": "Gallstones do not cause this pattern of itch without pain.", "E": "Acute viral hepatitis usually causes malaise, nausea and jaundice; bile acids are the key test here."}, "key_takeaways": ["Third-trimester itch of palms and soles without a rash: serum bile acids and LFTs for intrahepatic cholestasis of pregnancy; repeat if normal and itch persists."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,0.0,0.5,'expert_prior','author_estimate (batch01)'),
('c113dc29-04e0-5af0-953d-315a6e1eeb92'::uuid,'ZQ-0279','womens-health','gynaecology','A 24-year-old woman, gravida 1, attends an early pregnancy service in Cairns, Queensland, at 10 weeks by dates with 2 weeks of intermittent vaginal bleeding and severe nausea and vomiting. She has lost 3 kg.

Her blood pressure is 142/90 mmHg and pulse 104/min. The uterus is palpable just below the umbilicus. Urinalysis shows ketones 2+ and protein 1+.

Investigations:
- Serum β-hCG 410 000 IU/L
- TSH 0.02 mIU/L (0.4–4.0); free T4 26 pmol/L (9–19)
- Transvaginal ultrasound: enlarged uterus filled with heterogeneous echogenic tissue containing many small cystic spaces; no fetus or gestational sac. Both ovaries contain large multiloculated cysts.

Which one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?','["Partial hydatidiform mole", "Twin pregnancy with hyperemesis gravidarum", "Incomplete miscarriage with retained products", "Graves disease with a missed miscarriage", "Complete hydatidiform mole"]'::jsonb,'E','CORRECT ANSWER

E. Complete hydatidiform mole

---

Bleeding, a uterus large for dates, hyperemesis, hypertension with proteinuria before 20 weeks, a very high β-hCG, biochemical hyperthyroidism (hCG stimulates the TSH receptor), bilateral theca lutein cysts and a ''snowstorm'' appearance with no fetus are classic features of a complete hydatidiform mole, which is diploid and entirely paternal in origin. Treatment is suction curettage with histology, then serial hCG until normal (with reliable contraception) and registration with a trophoblastic disease service, as about 15–20% develop gestational trophoblastic neoplasia.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Very high hCG + uterus large for dates + hyperemesis + early pre-eclampsia ± hyperthyroidism + ''snowstorm'' with no fetus = complete mole. Suction curettage and serial hCG.

---

REFERENCES
