• {"name": "RANZCOG Statements and Guidelines directory", "url": "https://ranzcog.edu.au/resources/statements-and-guidelines-directory/"}','difficult','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "batch01", "review_status": "draft_pending_owner_review", "tags": ["ZQ-0279", "zyntra_bank", "batch01", "level_4", "task_data_interpretation_synthesis", "group_womens_health", "sys_gynaecology", "irtb_+1.00", "pending_owner_review"], "clinician_task": "data_interpretation_synthesis", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"Queensland Clinical Guidelines. Early pregnancy loss\", \"url\": \"https://www.health.qld.gov.au/qcg\"}; {\"name\": \"RANZCOG Statements and Guidelines directory\", \"url\": \"https://ranzcog.edu.au/resources/statements-and-guidelines-directory/\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "Partial moles are triploid, usually have fetal parts or a sac, and have lower hCG and fewer systemic features.", "B": "Twins can raise hCG and cause hyperemesis, but ultrasound would show two sacs or fetuses.", "C": "Retained products do not cause a very high hCG, early pre-eclampsia, thyrotoxicosis or theca lutein cysts.", "D": "The thyroid changes are explained by hCG, and the ultrasound does not fit a missed miscarriage."}, "key_takeaways": ["Very high hCG + uterus large for dates + hyperemesis + early pre-eclampsia ± hyperthyroidism + ''snowstorm'' with no fetus = complete mole. Suction curettage and serial hCG."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,1.0,0.5,'expert_prior','author_estimate (batch01)'),
('549ff5a1-62e1-5b19-bc0d-02bd31d3c42b'::uuid,'ZQ-0280','womens-health','gynaecology','A 28-year-old woman at 18 weeks'' gestation sees her general practitioner in Hervey Bay, Queensland, with 4 days of vulval itch, soreness and a thick white non-malodorous discharge. She has no abdominal pain or fever and no new sexual partner. She has no allergies.

The vulva is red and swollen, with a thick, curd-like white discharge on the vaginal walls. Vaginal pH is 4.2. High vaginal swab microscopy shows budding yeasts and pseudohyphae.

Which one of the following is the most appropriate treatment?','["Oral fluconazole 150 mg single dose", "Clotrimazole 500 mg vaginal tablet single dose", "Oral metronidazole 400 mg twice daily for 7 days", "Clotrimazole 100 mg vaginal tablet at night for 7 nights", "No treatment until after the birth"]'::jsonb,'D','CORRECT ANSWER

D. Clotrimazole 100 mg vaginal tablet at night for 7 nights

---

She has symptomatic vulvovaginal candidiasis confirmed on microscopy with a normal vaginal pH. In pregnancy, eTG recommends an intravaginal azole (clotrimazole or miconazole) for a longer 7-day course, because short courses are less effective in pregnancy. Oral fluconazole should be avoided in pregnancy because of concerns about miscarriage and congenital anomalies. Asymptomatic colonisation does not need treatment, and partners are not routinely treated.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Vulvovaginal candidiasis in pregnancy: intravaginal azole for 7 days. Avoid oral fluconazole.

---

