('8b314b41-9d99-5b25-b94c-685043a7c6b7'::uuid,'ZQ-0281','womens-health','obstetrics','A 29-year-old woman, gravida 2 para 1, at 34 weeks'' gestation phones the birth suite of a hospital in Toowoomba, Queensland, because her baby has been moving much less than usual since yesterday. She is asked to come in and arrives 40 minutes later. Her pregnancy has been uncomplicated, with a normal 20-week ultrasound.

Her blood pressure is 116/74 mmHg, and she has no pain, bleeding or fluid loss. Symphysis–fundal height is 33 cm. The fetal heart is heard on handheld Doppler at 140/min.

Which one of the following is the most appropriate next step?','["Cardiotocography (CTG)", "Reassure her because a fetal heartbeat has been heard", "Ask her to count kicks at home for 2 hours and call back", "Induce labour today", "Arrange an outpatient growth ultrasound in 2 weeks"]'::jsonb,'A','CORRECT ANSWER

A. Cardiotocography (CTG)

---

Decreased fetal movements can signal fetal compromise and are associated with stillbirth. The Australian Stillbirth CRE guideline (part of the Safer Baby Bundle) recommends that, after confirming the fetal heart, a woman at 28 weeks or more has a CTG (computerised where available), together with assessment of risk factors for growth restriction and stillbirth. Ultrasound for growth, liquor and umbilical artery Doppler is arranged (ideally within 24 hours) when there are risk factors, growth concerns, an abnormal CTG or recurrent decreased movements. Women should never be told to count kicks at home instead of attending.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Decreased fetal movements at 28 weeks or more: same-day assessment, confirm the fetal heart, then CTG; add ultrasound if there are risk factors or concerns.

---

