('ecd33907-69b2-512f-9ef9-041a830a938b'::uuid,'ZQ-0282','child-health','paediatric-medicine','A 9-month-old boy is brought to an emergency department in Ballarat, Victoria. Since this morning he has had episodes every 15–20 minutes of screaming, drawing up his legs and going pale, each lasting a few minutes. Between episodes he is unusually sleepy. He has vomited three times. He had a cold last week and passed a normal stool this morning.

His temperature is 37.4 °C, pulse 150/min and capillary refill 2 seconds. Between episodes his abdomen is soft, with a vague sausage-shaped mass in the right upper quadrant. There is no blood in the nappy.

Which one of the following is the most appropriate initial investigation?','["Plain abdominal X-ray", "CT abdomen", "Upper gastrointestinal contrast study", "Stool culture and viral PCR", "Abdominal ultrasound"]'::jsonb,'E','CORRECT ANSWER

E. Abdominal ultrasound

---

Episodic colicky pain with pallor and drawing up of the legs, lethargy between episodes, vomiting and a right upper quadrant mass in an infant aged 6–18 months suggests ileocolic intussusception, often with lymphoid hyperplasia after a viral illness as the lead point. Redcurrant jelly stool is a late sign; its absence does not exclude the diagnosis. Ultrasound is the investigation of choice (''target'' or ''doughnut'' sign): highly sensitive and radiation-free. If confirmed without peritonitis or perforation, treatment is air (pneumatic) enema reduction by an experienced paediatric radiology team with surgical backup, plus IV access and fluids.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Infant with episodic screaming, pallor and lethargy ± a mass: ultrasound for intussusception. No redcurrant jelly stool does not exclude it.

---

REFERENCES
