• {"name": "Royal Children''s Hospital. Intussusception clinical practice guideline", "url": "https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/Intussusception/"}','moderate','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "batch01", "review_status": "draft_pending_owner_review", "tags": ["ZQ-0282", "zyntra_bank", "batch01", "level_3", "task_data_gathering", "group_child_health", "sys_paediatric_medicine", "irtb_-0.20", "pending_owner_review"], "clinician_task": "data_gathering", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"Royal Children''s Hospital Melbourne Clinical Practice Guidelines\", \"url\": \"https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/\"}; {\"name\": \"Royal Children''s Hospital. Intussusception clinical practice guideline\", \"url\": \"https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/Intussusception/\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "An X-ray may show obstruction or free gas but is neither sensitive nor specific for intussusception.", "B": "CT is unnecessary and involves radiation; ultrasound is accurate in children.", "C": "This is for suspected malrotation with volvulus (bilious vomiting in a neonate).", "D": "Gastroenteritis does not explain episodic pain, pallor, lethargy and a mass, and waiting delays diagnosis."}, "key_takeaways": ["Infant with episodic screaming, pallor and lethargy ± a mass: ultrasound for intussusception. No redcurrant jelly stool does not exclude it."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,-0.2,0.5,'expert_prior','author_estimate (batch01)'),
('0d346204-24d4-552d-8dda-697d46015a77'::uuid,'ZQ-0283','child-health','neonatology','A 5-week-old boy is transferred to a paediatric surgical unit in Melbourne with 5 days of forceful non-bilious vomiting after every feed; he seems hungry straight afterwards. He was born at term weighing 3.6 kg and now weighs 3.7 kg.

He has a sunken fontanelle and dry mucous membranes; capillary refill is 2 seconds. An olive-shaped epigastric mass is felt during a test feed. Ultrasound shows pyloric muscle thickness 5 mm and channel length 19 mm.

Capillary blood gas:
- pH 7.53
- Bicarbonate 36 mmol/L (22–28)
- Chloride 86 mmol/L (95–110)
- Potassium 2.9 mmol/L (3.5–5.5)
- Sodium 132 mmol/L (135–145)
- Glucose 4.2 mmol/L

Which one of the following is the most appropriate management?','["IV 0.9% sodium chloride with 5% glucose and added potassium, then pyloromyotomy once chloride and bicarbonate have normalised", "Immediate laparoscopic pyloromyotomy", "IV 5% glucose alone at maintenance rate", "IV sodium bicarbonate to correct the low sodium", "Thickened feeds and a proton pump inhibitor"]'::jsonb,'A','CORRECT ANSWER

A. IV 0.9% sodium chloride with 5% glucose and added potassium, then pyloromyotomy once chloride and bicarbonate have normalised

---

He has infantile hypertrophic pyloric stenosis, confirmed on ultrasound, with the typical hypochloraemic, hypokalaemic metabolic alkalosis caused by loss of gastric acid. RCH says correcting dehydration, electrolyte and acid–base abnormalities is the priority, and surgery is delayed until they are corrected. It is particularly important to wait for the serum bicarbonate to normalise, because a metabolic alkalosis risks postoperative hypoventilation and apnoea. Fluids: 0.9% sodium chloride boluses only if needed for resuscitation, then 0.9% sodium chloride + 5% glucose for deficit and maintenance, with potassium added once urine output is adequate (1–2 mL/kg/h). Stop oral feeds, and pass a nasogastric tube if vomiting continues. Pyloromyotomy follows once the biochemistry has normalised.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Pyloric stenosis: correct the hypochloraemic hypokalaemic alkalosis with 0.9% saline + 5% glucose + KCl before pyloromyotomy, to avoid postoperative apnoea.

---

