('6f550698-d785-5423-bd29-c49f47979ec0'::uuid,'ZQ-0284','child-health','paediatric-medicine','A 7-month-old girl is referred by her general practitioner in Bathurst, NSW, to the local emergency department with 2 days of fever up to 39.4 °C, poor feeding and irritability. She has no cough, coryza, rash, diarrhoea or vomiting and is fully immunised.

Her temperature is 39.1 °C, pulse 158/min and capillary refill 2 seconds. She is irritable but consolable and well perfused. Ears, throat and chest are normal, with no rash or neck stiffness. She is assessed as not toxic. A urinary tract infection is suspected. She is not toilet-trained.

Which one of the following is the most appropriate way to collect urine for culture?','["Urine bag applied to the perineum", "Urine squeezed from a wet disposable nappy", "Clean-catch urine or a catheter specimen", "Cotton-wool ball placed in the nappy", "No sample; start empirical oral antibiotics"]'::jsonb,'C','CORRECT ANSWER

C. Clean-catch urine or a catheter specimen

---

UTI is a common cause of fever without a focus in infants, and a urine sample suitable for culture is needed before antibiotics. For a child who is not toilet-trained, Australian paediatric guidelines (such as RCH) recommend a clean-catch specimen, which can be encouraged by suprapubic stimulation (the Quick-Wee method), or a catheter specimen; suprapubic aspiration is an alternative mainly in young infants. Bag specimens are frequently contaminated and should not be sent for culture; a negative bag dipstick may help exclude UTI in a well child, but a positive result must be confirmed on a proper sample.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Urine culture in a child who is not toilet-trained: clean catch (Quick-Wee) or catheter (or SPA). Never culture a bag specimen.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "Royal Children''s Hospital. Urinary tract infection clinical practice guideline", "url": "https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/Urinary_tract_infection/"}','moderate','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "batch01", "review_status": "draft_pending_owner_review", "tags": ["ZQ-0284", "zyntra_bank", "batch01", "level_3", "task_data_gathering", "group_child_health", "sys_paediatric_medicine", "irtb_+0.20", "pending_owner_review"], "clinician_task": "data_gathering", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"Royal Children''s Hospital. Urinary tract infection clinical practice guideline\", \"url\": \"https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/Urinary_tract_infection/\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "Bag specimens have high contamination rates and many false positives; not suitable for culture.", "B": "Nappy urine is heavily contaminated and unsuitable for culture.", "D": "Pad samples have high contamination rates and are not recommended for diagnosis.", "E": "Without culture, a UTI cannot be confirmed, treatment cannot be guided by sensitivities, and follow-up imaging decisions are compromised."}, "key_takeaways": ["Urine culture in a child who is not toilet-trained: clean catch (Quick-Wee) or catheter (or SPA). Never culture a bag specimen."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,0.2,0.5,'expert_prior','author_estimate (batch01)'),
('655bdc93-ea42-559c-bc35-3dd9593b3b46'::uuid,'ZQ-0285','child-health','orthopaedics','A 13-year-old boy is brought to his general practitioner in Gladstone, Queensland, with 4 weeks of aching left knee pain and a limp, without injury or fever. He is otherwise well. His BMI is above the 97th centile.

He walks with an antalgic, out-toeing gait on the left. The left knee is normal, with full range of movement and no effusion or tenderness. When the left hip is flexed it rolls into external rotation, and internal rotation is markedly reduced and painful. The right hip is normal.

Which one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?','["Osgood–Schlatter disease", "Perthes disease", "Transient synovitis of the hip", "Slipped capital femoral epiphysis", "Patellofemoral pain syndrome"]'::jsonb,'D','CORRECT ANSWER

D. Slipped capital femoral epiphysis

---

An overweight early adolescent with a limp, knee pain (referred from the hip via the obturator nerve), a normal knee, loss of hip internal rotation and obligatory external rotation on hip flexion has SCFE until proven otherwise. He needs urgent AP and frog-leg lateral X-rays of both hips (the lateral view shows mild slips missed on AP, and the other side is often involved), non-weight-bearing and urgent orthopaedic referral for in situ fixation. Delay increases the risk of slip progression and avascular necrosis.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Knee pain + limp in an overweight adolescent with loss of hip internal rotation = SCFE until proven otherwise. AP and frog-leg lateral X-rays of both hips; non-weight-bearing; urgent orthopaedics.

---

