('271cb577-80b8-5bdb-bb53-f2a0bf373b69'::uuid,'ZQ-0306','adult-medicine','neurology','A 78-year-old woman in Bendigo, Vic, is seen by her GP because her daughter has noticed she is slower and shuffling. Over the past two months she has developed a symmetrical resting tremor, bradykinesia and rigidity in both arms. She has gastroparesis and started regular metoclopramide 10 mg three times daily three months ago. She has no other new medicines.

Which one of the following is the most likely cause of her symptoms?','["Idiopathic Parkinson disease", "Essential tremor", "Normal pressure hydrocephalus", "Dopamine D2 receptor blockade by metoclopramide", "Progressive supranuclear palsy"]'::jsonb,'D','CORRECT ANSWER

D. Dopamine D2 receptor blockade by metoclopramide

---

Symmetrical parkinsonism starting soon after a dopamine antagonist is typical of drug-induced parkinsonism. Metoclopramide is a common cause, especially in older women. The treatment is to stop the drug. Recovery may take weeks to months.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Symmetrical parkinsonism after starting metoclopramide is drug-induced (D2 blockade). Stop the drug.

---

