('478087fa-1eb3-5d58-8fca-668963cd0e41'::uuid,'ZQ-0307','adult-medicine','respiratory','A 69-year-old man with severe COPD (FEV1 32% predicted) is brought by ambulance to a hospital in Launceston, Tas, with three days of increasing breathlessness and purulent sputum. Paramedics started oxygen at 15 L/min by non-rebreather mask. His SpO2 is now 99%, respiratory rate 24/min and he is drowsy.

Venous blood gas: pH 7.29, pCO2 68 mmHg.

Which one of the following is the most appropriate immediate change to his oxygen therapy?','["Continue 15 L/min because he is hypoxic from the exacerbation", "Stop oxygen completely", "Change to nasal prongs at 6 L/min aiming for SpO2 above 94%", "Give IV sodium bicarbonate to correct the acidosis", "Titrate oxygen by Venturi mask to a target SpO2 of 88–92%"]'::jsonb,'E','CORRECT ANSWER

E. Titrate oxygen by Venturi mask to a target SpO2 of 88–92%

---

High-flow oxygen in COPD can worsen hypercapnia (through V/Q mismatch and the Haldane effect). His drowsiness and acidosis point to this. COPD-X recommends controlled oxygen titrated to SpO2 88–92%, with blood gases repeated. He will also need non-invasive ventilation if the acidosis persists.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
In a COPD exacerbation, titrate oxygen to SpO2 88–92%. Over-oxygenation worsens hypercapnic acidosis.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "Lung Foundation Australia. COPD-X Plan: X – Manage exacerbations", "url": "https://copdx.org.au/copd-x-plan/x-manage-exacerbations/"}','easy','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "batch02", "review_status": "draft_pending_owner_review", "tags": ["ZQ-0307", "zyntra_bank", "batch02", "level_2", "task_management", "group_adult_medicine", "sys_respiratory", "irtb_-1.00", "pending_owner_review"], "clinician_task": "management", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"Lung Foundation Australia. COPD-X Plan: X – Manage exacerbations\", \"url\": \"https://copdx.org.au/copd-x-plan/x-manage-exacerbations/\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "An SpO2 of 99% is well above target, and the high-flow oxygen is contributing to his hypercapnic acidosis.", "B": "Stopping oxygen abruptly can cause dangerous rebound hypoxaemia. Titrate to target instead.", "C": "This target is too high for a COPD patient with hypercapnia.", "D": "Respiratory acidosis is managed by controlling oxygen and supporting ventilation, not with bicarbonate."}, "key_takeaways": ["In a COPD exacerbation, titrate oxygen to SpO2 88–92%. Over-oxygenation worsens hypercapnic acidosis."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,-1.0,0.5,'expert_prior','author_estimate (batch02)'),
('a9207561-6fbf-5d10-852f-63cbd2112323'::uuid,'ZQ-0308','adult-medicine','infectious-diseases','A 26-year-old man who has sex with men attends a sexual health clinic in Darlinghurst, NSW. His routine screen showed a positive pharyngeal NAAT for Neisseria gonorrhoeae, with negative urine and anorectal NAATs. No culture swab was taken. He is treated today with ceftriaxone 500 mg IM and azithromycin 2 g orally. He asks what follow-up testing he needs.

Which one of the following is the most appropriate follow-up test?','["Repeat pharyngeal NAAT 2 days after treatment", "No further testing because the regimen is highly effective", "Repeat pharyngeal NAAT 2 weeks after treatment as a test of cure", "Serum gonococcal antibody titre", "Repeat urine NAAT only at 3 months"]'::jsonb,'C','CORRECT ANSWER

C. Repeat pharyngeal NAAT 2 weeks after treatment as a test of cure

---

The Australian STI Management Guidelines recommend a test of cure by NAAT at each infected site 2 weeks after treatment is completed. This is especially important for pharyngeal infection, when no culture was collected before treatment, or when resistance is suspected. If the test of cure is positive, seek specialist advice.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
After treating pharyngeal gonorrhoea, do a test of cure by NAAT 2 weeks after treatment.

---

