('055bdbfb-1d78-5ff5-a876-81d7c0d41817'::uuid,'ZQ-0309','adult-medicine','cardiology','A 64-year-old woman on a medical ward in Darwin, NT, is being treated for community-acquired pneumonia with azithromycin. She also takes citalopram and has had diarrhoea for two days. Her monitor shows recurrent runs of polymorphic ventricular tachycardia with a twisting QRS axis that end on their own. Between episodes she is alert, BP 122/74 mmHg, and her ECG shows sinus rhythm with a QTc of 590 ms.

Serum potassium is 3.1 mmol/L (3.5–5.2).

In addition to stopping QT-prolonging drugs and replacing potassium, which one of the following is the most appropriate immediate treatment?','["Magnesium sulfate 5 mmol intravenously over 10 minutes", "Intravenous amiodarone", "Intravenous sotalol", "Immediate synchronised DC cardioversion", "Oral metoprolol"]'::jsonb,'A','CORRECT ANSWER

A. Magnesium sulfate 5 mmol intravenously over 10 minutes

---

This is torsades de pointes from an acquired long QT (macrolide, SSRI, hypokalaemia, possibly hypomagnesaemia from diarrhoea). ANZCOR Guideline 11.9 says to treat immediately: stop all QT-prolonging drugs, correct electrolytes (especially potassium), and give IV magnesium 5 mmol over 10 minutes. This can be repeated once and followed by 20 mmol over 4 hours. Pacing or isoprenaline may be needed to prevent relapse. Amiodarone should be avoided because it can make things worse. Synchronised cardioversion is for adverse signs.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Torsades: stop QT-prolonging drugs, correct potassium, give IV magnesium 5 mmol over 10 minutes, and avoid amiodarone.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "ANZCOR Guideline 11.9 – Managing Acute Dysrhythmias", "url": "https://www.anzcor.org/home/adult-advanced-life-support/guideline-11-9-managing-acute-dysrhythmias"}','moderate','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "batch02", "review_status": "draft_pending_owner_review", "tags": ["ZQ-0309", "zyntra_bank", "batch02", "level_3", "task_management", "group_adult_medicine", "sys_cardiovascular", "irtb_+0.40", "pending_owner_review"], "clinician_task": "management", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"ANZCOR Guideline 11.9 – Managing Acute Dysrhythmias\", \"url\": \"https://www.anzcor.org/home/adult-advanced-life-support/guideline-11-9-managing-acute-dysrhythmias\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"B": "ANZCOR states amiodarone should be avoided in torsades because it can worsen the situation.", "C": "Sotalol prolongs QT and is contraindicated.", "D": "ANZCOR reserves cardioversion for adverse signs. She is alert and normotensive between self-terminating runs.", "E": "Beta-blockade is not the acute treatment for acquired torsades."}, "key_takeaways": ["Torsades: stop QT-prolonging drugs, correct potassium, give IV magnesium 5 mmol over 10 minutes, and avoid amiodarone."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,0.4,0.5,'expert_prior','author_estimate (batch02)'),
('4a79785e-5656-57bc-a29d-7514225101b5'::uuid,'ZQ-0310','adult-medicine','renal','A 55-year-old man in Ballarat, Vic, has had swelling of his legs for two months. He has no diabetes and takes no regular medicines. BP 136/84 mmHg, pitting oedema to the knees.

Investigations:
- Urine albumin:creatinine ratio 620 mg/mmol
- Serum albumin 22 g/L (35–50)
- Creatinine 88 µmol/L (60–110)
- Total cholesterol 9.1 mmol/L
- Urine microscopy: no red cell casts
- Hepatitis B, hepatitis C and HIV serology negative; ANA negative; complement normal
- Serum anti-phospholipase A2 receptor (PLA2R) antibodies: strongly positive

Which one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?','["Primary membranous nephropathy", "Minimal change disease", "Focal segmental glomerulosclerosis", "IgA nephropathy", "Lupus nephritis"]'::jsonb,'A','CORRECT ANSWER

A. Primary membranous nephropathy

---

Anti-PLA2R antibodies are highly specific for primary membranous nephropathy, the commonest cause of nephrotic syndrome in middle-aged and older white adults. KDIGO 2021 says a kidney biopsy is no longer required to confirm membranous nephropathy in patients with nephrotic syndrome and a positive anti-PLA2R test. Secondary causes (malignancy, infection, lupus, drugs) should still be looked for.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Strongly positive anti-PLA2R antibodies in adult nephrotic syndrome point to primary membranous nephropathy.

---

