• {"name": "KDIGO 2021 Glomerular Diseases Guideline – Executive summary (Kidney Int)", "url": "https://kdigo.org/wp-content/uploads/2017/02/KDIGO-2021-GD-Guideline-Executive-Summary.pdf"}','difficult','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "batch02", "review_status": "draft_pending_owner_review", "tags": ["ZQ-0310", "zyntra_bank", "batch02", "level_4", "task_data_interpretation_synthesis", "group_adult_medicine", "sys_renal", "irtb_+1.40", "pending_owner_review"], "clinician_task": "data_interpretation_synthesis", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"KDIGO 2021 Glomerular Diseases Guideline – Executive summary (Kidney Int)\", \"url\": \"https://kdigo.org/wp-content/uploads/2017/02/KDIGO-2021-GD-Guideline-Executive-Summary.pdf\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"B": "Minimal change is the main cause in children and can occur in adults, but it is not associated with PLA2R antibodies.", "C": "FSGS is a common cause in adults, but PLA2R antibodies are negative.", "D": "IgA nephropathy usually causes haematuria, often after an upper respiratory infection, and rarely causes heavy nephrotic syndrome.", "E": "ANA is negative and complement is normal, which argues against lupus."}, "key_takeaways": ["Strongly positive anti-PLA2R antibodies in adult nephrotic syndrome point to primary membranous nephropathy."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,1.4,0.5,'expert_prior','author_estimate (batch02)'),
('c409cecd-6fb6-5f5b-a659-2037ab292dae'::uuid,'ZQ-0311','adult-medicine','endocrinology','A 34-year-old woman in Cairns, Qld, had a prolactin level included in tests for fatigue. Her periods are regular, she has no galactorrhoea or headaches, and a pregnancy test is negative. She takes no medicines.

Investigations:
- Prolactin 1,150 mIU/L (<500), unchanged on repeat
- TSH normal
- eGFR normal

Which one of the following is the most appropriate next investigation?','["MRI of the pituitary", "Formal visual field testing", "Macroprolactin screening of the serum (for example, polyethylene glycol precipitation)", "Start cabergoline", "Insulin tolerance test"]'::jsonb,'C','CORRECT ANSWER

C. Macroprolactin screening of the serum (for example, polyethylene glycol precipitation)

---

Australian Prescriber notes that at least 10% of hyperprolactinaemia is due to macroprolactin, a complex that is cleared slowly from the circulation and is largely biologically inactive. That is why most people with macroprolactinaemia have no symptoms. In a woman with confirmed mild hyperprolactinaemia, no symptoms, and pregnancy, drugs, hypothyroidism and kidney disease excluded, macroprolactin screening comes before pituitary imaging.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Mild hyperprolactinaemia without symptoms: check for macroprolactin before pituitary MRI.

---

