('ba83586f-d3cb-5639-9c38-c41e99905372'::uuid,'ZQ-0312','adult-medicine','haematology-oncology','A 72-year-old man in Orange, NSW, has had tingling in both feet for four months and feels more tired. He has had type 2 diabetes for 12 years treated with metformin 1 g twice daily. His HbA1c is 6.9%. He eats a mixed diet. There is reduced vibration sense at the toes.

Investigations:
- Haemoglobin 118 g/L (130–180)
- MCV 104 fL (80–100)
- Serum B12 105 pmol/L (>150)
- Folate normal
- TSH normal

Which one of the following is the most likely cause of his macrocytosis?','["Pernicious anaemia", "Dietary B12 deficiency", "Hypothyroidism", "Metformin-associated vitamin B12 malabsorption", "Diabetic peripheral neuropathy"]'::jsonb,'D','CORRECT ANSWER

D. Metformin-associated vitamin B12 malabsorption

---

Long-term metformin reduces B12 absorption in the ileum. B12 deficiency can cause macrocytic anaemia and a neuropathy that can be mistaken for diabetic neuropathy. B12 should be checked periodically in long-term metformin users, especially those with anaemia or neuropathy. He needs B12 replacement, and metformin can usually continue.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Long-term metformin can cause B12 deficiency, with macrocytosis and neuropathy. Check B12 in these patients.

---

