('1d38579d-77da-5480-a883-5e40cd0adc49'::uuid,'ZQ-0313','adult-medicine','ent-ophthalmology','A 70-year-old woman presents to her GP in Mackay, Qld, with two days of burning pain over her right forehead followed by a vesicular rash in the distribution of the right ophthalmic division of the trigeminal nerve. Valaciclovir is started.

Which one of the following examination findings most strongly predicts eye involvement and the need for urgent ophthalmology review?','["Vesicles on the upper eyelid", "Vesicles on the tip of the nose", "Swollen preauricular lymph nodes", "Rash extending to the scalp vertex", "Pain over the forehead before the rash"]'::jsonb,'B','CORRECT ANSWER

B. Vesicles on the tip of the nose

---

Hutchinson sign is vesicles on the tip, side or root of the nose. It means the nasociliary branch of V1 is involved, and that branch also supplies the cornea. It strongly predicts ocular herpes zoster (keratitis or uveitis). Any patient with HZO, and especially Hutchinson sign, red eye or reduced vision, needs prompt ophthalmology assessment with a slit lamp.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Hutchinson sign (vesicles on the nasal tip) means nasociliary involvement. Expect ocular zoster and refer urgently.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "CMAJ. Herpes zoster ophthalmicus (Practice)", "url": "https://www.cmaj.ca/content/190/21/e656"}','moderate','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "batch02", "review_status": "draft_pending_owner_review", "tags": ["ZQ-0313", "zyntra_bank", "batch02", "level_3", "task_data_gathering", "group_adult_medicine", "sys_ent_ophthalmology", "irtb_+0.00", "pending_owner_review"], "clinician_task": "data_gathering", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"CMAJ. Herpes zoster ophthalmicus (Practice)\", \"url\": \"https://www.cmaj.ca/content/190/21/e656\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "Eyelid involvement is common in HZO but predicts eye involvement less strongly than nasal tip lesions.", "C": "Lymphadenopathy is non-specific.", "D": "The vertex lies in the V1 dermatome via the supraorbital and supratrochlear branches, which do not supply the globe.", "E": "Prodromal pain is typical of shingles and does not predict eye involvement."}, "key_takeaways": ["Hutchinson sign (vesicles on the nasal tip) means nasociliary involvement. Expect ocular zoster and refer urgently."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,0.0,0.5,'expert_prior','author_estimate (batch02)'),
('9aed5f9e-88c5-507f-8785-754b67d429e2'::uuid,'ZQ-0314','adult-medicine','infectious-diseases','A 29-year-old man in Adelaide, SA, has had dysuria and a mild urethral discharge for three weeks. Urine NAATs for chlamydia and gonorrhoea are negative. His symptoms continued after 7 days of doxycycline. A first-pass urine NAAT is now positive for Mycoplasma genitalium.

Which one of the following additional laboratory results will most directly determine his antibiotic regimen?','["Urethral swab culture for M. genitalium", "Gram stain of the urethral discharge", "Macrolide-resistance mutation testing on the M. genitalium-positive specimen", "Serum syphilis serology", "Repeat chlamydia NAAT"]'::jsonb,'C','CORRECT ANSWER

C. Macrolide-resistance mutation testing on the M. genitalium-positive specimen

---

The Australian STI Management Guidelines report that macrolide resistance in M. genitalium is above 60% in Australia and above 80% in men who have sex with men. They recommend resistance-guided therapy. After doxycycline, susceptible infection is treated with azithromycin (1 g, then 500 mg daily for 3 days), and macrolide-resistant infection with moxifloxacin 400 mg daily for 7 days. NAATs that detect macrolide-resistance mutations make this choice possible.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
For M. genitalium, use resistance-guided therapy: doxycycline, then azithromycin if macrolide-susceptible or moxifloxacin if resistant.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "Australian STI Management Guidelines. Mycoplasma genitalium", "url": "https://sti.guidelines.org.au/sexually-transmissible-infections/mycoplasma-genitalium/"}','difficult','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "batch02", "review_status": "draft_pending_owner_review", "tags": ["ZQ-0314", "zyntra_bank", "batch02", "level_4", "task_data_gathering", "group_adult_medicine", "sys_infectious_disease", "irtb_+1.30", "pending_owner_review"], "clinician_task": "data_gathering", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"Australian STI Management Guidelines. Mycoplasma genitalium\", \"url\": \"https://sti.guidelines.org.au/sexually-transmissible-infections/mycoplasma-genitalium/\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "M. genitalium grows very poorly in culture, which is not used clinically.", "B": "A Gram stain may show urethritis but cannot guide M. genitalium treatment.", "D": "Syphilis serology is part of STI screening but does not affect the M. genitalium regimen.", "E": "Chlamydia was already negative, and repeating the test will not change the regimen."}, "key_takeaways": ["For M. genitalium, use resistance-guided therapy: doxycycline, then azithromycin if macrolide-susceptible or moxifloxacin if resistant."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,1.3,0.5,'expert_prior','author_estimate (batch02)'),
('201f6547-c5b5-5541-ac5e-a9d716a689af'::uuid,'ZQ-0315','adult-medicine','cardiology','A 76-year-old man in Wagga Wagga, NSW, collapsed while walking up a hill and recovered within a minute. He has had increasing breathlessness on exertion for three months. He has an ejection systolic murmur at the right upper sternal edge radiating to the carotids, with a soft second heart sound. ECG shows sinus rhythm with left ventricular hypertrophy.

Which one of the following echocardiographic findings best indicates that he needs aortic valve intervention?','["Peak velocity 2.8 m/s with a mean gradient of 18 mmHg", "Aortic valve sclerosis without restricted leaflet opening", "Moderate mitral regurgitation", "Peak aortic jet velocity 4.4 m/s with a mean gradient of 46 mmHg", "Left ventricular ejection fraction of 60%"]'::jsonb,'D','CORRECT ANSWER

D. Peak aortic jet velocity 4.4 m/s with a mean gradient of 46 mmHg

---

Severe aortic stenosis is defined by a peak velocity of at least 4 m/s, a mean gradient of at least 40 mmHg or a valve area under 1.0 cm². When a patient with severe AS has symptoms (exertional syncope, angina or breathlessness), surgical or transcatheter valve replacement is indicated, because prognosis is poor without it.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Severe AS (≥4 m/s, mean gradient ≥40 mmHg, AVA <1.0 cm²) with symptoms is an indication for valve replacement.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "2021 ESC/EACTS Guidelines for the management of valvular heart disease. Eur Heart J 2022;43:561-632", "url": "https://academic.oup.com/eurheartj/article/43/7/561/6358470"}','difficult','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "batch02", "review_status": "draft_pending_owner_review", "tags": ["ZQ-0315", "zyntra_bank", "batch02", "level_5", "task_data_interpretation_synthesis", "group_adult_medicine", "sys_cardiovascular", "irtb_+1.60", "pending_owner_review"], "clinician_task": "data_interpretation_synthesis", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"2021 ESC/EACTS Guidelines for the management of valvular heart disease. Eur Heart J 2022;43:561-632\", \"url\": \"https://academic.oup.com/eurheartj/article/43/7/561/6358470\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "These values indicate mild aortic stenosis.", "B": "Sclerosis is not stenosis and does not need intervention.", "C": "This does not explain his symptoms or carry the same indication.", "E": "A preserved ejection fraction does not by itself indicate the need for intervention."}, "key_takeaways": ["Severe AS (≥4 m/s, mean gradient ≥40 mmHg, AVA <1.0 cm²) with symptoms is an indication for valve replacement."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,1.6,0.5,'expert_prior','author_estimate (batch02)'),
('8a98b03f-0811-5c35-92d7-e003f8d22d9c'::uuid,'ZQ-0316','adult-surgery','general-surgery','A 48-year-old man presents to an emergency department in Bunbury, WA, with 10 hours of severe constant epigastric pain radiating to his back and vomiting. He drinks about 8 standard drinks a day. Pulse 104/min, BP 128/80 mmHg, temperature 37.6°C. He has epigastric tenderness without peritonism.

Which one of the following blood tests is most useful to confirm the likely diagnosis?','["Serum lipase", "Serum amylase", "C-reactive protein", "Serum calcium", "Serum triglycerides"]'::jsonb,'A','CORRECT ANSWER

A. Serum lipase

---

Acute pancreatitis is diagnosed when at least two of three criteria are met: characteristic pain, raised amylase or lipase (at least three times the upper limit of normal), and imaging findings. Australian Prescriber states lipase is now the preferred test. It is more sensitive, particularly in alcohol-related pancreatitis, and stays raised longer, so the diagnostic window is wider.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Lipase (≥3 × ULN) is the preferred blood test for diagnosing acute pancreatitis.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "Basnayake C, Ratnam D. Blood tests for acute pancreatitis. Australian Prescriber", "url": "https://australianprescriber.tg.org.au/articles/blood-tests-for-acute-pancreatitis.html"}','easy','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "batch02", "review_status": "draft_pending_owner_review", "tags": ["ZQ-0316", "zyntra_bank", "batch02", "level_2", "task_data_gathering", "group_adult_surgery", "sys_general_surgery", "irtb_-0.80", "pending_owner_review"], "clinician_task": "data_gathering", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"Basnayake C, Ratnam D. Blood tests for acute pancreatitis. Australian Prescriber\", \"url\": \"https://australianprescriber.tg.org.au/articles/blood-tests-for-acute-pancreatitis.html\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"B": "Amylase is less sensitive, especially in alcohol-related disease, and returns to normal sooner.", "C": "CRP helps assess severity at about 48 hours but does not make the diagnosis.", "D": "Calcium is part of severity assessment and looking for a cause, not diagnosis.", "E": "Triglycerides help find a cause. They do not confirm pancreatitis."}, "key_takeaways": ["Lipase (≥3 × ULN) is the preferred blood test for diagnosing acute pancreatitis."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,-0.8,0.5,'expert_prior','author_estimate (batch02)'),
('6c68abc8-d935-5868-b9bb-6652f9311aa9'::uuid,'ZQ-0317','adult-surgery','urology','A 61-year-old man in Toowong, Qld, has a PSA of 6.8 µg/L on testing he chose after discussion with his GP. A repeat PSA six weeks later is 7.1 µg/L. Digital rectal examination finds a smooth, mildly enlarged prostate. He has no urinary symptoms and a urine dipstick is normal. He is referred to a urologist.

Which one of the following is the most appropriate next investigation?','["Multiparametric MRI of the prostate", "Systematic 12-core transrectal biopsy", "CT abdomen and pelvis", "Nuclear medicine bone scan", "Repeat PSA in 12 months"]'::jsonb,'A','CORRECT ANSWER

A. Multiparametric MRI of the prostate

---

The 2026 Australian guidelines for early detection of prostate cancer recommend multiparametric MRI as the next test after a confirmed raised PSA, before any biopsy. Biopsy is recommended for PI-RADS 4–5, or for PI-RADS 3 with a PSA density of 0.15 or more. This approach avoids unnecessary biopsies and finds fewer insignificant cancers.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
After a confirmed raised PSA, the next test is mpMRI. Biopsy depends on the PI-RADS score and PSA density.

---

REFERENCES
