('b5996e7b-a21e-56a3-b46d-0dc19c835d2b'::uuid,'ZQ-0318','adult-surgery','trauma','A 24-year-old man was tackled during a rugby league match in Penrith, NSW, and now has left upper quadrant pain and left shoulder-tip pain. At a major trauma centre, after 1 L of crystalloid, his pulse is 88/min and BP 128/76 mmHg, stable over an hour. He has left upper quadrant tenderness without peritonism. FAST shows a small amount of free fluid in the left upper quadrant.

Which one of the following is the most appropriate next investigation?','["CT abdomen with intravenous contrast in arterial and portal venous phases", "Emergency laparotomy", "Non-contrast CT abdomen", "Diagnostic peritoneal lavage", "Abdominal X-ray"]'::jsonb,'A','CORRECT ANSWER

A. CT abdomen with intravenous contrast in arterial and portal venous phases

---

The Queensland adult blunt splenic injury guideline states that patients with suspected intra-abdominal injury or bleeding should have a CT abdomen with both arterial and portal venous phases. This grades the injury and shows contrast extravasation (blush). Those findings decide between non-operative management, angioembolisation and surgery in a haemodynamically stable patient.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
In stable blunt abdominal trauma, CT with arterial and portal venous phases grades splenic injury and detects a contrast blush.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "Queensland Health. Clinical practice guideline: Blunt splenic injury (adult)", "url": "https://www.health.qld.gov.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0021/1430085/blunt-splenic-injury-clinical-guideline.pdf"}','moderate','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "batch02", "review_status": "draft_pending_owner_review", "tags": ["ZQ-0318", "zyntra_bank", "batch02", "level_3", "task_data_gathering", "group_adult_surgery", "sys_trauma", "irtb_+0.50", "pending_owner_review"], "clinician_task": "data_gathering", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"Queensland Health. Clinical practice guideline: Blunt splenic injury (adult)\", \"url\": \"https://www.health.qld.gov.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0021/1430085/blunt-splenic-injury-clinical-guideline.pdf\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"B": "Surgery is for haemodynamic instability or peritonitis. He is stable.", "C": "Without contrast, CT cannot show active extravasation or vascular injury.", "D": "DPL is non-specific and largely replaced by FAST and CT in stable patients.", "E": "X-ray cannot assess solid organ injury."}, "key_takeaways": ["In stable blunt abdominal trauma, CT with arterial and portal venous phases grades splenic injury and detects a contrast blush."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,0.5,0.5,'expert_prior','author_estimate (batch02)'),
('fae04529-bd55-5b99-9baf-6bb737f3f574'::uuid,'ZQ-0319','adult-surgery','trauma','A 35-year-old motorcyclist arrives at an emergency department in Albury, NSW, after a high-speed crash. He is agitated and in severe respiratory distress. Pulse 138/min, BP 78/40 mmHg, SpO2 82% on high-flow oxygen. The trachea is deviated to the left, his neck veins are distended, and the right chest is hyper-resonant with absent breath sounds.

Which one of the following is the most appropriate immediate action?','["Urgent chest X-ray to confirm the diagnosis", "Large-volume crystalloid bolus for hypotension", "Immediate decompression of the right chest without waiting for imaging", "Rapid sequence intubation before any chest procedure", "Bedside echocardiography to look for tamponade"]'::jsonb,'C','CORRECT ANSWER

C. Immediate decompression of the right chest without waiting for imaging

---

This is a clinical diagnosis of tension pneumothorax with obstructive shock. The RCH trauma guideline states that tension pneumothorax with obstructive shock needs urgent pleural decompression by thoracostomy, using anatomical landmarks within the ''triangle of safety'', followed by a chest drain. Treatment must not wait for imaging.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Tension pneumothorax is a clinical diagnosis. Decompress immediately; do not wait for imaging.

---

REFERENCES
