('6fab4056-b75b-5eeb-a251-32955d299a52'::uuid,'ZQ-0320','adult-surgery','orthopaedics','A 22-year-old man fell onto his outstretched arm while skateboarding in Fremantle, WA. His right shoulder is squared off, he holds the arm slightly abducted and externally rotated, and there is a palpable fullness in front of the shoulder. Radial pulse is present.

Before reduction, which one of the following is the most important neurological assessment to document?','["Sensation over the first dorsal web space", "Little finger sensation", "Sensation over the lateral aspect of the upper arm (regimental badge area)", "Biceps reflex", "Thumb abduction power"]'::jsonb,'C','CORRECT ANSWER

C. Sensation over the lateral aspect of the upper arm (regimental badge area)

---

Anterior dislocation can stretch the axillary nerve where it winds around the surgical neck of the humerus. Check and document sensation over the regimental badge area (lateral deltoid) before and after reduction. A review of axillary nerve injury in glenohumeral dislocation warns that preserved sensation there does not rule out motor palsy, so deltoid function must be reassessed after reduction.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
In anterior shoulder dislocation, document regimental badge sensation (axillary nerve) before and after reduction.

---

