('aaae31af-a612-5624-ab17-e63fcb26b094'::uuid,'ZQ-0321','adult-surgery','vascular-surgery','A 74-year-old man who smokes presents to a regional emergency department in Dubbo, NSW, with sudden severe back and left flank pain and a brief collapse. Pulse 112/min, BP 86/54 mmHg. He is alert and talking. There is a tender pulsatile mass in the epigastrium. Bedside ultrasound shows an infrarenal aorta of 7.2 cm.

Which one of the following is the most appropriate management?','["Give fluids to keep systolic BP above 120 mmHg before transfer", "Permissive hypotension while he is alert, with immediate referral to vascular surgery for repair", "CT angiogram first, with surgical referral only if rupture is confirmed", "IV labetalol to lower blood pressure", "Admit under general medicine for analgesia and serial ultrasound"]'::jsonb,'B','CORRECT ANSWER

B. Permissive hypotension while he is alert, with immediate referral to vascular surgery for repair

---

Suspected ruptured AAA in a patient whose cerebration is preserved should be managed with permissive (controlled) hypotension. Avoid aggressive fluids, which dilute clotting factors and can dislodge the clot. Then arrange immediate transfer for endovascular or open repair. The ESVS 2024 guideline recommends permissive hypotension. A CT angiogram is only done if he is stable enough and it will not delay repair.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
For a ruptured AAA: permissive hypotension while he remains alert, and immediate vascular repair.

---

REFERENCES
