('3320a622-fa36-5427-9709-020df63b2997'::uuid,'ZQ-0322','adult-surgery','urology','A 23-year-old man presents to a GP in Newcastle, NSW, with three days of right scrotal pain and swelling that began gradually. He has a new female partner and does not use condoms. Temperature 37.8°C. The right epididymis is swollen and tender, the testis lies normally, and the cremasteric reflex is present. There is no urethral discharge.

Which one of the following specimens is most important to collect before starting antibiotics?','["First-pass urine for chlamydia and gonorrhoea NAAT, plus a midstream urine", "Midstream urine for culture only", "Scrotal ultrasound before any other test", "Serum tumour markers (AFP, β-hCG)", "Blood cultures"]'::jsonb,'A','CORRECT ANSWER

A. First-pass urine for chlamydia and gonorrhoea NAAT, plus a midstream urine

---

The Australian STI Management Guidelines recommend collecting first-pass urine (for chlamydia and gonorrhoea NAAT) and a midstream urine for epididymo-orchitis, plus a urethral swab for MC&S if there is a discharge. In men under 35, sexually transmitted organisms are the usual cause. Doppler ultrasound is only needed if the diagnosis is uncertain. If torsion is suspected, refer urgently without waiting for an ultrasound.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
In suspected epididymo-orchitis, collect first-pass urine for chlamydia and gonorrhoea NAAT and an MSU before treatment.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "Australian STI Management Guidelines. Epididymo-orchitis", "url": "https://sti.guidelines.org.au/syndromes/epididymo-orchitis/"}','moderate','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "batch02", "review_status": "draft_pending_owner_review", "tags": ["ZQ-0322", "zyntra_bank", "batch02", "level_3", "task_data_gathering", "group_adult_surgery", "sys_urology", "irtb_+0.40", "pending_owner_review"], "clinician_task": "data_gathering", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"Australian STI Management Guidelines. Epididymo-orchitis\", \"url\": \"https://sti.guidelines.org.au/syndromes/epididymo-orchitis/\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"B": "MSU alone misses chlamydia and gonorrhoea, the likely causes at his age.", "C": "Ultrasound is for diagnostic uncertainty. His presentation is typical.", "D": "Tumour markers are for a testicular mass, not acute inflammation.", "E": "He is not septic."}, "key_takeaways": ["In suspected epididymo-orchitis, collect first-pass urine for chlamydia and gonorrhoea NAAT and an MSU before treatment."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,0.4,0.5,'expert_prior','author_estimate (batch02)'),
('6f1b7ef0-f833-55f4-9030-4c56e610c5da'::uuid,'ZQ-0323','adult-surgery','general-surgery','A 56-year-old man in Rockhampton, Qld, has had severe epigastric pain for three hours that came on suddenly and is now generalised. He takes ibuprofen regularly for knee osteoarthritis. He is lying very still. Pulse 110/min, BP 108/70 mmHg, temperature 37.6°C. His abdomen is rigid with generalised guarding and absent bowel sounds. Lipase is 90 U/L (<60).

Which one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?','["Acute pancreatitis", "Biliary colic", "Inferior myocardial infarction", "Acute mesenteric ischaemia", "Perforated peptic ulcer"]'::jsonb,'E','CORRECT ANSWER

E. Perforated peptic ulcer

---

Sudden severe epigastric pain that becomes generalised peritonitis (a board-like abdomen in a patient lying still) in a regular NSAID user strongly suggests a perforated peptic ulcer. The WSES guideline notes that a negative erect X-ray does not exclude perforation, so when there are clear signs of peritonitis CT should be the first imaging test. Lipase can rise slightly with perforation, while pancreatitis needs a lipase at least three times normal.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Sudden epigastric pain with peritonitis in an NSAID user suggests a perforated peptic ulcer. Mild lipase rises can occur.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "Tarasconi A et al. Perforated and bleeding peptic ulcer: WSES guidelines. World J Emerg Surg 2020;15:3 (PMC)", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6947898/"}','moderate','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "batch02", "review_status": "draft_pending_owner_review", "tags": ["ZQ-0323", "zyntra_bank", "batch02", "level_3", "task_data_interpretation_synthesis", "group_adult_surgery", "sys_general_surgery", "irtb_+0.30", "pending_owner_review"], "clinician_task": "data_interpretation_synthesis", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"Tarasconi A et al. Perforated and bleeding peptic ulcer: WSES guidelines. World J Emerg Surg 2020;15:3 (PMC)\", \"url\": \"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6947898/\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "Lipase is only mildly raised (pancreatitis needs more than three times the upper limit) and a board-like abdomen is less typical.", "B": "Biliary colic does not cause generalised peritonitis.", "C": "An MI can cause epigastric pain but not a rigid abdomen.", "D": "Mesenteric ischaemia causes pain out of proportion to examination findings early on. A rigid abdomen from the start fits perforation better."}, "key_takeaways": ["Sudden epigastric pain with peritonitis in an NSAID user suggests a perforated peptic ulcer. Mild lipase rises can occur."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,0.3,0.5,'expert_prior','author_estimate (batch02)'),
('c62f37a2-8a73-523b-994d-bf17e7fd95f5'::uuid,'ZQ-0324','adult-surgery','ent-ophthalmology','A 28-year-old woman presents to an emergency department in Bundaberg, Qld, with five days of worsening sore throat, now worse on the left. She can barely open her mouth, has a muffled ''hot potato'' voice and is drooling. Temperature 38.6°C. Her airway is patent and SpO2 is 98% on room air. The left soft palate is swollen and the uvula is pushed to the right.

Which one of the following is the most appropriate management?','["Oral phenoxymethylpenicillin and discharge", "Emergency tracheostomy", "ENT referral for drainage (needle aspiration or incision) with intravenous antibiotics", "Immediate tonsillectomy", "IV dexamethasone alone"]'::jsonb,'C','CORRECT ANSWER

C. ENT referral for drainage (needle aspiration or incision) with intravenous antibiotics

---

Trismus, a hot potato voice and a uvula pushed away from the affected side indicate a peritonsillar abscess (quinsy). The PCH WA guideline lists trismus, a unilateral fluctuant swelling extending to the soft palate and uvular deviation as typical. Management is admission under ENT for IV antibiotics with abscess drainage, plus airway assessment and a dose of IV corticosteroid.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Quinsy: drain (aspirate or incise) and give IV penicillin-based antibiotics.

---

REFERENCES
