('f1ad0554-9523-5283-8288-1809f8e08545'::uuid,'ZQ-0325','adult-surgery','renal','A 69-year-old woman is on the surgical ward in Townsville, Qld, on day 1 after an open right hemicolectomy. Her urine output has been 15 mL/h for four hours (weight 70 kg). Her IV fluids were reduced overnight. Pulse 106/min, BP 104/62 mmHg, capillary refill 4 seconds. Her catheter flushes freely.

Investigations:
- Creatinine 128 µmol/L (pre-op 72)
- Urea 14.2 mmol/L
- Urine sodium 9 mmol/L
- Fractional excretion of sodium 0.5%

Which one of the following is the most likely cause of her oliguria?','["Acute tubular necrosis", "Blocked urinary catheter", "Ureteric injury during surgery", "Post-renal obstruction from urinary retention", "Prerenal acute kidney injury due to hypovolaemia"]'::jsonb,'E','CORRECT ANSWER

E. Prerenal acute kidney injury due to hypovolaemia

---

She is tachycardic with slow capillary refill, her fluids were cut back, the catheter is patent, and her urine sodium is low (<20 mmol/L) with an FENa below 1%. KDIGO notes that a high urine osmolality with low urine sodium in oliguria shows intact tubular function. In this setting that points to a volume-responsive (prerenal) cause. Give a fluid bolus and reassess, keeping in mind KDIGO''s caution that such indices do not mean the AKI is benign.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Post-operative oliguria with low urine sodium and FENa below 1% is prerenal. Give fluid and reassess.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "KDIGO Clinical Practice Guideline for Acute Kidney Injury (2012)", "url": "https://kdigo.org/wp-content/uploads/2016/10/KDIGO-2012-AKI-Guideline-English.pdf"}','difficult','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "batch02", "review_status": "draft_pending_owner_review", "tags": ["ZQ-0325", "zyntra_bank", "batch02", "level_4", "task_data_interpretation_synthesis", "group_adult_surgery", "sys_renal", "irtb_+1.00", "pending_owner_review"], "clinician_task": "data_interpretation_synthesis", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"KDIGO Clinical Practice Guideline for Acute Kidney Injury (2012)\", \"url\": \"https://kdigo.org/wp-content/uploads/2016/10/KDIGO-2012-AKI-Guideline-English.pdf\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "ATN causes a urine sodium above 40 mmol/L and an FENa above 2%.", "B": "The catheter flushes freely.", "C": "A ureteric injury would not fit these urine indices or the fluid history.", "D": "She has a working catheter."}, "key_takeaways": ["Post-operative oliguria with low urine sodium and FENa below 1% is prerenal. Give fluid and reassess."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,1.0,0.5,'expert_prior','author_estimate (batch02)'),
('d6e6ba18-bdd9-5b67-8f31-85e03fae050f'::uuid,'ZQ-0326','womens-health','obstetrics','A 24-year-old woman at 11 weeks'' gestation is admitted to a hospital in Shepparton, Vic, after two weeks of persistent vomiting. She has kept almost nothing down for five days and has lost 6% of her body weight. Pulse 108/min. Urine ketones 3+.

Investigations:
- Sodium 136 mmol/L, potassium 3.2 mmol/L
- Glucose 3.9 mmol/L

IV fluids, potassium replacement and antiemetics are planned.

Which one of the following should be given before any glucose-containing IV fluid?','["Intravenous ondansetron", "Oral pyridoxine", "Intravenous hydrocortisone", "Intravenous thiamine", "Intravenous iron"]'::jsonb,'D','CORRECT ANSWER

D. Intravenous thiamine

---

After prolonged vomiting and poor intake, thiamine stores are depleted. Glucose can then precipitate Wernicke encephalopathy. The NSW Health 2022 guideline recommends IV thiamine 100 mg daily when oral intake is poor or IV glucose is needed. Glucose-containing fluids should only be used after thiamine has been given and hyponatraemia excluded. Rehydration uses 0.9% sodium chloride with potassium as needed.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
In hyperemesis with poor intake, give IV thiamine before glucose to prevent Wernicke encephalopathy.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "NSW Health. Nausea and Vomiting in Pregnancy and Hyperemesis Gravidarum guideline (2022)", "url": "https://www.seslhd.health.nsw.gov.au/sites/default/files/groups/Royal_Hospital_for_Women/Mothersafe/documents/Factsheets/NVP/Guideline-NSWHealthNauseaandVomitinginPregnancyandHyperemesisGravidarum2022.pdf"}','moderate','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "batch02", "review_status": "draft_pending_owner_review", "tags": ["ZQ-0326", "zyntra_bank", "batch02", "level_3", "task_management", "group_womens_health", "sys_obstetrics", "irtb_+0.30", "pending_owner_review"], "clinician_task": "management", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"NSW Health. Nausea and Vomiting in Pregnancy and Hyperemesis Gravidarum guideline (2022)\", \"url\": \"https://www.seslhd.health.nsw.gov.au/sites/default/files/groups/Royal_Hospital_for_Women/Mothersafe/documents/Factsheets/NVP/Guideline-NSWHealthNauseaandVomitinginPregnancyandHyperemesisGravidarum2022.pdf\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "Antiemetics are part of treatment but do not prevent Wernicke encephalopathy.", "B": "Pyridoxine is used for mild nausea and vomiting in pregnancy. It does not replace thiamine.", "C": "Steroids are reserved for refractory hyperemesis.", "E": "Iron has no relevance to this risk."}, "key_takeaways": ["In hyperemesis with poor intake, give IV thiamine before glucose to prevent Wernicke encephalopathy."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,0.3,0.5,'expert_prior','author_estimate (batch02)'),
('65c2a05b-f2da-5082-9a0a-1f937efdd550'::uuid,'ZQ-0327','womens-health','gynaecology','A 27-year-old woman presents to an emergency department in Mount Gambier, SA, with sudden severe right iliac fossa pain that began four hours ago while she was exercising, with vomiting. Urine β-hCG is negative. She is very tender in the right adnexa. Pelvic ultrasound shows a 7 cm right ovarian cyst, an enlarged right ovary and free fluid.

Which one of the following is the most appropriate next step?','["Urgent gynaecology review for laparoscopy", "Repeat ultrasound in 24 hours", "CT abdomen to exclude appendicitis", "Discharge with analgesia and outpatient gynaecology review", "Serum CA-125"]'::jsonb,'A','CORRECT ANSWER

A. Urgent gynaecology review for laparoscopy

---

An Australian Family Physician review describes ovarian torsion as a gynaecological emergency that needs urgent review. The main risk factor is an ovarian mass larger than 5 cm. Surgery is laparoscopic de-torsion, keeping the ovary where possible, with oophorectomy if it is not viable. Sudden pain with vomiting, a 7 cm cyst and an enlarged ovary make torsion likely, so she should not wait for further tests.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Suspected ovarian torsion (especially with a mass over 5 cm) is a gynaecological emergency. Get urgent gynaecology review for laparoscopy.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "RACGP Australian Family Physician 2015. Investigation and management of an ovarian mass", "url": "https://www.racgp.org.au/afp/2015/january-february/investigation-and-management-of-an-ovarian-mass"}','easy','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "batch02", "review_status": "draft_pending_owner_review", "tags": ["ZQ-0327", "zyntra_bank", "batch02", "level_2", "task_data_gathering", "group_womens_health", "sys_gynaecology", "irtb_-0.80", "pending_owner_review"], "clinician_task": "data_gathering", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"RACGP Australian Family Physician 2015. Investigation and management of an ovarian mass\", \"url\": \"https://www.racgp.org.au/afp/2015/january-february/investigation-and-management-of-an-ovarian-mass\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"B": "Delay risks losing the ovary.", "C": "CT delays definitive treatment when torsion is clinically likely.", "D": "Torsion is an emergency.", "E": "CA-125 does not change urgent management."}, "key_takeaways": ["Suspected ovarian torsion (especially with a mass over 5 cm) is a gynaecological emergency. Get urgent gynaecology review for laparoscopy."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,-0.8,0.5,'expert_prior','author_estimate (batch02)'),
('93abec03-51a6-5d86-951a-979b91564e8f'::uuid,'ZQ-0328','womens-health','gynaecology','A 22-year-old woman sees her GP in Ipswich, Qld, with severe period pain for four years that is not controlled by regular NSAIDs. She also has pain with deep penetration and painful defecation during her periods. She has had vaginal intercourse and agrees to an internal examination, which shows mild posterior fornix tenderness.

Which one of the following is the most appropriate next investigation?','["Diagnostic laparoscopy before any imaging", "Serum CA-125", "CT of the pelvis", "No investigation; reassure that this is primary dysmenorrhoea", "Transvaginal pelvic ultrasound performed by an experienced sonographer"]'::jsonb,'E','CORRECT ANSWER

E. Transvaginal pelvic ultrasound performed by an experienced sonographer

---

RANZCOG''s Australian Living Evidence Guideline on endometriosis (endorsed by the RACGP) notes that more cases can now be diagnosed accurately with non-invasive techniques such as transvaginal ultrasound and MRI. Endometriosis no longer always needs surgery for diagnosis, and it can be diagnosed and managed in primary care. A normal scan does not exclude superficial disease. First-line hormonal treatment can still be offered.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Suspected endometriosis can be investigated first with an expert transvaginal ultrasound. Laparoscopy is not required first.

---

REFERENCES
