('c3ea305d-d72f-5861-874a-490845af0182'::uuid,'ZQ-0329','womens-health','gynaecology','A 19-year-old woman phones a clinic in Wollongong, NSW. She takes a combined oral contraceptive pill. She started her new pack three days ago, two days late after the hormone-free week, and has since missed the pills for day 2 and day 3. She had unprotected sex two nights ago. She feels well.

Which one of the following is the most appropriate advice?','["Take a pill now; no other precautions needed", "Stop the pack and start a new one next week", "Take a pill now, use condoms for 7 days, and take emergency contraception", "Take two pills now and use condoms for 2 days", "Do a pregnancy test now and decide afterwards"]'::jsonb,'C','CORRECT ANSWER

C. Take a pill now, use condoms for 7 days, and take emergency contraception

---

Family Planning NSW advice: a pill more than 24 hours late means taking the most recent missed pill, continuing the pack, and using condoms for 7 days. When more than one pill is missed in the first week of a pack (after the hormone-free interval) and there has been unprotected sex in the previous 5 days, emergency contraception is recommended.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
More than one pill missed in week 1 with unprotected sex in the past 5 days: take a pill, use condoms for 7 days, and take EC.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "Family Planning NSW. Combined oral contraceptive pill troubleshooting", "url": "https://www.fpnsw.org.au/factsheets/individuals/contraception/combined-oral-contraceptive-pill-troubleshooting"}','moderate','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "batch02", "review_status": "draft_pending_owner_review", "tags": ["ZQ-0329", "zyntra_bank", "batch02", "level_3", "task_management", "group_womens_health", "sys_gynaecology", "irtb_+0.60", "pending_owner_review"], "clinician_task": "management", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"Family Planning NSW. Combined oral contraceptive pill troubleshooting\", \"url\": \"https://www.fpnsw.org.au/factsheets/individuals/contraception/combined-oral-contraceptive-pill-troubleshooting\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "With more than one pill missed, she is not protected.", "B": "This leaves her unprotected.", "D": "Seven days of condoms is the rule.", "E": "It is too early for a pregnancy test to be positive, and EC is time-dependent."}, "key_takeaways": ["More than one pill missed in week 1 with unprotected sex in the past 5 days: take a pill, use condoms for 7 days, and take EC."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,0.6,0.5,'expert_prior','author_estimate (batch02)'),
('e40fa06e-682f-5e8f-8261-7b0198fa4dce'::uuid,'ZQ-0330','womens-health','obstetrics','A 29-year-old woman at 27+3 weeks'' gestation presents to a tertiary hospital in Randwick, NSW, with regular painful contractions. Her cervix is 6 cm dilated, membranes are intact and fetal monitoring is normal. Birth is expected within hours. Betamethasone has been given.

Which one of the following is the most appropriate additional treatment?','["Nifedipine to stop labour", "Intravenous magnesium sulfate for fetal neuroprotection", "Indomethacin", "A second course of corticosteroids", "Progesterone pessaries"]'::jsonb,'B','CORRECT ANSWER

B. Intravenous magnesium sulfate for fetal neuroprotection

---

When birth is planned or expected within 24 hours at 24 to under 30 weeks, magnesium sulfate is given for fetal neuroprotection. It reduces the risk of cerebral palsy. This is the indication in the SESLHD/RHW local guideline, which follows the Australian national guideline.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Give MgSO4 for fetal neuroprotection when birth is expected within 24 hours at 24 to under 30 weeks.

---

