('55a41866-236c-5cfd-b80d-92b0693cdd36'::uuid,'ZQ-0333','child-health','paediatric-medicine','A 2-year-old boy is brought to a GP in Warrnambool, Vic, at 9 pm with a barking cough and hoarse voice that started that evening, after two days of a runny nose. He has soft stridor only when he is upset. There is no stridor at rest, no chest wall recession, and he is alert and playing. SpO2 98%.

Which one of the following is the most appropriate management?','["Nebulised adrenaline", "Oral amoxicillin", "A single dose of oral dexamethasone 0.15 mg/kg and discharge with safety-net advice", "Nebulised salbutamol", "Chest X-ray"]'::jsonb,'C','CORRECT ANSWER

C. A single dose of oral dexamethasone 0.15 mg/kg and discharge with safety-net advice

---

RCH guidance for mild croup (stridor only when upset) is a single dose of oral dexamethasone 0.15 mg/kg, or prednisolone 1 mg/kg. Steroids reduce reattendance and shorten symptoms. A barking cough with no stridor at all does not need steroids. Nebulised adrenaline is for severe croup.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Croup with stridor: oral dexamethasone 0.15 mg/kg. Nebulised adrenaline only for severe croup.

---

REFERENCES
