('516d861e-d5e0-5197-a708-0aa2008fceeb'::uuid,'ZQ-0334','child-health','paediatric-medicine','A 20-month-old girl is brought to an emergency department in Gosford, NSW, after a 2-minute generalised tonic–clonic seizure at home during a fever. She had a runny nose for two days. She is now alert, interactive and back to normal. Temperature 39.0°C. There is no neck stiffness, rash or focal neurological signs, and she is fully immunised.

Which one of the following investigations is most appropriate?','["EEG", "CT brain", "Lumbar puncture", "Full blood count, electrolytes and blood culture", "No routine investigations; look for the source of the fever clinically"]'::jsonb,'E','CORRECT ANSWER

E. No routine investigations; look for the source of the fever clinically

---

This is a simple febrile seizure. RCH guidelines state that investigations are low yield and not routinely needed. Assess for the focus of the fever clinically. Investigations are targeted only for atypical or complex seizures or a suspected serious infection.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Simple febrile seizure in a well child: no routine tests; find the fever source clinically.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "RCH Clinical Practice Guidelines: Febrile seizure", "url": "https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/Febrile_seizure/"}','moderate','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "batch02", "review_status": "draft_pending_owner_review", "tags": ["ZQ-0334", "zyntra_bank", "batch02", "level_3", "task_data_gathering", "group_child_health", "sys_paediatric_medicine", "irtb_-0.30", "pending_owner_review"], "clinician_task": "data_gathering", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"RCH Clinical Practice Guidelines: Febrile seizure\", \"url\": \"https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/Febrile_seizure/\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "An EEG does not predict recurrence or epilepsy after a simple febrile seizure.", "B": "CT is not indicated.", "C": "She is alert with no meningism, so LP is not indicated.", "D": "These are not routine."}, "key_takeaways": ["Simple febrile seizure in a well child: no routine tests; find the fever source clinically."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,-0.3,0.5,'expert_prior','author_estimate (batch02)'),
('5f907969-2b2c-5c08-ab03-376823844205'::uuid,'ZQ-0335','child-health','renal','A 4-year-old boy in Hobart, Tas, has had puffy eyes for one week and now has swollen legs. BP is normal. Urinalysis shows 4+ protein with no blood. Serum albumin is 15 g/L, creatinine is normal and complement is normal. A diagnosis of likely steroid-sensitive nephrotic syndrome is made. He weighs 18 kg (body surface area 0.74 m²).

Which one of the following is the most appropriate initial treatment?','["Renal biopsy before treatment", "IV albumin and furosemide only", "Cyclophosphamide", "Prednisolone 60 mg/m² daily for 4 weeks, then 40 mg/m² on alternate days for 4 weeks", "Prednisolone 1 mg/kg daily for 7 days"]'::jsonb,'D','CORRECT ANSWER

D. Prednisolone 60 mg/m² daily for 4 weeks, then 40 mg/m² on alternate days for 4 weeks

---

RCH guidelines for a first presentation recommend prednisolone 60 mg/m² (or 2 mg/kg, maximum 60 mg) daily for 4 weeks, then 40 mg/m² (or 1.5 mg/kg, maximum 40 mg) on alternate days for 4 weeks. A renal biopsy is not routinely needed in typical cases at this age.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
First-episode nephrotic syndrome: prednisolone 60 mg/m² daily for 4 weeks, then 40 mg/m² alternate days for 4 weeks.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "RCH Clinical Practice Guidelines: Nephrotic syndrome", "url": "https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/Nephrotic_syndrome/"}','moderate','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "batch02", "review_status": "draft_pending_owner_review", "tags": ["ZQ-0335", "zyntra_bank", "batch02", "level_3", "task_management", "group_child_health", "sys_renal", "irtb_+0.40", "pending_owner_review"], "clinician_task": "management", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"RCH Clinical Practice Guidelines: Nephrotic syndrome\", \"url\": \"https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/Nephrotic_syndrome/\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "A typical presentation at this age does not need a biopsy.", "B": "This is not treatment of the disease itself.", "C": "Cyclophosphamide is for frequently relapsing or steroid-dependent disease.", "E": "This dose and duration are wrong."}, "key_takeaways": ["First-episode nephrotic syndrome: prednisolone 60 mg/m² daily for 4 weeks, then 40 mg/m² alternate days for 4 weeks."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,0.4,0.5,'expert_prior','author_estimate (batch02)'),
('38557871-25f7-5fc1-80db-333d31118d3e'::uuid,'ZQ-0336','child-health','neonatology','A 4-week-old breastfed boy is brought to a GP in Kalgoorlie, WA, because he is still jaundiced. He is feeding well and gaining weight. His mother thinks his stools have been pale for a week. His urine is dark yellow.

Investigations:
- Total bilirubin 160 µmol/L
- Conjugated bilirubin 70 µmol/L
- Liver ultrasound: normal liver; gallbladder seen

Which one of the following is the most appropriate next step?','["Reassure: breast milk jaundice", "Urgent same-day discussion and referral to paediatric gastroenterology", "Repeat bilirubin in 2 weeks", "Stop breastfeeding for 48 hours", "Phototherapy"]'::jsonb,'B','CORRECT ANSWER

B. Urgent same-day discussion and referral to paediatric gastroenterology

---

A conjugated bilirubin above 20 µmol/L (or more than 20% of the total) is abnormal, and with pale stools it suggests biliary atresia until proven otherwise. RCH guidelines state that a normal ultrasound does not exclude biliary atresia. Urgent referral matters because outcomes of Kasai surgery are better when it is done early.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Prolonged jaundice with conjugated bilirubin above 20 µmol/L or pale stools needs urgent gastroenterology referral. A normal US does not exclude biliary atresia.

---

