• {"name": "RCH Clinical Practice Guidelines: Jaundice in early infancy", "url": "https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/Jaundice_in_early_infancy/"}','difficult','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "batch02", "review_status": "draft_pending_owner_review", "tags": ["ZQ-0336", "zyntra_bank", "batch02", "level_4", "task_data_interpretation_synthesis", "group_child_health", "sys_neonatology", "irtb_+1.30", "pending_owner_review"], "clinician_task": "data_interpretation_synthesis", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"RCH Clinical Practice Guidelines: Jaundice in early infancy\", \"url\": \"https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/Jaundice_in_early_infancy/\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "Breast milk jaundice is unconjugated.", "C": "Delay worsens the outcome.", "D": "Not indicated.", "E": "Phototherapy does not treat conjugated hyperbilirubinaemia."}, "key_takeaways": ["Prolonged jaundice with conjugated bilirubin above 20 µmol/L or pale stools needs urgent gastroenterology referral. A normal US does not exclude biliary atresia."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,1.3,0.5,'expert_prior','author_estimate (batch02)'),
('72e4b7f5-5aa3-5037-850a-7c5ef7594a31'::uuid,'ZQ-0337','child-health','orthopaedics','A 5-year-old girl is brought to an emergency department in Geraldton, WA, with one day of refusing to walk on her right leg. Temperature 38.9°C. She holds her hip flexed and externally rotated and resists all movement.

Investigations:
- WCC 17.8 × 10⁹/L
- CRP 96 mg/L
- ESR 55 mm/h
- Hip ultrasound: effusion

Which one of the following is the most appropriate interpretation and plan?','["Transient synovitis: NSAIDs and discharge", "MRI next week", "SUFE", "Likely septic arthritis; urgent orthopaedic review for joint aspiration and washout", "Perthes disease"]'::jsonb,'D','CORRECT ANSWER

D. Likely septic arthritis; urgent orthopaedic review for joint aspiration and washout

---

RCH guidelines describe septic arthritis as an orthopaedic emergency. An effusion on ultrasound does not distinguish septic arthritis from transient synovitis, but fever, refusal to bear weight and raised WCC, CRP and ESR all point to infection. She needs urgent orthopaedic referral for aspiration and washout, with IV antibiotics given after cultures are taken.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
A febrile child who won''t bear weight and has raised markers and an effusion has septic arthritis until proven otherwise; it is an orthopaedic emergency.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "RCH Clinical Practice Guidelines: The limping or non-weight bearing child", "url": "https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/child_with_limp/"}','moderate','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "batch02", "review_status": "draft_pending_owner_review", "tags": ["ZQ-0337", "zyntra_bank", "batch02", "level_3", "task_data_interpretation_synthesis", "group_child_health", "sys_orthopaedics", "irtb_+0.50", "pending_owner_review"], "clinician_task": "data_interpretation_synthesis", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"RCH Clinical Practice Guidelines: The limping or non-weight bearing child\", \"url\": \"https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/child_with_limp/\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "Her fever and inflammatory markers argue strongly against transient synovitis.", "B": "This is an emergency.", "C": "Slipped epiphysis is unlikely at age 5 and does not cause fever.", "E": "Perthes disease does not cause fever."}, "key_takeaways": ["A febrile child who won''t bear weight and has raised markers and an effusion has septic arthritis until proven otherwise; it is an orthopaedic emergency."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,0.5,0.5,'expert_prior','author_estimate (batch02)'),
('cb2bea47-999d-511c-821c-bfd08ffa9a90'::uuid,'ZQ-0338','child-health','paediatric-medicine','A 9-year-old boy with asthma eats a muesli bar containing peanut at school camp near Kiama, NSW. Within 10 minutes he has hives, lip swelling, a hoarse voice, wheeze and difficulty breathing. A nurse is present with an adrenaline autoinjector, salbutamol and oral antihistamine.

Which one of the following should be given first?','["Salbutamol via spacer", "Oral cetirizine", "Oral prednisolone", "Intramuscular adrenaline into the outer thigh", "Subcutaneous adrenaline"]'::jsonb,'D','CORRECT ANSWER

D. Intramuscular adrenaline into the outer thigh

---

He has anaphylaxis with respiratory involvement. RCH guidelines say IM adrenaline must be given first, and in a child with asthma, adrenaline comes before any asthma medicine. Antihistamines and steroids should not be given as treatment of anaphylaxis. Adrenaline should not be given subcutaneously.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Anaphylaxis in a child with asthma: IM adrenaline first, before salbutamol.

---

