('cc98feae-444b-5569-9157-f01c4131ec26'::uuid,'ZQ-0339','mental-health','anxiety-disorders','A 29-year-old man sees his GP in Perth, WA, after three emergency department visits in four months with sudden episodes of palpitations, chest tightness, tingling and fear of dying, each lasting about 15 minutes. ECG, troponin, TSH and full blood count were normal each time. He now avoids trains in case an attack happens. He drinks little alcohol and uses no drugs.

Which one of the following is the most appropriate first-line treatment?','["Regular alprazolam", "Propranolol as needed", "Referral to cardiology for Holter monitoring", "Cognitive behavioural therapy including interoceptive and in-vivo exposure", "Quetiapine"]'::jsonb,'D','CORRECT ANSWER

D. Cognitive behavioural therapy including interoceptive and in-vivo exposure

---

This is panic disorder with avoidance. The Australian comorbidity guidelines, citing the RANZCP guideline, recommend 8–12 sessions of face-to-face or guided digital CBT as first-line treatment for panic disorder. Medication can be used as an alternative or addition for moderate to severe illness or when CBT does not help enough. Benzodiazepines are not recommended for ongoing treatment.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
First-line treatment for panic disorder is CBT with exposure, with an SSRI as an alternative or addition. Avoid benzodiazepines.

---

