('ca6f68c8-f5ee-5bb7-ae89-6ae582ce6a62'::uuid,'ZQ-0340','mental-health','psychosis','A 26-year-old man with schizophrenia is reviewed by a community mental health team in Footscray, Vic. He still has persecutory delusions and auditory hallucinations that stop him working. He had 6 weeks of risperidone, with adherence confirmed because it was given as a long-acting injection at a therapeutic dose, and then 8 weeks of olanzapine 20 mg daily, with adherence confirmed by plasma levels. Neither helped. He does not use substances, and organic causes have been excluded.

Which one of the following best describes his illness, and what does it imply?','["Pseudo-resistance from non-adherence", "Schizoaffective disorder needing a mood stabiliser", "Treatment-resistant schizophrenia; a clozapine trial should be started promptly", "Ultra-treatment-resistant schizophrenia needing ECT", "Inadequate trial; continue olanzapine for another 6 months"]'::jsonb,'C','CORRECT ANSWER

C. Treatment-resistant schizophrenia; a clozapine trial should be started promptly

---

He has had two adequate antipsychotic trials, with confirmed adherence and adequate dose and duration, and still has significant symptoms. This meets the definition of treatment-resistant schizophrenia. The RANZCP-endorsed INTEGRATE guidelines say that if two adequate trials fail, a clozapine trial should be started promptly, aiming for plasma levels of 350–500 ng/mL.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Two adequate antipsychotic trials that have failed define treatment-resistant schizophrenia. Start clozapine promptly.

---

REFERENCES
