('8daaa145-5789-5085-af64-5eacd7cdb721'::uuid,'ZQ-0341','mental-health','neurology','A 32-year-old woman presents to a hospital in Logan, Qld, with sudden weakness of her right leg that began after a stressful week at work. She can barely lift her right leg off the bed. When she is asked to flex her left hip against resistance, strong involuntary extension of the right hip is felt. Reflexes are symmetrical, plantar responses are flexor and sensation is normal. MRI of the brain and spine is normal.

Which one of the following is the most appropriate interpretation?','["Malingering", "Positive Hoover sign consistent with functional neurological disorder", "Early multiple sclerosis", "Lumbar radiculopathy", "Stroke"]'::jsonb,'B','CORRECT ANSWER

B. Positive Hoover sign consistent with functional neurological disorder

---

A positive Hoover sign means hip extension is weak when tested directly but returns to normal during contralateral hip flexion. It is a positive clinical sign of functional weakness. FND is diagnosed on positive features, not by exclusion alone. It is not feigning. Treatment involves explaining the diagnosis clearly, then physiotherapy and psychological therapy.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Hoover sign is a positive sign of functional weakness. FND is diagnosed on positive features, and it is not feigning.

---

