('c7a644a3-7e6f-5a5c-8648-19fb3dc17b39'::uuid,'ZQ-0342','mental-health','eating-disorders','A 20-year-old university student in Newcastle, NSW, tells her GP she binge-eats several times a week and then makes herself vomit. Her BMI is 22 kg/m². She has bilateral parotid swelling and calluses on the backs of her fingers. She feels lightheaded at times.

Which one of the following investigations is most important at this visit?','["Serum amylase", "DEXA scan", "Thyroid function", "CT brain", "Serum electrolytes including potassium"]'::jsonb,'E','CORRECT ANSWER

E. Serum electrolytes including potassium

---

Self-induced vomiting causes hypokalaemic, hypochloraemic metabolic alkalosis, which can lead to arrhythmias. Australian Prescriber states that a physical examination, FBC, urea and electrolytes (including phosphate) and an ECG are mandatory to assess the physical complications of purging. Hypokalaemia may even be the first clue to an eating disorder. Psychological treatment is eating-disorder-focused CBT.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
In bulimia with purging, check potassium (hypokalaemic alkalosis can cause arrhythmia).

---

