('c600837c-a70d-5cd5-8faf-61fd940ac9fd'::uuid,'ZQ-0343','mental-health','cognitive-disorders','An 84-year-old woman with Alzheimer disease in a residential aged care facility in Launceston, Tas, has become agitated in the late afternoons over the past five days, calling out and pacing. She communicates little verbally. Staff ask the GP for ''something to settle her''. Her regular medicines have not changed.

Which one of the following is the most appropriate first step in assessment?','["Start regular risperidone", "CT brain", "Assess for delirium, pain and other unmet physical needs such as constipation or urinary retention", "Repeat cognitive testing (MMSE)", "EEG"]'::jsonb,'C','CORRECT ANSWER

C. Assess for delirium, pain and other unmet physical needs such as constipation or urinary retention

---

The NSW Health BPSD handbook asks first whether delirium is present and whether the person could be in pain, need the toilet, or have other unmet basic needs. Delirium causes and pain must be treated before behaviour is attributed to dementia. A structured pain tool (for example, the Abbey Pain Scale), examination, bowel and bladder review and a look for infection are first steps. Non-pharmacological strategies come before medication.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
New BPSD: look for delirium, pain and unmet needs first.

---

