('d60ea23f-ac8e-51f1-9716-ea07b44ae5cb'::uuid,'ZQ-0344','mental-health','substance-use','A 58-year-old man in Toowoomba, Qld, has taken oxazepam 30 mg three times daily for six years. It was first prescribed after a bereavement. He would now like to stop. He has no history of seizures and does not drink alcohol.

Which one of the following is the most appropriate plan?','["Switch to an equivalent dose of diazepam and reduce gradually over weeks to months", "Stop immediately", "Reduce by 50% each week", "Swap to zolpidem", "Start flumazenil"]'::jsonb,'A','CORRECT ANSWER

A. Switch to an equivalent dose of diazepam and reduce gradually over weeks to months

---

Stopping benzodiazepines abruptly after long-term use risks withdrawal seizures. Australian Prescriber advises a gradual dose reduction over weeks to months, often after switching to diazepam because its long half-life gives a smoother taper.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Stop long-term benzodiazepines with a slow taper, often after switching to diazepam.

---

