('14f024c0-4ec9-5d99-a656-46e40e596ca4'::uuid,'ZQ-0348','population-health-ethics','ethics-law','A 24-year-old unvaccinated man returned to Sydney from Southeast Asia nine days ago. He sees a GP with three days of fever, cough, conjunctivitis and coryza, and since this morning a maculopapular rash spreading from his face. The GP isolates him in a separate room. Serology and PCR swabs are collected.

Which one of the following is the most appropriate next action regarding public health?','["Wait for PCR confirmation before notifying", "Notify the local public health unit promptly by telephone, without waiting for laboratory confirmation", "Notify only if the patient consents", "Contact his workplace directly to warn colleagues", "No notification needed because he is an adult"]'::jsonb,'B','CORRECT ANSWER

B. Notify the local public health unit promptly by telephone, without waiting for laboratory confirmation

---

The NSW Health measles control guideline asks emergency departments and medical practitioners to report suspected cases to the local public health unit promptly, and to seek laboratory confirmation in all suspected cases. Early notification of a suspected case allows contact tracing and post-exposure prophylaxis within the time when it works. Cases should stay home until 4 days after rash onset.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Suspected measles: isolate, test and notify the public health unit promptly on clinical suspicion.

---

