('a7f1f579-3926-5e4a-a81f-93fc51879d2d'::uuid,'ZQ-0349','population-health-ethics','screening','A 46-year-old woman sees her GP in Darwin, NT, with a new 2 cm firm lump in her left breast that she noticed three weeks ago. She asks whether she should book a free BreastScreen mammogram.

Which one of the following is the most appropriate advice?','["BreastScreen is for women without symptoms; she needs diagnostic assessment through her GP", "Book BreastScreen because she is over 40", "Wait until she is 50", "Self-examine for 3 months", "Genetic testing first"]'::jsonb,'A','CORRECT ANSWER

A. BreastScreen is for women without symptoms; she needs diagnostic assessment through her GP

---

BreastScreen Australia invites women aged 50–74 every 2 years, and women aged 40–49 or 75 and over may book if they have no symptoms. Women with symptoms should see their doctor for diagnostic imaging (the triple test).

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
BreastScreen is for women without symptoms. A breast lump needs the diagnostic triple-test pathway.

---

