('4e16107b-45d4-5bba-8543-9bcd6cbe78ab'::uuid,'ZQ-0350','population-health-ethics','screening','A 23-year-old woman starting work at an abattoir in Wagga Wagga, NSW, attends for Q fever vaccination. Pre-vaccination testing shows a negative Q fever antibody serology, and the skin test, read 7 days later, is positive (induration 6 mm).

Which one of the following is the most appropriate interpretation and action?','["She is sensitised and should not be vaccinated", "Vaccinate because serology is negative", "Repeat the skin test in 1 week", "Vaccinate with a half dose", "Treat with doxycycline then vaccinate"]'::jsonb,'A','CORRECT ANSWER

A. She is sensitised and should not be vaccinated

---

The Australian Immunisation Handbook states that a positive result on either serology or skin test means the person is sensitised and must not be vaccinated, because of the risk of severe local reactions. Vaccination goes ahead only if both tests are negative.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Q fever: vaccinate only if both serology and skin test are negative.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "Australian Immunisation Handbook. Interpreting serological and skin test results before Q fever vaccination", "url": "https://immunisationhandbook.health.gov.au/resources/tables/table-interpreting-serological-and-skin-test-results-before-q-fever-vaccination"}','difficult','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "batch02", "review_status": "draft_pending_owner_review", "tags": ["ZQ-0350", "zyntra_bank", "batch02", "level_4", "task_data_interpretation_synthesis", "group_population_health_ethics", "sys_screening", "irtb_+1.10", "pending_owner_review"], "clinician_task": "data_interpretation_synthesis", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"Australian Immunisation Handbook. Interpreting serological and skin test results before Q fever vaccination\", \"url\": \"https://immunisationhandbook.health.gov.au/resources/tables/table-interpreting-serological-and-skin-test-results-before-q-fever-vaccination\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"B": "A positive skin test still means she is sensitised.", "C": "This is not recommended.", "D": "There is no half-dose regimen.", "E": "Not indicated."}, "key_takeaways": ["Q fever: vaccinate only if both serology and skin test are negative."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,1.1,0.5,'expert_prior','author_estimate (batch02)'),
('df11b8d6-10ac-556e-93a3-55d3653ee661'::uuid,'ZQ-0351','adult-medicine','cardiology','A 74-year-old man is brought to an emergency department in Ballina, NSW, after feeling faint all morning. He is pale and sweaty. Pulse 36/min and regular, BP 82/50 mmHg. His ECG shows sinus bradycardia at 36/min with a normal QRS. He takes no rate-slowing medicines and has not had a cardiac transplant.

Which one of the following is the most appropriate initial drug treatment?','["Intravenous adrenaline infusion as the first drug", "Intravenous adenosine 6 mg", "Intravenous amiodarone 300 mg", "Observation only because sinus rhythm is present", "Intravenous atropine 500–600 micrograms, repeated every 3–5 minutes as needed"]'::jsonb,'E','CORRECT ANSWER

E. Intravenous atropine 500–600 micrograms, repeated every 3–5 minutes as needed

---

He has a bradyarrhythmia with adverse signs (systolic BP <90 mmHg, heart rate <40/min). ANZCOR Guideline 11.9 says atropine is the first treatment: 500–600 micrograms IV, repeated every 3–5 minutes up to 3 mg. If this fails, low-dose adrenaline is the second-line drug, with pacing for patients who do not respond or who are at high risk of asystole.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Bradycardia with adverse signs: give IV atropine 500–600 mcg (up to 3 mg), then adrenaline or pacing if that fails.

---

