('eaf9b9e6-7f41-54eb-a214-5e2da74390ec'::uuid,'ZQ-0352','adult-medicine','cardiology','A 66-year-old woman presents to an emergency department in Warrnambool, Vic, with palpitations for three hours. She is alert, BP 128/78 mmHg. ECG shows a regular narrow-complex tachycardia at 150/min. A modified Valsalva manoeuvre has no effect. During IV adenosine 6 mg, the rhythm strip shows the ventricular rate slow briefly, revealing regular saw-tooth atrial activity at about 300/min, before the tachycardia at 150/min returns.

Which one of the following is the most likely rhythm?','["Atrial flutter with 2:1 atrioventricular conduction", "AV nodal re-entry tachycardia", "AV re-entry tachycardia via an accessory pathway", "Sinus tachycardia", "Ventricular tachycardia"]'::jsonb,'A','CORRECT ANSWER

A. Atrial flutter with 2:1 atrioventricular conduction

---

ANZCOR Guideline 11.9 notes that if the ventricular rate slows briefly with adenosine but the arrhythmia continues, you should look for atrial flutter or another atrial tachycardia. Saw-tooth flutter waves at about 300/min with a ventricular rate of 150/min indicate atrial flutter with 2:1 conduction. Adenosine blocks the AV node but does not end a rhythm generated within the atria.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
If adenosine only briefly slows a regular narrow-complex tachycardia and reveals saw-tooth waves, it is atrial flutter.

---

