('9465db7a-ad29-5aa9-ba54-df24f17322c7'::uuid,'ZQ-0353','adult-medicine','cardiology','A 24-year-old man with known Wolff–Parkinson–White pattern presents to an emergency department in Newcastle, NSW, with palpitations for one hour. He is alert, BP 112/70 mmHg. ECG shows an irregularly irregular broad-complex tachycardia at 210–250/min, with QRS complexes that vary in width from beat to beat.

Which one of the following is the most appropriate interpretation and plan?','["AF with bundle branch block; give IV verapamil for rate control", "Monomorphic VT; give IV adenosine to clarify the rhythm", "Pre-excited atrial fibrillation; avoid adenosine, digoxin, verapamil and diltiazem, and plan electrical cardioversion", "AF with rapid ventricular response; give IV digoxin", "Sinus tachycardia with aberrancy; give fluids"]'::jsonb,'C','CORRECT ANSWER

C. Pre-excited atrial fibrillation; avoid adenosine, digoxin, verapamil and diltiazem, and plan electrical cardioversion

---

An irregular broad-complex tachycardia with varying QRS width in a patient with WPW suggests atrial fibrillation conducting down the accessory pathway. ANZCOR Guideline 11.9 states that if pre-excited AF is suspected, adenosine, digoxin, verapamil and diltiazem should be avoided. These drugs block the AV node and cause a relative increase in pre-excitation, which can degenerate to VF. Electrical cardioversion is usually the safest treatment.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Pre-excited AF (WPW with irregular broad complexes): avoid AV nodal blockers. Electrical cardioversion is usually safest.

---

