('da6ae6ee-a9c7-5464-af66-206d8be60be2'::uuid,'ZQ-0354','adult-medicine','cardiology','A 59-year-old man in Bendigo, Vic, has had palpitations for about five days. ECG shows atrial fibrillation at 105/min. He is stable, with BP 134/82 mmHg. He is not taking an anticoagulant. The cardiology team would like to restore sinus rhythm during this admission rather than wait weeks.

Which one of the following investigations is required before early cardioversion?','["Transthoracic echocardiography", "Serum troponin", "Transoesophageal echocardiography to exclude left atrial thrombus", "Thyroid function tests alone", "24-hour Holter monitor"]'::jsonb,'C','CORRECT ANSWER

C. Transoesophageal echocardiography to exclude left atrial thrombus

---

ANZCOR Guideline 11.9 states that patients who have been in AF for more than 48 hours should not have electrical or chemical cardioversion until they are fully anticoagulated, unless transoesophageal echocardiography has shown there is no atrial clot. Transthoracic echo cannot reliably exclude left atrial appendage thrombus.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
AF for more than 48 hours: cardiovert only after full anticoagulation or after TOE excludes atrial clot.

---

