('4be420ba-45e9-50ee-a4b0-355ee38e5f8d'::uuid,'ZQ-0355','adult-medicine','gastroenterology','A 45-year-old woman in Launceston, Tas, has just finished 12 weeks of sofosbuvir/velpatasvir for chronic hepatitis C. She has no cirrhosis. She took every dose, and her ALT is now normal. She asks how she will know she is cured.

Which one of the following tests best confirms cure?','["HCV RNA by sensitive PCR at least 12 weeks after completing treatment", "HCV antibody 12 weeks after treatment", "HCV RNA on the last day of treatment", "ALT 12 weeks after treatment", "HCV genotype"]'::jsonb,'A','CORRECT ANSWER

A. HCV RNA by sensitive PCR at least 12 weeks after completing treatment

---

The Australian HCV guidelines define cure (sustained virological response, SVR12) as undetectable HCV RNA on a highly sensitive PCR assay at least 12 weeks after DAA therapy finishes. Late relapse after SVR is very uncommon. Anti-HCV antibodies are expected to stay positive and do not indicate active infection.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Hepatitis C cure is SVR12: undetectable HCV RNA at least 12 weeks after DAA treatment ends.

---

