('d3be24ba-564b-53b1-987f-313d1c934410'::uuid,'ZQ-0356','adult-medicine','gastroenterology','A 52-year-old man in Wollongong, NSW, was treated for hepatitis C three years ago. HCV RNA was undetectable 12 weeks after treatment, and he has no cirrhosis. A recent insurance medical showed a positive HCV antibody. He has not injected drugs since treatment. Today his HCV RNA is undetectable and his ALT is 24 U/L.

Which one of the following is the most appropriate interpretation?','["Relapse of hepatitis C requiring retreatment", "Reinfection requiring retreatment", "Protective immunity against future HCV infection", "Persistent antibody after cure; he does not have active infection and is not immune to reinfection", "A false-positive antibody result that should be investigated with a liver biopsy"]'::jsonb,'D','CORRECT ANSWER

D. Persistent antibody after cure; he does not have active infection and is not immune to reinfection

---

The Australian HCV guidelines state that anti-HCV antibodies are expected to persist after SVR. They do not mean active infection, and they do not give immunity to reinfection. People without cirrhosis whose LFTs are normal after SVR do not need further specialist liver follow-up. Future testing for reinfection must use HCV RNA, not antibody.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
After HCV cure, antibodies stay positive. Use HCV RNA to test for reinfection.

---

