('2abe81b6-3d57-5311-a022-43eb0547fbca'::uuid,'ZQ-0357','adult-medicine','infectious-diseases','A 21-year-old woman in Townsville, Qld, was treated with doxycycline for 7 days for genital chlamydia found on screening. She is not pregnant, and her symptoms have gone. Her partner has also been treated. She asks whether she needs another test.

Which one of the following is the most appropriate follow-up testing?','["Test of cure NAAT one week after treatment", "Repeat chlamydia NAAT at 3 months to detect reinfection", "No further testing at any time", "Chlamydia serology", "Pelvic ultrasound"]'::jsonb,'B','CORRECT ANSWER

B. Repeat chlamydia NAAT at 3 months to detect reinfection

---

The Australian STI Management Guidelines say a test of cure is not routinely recommended for chlamydia, except in pregnancy and for anorectal infection treated with azithromycin. Reinfection is common, so retesting at 3 months is recommended.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
After treating uncomplicated chlamydia, retest at 3 months for reinfection. A routine test of cure is not needed.

---

