('5d92bc1d-52a6-5bae-95b3-5ad38df180fe'::uuid,'ZQ-0360','adult-medicine','infectious-diseases','A 34-year-old nurse in Brisbane, Qld, had a needlestick injury two hours ago from a patient known to be HBsAg-positive. She is documented as a persistent non-responder to hepatitis B vaccine: anti-HBs stayed <10 mIU/mL after a full course and three further doses, and she has tested negative for hepatitis B infection.

Which one of the following is the most appropriate hepatitis B prophylaxis?','["No prophylaxis because she has been vaccinated", "A single booster dose of hepatitis B vaccine alone", "Start tenofovir post-exposure prophylaxis", "Hepatitis B immunoglobulin as soon as possible, within 72 hours", "Wait for her anti-HBs result before deciding"]'::jsonb,'D','CORRECT ANSWER

D. Hepatitis B immunoglobulin as soon as possible, within 72 hours

---

The Australian Immunisation Handbook says persistent non-responders are probably not protected and should be told they need hepatitis B immunoglobulin within 72 hours of any parenteral or mucosal exposure to hepatitis B virus. Its post-exposure prophylaxis table covers exposure to an HBsAg-positive source. HBV and HIV/HCV baseline testing and follow-up also apply.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Persistent HBV vaccine non-responders exposed to HBsAg need HBIG within 72 hours.

---

