('8ca21cbd-0257-5548-8d04-74e6fa849a90'::uuid,'ZQ-0361','adult-medicine','cardiology','An 86-year-old woman in Bundaberg, Qld, is brought in with three days of nausea, confusion and yellow-tinged vision. She takes digoxin 125 micrograms daily for atrial fibrillation. Her furosemide dose was recently increased, and she has had diarrhoea. Pulse 38/min with intermittent junctional rhythm, BP 86/50 mmHg.

Investigations:
- Serum digoxin 3.4 nmol/L
- Potassium 5.9 mmol/L
- Creatinine 190 µmol/L (baseline 95)

Which one of the following is the most appropriate specific treatment?','["IV calcium gluconate for the hyperkalaemia", "Haemodialysis to remove digoxin", "Activated charcoal 50 g", "Digoxin immune Fab, 1 vial (40 mg) IV over 30 minutes", "Stop digoxin and observe without other treatment"]'::jsonb,'D','CORRECT ANSWER

D. Digoxin immune Fab, 1 vial (40 mg) IV over 30 minutes

---

This is chronic digoxin accumulation (worse kidney function, diuretics) with cardiovascular compromise (bradycardia and hypotension). Queensland Poisons Information Centre antidote guidance for chronic digoxin accumulation with compromise is digoxin immune Fab, 1 vial (40 mg) diluted in 100 mL saline over 30 minutes. Chronic accumulation is more common than acute overdose.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Chronic digoxin toxicity with compromise: digoxin Fab, 1 vial (40 mg) IV over 30 minutes (Qld PIC).

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "Queensland Poisons Information Centre. Antidote stocking recommendations: Digoxin Immune Fab", "url": "https://www.poisonsinfo.health.qld.gov.au/for-health-professionals/antidote-stocking-recommendations/digoxin-immune-fab"}','difficult','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "batch03", "review_status": "draft_pending_owner_review", "tags": ["ZQ-0361", "zyntra_bank", "batch03", "level_4", "task_management", "group_adult_medicine", "sys_cardiovascular", "irtb_+1.40", "pending_owner_review"], "clinician_task": "management", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"Queensland Poisons Information Centre. Antidote stocking recommendations: Digoxin Immune Fab\", \"url\": \"https://www.poisonsinfo.health.qld.gov.au/for-health-professionals/antidote-stocking-recommendations/digoxin-immune-fab\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "Calcium is not the specific treatment, and digoxin Fab treats the hyperkalaemia caused by digoxin.", "B": "Digoxin has a large volume of distribution and is not removed effectively by dialysis.", "C": "Charcoal is for recent acute ingestion, not chronic accumulation.", "E": "She has bradycardia with hypotension, which needs antidote treatment."}, "key_takeaways": ["Chronic digoxin toxicity with compromise: digoxin Fab, 1 vial (40 mg) IV over 30 minutes (Qld PIC)."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,1.4,0.5,'expert_prior','author_estimate (batch03)'),
('3555f0d7-761c-5b4d-b8d6-434bd66303a1'::uuid,'ZQ-0362','adult-medicine','respiratory','A 72-year-old woman with COPD sees her GP in Port Macquarie, NSW, with four days of increased breathlessness and more sputum, which has changed from white to green. She is not febrile. SpO2 93% on room air, respiratory rate 20/min. She is not drowsy. Her GP plans to increase her bronchodilators and start oral prednisolone.

Which one of the following is the most appropriate antibiotic plan?','["No antibiotics because she is afebrile", "Oral amoxicillin/clavulanate for 14 days", "IV ceftriaxone", "Oral azithromycin for 3 days", "Oral amoxycillin or doxycycline for 5 days"]'::jsonb,'E','CORRECT ANSWER

E. Oral amoxycillin or doxycycline for 5 days

---

COPD-X recommends antibiotics (amoxycillin or doxycycline for 5 days) for exacerbations with clinical features of infection, such as increased sputum volume with a change in colour, and/or fever. Treatment also includes bronchodilators and a short course of oral corticosteroids.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
COPD exacerbation with purulent sputum: amoxycillin or doxycycline for 5 days.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "Lung Foundation Australia. COPD-X Plan: X – Manage exacerbations", "url": "https://copdx.org.au/copd-x-plan/x-manage-exacerbations/"}','easy','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "batch03", "review_status": "draft_pending_owner_review", "tags": ["ZQ-0362", "zyntra_bank", "batch03", "level_2", "task_management", "group_adult_medicine", "sys_respiratory", "irtb_-0.80", "pending_owner_review"], "clinician_task": "management", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"Lung Foundation Australia. COPD-X Plan: X – Manage exacerbations\", \"url\": \"https://copdx.org.au/copd-x-plan/x-manage-exacerbations/\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "A change in sputum colour with increased volume is enough to indicate infection.", "B": "This is broader and longer than recommended.", "C": "She does not need IV therapy.", "D": "Azithromycin is not the recommended first-line choice for an exacerbation."}, "key_takeaways": ["COPD exacerbation with purulent sputum: amoxycillin or doxycycline for 5 days."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,-0.8,0.5,'expert_prior','author_estimate (batch03)'),
('3e6e42b3-2ba8-57e6-8e44-6e6a27d5917f'::uuid,'ZQ-0363','adult-medicine','respiratory','A 68-year-old man with COPD is admitted to a hospital in Mildura, Vic, with five days of worsening breathlessness and new right-sided pleuritic chest pain. Sputum is unchanged and white, and he is afebrile. He had a total knee replacement four weeks ago. Pulse 112/min, SpO2 87% on room air. Chest X-ray shows hyperinflation only.

Which one of the following investigations is most important next?','["Sputum culture", "Spirometry", "CT pulmonary angiography", "D-dimer alone to decide whether to image", "Repeat chest X-ray in 24 hours"]'::jsonb,'C','CORRECT ANSWER

C. CT pulmonary angiography

---

COPD-X cites a systematic review in which 16% of patients hospitalised with a COPD exacerbation who had CT pulmonary angiography were found to have a pulmonary embolism. With no infective features, pleuritic pain, tachycardia, hypoxaemia and recent major orthopaedic surgery, PE must be actively excluded. Here the best test is CTPA.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
In a COPD exacerbation without infective features, especially with pleuritic pain or VTE risk, look for PE (CTPA).

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "Lung Foundation Australia. COPD-X Plan: X – Manage exacerbations", "url": "https://copdx.org.au/copd-x-plan/x-manage-exacerbations/"}','difficult','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "batch03", "review_status": "draft_pending_owner_review", "tags": ["ZQ-0363", "zyntra_bank", "batch03", "level_4", "task_data_gathering", "group_adult_medicine", "sys_respiratory", "irtb_+1.00", "pending_owner_review"], "clinician_task": "data_gathering", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"Lung Foundation Australia. COPD-X Plan: X – Manage exacerbations\", \"url\": \"https://copdx.org.au/copd-x-plan/x-manage-exacerbations/\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "There are no infective features, and the result will not exclude PE.", "B": "Spirometry is not done during an acute exacerbation.", "D": "With a high pre-test probability, go straight to imaging.", "E": "A repeat X-ray delays diagnosis."}, "key_takeaways": ["In a COPD exacerbation without infective features, especially with pleuritic pain or VTE risk, look for PE (CTPA)."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,1.0,0.5,'expert_prior','author_estimate (batch03)'),
('e9597c56-38f6-5485-beac-be67ea4e5338'::uuid,'ZQ-0364','adult-medicine','infectious-diseases','A 29-year-old medical student in Adelaide, SA, completed a 3-dose hepatitis B vaccine course as a child. Pre-placement anti-HBs was <10 mIU/mL, so she had a single booster dose. Four weeks after the booster, anti-HBs is still <10 mIU/mL.

Which one of the following is the most appropriate next step?','["Declare her immune because she completed a childhood course", "Repeat a full 3-dose course straight away without checking for infection", "Give hepatitis B immunoglobulin now", "Test for hepatitis B infection (HBsAg and anti-HBc); if negative, give 2 more doses 1 month apart and recheck anti-HBs at least 4 weeks later", "Recheck anti-HBs in 12 months"]'::jsonb,'D','CORRECT ANSWER

D. Test for hepatitis B infection (HBsAg and anti-HBc); if negative, give 2 more doses 1 month apart and recheck anti-HBs at least 4 weeks later

---

The Australian Immunisation Handbook advises that people who still have anti-HBs <10 mIU/mL after a booster should be tested for hepatitis B infection. If that is negative, they are recommended to have 2 more doses 1 month apart, with the booster counted as the first of the 3 repeat doses. Anti-HBs is rechecked at least 4 weeks after the last dose. Persistent non-responders need HBIG after any exposure.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
No response after a hepatitis B booster: exclude infection, then give 2 more doses and recheck anti-HBs at 4 weeks or more.

---

