('b8502629-8a28-56ce-a9b2-5227d7082efb'::uuid,'ZQ-0365','adult-medicine','neurology','A 77-year-old woman in Lismore, NSW, has had prochlorperazine 5 mg three times daily for dizziness for the past 10 weeks. Her daughter has noticed repetitive lip-smacking, chewing movements and tongue protrusion over the last two weeks. Her gait and arm movements are otherwise normal.

Which one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?','["Acute dystonic reaction", "Tardive dyskinesia", "Drug-induced parkinsonism", "Akathisia", "Huntington disease"]'::jsonb,'B','CORRECT ANSWER

B. Tardive dyskinesia

---

Australian Prescriber notes that tardive movement disorders need a minimum drug exposure of three months, or one month in adults over 60. Antiemetics such as metoclopramide and prochlorperazine are among the drugs most often responsible. Tardive dyskinesias are typically orobuccolingual, as in this patient.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Orobuccolingual dyskinesia after ≥1 month of a dopamine blocker in a person over 60 suggests tardive dyskinesia.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "Australian Prescriber. Drug-induced movement disorders", "url": "https://australianprescriber.tg.org.au/articles/drug-induced-movement-disorders.html"}','moderate','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "batch03", "review_status": "draft_pending_owner_review", "tags": ["ZQ-0365", "zyntra_bank", "batch03", "level_3", "task_data_interpretation_synthesis", "group_adult_medicine", "sys_neurology", "irtb_+0.60", "pending_owner_review"], "clinician_task": "data_interpretation_synthesis", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"Australian Prescriber. Drug-induced movement disorders\", \"url\": \"https://australianprescriber.tg.org.au/articles/drug-induced-movement-disorders.html\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "Acute dystonia usually occurs in younger patients soon after starting the drug.", "C": "Parkinsonism causes bradykinesia and rigidity, not choreiform orobuccolingual movements.", "D": "Akathisia is a subjective restlessness with an urge to move.", "E": "Onset at 77 shortly after a dopamine blocker points to the drug."}, "key_takeaways": ["Orobuccolingual dyskinesia after ≥1 month of a dopamine blocker in a person over 60 suggests tardive dyskinesia."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,0.6,0.5,'expert_prior','author_estimate (batch03)'),
('88fedf80-6b9a-5d6d-8ea9-dae9fd15150f'::uuid,'ZQ-0366','adult-surgery','general-surgery','A 41-year-old woman in Hobart, Tas, with immune thrombocytopenia that has not responded to treatment is booked for elective laparoscopic splenectomy in five weeks. She is up to date with her routine adult vaccines but has never had additional pneumococcal, meningococcal or Hib vaccines.

Which one of the following is the most appropriate vaccination plan?','["Vaccinate on the day after surgery", "Vaccinate 6 months after surgery", "No vaccines are needed if she takes lifelong antibiotic prophylaxis", "Give the recommended additional asplenia vaccines now so they are completed at least 2 weeks before surgery", "Give only influenza vaccine"]'::jsonb,'D','CORRECT ANSWER

D. Give the recommended additional asplenia vaccines now so they are completed at least 2 weeks before surgery

---

The Australian Immunisation Handbook recommends that people having a planned splenectomy receive all scheduled vaccines at least 2 weeks before surgery, when possible. This includes extra doses of pneumococcal, meningococcal (MenACWY and MenB) and Hib vaccines and annual influenza vaccine, to optimise the immune response. After an emergency splenectomy, vaccination is ideally delayed until 2 weeks after surgery.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Planned splenectomy: complete asplenia vaccines at least 2 weeks before surgery.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "Australian Immunisation Handbook. People with asplenia and hyposplenia", "url": "https://immunisationhandbook.health.gov.au/contents/vaccination-for-special-risk-groups/vaccination-for-people-who-are-immunocompromised/people-with-asplenia-and-hyposplenia"}','moderate','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "batch03", "review_status": "draft_pending_owner_review", "tags": ["ZQ-0366", "zyntra_bank", "batch03", "level_3", "task_management", "group_adult_surgery", "sys_general_surgery", "irtb_+0.00", "pending_owner_review"], "clinician_task": "management", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"Australian Immunisation Handbook. People with asplenia and hyposplenia\", \"url\": \"https://immunisationhandbook.health.gov.au/contents/vaccination-for-special-risk-groups/vaccination-for-people-who-are-immunocompromised/people-with-asplenia-and-hyposplenia\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "The ideal timing for planned splenectomy is at least 2 weeks before surgery.", "B": "This leaves her unprotected during the highest-risk period.", "C": "Vaccination is still recommended. Prophylaxis is an additional measure.", "E": "She also needs pneumococcal, meningococcal and Hib vaccines."}, "key_takeaways": ["Planned splenectomy: complete asplenia vaccines at least 2 weeks before surgery."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,0.0,0.5,'expert_prior','author_estimate (batch03)'),
('cac41051-4ec4-5893-8488-b963007ae245'::uuid,'ZQ-0367','adult-surgery','general-surgery','A 30-year-old man from a remote station in western Queensland had an emergency splenectomy in Townsville five days ago after a motorbike crash. He is recovering well and wants to go home tomorrow. He lives 900 km away, and the team thinks he is unlikely to return for review.

Which one of the following is the most appropriate timing of his asplenia vaccines?','["Give the vaccines before discharge because follow-up is unlikely", "Delay vaccination until a 2-week review regardless of circumstances", "No vaccination because the spleen was removed after trauma, not disease", "Give only antibiotic prophylaxis and no vaccines", "Vaccinate after 6 months"]'::jsonb,'A','CORRECT ANSWER

A. Give the vaccines before discharge because follow-up is unlikely

---

The Handbook advises that after emergency splenectomy, vaccines are ideally given 2 weeks after surgery because antibody responses, especially to pneumococcal vaccines, are better then. If there is concern that the patient may not return or may be lost to follow-up, vaccines may be given once the person is stable and before discharge. The risk of infection is highest in the first 1–2 years.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
After emergency splenectomy, vaccinate at 2 weeks, or before discharge if he may be lost to follow-up.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "Australian Immunisation Handbook. People with asplenia and hyposplenia", "url": "https://immunisationhandbook.health.gov.au/contents/vaccination-for-special-risk-groups/vaccination-for-people-who-are-immunocompromised/people-with-asplenia-and-hyposplenia"}','moderate','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "batch03", "review_status": "draft_pending_owner_review", "tags": ["ZQ-0367", "zyntra_bank", "batch03", "level_3", "task_management", "group_adult_surgery", "sys_general_surgery", "irtb_+0.60", "pending_owner_review"], "clinician_task": "management", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"Australian Immunisation Handbook. People with asplenia and hyposplenia\", \"url\": \"https://immunisationhandbook.health.gov.au/contents/vaccination-for-special-risk-groups/vaccination-for-people-who-are-immunocompromised/people-with-asplenia-and-hyposplenia\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"B": "If follow-up is unlikely, the Handbook allows vaccination before discharge.", "C": "All asplenic people need the additional vaccines.", "D": "Vaccines are still recommended.", "E": "This misses the period of highest risk."}, "key_takeaways": ["After emergency splenectomy, vaccinate at 2 weeks, or before discharge if he may be lost to follow-up."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,0.6,0.5,'expert_prior','author_estimate (batch03)'),
('067918b9-589e-562d-a7a2-67c540cf5088'::uuid,'ZQ-0368','adult-surgery','trauma','A 36-year-old woman was a restrained driver in a crash near Toowoomba, Qld. CT on arrival showed an AAST grade IV splenic laceration without active extravasation. She has stayed haemodynamically stable and is being managed non-operatively in the high dependency unit. Today is day 3, and her haemoglobin is stable.

Which one of the following investigations is recommended now?','["Repeat CT angiography of the abdomen", "No imaging if she remains stable", "Abdominal ultrasound only", "Diagnostic laparoscopy", "Repeat CT only at 6 weeks before she returns to sport"]'::jsonb,'A','CORRECT ANSWER

A. Repeat CT angiography of the abdomen

---

The Queensland adult blunt splenic injury guideline recommends that high-grade injuries (AAST grade IV–V) be managed in HDU or ICU with repeat CT angiography 3 to 5 days after injury. This looks for delayed vascular injury such as pseudoaneurysm, which may need embolisation. Grade III injuries are managed on a specialised ward, with repeat CTA considered according to clinical need.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Grade IV–V blunt splenic injury managed non-operatively: HDU or ICU care and repeat CTA at days 3–5.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "Queensland Health. Clinical practice guideline: Blunt splenic injury (adult)", "url": "https://www.health.qld.gov.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0021/1430085/blunt-splenic-injury-clinical-guideline.pdf"}','difficult','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "batch03", "review_status": "draft_pending_owner_review", "tags": ["ZQ-0368", "zyntra_bank", "batch03", "level_4", "task_data_gathering", "group_adult_surgery", "sys_trauma", "irtb_+1.30", "pending_owner_review"], "clinician_task": "data_gathering", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"Queensland Health. Clinical practice guideline: Blunt splenic injury (adult)\", \"url\": \"https://www.health.qld.gov.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0021/1430085/blunt-splenic-injury-clinical-guideline.pdf\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"B": "Repeat CTA is recommended for grade IV–V injuries even when the patient is stable.", "C": "Ultrasound cannot reliably detect a pseudoaneurysm.", "D": "Surgery is not indicated while non-operative management is succeeding.", "E": "Delayed vascular injury occurs earlier than this."}, "key_takeaways": ["Grade IV–V blunt splenic injury managed non-operatively: HDU or ICU care and repeat CTA at days 3–5."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,1.3,0.5,'expert_prior','author_estimate (batch03)'),
('2c2777cd-5a49-5135-8cdd-2ee6aefb855a'::uuid,'ZQ-0369','adult-surgery','urology','A 63-year-old man in Toowong, Qld, had a PSA of 4.6 µg/L, confirmed on repeat testing. Multiparametric MRI shows a prostate volume of 70 mL with a single PI-RADS 3 lesion, giving a PSA density of 0.07 µg/L/mL. Digital rectal examination is unremarkable. He has no family history of prostate cancer.

Which one of the following is the most appropriate interpretation?','["Biopsy is required for any PI-RADS 3 lesion", "Proceed to radical prostatectomy", "A PSMA PET scan is needed", "Discharge from all follow-up because the MRI is normal", "PI-RADS 3 with PSA density below 0.15; biopsy is not routinely needed and PSA monitoring can continue"]'::jsonb,'E','CORRECT ANSWER

E. PI-RADS 3 with PSA density below 0.15; biopsy is not routinely needed and PSA monitoring can continue

---

The 2026 Australian early detection guideline recommends biopsy for PI-RADS 4–5 lesions, or for PI-RADS 3 lesions when PSA density is 0.15 or more. With PI-RADS 3 and a PSA density of 0.07, the risk of clinically significant cancer is low, so the guideline says biopsy may not be required, subject to clinical assessment. Ongoing monitoring is appropriate. The higher PSA is explained by his large gland.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
PI-RADS 3 lesion: biopsy if PSA density is ≥0.15. Below that, monitor.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "Prostate Cancer Foundation of Australia. 2026 Guidelines for the Early Detection of Prostate Cancer – Summary of Recommendations", "url": "https://www.prostate.org.au/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/2026-Guidelines-for-the-Early-Detection-of-Prostate-Cancer-Summary-of-Recommendations.pdf"}','difficult','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "batch03", "review_status": "draft_pending_owner_review", "tags": ["ZQ-0369", "zyntra_bank", "batch03", "level_5", "task_data_interpretation_synthesis", "group_adult_surgery", "sys_urology", "irtb_+1.60", "pending_owner_review"], "clinician_task": "data_interpretation_synthesis", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"Prostate Cancer Foundation of Australia. 2026 Guidelines for the Early Detection of Prostate Cancer – Summary of Recommendations\", \"url\": \"https://www.prostate.org.au/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/2026-Guidelines-for-the-Early-Detection-of-Prostate-Cancer-Summary-of-Recommendations.pdf\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "PSA density decides whether to biopsy a PI-RADS 3 lesion.", "B": "Cancer has not been diagnosed.", "C": "PSMA PET is a staging test, not a test to decide on biopsy.", "D": "The MRI is not normal, and ongoing PSA surveillance is still needed."}, "key_takeaways": ["PI-RADS 3 lesion: biopsy if PSA density is ≥0.15. Below that, monitor."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,1.6,0.5,'expert_prior','author_estimate (batch03)'),
('9353c2d4-42c4-520f-a2cf-5fa1b27c7ee3'::uuid,'ZQ-0370','adult-surgery','urology','A 45-year-old man sees his GP in Darwin, NT. His father was diagnosed with prostate cancer at 58. He has no urinary symptoms. After discussing the benefits and harms of testing, he decides he wants to be tested.

Which one of the following is the most appropriate testing approach?','["Wait until he is 50", "PSA test now, repeated every 2 years", "Annual PSA with digital rectal examination", "mpMRI prostate as the first screening test", "Germline BRCA testing before any PSA"]'::jsonb,'B','CORRECT ANSWER

B. PSA test now, repeated every 2 years

---

The 2026 Australian early detection guideline proposes offering PSA testing every 2 years from age 45 to men at higher risk who choose testing after discussing benefits and harms. Higher risk includes a family history of prostate cancer, men of Black sub-Saharan African ancestry, and confirmed BRCA2 carriers. Men aged 50–69 at average risk are offered PSA testing every 2 years.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Men at higher risk (family history, BRCA2, Black African ancestry) can be offered PSA every 2 years from age 45.

---

