• {"name": "Prostate Cancer Foundation of Australia. 2026 Guidelines for the Early Detection of Prostate Cancer – Summary of Recommendations", "url": "https://www.prostate.org.au/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/2026-Guidelines-for-the-Early-Detection-of-Prostate-Cancer-Summary-of-Recommendations.pdf"}','moderate','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "batch03", "review_status": "draft_pending_owner_review", "tags": ["ZQ-0370", "zyntra_bank", "batch03", "level_3", "task_data_gathering", "group_adult_surgery", "sys_urology", "irtb_+0.30", "pending_owner_review"], "clinician_task": "data_gathering", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"Prostate Cancer Foundation of Australia. 2026 Guidelines for the Early Detection of Prostate Cancer – Summary of Recommendations\", \"url\": \"https://www.prostate.org.au/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/2026-Guidelines-for-the-Early-Detection-of-Prostate-Cancer-Summary-of-Recommendations.pdf\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "His family history puts him at higher risk, so testing is offered from 45.", "C": "The guideline recommends a 2-yearly PSA interval.", "D": "MRI follows a raised PSA. It is not the first screening test.", "E": "This is not required before testing."}, "key_takeaways": ["Men at higher risk (family history, BRCA2, Black African ancestry) can be offered PSA every 2 years from age 45."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,0.3,0.5,'expert_prior','author_estimate (batch03)'),
('1a5ebaa3-a112-5dea-8cc2-c7cfda6c68db'::uuid,'ZQ-0371','adult-surgery','urology','A 57-year-old man in Ballarat, Vic, chose PSA testing after discussing it with his GP. His result is 3.6 µg/L. He has no urinary symptoms, no recent urinary infection and no family history of prostate cancer. Urine dipstick is normal.

Which one of the following is the most appropriate next step?','["Refer immediately for systematic biopsy", "Reassure him because the PSA is below 4.0 µg/L", "Repeat the PSA in 2 years", "Start a 5-alpha reductase inhibitor and recheck", "Repeat the PSA within 1–3 months and refer for further investigation if it is still 3.0 µg/L or more"]'::jsonb,'E','CORRECT ANSWER

E. Repeat the PSA within 1–3 months and refer for further investigation if it is still 3.0 µg/L or more

---

The 2026 Australian early detection guideline says that if total PSA is 3.0 µg/L or more, the test should be repeated within 1–3 months. If the result is confirmed, offer referral and further investigation, with mpMRI before biopsy.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
PSA of 3.0 µg/L or more: repeat within 1–3 months and, if confirmed, refer for further investigation.

---

