('26870253-fda2-5205-8ba6-66e7ca0512cd'::uuid,'ZQ-0372','adult-surgery','general-surgery','A 55-year-old woman is admitted to a hospital in Wagga Wagga, NSW, with gallstone pancreatitis. On admission her lipase was 4,800 U/L (<60). At 48 hours her lipase has fallen to 900 U/L and her CRP is 240 mg/L. A registrar suggests she is improving because her lipase has fallen.

Which one of the following is the most accurate interpretation?','["The CRP above 150 mg/L at 48 hours suggests severe pancreatitis; falling lipase does not indicate improvement", "Falling lipase shows the pancreatitis is resolving", "The very high initial lipase predicted severe disease", "Repeat lipase daily to guide discharge", "CRP is unhelpful in pancreatitis"]'::jsonb,'A','CORRECT ANSWER

A. The CRP above 150 mg/L at 48 hours suggests severe pancreatitis; falling lipase does not indicate improvement

---

Australian Prescriber notes that neither amylase nor lipase helps monitor or predict the severity of pancreatitis in adults, and the size of the rise does not predict severity. Daily measurements should not be used to judge disease activity. A CRP above 150 mg/L at 48 hours after symptom onset is the best single laboratory predictor of severity, alongside composite scores.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
In adult pancreatitis, a CRP above 150 mg/L at 48 hours predicts severity. Lipase levels do not.

---

