('ee5eedc0-9ad3-5d20-a1c6-7ed06ed84ba7'::uuid,'ZQ-0373','adult-surgery','trauma','A 38-year-old farm worker in Gympie, Qld, has a deep laceration to his right thigh from a chainsaw. Bright red blood is spurting from the wound despite firm direct pressure with a pad from a colleague. He is pale and sweaty, with a pulse of 130/min. An ambulance has been called, and a commercial windlass tourniquet is in the first aid kit. The colleague is trained in its use.

Which one of the following is the most appropriate immediate action?','["Elevate the leg and continue the same pressure", "Apply an elastic venous tourniquet", "Prioritise airway positioning before bleeding control", "Apply an arterial tourniquet above the wound, tighten until bleeding stops, and note the time", "Remove the pad to inspect the wound every few minutes"]'::jsonb,'D','CORRECT ANSWER

D. Apply an arterial tourniquet above the wound, tighten until bleeding stops, and note the time

---

ANZCOR Guideline 9.1.1 states that firm direct pressure is the most effective way to stop most bleeding. Where life-threatening limb bleeding is not controlled by direct pressure, an arterial tourniquet is suggested if one is available and the first aider is trained in its use. It is applied above the bleeding point (5 cm above if there are no manufacturer instructions), not over a joint, and tightened until the bleeding stops. The time is recorded and passed on, and the tourniquet is not removed until the person reaches specialist care.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
If life-threatening limb bleeding continues despite pressure, apply an arterial tourniquet above the wound and record the time.

---

REFERENCES
