('823b82ea-4fd3-5785-ba39-fbbe34946a5c'::uuid,'ZQ-0374','adult-surgery','trauma','A 26-year-old chef in Fremantle, WA, spilt boiling water over his left forearm 40 minutes ago. He ran it under the tap for about two minutes and then wrapped it in a towel. He now attends the emergency department with a painful, blistered, red area about the size of his palm.

Which one of the following is the most appropriate immediate first aid?','["Apply ice packs for 10 minutes", "Cool the burn under cool running water for 20 minutes", "Apply a burn cream and dressing; it is too late for cooling", "Pop the blisters and dress the wound", "Cool for 5 minutes only, to avoid hypothermia"]'::jsonb,'B','CORRECT ANSWER

B. Cool the burn under cool running water for 20 minutes

---

ANZCOR Guideline 9.1.3 recommends cooling burns with cool running water for at least 20 minutes, as soon as possible. Cooling still helps when started up to 3 hours after the burn. Afterwards, cover loosely with a non-stick dressing such as cling film. Ice, creams and breaking blisters should be avoided.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Burn first aid: at least 20 minutes of cool running water, effective up to 3 hours after the burn. No ice.

---

