('afd2aa0b-355b-5e09-a931-0b1b4d0e442d'::uuid,'ZQ-0375','adult-surgery','general-surgery','A 61-year-old man in Rockhampton, Qld, has had sudden severe upper abdominal pain for four hours. He takes naproxen for back pain. He has a rigid abdomen with generalised guarding. Pulse 108/min, BP 112/70 mmHg. An erect chest X-ray done in the department shows no free gas under the diaphragm.

Which one of the following is the most appropriate next investigation?','["Reassure: perforation is excluded by the chest X-ray", "CT abdomen and pelvis", "Left lateral decubitus X-ray first, then decide", "Upper GI endoscopy", "Abdominal ultrasound"]'::jsonb,'B','CORRECT ANSWER

B. CT abdomen and pelvis

---

The WSES perforated peptic ulcer guideline notes that a negative X-ray does not rule out perforation. When there are clear signs of peritonitis, abdominal CT should be the first imaging test. CT shows free extraluminal gas and fluid and can help locate the perforation, so a normal erect X-ray should not delay CT and surgical review.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Suspected perforation with peritonitis: go to CT. A normal erect chest X-ray does not exclude it.

---

