('00ed1efd-c766-5f4b-bb3b-7cab8e83f712'::uuid,'ZQ-0376','womens-health','obstetrics','A 31-year-old woman in her second pregnancy attends her GP in Toowoomba, Qld, at 21 weeks'' gestation. She had a dTpa vaccine during her first pregnancy two years ago and asks whether she needs another.

Which one of the following is the most appropriate advice?','["No vaccine is needed because she had one 2 years ago", "Delay until 36 weeks", "Vaccinate the baby at birth instead", "Give dTpa now; it is recommended in every pregnancy, ideally between 20 and 32 weeks", "Give it after delivery"]'::jsonb,'D','CORRECT ANSWER

D. Give dTpa now; it is recommended in every pregnancy, ideally between 20 and 32 weeks

---

The Australian Immunisation Handbook recommends a single dose of pertussis-containing vaccine (dTpa) in each pregnancy, ideally between 20 and 32 weeks'' gestation, whatever the time since a previous dose. This maximises the transfer of maternal antibodies to protect the newborn.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
dTpa in every pregnancy, ideally at 20–32 weeks.

---

