('6453cb7b-05df-5fff-8ca1-43e213f0fdc1'::uuid,'ZQ-0377','womens-health','obstetrics','A 22-year-old woman at 14 weeks'' gestation in Cairns, Qld, has a positive chlamydia NAAT on her first-pass urine at her antenatal booking visit. She has been given azithromycin 1 g orally as a single dose. Her partner has been contacted for treatment.

Which one of the following follow-up tests is most appropriate?','["No further testing is needed after single-dose azithromycin", "Test of cure at 1 week", "Chlamydia serology", "Repeat chlamydia NAAT at least 4 weeks after treatment as a test of cure, plus retesting at 3 months", "Endocervical culture only at delivery"]'::jsonb,'D','CORRECT ANSWER

D. Repeat chlamydia NAAT at least 4 weeks after treatment as a test of cure, plus retesting at 3 months

---

The Australian STI Management Guidelines recommend a test of cure for chlamydia in pregnancy, done at least 4 weeks after treatment so that residual non-viable DNA does not give a false-positive result. All people with chlamydia should also be retested at 3 months to detect reinfection.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Chlamydia in pregnancy: test of cure at 4 weeks or more after treatment, then retest at 3 months.

---

