('938fb60d-efcc-5e5d-880a-5606930431ec'::uuid,'ZQ-0378','womens-health','gynaecology','A 34-year-old woman in Alice Springs, NT, has had a frothy yellow-green vaginal discharge and vulval itch for one week. Speculum examination shows a punctate erythematous cervix. She has a new partner.

Which one of the following is the most appropriate test to confirm the likely diagnosis?','["Wet-mount microscopy only", "Trichomonas vaginalis NAAT on a vaginal swab", "High vaginal swab culture for Candida", "Serology for Trichomonas", "Gram stain for clue cells"]'::jsonb,'B','CORRECT ANSWER

B. Trichomonas vaginalis NAAT on a vaginal swab

---

The Australian STI Management Guidelines recommend NAAT (PCR) for Trichomonas vaginalis because it is far more sensitive than wet-mount microscopy. A positive result is treated with metronidazole 400 mg twice daily for 7 days (or 2 g stat as an alternative), along with treatment of her partner and testing for other STIs.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Trichomoniasis is diagnosed by NAAT.

---

