('987ea8a2-6bfc-5421-bda4-528325fcb86a'::uuid,'ZQ-0379','womens-health','gynaecology','A 32-year-old woman in Newcastle, NSW, has a pelvic ultrasound for an unrelated reason. It shows a 3.2 cm unilocular, thin-walled, anechoic left ovarian cyst with no solid components, septations or internal blood flow. She has no symptoms, and her periods are regular.

Which one of the following is the most appropriate interpretation?','["Measure CA-125 and refer to a gynaecological oncologist", "Laparoscopic cystectomy", "Repeat ultrasound every 3 months for 2 years", "Almost certainly a benign functional cyst; no follow-up is needed", "MRI pelvis"]'::jsonb,'D','CORRECT ANSWER

D. Almost certainly a benign functional cyst; no follow-up is needed

---

An Australian Family Physician review of ovarian masses states that a simple anechoic cyst smaller than 5 cm in a premenopausal woman is almost certainly benign (often functional) and needs no follow-up. CA-125 is not needed in premenopausal women unless the ultrasound features are suspicious.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
A simple cyst under 5 cm in a premenopausal woman is benign and needs no follow-up.

---

