('21d2651f-e101-59c0-8ad7-ecaaa5c8b13c'::uuid,'ZQ-0381','womens-health','gynaecology','A 38-year-old woman in Launceston, Tas, has a 6 cm left adnexal mass found on transvaginal ultrasound for pelvic discomfort. The ultrasound describes a multilocular cyst with a solid papillary projection. She has no family history of ovarian cancer.

Which one of the following investigations is most appropriate next?','["No test; repeat the ultrasound in 6 months", "CA-19-9", "Serum CA-125, because the ultrasound features are suspicious", "Serum hCG only", "Laparoscopic aspiration of the cyst"]'::jsonb,'C','CORRECT ANSWER

C. Serum CA-125, because the ultrasound features are suspicious

---

The Australian Family Physician review advises that in premenopausal women, CA-125 should be measured only when ultrasound shows suspicious features, because it is raised by many benign conditions such as endometriosis and menstruation. Solid papillary projections in a multilocular cyst are suspicious. The result guides referral, including possible gynaecological oncology review.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
In premenopausal women, measure CA-125 only if the ultrasound is suspicious.

