('bdf2fead-3d51-5f83-8711-6bd50ddf073a'::uuid,'ZQ-0382','child-health','paediatric-medicine','A 2-year-old boy is brought to an emergency department in Bendigo, Vic, with 24 hours of diarrhoea and vomiting. He has moderate dehydration: dry mucous membranes, reduced urine output and slightly sunken eyes. He has refused or vomited small, frequent amounts of oral rehydration solution given over the last hour despite ondansetron. He is alert, with normal perfusion.

Which one of the following is the most appropriate next step?','["Nasogastric rehydration with oral rehydration solution", "IV fluid bolus", "Loperamide", "Switch to diluted apple juice and keep trying orally", "Admit and keep nil by mouth"]'::jsonb,'A','CORRECT ANSWER

A. Nasogastric rehydration with oral rehydration solution

---

The RCH Gastroenteritis guideline recommends oral rehydration solution first. If oral rehydration fails and there is an ongoing risk of dehydration, rapid or slow NG rehydration may be appropriate. If NG is not tolerated, the child should have IV rehydration if clinically dehydrated. Breastfeeding should continue.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
In paediatric gastroenteritis, if ORS fails, use NG rehydration before IV fluids (and IV if NG is not tolerated).

---

