• {"name": "RCH Clinical Practice Guideline: Gastroenteritis", "url": "https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/Gastroenteritis/"}','easy','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "batch03", "review_status": "draft_pending_owner_review", "tags": ["ZQ-0382", "zyntra_bank", "batch03", "level_2", "task_management", "group_child_health", "sys_paediatric_medicine", "irtb_-0.70", "pending_owner_review"], "clinician_task": "management", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"RCH Clinical Practice Guideline: Gastroenteritis\", \"url\": \"https://www.rch.org.au/clinicalguide/guideline_index/Gastroenteritis/\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"B": "He is not shocked and does not have severe dehydration.", "C": "Antidiarrhoeals are not recommended in children.", "D": "Diluted juice is an option for mildly dehydrated children refusing ORS, but oral intake has already failed here.", "E": "This would make his dehydration worse."}, "key_takeaways": ["In paediatric gastroenteritis, if ORS fails, use NG rehydration before IV fluids (and IV if NG is not tolerated)."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,-0.7,0.5,'expert_prior','author_estimate (batch03)'),
('36a28561-bcb8-5cb2-a393-5ae11eba92b7'::uuid,'ZQ-0383','child-health','paediatric-medicine','A 4-year-old girl weighing 17 kg is seen in an emergency department in Albury, NSW, with gastroenteritis. She has mild dehydration and keeps vomiting with every attempt to drink oral rehydration solution.

Which one of the following is the most appropriate antiemetic plan?','["Ondansetron 8 mg", "Metoclopramide", "A single oral dose of ondansetron 4 mg, then retry oral rehydration solution", "Prochlorperazine suppository", "Antiemetics are contraindicated in children"]'::jsonb,'C','CORRECT ANSWER

C. A single oral dose of ondansetron 4 mg, then retry oral rehydration solution

---

The RCH Gastroenteritis guideline says a single dose of ondansetron can be given to support hydration in children over 6 months with persistent nausea or vomiting. The weight-based doses are 8–15 kg: 2 mg; 15–30 kg: 4 mg; over 30 kg: 8 mg. A 17 kg child gets 4 mg.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Ondansetron in children over 6 months: 2 mg (8–15 kg), 4 mg (15–30 kg), 8 mg (over 30 kg).

---

