('9e5c6ad3-275b-5623-8a73-e5a58b57f025'::uuid,'ZQ-0384','child-health','paediatric-medicine','A 3-year-old girl in Mackay, Qld, has had right ear pain and a fever of 38.4 °C since yesterday. Otoscopy shows a red, bulging right tympanic membrane with no discharge. She is well, eating and drinking, and has no other medical problems. She is not an Aboriginal or Torres Strait Islander child.

Which one of the following is the most appropriate management?','["Amoxicillin for 10 days", "Regular simple analgesia such as paracetamol or ibuprofen; no antibiotics", "Ear wick and topical antibiotic drops", "Urgent ENT referral for myringotomy", "Oral prednisolone and a decongestant"]'::jsonb,'B','CORRECT ANSWER

B. Regular simple analgesia such as paracetamol or ibuprofen; no antibiotics

---

The RCH Acute otitis media guideline states that most cases of AOM in children resolve without treatment and antibiotics are not recommended. Pain should be treated with adequate, regular simple analgesia. Watch for complications such as mastoiditis. Some higher-risk groups have different recommendations.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Uncomplicated AOM in a well child: regular simple analgesia, no antibiotics.

---

