('38d84f9d-aa98-51d5-aae2-630110a9f6b0'::uuid,'ZQ-0385','child-health','paediatric-medicine','A 6-year-old boy in Ipswich, Qld, has had infrequent hard stools for 8 months and recent soiling of his underwear. Abdominal examination shows a palpable faecal mass. He has no red flags: normal growth, normal neurological examination of the lower limbs, and meconium passed in the first day of life.

Which one of the following is the most appropriate initial management?','["Oral disimpaction with high-dose macrogol at home, then move straight to maintenance therapy", "Phosphate enema in the clinic", "Glycerol suppositories daily", "Increase dietary fibre alone", "Abdominal X-ray before any treatment"]'::jsonb,'A','CORRECT ANSWER

A. Oral disimpaction with high-dose macrogol at home, then move straight to maintenance therapy

---

The RCH Constipation guideline prefers oral outpatient disimpaction with macrogol for faecal impaction. Rectal treatment with suppositories or enemas should be avoided (at most a one-off dose may be considered). Once disimpaction is achieved, maintenance treatment should start immediately and continue, along with behavioural measures such as toilet routines.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Paediatric faecal impaction: oral macrogol disimpaction, then maintenance immediately. Avoid enemas.

---

