('e0043ad4-48cb-543a-9d98-5d73f66c1979'::uuid,'ZQ-0386','child-health','paediatric-medicine','An 18-month-old girl is brought to a GP clinic in Broome, WA, with two days of diarrhoea. Her mother is worried about dehydration. The child''s health record shows she was weighed at a clinic visit 5 days ago, before she became unwell.

Which one of the following is the most accurate way to assess her degree of dehydration?','["Weigh her now and compare with her recent pre-illness weight", "Capillary refill time alone", "Serum urea", "Urine specific gravity", "Presence of tears"]'::jsonb,'A','CORRECT ANSWER

A. Weigh her now and compare with her recent pre-illness weight

---

The RCH Dehydration guideline states that the difference between pre-morbid weight and current weight is the most accurate assessment of dehydration. If a recent weight (within 2 weeks) is not available, history and examination are used to estimate dehydration.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
The change from pre-illness weight is the most accurate measure of dehydration.

---

