('d4a878f0-5002-5809-a700-99141fb851a4'::uuid,'ZQ-0387','child-health','paediatric-medicine','A 5-year-old boy falls from a 1-metre playground platform in Wollongong, NSW, and bumps his forehead. His father saw a brief loss of consciousness lasting a few seconds before he cried. He has not vomited and has no headache. He now has a GCS of 15 and is playing, with a small frontal haematoma. He has no bleeding disorder, takes no anticoagulants, has no VP shunt and has no neurodevelopmental disorder.

Which one of the following is the most appropriate approach?','["Immediate CT brain because of the loss of consciousness", "Discharge immediately with no observation", "A period of observation with regular neurological observations, with CT if he deteriorates or symptoms persist", "Skull X-ray", "MRI brain under general anaesthetic"]'::jsonb,'C','CORRECT ANSWER

C. A period of observation with regular neurological observations, with CT if he deteriorates or symptoms persist

---

The RCH Head injury guideline lists a history of loss of consciousness as a relative indication for imaging. If more than one relative indication is present, the child is observed and neuroimaging is considered. For a mild head injury with a risk factor, the child is observed for up to 4 hours after the injury, with 30-minutely neurological observations for the first 2 hours and then hourly. A persistent headache, ongoing vomiting, a GCS of 14 or persistent altered mental status means further observation and likely investigation. He can go home once he has been back to a normal conscious state for at least an hour, is acting normally and is tolerating fluids.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Mild head injury with a single risk factor: observe for up to 4 hours with neurological observations, and image if he deteriorates.

---

