('12723c2c-ca68-57b1-bc30-a0f7117b3f6f'::uuid,'ZQ-0388','child-health','paediatric-medicine','A 3-year-old boy with atopic dermatitis is brought to a clinic in Toowoomba, Qld. For two days he has had rapidly spreading, painful clusters of monomorphic punched-out erosions on his face and neck, with some small vesicles. He has a fever of 38.6 °C and is irritable. His eczema had been flaring.

Which one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?','["Impetiginised eczema", "Eczema herpeticum", "Eczema flare only", "Varicella", "Contact dermatitis"]'::jsonb,'B','CORRECT ANSWER

B. Eczema herpeticum

---

Monomorphic punched-out erosions and vesicles appearing in an area of active eczema, with fever and systemic upset, are typical of eczema herpeticum (disseminated HSV). The RCH Eczema guideline lists this as a complication needing prompt antiviral treatment (IV in severe cases), with consultation with the paediatric team. Periorbital involvement needs urgent ophthalmology review.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Punched-out erosions in eczematous skin with fever suggest eczema herpeticum, which needs prompt antiviral treatment.

---

