('2151f20f-b15a-56ba-bca1-bb9082b682c3'::uuid,'ZQ-0389','mental-health','psychosis','A 39-year-old man with treatment-resistant schizophrenia has been stable on clozapine 400 mg daily for two years under shared care. He sees his GP in Logan, Qld, for a routine review. His blood monitoring is up to date and normal. He says he feels ''fine'' and has no complaints.

Which one of the following is the most important additional history to take at this visit?','["Bowel habit, including stool frequency and any straining, pain or distension", "Family history of schizophrenia", "Childhood developmental history", "Caffeine intake only", "History of night sweats"]'::jsonb,'A','CORRECT ANSWER

A. Bowel habit, including stool frequency and any straining, pain or distension

---

Australian Prescriber notes that constipation affects 15–60% of people on clozapine at any time, is potentially life-threatening and should be treated and prevented aggressively. Clozapine-induced gastrointestinal hypomotility can progress to obstruction or ileus. Patients often do not volunteer symptoms, so bowel habit should be asked about at every review, and preventive aperients started at the first sign of constipation.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Ask every patient on clozapine about bowel habit. Constipation is common and can be fatal.

---

