('debf3b8d-f768-5121-8f0f-5b7eee9e65a4'::uuid,'ZQ-0390','mental-health','mood-disorders','A 45-year-old woman in Bunbury, WA, stopped venlafaxine 225 mg daily abruptly four days ago after 3 years of treatment because she felt well. She now has dizziness, ''electric shock'' sensations in her head when she moves her eyes, vivid dreams, irritability and tearfulness. She has no suicidal thoughts. Her symptoms began within 48 hours of stopping.

Which one of the following is the most likely explanation?','["Relapse of major depression", "Serotonin syndrome", "Vestibular neuritis", "Hypomania", "Antidepressant withdrawal symptoms"]'::jsonb,'E','CORRECT ANSWER

E. Antidepressant withdrawal symptoms

---

The Australian Prescriber article on stopping antidepressants (2026) describes withdrawal symptoms that include physical symptoms (dizziness, sensitivity to light and sound, ''brain zaps'' or electric shock sensations, vivid dreams, akathisia) and emotional symptoms (anxiety, irritability, low mood, tearfulness). Abrupt cessation, longer duration of therapy and certain drugs (venlafaxine, duloxetine, paroxetine) raise the risk. Her rapid onset after stopping, with brain zaps, points to withdrawal rather than relapse.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Brain zaps, dizziness and mood symptoms soon after abruptly stopping venlafaxine suggest withdrawal.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "Wallis K. Stopping antidepressants: when and how. Australian Prescriber 2026", "url": "https://australianprescriber.tg.org.au/articles/stopping-antidepressants-when-and-how.html"}','moderate','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "batch03", "review_status": "draft_pending_owner_review", "tags": ["ZQ-0390", "zyntra_bank", "batch03", "level_3", "task_data_interpretation_synthesis", "group_mental_health", "sys_mood", "irtb_+0.40", "pending_owner_review"], "clinician_task": "data_interpretation_synthesis", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"Wallis K. Stopping antidepressants: when and how. Australian Prescriber 2026\", \"url\": \"https://australianprescriber.tg.org.au/articles/stopping-antidepressants-when-and-how.html\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "Relapse usually develops more gradually and does not cause brain zaps.", "B": "This occurs with excess serotonergic drug, not withdrawal.", "C": "This does not explain the emotional symptoms or the timing.", "D": "Her features are not those of elevated mood."}, "key_takeaways": ["Brain zaps, dizziness and mood symptoms soon after abruptly stopping venlafaxine suggest withdrawal."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,0.4,0.5,'expert_prior','author_estimate (batch03)'),
('0df8122f-8b60-529d-8a6d-cd13c3d89f41'::uuid,'ZQ-0391','mental-health','mood-disorders','A 30-year-old man in Canberra, ACT, has taken sertraline 100 mg daily for 9 months for a first episode of depression, which is now in full remission. After discussing it with his GP, he wants to stop. He has never had difficulty stopping a medication before.

Which one of the following is the most appropriate plan?','["Stop today", "Taper over 12 months using 1 mg reductions", "Switch to fluoxetine and stop in 2 days", "Halve the dose every day for 4 days", "Taper by 25–50% dose reductions over about 2–6 weeks, for example 100 mg to 50 mg to 25 mg and then stop"]'::jsonb,'E','CORRECT ANSWER

E. Taper by 25–50% dose reductions over about 2–6 weeks, for example 100 mg to 50 mg to 25 mg and then stop

---

Australian Prescriber (2026) states that in patients at low risk of withdrawal symptoms, guidelines recommend 25 to 50% dose reductions over 2 to 6 weeks, for example sertraline tapered at 2-weekly intervals from 100 mg to 50 mg, then 25 mg, then stopped. Patients at higher risk may need smaller reductions over months.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Low-risk antidepressant cessation: reduce by 25–50% over 2–6 weeks.

---

