• {"name": "Wallis K. Stopping antidepressants: when and how. Australian Prescriber 2026", "url": "https://australianprescriber.tg.org.au/articles/stopping-antidepressants-when-and-how.html"}','easy','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "batch03", "review_status": "draft_pending_owner_review", "tags": ["ZQ-0391", "zyntra_bank", "batch03", "level_2", "task_management", "group_mental_health", "sys_mood", "irtb_-0.90", "pending_owner_review"], "clinician_task": "management", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"Wallis K. Stopping antidepressants: when and how. Australian Prescriber 2026\", \"url\": \"https://australianprescriber.tg.org.au/articles/stopping-antidepressants-when-and-how.html\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "Stopping abruptly can cause withdrawal symptoms.", "B": "This is for higher-risk patients or those who have had difficulty stopping before.", "C": "This is not the recommended approach for a low-risk patient.", "D": "This is too fast."}, "key_takeaways": ["Low-risk antidepressant cessation: reduce by 25–50% over 2–6 weeks."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,-0.9,0.5,'expert_prior','author_estimate (batch03)'),
('110208bf-44c5-5e39-8616-d9c7f6f6e0d3'::uuid,'ZQ-0392','mental-health','mood-disorders','A 26-year-old woman in Darwin, NT, has a new diagnosis of bipolar I disorder after a manic episode, which has been treated as an inpatient. She is not pregnant, is sexually active and may want children in future. The psychiatry registrar is choosing a long-term mood stabiliser and suggests sodium valproate because it worked quickly for her uncle.

Which one of the following is the most appropriate response regarding valproate?','["Valproate is the preferred first-line option for women of reproductive age", "Valproate is acceptable if she takes folic acid 5 mg", "Valproate is safe if stopped once pregnancy is confirmed", "Valproate is only a concern for epilepsy, not bipolar disorder", "Valproate should not be used unless other treatments are ineffective or not tolerated, because of its high teratogenic risk"]'::jsonb,'E','CORRECT ANSWER

E. Valproate should not be used unless other treatments are ineffective or not tolerated, because of its high teratogenic risk

---

The Australian Epilim (sodium valproate) Product Information states that valproate should not be used in female children and women of childbearing potential unless other treatments are ineffective or not tolerated. This is because valproate has a high teratogenic potential, and children exposed in utero have a high risk of congenital malformations and neurodevelopmental disorders. Treatment must be started and supervised by a specialist. Other options should be used first.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Avoid valproate in women of childbearing potential unless other treatments are ineffective or not tolerated.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "Australian Product Information: Epilim (sodium valproate)", "url": "https://medsinfo.com.au/api/documents/Epilim_PI?format=pdf"}','difficult','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "batch03", "review_status": "draft_pending_owner_review", "tags": ["ZQ-0392", "zyntra_bank", "batch03", "level_4", "task_management", "group_mental_health", "sys_mood", "irtb_+1.30", "pending_owner_review"], "clinician_task": "management", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"Australian Product Information: Epilim (sodium valproate)\", \"url\": \"https://medsinfo.com.au/api/documents/Epilim_PI?format=pdf\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "The Product Information says the opposite.", "B": "Folic acid does not remove the risk.", "C": "Exposure in early pregnancy carries the risk.", "D": "The restriction applies across indications."}, "key_takeaways": ["Avoid valproate in women of childbearing potential unless other treatments are ineffective or not tolerated."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,1.3,0.5,'expert_prior','author_estimate (batch03)'),
('bebc650d-4b02-5e31-9f22-09d90ad58e5d'::uuid,'ZQ-0393','mental-health','psychosis','A 48-year-old woman on clozapine 500 mg daily is brought to an emergency department in Shepparton, Vic. She has had three days of abdominal distension, colicky pain and vomiting, and today passed watery stools. Temperature 38.0 °C, pulse 118/min. Her abdomen is distended and tender, with absent bowel sounds. She also takes oxybutynin.

Which one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?','["Viral gastroenteritis", "Clozapine-induced agranulocytosis with neutropenic enterocolitis as the first diagnosis", "Neuroleptic malignant syndrome", "Irritable bowel syndrome", "Clozapine-induced gastrointestinal hypomotility with obstruction or ileus and overflow diarrhoea"]'::jsonb,'E','CORRECT ANSWER

E. Clozapine-induced gastrointestinal hypomotility with obstruction or ileus and overflow diarrhoea

---

Australian Prescriber lists red flags of serious clozapine-induced constipation: abdominal pain, distension, vomiting, overflow diarrhoea, absent bowel sounds, and signs or symptoms of sepsis. Anticholinergic drugs such as oxybutynin add to the risk and should be avoided when possible. This needs urgent hospital assessment.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Clozapine plus pain, distension, vomiting, overflow diarrhoea and absent bowel sounds suggests hypomotility or ileus, which is an emergency.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "Australian Prescriber. Clozapine in primary care", "url": "https://australianprescriber.tg.org.au/articles/clozapine-in-primary-care.html"}','difficult','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "batch03", "review_status": "draft_pending_owner_review", "tags": ["ZQ-0393", "zyntra_bank", "batch03", "level_5", "task_data_interpretation_synthesis", "group_mental_health", "sys_psychosis", "irtb_+1.50", "pending_owner_review"], "clinician_task": "data_interpretation_synthesis", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"Australian Prescriber. Clozapine in primary care\", \"url\": \"https://australianprescriber.tg.org.au/articles/clozapine-in-primary-care.html\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "Absent bowel sounds and distension in a patient on clozapine point to hypomotility. The ''diarrhoea'' is likely overflow.", "B": "Possible but less likely. The full blood count should still be checked.", "C": "There is no rigidity or altered mental state.", "D": "Fever and absent bowel sounds exclude this."}, "key_takeaways": ["Clozapine plus pain, distension, vomiting, overflow diarrhoea and absent bowel sounds suggests hypomotility or ileus, which is an emergency."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,1.5,0.5,'expert_prior','author_estimate (batch03)'),
('95bc0d65-7876-557e-b4df-3c1893423654'::uuid,'ZQ-0394','mental-health','psychosis','A 22-year-old man with first-episode schizophrenia in Wollongong, NSW, is to start olanzapine after a shared decision with his psychiatrist. His BMI is 24 kg/m², and his fasting glucose and lipids are normal. He is worried about weight gain.

Which one of the following is the most appropriate additional measure at the time of starting olanzapine?','["Wait until he has gained 7% of body weight before acting", "Start orlistat", "Add topiramate routinely", "Start metformin at the same time, alongside lifestyle advice", "No measures because his BMI is normal"]'::jsonb,'D','CORRECT ANSWER

D. Start metformin at the same time, alongside lifestyle advice

---

The RANZCP-endorsed INTEGRATE guidelines recommend lifestyle interventions for everyone and co-initiating metformin when starting antipsychotics with high metabolic risk, such as olanzapine or clozapine, to limit weight gain.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
INTEGRATE: co-initiate metformin with olanzapine or clozapine.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "RANZCP. INTEGRATE: international guidelines for the algorithmic treatment of schizophrenia", "url": "https://www.ranzcp.org/clinical-guidelines-publications/clinical-guidelines-publications-library/integrate-international-guidelines-for-the-algorithmic-treatment-of-schizophrenia"}','moderate','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "batch03", "review_status": "draft_pending_owner_review", "tags": ["ZQ-0394", "zyntra_bank", "batch03", "level_3", "task_management", "group_mental_health", "sys_psychosis", "irtb_+0.50", "pending_owner_review"], "clinician_task": "management", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"RANZCP. INTEGRATE: international guidelines for the algorithmic treatment of schizophrenia\", \"url\": \"https://www.ranzcp.org/clinical-guidelines-publications/clinical-guidelines-publications-library/integrate-international-guidelines-for-the-algorithmic-treatment-of-schizophrenia\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "INTEGRATE recommends preventive co-initiation of metformin.", "B": "This is not the recommended agent.", "C": "This is not the first-line recommendation.", "E": "Weight gain with olanzapine is predictable."}, "key_takeaways": ["INTEGRATE: co-initiate metformin with olanzapine or clozapine."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,0.5,0.5,'expert_prior','author_estimate (batch03)'),
('d7c19b56-d8cb-5b26-948a-ac7216ba0dfa'::uuid,'ZQ-0399','population-health-ethics','infectious-diseases','A 40-year-old man in Lismore, NSW, was scratched on the forearm this morning while removing a live microbat from his shed. He has never had rabies vaccine. He has captured the bat in a box, and the local public health unit says a lyssavirus result can be available within 24 hours. He has washed the wound well with soap and water.

Which one of the following is the most appropriate next step?','["Release the bat and start PEP", "Arrange ABLV testing of the bat and withhold rabies vaccine and HRIG while the result is awaited", "Start HRIG and vaccine immediately regardless of testing", "No action because it was only a scratch", "Check his rabies antibody titre"]'::jsonb,'B','CORRECT ANSWER

B. Arrange ABLV testing of the bat and withhold rabies vaccine and HRIG while the result is awaited

---

The Australian Immunisation Handbook advises that, if possible, the bat should be kept and tested for Australian bat lyssavirus. After wound management, HRIG and rabies vaccine are withheld if the bat''s ABLV status will be available within 48 hours of the exposure. If not, PEP starts as soon as possible. Bites to the head and neck are an exception: PEP is given promptly, preferably within 48 hours, even if the animal is being tested. If the bat tests negative, no PEP is needed.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
After a bat exposure, test the bat. If the result will be available within 48 hours (and there is no head or neck bite), PEP can wait.

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "Australian Immunisation Handbook. Rabies and other lyssaviruses", "url": "https://immunisationhandbook.health.gov.au/contents/vaccine-preventable-diseases/rabies-and-other-lyssaviruses"}','moderate','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "batch03", "review_status": "draft_pending_owner_review", "tags": ["ZQ-0399", "zyntra_bank", "batch03", "level_3", "task_data_gathering", "group_population_health_ethics", "sys_infectious_disease", "irtb_+0.40", "pending_owner_review"], "clinician_task": "data_gathering", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"Australian Immunisation Handbook. Rabies and other lyssaviruses\", \"url\": \"https://immunisationhandbook.health.gov.au/contents/vaccine-preventable-diseases/rabies-and-other-lyssaviruses\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "Testing the bat avoids unnecessary PEP.", "C": "With a result due within 48 hours and a limb wound, PEP can be withheld.", "D": "Bat scratches are category II or III exposures.", "E": "Serology is not used to decide on PEP for an unvaccinated person."}, "key_takeaways": ["After a bat exposure, test the bat. If the result will be available within 48 hours (and there is no head or neck bite), PEP can wait."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,0.4,0.5,'expert_prior','author_estimate (batch03)'),
('3d84d251-ff3a-5203-9c94-ea9874b05ab0'::uuid,'ZQ-0400','population-health-ethics','infectious-diseases','A 52-year-old wildlife carer in Townsville, Qld, was bitten through the skin on the hand by a flying fox two days ago. The bat escaped and cannot be tested. She has never had rabies vaccine and is immunocompetent. The wound has been washed.

Which one of the following is the most appropriate post-exposure prophylaxis?','["Rabies vaccine on days 0 and 3 only", "No PEP because Australia is rabies-free", "HRIG alone", "Five vaccine doses with serology", "HRIG infiltrated into and around the wound, plus rabies vaccine on days 0, 3, 7 and 14"]'::jsonb,'E','CORRECT ANSWER

E. HRIG infiltrated into and around the wound, plus rabies vaccine on days 0, 3, 7 and 14

---

The Australian Immunisation Handbook bat-exposure algorithm states that a non-immune, immunocompetent person with a category II or III bat exposure should receive HRIG (if 7 days or less since the first vaccine dose) and 4 vaccine doses intramuscularly on days 0, 3, 7 and 14. HRIG is infiltrated in and around the wound as much as possible, with any remainder given intramuscularly away from the vaccine site.

---

KEY LEARNING POINT
Non-immune, immunocompetent person with a bat bite: HRIG plus 4 vaccine doses (days 0, 3, 7, 14).

---

REFERENCES
• {"name": "Australian Immunisation Handbook. Rabies and other lyssaviruses", "url": "https://immunisationhandbook.health.gov.au/contents/vaccine-preventable-diseases/rabies-and-other-lyssaviruses"}','difficult','{"source": "zyntra_qbank", "source_batch": "batch03", "review_status": "draft_pending_owner_review", "tags": ["ZQ-0400", "zyntra_bank", "batch03", "level_4", "task_management", "group_population_health_ethics", "sys_infectious_disease", "irtb_+1.10", "pending_owner_review"], "clinician_task": "management", "guideline_reference": "{\"name\": \"Australian Immunisation Handbook. Rabies and other lyssaviruses\", \"url\": \"https://immunisationhandbook.health.gov.au/contents/vaccine-preventable-diseases/rabies-and-other-lyssaviruses\"}", "incorrect_answer_explanations": {"A": "This is the regimen for previously immunised people.", "B": "Australian bats can carry ABLV.", "C": "Vaccine is always needed.", "D": "This is the regimen for immunocompromised people."}, "key_takeaways": ["Non-immune, immunocompetent person with a bat bite: HRIG plus 4 vaccine doses (days 0, 3, 7, 14)."], "imported_by": "0057"}'::jsonb,1.1,0.5,'expert_prior','author_estimate (batch03)')
